AI Consultation Team  
Industry House 
Department of Industry, Science and Resources 
10 Binara St 
Canberra ACT  2600 
 
9 October 2024 
  
Dear Helen, 
Proposals paper for introducing mandatory guardrails for AI in high-risk settings 
CPA Australia represents the diverse interests of more than 173,000 accounting and finance professionals, working in 
over 100 countries and regions around the world. We make this submission in response to the  Proposals Paper for 
introducing mandatory guardrails for AI in high-risk settings  on behalf of our members and in the broader public 
interest. 
CPA Australia supports the development of regulatory options for the safe and responsible use of AI in high-risk 
situations. Such regulatory options need to strike a balance between protecting the community from damaging uses of 
AI and encouraging greater development and deployment of such technology in Australia. We acknowledge that a 
major difficulty in developing such options is the lack of well-established regimes from other jurisdictions that Australia 
can draw on for considered examples. EU, US and Canadian experiences in regulating AI are in their infancy and are 
evolving. 
We believe a well-designed, clear and proportionate regulatory regime targeted at likely high-risk AI applications is 
appropriate to support the safe application of such technology. Such regulation must be supported by education and 
tools, including for non-high-risk AI applications.  
We emphasise the importance of the regulatory regime targeting likely high-risk applications of AI. If the mandatory 
guardrails are required for AI applications that could be high-risk but are unlikely to occur, it may lead to significant 
numbers of businesses being required to apply the mandatory guardrails to their use of AI. This will discourage AI 
uptake by business, especially amongst smaller businesses.  
Unfortunately, what constitutes ‘high-risk AI’ in the Proposals Paper is unclear. We therefore recommend the 
government issue a further consultation paper with a clearer definition of the term and use cases of what is ‘high-risk’ 
and what is not. It is important for the examples to include situations where it may be 50-50 whether the AI application 
is high-risk. 
If the government proceeds to draft regulation based on this Proposals Paper, the unintended consequences could be 
significant, and it will stifle the development and deployment of AI. More thinking and consultation are needed with a 
broader range of stakeholders, especially small business groups, before the government proceeds to exposure draft 
legislation. 
In short, the government needs to be much clearer on what constitutes high-risk and not high-risk AI, and who are 
developers, deployers and users of AI. A narrower definition of general purposed AI (GPAI) is also needed. Without a 
tighter, more targeted regulatory regime, the government risks undermining rather than supporting AI use. 
    
 
2 
 Summary of key recommendations: 
 To decide whether a principles or list-based definition of high-risk and non-high-risk AI applications is most 
effective, the government should issue a further consultation paper with: 
o changes to the principles to make it clearer what constitutes high-risk and non-high-risk AI applications 
o a developed list of high-risk and non-high risk use cases. 
 This further consultation paper also needs clearer definitions of developers, deployers and users, and a narrower 
definition of GPAI. The definition of ‘developer’ should exclude businesses that input additional data into an AI 
model.  
 If the government is to go ahead with a principles approach to deciding high-risk AI, it should explicitly include 
“probability” or “likelihood” into the considerations of whether an AI use is high-risk.  
 The government focus resources on developing education and tools to help small business use AI safely and 
responsibly. Examples include the Essential Eight  for cybersecurity and the Cyber Wardens program .  
 The mandatory guardrails should not apply to all GPAI models. They should only apply to likely high-risk uses of AI, 
regardless of the AI model used.  
 We agree that the regulatory regime should be consistent with other jurisdictions, but only if it is well-designed, 
proportionate, and targeted at likely high-risk uses of AI.  
 Responsibility for the development of this policy be transferred to a central policy agency such as Treasury.  
Please refer to the Appendix below for our detailed responses to the questions in the Proposals Paper. If you have any 
queries, please contact Gavan Ord on gavan.ord@cpaaustralia.com.au . 
Yours sincerely, 
      
Ram Subramanian FCPA 
Interim Head of Policy and Advocacy  
 
 
  
  
 
3 
 Appendix 
Below is CPA Australia’s response to some of the questions raised in the Proposals Paper, plus other observations. 
Q1: Low risk use cases that may be unintentionally captured 
We support the principle that the definition of ‘high-risk’ should not ‘inadvertently catch low-risk applications’. We 
recommend that the wording of the principles designating AI systems as high risk be tightened to prevent this or be 
replaced with a list of high-risk and non-high-risk applications of AI. For example, ‘the risk of adverse impacts to an 
individual’s physical or mental health or safety,’ should be limited to risks that are both ‘foreseeable and likely’.  
Accounting firms and businesses use AI built into financial software to aid their analysis of the financial performance of 
a business. Based on the principles, this should be considered ‘low risk’, however there is a potential (albeit low) that 
such financial analysis could have an adverse impact on the mental health of the people involved in the business under 
review. Accounting firms and businesses use AI in this situation to improve their analysis of business performance, 
however such AI tools do not make decisions - AI in this situation is meant to improve advice but not to replace the 
professional judgment of accountants. Due to its possible (but unlikely) adverse impact on mental health, would AI 
used to analyse the performance of a business be considered high-risk? 
Is it the intention of the government that the proposed principle on health and safety only target AI focused on health 
care? There are many potential non-health care AI applications that could unintentionally affect a user’s mental health, 
although the potential for such AI to directly contribute to adverse mental health issues is low.  
We recommend that “f” in the Proposed principles of designating an AI system as high-risk on page 19 of the 
Proposals Paper be amended to: 
The severity, extent and likelihood of those impacts outlined in principles (a) to (e) above. 
Explicitly including probability into the principles (rather than mentioning it in passing) of what is high-risk AI will better 
target the regime. It will reduce the risk of the Principles unintentionally capturing large numbers of AI applications 
that could be high-risk, however the likelihood is remote. 
Q3: Do the proposed principles, supported by examples, give enough clarity and certainty on high-risk 
AI settings and high-risk AI models? Is a more defined approach, with a list of illustrative uses, 
needed? 
 If you prefer a list-based approach (similar to the EU and Canada), what use cases should we 
include? How can this list capture emerging uses of AI? 
 If you prefer a principles-based approach, what should we address in guidance to give the 
greatest clarity? 
As currently drafted, the proposed principles may unintentionally capture many low-risk AI use cases – potentially 
requiring many more businesses to implement mandatory guardrails for AI uses than is expected.  We therefore 
recommend the government develop a list-based approach to show high and non-high-risk uses of AI for 
consultation.  This would allow the community to compare and consider whether a principle or list-based approach is 
best.   
 
4 
 To aid with clarity,  we also recommend that the government include in future consultation papers a graphical 
representation of when an AI application is high-risk (red light) or not (green light).  For example, is an accounting 
practice using AI to assist it analyse the financial performance of a business considered as high-risk or not? 
6. Should mandatory guardrails apply to all GPAI models? 
No. We recommend that the mandatory guardrails should only apply to high-risk uses on GPAI . Having a non-
risk-based regulatory approach for GPAI and a risk-based approach for other AI models creates uncertainty. 
Further, the proposed definition of GPAI is broad. Therefore, if the mandatory guardrails were to apply to all GPAI 
models, it would unnecessarily bring many businesses into the regulatory regime.  
We are concerned that the Proposals Paper suggests the regulation of GPAI be based on what technology could do, 
rather than what it’s intended to do. There are potentially large numbers of possible applications of technology – 
however many of those possible applications are unlikely to occur.  
The Proposals Paper says it only relates to “advanced, highly-capable GPAI”. It is unclear what that means. If this is the 
intention, then the definition of GPAI should be narrowed to reflect this. The definition should also include examples.  
8. Do the proposed mandatory guardrails appropriately mitigate the risks of AI used in high-risk 
settings? Are there any guardrails that we should add or remove? 
The Proposals Paper is unclear on how the mandatory guardrails are to be oversighted. This is a fast-evolving area, and 
the government will need to be agile to deal with emerging threats and opportunities. It will also be a new area of law, 
with the risk of unintended consequences high. Waiting for a post-implementation review after three or five years will 
not be sufficient -  we therefore recommend the Government set up a taskforce to continuously monitor the 
effectiveness of the guardrails and make regular suggestions for improvement and changes.  The quicker the 
government can address unintended consequences and emerging threats and opportunities; the more effective the 
regime will be.  
Guardrail 1 says organisations must publicly disclose their AI accountability processes. Will such disclosures be subject 
to independent assurance? If the objective is to build public trust, then we suggest the government consider requiring 
such disclosures be independently assured, especially for larger businesses.  
Guardrail 8 requires businesses that use high-risk AI to report ‘adverse incidents and significant model failures’ to 
developers. We are not certain of the value of imposing this reporting obligation on users of AI. We suggest the 
government make such reporting voluntary. 
Guardrail 10 requires organisations to show that they have adhered to the guardrails for high-risk AI systems by 
carrying out conformity assessments. It is unclear who will undertake such conformity assessments. If it is done by the 
business itself, they should not be able to self-certify their compliance with the mandatory guardrails.    
 
5 
 10. Do the proposed mandatory guardrails distribute responsibility across the AI supply chain and  
throughout the AI lifecycle appropriately? For example, are the requirements assigned to  
developers and deployers appropriate? 
We recommend that the definition of ‘developer’ be changed so that it excludes businesses that input 
additional data into an AI model.  
Any business inputting data into a generative AI model is training that model. As presently defined, they could be 
considered ‘developers’ – this is inconsistent with the generally understood definition of developer. Many businesses 
would be surprised to learn that under the Proposals Paper they would be considered ‘developers’ because they are 
entering data into an AI model. 
We also recommend that the government provide examples of when a business is considered a “developer” and 
a “deployer” to aid understanding of these terms. 
12. Do you have suggestions for reducing the regulatory burden on small-to-medium sized businesses  
applying guardrails? 
The best way to reduce the regulatory burden on small to medium-sized enterprises (SMEs) is to target the design of 
the mandatory guardrails so they only apply to likely high-risk uses of AI. A well-designed, targeted regulatory regime 
will reduce the risk of the mandatory guardrails applying to low-risk AI uses. 
To help smaller businesses use AI safely and responsibly,  we recommend that the government focus its efforts on 
developing educative tools and other resources . The 70-page Voluntary AI Safety Standard will by itself, not lift the 
governance skills and capabilities of smaller businesses to use AI responsibly. Such businesses are time and resource 
constrained – that is, they have little to no capacity to adapt a 70-page standard into their business.  
Such standards do not replace the need for simple, easy to apply guidance for SMEs.  We therefore recommend that 
the principles in that standard should be turned into short guidance materials and tools like the Essential Eight 
for cyber security.  It should also include tips on how to mitigate risks for businesses using AI such as data poisoning 
and prompt engineering. Education on the safe and responsible application of AI could be delivered to smaller 
businesses through a program like Cyber Wardens.  
For example, a small business may use an open AI platform to develop marketing material, customer correspondence 
or analyse their performance. To help such businesses use AI safely in that situation, the government should develop 
simple guidance, tools and education. 
SMEs using high-risk AI will struggle to meet the proposed accountability process for Guardrail 1 on page 36 of the 
Proposals Paper. They will also struggle to set up, implement and maintain their own risk management processes as 
needed in Guardrail 2 and set up processes for people to contest AI-enabled decisions in Guardrail 7. We suggest the 
government consider tailored regulatory requirements for SMEs that must apply the mandatory guardrails. Such 
requirements should be developed in consultation with small business organisations.    
 
6 
 13. Which legislative option do you feel will best address the use of AI in high-risk settings? What 
opportunities should the government take into account in considering each approach? 
CPA Australia does not have a set position on which legislative option would be best. However, from the available 
information, Option 1 - adopting guardrails within existing regulatory frameworks, maybe best. To ensure consistency 
and address gaps, this could be done through an omnibus Bill, with an overarching framework articulated in the 
drafting instructions.  
Other observations 
We recommend moving responsibility for the development of this policy to a central policy agency such as 
Treasury.  What is being proposed will have an economy-wide and whole of government impact. We believe Treasury 
is better placed to progress this issue. 
