October 8, 2024
Tech-Policy Lead Joonho Kwak,
Principal Researcher Jaeyoung Hwang
AI Policy and Research Team, Telecommunications Technology Association(TTA)
47, Bundang-ro, Bundang-gu,
Seongnam-city, Gyeonggi, Republic of Korea

RE: Introducing mandatory guardrails for AI in high-risk settings: proposals paper

TTA appreciates the opportunity to submit opinions to the Australian Government related to its Report on “Introducing mandatory guardrails for AI in high-risk settings: proposals paper”. TTA in Korea is a key organization responsible for developing standards and promoting advancements in the telecommunications and information technology sectors. Recently, TTA has been closely working with MSIT(Ministry of Science and ICT)  in order to help foster the development of trustworthy AI systems in the nation. We have analysed the report and provided feedback below, which we hope will help the Australian government to ensure trust in AI.



TTA’s Comments

(Feedback1: High-Risk classification): 
With the emergence of AGI, it is important to classify risky AI services and conduct appropriate evaluations or regulations. In this regard, the 6 principles for analyzing and determining the risks of AI services in advance are considered timely. However, since AI services classified as high-risk may have different risk levels, it is thought that leveling criteria for classifying them are necessary.

For example, as in the case of the paper ‘Position: Levels of AGI for Operationalizing Progress on the Path to AGI’, it is necessary to classify risky AI services by classifying them into 6 levels according to AGI levels and to set different regulatory proposals or mandatory guardrails for each level and enforce them.

(Feedback2: Guardrails):
The report presents a total of 10 guardrails for ensuring Trustworthy AI  and proposes three measures to enforce them. First,  we was particularly impressed that, unlike most Trustworthy AI guidelines which often do not mention specific enforcement measures after their establishment, this report provides practical strategies for implementation.

The 10 guardrails are presented as requirements that should be considered to ensure Trustworthy AI. We agree that these requirements are essential as preventive measures for securing trustworthiness. However, we believe that including more specific technical requirements within the guardrails would be beneficial, as it would provide clearer guidance for their implementation.
Currently, TTA has also developed a guide that presents technical requirements similar to the this report. In this regard, we would like to share our work as follows:

OECD.AI: https://oecd.ai/en/catalogue/tools/guidelines-for-development-of-trustworthy-ai
Guideline: we have attached our English version guidelines in this email.

The development guidelines are in the process of establishing a cooperation for NIST RMF Crosswalk, and are in the process of collecting actual guideline application use cases through certification program. In the future, if collaboration is possible, it would be good to continue technical dialogue to specify the guidelines between the two countries.
