The Chief Executive Officer of the Law Council is Dr  James Popple.  The Secretariat serves the Law 
Council nationally and is based in Canberra.  
The Law Council ’s website is www.lawcouncil.au .  
 
Introducing mandatory guardrails for AI in high-risk settings  4 Acknowledgement s 
This submission is informed by contributions from the following Constituent Bodies:  
• the Law Institute of Victoria;  
• the Law Society of New South Wales ; and  
• the Queensland Law Society . 
 
The Law Council also acknowledges the guidance of its Futures Committee, and the 
contributions of its Business Law Section, specifically the Intellectual Property Committee 
and the Media and Communications Committee .  
 
Introducing mandatory guardrails for AI in high-risk settings  5 Introduction 
1. The Law Council  of Australia welcomes the opportunity  to provide a submission to the 
Department  of Industry, Science and Resources  in response to its P roposal s Paper  
on the introduction of mandatory guardrails for artificial intelligence ( AI) in high -risk 
settings . 
2. In our view, the Proposals Paper has been thoughtfully prepared .  It is based on the 
premise that AI (in its various forms) has the potential to improve social and economic 
well-being, whilst  also bringing  both foreseeable and unforeseeable risks of harm that 
may not be adequately remediable under Australia’ s current regulatory framework . 
3. The tenor of the Proposals Paper is to minimise the risk of potential harms without 
unduly limiting innovation or driving it offshore .  The Law Council considers it critical 
that any regulatory framework adopted in Australia should be flexible and scalable to 
future technological change, and so will allow for sufficient innovation, while 
maintaining appropriate guardrails .  To this end, we agree with the Proposals Paper ’s 
focus on a risk -based approach with an emphasis on preventative measures . 
4. In August 2023 , we provided a submission to the Department as part of its ‘ Safe and 
Responsible AI in Australia’  consultation.1  In that submission, we  recommended that 
the Australian Government should adopt a risk -based approach that, in the short term, 
focuses on the regulation of high- risk AI technology and applications that are clearly 
egregious and pose significant risks to the privacy and way of life of Australians , as 
per the approach in the European Union Artificial Intelligence Act  (EU AI Act ).2  
In particular, we recommended enhanced regulation of the collection and use of 
biometric information (such as the use of automated facial recognition technology) and 
‘social scoring’ practices, and options to reduce the risk of people being misled by AI -
generated fakes and scams.3 
5. The Proposals Paper canvasses various approaches to AI regulation across other, 
pro-active jurisdictions, including the European Union, Canada and the United States 
of America, and has, commendably,  collated considerable research.  The Proposals 
Paper  poses important questions and offers pathways forward for legislating the use of 
AI in high- risk settings, and the use of general -purpose AI ( GPAI ) in Australia, 
underpinned by proposed mandatory guardrails that  closely mirror the  existing 
Voluntary AI Safety Standard.4 
6. Identification of high- risk settings can facilitate further discussion on the additional 
guardrails and the extent of regulatory intervention required to reduce the likelihood of harm, whether through existing, technology -neutral frameworks (such as privacy laws, 
healthcare, anti -discrimination, and financial sector laws) or, in specific cases, through 
dedicated legislation.  
7. Doing business in Australia means doing business in a multi -obligation environment 
that is already complex .  For many, this complexity is a barrier to entry .  There are 
consumer laws (dealing with misleading and deceptive conduct), privacy laws (governing use of sensitive personal information including biometric information), 
corporations laws (imposing directors ’ duties requiring due care and skill), online 
safety laws, intellectual property rights laws, anti -discrimination laws and sector -
 
1 Law Council of Australia, Submission  to Department of Industry, Science and Resources, Safe and 
Responsible AI in Australia (17 August 2023) 16-21.  
2 Ibid 16-21.  
3 Ibid. 
4 Commonwealth of Australia, Voluntary AI Safety Standard (August 2024).   
 
Introducing mandatory guardrails for AI in high-risk settings  6 specific laws .  Many of these laws have the capacity to —and already —deal with data 
and technology use and misuse.  
8. However, given the pace of technological change and the economy -wide embrace of 
AI (albeit in many cases still being tested), we agree that mandatory guardrails 
applying across the AI supply chain and throughout the AI lifecycle could be effective 
to reduce the likelihood of harms .  There is merit in limiting specific AI -related laws to 
high risk and GPAI use cases, until a further, more meaningful review can be 
undertaken, based on a growing body of evidence and global AI use case examples.  
Intersection with other consultations  
9. As a general note, the Proposal s Paper  should not be viewed in isolation.  There are 
several  ongoing and recent consultations that will be relevant to discussions about  the 
regulatory approaches to  AI in Australia, including:  
• the Parliamentary Joint Committee on Human Rights ’ inquiry into Australian’s 
Human Rights Framework, and its report of May 2024;5 
• the Privacy Act Review Report and the subsequent Government response, in 
addition to the first tranche of reforms to the Privacy Act  1988  (Cth)  introduced 
to Parliament in September 2024 .6  The Privacy and Other Legislation 
Amendment Bill is currently the subject of an inquiry by the Senate Legal and 
Constitutional Affairs Legislation Committee , due to report by 14  November  
2024;7 and 
• recommendations arising from the  inquiry into the opportunities and impacts 
for Australia arising out of the uptake of AI technologies, currently being 
undertaken by the  Select Committee on Adopting Artificial Intelligence, due to 
report by  26 November  2024.8 
10. We also anticipate that a variety of  action items and recommendations will arise  from 
the Copyright and Artificial Intelligence Reference Group, established by the Commonwealth Attorney -General in December 2023.
9 
Bans on high-risk AI  
11. In terms of whether the Government should consider banning any high- risk use cases, 
we consider that  any regulatory response should carefully balance mitigating risk with 
enabling innovation in AI . 
12. In our view, there is merit in banning AI practices that have an unacceptable level of 
risk.  That is, where the risk cannot be mitigated, or the consequences of the practice 
pose unacceptable and irremediable harm to individuals and communities. 
13. However, to ensure that innovation in AI is not simultaneously stifled, any ban of AI practices could potentially be implemented by way of subordinate legislation, to allow 
 
5 Parliamentary Joint Committee on Human Rights, Inquiry into Australia’s Human Rights Framework (Report , 
May 2024)  
6 Attorney-General’s Department, Privacy Act Review Report  (February 2023); Commonwealth of Australia, 
Government Response : Privacy Act Review Report (September 2023); Privacy and Other Legislation 
Amendment Bill 2024 (Cth);  
7 Senate Legal and Constitutional Affairs Committee, Privacy and Other Legislation Amendment Bill 2024 
[Provisions] (Web Page , 2024).  
8 Select Committee on Adopting Artificial Intelligence ( Web P age, 2024).  
9 Commonwealth Attorney -General’s Department, Copyright and Artificial Intelligence Reference Group ( Web 
Page, 2024).   
 
Introducing mandatory guardrails for AI in high-risk settings  7 sufficient flexibility while AI continues to evolve .  We suggest  that any ban contain 
sufficient certainty in the definition and interpretation of the prohibited practice, and 
clarity about why the risk is unacceptable.  
14. It may be instructive to refer to the AI practices that are prohibited under the EU AI Act 
for their incompatibility with individual and collective rights and fundamental values, 
such as the rule of law.10 
15. AI practices  that are  prohibited under Article  5 of the EU AI Act include:  
(a) Subliminal techniques that  can materially distort a person ’s behaviour by 
impairing their ability to make an informed decision in a way that causes, or is reasonably likely to cause, them significant harm.  
(b) Exploiting the vulnerabilities of a person or specific groups of people 
(for example, due to their age, disability or economic situation) which can 
materially distort their behaviour in a way that causes, or is reasonably likely to 
cause, them significant harm.  
(c) Social scoring systems based on known, inferred, or predicted personality 
characteristics which cause detrimental or unfavourable treatment that is 
disproportionate, or used in a way that is  unrelated to the context in which the 
data was originally collected.  
(d) Risk assessment systems that  assess the risk of a person commit ting a crime 
or re-offending  (except in support of a human assessment based on verifiable 
facts).  
(e) Indiscriminate or untargeted web- scraping for the purposes of creating or 
enhancing facial recognition databases.  
(f) Emotion recognition systems in the workplace or educational institutions 
(except for medical or safety reasons).  
(g) Biometric categorisation systems used to infer characteristics, such as race, political opinions , or religion.  
(h) Real-time, remote biometric identification systems in publicly accessible 
spaces for the purpose of law enforcement except —subject to safeguards and 
within narrow exclusions —searching for victims of abduction, preservation of 
life, and finding suspects of certain criminal activities .  ‘Real-time’ must mean 
live or near -live material, to avoid short recording delays circumventing this  
prohibition.  
16. We note that some categories  of prohibited AI practices —such as those in paragraphs  
(e) and (f) , above—could leave the meanings of ‘indiscriminate ’, ‘untargeted’ , and 
‘emotional recognition’ open for interpretation .  However, if sufficient certainty is able to 
achieved, this would assist to provide clear expectations and safeguards , from the 
outset , about the necessity of protecting fundamental rights and values .  This certainty 
could also remove ambiguity for organisations that  might seek to interpret the 
principles in a way that compromises the rights of the end user.  
 
10 See Law Council of Australia, Rule of Law Principles (Policy Statement , March 2011).   
 
Introducing mandatory guardrails for AI in high-risk settings  8 Defining high-risk AI  
17. The Proposals Paper sets out the following principles , designed to guide consistent 
assessment by organisations of whether the use of an AI system is high- risk, and 
therefore  subject to mandatory guardrails shaping its development and use, based on 
intended and foreseeable uses:11 
In designating an AI system as high- risk due to its use, regard must be 
given to:  
a.   The risk of adverse impacts to an individual’ s rights recognised in 
Australian human rights law without justification, in addition to 
Australia ’s international human rights law obligations  
b.   The risk of adverse impacts to an individual’ s physical or mental 
health or safety  
c.   The risk of adverse legal effects, defamation or similarly significant 
effects on an individual  
d.   The risk of adverse impacts to groups of individuals or collective 
rights of cultural groups  
e.   The risk of adverse impacts to the broader Australian economy, 
society, environment and rule of law  
f.   The severity and extent of those adverse impacts outlined in 
principles (a) to (e) above.  
Principles versus a list -based approach  
18. We recognise that the nature of risk in the development and use of Al is a sliding 
scale, and risk frameworks are likely to evolve over time.  In this respect, AI ‘risk’ is 
contextual, with risks arising from its use by individuals and organisations, as well as from the technology itself.
12 
19. There are also different kinds of Al systems —that is, expert, scientific and generative 
systems—some of which may be used every day, which are not differentiated in the Proposals Paper .  This distinction is important when considering risks associated with 
Al systems, and therefore any accompanying regulatory responses . 
20. We acknowledge the advantages of a principles -based approach, in that it avoids  
inadvertently capturing some uses of AI that are, in fact, low -risk.  However, while this 
approach seeks to respond to the contextual basis of risk of harm that may be 
presented by an Al tool, it may create challenges in terms of providing sufficient clarity 
and certainty  for organisations .  For example:  
• It is currently unclear whether proposed principles (c) and (d) are intended to 
be applied to people who are not users of the AI system, such as third parties, 
or groups whose rights could be impacted; and  
• proposed principle (e) , in particular , lacks specificity . 
 
11 Proposals Paper, 19.  
12 Lisa M. Given, 18 January 2024, Australia plans to regulate ‘high-risk’ AI. Here’s how to do that 
successfully, The Conversation (Online , 18 January 2024).   
 
Introducing mandatory guardrails for AI in high-risk settings  9 21. We consider that, as a general matter, it is appropriate for the creator of an AI system 
to consider all third parties and groups whose legal rights are—or could be—affected 
by the creation, operation or use of the AI system . 
22. The proposed principles for categorising AI as ‘ high risk ’ are broadly framed, and this 
will likely make them difficult to apply in practice.  For example, the Proposals Paper 
considers what the characteristics of high risk might  mean, but it does not  seek to  
define the circumstances under which an Al system will be high risk or produce a high-
impact  result . 
23. On the other hand, while a list -based approach has the advantage of promoting clarity 
and certainty, we are concerned that a static list may not evolve quickly enough to 
capture novel use cases.  
24. Accordingly , we support a balanced approach to regulating AI, combining both a 
prescriptive and principles -based approach to high- risk settings . 
25. Given the narrow scope of the Proposals Paper (focusing only on AI in high-risk settings) there would be benefit in being more prescriptive by  giving  illustrative  
examples of what constitutes high- risk scenarios .  We consider that  more clarity and 
certainty will result from combining a principles approach with non- exhaustive lists of 
examples under each of the principles, similar to the approach taken to the guidance 
provided under  the Security of Critical Infrastructure Act 2018 (Cth) . 
26. We acknowledge that AI systems are likely to develop more rapidly than any 
accompanying guidance materials .  As such, if a principles -based approach is 
adopted, it is important that the principles are workable, and can be readily applied by the AI system owner to determine whether a particular AI system is high- risk. 
Intellectual property  rights  
27. It is a question of policy as to whether an AI system is to be designat ed as high- risk 
(and thereby attracting the ex ante regulatory obligations of such systems) if it may 
involve —in its creation or use —the infringement of intellectual property rights. 
28. If this is not the intended policy outcome, then some clarification to the definition of ‘high- risk’ may be required.  Clarification would also be required if it is intended that 
the risk of infringement of only certain types of intellectual property rights —such as 
Indigenous cultural and intellectual property rights —are to be taken into account in the 
‘high- risk’ determination.  
29. On the other hand, if it is intended that the risk of infringement of intellectual property rights generally may make an AI system ‘ high- risk’, then it is important that intellectual 
property law reform in relation to AI systems keep s pace with the proposed ex  ante 
regulatory regime.  This will ensure that there is sufficient clarity concerning, for 
example, questions of infringement . 
30. We note the  law reform activity currently  being conducted by the Attorney -General ’s 
Department in relation to copyright law , in consultation with the Copyright and Artificial 
Intelligence Reference Group,
13 and to the proposed development of stand- alone 
legislation in respect of Indigenous cultural and intellectual property.14 
 
13 Commonwealth Attorney -General’s Department, Copyright and Artificial Intelligence Reference Group ( Web 
Page, 2024).  
14 See Commonwealth Department of Infrastructure, Transport, Regional Development, Communications and 
the Arts, New stand -alone legislation to protect ICIP – Topics for discussion (Web Page , 2024).   
 
Introducing mandatory guardrails for AI in high-risk settings  10 31. We consider that any implementation of the proposed mandatory guardrails may lend 
a degree of urgency to those related reforms, especially if  the mandatory guardrails 
create unintended policy settings . 
Comments on specific principles  
Principle (c): The risk of adverse legal effects, defamation or similarly significant 
effects on an individual  
32. From an intellectual property perspective, proposed principle (c) may be difficult to apply in circumstances where (as is currently the case) the copyright infringement 
risks of inpu ts and outputs of AI systems have not been clarified, unless  it is intended 
that principle (c) be interpreted very narrowly .  This means that principle (f) ( “the 
severity and extent of those adverse impacts ”) may also be difficult to apply in 
practice.  
33. For instance, the discussion on principle (c) in the Proposals Paper focuses on the 
effect s on an individual who engages with the AI system,
15 rather than posing the 
broader question of whether the AI system affects the legal rights of any individual .  
In an intellectual property context, this includes: 
(a) copyright owners whose rights are infringed by the training of the AI system, the use of accessing of information by the AI system in response to a request 
or instruction, and/or the AI system ’s creation of a work in response to queries 
or instructions; and/or  
(b) individuals whose ability to make use of works generated by AI, with minimal human input, might be curtailed by the inability to obtain licences, or, at least, 
have clarity regarding copyright status and ownership. 
34. As such, it is not clear whether  principle (c) is intended to require the AI system owner 
to assess only the impact of the system on the legal rights of individual end users who 
are actually engaging with that AI system.  
Principle (d): The risk of adverse impacts to groups of individuals or collective 
rights of cultural groups  
35. As with  principle (c), we consider that principle (d) could be difficult for an AI system 
owner to assess, particularly if the assessment is based on the potential of the AI system to cause such an adverse impact.  
36. In response to  Consultation Question 2, relating to First Nations peoples , we make the 
following preliminary comments for the Department ’s consideration in respect of 
proposed principle (d) : 
• In Australia, the consequences of algorithmic bias have the potential to be 
especially concerning with respect to First Nations peoples .  Algorithmic bias 
must, therefore, be front of mind when introducing Australia’ s AI regulatory 
regime. 
• It is especially important that AI regulations strive to ensure that inputs, 
throughputs (being material or items passing through a system or process) ,16 
and outputs are free from bias .  The risk of inadequate, or unacceptably 
 
15 Proposals Paper, 22.  
16 Cambridge Dictionary (Online at 25 September 2024) ‘throughput’.   
 
Introducing mandatory guardrails for AI in high-risk settings  11 limited, datasets, being used to train AI technologies must be addressed 
through suitable risk management procedures, including greater transparency . 
• Further, concerns have been raised within our membership about the utilisation of ICIP, including literary and artistic works, in the training of AI .  
We recently provided a submission to the Department of Infrastructure, 
Transport, Regional Development, Communication, and the Arts in response to its consultation on a new legislative framework to address and protect 
Indigenous cultural and intellectual property rights,
17 and look forward to 
continuing to engage with the Government as its consideration of that  
framework progresses.  
37. Beyond this, we submit that the Department should conduct meaningful consultation 
with First Nations peoples and communities to ensure that the proposed principles (particularly principle (d)) will sufficiently capture high -risk AI that may inappropriately 
use Indigenous cultural and intellectual property, or may, more broadly, utilise data 
about individuals within First Nations communities.  
Social utility  
38. Feedback received by the Law Council suggests that what may be missing from both 
the principle and list -based approaches is a focus on the social utility of the use case 
from which the high or low risks emanate.  If a graduated regulatory approach is 
adopted—which would allow more time for use cases to be developed and risks  
(particularly the ‘ unknown unknowns ’) to emerge for a more informed legislative 
approach (if needed) —then it may be helpful to move beyond a focus on use case 
‘type’. 
39. In addition to use case ‘ type’, further consideration could be given to the context or the 
‘purpose’ of the use case and its social utility .  This might require a re -shaping of the 
current risk -based approach to one that is both risk -based and purpose- driven .  This 
may also be helpful in the absence of a formal bill of rights in Australia, which exist in the other jurisdictions  that are  currently undertaking pro -active AI regulatory reforms.  
40. One source of guidance in this regard is Annex III of the EU AI Act , which sets out a 
list of high -risk AI systems, referred to in Article  6.2.  However, Article  6.3 sets out 
exceptions to the blanket approach in Article  6.2: 
… an AI system referred to in Annex III shall not be considered to be 
high- risk where it does not pose a significant risk of harm to the health, 
safety or fundamental rights of natural persons, including by not materially influencing the outcome of decision making.
18 
41. Article  6.3 provides the approach required to meet the exception.  Article  6.4 requires 
that a provider seeking to use the exception shall “document its assessment before 
that system is placed on the market or put into service”. 
42. Examples of how such an approach could apply against identified risk domains are 
outlined below . 
 
17 Law Council of Australia, Submission  to the Department of Infrastructure, Transport, Regional 
Development, Communication, and the Arts, A new legislative framework to address and protect Indigenous 
cultural and intellectual property rights (5 July 2024).  
18 EU AI Act , Article 6.2.   
 
Introducing mandatory guardrails for AI in high-risk settings  12 Biometrics  
43. In the Proposals Paper, biometrics is listed as a domain.19  Its high -risk use case 
description is an AI system “used to identify or categorise individuals, assess 
behaviour or mental state, or monitor and influence emotions ”.20 
44. To help inform the overall assessment of ‘ risk’ in the biometrics domain, identifying the 
outcome to be achieved,  and, by extension,  its social utility might also be highly 
beneficial .  The question could be asked: is the purpose behind using AI systems 
processing higher -risk biometric information to diagnose medical conditions, to 
increase retail sales, to predict criminality, to screen for employment prospects , or to 
assess health insurance eligibility ? 
45. Articulating the purpose ( or the ‘end game’ ) sought to be achieved could  be useful in 
establishing not only whether —and to what extent —any proposed mandatory 
guardrails apply , but whether the ultimate purpose justifies those higher risks , 
notwithstanding compliance with the guardrails . 
46. For example, there may be limited social utility in a high- risk biometrics use case 
where the outcome is to socially ‘ score ’ individuals for employment or for eligibility to 
rent or purchase property in certain areas, or to qualify for organ donation.  A purpose -
driven approach might require consistency with, or be  complementary to, express and 
implied rights in the Commonwealth Constitution  and other Australian laws —and 
representative democracy.  
47. We note that  the exception provided in Article  6.3 of the EU AI Act would exempt some 
applications of biometrics, despite biometric AI systems being defined as high risk .  
For example, a medical diagnosis would potentially fall into the exemption by meeting 
Article  6.3(a): “the AI system is intended to perform a narrow procedural task ”. 
Administration of justice and democratic processes 
48. A further example is in the domain of the administration of justice and democratic 
processes.21 In the Proposals Paper, its high- risk use case description is  as follows:  
AI systems used for making a determination about an individual in a 
court or administrative tribunal, such as systems used for evaluating 
facts, evidence and submission to proceedings. ”22 
49. Concerns arise in this domain partly because the law is not reliant on approximation or 
a synthesis of multiple sources .23  As such, i n common law jurisdictions, AI ’s 
information- synthesis approach to case law may not identify accurately legal 
precedent or a case’ s ratio decidendi  for the purposes of applying precedent . 
50. Accuracy is also critical to the democratic process and the ability to exercise 
democratic voting rights based on accurate information.  Those democratic rights and 
processes are clearly compromised by misinformation and disinformation.  
 
19 Proposals Paper, 26 61.  
20 Ibid 26.  
21 Ibid 26, 63.  
22 Ibid 26.  
23 Research itself has traditionally been an important ‘tool of trade’ for lawyers.  It is also notable that lawyers’ 
use of ‘search’ engines, like Google, in their research has provided multiple possible answers to assess rather 
than an attempt at ‘the’ answer, resulting from a culmination of sources that can be unknown and 
undiscoverable.   
 
Introducing mandatory guardrails for AI in high-risk settings  13 51. Concerns further arise in the law and, by extension,  the legal profession , because 
laws are both ancient and living.  It would be difficult to use tools that have ‘cut-off 
dates ’ for training data and expect them to deliver ‘ justice ’.  It is critical that any 
reliance on GPAI in assisting to dispense the law is appropriate and proportionate.  
52. Other important aspects in the context of the administration of justice use cases are 
that the law : 
• is language- based;  
• often contains a mental or cognitive element in order to apply it ; and 
• is often open to interpretation or nuance . 
53. Rather than being codifiable or reduced to logical ‘ if this, then that ’ rules, the Australian 
legal system recognises the need for flexibility  when applying the law to the facts of a 
specific case .  We acknowledge, however, that this is not the case in all matters : for 
example, where Parliaments have passed strict liability laws . 
54. Nonetheless , in most cases , the law is applied by practitioners and judges using a 
process of interpretation and the exercise of professional judgment .  The application of 
the law is then relied on by clients, parties in legal proceedings and , ultimately , the 
broader community .  If the application of the law is incorrect, then l awyers and judges 
are answerable to the participants in the legal system . 
55. In 1881, O liver Wendell Holmes observed that ‘ the life of the law has not been logic: it 
has been experience’ .24  Not every experience, even today, is captured or represented 
in our digital lives by data or  a digital footprint .  Another way to see the law is ‘ open-
textured’ , as Professor Simon Chesterman notes; it is why a rule such as ‘ no vehicles 
in the park ’—an example first offered by H.L.A . Hart25—is open- textured enough to 
extend to cars, trucks, bicycles , and even  drones , but perhaps not toy trucks or toy 
trains.26  This demonstrates how  the law often requires interpretation and experience.  
56. Defining high- risk use cases in the administration of justice as those making 
determinations about an individual may benefit from the further assessment of the purpose or social utility underlying the use case.  For instance, i f the purpose 
(or outcome)  is to ease the burden on courts and increase efficiency in administering 
the law, does the margin for error or false convictions that using AI may bring, justify its 
use?  Or should a social utility dimension also require that the efficient administration 
of justice must not result in denying any  person justice?  
57. We consider that t he advantage of a risk -based, purpose- driven approach could help 
ensure that high- risk uses are limited to those having social utility (by reference to 
consistency with, or being complementary to existing Australian law) .  This approach 
could further minimis e any unintended, unforeseeable harms or outcomes that are 
inconsistent with the rule of law and our democratic processes.  
 
24 Anonymous (Oliver Holmes), ‘Book Notices’ (1880) 14 American Law Review 233, 234.  
25 H.L.A. Hart, Positivism and the Separation of Law and Morals , 71 HARV. L. REV. 593, 607 (1958), cited in 
Frederick Schauer, “A critical guide to vehicles in the park” New York University Law Review, 83:1109, 2008.  
26 Simon Chesterman, ‘We, the Robots: Regulating Artificial Intelligence and the Limits of the Law’ (NUS Law 
Working Paper No 2022/015, Cambridge University Press, 2021).   
 
Introducing mandatory guardrails for AI in high-risk settings  14 Categories of use to be designated ‘ high-risk ’ 
58. If a general principles -based approach is to be retained, we suggest that certain 
categories of use should also be designed ‘ high- risk’.  Such designation would have 
the effect of imposing a requirement that such uses comply with the proposed 
mandatory guardrails.  
59. This approach would ensure greater clarity for organisations developing and deploying 
AI, and enhanced transparency for users, whilst ensuring that novel applications are 
appropriately regulated . 
60. We a cknowledge  work that is already underway at the Commonwealth level to reform 
the Privacy  Act, combat misinformation and misinformation, review the Online Safety 
Act 2021 (Cth), and develop a consistent legal framework for automated decision-
making by government .  In light of this work, we submit that consideration should be 
given to designating the following categories as high- risk: 
(a) Uses of AI systems to make decisions impacting people’ s health, safety and 
human rights, or which have an impact on an individual ’s legal rights or 
entitlements, or a similarly significant effect on an individual.  
(b) Uses of AI involving the input of sensitive data in medical and health 
environments (and in other industries involving sensitive information 
processing).  
(c) Uses of AI systems to generate or replicate personhood or aspects of 
personhood (i.e., appearances, voices, etc.) for any identifiable individuals, 
especially minors.  
(d) Uses of AI -generated content in political campaigns.  
61. Regardless, we suggest that the Department seek expert guidance from independent 
and appropriately qualified individuals as to the risk profile of categories of AI use.  
Options for introducing mandatory guardrails  
62. The Proposal  Paper poses three options for implementing the proposed mandatory 
guardrails, namely:  
• Option 1 : Adapting existing regulatory frameworks to include the proposed 
mandatory guardrails;27 
• Option 2 : Introducing framework legislation, with associated amendments to 
existing legislation;28 or 
• Option 3 : A whole -of-economy approach through the introduction of a new AI -
specific Act.29 
 
27 Proposals Paper, 46-48.  
28 Ibid 48-49.  
29 Ibid 49-51.   
 
Introducing mandatory guardrails for AI in high-risk settings  15 General comments 
63. The Law Council has previously indicated a preference for a ‘ multi- faceted’ approach 
to the regulation of AI based on the following elements:  
• an initial focus on the regulation of high -risk AI technology and applications;  
• a strengthened and precautionary approach to Al regulation, where there is 
evidence that existing laws and regulations are insufficient to address the issues and harms arising;  
• comprehensive regulatory reform to ensure that the use of automated decision making, including by the Australian Government, is transparent, capable of 
review, and consistent with administrative law principles;  and 
• consideration of the regulatory models adopted by other jurisdictions to 
determine an optimal and bespoke approach for Australia that reflects the 
nuances of Australia’ s pre -existing constitutional and regulatory framework, 
and unique local market environment.30 
64. We have previously expressed support for the expansion of current legislation and, 
where necessary, new targeted legislation, rather than seeking to explicitly regulate AI 
via a comprehensive ‘ AI Act ’. 
65. The majority of responses we received in response to this consultation favour an 
approach that  minimises the potential for conflict—or duplication—with existing laws . 
Views  on the proposed options  
66. When consulting on the Proposals Paper, it was apparent that there  are differing views  
amongst the legal profession as to the appropriateness of each option, particularly the 
merits of a new AI -specific Act , as proposed in Option 3.  
67. For example, the Law Institute of Victoria and Queensland Law Society have  
expressed a preference for Option 3, supporting the approach taken by the EU AI Act, which presents a clear articulation of what  uses of AI pose the greatest risk , with a 
consequent regulatory focus in those areas .  These bodies submit that Option 3 would 
best address the use of AI in high- risk settings, and would allow for additional reforms 
to be undertaken, by way of legislative amendment, as AI development and use 
evolves over time.  
68. We acknowledge that Option 3 may allow for Australia to be more consistent with the 
broader global scheme, given the recent implementation of the EU AI Act, and the UK’s plan to introduce a standalone AI Act .  This comment is also informed by the 
consideration that the guardrails are intended to only apply to AI in high- risk settings, 
thereby limiting the scope of application to a limited set of AI uses and outcomes . 
69. Other contributors , however,  have  raise d concern s that the Option 3 approach will 
most likely result in overlap in existing laws and may require frequent amendment for 
currency to keep up with the fast -evolving pace of AI development .  We also recognise 
that the implementation of this option may not be feasible in the immediate short -term.  
 
30 See, e.g, Law Council of Australia, Submission  to Department of Industry, Science and Resources, Safe 
and Responsible AI in Australia (17 August 2023) 1 1; Submission  to Senate Committee on Adopting Artificial 
intelligence, Inquiry into the opportunities and impacts of the uptake of artificial intelligence technologies in 
Australia (20 May 2024).   
 
Introducing mandatory guardrails for AI in high-risk settings  16 70. On the other hand, Option 1 appears to be beneficial for reducing the risk of 
inconsistency or duplication across regulatory frameworks .  However, it is likely to be a 
more time -consuming project, given the need to review and amend existing laws to 
embed the guardrails .  Further, this domain- specific approach could lead to 
inconsistent regulatory outcomes and enforcement mechanisms . 
71. We observe that Option 2 provides an appropriate middle -ground approach between 
Options 1 and 3, by creating a new framework legislation that mitigates risks , while 
allowing  AI development .  In addition, this option could potentially be implemented as 
an interim measure until new legislation prescribing specific uniform requirements is introduced.  
72. On balance, we  do not consider a comprehensive AI -specific Act is required at this 
early stage.  However, the gradual introduction of regulations that address high- risk AI 
and applications, having social utility, will be helpful in minimising risks, both known and unknown.  
73. We consider that the areas in which regulation is likely to be introduced earliest are the areas regulated by the:  
• Australian Competition and Consumer Commission; 
• Australian Communications and Media Authority;  
• eSafety Commissioner; and 
• Office of the Australian Information Commissioner . 
74. These four regulators have a mechanism for collaborating on digital platform regulations called the Digital Platform Regulators Forum ( DP-REG ).  The DP -REG is 
welcome initiative that is likely to facilitate the effective introduction of domain-specific 
solutions.  
Proposed mandatory guardrails  
General comments  
75. The Proposals Paper sets out proposed mandatory guardrails for AI systems used in 
high- risk settings, as well as high- risk GPAI systems.31  The guardrails are 
preventative measures that would require developers and deployers of AI used in 
high- risk settings to take specific steps across the AI lifecycle.32 
76. It is acknowledged that nine of the 10 proposed mandatory guardrails is  identical to 
the Voluntary AI Safety Standard .  The Voluntary AI Safety Standard has been 
mapped against Australia’ s AI Ethics Principles and broadly aligns with  relevant  
existing frameworks33 (such as the ISO 42001:2023 and the US National Institute of 
Standards and Technology ’s Artificial Intelligence Risk Management Framework) . 
77. We agree that  the guardrails should apply to all GPAI models in Australia.  As GPAI 
models may exceed even what are currently perceived as their potential uses, there 
should be a willingness on the part of the government to modify the regulatory 
approach as the technology becomes more sophisticated.  
 
31 Proposals Paper, 35-42.  
32 Ibid 19.  
33 See, e.g., the ISO/IEC 42001:2023  (Information Technology – Artificial Intelligence – Management system) ; 
US National Institute of Standards and Technology, Artificial Intelligence Risk Management Framework  
(January 2023).   
 
Introducing mandatory guardrails for AI in high-risk settings  17 78. As the mandatory guardrails are broadly framed, we suggest it would be beneficial to 
develop a more detailed set of minimum standards, derived from these guardrails .  
Organisations developing or deploying AI systems should subsequently be required to 
adopt these more detailed standards, unless they establish their own standards that 
are compliant with the overarching guardrails . 
79. For example, in respect of guardrail  3 (“protect AI systems, and implement data 
governance measures to manage data quality and provenance ”), we recommend that 
developers and deployers should be more specifically obliged to establish and 
implement measures that sufficiently protect the data that is involved in the 
development of AI systems, including personally identifiable information of any 
individuals .  We consider that it would be preferable if, unless an organisation 
developed its own standards to comply with guardrail 3, that organisation would be 
required to comply with the government -developed minimum standards, including one 
requiring the establishment and development of measures to protect personal data.  
Intellectual property enfor cement  
80. From an intellectual property enforcement perspective, proposed transparency in 
respect of the use and operation of AI (guardrail 7) will be of critical importance, as will 
establishing processes for people impacted (guardrail 8) .  The nature and accessibility 
of these processes will also be important to ensure that people or groups who are impacted can access information and exercise their rights . 
81. Specific  issues arise in relation to transparency in the context of intellectual property 
rights .  For example, there is a question whether a transparency obligation may 
require the disclosure of information concerning whether —and the extent to which—
copyright protected materials may have been used in the creation of an AI system  
(for example,  in training it ).  That is a question requiring detailed consideration of 
policy and practical matters , and we note that  it is currently being considered by the 
Copyri ght and Artificial Intelligence Reference Group . 
82. Further, questions  arise as to : 
• how any transparency obligation will avoid the risk of inappropriately requiring 
the disclosure of valuable trade secrets ; and  
• whether intellectual property rights holders should have a means of enforcing 
the transparency obligation.  
83. Consideration could particularly  be given to whether it is necessary to have an 
external means of reviewing the outcome of such a process .  Currently, in order to 
enforce legal rights, individuals would most likely be required  to resort to litigation, 
which is unlikely to be an efficient or affordable option.  
84. These matters illustrate the necessity for the proposed ex  ante guardrails to be 
accompanied by  reforms to  intellectual property law reform and  other forms of specific 
guidance.  
85. From a copyright perspective, we suggest that consideration be given to what remedies might be appropriate for an individual or group whose legal rights are 
impacted .  
For example, where a copyright work has been used as an input to train AI, 
or accessed in the process of answering a query, what remedy should be available to 
the copyright owner ?  We note that t hese questions are  currently  being explored in 
litigation in other jurisdictions .  However, those individual matters will not provide clear 
answers f or Australian copyright owners.   
 
Introducing mandatory guardrails for AI in high-risk settings  18 86. We also note the ongoing wider discourse about whether copyright infringement 
issues associated with AI systems might be addressed, or mitigated, by some form of 
licensing framework, statutory exceptions or defences to infringement, or some form of 
sui generis legislation . 
Protections for domestic end -users  
87. Recent commentary has observed that, even if the mandatory guardrails have 
extraterritorial effect —recognising that many AI developers are based outside of 
Australia —their efficacy in protecting Australian deployers and end- users may be 
low.34 
88. We suggest that further clarity is needed about  the protections that would be in force 
for end users within Australia under the current proposals .  Whilst enforcement will be 
critical, so too will be  ensuring that the legislative framework includes sufficient 
protections and avenues to pursue overseas developers or deployers.  
Specific comments  
89. We have received the following specific comments from our  membership about  each 
of the proposed mandatory guardrails. 
Proposed mandatory 
guardrail  Comments  
1. Establish, implement and 
publish an accountability 
process including 
governance, internal capability and a strategy for regulatory compliance.  The process for accountability needs to be a best 
practice process .   
2. Establish and implement a risk management process 
to identify and mitigate 
risks.   Examples of risk management processes or frameworks 
are necessary, particularly for an organisation that is the final end user of an AI system . 
We note that the Proposals Paper suggests that risk 
should be assessed on a use case basis .  However, this 
can be difficult for AI systems with multiple use cases, which supports our suggestion that  examples of risk 
management processes be provided.  
3. Protect AI systems, and implement data 
governance measures to 
manage data quality and provenance . Developers and deployers would benefit from guidance 
that establishes the benchmark for data quality, data provenance, and data security.  
4. Test AI models and systems to evaluate model performance and monitor the system once deployed . The testing of AI models and AI systems on an ongoing 
basis needs to be performed by persons with accreditation in the subject matter.  
 
