successfully, The Conversation (Online , 18 January 2024).   
 
Introducing mandatory guardrails for AI in high-risk settings  19 Proposed mandatory 
guardrail  Comments  
5. Enable human control or 
intervention in an AI 
system to achieve 
meaningful human 
oversight . This guardrail  is supported.  
6. Inform end -users 
regarding AI -enabled 
decisions, interactions with AI and AI- generated 
content . This guardrail is s upported. 
Given the recent  introduction of the Privacy and Other 
Legislation Amendment Bill 2024 (Cth) , it would be 
beneficial for further clarity to be provided on the 
relationship between this guardrail and the Bill ’s 
proposed requirement for disclosure in privacy policies 
where personal information is used in automated 
decision- making, which could involve AI systems.  
7. Establish processes for people impacted by AI 
systems to challenge use 
or outcomes . Dispute processes must be accessible and responsive.  However, members of our Business Law Section’s 
Intellectual Property Committee,  with experience 
engaging in the complaint processes for some g lobal 
entities, have reported that a complaint about a breach of legal rights, or a breach of terms of use, does not necessarily get answered.  
As such, processes should include clear guidelines for any challenge of the use or outcomes of AI systems to 
be dealt with—and resolved— within a reasonable 
timeframe , with clear communication mechanisms .   
8. Be transparent with other 
organisations across the 
AI supply chain about 
data, models and systems 
to help them effectively 
address risks.   We have consistently supported the need for transparency, on the basis that transparency brings robustness to AI regulation.  
Other issues that could be considered to support transparency include:  
• how the regulatory framework can respond to 
circumstances where the use of AI is not known.  
This will include continued exploration of ‘ marking ’ 
techniques to identify AI -generated outputs .  This 
would assist in clarifying for organisations and users the extent to which a product has an AI component;  
• where its use is difficult to contract out, the interaction between AI use and the unfair contract terms regime; and 
• requiring developers and distributors t o be 
transparent about  data providence and copyright 
works used, and how to implement options for opting 
out of machine learning.  
9. Keep and maintain records 
to allow third parties to 
assess compliance with guardrails .   The record- keeping processes should be consistent with 
external standards, with reference to examples such as the Archives Act 1983  (Cth) or the State Records Act 
1998 (NSW).   
 
Introducing mandatory guardrails for AI in high-risk settings  20 Proposed mandatory 
guardrail  Comments  
10. Undertake conformity 
assessments to 
demonstrate and certify 
compliance with the 
guardrails . We under stand that a ‘conformity assessment ’ is 
intended to be an accountability and quality assurance mechanism that relies on documentation captured pursuant to Guardrail s 1 and 9. 
Conformity assessments  should be carried out by an 
accredited third party, or by government entities or 
regulators, rather than the organisations themselves .  
The accountability and quality assurance metrics should 
be standardised.   
Other comments  
Environmental harm  
90. The Proposals Paper observes that the precautionary principle is usually applied 
where a technology has significant uncertainty about its risks , including environmental 
harms .  Nonetheless , we are concerned that environmental risks may not be 
sufficiently addressed in the Proposals P aper. 
91. We submit that this should be a core issue for consideration.  For example, 
environmental protection is a core value put forw ard in the EU Al Act with the energy 
consumption of Al models a central consideration.35  We support  the inclusion of  
enhanced environmental impact considerations in the Australian G overnment ’s 
regulatory response (whatever form that takes).  
Oversight  
92. Compounding the abovementioned concerns regarding uncertaint ies with a principles -
based approach  are issues of  ensuring compliance and enforcement . 
93. It has been suggested to us  that a mechanism of licensing is most appropriate.  
We note that there is some support within our membership for an independent 
oversight body that can consider testing and scientific results in a transparent way 
(at development and deployment levels) .  For example,  we note the Proposals P aper 
references Canada ’s requirements ‘for developers to  prepare and share model cards 
for machine learning models ’.36  Model cards aim to provide information about the 
trained machine learning model, and can include evaluation data, training data, 
performance metrics, ethical considerations, and any relevant model -specific details .37  
94. An oversight  body  with visibility of  such information  would support regulatory certainty 
and ensure that , where an Al tool has a particular impact, then appropriate 
enforcement and penalties regimes are applied. 
 
35 Sasha Luccioni et al,  The Environmental Impacts of AI —Policy Pr imer (Report , 2024) 8.  
36 Proposals Paper, 41.  
37 Ibid.  
 
Introducing mandatory guardrails for AI in high-risk settings  21 Use of AI to replicate personhood 
95. As a related matter, our Business Law Section’ s Intellectual Property Committee 
recommends that consideration be given to whether Australia needs specific laws to 
address the use of an individual’ s voice, or image, without consent, in the context of 
an AI system.38  These may be used, for example, in a political context ,39 or in an arts  
and entertainment  context (e.g., the use of a deepfake of a performer ’s voice).  
96. As Australia does not have a broad right of privacy that is attached to personal image, 
or rights of publicity, an individual who is affected by AI system outputs of this kind 
would currently be required to rely on laws relating to:  
(a) copyright, although this may be of limited utility, depending on whether an 
original copyright work can be identified as having been incorporated, and 
then only if the affected individual is the owner of that copyright work;  
(b) misleading and deceptive conduct, but principally if the individual ’s voice or 
image is used in certain commercial contexts;  or 
(c) defamation, noting that this may be difficult to prove, depending on the use. 
97. If there is no amendment to  Australian law, an individual who is affected by an AI 
system that generates this type of output  may not be able to point to an ‘ adverse legal 
effect ’ (as required under proposed principle (c) ). 
 
38 The recent Criminal Code Amendment (Deepfake Sexual Material) Act 2024 (Cth)  addresses similar issues, 
with respect to ‘deepfakes’ and pornographic content.  
