Tech Council of Australia www.techcouncil.com.au                        
Proposals paper for introducing mandatory guardrails for AI in high-risk settings  Tech Council of Australia Submission  October 2024 
techcouncil.com.au 
  
Tech Council of Australia www.techcouncil.com.au   Introduction Thank you for the opportunity to contribute to the consultation on the introduction of mandatory guardrails for AI in high-risk settings. The Tech Council of Australia (TCA) fully supports the Government’s efforts in progressing this important work to clarify Australia’s regulatory model for AI, and to ensure that Australia’s regulatory environment builds public trust and promotes the adoption of AI across the economy. The Tech Council is Australia’s peak industry body for the tech sector. We represent over 150 companies from a diverse cross-section of Australia’s tech sector, including companies working in business enterprise software, consumer software, tele-communications, and fintech. This includes companies that build and deploy AI-driven products and venture capital funds with investments in AI. AI is one of the most transformative technologies of our time, with the potential to offer our nation significant economic, social, environmental and strategic advantages. AI is already driving major advancements in areas such as healthcare, industry and public safety, and the future holds even greater potential. Generative AI alone has the potential to add between $45b billion - $115 billion a year to the whole Australian economy by 2030.1 Our research forecasts that AI also has the potential to create up to 200,000 jobs in Australia by 2030.2 In order to realise the benefits of this transformative technology, we must make clear, informed policy decisions that embrace a risk-based, technology-agnostic, and outcomes-focused approach that fosters innovation and economic growth through AI adoption. This will unlock the transformative potential of AI for Australia by adopting safe and responsible AI.  Australia has a unique opportunity to design an innovative regulatory approach that could be world-leading and gives Australia a competitive edge in the global technology landscape, while embedding strong safeguards. It is important for Australia’s AI governance framework to be internationally interoperable and to draw from the experiences of other jurisdictions to anticipate potential pitfalls and complexities. While the EU AI Act and Canada's AI and Data Act offer valuable insights, there are already significant concerns about the EU’s framework for tech regulation in stifling innovation and competitiveness,3 and significant uncertainties about how these regulatory frameworks will operate in practice.  We should also align our regulatory approach with those of key trading partners, such as the United States, the United Kingdom, and countries across the Asia-Pacific region, which are the markets where Australian tech companies operate, expand and grow. While regulation is important, regulation alone is not sufficient to enable the adoption of safe and responsible AI. Meaningful engagement with industry is essential for ensuring accountability and effective enforcement. Further action is needed to leverage policy tools to support the development of Australia’s AI capability. This requires investment in AI research, enabling infrastructure, attracting and nurturing top talent.    1 Tech Council of Australia and Microsoft (2023), ‘Australia's Generative AI opportunity’. 2 Tech Council of Australia, Microsoft, LinkedIn and Workday (2024), ‘Meeting the AI Skills Boom’. 3 Mario Draghi, ‘The Future of European Competitiveness’ (2024’), European Commission.   
Tech Council of Australia www.techcouncil.com.au   It also demands a strategy to prioritise education, training, and upskilling of the current and future workforce; supporting Australia’s AI ecosystem and partnering with market leaders. Only by integrating these efforts, along with a clear regulatory approach, Australia can be world leader in safe and responsible AI.    Summary of TCA recommendations   Recommendation 1. Adopt option 2 – introduce framework legislation to define common definitions and guardrails in a way that is integrated into Australia’s existing regulatory systems. Recommendation 2. Establish a mechanism to support regulatory coordination, consider leveraging or evolving existing bodies (AI Expert Group, DP-Reg Forum).  Recommendation 3. Establish an Australian AI Safety Institute to provide additional technical expertise and capacity to support Recommendation (2). Recommendation 4. Undertake further consultation on the detailed design of a regulatory option once selected to ensure that it is operational and fit-for-purpose. Defining high-risk AI Recommendation 5. Adopt principles (a)-(d) within framework legislation. Recommendation 6. Refine principle (e) to clearly articulate specific harms in clear language, ensuring that it remains distinct and does not overlap with other principles. Recommendation 7. Explicitly incorporate ‘likelihood’ into principle (f) to read: “severity, extent, and likelihood of adverse impacts occurring as outlined in principles (a) to (e)”. Recommendation 8. Adopt a list of high-risk use cases as supplementary guidance to support principles, ensuring these are sufficiently flexible and adaptable as new use cases emerge.  Recommendation 9. Consider tasking regulators to identify and define high-risk AI use cases, in consultation with the relevant sector, to develop guidance that is targeted, context-specific, and promotes enforceability.  Recommendation 10. Do not enshrine a list of use-cases within legislation, which risks becoming obsolete and will be difficult to amend. Developers and Deployers Recommendation 11. Incorporate ‘intentional’ and ‘substantial modification’ in defining developers to clarify responsibilities where models have been fine-tuned or retrained:    
Tech Council of Australia www.techcouncil.com.au   E.g. Developer: “organisations or individuals who design, build, train, or intentionally make substantial modifications to AI models and applications” Recommendation 12. Refine the definition of deployer to provide greater clarity:  i) Remove the word ‘supplies’  ii) Replace “AI system” with “AI model” E.g. Deployer: “any individual or organisation that uses an AI model to provide a product or service”. Mandatory Guardrails Recommendation 13. Ensure that mandatory guardrails are sufficiently adaptable to accommodate for the development of new AI governance and assurance techniques.  Recommendation 14. i) Guardrail 3: Clarify requirements for the disclosure of data sources do not (i) compromise proprietary or commercial data, models, or systems (ii) introduce vulnerabilities that could be exploited for the purposes of scams, fraud, or malicious cyber attacks. Recommendation 15. Guardrail 6:  ii) Ensure transparency is meaningful to end-users and avoid prescriptive notifications that result in excessive notifications iii) Clarify the requirement to ‘inform’ users be limited to consequential decisions Clarify the interaction of this guardrail with proposed amendments in the Privacy Act with regard to automated decision-making. Recommendation 16. Guardrail 7: Engage in further consultation with stakeholders to explore practical options to operationalise processes for people to challenge the use or outcomes of AI. Recommendation 17. Guardrail 8: See similar concerns in Guardrail 3.  Recommendation 18. Guardrail 9: Requirements to maintain records should be sufficiently interoperable to prepare for cross-recognition and equivalency with international governance mechanisms. Recommendation 19. Guardrail 10: Prioritise conformity assessments and do not prescribe certification at this time.  GPAI and Open-Source AI Models Recommendation 20. Reconsider the inclusion of GPAI models as inherently ‘high-risk’    
