Tech Council of Australia www.techcouncil.com.au   1.  Regulatory options  TCA support for Option 2 The TCA supports the adoption of Option 2 to introduce framework legislation that establishes common definitions and guardrails, ensuring consistency for a whole-of-economy approach. This is essential given that AI is a cross-cutting technology with diverse use cases and applications across various industries and sectors. As the proposals paper identifies, there are already existing economy-wide laws,4 as well as sector-specific laws that already apply to AI and its uses.5  In this context, Option 2 provides an approach that builds on our existing laws and regulatory architecture and leverages the distinct expertise that our well-known, trusted and familiar regulators have accumulated over time. It will also be the most efficient pathway to introducing the guardrails and enabling enforcement.  However, we acknowledge there are challenges to overcome in adopting Option 2:  • First, the maturity of regulators in addressing technology and AI-related issues varies significantly. Not all regulators possess the same level of capacity or technical expertise. There will need to be supporting technical resources to aid and assist in ‘pulling down’ the framework legislation.  • Second, coordination among regulators is crucial. Consistency in applying framework legislation across different regulatory bodies is necessary to prevent regulatory fragmentation in interpreting, applying, and enforcing AI laws. To resolve these issues, TCA supports a mechanism to support regulatory coordination. In addition to providing a single point to align, this would assist in providing necessary technical expertise, as well as enable efficient resourcing to ensure that regulations that flow from framework legislation align with the broader objectives set by Government and avoid fragmentation. Such a mechanism could, for example, support:  i) discovery of specific high-risk use cases; ii) interpreting framework legislation to apply to existing bodies of law and legislation; iii) development of regulator guidance on high-risk use cases; iv) coordination and continued alignment to prevent regulatory fragmentation v) recommendations for reform if required. We recommend leveraging or evolving existing bodies. This could include the AI Expert Group working alongside an expanded DP-REG forum, with technical support provided through the establishment of an Australian AI Safety Institute (which also has the benefit of further drawing on lessons learnt from international counterparts). In adopting a regulatory option, we encourage Government to undertake further  4 For example, privacy, administrative law, online safety, corporations, intellectual property, competition and consumer protection, and anti-discrimination laws. 5 For example, medical devices, motor vehicles, airline safety and financial services laws.   
Tech Council of Australia www.techcouncil.com.au   consultation in the detailed design to ensure that it is operational, achievable, and is fit-for-purpose.  The TCA does not support option 3 As previously submitted, TCA does not support the creation of a new overarching AI act or single regulator for AI (Option 3), either through the creation of a single regulator of AI, or through designating an existing regulator.  The creation of a single regulator of AI will not only be time-consuming and costly, it is also likely to: • discourage the important but necessary broader uplift required in capability building for addressing digital regulation issues, including on matters beyond AI • result in siloed expertise and capability across government entities, and • limit our overall capacity to adapt and consider innovative ways to evolve our regulatory architecture and coordination mechanisms. Ultimately, the creation of a single regulator for AI will reduce Australia’s ability to participate in an increasingly dynamic technological future. In the alternate, Option 3 proposes designating an existing regulator. While we acknowledge the deep expertise that regulators possess, relying solely on one regulator entity presents significant limitations. Drawing from a single source of domain knowledge could lead to interpreting broad AI issues through a narrow lens, resulting in enforcement that fails to appreciate the full spectrum of risks adverse impacts on affected entities posed by AI.  While we appreciate the Government's efforts to address concerns about complexity and duplication with existing laws by proposing carve-outs to enable domain-specific laws to take precedence over an Australian AI Act, we continue to have strong concerns with this approach. Legislative design undertaken this way:  • would increase complexity and compliance burdens, which would have a particularly significant impact on startups and scaleups;  • risks creating an uneven playing field that favours certain industries, sectors, or use-cases; and, • is likely to introduce perverse incentives that may create loopholes. An effective legislative approach would design AI regulations that are technology-neutral and that can accommodate for changing circumstances without relying on carve outs. TCA comments on option 1 TCA has consistently supported a domain-specific approach, known as Option 1, which empowers regulators to assess and interpret existing laws in response to the challenges posed by artificial intelligence. While we recognise the complexities associated with regulator coordination, the need for cohesive direction across regulatory bodies, resource capacity, and the risks of regulatory fragmentation, we believe that Option 2 represents a pragmatic path forward for considering the goals of Government in establishing whole-of-economy coverage.    
Tech Council of Australia www.techcouncil.com.au   We note that the majority of countries have not adopted the European approach to AI governance and that an internationally and globally agreed upon approach for AI governance has yet to have reach a common understanding or strategy,6 which comes with its challenges as a technology that is equally decentralised in terms of its technical components across global supply chains.   Recommendation 1. Adopt option 2 – introduce framework legislation to define common definitions and guardrails. Recommendation 2. Establish a mechanism to support regulatory coordination, consider leveraging or evolving existing bodies (AI Expert Group, DP-Reg Forum).  Recommendation 3. Establish an Australian AI Safety Institute to provide additional technical expertise and capacity to support Recommendation (2). Recommendation 4. Undertake further consultation on the detailed design of a regulatory option once selected to ensure that it is operational and fit-for-purpose.  2.  Defining high-risk AI   Principles to define high-risk AI TCA supports the Government adopting a risk-based approach to AI regulation, which aligns with international approaches to AI governance and ensures that regulatory resources are allocated effectively and efficiently. We generally endorse principles (a)-(d) and appreciate their intent to address a wide array of harms. We make two recommendations for principles (e) and (f), which are outlined below.  Principle (e): The risk of adverse impacts to the broader Australian economy, society, environment, and rule of law.  We urge Government to further refine this principle, given the breadth of the principle and the uncertainty it creates. As currently proposed, it is overly expansive and could potentially apply to an unwieldy range of scenarios that makes it difficult for businesses to interpret and apply.  From the examples provided in the proposals paper, there is also potential overlap with other principles (e.g. amplification of content that promotes discrimination, overlapping with principle (d) impacts on groups; deepfakes and synthetic child sexual abuse material, overlapping with principle (d) health and safety). We consider that this principle should be further refined to focus on specific harms, reducing overlap with other principles and to ensure that it does not create confusion or uncertainty in its application.  6 United Nations, ‘Governing AI for Humanity: Final report’ (2024).   
Tech Council of Australia www.techcouncil.com.au    Principle (f): severity and extent of adverse impacts outlined in principles (a) to (e).  The TCA strongly supports the inclusion of principle (f) which is crucial to the operation of principles (a) to (e) as it provides the means for an entity to evaluate risk levels. We support including ‘severity’ and ‘extent’ as factors to consider. We also recommend including the word ‘likelihood’ and making this explicit within principle (f). While the proposals paper includes references to likelihood and probability, making this explicit will provide greater certainty for businesses. It will also align with established best-practice risk assessment and evaluation frameworks, as well as Government’s existing Risk Assessment Matrix for AI.7  Principles and a list-based approach When applied in isolation, the principles are broad and will pose challenges for businesses who seek to implement them effectively. Further guidance is needed to provide clarity on high-risk use cases. We note that a principles-based approach and a list-based approach are not mutually exclusive; in fact, they can work in tandem to provide a more comprehensive regulatory framework. The success of a hybrid approach, however, will hinge on the regulatory mechanisms to implement this arrangement effectively.  It is also important to clarify the terms in use and when defining high-risk AI, distinguishing between:   a) the contexts in which AI systems are deployed which are the broad circumstances, settings or domains where AI applications are used (e.g. public health, education, law enforcement, human rights) and, b) the use cases for AI systems which are the specific examples where an AI application is deployed to support an end-user achieve a goal or complete a task (e.g. image recognition to tag objects in images, an application that translates text between different languages).  Focusing on contexts, rather than specific use cases, risks an approach that is too broad and has the potential to capture many low risk use-cases.   ‘high-risk’ context ‘high-risk’ use cases ‘low-risk’ use cases Appropriate use-cases that should be captured as high-risk and should be covered by guardrails Low risk use-cases that should not be captured by mandatory guardrails Public health  • Predictive health analytics for patient diagnoses • Patient booking system in a general practice Biometrics • Facial recognition tech used for surveillance in public spaces • Facial recognition tech to unlock personal devices where the user has given express consent  7 Australian Government, ‘Risk assessment for use of AI: risk matrix’, Artificial Intelligence in Government: https://www.digital.gov.au/policy/ai/risk-assessment   
Tech Council of Australia www.techcouncil.com.au   Critical Infrastructure • AI systems managing energy grids • Smart readers providing occupants with energy use analytics Education • Student evaluation and performance systems • Chatbots providing students information about their syllabus Employment • Automated application screening without human oversight • Application sorting to organise candidates based on the job requirements Law enforcement • Automated incident reporting systems • Police rostering systems Justice • Algorithms assessing risk for bail decisions • Document management systems to streamline case filing and retrieval TCA strongly advises against enshrining a fixed list of high-risk use cases in legislation. As highlighted in the proposals paper, this approach risks inadvertently capturing low-risk use cases. It also introduces rigidity into the regulatory framework. Not only will it be challenging to amend legislation, but this approach is also likely to leave significant risks unaddressed as new use cases emerge.  Instead, we suggest providing a list of high-risk use cases in the form of supplementary guidance which can be adjusted over time, enabling a degree of flexibility and adaptability to incorporate new use cases as they emerge.  As an additional consideration, we suggest that the Government explore the potential for this tailored guidance to be provided by our existing regulators, who possess a deep understanding of the domain-specific risks and use cases that arise within their respective contexts. This could be supported and enhanced by technical expertise provided by an Australian AI Safety Institute and the existing AI Expert Group. The benefit of this approach would be to produce guidance that is not only more relevant  and targeted but also context-specific, with the aim to facilitate better alignment with practical applications and ultimately, operational enforcement.    Recommendation 5. Adopt principles (a)-(d) within framework legislation Recommendation 6. Refine principle (e) to clearly articulate specific harms in clear language, ensuring that it remains distinct and does not overlap with other principles. Recommendation 7. Explicitly incorporate ‘likelihood’ into principle (f) to read: “severity, extent, and likelihood of adverse impacts occurring as outlined in principles (a) to (e)”. Recommendation 8. Adopt a list of high-risk use cases as supplementary guidance to support principles, ensuring these are sufficiently flexible and adaptable as new use cases emerge.  Recommendation 9. Consider tasking regulators to identify and define high-risk AI use cases, in consultation with the relevant sector,   
Tech Council of Australia www.techcouncil.com.au   to develop guidance that is targeted, context-specific, and promotes enforceability.  Recommendation 10. Do not enshrine a list of use-cases within legislation, which risks becoming obsolete and will be difficult to amend.  The AI tech stack TCA is pleased that the Government is considering the AI value chain and roles and responsibilities across the AI model lifecycle. In considering shared responsibility models, both developers and deployers play critical roles. While these are helpful conceptual distinctions, understanding the technology stack and the respective entities involved is crucial in determining levels of control, and therefore responsibility for adopting relevant guardrails.   The AI tech stack is comprised of a range of interconnected technical layers, each playing a crucial role in the use of AI technologies. Spanning from hardware and infrastructure to end-user applications and services, the tech stack includes the different stages and entities involved in AI systems. Notably, these are not mutually exclusive, and some entities may play more than one role across the tech stack. AI Services: These are entities that provide specialised skills and knowledge on how to leverage and adopt AI applications for specific use cases (for end-users), within business functions, operations, or industries. It includes entities that substantially modify existing models for a particular use case. Applications: These are the specific end-user products that use AI models as is, integrated, or fine-tuned to particular use cases. API Services: Application programming interfaces, or endpoints, through which applications access pre-trained models.   MLOps: Machine learning operations software, processes, or tools that curate, host, fine-tune, or manage the development, deployment and maintenance of AI models. AI Models: Pre-trained models hosted on cloud platforms from which AI applications can be built. Hardware & Infrastructure: Compute infrastructure forming the backbone of AI systems, including energy systems, networks, semiconductors, processors like CPUS/GPUs, data centers, and other components that enable AI systems.   
Tech Council of Australia www.techcouncil.com.au   
 Tech Council of Australia (2024) 
 The levels of control and visibility over AI outputs to end-users increase higher up the stack. For example, at the infrastructure layer, hardware operators have the least control and limited insight into the outputs and potential adverse impacts of AI systems on users. In contrast, for those operating at the application or services layers, have greater control and visibility over AI outputs and interactions with end-users.  Most entities in the AI ecosystem do not own all parts of the stack and so defining the responsibilities required for each entity at each layer becomes important in operationalising the guardrails. Developers and deployers At the outset, the core tenet in assigning responsibility is that the individual or entity in control of any given step within the AI lifecycle—whether during development or deployment—should be held responsible for implementing the relevant guardrail(s) at that step of the process. This ensures appropriate accountability and oversight for risk mitigation specific to each phase of the AI lifecycle.   For instance, developers are primarily responsible for the design and technical aspects of AI systems. They bear responsibility in ensuring AI models are built on high-quality datasets and undertake testing processes to identify and address potential issues. Developers also have responsibility in providing meaningful information for deployers on model details (e.g. date, version, type of model), intended use (primary intended users, out-of-scope use cases), performance limitations, and ongoing monitoring that a model is operating as intended over time.  On the other hand, deployers are entities that enable AI models to be used as practical applications. They are often closer in proximity to end-users with responsibility for monitoring AI systems in action, collecting user-feedback, and making adjustments to 
  
Tech Council of Australia www.techcouncil.com.au   improve user experience, and address concerns or issues that arise in the context of the specific use case. We also acknowledge that developer and deployer obligations may also overlap and we are pleased to see Government recognise these distinctions not mutually exclusive. Definition of developers   The proposals paper defines developers as “organisations or individuals who design, build, train, adapt or combine AI models and applications”.  We recommend incorporating the concepts of ""intentional"" and ""substantial modification"" into the definition of developers, in line with the EU AI Act and Colorado’s AI Act (US). This will provide greater clarity to organisations in situations where AI models have been fine-tuned or re-trained. For instance, where an individual or entity has intentionally and substantially modified a low-risk AI model that subsequently becomes high-risk in its application, it is reasonable for that person or entity to assume responsibility for any adverse impacts that may arise. Definition of deployers Deployers are defined as “any individual or organisation that supplies or uses an AI system to provide a product or service”.  We are concerned that the term “AI system” is overly broad and subject to multiple interpretations. It could mean an AI model or application, it could encompass a wide range of AI components constituting a ‘system’, or include elements beyond the intended scope for applying mandatory guardrails (e.g. socio-economic features that enable AI systems). We recommend aligning the definition of deployers with the definition of developers which refers to “AI models” and removing this ambiguity by replacing the word “system” with “models”. The term ""supplies"" also warrants reconsideration. Although the proposals paper includes a helpful view of the AI supply chain, it categorises a ‘service provider’ as a deployer that ""supplies access and integration of AI systems."" A distinction should be made between access and integration. Depending on how an AI system is modified during integration, a ‘supplier’ may be better characterised as a ‘developer’.  Moreover, the construction of “supplies…AI system” could also be interpreted to include a wide array of entities at the hardware and infrastructure layer including energy, networks, semiconductors, CPU/GPU, data centres, and other suppliers of AI-related components beyond AI models. To address this ambiguity, we recommend the word ‘supplies’ to be removed. We also we recommend the definition of deployers to focus only on ‘use’ of AI systems.   Recommendation 11. Incorporate ‘intentional’ and ‘substantial modification’ in defining developers to clarify responsibilities where models have been fine-tuned or retrained:  E.g. Developer: “organisations or individuals who design, build, train, or intentionally make substantial modifications to AI models and applications” Recommendation 12. Refine the definition of deployer to provide greater clarity:    
Tech Council of Australia www.techcouncil.com.au   i) Remove the word ‘supplies’  ii) Replace “AI system” with “AI model” E.g. Deployer: “any individual or organisation that uses an AI model to provide a product or service”.  3.  Mandatory guardrails   TCA is supportive of introducing mandatory guardrails for AI in high-risk settings. We support guardrails that are outcomes-focused, as well as flexible and adaptable to ensure that the regulatory framework can respond dynamically to new challenges. To achieve this, we emphasise the importance that these guardrails avoid being overly prescriptive. This will minimise compliance burdens on businesses, while being open to new innovative techniques and standards for AI governance to emerge and be adopted.   We support the Government in acknowledging that guardrails should be adaptable and remain fit-for-purpose as technology continues to evolve. This will be crucial as innovative mechanisms for AI governance continue to take emerge as the AI assurance and auditing market develops globally. Interoperability becomes even more crucial to enable the sharing of best practices for safe and responsible AI adoption.   Specific guardrails Guardrail #3: Protect AI systems, and implement data governance measures to manage data quality and provenance. TCA supports the inclusion of data governance, privacy and cybersecurity measures in the guardrails. We also support the proposals for this guardrail to complement requirements under privacy, copyright, and security of critical infrastructure laws.  However, we are concerned that requiring the disclosure of data sources could  compromise proprietary data, models, or systems that are vital for maintaining competitive advantage. In adopting this guardrail, care should also be taken to ensure transparency requirements do not create vulnerabilities that could be exploited for scams, fraud, data breaches, or cyber attacks.  Guardrail #6: Inform end-users regarding AI-enabled decisions, interactions with AI and AI-generated content The TCA agrees that transparency is crucial for fostering trust and adoption in AI. It is equally important to ensure meaningful transparency is provided to users at the right level of detail - which will be context dependent - and to avoid circumstances where excessive notifications result in users ignoring information altogether.  We recommend that this guardrail be clarified to apply specifically to situations where the requirement to ‘inform’ end users for AI-enabled decisions should be limited in the context where there are significant consequences at stake. We also encourage the Government to clarify how this guardrail aligns with the proposed amendments to the Privacy Act which has overlaps with automated decision-making.   Guardrail #7: Establish processes for people impacted by AI systems to challenge use or outcome  At this time, the TCA is not supportive of the establishment of Guardrail #7. While the TCA is supportive of the intention to establish processes for people impacted by AI   
Tech Council of Australia www.techcouncil.com.au   systems to challenge use or outcomes, the existence of these rights remains a relatively novel concept and their effectiveness is largely untested. Current frameworks, including the EU AI Act and Canada’s AIDA, do not establish clear rights for appeal or challenge, and best practices remain to be defined.  This guardrail will be best operationalised by deployers who have the closest proximity to interactions with end-users who are closer to the AI ‘use or outcome’. Further consideration is needed regarding this guardrail; it may help to monitor and consider globally emerging practices, or other jurisdictions, for guidance on operationalisation and implementation.  Guardrail #8: Be transparent with other organisations across the supply chain about data, models, and systems to help them effectively address risks.  As above with Guardrail #3 and #6, TCA encourages meaningful transparency that does not compromise the protection of proprietary and commercial assets, adversely impact cybersecurity measures. Information provided by entities about data, models, and systems across the supply chain should be meaningful to enable shared responsibility.  Guardrail #9: Keep and maintain records to allow third parties to assess compliance with guardrails. Record keeping requirements are helpful to document the development of models and their evolution over time. It is important that any requirements to maintain records that as part of Australia’s mandatory guardrails to be interoperable with similar international governance mechanisms to better enable interoperability and prepare for common conformity standards to be recognised. We note the broader international work underway for led by the OECD on Reporting Frameworks for advanced AI systems arriving out of the Hiroshima process. Guardrail #10: Undertake conformity assessments to demonstrate and certify compliance with the guardrails  This guardrail includes reference to both ‘conformity’ and ‘certification’ processes. While these are related terms, they embody distinct practices that we urge the Government to consider. Conformity is an assessment which can be carried out internally or by a third party. It assesses the extent to which an AI system adheres to established guidelines, standards, and regulations. Whereas certification is a formal process where an independent third party evaluates an AI system against specific criteria and standards to provide assurance that an AI system meets these benchmarks.  Certification holds significant promise for fostering accountability in the medium- to long- term. International organisations such as ISO and IEC are also making strides in developing relevant technical standards, for example as ISO/IEC 42001. However, given the current landscape of the AI assurance and audit market—both in Australia and globally—there is a notable lack of providers equipped to conduct these assessments at this time. We also have concerns regarding Government and regulator capacity to effectively evaluate conformity assessments at this time, especially considering the overall AI skills shortage. Therefore, we recommend that the Government prioritise conformity over certification in Guardrail 10. This approach will better facilitate compliance and support innovation as the AI assurance market continues to evolve and mature.    
Tech Council of Australia www.techcouncil.com.au   Recommendation 13. Ensure that mandatory guardrails are sufficiently adaptable to accommodate for the development of new AI governance and assurance techniques.  Recommendation 14. Guardrail 3: Clarify requirements for the disclosure of data sources do not (i) compromise proprietary or commercial data, models, or systems (ii) introduce vulnerabilities that could be exploited for the purposes of scams, fraud, or malicious cyber attacks. Recommendation 15. Guardrail 6:  iv) Ensure transparency is meaningful to end-users and avoid prescriptive notifications that result in excessive notifications v) Clarify the requirement to ‘inform’ users be limited to consequential decisions Clarify the interaction of this guardrail with proposed amendments in the Privacy Act with regard to automated decision-making. Recommendation 16. Guardrail 7: Engage in further consultation with stakeholders to explore practical options to operationalise processes for people to challenge the use or outcomes of AI. Recommendation 17. Guardrail 8: See similar concerns in Guardrail 3.  Recommendation 18. Guardrail 9: Requirements to maintain records should be sufficiently interoperable to prepare for cross-recognition and equivalency with international governance mechanisms. Recommendation 19. Guardrail 10: Prioritise conformity assessments and do not prescribe certification at this time.   General-purpose AI The proposals paper assumes that all General Purpose AI (GPAI) models are inherently high-risk. However, it is crucial to emphasise that levels of risk associated with any AI model, including GPAI models, is dependent on their application to specific contexts and the harms attached to their use cases.  The primary concern for oversight of any AI models, including GPAI models, should be on model capabilities rather than size or complexity. Large GPAI models do not equate to greater risk or danger; in fact, smaller, specialised models may pose risks that are equal to or even greater than GPAI models. As currently proposed, both the principles and mandatory guardrails will be sufficiently broad to encompass GPAI models. As such, the TCA does not support developing legislation based on compute thresholds and other technical criteria for GPAI models, including FLOPs, number of model parameters, quality/size of data sets,   
Tech Council of Australia www.techcouncil.com.au   inputs/modalities, number of end users etc. Legislation attached to technical criteria is likely to become outdated as advanced models emerge and researchers develop techniques requiring fewer computational resources.  Drawing lessons from California's SB 1047, it is also important that policy solutions are “informed by an empirical trajectory analysis of AI systems and capabilities…based on empirical evidence and science”.8 As such, TCA does not believe that a sub-set of guardrails, or a different set of guardrails is required at this time specifically for GPAI models. However, we acknowledge that GPAI models may present risks which are not yet well understood or unforeseeable. To this end, we recommend the establishment of an Australian AI Safety Institute which can help in better understanding and monitoring advanced AI models, including GPAI models. The Institute can also play a crucial role in coordinating with other AI Safety Institutes established globally. Open-Source AI Open-source AI models play a vital role in promoting transparency, enabling research, innovation and development, and experimentation in AI. They also help in progressing other responsible AI measures (e.g. explainability, bias, safety, and efficiency).9  The proposals paper raises an important concern on the issue of ‘open-washing’ with reference to an article discussing ‘openness’. However, it is crucial to ‘openness’ and genuine open-source initiatives. Open-source AI models have particular characteristics:  • publicly available source code / algorithms;  • modifiability where users can view, further develop and train;  • disclosure of architecture, training, documentation and datasets;  • licensing under open-source licences, usually at no cost and with attribution, and;  • levels of engagement from a community of users and developers.10  In contrast, ‘openness’ encompasses includes some of these practices above, but are crucially distinguished by the free and public availability of models and algorithms to users, which may not meet the standards of open-source practices.   Similar to the EU AI Act, we recommend that open-source AI models should be exempted from any Australian legislation. This will help preserve the culture of open technology development and innovation, as well as recent breakthrough techniques that have made AI safer. This exemption should only apply if developers are not monetising products and services developed on open-source AI models for commercial purpose.   8 Office of the Governor, Governor Gavin Newsom (29 September, 2024): https://www.gov.ca.gov/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/SB-1047-Veto-Message.pdf 9 National Telecommunications and Information Administration (NTIA), (2024) ‘Report on Dual-Use Foundation Models with Widely Available Model Weights’. 10 For more on open-source, see the Open Source Initiative definition (https://opensource.org/deepdive/drafts/open-source-ai-definition-draft-v-0-0-9) and Free Software Definition (https://www.gnu.org/philosophy/free-sw.en.html)   
Tech Council of Australia www.techcouncil.com.au   Recommendation 20. Reconsider the inclusion of GPAI models as inherently ‘high-risk’  Recommendation 21. Exempt open-source AI models from regulation for AI in high-risk settings, provided that developers are not using open-source AI models for commercial purpose  Additional considerations   Clarifying risk classifications for entities who have implemented guardrails The proposals paper does not address whether an AI system retains its high-risk classification after implementing mandatory guardrails. This raises uncertainty on the question of whether a system previously deemed high-risk can be reclassified as low- or medium- risk once the required guardrails have been adopted.  For the avoidance of doubt, we recommend the Government clarify that:  1. A system retains its classification as a high-risk system, where it has taken reasonable steps to implement all relevant mandatory guardrails.   2. Adopting mandatory guardrails enables an individual or entity to mitigate and mange its risk exposure, however this does not change the system’s inherent risk classification.  3. This is because ongoing measures will be needed to ensure that an individual or entity continues to apply the guardrails effectively. Consider ‘reasonable steps’ for mandatory guardrails  While developers and deployers are helpful concepts, further clarification is needed on the standards for entities to adopt guardrails. As the proposals paper appropriately notes: “[v]arious contextual factors are important when considering how accountability will be apportioned across the AI supply chain”. TCA acknowledges the challenge in arriving at a common accountability framework, as use cases and circumstances will differ. This is also reflected by leading research institutes such as the Ada Lovelace Institute:  Every AI system will have a different supply chain, with variations depending on the sector, the use case, whether the system is developed in-house or procured, and how the system is made available to those who use it (e.g. via APIs or hosted platform). Actors within each supply chain will have differing but overlapping obligations to assess and mitigate these risks, and some will have more responsibility than others. This makes developing a single framework for accountability along supply chains for AI systems challenging.11 We encourage the Government to explore options for developers and deployers to be held to a standard of ‘reasonableness’, i.e. to take “reasonable steps” in adopting the mandatory guardrails. This can help reduce the regulatory burden on small and    11 Brown, Ian (2023), ‘Allocating accountability in AI supply chains: a UK-centered regulatory perspective’, Ada Lovelace Institute.   
Tech Council of Australia www.techcouncil.com.au   medium businesses in applying the guardrails. It also aligns with established legal principles and common law in Australia to incorporate the guardrails within our regulatory framework.   Recommendation 22. Clarify that the adoption of mandatory guardrails does not change the risk-classification where a use-case has been identified as being high-risk. Recommendation 23. Consult on options to introduce a standard of reasonableness in the mandatory guardrails, i.e. requiring developers and deployers to take reasonable steps in adopting mandatory guardrails  ","TCA_Submission_vFINAL_mandatory_guardrails_for_high_risk_settings.2ad20d99","TCA_Submission_vFINAL_mandatory_guardrails_for_high_risk_settings.2ad20d99.pdf","pdf","C:\\Users\\james.barro\\Documents\\rsm-nvidia-hackathon\\data\\Submission_Docs\\Processed\\TCA_Submission_vFINAL_mandatory_guardrails_for_high_risk_settings.2ad20d99.pdf","1"
