4th October  2024  
 
Dear Sir/Madam,  
 
TechInnocens is delighted to provide input into our home Government ’s thinking  on the subject of AI 
Regulation and Guardrails. We have provided input into many  consultation s and regulatory think -tanks  for 
numerous  governments on such matters , including previous and upcoming input into European Union  
regulation , with input into Safety for Agentic AI being of recent note.  
TechInnocens is a highly regarded  global consultancy providing specialised guidance on the subject of AI 
technology  adoption , providing support to companies, public institutions and regulators on matters such as 
people impact , culture, regulation, governance and strategy. We are focused on the  practical  realit ies of 
imminent technological waves, have decades of pedigree in this space, and set ourselves apart through 
frank, honest and grounded  conversations. Having witnessed the progress of the last half decade , this 
response is offered in this spirit of setting  our government ’s efforts against  the realit y of current AI , where it 
is heading and the real challenges we face.  We stand ready to assist, should the Australian Government 
desire to step forward with more am bition to face th ese opportunities and challenges.  
Our response is centered on an understanding of Artificial Intelligence technologies  currently being funded , 
researched and  developed, not on assumptions based on previous generations  of computer technology . We 
successfully predicted the impact of GPT -4, almost uniquely for companies in the AI Ethics and Safety space. 
As such our response believes  that the proposal s provided use a  faulty set of assumptions regarding  risk, 
and it is this that must be addressed.  
 
Regarding Section “The Case for Regulating AI”  
Although there were no questions asked  on this section  in the consultatio n, TechInnocens recognises that 
the remainder of this document and its proposals rest upon this as a foundation and therefore the quality of 
the proposals made in this document are highly dependent on the accuracy and relevance of the foundation 
set in this section.  
On the basis of our work with organisations researching and deploying Artificial Intelligence at the cutting 
edge of innovation, in expert groups and standards defining suitable controls for current and future 
generations of AI technology, and with companies searching for market differentiation through the use of 
new AI technologies, we believe the context described  in this section is inadequate, in some places 
inaccurate and often  outdated . We feel i t does not serve as a basis for sound recommendations for 
guardrails or legislative methods for enacting adherence to these. Briefly , we have the  following strong 
concerns:   
• The implicit assumption that the risk profile of AI is wholly and only aligned to that of Machine 
Learning, it s limitations, development profile and traits is, in our opinion, incorrect . This section 
fails to consider  the wholly different risk profiles of adjacent technological approaches that are 
currently being funded and researched to address  gaps in GPAI models . We note t hese technologies  
tend  to amplify risks in current Sta te-of-the-Art (SotA ) GPAI models , solve limitations serving as 
mitigations for these and other risks , and can create  reinforc ing feedback loops of risk between the  
technologies.  
• The representation  of AI development lifecycles as that of a monolithic, discrete software 
engineering project developed by centrally managed engineering groups is , we feel , ill-advised. We 
have continued , over the last decade, to appeal to the major standards organisations to move away 
from this  understanding of AI development  as it fails to address risk vectors of appro aches such as 
self-improvement , genetic al gorithms, I RL. We also note the challenges the same organisations 
have had  adapting to the reality of Generative  AI and rapid democratisation of powerful GPA I 
capabilit ies that provide the masses with power to cause harm , due to this cur rent representation 
of development as a linear process.  
• The  section’s struggle to form a  defini tion  of the portfolio of risks and how these apply across 
different forms of AI technology hinders , we believe,  the ability later to form suitable threshold s for 
risk across the suggested  principles  and  leads to an artificial distinction between “known AI’ and 
“GPAI”. We feel that currently the risks are suggested as isolated categories , based on concepts 
populari sed in AI Ethics principles  which are aligned to Machine L earning concerns , but which fail to 
capture  the natu re of the harm , and are ch allenging to compare , scale and understand .  
• The omission of considerations regarding AI topologies such as Mixture of Experts, Agent ic 
Ensembles, AI Collectives and Decentralised AI excludes a large amount of current focus for AI 
research that  permits  increased cognitive capability at lower incremental cost than scaling current 
Transformer architectures.  Excluding these forms of top ology , and consideration for the unique 
challenges involved , risks a failure to regulate large swathes of future AI.  
 
In aggregate, the current form of this section lacks a full understanding of or insight into the current 
and upcoming risk vectors of AI, and so inadequately informs any proposed regulations.  To assist  
with visuali sing these technological  and development di fferences , we offer these examples:  
 
A small Australian startup develops a self -improving system that iteratively refines AI 
prompts for enhanced performance. After numerous iterations, the prompts evolve 
beyond natural language, achieving significantly improved capabilities. The lightweight 
system, requiring minimal computational resources, is shared as an open -source project 
on GitHub. This allows widespread access to dramatically improved AI performance, 
potentially outpacing safety considerations and regulatory oversight.  
 
An ambitious mobile app developer creates a new interface for a native AI assistant, 
implementing Inverse Reinforcement Learning  in-life to infer user preferences. The app 
then uses these inferred preferences to guide the AI assistant's behavior. The application 
gains immense popularity but operates without proper regulatory scrutiny  which is 
focused on traditional develop ment  lifecycles . Consequently, it sometimes infers user 
goals incorrectly, leading to subtle but potentially harmful outcomes as the AI assistant 
acts on misaligned objectives.  
 
A group of la rge technology companies  create ItMatter sAI, a new protocol enabling 
autonomous AI agents to cooperate and exchange tasks. The network expands rapidly as 
individuals contribute their personal AI agents. Within a short period, societal -scale issues emerge due to unexpected dynamics within the AI community. These include negative 
game theory outcomes, resource allocation conflicts, and other unforeseen consequences 
of large -scale AI collaboration . 
 
Regarding Section “ Defining High Risk AI ” 
 
In response to questions  1,3 and 5  
 
As currently stated , the principles give no indication of what might be understood as  high -risk  AI. In 
common with the Government ’s AI Ethics Principles,  hold merit when viewed  as a set of concerns 
that might  be considered during decision -making , but do not provide any guidance on what would 
actually be classif ied as  High -Risk  AI.  
 
The principles contain a final item which hints at what is required – “f. The severity and extent of 
those adverse impacts outlined in principles (a) to (e) above ” – but this is not specified or explained. 
Consequentially  the proposal provides  a set of considerations  that might be important when 
making a decision  but offers no guidance on how, when and by whom such a line would be drawn, 
how that might be safeguarded or indeed what that mechanism might be. As such this section 
offers no method for capturing  or understanding  High Risk AI , only for exploring its dimensions. We 
strongly believe that regulat ion cannot be formed on such a basis  and th at the absent mecha nism is 
just as, if not  more , significant tha n de finin g these categories . 
 
In response to questions 6 and  7 
 
We refer to the notes in the previous section which observed that the implicit presumption  of 
monolithic machine learning models as the only development pathway  for GPAI means the traits of 
other methods , and therefor e how these might be measured or managed , is not addressed . With 
this in mind  we observe that using FLOPS as a method for assessing potential risk of harmful 
outcomes , and so classifying GPAI , is not a reco mmendable  strategy  as it will likely hol d little or 
only partial relevance to capability and risk  in the near to mid -term . 
 
 
Regarding Section  “Guardrails ensuring testing, transparency and accountability of AI ” 
 
In response to questions 8,10 and  11 
 
Referring to previous notes recognising that AI development, increasingly , does not follow the 
traditional linear software development lifecycle , we regard the guardrails as incomplete.  
 
As currently stated,  the guardrails depend for their effectiveness on a design period and a training 
period in which risk becomes apparent . This less relevant  to other technologies already being 
partnered  with GPAI – self-improving /genetic -algorithm scaffolds, Inverse  Reinforcement 
Learning/Reinforcement Learning pairings, Knowledge -Graph grounding , for example. We 
recommend that the guardrails account for in -life emergence of risk  and milesto nes in an AI 
lifecycle . In addition, when considering agentic ensembles and similar, mechanisms for identifying 
emergent risk from aggregation of cognitive agents  not attributable to any one individual  are 
required, alo ngside recognition that the protocols and other orchestration  compon ents are also 
risk ve ctors.  
 We strongly note that the proposals in this section rest upon  a highly static, traditional software 
engineering mindset to AI development which does not reflect the reality of current research or 
approaches to development, and upon the con cept of  a static  service supply chain  more commonly 
associated with the production of physical goods . We feel these c oncepts do not lend well to AI 
development as guides  unless considered as only a single view  amongst others.  
 
Regarding Section “ Regulatory options to mandate guardrail s” 
 
We decline to offer formal input on an area outside our core expertise, but do note that the pace of 
innovation , the unique challenges that will be posed by AI , and the poten tial for rapidly 
deteriorating  incidents requir ing a response strategy  all suggest the need for a suitable delivery 
method.  We also note that there is a clear need for a broader set of inputs into this  process tha n 
currently utili sed, including more expert insights from people involved with the development of 
GPAI and adjacent technologies.  
 
TechInnocens remains willing to assist the Australian Government in these vital matters and  hopes 
to see broader engagement to increase understanding of this technology within the government . 
 
 
 
 
M.E. Newman  
 
 
Director – TechInnocens.  
 
 
 
