as
analytical
models
or
equations
describing
system
behaviour
rather
than
or
in
addition
to
the
training
data
that
is
nowadays
more
commonly
associated
with
the
training
of
deep
neural
networks.
This
tends
to
be
due
to
a
combination
of
appropriateness
to
those
applications
(e.g.,
the
AI
specifically
for
routing
an
ambulance
has
little
need
for
GPAI
or
generative
AI),
inertia
within
the
sector
that
tends
to
delay
the
introduction
of
new
technologies
and
an
increased
aversion
to
risk.
Such
techniques
tend
to
have
different
risks
compared
to
newer
techniques.
The
guardrails
can
still
apply,
and
it
is
highly
recommended
that
the
guidance
and
commentary
around
them
be
expanded
to
be
more
useful
to
these
types
of
AI
systems.
5Hidden
AI
Risks
and
End
Users
As
AI
becomes
more
pervasive,
it
becomes
much
harder
to
determine
who
is
impacted
by
an
AI
system
beyond
the
traditional
end
users.
For
example,
a
lawyer
might
use
an
AI
system
to
summarise
and
allow
them
to
review
many
more
cases
than
they
might
otherwise
be
able
to.
However,
if,
unbeknownst
to
them,
the
AI
system
has
some
bias
that
results
in
vital
information
being
consistently
omitted,
misrepresented,
or
hallucinated,
this
can
profoundly
affect
their
client
4
.
A
more
ominous
situation
may
involve
nefarious
actors
deliberately
poisoning
AI
models,
perhaps
by
generating
fake
resources
that
they
know
will
be
scraped
by
the
builder
of
an
LLM
and
that
results
in
targetted
bias
against
certain
groups
of
people
in
such
applications.
It
is
difficult
to
square
this
with
the
glossary
definition
of
“end-user”,
which
would
consider
both
the
lawyer
and
their
client
an
“end-user”.
The
risk
of
AI
is
hidden
from
the
client
-
they
may
not
even
know
that
the
lawyer
is
using
AI.
To
this
end,
it
may
be
necessary
to
instead
define
an
“end-user”
as
someone
who
knowingly
uses
a
system
with
declared
AI
and
an
“impacted
individual”,
such
as
the
client,
who
may
not
have
a
choice
or
visibility
into
the
system
but
may
be
impacted.
This
may
mean
that
the
end-user
is
now
subject
to
additional
guardrails,
such
as
guardrail
6
that
may
require
them
to
inform
the
“impacted
individual”.
The
idea
of
the
use
of
AI
being
hidden
has
other
profound
implications
that
should
be
considered
when
implementing
guardrails.
For
example,
users
are
often
unaware
that
photographs
taken
with
modern
mobile
phones,
with
default
settings,
already
have
AI
enhancements
applied
to
them.
This
often
includes
generative
components,
particularly
when
objects
are
unclear.
The
underlying
intent
of
helping
users
to
capture
more
pleasing
images
is
in
good
faith.
However,
the
implications
are
problematic.
At
one
level,
to
fulfil
the
letter
of
guardrail
6,
it
now
becomes
necessary
to
label
all
photos
taken
with
a
modern
mobile
phone
as
AI-generated
or
augmented,
even
if
this
is
not
the
intent
of
the
end-user.
This
has
the
same
effect
as
the
aforementioned
“may
contain
traces
of
nuts”
labelling,
where
the
presence
of
the
label
becomes
meaningless
in
the
marketplace
without
the
requirement
of
additional
nuance.
At
a
more
concerning
level,
however,
is
the
risk
of
distortion
of
truth
across
critical
uses
of
such
technologies.
For
example,
images
from
mobile
phones
of
a
crime
scene,
license
plates,
labels,
and
the
like,
taken
as
evidence,
can
no
longer
be
relied
upon.
Similarly,
the
identification
of
faces,
particularly
where
they
are
blurry,
becomes
problematic
as
it
will
be
unclear
if
the
AI
“enhancement”
has
caused
the
face
to
look
like
someone
else.
4
https://apnews.com/article/artificial-intelligence-chatgpt-fake-case-lawyers-d6ae9fa79d0542db9e1455397 
aef381c
6Transparency
Across
Deployers
and
Users
Within
the
cybersecurity
community,
sharing
information
about
risks
and
vulnerabilities
is
considered
normal
and
an
essential
part
of
managing
the
societal
risk
of
highly
connected
computer
systems.
An
important
addition
to
guardrail
6
on
transparency
must
be
the
requirement
for
sharing
of
the
equivalent
of
“Common
Vulnerabilities
and
Exposures”
(CVEs)
for
AI
across
the
ecosystem,
including
between
deployers,
end-users,
researchers,
and
regulators,
without
necessarily
requiring
the
co-operation
of
the
developer.
Just
like
CVEs
for
software
and
systems,
these
would
be
attached
to
specific
versions
of
models
and
systems
and
provide
crucial
information
that
allows
deployers
and
users
to
mitigate
their
risks,
be
it
on
an
ongoing
basis
or
until
the
developer
can
issue
a
patch.
Examples
might
include
consistent
hallucinations
or
demonstrated
jailbreaks.
More
broadly,
it
is
instructive
to
consider
the
progress
of
the
cybersecurity
community
regarding
transparency
over
the
past
decade
or
two.
In
a
sense,
AI
risk
management
now
has
a
similar
level
of
cultural
maturity
and
awareness
as
cybersecurity
did
in
the
mid-2000s.
Further
Emphasis
on
Avoiding
Bias
While
well-meaning,
it
is
essential
to
ensure
that
regulations
and
guidance
don’t
unintentionally
exacerbate
bias,
marginalisation,
and
risk
or
skew
incentives
such
that
the
easiest
way
for
an
organisation
to
satisfy
the
guardrail
also
serves
to
increase
bias
against
those
who
are
marginalised.
For
example,
regulations
around
systems
concerning
imagery
of
people,
such
as
those
that
detect
and
prevent
the
generation
and
dissemination
of
inappropriate
imagery,
should
also
emphasise
the
requirement
for
testing
and
reporting
of
performance
on
imagery
of
people
from
different
ethnic
groups,
those
with
unusual
appearances
and
body
types,
and
imagery
for
legitimate
purposes
such
as
providing
under-served
communities
with
remote
access
to
healthcare.
Transparency
over
significant
differences
in
performances,
such
as
certain
ethnic
groups
suffering
from
significant
false
negatives
or
false
positives,
is
crucial.
Similarly,
systems
that
are
required
to
evaluate
text
or
detect
AI-generated
text
should,
at
the
same
time,
be
required
to
report
their
performance
on
text
written
by
people
from
different
ethnic
backgrounds,
levels
of
education,
and
those
who
are
neurodivergent
to
ensure
that
they
are
treated
fairly.
This
reporting
allows
downstream
developers,
deployers,
and
end-users
to
make
decisions
between
alternative
systems
and
apply
additional
governance
controls
to
mitigate
bias.
Guardrails
5
and
7
on
human
oversight
and
the
ability
to
challenge
decisions
is
a
necessary
step
for
ensuring
this.
However,
by
itself,
it
is
insufficient,
particularly
as
those
who
are
disadvantaged
or
marginalised
may
not
realise
that
they
have
been
impacted.
Generative
and
summarisation
systems
are
similarly
at
risk
of
bias
that
should
be
addressed
with
greater
emphasis.
For
example,
the
use
of
AI
for
generating
and
summarising
information
7for
education
should
be
considered
a
high-risk
and
high-impact
use
case,
alongside
the
use
of
AI
for
evaluation,
assessment,
and
monitoring,
due
to
its
ability
to
mislead
students
and
perpetuate
bias.
The
use
of
summarisation
tools,
including
chatbots
and
retrieval
augmented
generation
(RAG)
in
high-impact
applications,
such
as
for
summarising
legal
text,
explaining
regulations,
and
collating
data
for
government
planning,
should
also
be
highlighted
as
causing
a
significant
risk
of
bias
due
to
the
outsized
influence
that
results
from
someone
acting
on
this
information.
The
risk
of
omissions,
hallucinations,
and
context
errors
resulting
in
bias
is
exacerbated
by
the
foreseeable
lack
of
double-checking
of
information.
Indeed,
if
the
resources
were
available
to
double-check
the
information,
such
systems
would
often
not
be
required.
Deanonymisation
and
Protected
Attributes
Statistical
machine
learning
systems,
and
neural
networks
in
particular,
are
very
good
at
finding
informative
combinations
of
attributes
and
generating
features
that
represent
this
information.
Unfortunately,
this
makes
them
very
good
at
internally
reconstructing
attributes
that
are
protected
under
anti-discrimination
laws,
such
as
race
and
gender,
from
other
information.
Similarly,
such
AI
systems
are
very
good
at
finding
correlations
between
disparate
datasets
and
recovering
the
identity
of
people
and
entities
from
anonymised
data.
The
risk
of
an
AI
system
internally
reconstructing
such
protected
attributes
and
then
using
them
in
decision-making
results
in
profound
risks.
These
range
from
invasion
of
privacy
and
personal
safety,
to
the
ability
for
organisations
to
claim
to
comply
with
anti-discrimination
laws
while,
in
fact,
their
AI
system
is
using
protected
attributes
to
make
decisions.
Again,
guardrails
5
and
7
are
useful
back-stops
but
by
themselves
are
insufficient
for
addressing
this
risk.
Such
issues
should
be
tackled
at
design
time
and
tested
for,
perhaps
as
part
of
guard
rails
1
on
governance,
2
on
risk
management,
and
4
on
testing.
International
Resources
There
are
several
US-based
initiatives
the
Australian
government
may
wish
to
be
involved
with.
These
are
free
to
join
and
welcome
foreign
representatives.
Involvement
can
provide
the
Australian
government
with
unique
visibility
into
the
process
by
which
guidance
and
regulations
are
created,
and
enable
the
Australian
government
to
harmonise
governance
in
both
directions.
I
am
happy
to
provide
further
information
and
introductions
if
necessary.
