NATIONAL  AUSTRALIA  BANK 
SUBMISSION  
 
Introducing mandatory guardrails for AI in high -risk settings : 
prop osals paper  
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
Octobe r 2024Introduction  
National Australia Bank ( NAB ) appreciates the opportunity to provide a submission on Introducing  mandatory 
guardrails for artificial intelligence ( AI) in high -risk settings : propos als paper . Representatives from NAB  have 
also participated in the Department of Industry , Science , and  Resources (DISR) round table to give additional 
commentary . As a member of both the Australian Banking Association  (ABA ) and Business Council of Australia 
(BCA ), NAB has also contributed to their respective  submission s.  
NAB’s submission is informed by being both an organisation which develops and deploys AI, and as Australia's 
largest business bank, with customers ranging from small to medium sized enterprises ( SMEs ) to large 
institutions, many of whom use AI. 
NAB’s response is based on the questions posed in the consultation paper around the potential harms of AI, 
however,  NAB also wishes to highlight the inherent harm in over -regulation, including limiting innovation, 
stifling economic growth and competition, and reducing productivity. While this consultation focuses on 
regulation of high -risk AI, this needs to be balanced against the risks posed by unnecessarily curbing 
innovation . Excessively broad or restrictive regulations placed on the development and deployment of AI 
could have unintended impacts on the ability of Australian businesses to compete globally, thrive in  the digital 
economy, as well as mitigate and adapt to online threats.  
The Productivity Commission  (PC) has identified AI as a tool that can help address sluggish wage growth, 
improve human outcomes in key areas such as health and education, and has the potent ial to transform our 
economy.1 NAB shares the P C’s view that “many potential harms have been encountered with past 
technologies and adequately dealt with by existing regulatory frameworks in areas such as consumer 
protection, privacy,  anti -discrimination, negligence and sector -specific and profession -specific 
requirements”.2 
The E uropean Union  (EU)’s recent AI Act  demonstrates the counter -productive  effect of heavy -handed 
regulation and has already drawn a strong response from global tech nology  firms, who have indicated they 
will not be deploying AI products in the region due to the regulatory environment. For example, Apple have 
announced their AI tool Apple Intelligence will not be available in the EU, citing concerns that the new 
regulatory re gime could “force us to compromise the integrity of our products in ways that risk user privacy 
and data security "".3 
NAB mitigates and address es harms posed by any new technology to ensure our colleagues and customers 
are safe. Regardless of the technolog y used, the intent behind the design of a given system, and the impact of 
the output or outcome, should be the focus when considering potential harms.  
AI development and depl oyment  currently  requires the consideration of a number of existing regulations . 
Data used  to train AI model s must give consideration  to existing IP and  privacy laws; and a model’s outputs 
will need to com ply with existing discrimination and Australian consumer laws.  Banking is a highly regulated 
industry , with  clearly defined legal obligations to customers , mature risk and governance frameworks and  a 
high degree of external oversight and accountability.  The current approach of evaluating gaps in the existing 
legislative and regulatory framework, and evolving laws where required to meet emerging threats, is 
preferable to taking a preventative ‘ex ant e’ approach. Should AI specific regulation or legislation be 
introduced, NAB believe s it should be very targeted  in the harms it seeks to address , to avoid  blanket 
regulation wit h unintended impacts on the development and deployment of safe and beneficial AI.  
 
1 King, S (2024) ‘The right approach to AI regulation’, Australian Government Productivity  Commission (link ) 
2 Productivity Commission (2024 ), ‘Making the most of the AI opportunity: The challenges of regulating AI’  (link ) 
3 Stolton, S & Gurman,M (2024) ‘Apple won’t roll out AI Tech in EU Market over regulatory concerns’, Bloomberg ( link ) Executive summary  
NAB’s submission focuses on the following key themes  from the consultation paper : 
• NAB believes that evaluating existing legislation and regulation for gaps should be the first step , 
and existing legal frameworks should be used to govern new and emerging use of AI  where possible .  
• Should the Government choose to introduce AI -specific regulation, we believe Option 2:  
Framework approach , could support  the prevention of high -risk AI and  the need to enable 
innovation , by establ ishing standard definitions and a central point for illustrative examples . 
• To guide which legal frameworks are evaluated and updated , NAB prefer  an illustrative  list-based 
definition of ‘high -risk’ AI which specifies industries and use  cases . If the principles -based approach 
is taken , there is a risk  that the current broad principles could be misinterpreted as applicable to low -
risk scenarios , in the absence  of additional clarification and illustrative use cases . 
• NAB is broadly supportiv e of the proposal for guardrails  for high -risk AI, granted they are applied 
appropriately to high -risk scenarios only. Should the guardrails be applied in their current form , NAB 
sees a high likelihood of unintended compliance burden  for developers and deployers of low -risk  AI, 
in particula r for SME  businesses . NAB outline s below how guardrails five, six and seven require further 
clari fication  to avoid adverse outcomes.  
• The risk assessment, and subsequent regulation  of any new or emerging technology, should  
focus on outputs and outcomes (including any potential harms ), and  not be administered based 
on complexity or computational power.  Due to  the rate of technological advancement of AI, 
identifying or enforcing arbitrary thresholds relating to computational power  or model complexity 
may inadvertently place unnecessary regulation on low -risk  use cases. Using these proxies also risks 
creating a regulatory environment which is inflexible and unable to keep up with ongoing innovation 
in the sector.  
NAB’s proposed approach - a focus on harms : 
• A technology neutral regulatory approach  that is focused on mitigating harms and ensuring 
accountability, rather than one which assumes certain technologies are more inherently harmful  
than others.  
• The principle of accountability for an output should have primacy over which technology 
generated the output. Any organisation or individual is ultimately responsible for their conduct, 
irrespective of whether a given process utilises AI, a human, both, and  some other technology.   
• Within NAB’s internal data ethics framework , risk and the subsequent level  of oversight is 
categorised based on the mat erial impact that any given model could have on the lives of 
customers or business operations . A focus on doing the right thing by customers , being transparent 
about new or emerging risks and collaborative in solving for them  is preferable to a focus on which 
tool is being used .  
• NAB believes AI can improve outcomes for our customers , ensure more consistency and r educ e 
unintended biases. Advanced AI capabilities are a key enabler for large o rganisations like NAB when 
detecting instance of customer vulnerability, harm and systemic issues, with speed and at a scale not 
possible using human or traditional analytical capabilities.  
• When evaluating the potential risks of AI, it's important to highlight that humans also carry 
inherent biases and can make mistakes.  For example , most  industry breaches of the  Banking Code of Practice are due to human error  (80% for the most recent period June -December 2023 ).4 Ensuring 
that, where appropriate, customers can  contest or challenge a decision is made by AI also helps in 
building trust. It may not always be practical, or  indeed  beneficial  to seek customer consent for each 
use of AI, for example , to deploy standard fraud and scam monitoring.  
• Increased grassroots public education , such as  financial literacy and school education, to raise the 
level of public understanding abo ut AI, subsequently addressing concerns which may flow from 
information gaps.  
 
Section 1: Defining high -risk AI  
1. Do the proposed principles* adequately capture high -risk AI? Are there any principles we should add 
or remove?  
Please identify any:  
• low risk  use cases that are unintentionally captured  
• categories of uses that should be treated separately, such as uses f or defence or national 
security purposes.  
A key consideration whe n defining, and subsequently regulating, high -risk AI is ensuring  the definition  is 
as specific as possible to the industry or use case, to avoid inadvertently capturing low  risk scenarios.  
Another key consideration should be avoiding unnecessary or duplicative regulation;  therefore,  any new 
regime should be targeted to specific harms not currently captured by existing legislation.  
Regarding whether a principle or list-based  definition best achieves this outcome, the proposed  principle -
based designation system is not sufficiently clear or specific  in its current form . If this approach was to be 
pursued, more granula r, domain specific  examples would have to be given, and illustrative  carve outs 
should be included, to ensure low -risk scenarios are not  inadvertently captured.  The domain specific list 
included in the consultation  paper (based on EU/ Canadian AI regulation), would be a good starting point 
for identifying illustrative examples of high -risk domains  (discussed further in question 3 ).  
Princip le B: the risk of adverse impact to an individual’s mental health  could conceivably be interpreted to 
appl y in an extremely broad range of scenarios and would differ greatly based on the individual. For 
example, NAB deploys transaction monitoring algorithms to detect potential fraud or scams in our 
customer’s accounts. When the algorithm detects a highly unus ual or suspicious transaction, it may be 
automatically blocked or suspended pending additional verification with the customer. In some 
instances, for example if a customer forgets to notify us that they will be overseas, this automatic 
transaction monito ring may block a legitimate transaction. In such circumstances, a customer could 
claim they have suffered adverse mental health impacts, however, NAB does not believe that this 
standard transaction monitoring constitutes high -risk AI, or that it should be subje ct to additional 
regulatory guardrails. If a customer was given the opportunity to opt out of scam and fraud protection, 
or if they were told the fraudulent transaction had been identified by AI, it may decrease behavioural 
compliance to NAB’s  warnings. Th is could leave customers  more  vulnerable to financial crime  and scams.  
If the principles -based approach was pursued, significant additional detail, supplementary criteria, and 
carve  in/ carve outs would be required to ensure they meet the objective of effectively defining high -risk 
AI.  
2. Do you have any suggestions for how the principles could better capture harms to Firs t Nations people, 
communities and Country ? 
NAB is committed to identifying and preventing harms to all customers, in cluding First Nations 
Australians . R epresentatives from  NAB’s  Indigenous affairs , vulnerability , environmental  social 
governance ( ESG ), and  customer advocate  team s, all support the work of protecting customers. They are 
 
4 ‘Compliance with the Banking Code of Practice - July to December 2023,‘ BCCC Report here  regularly consult ed to ensure diversity of thought and approaches , and to ensure that the needs of 
customers experiencing vulnerability front and centre. This  approach  helps NAB  understand what the 
views , needs or expectations may be of certain groups in relation to automati on. 
NAB  continu es to  identify  and test new ways that AI  can be deployed to protect and help customers, in 
particular those experiencing vulnerab ility . This may be by detecting suspicious transaction patterns, 
which could indicate  a change in their  financia l hardship or vulnerability  status , allowing us to reach out 
proactively to  help  individuals in these circumstances.  
NAB recognise s the importance of constantly listening and learning from  all First Nations colleagues and 
customers , including when developing new processes or systems.   
3. Do the proposed principles, supported by examples, give enough clarity and certainty on high -risk AI 
settings and high -risk AI models? Is a more defined approach, with a list of illustrative uses, needed?  
• If you prefer  a list -based approach (similar to the EU and Canada), what use cases should we 
include? How can this list capture emerging uses of AI?  
• If you prefer a principles -based approach, what should we address in guidance to give the 
greatest clarity?  
The proposed  principles -based approach  is not targeted enough . NAB  suggest s a more illustrative list of 
high -risk scenarios and use cases is needed  to give clarity to businesses and the community and  ensure 
low -risk use cases are not captured in this definition . 
Considering this, NAB see s merit in a  list-based approach for defining high risk AI, which gives greater  
clarity and certainty to community an d industry regarding what constitutes high -risk AI. This model is 
preferable to a pri nciples -based model which , in its current form , lacks the necessary specificity  and may 
result in divergent interpretations which potentially include low risk AI.  
In Figure 1, NAB  give s specific commentary on each of the high -risk domains identified in the EU AI Act . 
Generally,  this  list appropriately captures the consequential uses of AI that pose  a greater potential for 
harm  in relation to individuals ’ wellbeing and society more broadly.  The use of AI in these scenarios  
should be subject to a high er level of scrutiny, governance, transparency  and oversight. NAB believe s high 
risk designation should not be based on the technical capability or complexity of these models. When 
evaluating th e list below, regardless of the type of technology deployed in these scenarios, the risk level 
would be the same. Further detail about why output is more important than technical capability or 
complexity  is provided  below in response to question 7.  
Regardi ng the mechanism through which to regulate the below high -risk AI scenarios, NAB believes  the 
most effective strategy would be to evaluate existing laws which affect  these practises today an d update 
them where required to cover emerging threats.  This could either be achieved through a domain specific 
review, or with the introduction of an overarching AI framework for clarity of key definitions.  
For example, t here are various laws that do no t seek to regulate AI, but that may affect the development 
or use of AI in Australia.   Examples (non -exhaustive) include:  
(i) the Online Safety Act 2021  (which includes mechanisms to address online safety issues,  and 
which can be extended to AI generated materials ).  
(ii) the Australian Consumer Law , which was applied to algorithmic decision making in a Federal 
Court case that ordered Trivago to pay $44.7 millio n for misleading consumers about room rates 
in the recomme ndations made by its algorithm ;5  
(iii) the Privacy Act 1988  (including the most recent regulatory reforms relating to Automated 
Decision Making ).  
(iv) the Corporations Act 2001 .  
 
