1 Department of Science, Industry and Resources , Safe and Responsible AI in Australia: Proposals Paper for 
Introducing Mandatory Guardrails for AI in High -Risk Settings  (2024)  (Proposal ). 
 2 We commend the Department for active ly engag ing with industry and align with its goals 
to progress Australia’s AI capabilities safely and responsibly.2 We further appreciate the 
Department’s approach of interoperability with civil and common law jurisdictions considering 
the ubiquity of AI technology,3 and ipso facto  the extraterritorial scope of relevant regulations.   
Recognizing the fundamental challenge of balance between innovation and regulatory 
oversight, the hope of this submission is to assist the Department to develop and implement an 
AI regulatory regime that is fit for purpose.  
This paper is structured as follows:  
First, we identify an inconsistency with Australia’s existing laws for the development and 
deployment of AI. Specifically, a lacuna in the Privacy Act  1988  of what constitutes “secondary 
purpose” under the Australian Privacy Principle 6 (APP 6),4 the definition of which may provide 
clarity for the handling and usage of data given the criticality of data flows in AI systems.  
Building from such policy framework and considering the risk -based approach raised in 
the Proposal ,5 we further posit a distinction between ex ante  versus ex post facto  in AI outputs —
i.e., prospective versus retrospective —and the extent to which those outputs change the risk 
profile of an AI use case. Though the future - versus past -based output distinction applies in most 
AI use cases and is thus largely industry agnosti c, we exemplify the security industry to expound 
our analysis. As such, the exposition will show the importance of human -centered AI -by-design 
principles.6 
Second, because responsible implementations of AI systems require a thorough 
understanding of its workings, we present a multilayered approach to address vulnerabilities in 
 
