(2022).   3 AI-powered systems. This involves comparing traditional platform trust boundaries versus AI 
platforms with AI components factored in. Arguably, the analysis could serve as first principles 
to any guardrails. However, it may be worth noting the extent of te chnical knowledge needed —
i.e., requiring both research and development and industrial experience —for any subsequent 
assessment of an AI system to be effective.  
Third, we submit that an AI regulatory regime that is implemented consider the 
multistakeholder principle.7 In particular, that an AI ecosystem  involves not only developers and 
deployers (i.e., as defined by the Proposal ), regulators, and end -users, but likewise malicious 
actors. Through such lens, the perspective of an AI ecosystem  under the multistakeholder 
principle would build a solid foundation for an effective AI regulatory regime.  
Finally, we present additional recommendations that severally are direct responses to 
questions in the Proposal  to assist in effectuating the Department’s goals.  
 
II. THE INTERSECTION BETWEEN AI AND THE PRIVACY ACT  
There is common ground across jurisdictions that tensions exist between regulatory solutions and 
the fast pace of innovation. It is further established that data flows in technology and the Privacy 
Act intersect.8 Given the role of data in AI systems, this section examines the extent to which 
refining secondary purpose in APP 6 may provide the initial groundwork for AI regulation.  
A. Data Flows and Secondary Purpose  
A key issue in the Privacy Act —or inter alia  the tort of privacy discourse9—is the lack of a clear 
definition of “secondary purpose.” Although there is jurisprudence on how the courts have 
treated secondary purpose,10 specific language of APP 6 arguably sits within the contours of 
administrative law.11  
 
