It follows that:  
(a) Because end users expect  entities to continue to improve their product or service, it 
should be permissible when Company A processes primary purpose data to improve their 
product or service —i.e., subject to end -user explicit consent on initial collection, or 
implied when the user  signs up for the product or service. Notwithstanding the fact that 
industrial compliance has been amorphous, this is already being implemented generally.  
However, grounding legal bases as primary purpose versus a refined definition of 
secondary purpose (per Figure 1) could provide a solid legal mechanism that is not 
antithetical to technology innovation. Seen through the latter lens, the GDPR’s 
 5 “legitimate interest” has broad scope given its treatment by the courts,12 yet it does not 
fully capture the challenges brought forth by advanced innovation. Likewise, the U.S. 
Federal Trade Commission’s advance notice of proposed rulemaking (ANPR) addresses 
“commercial surveillance” and data security.13 The FTC’s proposal concerns:  
[T]he ways in which companies collect, aggregate, protect, use, analyze, and retain 
consumer data, as well as  transfer, share, sell, or otherwise monetize data in ways that are 
unfair and deceptive,” noting that “ [s]ome companies… reportedly claim to collect 
consumer data for one stated purpose  but then also use it for other purposes  (emphasis 
added).14  
It may be argued that except for the need for a refined secondary purpose definition, the 
APP 6 would be uniquely positioned to effectuate the regulatory goals of the above 
ANPR in the Australian context. However, there would be one major distinction: beca use 
of the clarified definition of purpose, a refined secondary purpose definition would not 
have the legal effect of thwarting innovation. This is because there would be clear 
demarcations of data flows from the legal perspective, and the extent to which data might 
be used to advance AI innovation.  
(b) Where Company A processes primary purpose data that is de -identified (such as 
training data), such practice should continue to be exempt from the rule. This is in line 
with the Privacy Act’ s  guidance on de -identification.15 
 
