B. Risk Profile in Ex Ante versus Ex Post Facto Outputs  
Concomitant to purpose classifications is the change in risk profile where temporal output of an 
AI system is concerned. It may be argued that identification of risk is not only contingent upon 
specific domains,19 but is likewise dependent on whether an AI output or inference is ex ante  or 
ex post facto —i.e., prospective or retrospective.  
To ground the analysis, Figure 3 is a temporal examination of the future - versus past -
based dichotomy.  
 
 
 
18 Depiction of Individual Using Digital or Electronic Technology: Sexually Explicit Material: Cause of Action , Cal. 
Assemb. B. 602, 2019 -2020 Leg., Reg. Sess. § 1708.86 ( Cal. 2019);  European Commission, 2022 Code of Practice 
on Disinformation  (2022) ; FTC ANPR, R111004 . 
19 Proposal  26.  8  
Figure 3: A demonstration showing risk profile changing depending on the temporal type of output.  
As Figure 3 shows, risk profile in the legal perspective changes depending on the temporal type 
of output. Put simply, prospective is predictive, and retrospective is diagnostic. The more future -
based the output, the higher the risk. For this discussion, p rospective can be named as Class 1 
outputs and retrospective as Class 2 outputs.  
To expound on this analysis, we consider AI technologies or “safety product[s]” using the 
security industry as a domain.20 Specifically:  
Class 1(a) – is an AI output that uses traditionally disparate data sources that is 
contextualized to predict an outcome. This should be characterized as:  
• Requir ing the highest  level of  qualified human intervention . 
• Applicable to domain -specific use case including defense, human intelligence, 
and large, mission - and business -critical enterprise use cases.  
 
20 Id. at Table 1: High -risk use cases.  
 9 • Availability of such AI technologies is in its nascent stages.  
Class 1(b) – is an AI output that uses real -time data for the purpose of acting on that 
output in real time. This should be characterized as:  
• Requiring human intervention from licensed security professionals or law 
enforcement  to act in close to real time.  
• Applicable to entities such as government and large enterprises.  
• Available technologies include facial recognition and other computer vision -based 
systems but are generally not cost efficient.  
Class 2 – is an AI output that uses past data for strategic decision making. Because the 
output uses past data, this use case is effectively low risk and should thus be exempt from 
high-risk guardrails.  
To a certain extent, this classification structure may be applied to other domains identified in the 
Proposal . In effect, Class 1(a) and (b) require stringent guardrails given factors including inter 
alia model and data accuracy due to vulnerabilities.  
 
III. A MULTILAYERED APPROACH TO ADDRESS VULNERABILITIES  
The overarching goal of the proposed Voluntary AI Safety Standard  is to provide a framework for 
the development and deployment of trustworthy AI -powered systems.21 In order to achieve this, 
it is necessary to understand the unique challenges which modern AI technology introduces as 
well as how traditional security paradigms must be updated to compensate for them.  
Modern AI systems consist of models which accept query inputs (e.g. a text prompt or 
image), perform an inference process, and output a result (e.g. a text answer or generated image). 
The inference process typically consists of a multitude of simple decisi on-making components 
arranged in complex network structures which are trained on extremely large datasets.   
 
21 Department of Science, Industry and Resources , Voluntary AI Safety Standard  (2024)  available at  
https://www.industry.gov.au/sites/default/files/2024 -09/voluntary -ai-safety -standard.pdf   10 A common taxonomy of security issues which are unique to AI models has been 
established in both industry and academic literature.22 These issues include vulnerabilities in 
model inputs, such as training data poisoning and prompt injection, as well as vulnerabilities in 
model outputs, such as information leakage and model theft.  
In addition to these explicit security vulnerabilities, AI systems have properties distinct 
from traditional software architectures that cause them to be difficult to secure. Due to the 
complexity of the data networks which AI models are built on and the s heer volume of data on 
which they are trained, their functionality is not interp retable23—deriving an explanation of why 
an AI model performed a specific chain of inferences is not usually possible.24  
The design of AI models is non -deterministic,25 which means that their output is often 
unpredictable both within a given trained model and between training iterations; small changes 
in model inputs may result in substantial alterations in model behavior.26 A well -known example 
of these challenges in practice is the existence of “hallucinations” in which models fabricate 
data, the cause of which is still not fully understood.27 
Securing a software system which leverages AI modeling thus must deal to some extent 
with this unpredictability, which presents a security challenge in itself. Furthermore, any systems 
designed to interface with large language model ( LLM ) inferences must necessarily be flexible 
enough to handle such LLM fluidity. This creates an attack surface which is more difficult to 
secure than in traditional systems.  
 
