 About the FSU  
The Finance Sector Union ( FSU) is a registered industrial organisation under the Fair Work (Registered 
Organisations) Act 2009 .   
The FSU represents members in the banking, finance and insurance industries (finance sector) in 
Australia.  
The finance sector consists of approximately 541,500 employees nationwide (approximately 4% of the 
workforce) with women making up approximately 48% of the total number of workers within the sector.  
The FSU is a democratic organisation with representative governance by elected officials and volunteer 
members. The FSU is a member -led organisation in which rank and file members are elected to 
honorary official positions to governing bodies within the Un ion.  
This includes the National Congress which is the supreme governing body of the Union. National 
Congress has the management and control of the affairs of the Union and is comprised of 
predominately volunteer members. These members are elected by the wider m embership to represent 
their interests. They are not paid officials.  
The FSU is proud of its long history representing members to achieve better wages and fairer 
conditions.  
Our members are committed to higher integrity in the finance sector and see their role as advocates for 
a better industry.     
 
 
5 
Consultation on the introduction of mandatory guardrails for AI in high-risk settings  
 Introduc tion 
The Finance Secto r Union ( FSU) adopts and supports the position of the ACTU provided to the 
Department  of Indus try, Science and Res ources  (Department ) and makes the following submissions .  
At a high level, the FSU is  concerned about the approach taken by the Department in developing the 
Voluntary AI Safety Standard  (Stan dard ) and the mandatory guardrails  (Guardrails ). FSU has already 
written to the Minister to express our disappointment at the lack of consideration a nd consultation given 
to workers and their unions through the development o f the Standard and Guardrails . It was not until 
the end of September 2024 that the union movement was even notified about the process that is being 
undertaken. Workers are not only the primary users of  artificial intelligence ( AI) and similar technology, 
but members of the community at large. The Government’s failure to consider the needs of workers is 
beyond disappointing .  
The Government does not appear to have a coherent or consistent approach to the regulation of AI. 
The FSU had made submissions to Senate, and House of Representatives  inquiries regarding the 
regulation of AI and the role the Government  should be taking to protect worker s’ rights. The process 
that is being undertaken by the Department does not appear to be in concert with those inquiries and is 
sending mixed messages to our members who are eager to see the Government take decisive  action 
on AI.   
FSU is concerned about the lack of a human focus in the Standard and the Guardrails.  The proposals 
appear to be primarily  focused on the protection of AI technology in its own right , and secondarily the 
interest s of the business es that adopt the technology , rather than the people interacting  with AI and 
workers who jobs and livelihoods  will be radically  reshaped by  this tech nology.  
Human risk factor s such as health and safety, job security, confidence and certainty are not 
meaningfully addressed in the proposal .  The FSU demands that the Government properly consider 
these factors and how the Standard and Guardrails can be used to adopt AI in a way that is fair, ethical 
and prop erly regulated.  
Definition of High -Risk  AI  
(Question 1,3, and 4)   
The FSU supports the European approach to the definition of high-risk AI, that the following areas of 
usage should be considered to be high risk:      
 
 
