• Law enforcement that may interfere with people’s fundamental rights ;  
• Migration, asylum, and border control management ; and 
• Administration of justice and democratic processes . 
Further, the approach that is taken in the Standard  and the Guardrails  does not in deal with the human 
risk factors in any meaningful way. The risk factors that are being considered by the Government are 
those which present risks to the A I technology itself, and secondly , create  potential threats to the 
business es that use the technology. The Standards and Guardrails should prioritise and take a human -
centred  approach .  The  the risks associated with the use and adoption  of the technology to the human 
being should be at the core of any risk assessment of AI.  
Where risk -based approaches are used in other domains such as health and safety, consideration for 
the impact on the business only occurs once the hazard is identified and the risk assessed. Impact on 
business  is not amongst the primary array of risks  considered.  The FSU therefore questions why the 
impact on business is being consi dered at the primary risk assessment stage of AI with no at tendant 
consideration of the im pact on workers.  
FSU members have reported substantial health and safety risks associated with AI including  the 
psychosocial impact of  worker  surveillance ; the risk of increasing workloads and the managerial 
relationship breakdown that can occur where AI is shown to be used . These risks do not appear at all in 
the Stand ard, Guardrails or accompanying documents, and consultation  on the risk s of AI  is not 
mentioned in any appropriate  way.  Worker  involve ment  at all levels of the decision making on AI is 
essential to provide them with control over their workplaces  and to ens ure the ethical  use of AI .   
The risks that  AI pose s to workers in transitioning workplaces and jobs across the economy  are 
significant , ever  present and solely  within the purview of the business. If the broad ch anges envisaged  
by Government are li kely to occur, then it is critical that worker voice is pa rt of all decision -making 
processes  in respect of AI.      
 
 
7 
Consultation on the introduction of mandatory guardrails for AI in high-risk settings  
 First  Nations Workers   
(Question 2, 9)   
In relation to the connection between these technologies  and First Nations workers, the FSU would 
suggest that Government  establish a First Nations advisory committee  in order the gather this kind of 
information . These advisors should be paid and able to have a meaningful impact on decisions mad e 
regarding the  implementation  of these technologies  and the regulatory framework .   
FSU notes that the employment of Blak and First Nations workers in the finance sector  is woefully 
lacking and that the major employers including the big four banks are not making meaningful efforts to 
change this situation. More needs to be done to promote the employment of First Nations workers  in 
the finance sector .   
General Purpose AI   
(Question 5,6,7)   
General purpose AI should be included in any regulation  that is being considered by the Government. 
This includes the Guardrails and the legislative approach  that the Government should be taking on AI.   
By adopting the European model of ident ifying high -risk application s of AI, it will ensure that regardless 
of the label, or the subclass of AI technologies , or the marketing designation or development in terms, it 
will still be covered as it is being used in connection with the relevant application .  
The use of these kinds of AI technologies in connection to work  needs to be in the context of  robust 
regulation  including an  obligation  to consult  with workers  on the introduction and use of AI.   It is 
essential that the benefits of  AI technologies  are being shared with workers.  
AI technologi es are fundamentally  products with a social purpose,  and they should be developed and 
implemented with  social utility in mind. AI should not be treated as a separate  sector or technology  for 
some special  purpose , but rather a form of enabling te chnolog y – it is a means, not an end.     
     
 
 
8 
Consultation on the introduction of mandatory guardrails for AI in high-risk settings  
 Adequacy of Guardrails   
(Question 8)   
FSU does not believe the Guardrails approach that is proposed is adequate to provide any protection, 
or control to workers who are confronting the increasing  use of AI technology.    
There are significant omissions  in the risks that are being considered in the Guardrails. There would 
appear to be a serious lack of detail in risks that need t o be considered and mitigated throughout the 
framework.    
We already know that AI technology is being used in workplaces across the finance  industry. From use 
in hiring practices, to sentiment analysis within workplaces to the use of AI in the setting of performance 
standards , AI is being used across the breadth of  finance sector emplo yers. The use of AI in each of 
these  instance s is significant and pose s potential risk to workers. From health and safety risks in setting 
performance standards, to the concerns about constant monitoring within the workplace, these types of  
risk must  be mitigated .  These risks are not dealt with in the Guard rails in any w ay.  
FSU members are reporting th at these risks are arising for them with increasing  regularity.  In the past 
few months, w e have seen members have to respond to disciplinary action  based on information from 
sentiment analysis bots and message monitoring  where the workers did not know that these tool s were 
even being used.   This raises not only the risk of surveillance without disclosure, but also the quality of  
the output  from these tools.  In th e cases of these FSU members, none of the allegations brought 
against them were found to have any substance.  
The approach  of the Guardrails  claims to be human -centered , however, there does not appear to be 
any serious consideration  of the needs of workers and the risks that are posed to them. The risks that 
are mentioned  in the Guardrails  include the risk to the technology itself and the reputation of the 
business that utilises the technology. This is not an adequate approach  and the these more significant 
risks need to be addressed  in the Guardrails.    
While Guardrail 4 is a step that FSU generally supports as necessary for testing the effectiveness of 
any system , it must include a process for consulting with workers. A process to consult with workers 
and seek their feedback must include a discussion of whether the technology was useful, effective and 
implemented effec tively  into the workplace. This is a significant part in the assessment of the 
usefulness of any AI technology including General Purpose Artificial Intelligence .     
 
 
9 
Consultation on the introduction of mandatory guardrails for AI in high-risk settings  
 Workers are not merely stakeholders;  they are essential to the efficient and ethical implementation of 
AI.  Workers are the only group  who will have a complete understanding of the use and effect of AI 
technology on the workplace and the processes for which they are being used. FSU recommends that 
the Guardrail be rewritten to ensure that  workers are included in the assessment phase of the process 
and have a strong representative voice.  
Guardrail 5 is one of the most important pillars in the whole process. This is central to whether the 
technology is appropriate at all. The FSU is concerned that the Guardrails prioritise the suc cess of AI 
technologies in their own r ight, rather than centring  the purpose of the technology being  to serve the 
human being that interact s with it . This must be  embedded in the regulatory schem e for AI across the 
country.  
The reasons for the using AI technology should not be  purely financial or economic  but should ra ther 
focus on  technology  serving  workers and the community. The process of monitoring  and assessing the 
introduction and ongoing use of AI technology should be purely human -centred and include workers, 
and members of the community and not be one made in boardrooms based on efficiency gains or 
financial windfalls . We recommend that the guardrail to rewritten to have a stronger focus on workers  
being consulted and having a say over the  use and effect of AI technology.  
Guardrail 7 does not appear to have any consideration for what is being  contempla ted through the 
consultation process. It reads as a perfunctory and minor matter that can be handwaved off in the same 
way that business already does with industrial consultation rights. This is not acceptable  and must be 
develo ped further . This Guardrail must be prescriptive  about what business is required to consult on, 
when, with whom and for how long . 
There must be an opportunity for the workers being consulted to influence any decision s and to afford 
workers  a chance to influence and change th e mind  of the decision maker. This means that 
consultation  needs to occur at the earliest possible  point and before a decision to introduce these 
technologies has been made. Where a business is considering the use  of AI technology there must 
then be a n obligation to consult with affected workers .   
FSU recommends that this Guardrail be more prescriptive . Allowing the business community in 
Australia to determine where and when decisions about consultation  are made  will mean, as in the 
industrial context, that consultation  will be limited, it will occur after  the decision to introduce the 
technology , and there will be no opportunity for workers to influence the decision.     
 
 
10 
Consultation on the introduction of mandatory guardrails for AI in high-risk settings  
 Within Guardrail 8, FSU believes that there needs to be more detail about the level of transparency that 
is required. This Guardrail must  include  a comprehensive list of the things that businesses need to 
disclose  including the road map for the use of AI in their institutions.   At a minimum , busine sses must be 
required to disclose what sorts of technologies are being used, how they  are being used, the impa cts 
on workers, how they are mitigating those impacts, and their plan for future technology introduction.  
We cannot allow the secrecy of big business in Australia to continue to keep workers in the dark about 
how technology is being used.    
Allowing business to hide behind their own lack of understanding and the proprietary  nature of the 
software is not acceptable  and where this technology is being used , employers must be able to explain 
its use and ou tputs , and the technolo gy must b e controlled by the user o r worker.   
FSU recommends that the guardrail  be redesigned  and written to require  robust consultation process 
that allow for workers to have a say about the introduction of technology prior to a ny decision  to 
introduce it. This Guardrail must include the requirement for businesses to consult on health  and safety 
matters and  prioritise worker participation prior to any decision to int roduce AI technology .  
Guardrail 10 is also inadequate in the way that it allows for business self-governance  and compliance 
with the meagre Guardrails that are being proposed. This regime  must includ e a role for  an 
enforcement agency with powers to monitor compliance and compel adherence to the standards that 
are expected by the community. Without a watchdog there is no way that the major employers in the 
finance sector can be trusted to comply any obligation wha tsoever.   The Finance Sector Union has 
myriad examples of finance sector employers fa iling to comp ly with voluntary standards  and even 
compulsory  standards  – it is clea r that a ny sort of non-compulsory regulation simpl y will not work.   
Indeed, any regulation without a dedicated regulator to oversee compliance is almos t nece ssarily 
doomed to fail in the fina nce sector.   
This is a very significant  point when considered in the context of the lacking detail about what 
specifically an employer is required to do under these Standards.    
Were the Guardrails to be included in their current form, there would be little more required  of an 
employer than to have some policy positions, and to disclose a list of relevant  technology to the 
community in order to comply. This is clearly inadequate.  The Guardrails  need  to be more proactive 
and direct in what is required from some of the most profitable companies in the country.      
 
 
11 
Consultation on the introduction of mandatory guardrails for AI in high-risk settings  
 Regulatory Approach   
(Question 13 -16)  
Without legislative reform to ensure that the use of AI in the workplace is safe, ethical and socially 
responsible , business es in the sector will take the benefits of the technology in the form of high er profit 
margins without consideration of the effect on workers or the community at large.    
Legislation is crucial to ensure  that the benefits of AI are shared with workers and to benefit our 
community . These technologies  by themselves are neutral – it is the appl ication and context which 
determines their ethics . This means that the shape and kind of regulation  in this space needs to be 
aimed at ensuring  that the social benefits of the technology are shared amongst workers and the 
community.    
The FSU sees a role for the Government to legislate for changes to the consultation  obligation s in a 
workplace setting, as well as have a regulatory  body to ensure that there is compliance with the 
transparency, consultation, disclosure and data management  requirements  placed on organi sations  
within the Standard, and Guardrails.    
Legislation  that covers similar matters as those described in the Guardrails would be a significant 
improvement on the current  obligations and would be a good start for ensure  that workers are protected 
into the future.    
By ensuring that the definition of high-risk AI covers, amongst other things, technology of a social or 
work -related  character , we can have greater  confidence that worker right s into the unforeseen  future 
will be protected and safe guarded against employers who are primarily concerned with the profit 
motive and not the welfare of their workers.    
Concluding Remarks  
AI technol ogies present some of the most significant opportunities and challen ges to our sector, the 
economy and our community.  At the heart of the issue is workers.  It is imperative  that any attempt to 
regulate AI start s with  the voi ce of workers whi ch is genuinely heard by employers  and regulators.  
While the FSU has significant  concerns with elements of the Standard and Guardra ils, we are pleased 
to see that there is increasing appreciation of the need for regulation of AI in Australia.     
 
 
