1. Do the proposed principles adequately capture high-risk AI?
No
Please provide any additional comments.
1. The overarching proposed principles capture some high risk AI. HOWEVER, they are inadequately specific.
My suggestions are to
a) Emphasise in every white paper that is written, including this one, that the overarching philosophy is that AI/ML is here to SERVE the humans. Therefore HUMAN-CENTRICITY AND BIOETHICS as the basis for all AL/ML needs to be emphasised and re-emphasised REPEATEDLY in all papers
b) Emphasise in every paper that the intent of AI/ML is NOT to enable a small group of corporates to CAPTURE MARKET SHARE or for promotion of selfish agendas such as fame and fortune
c) Emphasise and discuss in detailed principles the methods the government intend to regulate AI/M in a TIMELY WAY and the repercussions of misuse of AI/ML that contravene the guardrails, including the threat of significant fines. The government is already well-known in the public space (i) to take substantial time to consider alleged wrong-doings, (ii) give many chances of right of reply to the alleged offender (eg corporate), (iii) issue relatively low amounts of fines that are not necessarily considered as punitive by the wrong-doer. All this is too slow for the AI/ML sector that moves at a rapid pace. If this is not rectified (and/or there is delay in action by the government), then the negative issues of AI/ML will quickly outpace any regulatory action.
d) In Health (a high risk setting that keeps many regulators of the world awake), this means patients and consumers must be considered as paramount, and that AI’ML tool must SERVE patients and consumers (as addressed above). I recommended that the principles addressed by the WHO, United Nations ITU paper ‘Clinical Assessment of AI for Health’ be considered with due respect and regard and incorporated in this paper. Link to this paper https://www.itu.int/pub/T-FG-AI4H-2023-3
No, there are NO principles that should be removed.
I understand that these guardrails cover AI in numerous sectors, in addition to Health, but risk in this sector may be grave and needs utmost consideration
Are there any principles we should add or remove?
No
Please provide any additional comments.
See above
Please identify any:
(dot point #1)
Aside from computer-generated schedulers, organisers, calculators, and similar items (that are already known about), there is NO low-risk use cases.
An AI/ML tool that is PRESUMED to be low-risk is NOT necessarily low risk. This is an ethical vulnerability of AI/ML tools that may be discovered to be lethal subsequently. Case in point is an electronic health record alerts trial for acute kidney injury that was thought to be of such low risk that informed consent was NOT even required BUT the outcome of this trial was significantly increased risk of death in the alert group of patients (Wilson FP, Martin M, Yamamoto Y, Partridge C, Moreira E, Arora T, Biswas A, Feldman H, Garg AX, Greenberg JH, Hinchcliff M, Latham S, Li F, Lin H, Mansour SG, Moledina DG, Palevsky PM, Parikh CR, Simonov M, Testani J, Ugwuowo U. Electronic health record alerts for acute kidney injury: multicenter, randomized clinical trial. BMJ. 2021 Jan 18;372:m4786. doi: 10.1136/bmj.m4786. PMID: 33461986; PMCID: PMC8034420. https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC8034420/)
Because predictive AI/ML software does NOT always give the same answer - it has a STOCHASTIC component which cannot be reverse-engineered or predicted, therefore, there is NO low-risk use cases
(dot point #2)
Health is a category of use that should be treated separately. Currently, there is a self-classification (self, meaning that it is the manufacturer or sponsor of the medical device that classifies their own device, for inclusion in the ARTG). This can be subjected to misuse, particularly in AI/ML. This is because, as addressed and discussed above, there is NO low-risk predictive AI/ML. There is always the risk that a high-risk stochastic component (such as death and disability from usage of the AI/ML) has NOT YET surfaced. In Health, sometimes there is NO second chance
4. Are there high-risk use cases that government should consider banning in its regulatory response (for example, where there is an unacceptable level of risk)?
Yes
If so, how should we define these?
The stochastic nature of AI/ML and its opacity/non-transparency are some of many reasons why these tools are high-risk in all instances. Whilst this may not be possible, then the government should consider the following (a) all AI/ML tools that make predictions in healthcare must be assessed in Australia as fit for purpose for use in Australians (and NOT be automatically approved in Australia if they have been certified in other jurisdictions eg FDA, EU) - many reasons including differences in patient population profiles, differences in workflow in Australian health system, etc)
(b) AI.ML tools to be assessed to show that they perform equal to and/or better than humans in healthcare prior to their deployment. Otherwise, there is no advantage to the human (especially considering that AI/ML exist to serve the human)
(c ) In the Health sector, there needs to be significantly powered prospective randomised controlled AI/ML clinical trial of similar subject/patient profile groups (to prevent bias) and end-points to show clinical significance (eg number of lives saved as result of using a particular AI/ML) prior to deployment
(d) NOTE: Economic evaluation is generally NOT assessed with sufficient robustness in the ecosystem in which AI/ML software will be utilised. For example, cost of loss of expertise and experience in the workforce that has been made redundant may not be calculated robustly (eg The value of having a human medical doctor who advocates for you is lost)
(d) It is essential that the government assesses that any AI (in, or intending to be in, the market place) is truly an assistive tool and NOT, in reality, a human-substitute tool masquerading behind rationalisation and empty statements that it is an assistive tool. This can be assessed against parameters including (but not limited to) the breadth of job descriptions of, the tertiary education curriculum of those impacted, or potentially impacted, (human) groups
(e ) Workforce shortage and increasing workload are purported reasons for escalation of AI in the workplace. However, this is NOT necessarily a true reason for introduction of AI. There may be POCKETS of workforce shortages. However, the broad stroke assumption of general and global workforce shortage as rationalisation for deployment of AI will LEAD to worsening workforce shortages and increased use of AI owned by limited number of large corporates who then corner the market, causing the self-fulfilling prophecy of shortages. Significant flow-on effect includes deskilling of the human workforce, without alternatives for re-training as AI creeps into most facets of human lives.
(f) Any AI that has the backend already in place to go autonomous must NOT do so until assessed rigorously to be (a) superior to humans in significantly useful endpoints (eg numbers of lives saved), (b) in sufficiently powered prospective randomised controlled trials of subjects of proven-to-be-similar demographic groups
The above are some of the reasons that define and characterise certain AI/ML as high risk.
These are some reasons are some why AI/ML in Health ought to be cordoned and assessed with greater rigor than is currently undertaken in Australia.
Provision of evidence of AI/ML’s superiority against actually useful and significant endpoints is paramount because of what is at risk.
Such evidence of superiority does NOT indicate lack of competition in the marketplace. Such evidence of superiority is to ensure that such high risk novel technology (for example, AI/ML in health) is assessed with rigor, that Australia doesn’t become saturated with suboptimal AI/ML at the risk of losing its workforce that are currently expert stewards of their professional craft (even if humans are imperfect, because, in my line of work, I have noted that AI/ML tools are even more imperfect than humans. I had hoped to see an AI/ML tool that is at least equal to (or better than) humans but so far, there is NO AI/ML THAT PERFORMS EQUIVALENT TO HUMANS, as shown in clinical trials
As a scientist, this is only logical because AI/ML training data originates from humans. An analogy of this is that you CANNOT turn BASE METAL into GOLD
Given this self-evident truth, all AI/ML that make predictions, especially in the healthcare sector, are HIGH RISK
