8. Do the proposed mandatory guardrails appropriately mitigate the risks of AI used in high-risk settings?
No
Please provide any additional comments.
My recommendation is to include the following discussions in this paper (a) That it is paramount to address AI/ML from the HUMAN-CENTRIC perspective first and foremost in this paper (eg AI/ML is ‘invented’ to serve humans. Also, the principles raised by author Isaac Asimov’s ‘Three Laws of Robotics’ make sense, could be included in this paper and may engender greater trust in AI/ML in the Australian general public (even if this was coined many years ago) (b) This paper highlights the importance of BIOETHICS in AI/ML, to ensure that the people are served first and foremost
(c ) The fiduciary duties and responsibilities of the officers of the manufacturing company/ies and officers of the sponsor company/ies of every AI/ML tool must also be emphasised
(d) AI/ML must NOT become agentic/autonomous without significant and rigorous assessment PRIOR to such activity. Currently, ‘variance’ of a medical device does not need to be rigorously and significantly reassessed. It can be argued that enabling AI/ML to become autonomous is a variance, hence the loophole
(e ) Currently, it is also noted that ‘tweaking’ of the backend of AI/ML tool (that is, ‘tailoring’ of AI/ML) may be undertaken by the owners/deployers of the AI/ML without informing the Australian Regulatory body. This is also a loophole because such ‘tailoring’ means these is loss of oversight of the medical device by the government. There are incidences where such 'tailoring' has changed the stated intent of the device
(f) The ability of AI/ML to give DIFFERENT outputs, even if inputs are identical, UNexpectedly means that ALL predictive AI/MLs are high-risk. How do these guardrails mitigate such risks? Such risk is UNACCEPTABLE in Health and I opine that the guardrails are INSUFFICIENT to mitigate this risk to a sufficiently low level in Health. It can be rationalised that humans have oversight in this matter and give the final say over the machine. However, this is merely a rationalisation and NOT true in some forms of AI in Health. Many reasons, including automation bias
