Yes
Please provide any additional comments
It is important to ensure that responsibility is distributed across the AI supply chain. However, it is my recommendation that (a) developers and deployers need to shoulder MOST of the responsibilities pertaining to the AI/ML tool, regardless of version and/or variance for the entire lifecycle of the tool. (b) In Health, in some situations, this ought NOT lie with the medical professional (who is employee of the deployer, who has no understanding and no control of the backend of such AI/ML and who may not be in a position to opt-out of using such a tool in his/her work) or with the patient (who may not even know that such a tool is being used on them)
12. Do you have suggestions for reducing the regulatory burden on small-to-medium sized businesses applying guardrails?
Yes
Please provide any additional comments
The following applies to both small-to-medium sized businesses as well as corporates >>> The following applies to Health. It could be possible that the following could apply to other sectors that use AI/ML as well.
The Silent Trial (also known as translational trial) is recommended. More below>>>
Currently, some AI/ML undergo only technical assessment prior to deployment. Then it immediately goes straight to Clinical trials (where patient management may be influenced by still-unverified AI/ML predictions)
In the Silent Trial, the mature algorithm of the AI/ML device is evaluated clinically in a live environment. However, the predictions of this device do not reach the clinical team, whose interactions with patients continue as though there is not AI/ML device. This prevents potential harms to the patient via AI/ML device’s unvalidated predictions. Instead, the predictions of this device reach the non-clinical AI team, where evaluations of human factors, consultation/engagement and other factors (undertaken in a live clinical environment) are undertaken, in order to determine if these AI/ML predictions are valid in a clinical environment. If these are valid, then a full-scale clinical trial involving patients of the same profiles in the same clinical situations may be undertaken next, using the same version of this AI/ML device.
This is particularly important in view of the non-explainability, lack of transparency and trust in AI systems. It must also be noted that research advances in the future do not negate the importance of a Silent Trial in protecting the recipient of such AI/ML devices.
Therefore, Model examination/ assessment is conducted PROSPECTIVELY in the live/clinical environment BUT WITHOUT impact (of non-clinically validated AI/ML device) upon the clinician-patient relationship, upon clinical decision or upon patient management
The predictions of this device do not reach the clinical team, whose interactions with patients continue as though there is no AI/ML device
The predictions of this device reach the non-clinical AI team, who assess parameters including but not limited to the following:
- Has use of the algorithm been shown to fit into and/or complement current clinical workflows?
- Does the study answer the question about the possible patient harm or side effects (ex: does this cause a delay in diagnosis?) that could be caused by the algorithm?
- Does use of the algorithm raise ethical, legal, or social concerns?
- Reproducibility / generalizability in clinical practice and Code source / code availability
It is in the environment that clinical care is not influenced that the above questions can be answered objectively. Otherwise, influence upon clinical care represents confounder/s which render some of the manufacturer’s assertions regarding the AI/ML device inapplicable or irrelevant.
Additionally, it is the prospective nature of such a trial that allows the device to be assessed objectively.
Classically, AI/ML undergoes (1) Design and Development, then (2) Prospective Clinical Evaluation, then (3) Monitoring and Oversight.
The Silent Trial inserts itself between (1) Design and Development and (2) Prospective Clinical Evaluation, resulting in the overview becoming
(1) Design and Development, then (2) Silent Trial, then (3) Prospective Clinical Evaluation, then (4) Monitoring and Oversight.
In summary, the Silent trial enables assessment of the user-interface of the AI model clinically without risking the patient.
This is already being undertaken of mature AI/ML algorithms in places such as Stanford University, Duke University, Uni of Toronto and elsewhere. However, this is not yet mainstream in Australia. I recommend that Silent trials be undertaken prior to full scale clinical trials, to mitigate against patient harm. This is especially important as AI in Health is high risk
More about the Silent Trial can be found here
(1) Mccradden, M., Anderson, J.A., A Stephenson, E., Drysdale, E., Erdman, L., Goldenberg, A., & Zlotnik Shaul, R. (2022). A Research Ethics Framework for the Clinical Translation of Healthcare Machine Learning. The American Journal of Bioethics, 22, 8 - 22
(2) Kwong, J. C. C., Erdman, L., Khondker, A., Skreta, M., Goldenberg, A., McCradden, M. D., Lorenzo, A. J., & Rickard, M. (2022). The silent trial - the bridge between bench-to-bedside clinical AI applications. Frontiers in digital health, 4, 929508. https://doi.org/10.3389/fdgth.2022.929508
14. Are there any additional limitations of options outlined in this section which the Australian Government should consider?
Yes
Please provide any additional comments.
My answer to Q 13:
All Guardrails 1 - 10 are all reasonable. I would recommend using ALL, rather than choosing one particular option only. However, I note the following issues with some of them:
General comments:
Some Manufacturers/sponsors pay lip service to governance, to risk mitigation, to keeping detailed records and in reality, this doesn’t work optimally
More specific comments on some of these guardrails:
Guardrail #2: Risk mitigation is a paper exercise of subjectivity, especially that of the mitigation table. In my opinion, this has NOT made useful impact upon regulation. Mitigation plans are, as it is stated, only PLANS, and there may be little action. If there was to be action, because of the above mentioned subjectivity nature of this concept, rationalisation by manufacturers/sponsors may occur.
Guardrail #5: Good in principle, but I can see how this could be mis/re-interpreted in real life, to the detriment of end-users. It also depends on *who* this human with oversight is and his/her agenda in the matter.
Guardrail #6: This would only work in an ideal world. The eyes of many of those in the general public glaze over when AI is mentioned. They are hopeful that someone else (eg the Government) would sort all this out for them because, in many people’s opinion, AI is too hard to understand, too amorphous. Additionally,the marketing of this as ‘intelligence’, as ‘inexplicable’, as ‘magical’ have led consumers to become bamboozled and concurrently be seduced by this. Unless the advertising laws regarding AI changes, informing the end-user regarding AI will lead to negligible change. Many end-users will probably just agree without understanding
Guardrail #9: Some manufacturers of current ARTG devices are very poor at maintaining records. The general response is that there are no/negligible adverse events because these are either not reported or categorised in different category. Their agenda is different from the regulators. This is the disadvantage of this guardrail. I would recommend OBJECTIVE third party registry, such as that of the Australian Orthopaedic Association National Joint Replacement Registry, which is world-renown. This can be undertaken by collecting a subscription fee from ALL manufacturers who have AI/ML registered for use in Australia. This is in contradistinction to payment for application/ongoing registration of specific AI/ML tool
Guardrail #10: Assessment of AI in Australia to determine if it is fit for purpose for the people of this country is, in principle, a good idea. This is particularly true because the profile of the population Ai/ML to be used in Australia differs from other countries. Additionally, the Australian workflow differences may mean that the use of AI/ML elsewhere may not apply here. However, I also know that there is much appetite for medical devices with certificates from other jurisdictions to be able to come to the Australian market WITHOUT further assessment. An example is the European Union certification which may NOT be optimal, because there is significant heterogeneity in certification of devices in the EU. Therefore, in principle, harmonisation of certification with other jurisdiction should NOT apply to AI/ML
My answer to Q 14
See my entry regarding Silent (or translational) trial, to mitigate unnecessary harms to patients (in AI in Health setting)
15. Which regulatory option(s) will best ensure that guardrails for high-risk AI can adapt and respond to step-changes in technology?
Other
16. Where do you see the greatest risks of gaps and inconsistencies with Australia’s existing laws for the development and deployment of AI?
There are many. Please see my submission in toto
Which regulatory option best addresses this?
Other

