"30","31","31","Matt Fisher","https://consult.industry.gov.au/ai-mandatory-guardrails/submission/view/289","
Matt Fisher
Published name
Matt Fisher
1. Do the proposed principles adequately capture high-risk AI?
No
Please provide any additional comments.
Regrettably, I believe the principles lack the necessary flexibility for identifying high-risk AI effectively. The future may present us with novel high-risk AI systems that we cannot envision today. Drawing from the Canadian Artificial Intelligence and Data Act (AIDA), which incorporates a mechanism for adding new high-risk AI use cases even when they do not align with existing legislation, Australia should consider infusing our regulations with similar adaptability to tackle unforeseen future developments.
4. Are there high-risk use cases that government should consider banning in its regulatory response (for example, where there is an unacceptable level of risk)?
Yes
If so, how should we define these?
Indeed, AI systems that could inflict “critical harm” or pose “systemic risks” deemed unacceptable following technical evaluations should be prohibited. The Californian SB1047 bill and the EU AI Act provide useful templates for defining critical and systemic risks. For instance, AI models that could aid in the creation of cyber, chemical, biological, radiological, or nuclear weapons, or engage in self-replication, deception, and loss of control, must be urgently banned. Legislation needs to define an intolerable risk threshold.
5. Are the proposed principles flexible enough to capture new and emerging forms of high-risk AI, such as general-purpose AI (GPAI)?
No
Please provide any additional comments.
Unfortunately, they are not. We need to distinguish between high-risk use of narrow AI, risks associated with current GPAI systems, and the systemic or catastrophic risks associated with future GPAI systems with enhanced capabilities. A regulatory scheme that categorises models will help to alleviate regulatory burden and apply the precautionary principle as future systems emerge.
6. Should mandatory guardrails apply to all GPAI models?
No
Please provide any additional comments.
Mandatory guardrails should be proportional to the risks posed by the AI system to balance safety and innovation effectively. The EU AI Act provides a beneficial model for distinguishing between GPAI and GPAI with systemic risk and applying stricter mandatory guardrails for the latter. The principle of considering “severity and extent” is crucial to a risk-based approach to AI regulation and must also be considered when defining GPAI and imposing specific guardrails. The government should make targeted mandatory guardrails to tackle the catastrophic or unknown risks from future AI models.
7. What are suitable indicators for defining GPAI models as high-risk?
Base on technical capability
What technical capability should it be based on?
Other
8. Do the proposed mandatory guardrails appropriately mitigate the risks of AI used in high-risk settings?
No
Please provide any additional comments.
AI risks vary widely in magnitude. AI presents a unique regulatory challenge due to its potential ubiquity and ability to cause catastrophic harm. We need tailored guardrails that match the extent of the risks. The current guardrails are insufficient to address the novel and catastrophic risks from future advanced AI systems.
10. Do the proposed mandatory guardrails distribute responsibility across the AI supply chain and throughout the AI lifecycle appropriately?
No
Which of the guardrails should be amended?
Establish, implement and publish an accountability process including governance, internal capability and a strategy for regulatory compliance
Please provide any additional comments
We need to focus on developers and prevent them from shifting risks to others. The complexity of AI systems makes understanding how an AI arrived at a decision, and therefore who is responsible for the consequences of that decision, very difficult. The Government should create incentives that force AI developers to internalise risks.
11. Are the proposed mandatory guardrails sufficient to address the risks of GPAI?
No
How could we adapt the guardrails for different GPAI models, for example low-risk and high-risk GPAI models? 
The proposed guardrails need to be revised to deal with the risks posed by the development of the most advanced models. Australia needs a specific definition to capture AI models that might display dangerous behaviours. Once we have a way of defining these specific models, we need guardrails designed to address their risks during their development.
12. Do you have suggestions for reducing the regulatory burden on small-to-medium sized businesses applying guardrails?
No
13. Which legislative option do you feel will best address the use of AI in high-risk settings?
A whole of economy approach – introducing a new cross-economy AI Act
What opportunities should the Government take into account in considering each approach? 
Australia needs an AI Act that can shape the behaviour of overseas AI developers. Research shows that AI developers are best placed to control the most severe risks from AI during model development. Regulating offshore activity can be challenging, but it has to be our top priority. The best regulatory approach is to let established regulators lead within their fields of expertise. This would allow an Australian AI Act to cover any gaps and focus on shaping the behaviour of offshore AI developers.
15. Which regulatory option(s) will best ensure that guardrails for high-risk AI can adapt and respond to step-changes in technology?
A whole of economy approach – introducing a new cross-economy AI Act

