TECH  FEM M E FU SIO N TECH  FEM M E FU SIO N“Guardrail 4.4: Commit to implementing robust AI system performance monitoring
and evaluation, and to ensuring each system remains fit for purpose.”
The adoption of artificial intelligence (AI) has the potential to significantly boost
productivity for businesses of all sizes, reshaping industries and enhancing efficiency
across operations. Unlike previous technological advancements that primarily
benefited larger corporations, AI presents a unique opportunity for small and medium-
sized enterprises (SMEs) to compete on a more level playing field. By automating
repetitive tasks, improving decision-making, and optimising resource allocation, AI can
be a transformative tool for SMEs, offering productivity improvements that were once
beyond their reach.
Data across OECD countries illustrate a stark disparity in AI adoption rates between
small and large businesses. In 2023, 8% of all companies within OECD member states
reported using AI; however, this figure rises to over 25% for entities with more than 250
employees (OECD, 2024). Similarly, in Australia, the adoption rate among businesses
with 250 or more employees is substantially higher compared to smaller firms with 10–
49 employees, with respective adoption rates of 11% and 3% (OECD, 2023). Yet, AI
adoption among Australian SMEs is growing rapidly, with a recent survey indicating
that 90% of SMEs intend to integrate AI into their operations by 2026 (Australian Cyber
Security Magazine, 2023).
Adoption rate (%)
10-49 employees 250+ employees SMEs with AI plans by 2026020406080100
""PROTECTING SMALL AND MEDIUM BUSINESS IN AI ADOPTION: A
RESPONSE TO GOVERNMENT INQUIRY ON GUARDRAIL 4.4 AND
RESPONSIBLE AI REGULATION""
Al Adoption Rates Among Australian Businesses (2023)However, the regulatory and legislative landscape can impose disproportionate
burdens on smaller businesses. Litigation costs, often cited as one of the highest
business risks faced by SMEs (Virglerová et al., 2023), can be particularly challenging.
Large corporations are typically better positioned to absorb the costs associated with
professional insurance and litigation, but SMEs may face overwhelming financial stress
from a single commercial dispute, sometimes pushing them towards bankruptcy. In this
report, we examine how regulatory requirements, such as mandatory AI guardrails,
may inadvertently hinder innovation and adoption among SMEs, creating an uneven
playing field.
Guardrail 4.4, for instance, mandates that businesses implement rigorous performance
monitoring and evaluation for AI systems to ensure they remain ""fit for purpose."" While
this is a commendable objective, the distribution of responsibility for system quality is
unevenly balanced, especially disadvantaging smaller enterprises. SMEs using AI-
enabled tools may find themselves liable for AI-generated issues, such as
""hallucinations"" (inaccuracies or fabrications), which could lead to consumer
complaints. However, these businesses often rely on AI platforms developed by major
providers like OpenAI, Google, Microsoft, and Anthropic, and do not control the core
technology or foundational model design that may cause these issues.
For SMEs integrating AI into their processes or products, Guardrail 4.4 introduces a
potentially devastating liability risk. They depend on pre-existing AI platforms without
control over the underlying model's design, which is the very source of any
hallucination or inaccuracy. Under Guardrail 4.4, SMEs could face legal consequences if
a consumer deems an AI product not ""fit for purpose"" and decides to file a complaint
with a body such as the Victorian Civil and Administrative Tribunal (VCAT). This
liability risk discourages AI adoption among SMEs, which is concerning given the
essential role of AI in boosting productivity within the Australian economy.
Moreover, there is significant information asymmetry between the companies that
control and design the original large language models (LLMs) and the end businesses
selling the final AI-enabled products. SMEs adopting AI might not be fully aware of the
limitations or potential errors of the AI models they employ. This is further exacerbated
by the fact that most companies rely on off the shelf solutions, for example, Australian
companies reported using productivity applications with integrated generative AI (71%)
and enterprise platforms with integrated generative AI (61%) (Deloitte 2023). 
Understanding the training data sets, model specifications, and limitations of especially
for off-shelf products can be prohibitively costly and time-consuming. 
Developer tools like application programming interfaces (APIs) may obscure key model
attributes, while downstream adoption across extended supply chains may further
reduce transparency regarding the capabilities and limitations of AI, complicating
accountability (OECD, 2024). 
W hile consumer protection is essential, business protection is equally critical to ensure
sustainable AI adoption. One potential solution could involve allowing businesses to
disclose and waive liability for commonly known and reasonably expected AI-
generated errors. Under this framework, companies would transparently communicate
AI’s inherent limitations, such as the possibility of hallucinations or minor inaccuracies.
Such disclosures would set realistic consumer expectations while shielding SMEs from
liabilities over factors outside their control. By enabling this transparency, the
regulatory framework could balance consumer protection with business feasibility,
fostering AI adoption rather than stifling it.
