Safe and responsible AI 
in Australia – Proposals 
paper for introducing 
mandatory guardrails for 
AI in high -risk settings  
 
 
 
Department of Industry, Science and 
Resources  
 
 
Westpac Banking Corporation  
October  2024  
 1 Westpac Group   
OVERVIEW    
Westpac welco mes the opportunity to provide feedback on the Government’s proposal 
paper  to introduc e mandatory guardrails for AI in high -risk settings ( proposals paper ).  
 
The banking industry is anticipated to derive significant benefit s from the use of AI1, 
including  improved  customer service s and protections , a more personalised experience for 
our customers, and increas ed productivity . To maximise this opportunity, a regulatory 
framework that promotes innovation while maintaining appropriate guardrails  is essential.  
 
Westpac supports the Government’s objective to develop a regulatory environment that 
uplifts community trust in the application and oversight of AI, while facilitating innovation and 
the adoption of  AI. Any regulatory regime needs to be principles -based, technology neutral, 
and proportionate and targeted at the outcomes where there is the potential for serious  
harm.  
 
Westpac’s submission is focused on the following key areas:  
• Definitions  
• Proposed guardrails  
• Regulatory approach  
• Extra -territoriality  
It's noted that the Australian Banking Association ( ABA ) has also provided a detailed 
submission on behalf of its members, which Westpac supports.  
 
DEFINITION OF HIGH -RISK AI  
We support a nuanced approach to regulating the two broad categories of high -risk AI : 
  
• where the proposed use of the AI system or general -purpose AI (GPAI) model 
are known or foreseeable ; and 
• advanced, highly capable GPAI models where all possible applications and risks 
cannot be foreseen.  
Category One: Proposed uses are known or foreseeable  
Our view is that principles that are all encompassing of any risk of adverse impact are 
excessively broad and could potentially  classify  low-risk AI applications intended to support 
and enhance customer experience and outcomes  as high risk . 
 
As currently drafted, the  proposed principles defining high -risk AI would capture practices 
which have been in use by banks for years and are considered low risk, for example, credit 
decisioning using machine learning models.  How the various AI systems are used  present 
different levels of  risk, and this is not differentiated in the principles . In determining what is to 
be considered high -risk AI, the Government needs to focus on the type of harm it is trying to 
prevent  to ensure the regulatory regime is targeted  at those use cases with the potential for 
serious  harm , noting that some  of these  harms are already prohibited through existing  laws.  
 
In addition,  greater clarity  is needed  in relation to how risk should be assessed  where  there 
is an AI system with high impact but low probability, or low impact but high probability.  The 
 
1 Capturing the full value of generative AI in banking, McKinsey, 2023.  
  
 2 Westpac Group  detail in the  proposed principles with respect to severity and extent could  disproportionate ly 
lead to low-risk AI system s being classified as high risk. 
 
Category Two: Advanced GPAI models  
Similar to the Canadian approach, the mandatory guardrails in Category One should apply 
equally for Advanced GPAI models. A more nuanced approach is required for the 
developers  of the GPAI models  which should focus on areas where there is lower 
transparency , for example, data measures, testing measures and any known vulnerabilities.  
 
Definition of a developer  
Westpac would expect that as organisations scale,  they will  adopt AI systems to support 
their business processes . This would require them to alter AI models whether it is to adapt or 
combine models to suit their specific needs. An organisation or individual altering an AI 
model , should not then result in them being classified as a developer. Any definition of 
developer needs to include a degree of materiality, like in the United States of America, such 
that the AI model is ‘significantly’ altered before an organisation or individual is to be 
considered a developer.  
 
A distinction should be made between developers who are developing for consumption by 
other organisations (external) and those that are developing entirely for internal use. A 
higher degree of risk applies to those developing for external consumption, as there is 
generally less transparency and explainability for end users, and as such more stringent 
obligations need to apply to these types of developers . 
 
PROPOSED GUARDRAILS  
We have considered the proposed guardrails  and have focused our feedback on the 
following.  
 
Guardrail 6: Inform end -users regarding AI -enabled decisions, interactions with AI 
and AI generated content  
The proposed guardrail is currently designed to apply broadly, encompassing not only 
decisions made by an AI system but also those influenced by AI. Imposing an obligation on 
the organisations to provide the en d user with detail about AI -enabled decisions or 
interaction would be burdensome and could potentially stifle innovation.  
 
Additionally, there is no distinction between internal and external applications of AI. With 
such a wide scope, Guardrail 6 might unintentionally cover low -risk applications, where 
decisions are ultimately made by a human or when the AI is used internally,  which are low -
risk scenarios that should not require disclosure. Westpac recommends  that a distinction is 
made between external and internal uses, and between AI used by experts and non -experts 
of AI systems . The requirement to inform should be narrowed t o those use  cases where 
there is the risk of serious harm to the end user.  
 
Guardrail 7: Establish processes for people impacted by AI systems to challenge use 
or outcomes  
The banking industry is already subject to a range of comprehensive, regulated processes 
for preventing customer harm and for resolving customer complaints. As an example, we are 
required by the Banking Code of Practice and the Australian Securities and Investments 
Commission Regulatory Guide 271 to implement internal dispute resolution ( IDR) 
mechanisms. There are also existing external dispute resolution mechanisms through the 
Australian Financial Complaints Authority that allow a customer to seek a review of the IDR 
outcome where they are unhappy with that outcome.   
 3 Westpac Group   
Proposed Guardrail 7  impos es regulations based on technology, rather than outcome s. The 
ability for the customer to contest should not be based on the technology and resources 
used to achieve the decision.  There are examples where banks use AI, such is in the 
detection and prevention of scams and fraud, where sharing of this information and 
providing the customer with the ability to contest could undermine the risk management 
practices of banks. Mandating this requirement would create additional compliance burden 
simply because AI was used .  
 
Banks currently deploy sophisticated AI capabilities to make lending decisions. Current 
obligations enable customers to seek information on the outcomes of lending decisions and 
requires banks to respond accordingly. These obligations apply irrespective of whether the 
approval was granted with or without human intervention.  Where such a process is 
enhanced through GPAI models, this Guardrail would effectively introduce a separate set of 
obligations where decisions are made reliant on GPAI models.  
 
Guardrail 10: Undertake conformity assessments to demonstrate and certify 
compliance with the guardrails  
As outlined by the proposals paper , banks are heavily regulated organisations, and already 
have  existing obligations  that support the safe and responsible use of AI. These obligations 
apply across a range of laws including the Privacy Act 1988, Corporations Act 2001, ASIC 
Act 2001, National Consumer Credit Protection Act 2009, APRA Prudential Standards, Age 
Discrimination Act 2004 , Disability Discrimination Act 1992, Racial Discrimination Act 1975 , 
etc. These laws and standards already require the bank to have multiple assessments and 
extensive documentation requirements in place, such that the  introduction of an additional 
conformity assessment to demonstrate compliance with AI guardrails would  result in 
unnecessary  duplicative obligations .  
 
More broadly, this requirement has the potential to disproportionately impact smaller 
organisations, which could find the imposition of conformity assessments too high a barrier 
given the prescriptive nature of the Guardrail. This would likely hinder innov ation and limit 
the organisation’s ability to adopt AI systems.  
 
We support some form of demonstr able compliance,  but we believe this will be materially 
achieved through existing laws and regulations.  
 
REGULATORY OPTIONS TO MANDATE THE GUARDRAILS  
The importance of balancing the safe and responsible use of AI and the enablement of 
innovation cannot be understated. A regulatory regime that is overly burdensome will have 
negative impacts on innovation and the productivity benefits that this technology  brings, 
while also hindering Australia’s ability to compete internationally.  
 
As a starting point, Westpac encourages the Government to conduct a mapping exercise of 
the application of existing laws relative to AI. Such an exercise  would allow  for the 
identification of any gaps . 
  
Westpac supports  the introduc tion of  framework legislation (Option 2) that defines guardrails  
and their thresholds. This framework legislation would provide  a consistent set of definitions 
and measures  that would then be implemented  through amendments to existing regulations.  
This approach would better support regulatory settings that balance innovati on with 
consumer protections.   
  
 4 Westpac Group  The f inancial services  industry  is already subject to extensive regulatory requirements, many 
of which will apply where AI is used (e.g. Privacy, Consumer Protection, Anti -Discrimination), 
with regulators already in place to support enforcement of those Acts. A frameworks 
approach would ens ure a more consistent approach to AI reform through standardised 
definitions that apply across industries yet offer flexibility to adapt the existing legislation and 
regulatory requirements in a way that is specific and tailored to each sector. We recognis e 
existing legislation may still need to be uplifted to address gaps where existing requirements 
do not adequately address AI systems, particularly when it comes to its application to 
developers, however having an overarching framework would ensure consiste ncy.  
 
We acknowledge that the framework approach has some limitations. T he scope and power 
of existing legislation may not apply consistently across all industries which may result in 
differences in sectorial enforcement approaches, particularly where some Acts may not 
apply to smaller organisations or individuals. Further, a framework approach would need to 
consider the division of regulator y responsibilities to ensure there is no overlap.  
 
Westpac is not supportive of an AI -specific Act (Option 3) as this has the potential to add 
complexity, duplication and increased cost of compliance to an already heavily regulated 
industry. This approach would also require the introduction of a dedicated oversight model, 
such as a new regulator to suppor t enforcement . A new specific -AI Act may limit local 
investment into research and development, or the availability of international tools where the 
cost to comply with legislation is untenable , thereby limi ting innovation and the  benefits from 
AI. 
 
EXTRA -TERRITORIAL APPLICATION OF THE MANDATORY 
GUARDRAILS  
In implementing a regulatory regime, clarity will need to be provided regarding the extra -
territorial application of the guardrails. Considerations include how the guardrails would 
apply to overseas entities that may operate in another jurisdiction but impact end users in 
Australia.  
 
Westpac works with global technology partners, essential for accessing cutting -edge AI 
capabilities. However, we recognise that new regulations could impact our partners' ability to 
operate in Australia if they are unable to meet the requirements. As examp les, Guardrail 8 
emphasises transparency across the AI supply chain, meaning our partners would need to 
share information about their AI models and systems with us to help us manage risks. 
Similarly, Guardrail 10 mandates conformity assessments to demonstr ate compliance, which 
could pose additional overheads  for our partners in evidencing this obligation.  
 
If the proposed regulations create technical constraints, or adversely impact the commercial 
viability for our international partners, their offerings to the Australian market may be limited 
or even withdrawn. This could restrict Australia's access to lead ing global technologies and 
significantly affect our current operational capabilities. Given that the most significant 
advancements in AI solutions and capabilities are not native to Australia, leveraging the best 
global capabilities is crucial to support innovation and maximise the benefits from AI.  
 
CONCLUSION  
Westpac is eager to support the Government as it develops the proposed guardrails and its 
approach to implementation. We are committed to the implement ation of  a regulatory regime 
that fosters innovation, ensures the safe and responsible use of AI, and allows Australia to 
compete internationally . We would welcome the opportunity to participate in any future 
consultation or AI advisory group established by the Government.  ","041024_Westpac_submission_Mandatory_guardrails_for_AI_in_high_risk_settings.f8c724b8","041024_Westpac_submission_Mandatory_guardrails_for_AI_in_high_risk_settings.f8c724b8.pdf","pdf","C:\\Users\\james.barro\\Documents\\rsm-nvidia-hackathon\\data\\Submission_Docs\\Processed\\041024_Westpac_submission_Mandatory_guardrails_for_AI_in_high_risk_settings.f8c724b8.pdf","1"
"43","44","44","Meta","https://consult.industry.gov.au/ai-mandatory-guardrails/submission/view/276","  Meta’s Submission on the Safe and Responsible AI in Australia: Proposals Paper for introducing mandatory guardrails for AI in high risk settings  4 October 2024            
2  Executive Summary Meta welcomes the opportunity to share feedback on the Australian Government’s Safe and Responsible AI in Australia: Proposals Paper for introducing mandatory guardrails for AI in high risk settings (the Proposals).   Meta fully supports risk-based approaches, which are technology-neutral and interoperable with regulatory frameworks around the world, many of which Australia is also a party to, such as the Seoul Frontier AI Safety Commitments,1 the Munich Tech Accord,2 the Thorn Principles,3 and the White House Frontier AI Commitments.4   We also note the establishment of the AI Safety Institutes under the Bletchley Park Declaration,5 and the work that the network is progressing, including Australia's participation in the upcoming inaugural meeting of the International Network of AI Safety Institutes6 in San Francisco in November. Meta firmly supports the shared ambition of the AI Safety Institutes to advance the science of AI safety and progress international coordination, including in areas such as mutual recognition among the Safety Institutes, to ensure that the benefits of AI development and deployment are shared equitably around the globe.   We are also aligned with the stated objective of the Government and we make the following comments to assist the Government to continue to ensure that Australia’s regulatory regime is aligned with existing frameworks. In particular, it is important to:   1 UK Government, Frontier AI Safety Commitments, AI Seoul Summit 2024, 21 May 2024, https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/frontier-ai-safety-commitments-ai-seoul-summit-2024/frontier-ai-safety-commitments-ai-seoul-summit-2024  2 Munich Security Conference, A Tech Accord to Combat Deceptive Use of AI in 2024 Elections, https://securityconference.org/en/aielectionsaccord  3 Thorn, ‘Thorn and All Tech Is Human Forge Generative AI Principles with AI Leaders to Enact Strong Child Safety Commitments’, 16 July 2024, https://www.thorn.org/blog/generative-ai-principles  4 The White House, ‘Ensuring Safe, Secure, and Trustworthy AI’, July 2023, https://www.whitehouse.gov/wp-content/uploads/2023/07/Ensuring-Safe-Secure-and-Trustworthy-AI.pdf  5 UK Government, ‘The Bletchley Declaration by Countries Attending the AI Safety Summit, 1-2 November 2023’, Policy Paper, 1 November 2023, https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/ai-safety-summit-2023-the-bletchley-declaration/the-bletchley-declaration-by-countries-attending-the-ai-safety-summit-1-2-november-2023 6 U.S. Department of Commerce, ‘U.S. Secretary of Commerce Raimondo and U.S. Secretary of State Blinken Announce Inaugural Convening of International Network of AI Safety Institutes in San Francisco’, Press Releases, 18 September 2024, https://www.commerce.gov/news/press-releases/2024/09/us-secretary-commerce-raimondo-and-us-secretary-state-blinken-announce  3 (1) Scope: (a) ensure that there is a clear and consistent distinction between “systems” and “models”; (b) bring additional clarity to the threshold above which the guardrails should apply  (i.e., the “most advanced AI,” “frontier AI”, or all AI); and (c) provide further differentiation between “traditional AI” and “generative AI.”    (2) Ex ante designations: ensure further alignment with the emerging norm of other risk-based approaches globally through additional consideration concerning the highly capable General Purpose AI (‘GPAI’) Models.   (3) Allocation of responsibilities along the AI supply chain: ensure that the intention set out in the Proposals that the guardrails would allow obligations along the supply chain where they are most effective is more concretely reflected across all the guardrails.   (4) Conformity assessments:  strike the right balance between baseline obligations on all model developers and introducing internationally unique obligations pre-deployment which may disproportionately affect open source models.    (5) Ease of compliance: consider technical feasibility on provenance and risk assessments as well as the continued protection of trade secrets in terms of the ease of compliance, and a goal of the Proposals to “minimise regulatory burdens on Australian businesses” and “help attract more foreign business investment in Australia.”   In addition to responding to the questions posed in the consultation (see Annex A), we offer our perspective on addressing the above concerns by focusing on definitional issues, all of the entities that play a role in the AI ecosystem (also known as the ‘AI value chain’) and adopting a marginal risk analysis framework when considering risks.   In response to the paper’s questions on “open source AI”, we also offer an overview at the outset of the importance of fully considering open source AI (and the context of the development and deployment of such models), issues regarding a  definition, and obligations for model developers.   We are committed to continued engagement with the Australian Government as it progresses its commitments under its interim response to the Safe and Responsible AI in Australia discussion paper to develop a regulatory environment that builds community trust and promotes AI adoption. Our comments are proposed in the spirit of that collaboration.   4 Table of contents  Executive Summary ................................................................................................................................... 4 Open Source AI ............................................................................................................................................ 7 (i) The definition of “open source AI” .................................................................................................. 8 (ii) Obligations on Model Developers .................................................................................................. 9 Feedback on the Proposals .................................................................................................................... 10 1. Unclear Scope [Guardrails #1-10] .................................................................................................... 10 (i) Systems and Models .................................................................................................................. 10 (ii) Traditional AI v Generative AI .................................................................................................. 10 (iii) Thresholds .................................................................................................................................. 11 2. Ex ante designation of ‘high-risk’ [Guardrail #4] ......................................................................... 11 (i) Risks Assessments ..................................................................................................................... 12 (ii) High Risk AI Systems ................................................................................................................ 13 3. Disproportionate allocation of responsibilities upstream in the AI supply chain [Guardrail # 2] ............................................................................................................................................................... 14 4. Conformity assessments [Guardrails #9, #10] ............................................................................ 15 5. Feasibility [Guardrails #3, #4, #5, #6, #8] .................................................................................... 17 (i) Technical Feasibility : Provenance and Risk Assessments / Evaluations (red-teaming) ............................................................................................................................................................ 17 (ii) Trade Secrets .............................................................................................................................. 19 6. Options for introducing mandatory guardrails ............................................................................ 19 Annex A: Table of Responses to Specific Consultation Questions ................................................... 21   5 Open Source AI   In the section of the paper, “Applying the guardrails across the AI supply chain and throughout the AI lifecycle”, the Government seeks further feedback on a number of issues pertaining to “open source AI”: the definition, “open-washing”, and obligations on model developers. Given the importance of open source AI to Australia, both for its macroeconomy and national security, we will provide our responses to these issues first and then offer our feedback on the specific guardrails.    Meta is committed to open source AI, which drives innovation and economic growth, and creates better, safer products from which everyone can benefit. That’s why we make Llama openly available to the AI community: since 2023, there have been around 350 million downloads of all Llama versions, demonstrating how Llama has been a bedrock for AI innovation, globally.7  An open approach to AI leads to better, safer products, faster innovation, and a larger market. Frontier-level open models in particular – based on text or multimodal – can turbocharge productivity, drive scientific research and add hundreds of billions of euros to the European economy (according to J.P. Morgan Research,8 AI could increase global GDP by US$10 trillion, or by as much as 10 per cent). Free and accessible open source AI is a quicker path to realising this growth. Public institutions and researchers are already using these models to speed up medical research and preserve languages, while established businesses and start-ups are getting access to tools they could never build or afford themselves. Without them, the development of AI will happen elsewhere – depriving Australians of the technological advances enjoyed in the US, China and India.  Open source democratises access to the benefits of AI. These benefits are potentially profound for Australia, and for societies around the world. We have seen a groundswell of enthusiasm for open source AI from small businesses. They recognise the transformative potential of AI for competition, innovation, and productivity, which is already contributing to Australian economic growth. Having access to state-of-the-art AI creates opportunities for everyone, not just a small handful of Big Tech companies. Open source provides the foundations on which developers can innovate that would otherwise be prohibitively costly.   7 MetaAI, ‘With 10x growth since 2023, Llama is the leading engine of AI innovation’ https://ai.meta.com/blog/llama-usage-doubled-may-through-july-2024/  8 J.P. Morgan, ‘Is generative AI a game changer?’, Global Research, 14 February 2024, https://www.jpmorgan.com/insights/global-research/artificial-intelligence/generative-ai#:~:text=Overall%2C%20J.P.%20Morgan%20Research%20estimates,shape%20of%20the%20economic%20cycle  6 We are already seeing significant innovation within the Australian ecosystem based on open source - including the Kangaroo LLM9 project to create Australia’s first open-source AI model, and the use of Meta’s Llama model to create simulated conversations for students undertaking journalism, legal and other courses at the University of New South Wales.   Across the Tasman, Netsafe New Zealand, an independent, non-profit online safety organisation, uses Meta Llama models to develop a robust redaction tool for harmful digital communications. The tool uses a tuned LLM model and the AI4Privacy dataset to automatically redact sensitive information, enabling faster and more effective harm resolution. Netsafe's data pipeline sources, redacts and aggregates data from various channels, minimising victim impact and augmenting Digital Harm Resolution Officers' skills.10  We have also heard from the U.N. and governments across the Global South that open source AI could finally light the path to achieve the Sustainable Development Goals. As reflected in the recently adopted U.N. General Assembly Resolutions on Artificial Intelligence, the U.N. and governments see the value of open source AI as a force multiplier to help deliver services to their populations, close inequality gaps, and fuel new technologies and enterprises.   This belief in open source AI was further underscored by the recent G20 Ministerial Declaration,11 which recognised the important role of open-source in unleashing the potential of AI and data to both public and private sectors for public interest, as well as the recent report by the UN High Level Advisory Body on AI.12  (i) The definition of “open source AI” There is no singular, global definition of what “open source” AI means. Defining open source within the context of AI foundation models is challenging, because existing open source definitions traditionally applied to software do not easily transfer to AI models, given the complexity and the capabilities of these models and how they are developed and deployed. The industry is still attempting to develop a vocabulary for talking about  9 Kangaroo LLM, ‘Kangaroo Bot: Powering Australia's AI Data Collection’, https://kangaroollm.com.au/kangaroo-bot  10 Meta, ‘Community stories: Discover the possibilities of building on Meta Llama’, https://llama.meta.com/community-stories   11 UK Government, ‘G20 Ministerial Declaration: 13 September, 2024’, Notice, 14 September 2024, https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/g20-ministerial-declaration-maceio-13-september-2024/g20-ministerial-declaration-13-september-2024  12 U.N. AI Advisory Body, ‘Final Report - Governing AI for Humanity’, September 2024, https://www.un.org/en/ai-advisory-body  7 a very new technology.  As recently as March 2024, for example, top AI researchers, led by staff at Linux, were proposing a more nuanced approach to openness focused on the categories of “open model, open tool and open science,” in what they called the Model Openness Framework. Indeed, many AI model developers and experts are already advocating for a more nuanced understanding of openness in AI that should be seen as a spectrum and gradient, not binary.   Any open source AI definition should allow developers to access a rich ecosystem of open source AI models and tools and avoid overly restrictive definitions, which may prevent access to state of the art technology and lead to an overly regulated environment where only a few large companies are able to access this type of technology. A key component of open source AI includes making model weights freely available for developers, researchers, academics and others to use.13  Of course, given the new capabilities of AI systems, it is important for open source approaches to include ways to help ensure the safety and responsible use of these models. Meta’s approach to model development emphasises innovation, safety, and responsibility through collaboration with the open source AI community.  For example, our Responsible Use Guide discusses limitations in performance that affect appropriate use and provides guidance to developers on how to safely build with large language models, including our latest Llama 3.2 models, and appropriate use cases for these models.14  (ii) Obligations on Model Developers On the question of what obligations should apply to the developers and deployers of open source models, we believe that all developers of models which meet an appropriate risk threshold should have baseline obligations, irrespective of the final form of the model’s release. Though there are variances in approaches, we have seen an emerging general international consensus through frameworks such as the Seoul Frontier AI Safety Commitments around the transparency and governance obligations which should apply across the board, regardless of the mode of release of the model, or the context of the model’s development and deployment. These obligations include establishing and publishing safety policy frameworks for the development and  13 To help developers start building with our open Llama model more easily, Meta has also released Llama Stack, which standardises the building blocks needed to bring generative AI applications to market. These building blocks span the entire development lifecycle: from model training and fine-tuning to evaluation and running AI agents in production.  14 See, for example, Meta’s ‘Responsible Use Guide’, which sets out resources and best practice for responsible development of products built with large language models: https://ai.meta.com/static-resource/sept-responsible-use-guide  8 deployment of frontier AI, including the thresholds model developers establish for risk assessments. Feedback on the Proposals 1. Unclear Scope [Guardrails #1-10] (i) Systems and Models The Proposals are not clear or consistent concerning what obligation(s) apply to systems or models, or both. To address this, we suggest that in the first instance, the Government looks to align with established definitions for systems and models found in the United States’ Executive Order on the Safe, Secure and Trustworthy Development and Use of Artificial Intelligence.15   The Government should then specify which guardrails apply to the developer, deployer and/or end user of the generative AI system based on the use/setting. Model developers should not be responsible or liable for system-level assessments and mitigations contemplated in the guardrails. This is aligned with the intention in the proposals paper to take account of the AI supply chain, and accordingly imposes guardrails where they can be most effectively applied.  (ii) Traditional AI v Generative AI  As the paper notes, “In Australia, the development and use of AI is shaped by a range of legal frameworks, including areas such as consumer, privacy, anti-discrimination, competition, data protection, and copyright law. The Australian Government announced an investment of $4.2 million in the 2024–25 Budget to clarify and strengthen existing laws impacted by AI, with reviews in priority areas of health care, consumer and copyright law.”  In addition to the Government’s work on existing regulation and bringing a contemporary interpretation to that for the generative AI age, rather than risk creating overlapping, conflicting, or duplicative obligations by applying guardrails to both traditional AI and generative AI, the Government should focus its efforts on the guardrails on generative AI only. This is the approach that has been adopted through the White House Voluntary AI Commitments, which are “designed to advance a  15 The U.S. Executive Order on the Safe, Secure, and Trustworthy Development and Use of Artificial Intelligence, 30 October 2023, https://www.whitehouse.gov/briefing-room/presidential-actions/2023/10/30/executive-order-on-the-safe-secure-and-trustworthy-development-and-use-of-artificial-intelligence 9 generative AI legal and policy regime”, and the Seoul Frontier AI Safety Commitments which apply to ‘frontier AI’ only, where ‘frontier AI’ is defined as “highly capable general-purpose AI models or systems (otherwise known as ‘generative models or systems’) that can perform a wide variety of tasks and match or exceed the capabilities present in the most advanced models.”  (iii) Thresholds  The Proposals are not sufficiently clear on the threshold for systems that will be in scope. For example, the paper suggests that “adopting an approach that focuses on the most advanced AI systems would also build on commitments developers have made through the Hiroshima AI Process Code of Conduct and the Frontier AI Safety Commitments”.16 However, elsewhere in the paper it is suggested that all AI systems will be in scope. To reflect the intention of aligning with existing frameworks such as the Seoul Frontier AI Safety Commitments, the Government should clarify that the guardrails only apply to ‘frontier AI’, where “frontier AI” is defined as, “highly capable general-purpose AI models or systems that can perform a wide variety of tasks and match or exceed the capabilities present in the most advanced models.”17 2. Ex ante designation of ‘high-risk’ [Guardrail #4] The Government should take an evidence-based approach, and avoid ascribing ex ante risk labels based on the technology itself. However, the Proposals assume that GPAI by its nature is high-risk. This is not supported by the vast science and evidence of GPAI capabilities and performance and would, in fact, go against the Government’s stated intention to not undermine the benefits of innovation in AI in lower risk contexts.   Internationally, there are variances in approaches, and none are perfect given the nascent state of the science.18 However, consensus is crystallising around applying enhanced obligations to models which are above certain thresholds - be that using a quantitative threshold (for example, by using a certain amount of compute), and/or qualitative criteria (for example, by using the demonstrable capabilities of the model to  16 Australian Government, ‘Safe and responsible AI in Australia: Proposals paper for introducing mandatory guardrails for AI in high-risk settings’, September 2024, https://storage.googleapis.com/converlens-au-industry/industry/p/prj2f6f02ebfe6a8190c7bdc/page/proposals_paper_for_introducing_mandatory_guardrails_for_ai_in_high_risk_settings.pdf, p5  17 UK Government, ‘Frontier AI Safety Commitments, AI Seoul Summit 2024’, Policy Paper, 21 May 2024,  https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/frontier-ai-safety-commitments-ai-seoul-summit-2024/frontier-ai-safety-commitments-ai-seoul-summit-2024  18 See R Bommasani, S Arora, Y Choi, et al, ‘A Path for Science‑ and Evidence‑based AI Policy’, https://understanding-ai-safety.org  10 significantly enhance the ability for a non-expert to create chemical, biological, explosive, radiological, or nuclear risks).   The Bletchley Park Declaration, to which Australia is a signatory, describes the risks of “highly capable general purpose AI models,” rather than the entire category of GPAI.19 Similarly, the Seoul Frontier AI Safety Commitments specifically address “highly capable general-purpose AI models” as high risk.20 There is no precedent globally of an in force regulation that defines the entire category of GPAI models as high risk, precisely because the evidence does not support this.   While the discussion of what the threshold should be and which models are and are not high risk is important, in order to even reach that, the Government’s proposal needs to move away from a blanket discussion of GPAI as high risk. GPAI models that do not meet the criteria for high risk should not have added guardrails, as the entire exercise of the proposal is to create guardrails for “AI in high risk settings”.   Specifically, Meta suggests that the Government might look to the threshold established in the United States’ Executive Order on the Safe, Secure, and Trustworthy Development and Use of Artificial Intelligence which creates a flexible, yet predictable, way to categorise which GPAI models above these thresholds21 could be required to comply with additional obligations.   19 UK Government, ‘The Bletchley Declaration by Countries Attending the AI Safety Summit, 1-2 November 2023’, https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/ai-safety-summit-2023-the-bletchley-declaration/the-bletchley-declaration-by-countries-attending-the-ai-safety-summit-1-2-november-2023   20 UK Government, ‘Frontier AI Safety Commitments, AI Seoul Summit 2024’, Policy Paper, 21 May 2024,  https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/frontier-ai-safety-commitments-ai-seoul-summit-2024/frontier-ai-safety-commitments-ai-seoul-summit-2024  21  The U.S. Executive Order on the Safe, Secure, and Trustworthy Development and Use of Artificial Intelligence, 30 October 2023, https://www.whitehouse.gov/briefing-room/presidential-actions/2023/10/30/executive-order-on-the-safe-secure-and-trustworthy-development-and-use-of-artificial-intelligence states in s 4.2(b) (Ensuring Safe and Reliable AI): (i) any model that was trained using a quantity of computing power greater than 10*26 integer or floating-point operations, or using primarily biological sequence data and using a quantity of computing power greater than 10*23 integer or floating-point operations; and(ii) any computing cluster that has a set of machines physically co-located in a single datacenter, transitively connected by data center networking of over 100 Gbit/s, and having a theoretical maximum computing capacity of 10*20 integer or floating-point operations per second for training AI; and In s 3(k) (Definitions):  that exhibits, or could be easily modified to exhibit, high levels of performance at tasks that pose a serious risk to security, national economic security, national public health or safety, or any combination of those matters, such as by: (i)  substantially lowering the barrier of entry for non-experts to design, synthesise, acquire, or use chemical, biological, radiological, or nuclear (CBRN) weapons; (ii) enabling powerful offensive cyber operations through automated vulnerability discovery and exploitation against a wide range of potential targets of cyber attacks; or (iii) permitting the evasion of human control or oversight through means of deception or obfuscation.  11 (i) Risks Assessments The proposed guardrail that sets up risk assessment requirements is overly broad and vague, which would make it a very difficult to apply standard. We believe that its scope should be anchored to areas of emerging consensus (in the forums and frameworks mentioned above), and be limited to reasonably foreseeable or known risks within specific areas, such as human rights, safety or health.  Risks should be cognisable and measurable based on standardised benchmarking and a marginal risk assessment of the use case. Such evaluations help industry and others to understand model performance, enabling documentation of the model's capabilities and limitations. However, AI evaluations and benchmarks remain a nascent field and, at present, there is a lack of consensus about how best to measure and evaluate certain risks.    In particular, evaluating general purpose models is challenging because these models can perform a wide range of tasks and involve interactions between the systems into which they are integrated. Determinations around model evaluations need to be based on repeatable measurements. Helpfully, valuable work is underway and progress is being made by numerous research institutions, the United States Department of Commerce’s National Institute of Standards and Technology (NIST), the United States AI Safety Institute, and the MLCommons initiative. The Australian Government should consider these efforts and focus on identifying if there are gaps that remain to be addressed. If there are gaps, the Government should consider various measurements related to the ability of the model in question.   The marginal risk of releasing a model should be the gold standard of measuring the risk of models, rather than relying on an ex-ante perspective and labelling it unsafe or inherently risky. A marginal risk approach is the generally accepted best method for measuring the risk as against potential benefits of generative AI models. In developing the guardrails, we believe that a marginal risk framework is the most appropriate framework through which to consider risks, and how they might be assessed and mitigated.  (ii) High Risk AI Systems  We believe that the best way to describe high risk AI is by providing a list of consequential use cases. That is, the list should enumerate specific use cases where the AI system plays a major role in a consequential decision. By focusing on consequential uses, there is a reduced likelihood of unintentionally capturing low risk use cases while also ensuring proper treatment of legitimate high risk uses.  12  In contrast, a principles-based approach as proposed by the government would generate legal and commercial uncertainty. Companies will likely interpret and apply each principle differently, and their interpretations will differ from those of any regulator. As a result, companies will not likely be able to confidently implement the guardrails and, to avoid enforcement risk, may over-apply the guardrails to low risk AI systems.   A vague principles-based approach may deter Australian businesses’ uptake of AI systems, even when these systems are low risk and would lead to productivity boosts. Businesses in Australia should be encouraged to harness the power of low risk AI. But any regulatory system that leaves companies in doubt about their compliance obligations is sure to undermine this goal. Thus, a list-based approach, rooted in consequential uses, would be a better approach. 3. Disproportionate allocation of responsibilities upstream in the AI supply chain [Guardrail # 2] In order to comprehensively and effectively address the risk of misuse for general purpose AI models, the guardrails must sufficiently cover not only model developers, but all entities in the AI value chain. We refer the Government to the PAI Guidance for Safe Foundation Model Deployment,22 which sets out the various actors: model providers (who train general purpose models that others build on to create AI systems for specific applications); downstream developers (who use open source models to build AI applications for their own use cases, often using their own data for fine-tuning the model); model deployers (who can be the same as the downstream developer); and end users (those who use the final AI application). By outlining different phases of AI development and deployment, it is then possible to pinpoint opportunities for effective interventions and determine specific mitigation strategies that various actors can feasibly and appropriately implement.23    Due to technology's dynamic nature, as well as its potential risks and benefits, policy measures should focus on differentiating responsibilities across this value chain in order to provide legal and business clarity. In this regard, the Government should prioritise  22 Partnership on AI, PAI’s Guidance for Safe Foundation Model Deployment: A Framework for Collective Action, https://partnershiponai.org/modeldeployment  23 Partnership on AI, ‘ Risk Mitigation Strategies for the Open Foundation Model Value Chain’: https://partnershiponai.org/wp-content/uploads/dlm_uploads/2024/07/open-foundation-model-risk-mitigation_rev3-1.pdf 13 standardising the threat models and evaluations along the AI value chain, and clarifying when and where risk assessments and mitigations are most effectively applied.    The proposal for assessing responsibility across the AI lifecycle is not rooted in the fundamentals of how GPAI models and the generative AI systems built on top of such models work. Importantly, the developer building the model is creating an engine that is capable of powering a substantial number of uses, whereas the deployer is the one that makes specific choices on how and in what form the “engine” gets used.  Accordingly, risk is context-dependent, and many of the risks associated with GPAI models will only become apparent and amenable to targeted mitigations once they are used for a defined purpose. It is therefore important to empower downstream developers with tools they can easily and effectively use to assess and mitigate risks presented by their specific AI applications,24 while also noting that ultimately, these developers are responsible for using such tools as appropriate for their specific use cases.   Having to address all foreseeable future use cases would be an impossible obligation for developers, whereas deployers can more effectively address contextual risks because they understand the use case, and can apply mitigations at the system-level. Thus, Meta suggests a re-alignment of the responsibility, to take into consideration the importance of the deployers in the AI lifecycle.  In the specific case of open source GPAI models, while Meta’s own guardrails – such as our model license and limitations on volume of use – provide us with important ways to manage some risks, no open source AI developer can monitor downstream use of their models. Therefore, it is crucial that the guardrails take into consideration the technical feasibility of such obligations.  4. Conformity assessments [Guardrails #9, #10] We are concerned by the lack of clarity regarding the proposed requirement to “undertake conformity assessments to demonstrate and certify compliance with the guardrails.”   The Proposals contemplate that prior to deploying a high risk AI system, developers will be required to conduct a conformity assessment either themselves, or via a third-party or government entities or regulators and “once completed, the organisation will attain  24 Meta open sources several safety tools which developers can use to build responsibly with Meta’s Llama models: https://www.llama.com/trust-and-safety/ 14 certification of compliance.” This suggests that deployers could be required to obtain certification by the Government before deploying high-risk systems in Australia.   The Proposals provide many examples of categories of systems that would be in scope as “high-risk”, in particular through the concept of “systemic impact”, with the result that deployers may have hundreds of different systems that  would require conformity assessments. Such an obligation at scale is neither proportionate nor warranted. We suggest clarifying that internal assessments would be sufficient for demonstrating  compliance.   Furthermore, as the Proposals would classify all GPAI models as high risk, the conformity assessment guardrail would create a disproportionate impact on the deployment of all open source AI models, because such models would be subject to “passing” the conformity assessment by the regulator before being made available in Australia on an open source basis.   A pre-deployment certification requirement could be incompatible with releasing an open-source AI model for several reasons: i) Restrictions on modification: A certification requirement might imply that the model cannot be modified or changed in any way once it has been “certified”, which is incompatible with the principles of open-source.  ii) Testing protocols and evaluation metrics: The certification process might involve testing protocols, or evaluation metrics that are not publicly available, making it difficult or impossible for open-source developers to replicate the testing process or evaluate and improve their models.  iii) Cost and accessibility barriers: The certification process might be costly and/or require significant resources, creating a barrier for open-source developers who may not have the necessary funding or infrastructure to obtain certification. iv) Time-consuming and bureaucratic process: The certification process might be time-consuming and bureaucratic, which could slow down the development and deployment of open-source AI models.  By requiring pre-deployment certification, the Proposals may inadvertently create a situation where open-source AI models are at a disadvantage compared to proprietary models. This could stifle innovation and limit the adoption of open-source AI solutions.  In addition to the points made previously regarding ex ante designation of all GPAI models as high risk, Meta suggests clarifying that any such certification be considered optional and limited to “frontier AI”, as stated above. 15 5. Feasibility [Guardrails #3, #4, #5, #6, #8] (i) Technical Feasibility : Provenance and Risk Assessments / Evaluations (red-teaming)  (a) Provenance The labelling / watermarking guardrail seems to apply indiscriminately to all generative AI outputs and is not, contrary to the Government’s stated goals, indexed to risk. Meta believes an approach that follows that stated goal would be to take such steps that are reasonable in the circumstances (if any), taking into account the risk of actual harm to individuals associated with the outputs. As such, outputs that are very low risk would not be subject to this requirement. One clear low risk output would be AI-generated stickers that are not photorealistic and that users interact with in a particular context. Such stickers pose a low risk of harm, even if used maliciously.   Any measures which should apply to the outputs of an AI system are most effectively applied by the developer of that system. This is particularly the case regarding provenance measures, such as labelling and watermarking, and is why the Proposals should clearly reflect the allocation of responsibility along the AI supply chain.   Further, Meta suggests that the Government add to the guardrail language that explicitly limits requirements to those that are technically feasible and commercially reasonable. The state of the science around provenance and watermarking is still in its infancy. Because so many of the tools available today are still in “beta” form, proscriptive rules are largely not yet appropriate or technically feasible. In addition, identifying photo-/audio-realistic AI-generated content is an industry-wide issue that requires industry-wide solutions.   Meta is committed to doing our part, both on our platforms and through our products, to ensure people can understand when they are engaging with AI-generated content. This includes investing significantly in disclosure mechanisms, including perceptible and imperceptible watermarks.25   When photorealistic images are created using our Meta AI feature, we put visible watermarks on the images, and both invisible watermarks and metadata are embedded  25 Meta, ‘Our Approach to Labeling AI-Generated Content and Manipulated Media’, Newsroom, 5 April 2024, https://about.fb.com/news/2024/04/metas-approach-to-labeling-ai-generated-content-and-manipulated-media; Meta, ‘Labeling AI-Generated Images on Facebook, Instagram and Threads’, Newsroom, 6 February 2024, https://about.fb.com/news/2024/02/labeling-ai-generated-images-on-facebook-instagram-and-threads  16 within image files. These disclosure mechanisms help both people and other platforms identify AI-generated images. We are also building industry-leading tools that can identify invisible markers at scale (specifically, the “AI generated” information in the C2PA and IPTC technical standards), so that we can label images from companies like Google, OpenAI, Microsoft, Adobe, Midjourney, and Shutterstock as they implement their plans for adding metadata to images created by their tools.26  In order to place political or social issues ads on our platforms, an advertiser is required to go through a verification process. We are also requiring advertisers to disclose when they use AI or digital techniques to create or alter ads when 1) the ad contains a photorealistic image or video, or realistic sounding audio, and 2) it was digitally created or altered to depict a real person as saying or doing something they did not say or do.27   Finally, since AI-generated content appears across the internet, we are also working closely with partners to develop industry-wide solutions, such as signing the Munich Tech Accord to Combat Deceptive Use of AI in Elections,28 joining the C2PA Steering Committee,29 and working with other companies to develop common standards for identifying AI-generated content through fora like the Partnership on AI (PAI).  (b) Risk Assessments / Evaluations (red-teaming) While we believe that red-teaming is an important evaluation tool, it should not be the sole evaluation method used or required in all instances.   Responsible development should include robust risk assessments and evaluations (which may include red-teaming for certain models) that are: commensurate with the potential risks involved; transparent; and include minimum guardrails. Common standards should be based on existing frameworks (e.g., the PAI Guidance for Safe Foundation Model Deployment) and take into account the extensive work in the field of international partners, such as that of the National Telecommunications and Information Administration (NTIA) and NIST in the US, the UK AI Safety Institute, and Australia’s own AI Expert Group.   26  Meta, ‘Our Approach to Labeling AI-Generated Content and Manipulated Media’, Newsroom, 5 April 2024, https://about.fb.com/news/2024/04/metas-approach-to-labeling-ai-generated-content-and-manipulated-media; Meta, ‘Labeling AI-Generated Images on Facebook, Instagram and Threads’, Newsroom, 6 February 2024, https://about.fb.com/news/2024/02/labeling-ai-generated-images-on-facebook-instagram-and-threads  27  Meta, ‘Helping people understand when AI or digital methods are used in political or social issue ads’, https://www.facebook.com/government-nonprofits/blog/political-ads-ai-disclosure-policy  28 Munich Security Conference, A Tech Accord to Combat Deceptive Use of AI in 2024 Elections, https://securityconference.org/en/aielectionsaccord  29 C2PA, ‘Meta Joins the C2PA Steering Committee’, 5 September 2024: https://c2pa.org/post/meta_pr/ 17  The Government should not anchor on red-teaming as the most important – or only – evaluation methodology in all circumstances. Red-teaming is a costly undertaking, and is limited by the expertise and scope of the red-teamers. For example, if a red-team group is only composed of experts in one particular area, it will not be best equipped to test for vulnerabilities outside of that scope. Developers need to be able to deploy a range of evaluations, including tools like benchmarking, human evaluation, A/B testing, cross-validation and adversarial attacks.   We encourage the Government to consider how it might facilitate establishing common standards for risk assessments, benchmarks and evaluations informed by science and in collaboration with the global network of AI Safety Institutes.  (ii) Trade Secrets Information within model evaluations can be highly commercially sensitive. Therefore, mandatory public disclosures should be carefully tailored to avoid compelling model developers to disclose commercial secrets, and be limited to areas where there is a significant public benefit. One possible solution might be to disclose information to authorities only.  Building datasets to train foundation models requires proprietary research into what data is effective and techniques for collection and preparation. While it makes sense for companies to provide high-level summaries about the type of data used (such as, for instance, time of collection or domain level info), many details about how data is collected, processed, and the final data composition are competitively valuable and sensitive. Flexibility is important. Because of how models are developed, the data used and the confidential business practices adopted will vary across different model developers. Any requirements to provide dataset transparency should not be overly prescriptive and should provide optionality for developers to provide lists or narrative descriptions as appropriate to preserve trade secrets and confidential information. 6. Options for introducing mandatory guardrails There are an increasing number of voluntary initiatives, domestic regulations, and international laws and principles emerging around AI, in particular generative AI. Given the increasing potential for regulatory fragmentation globally, it is important to ensure consistency and interoperability, particularly around definitions, taxonomies, risk identification, assessments, and mitigations. In this regard, the Government might usefully look to the US Executive Order on the Safe, Secure, and Trustworthy 18 Development and Use of Artificial Intelligence, or the Seoul Frontier AI Safety Commitments. We also note the work of the EU-US Trade and Technology Council on taxonomies, the report of the UN High Level Advisory Board on AI, the UN Resolutions on AI governance, and UN Global Digital Compact.   Finally, the Government has sought feedback on which of the three options would be the most successful in mandating the guardrails. We believe that the best way forward would be Option 2 (a framework approach): Adapting existing regulatory frameworks through framework legislation.   This Option, which recognises that Australia’s existing laws may be capable of giving effect – or can be amended to give effect – to one or more of the proposed mandatory guardrails in individual regimes, would take cognisance of the applicability of existing laws and regulations already to AI and look to address gaps by providing a consistent set of definitions, defining the guardrails to apply and establishing thresholds for when they would apply. This is the most appropriate for achieving the policy outcomes being pursued - namely the safe and responsible use of AI in Australia in a manner that is aligned with emerging international consensus on AI governance.                  19 Annex A: Table of Responses to Specific Consultation Questions  No Question Summary of Meta Response Defining high-risk AI 1 Do the proposed principles adequately capture high-risk AI? Are there any principles we should add or remove? Please identify any: ● low-risk use cases that are unintentionally captured ● categories of uses that should be treated separately, such as uses for defence or national security purposes.  The way high-risk AI is being defined is not conducive to the stated goal of the government, and is not rooted in prevailing global frameworks.  Firstly, the proposal does not make a clear distinction between AI models and  AI systems. Secondly, unlike the assertions of the Government, GPAI models are not high risk in and of themselves. Thirdly, those that are should be assessed by already-established thresholds, such as the Seoul Frontier AI Commitments, of which Australia is a member.  2   Do you have any suggestions for how the principles could better capture harms to First Nations people, communities and Country? N/A 
3 Do the proposed principles, supported by examples, give enough clarity and certainty on high-risk AI settings and high-risk AI models? Is a more defined approach, with a list of illustrative uses, needed? ● If you prefer a list-based approach (similar to the EU and Canada), what use cases should we include? How can this list capture emerging uses of AI? The best way to describe high risk AI is via a list of consequential use cases. That is, the list should enumerate specific use cases where the AI model used is playing a major role in a consequential decision. This makes sure that there is very little unintended capture of low risk use cases while also making sure that legitimate high risk use cases are properly assessed. 20 ● If you prefer a principles-based approach, what should we address in guidance to give the greatest clarity? 4 Are there high-risk use cases that government should consider banning in its regulatory response (for example, where there is an unacceptable level of risk)? If so, how should we define these? N/A 5 Are the proposed principles flexible enough to capture new and emerging forms of high-risk AI, such as general-purpose AI (GPAI)? GPAI models are not by themselves ”forms of  high-risk AI”. Risk is context-dependent, and many of the risks associated with general purpose AI models will only become apparent and amenable to targeted mitigations once they are used for a defined purpose.  6 Should mandatory guardrails apply to all GPAI models? As currently written, mandatory guardrails are designed for AI systems. GPAI models, whether high- or low-risk, are not systems and should not be treated similarly. Further, any specific guardrails that relate to models should be applied only to those GPAI models that have passed the threshold of high risk. 7 What are suitable indicators for defining GPAI models as high-risk? For example, is it enough to define GPAI as high-risk against the principles, or should it be based on technical capability such as FLOPS (e.g. 10^25 or 10^26 threshold), advice from a scientific panel, government or other indicators? Only certain GPAI models are high risk, and the threshold for this should be based on the Seoul AI Frontier Commitments.  Guardrails ensuring testing, transparency and accountability of AI 8 Do the proposed mandatory guardrails appropriately mitigate the risks of AI used in high-risk settings? Are there any guardrails that we should add or remove? Please see our detailed comments on the proposed guardrails throughout this submission for our suggestions on ways in which they could be best adapted to appropriately mitigate the risks of AI in 21 high-risk settings.   9 How can the guardrails incorporate First Nations knowledge and cultural protocols to ensure AI systems are culturally appropriate and preserve ICIP? N/A      10 Do the proposed mandatory guardrails distribute responsibility across the AI supply chain and throughout the AI lifecycle appropriately? For example, are the requirements assigned to developers and deployers appropriate?  In order to comprehensively and effectively address misuse risk for general purpose AI models, the guardrails must sufficiently cover not only model developers, but all of the entities in the AI value chain. We refer the Government to the PAI Risk Mitigation Strategies for the Open Foundation Model Value Chain, which sets out the various actors: model providers (who train general purpose models that others build on to create AI systems for specific applications); downstream developers (who use open source models to build AI applications for their own use cases, often using their own data for fine-tuning the model); model deployers (who can be the same as the downstream developer); and end users (those who use the final AI application).   Due to technology's dynamic nature, as well as its potential risks and benefits, policy measures should focus on differentiating responsibilities across this value chain in order to provide legal and business clarity.   22 In this regard, the Government should prioritise standardising the threat models and evaluations along the AI value chain, and clarifying when and where risk assessments, and mitigations are most effectively applied.   11 Are the proposed mandatory guardrails sufficient to address the risks of GPAI? How could we adapt the guardrails for different GPAI models, for example low-risk and high-risk GPAI models? Meta believes that there are no ex-ante high-risk GPAI models. Models should be considered high-risk if they pass a threshold of high risk, and only then should model-specific guardrails be imposed. Low-risk GPAI models do not require guardrails. 12 Do you have suggestions for reducing the regulatory burden on small-to-medium sized businesses applying guardrails? The government should  facilitate partnerships in order to share knowledge, tools, and resources for being in compliance with the guardrails. For example, Meta has open sourced a leading suite of security and safety tools, which helps SMEs and startups take risk assessment measures appropriate for their use cases. Regulatory options to mandate guardrails 13 Which legislative option do you feel will best address the use of AI in high-risk settings? What opportunities should the government take into account in considering each approach?  Meta  believes that the best way forward would be Option 2 (a framework approach): Adapting existing regulatory frameworks through framework legislation. This Option, which recognises that Australia’s existing laws may be capable of giving effect, or can be amended to give effect to one or more of the proposed mandatory guardrails in individual regimes would take cognisance of the applicability of existing laws and regulations already to AI and look to address gaps by providing a consistent set of definitions, defining the guardrails to apply and establishing thresholds for when they would apply. This is the most 23 appropriate for achieving the policy outcomes being pursued - namely the safe and responsible use of AI in Australia in a manner that is aligned with emerging international consensus on AI governance.  14 Are there any additional limitations of options outlined in this section which the Australian Government should consider? N/A     15 Which regulatory option/s will best ensure that guardrails for high-risk AI can adapt and respond to step-changes in technology? For the reasons set out in response to Question 13, Meta  believes that the best way forward would be Option 2 (a framework approach). 16 Where do you see the greatest risks of gaps or inconsistencies with Australia’s existing laws for the development and deployment of AI? Which regulatory option best addresses this, and why? There are an increasing number of voluntary initiatives, domestic regulations, and international laws and principles emerging around AI, and generative AI in particular.  Given the increasing potential for regulatory fragmentation globally, it is important to ensure consistency and interoperability, particularly around definitions, taxonomies, risk identification, assessments, and mitigations. In this regard, the Government might usefully look to the US Executive Order on the Safe, Secure, and Trustworthy Development and Use of Artificial Intelligence, or the Seoul Frontier AI Safety Commitments. We also note the work of the EU-US Trade and Technology Council on taxonomies, the report of the UN High Level Advisory Board on AI, the UN 24 Resolutions on AI governance, and the G20 Leaders’ Declaration.    ","Meta_s_Submission_on_the_Proposals_Paper_for_mandatory_guardrails_for_AI_in_high_risk_settings.0cb85899","Meta_s_Submission_on_the_Proposals_Paper_for_mandatory_guardrails_for_AI_in_high_risk_settings.0cb85899.pdf","pdf","C:\\Users\\james.barro\\Documents\\rsm-nvidia-hackathon\\data\\Submission_Docs\\Processed\\Meta_s_Submission_on_the_Proposals_Paper_for_mandatory_guardrails_for_AI_in_high_risk_settings.0cb858