1. Do the proposed principles adequately capture high-risk AI?
No
Please provide any additional comments.
the risk categories need to provide specific guidance for High, Limited, Low risk with guidance for each. Without a prescriptive approach classifications will be compromised either wilfully or by lack of understanding. Furthermore, the risk categories need to be different for class of user. An organisation that creates and trains an LLM for public consumption has different risk attributes than an SME using a GenAI tool to retain the knowledge of its workers, or a customer service agent acting on corporate manuals.
Are there any principles we should add or remove?
No
Please identify any:
its not, in my opinion, that low-risk cases are unintentionally captured, or low-risk uses and users, its that the risk classifications need to be articulated differently for each class of stakeholder: Providers, Deployers, Users, Consumers, Importers, Distributors, Representatives, National Authorities (to use the EU AI Act framing). We need a matrix: X-Axis = Stakeholder, Y-Axis is AI Risk Classification .. and the intersecting cells incorporate the appropriate guardrail
2. Do you have any suggestions for how the principles could better capture harms to First Nations people, communities and Country?
No
3. Do the proposed principles, supported by examples, give enough clarity and certainty on high-risk AI settings and high-risk AI models? Is a more defined approach, with a list of illustrative uses, needed?
NO a more defined list-based approach is needed
If you prefer a list-based approach (similar to the EU and Canada), what use cases should we include? How can this list capture emerging uses of AI?
Biometrics Critical infrastructure Education/training Employment Access to essential public services and products Access to essential private services Products and services affecting individual and public health and safety Law enforcement Administration of justice and democratic processesOther
How can this list capture emerging uses of AI?
you still use the principle based approach for ""any other""
4. Are there high-risk use cases that government should consider banning in its regulatory response (for example, where there is an unacceptable level of risk)?
Yes
If so, how should we define these?
social scoring,
5. Are the proposed principles flexible enough to capture new and emerging forms of high-risk AI, such as general-purpose AI (GPAI)?
Yes
6. Should mandatory guardrails apply to all GPAI models?
Yes
Please provide any additional comments.
but again, it additionally needs another dimension of allocation of risk against stakeholder type
7. What are suitable indicators for defining GPAI models as high-risk?
Define high-risk against the principles
8. Do the proposed mandatory guardrails appropriately mitigate the risks of AI used in high-risk settings?
No
Please provide any additional comments.
the proposed risk settings are principle based, and rely on honesty. There have been many examples where principles are ignored, e.g. Cambridge Analytica, and more recently Meta Glasses with facial recognition without consent
Are there any guardrails that we should add or remove?
No
9. How can the guardrails incorporate First Nations knowledge and cultural protocols to ensure AI systems are culturally appropriate and preserve Indigenous Cultural and Intellectual Property?
all AI can be used for retention of knowledge, its a key use-case for LLM's in education and industry, e.g. a plumbing business using LLM's to store knowledge of experienced and ageing workforce to retain for junior staff
10. Do the proposed mandatory guardrails distribute responsibility across the AI supply chain and throughout the AI lifecycle appropriately?
No
Please provide any additional comments
they need to be applied with discretion based upon which stakeholder group is considering the risk/guardrail application ... the risk changes in the context of the stakeholder entity
11. Are the proposed mandatory guardrails sufficient to address the risks of GPAI?
No
How could we adapt the guardrails for different GPAI models, for example low-risk and high-risk GPAI models? 
not with the emergence of quantum computing
12. Do you have suggestions for reducing the regulatory burden on small-to-medium sized businesses applying guardrails?
Yes
Please provide any additional comments
templated compliance models
13. Which legislative option do you feel will best address the use of AI in high-risk settings?
A whole of economy approach – introducing a new cross-economy AI Act
What opportunities should the Government take into account in considering each approach? 
we are only on the leading edge of this change, the potential for abuse is overwhelming, and we will never be able to close the door after that horse has bolted.
14. Are there any additional limitations of options outlined in this section which the Australian Government should consider?
No
15. Which regulatory option(s) will best ensure that guardrails for high-risk AI can adapt and respond to step-changes in technology?
A whole of economy approach – introducing a new cross-economy AI Act
16. Where do you see the greatest risks of gaps and inconsistencies with Australia’s existing laws for the development and deployment of AI?
They allow too much interpretation and rely on the courts to resolve lack of clarity, this is time consuming and expensive and will leave open the opportunity for misuse by wilful actors, and there will be bad state actors as well as bad corporate and individual rogue actors. The potential for AI to be a malicious tool is too great to ignore.
Which regulatory option best addresses this?
A whole of economy approach – Introducing a new cross-economy AI-specific Act (for example, an Australian AI Act).
Please explain why.  
that said, there are specific domains with additional regulatory requirements, eg: TGA/Medical, Nuclear Reactors, Autonomous Vehicles, Aircraft control systems - for which more stringent domain specific controls are required over and above the Whole of Economy Prescriptive regulations
Make a general comment
The current voluntary standards leave too much room for wilful actors to manipulate risk classification and regulation. A prescriptive approach is required with consideration of Stakeholder Role as a modifier of the Risk/Regulation implementation of Guardrails. Furthermore, no mandatory standard is of any benefit without an enforcement capability with the legislated authority to act

