Atlassian’s Submission to the Australian Government in relation to Safe and Responsible AI in Australia: Proposals Paper  Department of Industry, Science and Resources aiconsultation@industry.gov.au  4 October 2024  We appreciate this opportunity to provide feedback to the Department on the further development of the appropriate regulatory framework to build trust and promote the adoption of AI, through mandatory guardrails on developing and deploying AI in Australia in high-risk settings, as set out in the Proposals Paper of September 2024 (the Proposals Paper). At Atlassian, we build enterprise software products to help teams collaborate, including for software development, project management and content management. As one of Australia’s most successful home-grown technology companies — and one that provides products and services (including AI-powered products and services) to customers around the world — we believe that we are in a unique position to contribute to this consultation. We know that we all have a role to play in fostering a thriving, trusted emerging technology ecosystem. We believe that the risk-based approach articulated in the Proposals Paper, incorporating both mandatory and voluntary guardrails, both reflects international practice around safe and responsible AI and takes account of Australia’s unique legal, economic and cultural environment. For this reason, Atlassian broadly supports the approach set out in the Proposals Paper. However, as the Proposals Paper notes, there remain open questions about the framework within which this approach will be situated. This extends not only to what framework should be implemented, but also how it should be achieved (and why) — and how this aligns to work planned or already underway. In this submission, we detail how our recommended model and framework aligns to and provides a pathway to implement the options set forth in the Proposals Paper. Our approach and proposed model Atlassian has been pleased to contribute to the Government’s Safe and Responsible AI in Australia consultation process. As previously recommended in our earlier submissions to that process, we believe that the best response to emerging technologies like AI is situated within an overarching, coordinated digital regulatory framework that is: ● governed by core principles, which may be enshrined in legislation and would set forth a consistent, scalable and risk-based framework for all stakeholders, and inform the formulation and implementation of specific measures and tools within that framework; ● supported through one or more central advisory and coordination bodies that are capable of providing advice and assisting with coordination and alignment (including by acting as a ‘clearinghouse’) across government agencies and regulators with responsibility across various sectors and areas of law, which would also allow government to build expertise and connections with industry; and ● bolstered by an appropriate mix of targeted and objective governance measures, guidance and tools (including regulatory measures where required), which respond  2 
clearly to identified issues in a manner that aligns to and has the benefit of the overarching principles and institutional expertise. In our view, this proposed model accounts for the multi-dimensional nature of many of the opportunities, risks and issues that can be raised by AI. It also allows the governance model to be imbued with Australian values and priorities in its overarching principles; while also retaining the flexibility to both interoperate with and take advantage of emerging international standards and best practice through its agile approach to different policy and regulatory responses. Of the options outlined in the Proposals Paper, Atlassian considers that the ‘framework approach’ in Option 2 aligns most closely to this model. By providing a unified set of principles and governance structures while still allowing for sector-specific adaptations, the framework approach has the capacity to strike a careful balance between: ● the domain-specific approach, which risks creating a fragmented regulatory landscape with inconsistent standards and enforcement mechanisms; and  ● the whole of economy approach, which may be too broad and inflexible to address the nuanced challenges posed by AI technologies across different sectors and use cases.  As the Proposals Paper suggests, we agree that the Regulatory Powers (Standard Provisions) Act 2014 (Cth) provides a helpful model for how such framework legislation could operate.  However, to be truly effective, we believe that any framework legislation needs to be accompanied by the other elements of the model we articulated above, to enable the framework to truly operate in a coordinated and standardised fashion.  Working from that model, we recommend that Option 2 be implemented with the following four key, interdependent elements: (1) enabling framework legislation, which establishes legal definitions, baseline obligations and a regulatory coordination mechanism; (2) a ‘clearinghouse’ or coordination mechanism, empowered to foster collaboration across the regulatory system and act as a central resource for agencies and regulators; (3) independent, expert oversight from the Government’s AI Advisory Body; and (4) clearly defined roles and responsibilities for existing regulators. Each of these elements is described further below and summarised in Figure 1. Figure 1: Proposed Atlassian model under Option 2 AI Regulatory Framework Act (AIRFA) ● Establishes legal definitions, baseline obligations and regulatory coordinating mechanism. ● Legislative instruments define (among other things) regulatory roles and responsibilities. AI Advisory Body ● Representative body (including industry, academia and civil society). ● Provides independent advice to Government and regulators on the operation of the framework. AI Regulatory Coordination Office (AIRCO) ● Develops centralised expertise in AI regulation. ● Administers coordination arrangements. ● Maintains legislative instrument to provide clear advice on regulators’ roles and responsibilities. ● Assumes active role on voluntary standards. Existing regulators (e.g. ACCC, OAIC, ASIC, eSafety, ACMA, APRA and others) ● Existing authorities clarified by AIRFA. ● Capacity to leverage the expertise and facilitation arrangements provided by AIRCO in guidance, enforcement and exercise of powers.  3 
1. Framework legislation This framework legislation should have a clear and defined purpose: it is designed to facilitate a high degree of consistency in terminology and concepts across the regulatory environment as it applies to AI, in order to ultimately build community trust and promote AI adoption in Australia.  The legislation will do so by establishing the underpinning legal definitions, baseline obligations and a regulatory coordination mechanism that will be relied upon — and implemented — across that regulatory environment. To maintain the capacity to be flexible and evolve over time, this is likely to require a mix of primary and subordinate legislation. For example: ● The mandatory guardrails themselves might be set forth in a separate schedule, which can be more easily reviewed, updated and expanded through supporting legislative instruments or regulatory guidance over time (for example, similar to the Australian Privacy Principles and the Australian Consumer Law). ● Matters that require more detailed and up-to-date consideration, consultation and guidance — such as the roles and responsibilities of, and intersections between, existing regulators — can similarly be set out in legislative instruments under the Act. 2. Clearinghouse Atlassian supports the establishment of a central ‘clearinghouse’ (or coordinating body) that could serve as a central repository for information on the guardrails, including best practices, guidelines and compliance resources. This body would also be responsible for:  ● fostering collaboration across the regulatory environment; and  ● developing, reviewing and updating legislative instruments under the framework Act. Unlike a new regulator, which would require significant resources and time to establish, a clearinghouse model could leverage existing regulatory structures and capacities and foster a collaborative, cross-sectoral approach to AI regulation. This will build on, and bring together, existing cooperative arrangements between these regulators.  In the interests of bringing together and ensuring consistency in the implementation of the Government’s Safe and Responsible AI approach, we also believe that this body would assume oversight of the National AI Centre’s work on voluntary standards and guardrails. 3. AI Advisory Body To support this framework, we also believe that the Government’s proposed AI Advisory Body can play a critical role in ensuring that this regulatory model is and remains able to adapt effectively to the evolving landscape. A standing body of external advisors with relevant expertise across industry, civil society and academia would provide government agencies and regulators (including via the clearinghouse) with ready, swift access to a deeper and wider bench of resources to bolster its own expertise and more readily respond to the fast-changing and technical environment where AI is often deployed. In addition, this body allows for a diverse set of views from outside of the public sector to be included in the Government’s ongoing decision-making on, and oversight of, this model, contributing to its ongoing effectiveness and continuous improvement. 4. Existing regulators Under this principles-based, framework legislation, underlying legislative instruments should help to define and guide the roles and responsibilities of both economy-wide and sector-specific regulators when it comes to the application of these principles in high-risk scenarios.  4 
Ideally, these instruments should be guided by an overarching intent to augment and defer to existing enforcement regimes and frameworks wherever possible, depending on the guardrail to which the relevant non-compliance relates. That is: ● Where a guardrail naturally relates to an existing legislative regime or framework, enforcement should adhere to the established remedies for breaches of that framework. For example: a breach of Guardrail 3 (relating to data governance) could tie into the existing penalties regime under the Privacy Act, while a breach of Guardrail 6 (relating to transparency to end-users) could tie into penalties under the Australian Consumer Law.  ● Where a guardrail does not squarely fit into existing law (for example, Guardrail 4 relating to testing), the framework should be implemented in a way that creates consistent mechanisms for implementing remedies in the regulatory framework operating closest to the anticipated harm.  These overarching guidelines will help to ensure that, once implemented, the roles and authorities of existing regulators are clearly established and understood. Individual regulators could then rely on the clearinghouse body to provide support and advice on the implementation of these guardrails — although several matters, including the manner of implementation (as well as appropriate resourcing across and between those regulators) may be less straightforward and will need to be resolved in the implementation process. Implementation of the model In our submission, we have focused on the overall approach and model, and how we believe that the Government’s options as set out in the Proposals Paper can be most effectively established. However, as the Proposals Paper also makes clear, there are several substantive considerations that remain to be confirmed with respect to the mandatory guardrails and the settings in which they will be applied. In our view, these considerations and concerns are in many cases tied to the overall model and framework within which they will be implemented. We believe that these considerations are best considered once that model and framework are confirmed, as this allows for a clearer and better understanding of how and why they are likely to work in practice. For example: ● As the Proposals Paper illustrates (in Figure 3), the AI supply chain is increasingly complex. The context-specific nature of AI technologies is such that the stakeholders in any given AI system will change over its lifecycle, and linear distinctions like ‘developer’ and ‘deployer’ risk oversimplifying this value chain. If not appropriately considered within the context of the overall regulatory and enforcement environment, this could lead to confusion and inefficiencies due to duplicative, overlapping or even unclear responsibilities depending on the proposed allocation of responsibility. ● The application and consideration of ‘risk’ is likely to require further clear and detailed guidance from relevant advisory bodies, agencies and regulators (as applicable), regardless of which approach is taken to defining what is ‘high-risk’ as outlined in the Proposals Paper. ● Similarly, and as noted above, both the content and manner of implementation and enforcement of the guardrails will require more detailed consideration, guidance and supporting legislative instruments. This can include consideration of relevant examples where Australian organisations are currently expected to demonstrate their compliance with other principles-based domestic regimes (like the taking of ‘reasonable steps’ under the Australian Privacy Principles or the following of relevant standards under the Security of Critical Infrastructure Act 2018 (Cth)’s Risk Management Program Rules). We also welcome the Government’s consideration of how the guardrails align with emerging risk-based framework and legislation elsewhere.  5 
 Atlassian would be pleased to further discuss these comments with the Department, and looks forward to working with the Australian Government to ensure that all Australians will benefit from our AI-powered future. Yours sincerely,   Anna Jaffe Director of Regulatory Affairs & Ethics Atlassian David Masters Head of Global Policy & Regulatory Affairs Atlassian   ","Atlassian_Safe_Responsible_AI_Proposals_Paper_Submission_04.10.2024_.9f1609c2","Atlassian_Safe_Responsible_AI_Proposals_Paper_Submission_04.10.2024_.9f1609c2.pdf","pdf","C:\\Users\\james.barro\\Documents\\rsm-nvidia-hackathon\\data\\Submission_Docs\\Processed\\Atlassian_Safe_Responsible_AI_Proposals_Paper_Submission_04.10.2024_.9f1609c2.pdf","1"
