OCT  
2024  
Consultation response  
DISR: Proposals paper for introducing mandatory 
guardrails for AI in high -risk settings  
Key comments  
The Pharmaceutical Society of Australia (PSA)  welcomes the opportunity to respond to the 
Australian Government ’s consultation on Proposals paper for introducing mandatory guardrails 
for AI in high -risk settings . PSA commends the government for undertaking this work, and for the 
broad consultation approach which has been taken.  
PSA recognises AI is a rapidly evolving area of technology , and its risks and impacts will continue 
to emerge over time. A key challenge for regulators, stakeholders and the Australian community 
is that the pace of AI development is currently outpacing our ability to comprehend, understand 
and use it effectively.  
About PSA  
PSA is the peak national professional pharmacy organisation representing all of Australia’s 
37,000 pharmacists working  in all sectors and across all locations.  
PSA is committed to supporting pharmacists in helping Australians to access quality, safe, 
equitable, efficient and effective health care. PSA believes the expertise of pharmacists can be 
better utilised to address the healthcare needs of all Australians.  
PSA works to identify, unlock and advance opportunities for pharmacists to realise their full 
potential, to be appropriately recognised and fairly remunerated.  
PSA has a strong and engaged membership base that provides high -quality health care and are 
the custodians for safe and effective medicine use for the Australian community.  
PSA leads and supports innovative and evidence -based healthcare service delivery by 
pharmacists. PSA provides high -quality practitioner development and practice support to 
pharmacists and is the custodian of the professional practice standards and guidelines to ensure 
quality and integrity in the practice of pharmacy.  
PSA has been a leader in the pharmacy -sector in Australia through incorporation of AI into our 
professional  development and training products for pharmacists, particularly in relation to more 
sophisticated and flexible assessment tools for online learning.  
  
© Pharmaceutical Society of Australia Ltd. I  2 Response to questions  
1. Do the proposed principles adequately capture high -risk AI? Are there any principles 
we should add or remove?  
Please identify any:  
▪ low-risk use cases that are unintentionally captured  
▪ categories of uses that should be treated separately, such as uses for defence 
or national security purposes.  
Health and wellbeing are fundamental to an individual’s ability to li ve a full and meaningful life. It 
is deeply personal, individual and complex. PSA strongly supports the inclusion of Principle (b) 
the risk of adverse impacts to an individual’s physical of mental health or safety.  
PSA considers that it is difficult to stratify risk of AI in the context of what aspects of health care 
should have guardrails apply to use of AI. A starting point that all use of AI in health care may 
potentially be high risk should be adopted as a precautionary approach.  This could apply even to 
seemingly very simple healthcare interventions.  
We note many of the applications of AI in health  care fall outside of the jurisdiction of the 
Therapeutic Goods Administration, whos e primary oversight with regards to AI is its use in 
therapeutic devices.  This may include, for example, dictation tools which can create medical 
notes based on audio recordings of patient consultations, or use of AI to translate health 
information to patients.  
It is crucial that there is effective regulatory oversight of these applications to manage risks such 
as privacy, risk of misinterpretation of data or inadequate consideration of all of an individual ’s 
circumstances by AI when producing clinical records.  
2. Do you have any suggestions for how the principles could better capture harms to 
First Nations people, communities and Country?  
PSA does not have specific suggestions in relation to this question. We note that  AI learns from 
large data sets, and First Nations peoples, migrants, and refugees are often small populations  
which exist within a larger dataset . There is a real risk that AI trained on large populations has 
insights into the health patterns of the prevailing dominant cultures. That is, AI could repeat the 
problems of “anglicised” healthcare which isn’t immediately suitable for other cultural groups.  
The response to this question will require effective engagement with First Nations people , as well 
as AI ethicists/technicians who can provide guidance on how to minimise this risk of  harm from 
homogenising bias . 
3. Do the proposed principles, supported by examples, give enough clarity and 
certainty on high -risk AI settings and high -risk AI models? Is a more defined 
approach, with a list of illustrative uses, needed?  
▪ If you prefer a list -based approach (similar to the EU and Canada), what use 
cases should we include? How can this list capture emerging uses of AI?   
© Pharmaceutical Society of Australia Ltd. I  3 ▪ If you prefer a principles -based approach, what should we address in guidance 
to give the greatest clarity?  
Generally speaking, PSA considers risk -based approaches to regulation to be more effective and 
enduring than regulation based on defined lists. However, we do not have specific experience in 
artificial intelligence which allows a conclusive response to this question. Given the global nature 
of many products which harness the power of AI, we note that it would be preferable to minimise 
the degree of variation in regulatory approaches of different countries.  
4. Are there high -risk use cases that government should consider banning in its 
regulatory response (for example, where there is an unacceptable level of risk)? If 
so, how should we define these?  
PSA has not identified high -risk use cases in this consultation which would warrant a specific ban. 
However, we recognise that the use of AI in supporting human health contains substantial quality 
and safety risks, for which an effective level of oversight or management is necessary.  
PSA considers the  privacy and consent for the training  of AI requires close consideration. 
Consent for use of personal data to train AI systems should be firsthand  and not covered under 
broad consent agreements . For instance, a person providing consent to a healthcare professional 
(HCP ) that their data can be shared “when judged necessary by that HCP” should not include the 
ability to share that data with an AI learner. Any use of data for AI training should be explicitly 
consented.  In contrast, c onsent for use of an AI tool by an HCP is less of a concern since  the 
HCP applying the use of that AI has the capacity to seek specific consent.  
Many of the strongest risks associated with AI may not yet be foreseen. Where unacceptable 
risks are identified in future, the regulatory approach established needs to be agile enough to 
apply oversight and intervene if necessary  in a timely manner . 
5. Are the proposed principles flexible enough to capture new and emerging forms of 
high -risk AI, such as general -purpose AI (GPAI)?  
PSA has no specific comment on this question.  
6. Should mandatory guardrails apply to all GPAI models?  
PSA has no specific comment on this question.  
7. What are suitable indicators for defining GPAI models as high -risk? For example, is 
it enough to define GPAI as high -risk against the principles, or should it be based on 
technical capability such as FLOPS (e.g. 10^25 or 10^26 threshold), advice from a 
scientific panel, government or other indicators?  
PSA has no specific comment on this question.  
8. Do the proposed mandatory guardrails appropriately mitigate the risks of AI used in 
high -risk settings? Are there any guardrails that we should add or remove?  
While at first reading the guardrails sounds reasonable and should be effective, the limited 
experience health professionals have with AI makes it difficult to comment on the  likely efficacy or 
completeness of the guardrails. PSA supports the establishment of these guardrails and 
recommends periodic review to ensure they address emerging issues and trends.   
© Pharmaceutical Society of Australia Ltd. I  4 9. How can the guardrails incorporate First Nations knowledge and cultural protocols 
