 Through this  AI development , Australians have remained protected by our  framework of existing, technology -neutral 
regulations , such as consumer law , privacy protections and financial system  regulation.  
Australia must learn from the experiences of other countries to ensure our regulatory approach is best practice , 
internationally interoperable  and in line with consensus -based, internationally recognised technical standards . 
The BCA supports a regulatory approach  to address genuine , high risks arising from  the increased use and capability of 
AI. But  the role our existing regulations play —and where they may be falling short —must be made clear.  Australia 
must be deliberate about who in the AI value chain would be regulated and why.  
Most importantly, we must avoid regulation that undermines Australian businesses’ ability to safely and responsibly 
develop or deploy AI systems  and thus stifles the growth of the digital economy in Australia .  
  Proposals paper: Intro ducing mandatory guardrails for AI in high -risk settings , BCA submission  4  
   
 2. Key recommendations  
1. Assess existing regulation .  
a. Undertake a detailed review of the applicability of current regulation as a first step.  
b. Work with the current regulators as part of this process . 
c. Provide centralised guidance and coordination to avoid potential fragmentation across different 
sectors.  
d. Lift the capacity and capability of the current regulators to apply existing legislation regarding  AI. 
e. Raise awareness and education of the protections that do exist, particularly for consumers.  
f. Educate business about the AI guardrails for voluntary adoption . 
g. Assess the appropriateness of the guardrails and if there are any areas where they are not fit for 
purpose, ahead of a mandatory application.  
h. Identify any potential gaps in existing legislation and assess whether  a legislative amendment would 
be appropriate . 
2. Review international experiences.  
a. Identify lessons learnt from the international experience, particularly from the current negative 
responses coming from the recent E uropean Union (E U) Act, the challenges with the Canadian reform 
and developments in other countries such as the US and the UK.   
b. Consider approaches taken by our major trading partners, including Japan and New Zealand, to avoid a major divergence and  significant business costs . 
c. Consider how Australia ’s approach to safe and responsible AI could achieve an economic advantage in 
AI and attract tech investment.  
d. Australia to continue to be strongly engaged in the global collaboration stemming from the Bletchley 
Declaration and use this to inform the next steps in domestic regulation or  legislation . 
e. Engage with the international AI Safety Institutes to ensure Australia is aware of emerging developments . 
f. Focus on identifying how an Australian system will be  globally interoperable, including potentially 
recognising other systems as being deemed to meet Australia’s standards.  
3. If a comprehensive review of existing legislation finds that additional regulation is appropriate  and 
there is a decision to proceed with the proposed Option 2, the following would be required : 
a. The principles are more refined, as they are currently too broad, particularly p rinciple (e).  
b. Regulation is targeted at  situations where AI is high risk  and where risks are not mitigated through 
existing legislation . 
c. Clarify the definitions of a developer and a deployer, with consideration given to a greater focus on the 
roles and potential outcomes from their respective actions in the development and use of AI , and who 
else should be considered through the supply chain . 
d. Liability and accountability are clearly assigned . 
e. All General Purpose AI nor “advanced, highly capable GPAI models”  should not be categorised as high -
risk.  
f. The broad definition of General Purpose AI is not supported, and it should be narrowed.  
g. Promote clarity  of compliance by having existing regulators apply  consisten t terminology  and 
common, internationally aligned , principles -based guardrails, such as those compliant with ISO/ IEC. 
 
4. Do not proceed with Option 3 .  Proposals paper: Intro ducing mandatory guardrails for AI in high -risk settings , BCA submission  5  
   
 a. Understand the current legislation and protections , the evolution of the technology  and learn from the 
international approaches,  prior to consideration of an overarching AI Act. 
  Proposals paper: Intro ducing mandatory guardrails for AI in high -risk settings , BCA submission  6  
   
 3. An appropriate way forward  
3.1 Recommendation 1: Assess existing regulation 
3.1.1 Improv e guidance on existing laws  
Clarification and review of existing regulation must be government’s first steps.  
Government should not apply additional regulation to AI developers and deployers before sufficiently assessing the 
coverage of our existing body of tech -neutral, fit -for-purpose regulation. Government should firstly clarify for 
consumers, businesses and corporate leaders how existing regulation applies to AI . It should undertake a 
comprehensive review of the current legal requirements and  be informed by a central assessment framework that gives 
overarching clarity around intent . 
This will give industry  across Australia and internationally the confidence to invest in AI capabilities in Australia, and 
contribute to developing the digital economy, while giving industry  certainty they are meeting the necessary regulatory 
obligations. It will also give government clear evidence to determine where additional regulation targeting high risk AI 
use cases may be required.  
Many of the potential risks posed by AI are covered by existing technology neutral regulato ry frameworks . Existing 
obligations relating to the design, development and use of AI apply through overarching directors’ duties, as well as 
laws such as privacy laws , data -security obligations, the  Australian Consumer Law, anti -discrimination laws, work, 
health and safety laws, and more.6  
Existing laws relating to specific sectors will also apply  to the development and deployment of AI  within that sector , 
such as through the regulation of medical device software under the Therapeutic Goods Administration  (TGA).7 
80 per cent of the problem is about applying and enforcing existing laws more effectively. We also need to 
modernise our laws. So about 20 per cent of the problem is, for  example, modernising privacy legislation.8 
Professor Santow, Human Technology Institute, UTS  
Applying blanket , new obligations without clarifying existing regulation risks creating a framework of overlapping and 
potentially conflicting requirements . This  has been seen in the EU  where a lack of alignment with existing legislation 
means full compliance with EU law has becom e impossible . For example, compliance with the General Data Protection 
Regulation ( GDPR ) data -sharing obligation under Article 6(10) directly conflicts with compliance with the Digital 
Markets Act’s (DMA) data minimis ation principle .9 
Businesses will need to focus on untangling their compliance obligations, rather than protecting their users and 
customers. This will be particularly detrimental to small businesses that seek to make the most of Australia’s AI 
opportunity but lack the legal resources and financial uplift to navigate  overly  complex, potentially uncertain  
obligations.  A number of larger businesses have already made the decision to pull their services from the EU due to 
their inability to comply with the EU Act—small businesses  do not stand a chance . 
We are already seeing a range of changes being progressed following legislative review processes, including to the 
Privacy Act  1988, the Broadcasting Services Act 1992 , the Criminal Code Act 1995, Competition and Consumer Act 2010  
and the copyright system. These processes will continue to address regulatory gaps without imposing blanket requirements.  The BCA supports the intent of these processes , but they could be made more effective by ensuring they 
adhere to a cross -cutting framework of stated objectives.  
3.1.2 An ex ante  approach   
The BCA acknowledges that where there are identified regulation gaps  and high risks of harm,  additional legislation  
may be justified.   
 
