7 Therapeutic Good Administration, 2024, ‘Artificial Intelligence (AI) and medical device software’, https://www.tga.gov.au/how- we-
regulate/manufacturing/manufacture- medical -device/manufacture -specific -types -medical -devices/artificial -intelligence -ai-and-medical -device -
software   
8 Select Committee On Adopting Artificial Intelligence (AI) , 21 May 2024 , Official Committee Hansard , pp. 22.  
9 Wilson Sonsini, 2024 , ‘EU Agencies to Develop Guidance on the Interplay Between the DMA and GDPR ’, https://www.wsgr.com/en/insights/eu -
agencies -to-develop -guidance -on-the-interplay -between -the-dma -and-gdpr.html  Proposals paper: Intro ducing mandatory guardrails for AI in high -risk settings , BCA submission  7  
   
 However, i mposing ex ante  regulatory obligations on a wide range of AI developers or deployers ‘just in case’ a nd 
purely on the basis that they are developing  or deploying a potentially ‘high risk’ system will creat e overlapping and 
conflicting legislation. There must be a clear rationale for what obligations are considered, why they are necessary on 
top of existing regulation, and how they can be implemented effectively.  As already stated, the evidence base has not 
yet been established for this approach  as the assessment of th e current regulatory system has not been undertaken.  
The A ustralian Competition and Consumer Commission ( ACCC ) has previously strongly advocated for ex ante 
regulations of digital platform markets , arguing they will reduce harms to consumers and markets, while remaining 
flexible and adaptive.10 However, the early feedback from complying with the EU’s DMA and the UK’s Digital Markets 
Consumer & Competition Act, for example, suggests the opposite may occur. There is evidence to suggest that poorly 
designed and executed ex ante regulation can stifle innovation and productivity, leading to a decline in competitiveness of the regulated sector .
11  
Although ex ante regulation is seen by some governments as a solution to fast- moving sectors such as app stores or 
social media, a rush to design and implement ex  ante regulation would mean failing to understand the market, the 
players and the dynamics within, instead relying on mere speculation. This would discourage innovation, investment 
and hamper  the enormous productivity gains available to the Australia n economy through the adoption of AI 
technology.12  
An ex ante approach risks a two -tiered system of new legal standards, that set a different standard for where AI is used, 
than is currently applied under existing legislation. This would create enormous confusion, complexity and inconsistencies in standards. Potential c osts and benefits of any proposed ex ante regulation should be carefully 
analysed and consulted.  
Government must do more to justify an ex  ante  approach  before it takes this action , especially given its clear 
shortcomings in recent international examples of digital platform markets regulation .  
3.2 Recommendation 2: Review international experiences  
3.2.1 EU and Canada  
The Proposals Paper  has drawn heavily from the approaches being taken forward in the EU and Canada, both of which 
are adopting distinct ‘AI Acts’. Globally, we are far from certain thi s approach is best practice . 
Issues are already emerging in the EU mere months after its development, including : 
 the definition of high -risk AI capturing a far broader range of use cases than originally intended  
 legal uncertainty resulting in the systems becoming too complex and vague , with conflicting approaches being 
taken by different regulators  
 ongoing examples of businesses pulling out due to regulatory uncertainty and their inability to comply with the 
onerous obligations , and  
 a general lack of AI skills among regulators to properly understand and enforce the Act, exacerbated by market competition for skilled individuals.  
The European Commission’s recent report on EU competitiveness identified complex and onerous regulatory barriers 
for businesses operating in the tech sector , caused by the AI Act : 
“…while the ambitions of the EU’s GDPR and AI Act are commendable, their complexity and risk of overlaps 
and inconsistencies can undermine developments in the field of AI by EU industry actors.  The differences 
among Member States in the implementation and enforcement of the GDPR  […] as well as overlaps and areas 
of potential inconsistency with the provisions of the AI Act create the risk of European companies being 
excluded from early AI innovations because of uncertainty of regulatory frameworks as well as higher 
burdens for EU resea rchers and innovators to develop homegrown AI.  […] This calls for developing simplified 
 
