11 European Centre for International Political Economy, Economic Costs of Ex Ante Regulations , ECIPE Occasional Paper, 07/2020 
https://ecipe.org/wp -content/uploads/2020/10/ECI_20_OccPaper_07_2020_ Ex ante _Regulations_LY06.pdf  
12 CPI Columns, 2022, ‘The Ten Principles of Ex Ante Competition Regulation’, pp.3. https://www.competitionpolicyinternational.com/wp -
content/uploads/2022/11/OECD -Column -October -2022 -2-Full.pdf   Proposals paper: Intro ducing mandatory guardrails for AI in high -risk settings , BCA submission  8  
   
 rules and enforcing harmonised implementation of the GDPR in the Member States, while removing 
regulatory overlaps with the AI Act. ”13  
The EU experience has shown that charging ahead with blanket new obligations (applied through either existing or new 
regulation) is a mistake .  
3.2.2 United Kingdom   
In the UK, t he Sunak Government’s approach of relying on existing regulation  is a good example of an alternate model. 
The UK has consistently taken a pro -innovation approach to AI. Instead of rushing into rigid regulations, the 
government seeks to foster innovation and economic growth. This is reflected in its desire to create a flexible 
regulatory framework that balances  innovation with responsible oversight.  
The 2023 UK AI White Paper emphasised the goal of making the UK an attractive environment for AI  development 
while ensuring public trust.  Rather than establishing a single overarching AI regulator, the UK has opted for a 
decentrali sed approach, assigning different regulators across sectors ( for example , health, financial services, data 
protection) the responsibility to develop AI -specific guidelines for their respective areas. This enables domain -specific 
expertise to shape how AI is governed across industries. Regulators like the Information Commissione r’s Office (ICO) 
and the Financial Conduct Authority (FCA) play pivotal roles in guiding the use of AI in their sectors.   
Since July 2024, the new Starmer Government has sharply turned to an  intention to introduce a  stricter  ""overarching 
regulatory framework"" for AI , although a bill is yet to be introduced.  
3.2.3 New Zealand  
New Zealand is not rushing to impose rigid, one -size -fits-all rules ; it has  taken a different path —a path rooted in 
flexibility and trust.  Rather than create entirely new laws, New Zealand is building on the foundation that  is already in 
place. Laws like the Privacy Act of 2020, the Human Rights Act, and the Fair Trading Act were not written specifically 
for AI, but they  are broad and adaptable enough to cover the emerging realities of this new era.  
This approach is  not about avoiding regulation. It ensur es that regulation evolves  and strik es a balance, allowing 
businesses to innovate  and experiment, while also protecting the rights and privacy of individuals. New Zealand is  
doing this not with sweeping, pre -emptive measures, but by addressing issues as they arise, through voluntary 
guidelines, industry standards, and technical safeguards.   
3.2.4 United States  
The US  does not have federal AI regulation , with different states pursuing different approaches , including Colorado's 
protections for consumers from algorithmic discrimination in consequential decisions made by high -risk artificial 
intelligence systems  through the Colorado Anti -Discrimination in AI Law , or California’s proposed wide -reaching  
requirements  on AI developers through the Safe and Secure Innovation for Frontier Artificial Intelligence Models Act (SB 
1047) .  
3.2.5 Australia’s opportunity  
We understand that the Australian G overnment is reviewing the international examples and engaging with 
international partners on their regulatory approaches.  Australia’s signing of the Bletchley Declaration is supported, and 
ongoing engagement in the global forum is essential .  
This provides A ustralia with the opportunity  to learn from  the implementation of regulation in other jurisdictions. We 
should study  these experiences, both good and bad, to determin e a way forward . Australia wants AI regulation that 
aligns and works globally , to support the growth of Australia’s digital economy and harness the opportunities and 
productivity gains AI technology brings , while also minimising disruption to our domestic industries .  
It will therefore be important to take the necessary time to contribute to the development of the global landscape and  
to be aware of emerging issues.  This does not mean that Australia stands still.  AI is a global development,  and Australia 
must be part of the global process and cooperation.  Government should play an active role in shaping international 
standards relevant to AI development and deployment. Standards reduce regulatory fragmentation and aid 
 
