 interoperability. They are also more agile than  regulations and can be adapted quickly, and for consumers they can 
enhance trust.   This is why we support the promotion of the ISO’s AI Management System . 
3.3 Recommendation 3: Regulatory options   
3.3.1 Summary  
The BCA takes the following positions on the three key areas:  
 Defining high -risk AI : 
- The BCA supports a principles -based  approach, however the principles as currently set out in the 
Proposal Paper  need clarification and refinement as they are too broad.  
- A ‘hybrid’ approach  should be adopted,  that uses a list of impactful  AI uses to filter  a narrow er 
selection of AI use cases into requiring a principles -based assessment . 
- The BCA recommends inclusion of definition s and metrics to guide principles -based risk assessments.  
- The BCA recommends that the risk assessment process acknowledges  that risk s may  be sufficiently  
reduced through existing regulatory requirements  and not require supplementary  obligations  to 
reduce any residual risk.  
- The BCA does not support categorising all GPAI nor “advanced, highly capable GPAI models” as high -
risk.  
 Guardrails ensuring testing, transparency and accountability of AI : 
- The BCA recommends that greater specificity is required in allocating  obligations between developers 
and deployers , and that  direct obligations should be  applied to both deployers and developers for 
actions within their control . 
- The BCA does not support all the proposed mandatory guardrails as they are currently worded .  
- The BCA recommends that before the guardrails are made mandatory, they are deployed for a time to 
test if they are effective , make improvements  and develop appropriate guidance.  
 Regulatory options to mandate guardrails :  
- The BCA supports Option 1, to test the guardrails before they are mandatory . 
- If Option 2  to make the guardrails mandatory proceeds, there will need to be significant changes to 
enable the system to be implemented.  
3.3.2 Defining high -risk AI  
The BCA supports a risk -based approach  to AI regulation , but we do not support all the proposed principles as outline d. 
The BCA believes that the approach is too broad,  particularly principle (e), which  does not give enough clarity and 
certainty in assessing AI use cases as high -risk , and will introduce significant compliance costs that will dissuade use .  
The BCA recommends a ‘hybrid’ approach that  filters in potentially high -risk  use cases through a list of impactful  
purposes , before requiring a principles -based  assessment  of actual risk using clear metrics . The BCA is also supportive 
of explicitly prohibiting certain uses of AI, such as through the Criminal Code Amendment (Deepfake Sexual Material) Act 2024.  
The BCA recommends that deployers should only be required to undertake a principles -based risk assessment where 
they intend to use the AI system for a purpose  that has been listed as being potentially impactful . There should be clear 
guidance which  should include specific use cases and avoid broad sector categorisation where possible ; for example,  
‘health’ is too broad a category.  
 The domain areas provided in Table 1 of the Proposals Paper  are a reasonable starting point from which to 
scope high -risk AI uses. However, regulation must more tightly define  use cases within these categories to  be 
clear that not every use of AI within a domain area would be high risk . For example, “using AI within law 
enforcement to make a decision which risks having a legal, financial or other significant negative impact on individuals ” would likely warrant a risk assessment , while “using AI to draft an email” would not.   Proposals paper: Intro ducing mandatory guardrails for AI in high -risk settings , BCA submission  10  
   
  Inclusion on this list should not mean that high er order obligations will always be necessary for the use case, 
just that an assessment of residual risk is required.  
 Government should have the ability to expand the categories of ‘high -risk ’ over time where a risk assessment 
demonstrates a harm that needs to be addressed . 
 Government should streamline the process for business by articulat ing and including c lear ‘offramps ’ for when 
a risk assessment is not required.   
The BCA recommends  that deployers  are best placed to undertake an assessment of  the risk  that their use of an AI 
system will pose . Deployers will best understand their intended use of a system and the accompanying risks associated.  
Developers  should be encouraged to  provide any necessary information to deployers  undertaking a risk assessment, 
rather than undertaking assessments themselves.  
The best compliance mechanism for developers is for them to document their internal efforts to evaluate and mitigate 
potential risks and provide sufficient, appropriate documentation to their customers. This documentation should 
provide necessary informati on for customers to make informed decisions about how to deploy the system in a 
responsible way . 
The kinds of information that a developer will be able to provide will vary between narrow and GP AI systems. 
Developers of narrow AI systems can likely assess the risk their use of a system may introduce and  provide specific 
details through to deployers.  
However, d evelopers of GP AI models mostly lack visibility and control of how their systems will be used. It is not 
appropriate to expect them to anticipate and provide specific information . Instead, they could provide more general 
information.  
3.3.3 More clearly defining risk  
The BCA recommends that it be made clear that a risk refers to  a specific possible event, where the risk magnitude is 
calculated as  event severity  by event likelihood . 
Following this, the BCA recommends that the scale and measures for s everity and likelihood are provided , as illustrated 
below in Figure 1.  
 
Figure 1: Example generic risk matrix  
This provides a much clearer depiction of how to assess the risk. If a five -point risk matrix like in Figure 1 is used, the 
framework then must state that any risk that is assessed as, for example, ‘high’ or ‘extreme’ means the AI is classified as 
‘high -risk’.  
Proposals paper: Intro ducing mandatory guardrails for AI in high -risk settings , BCA submission  11  
   
 An assessment of risk must then also include whether the potential harm can be mitigated through additional actions. 
This assessment should also consider the risk inherent in performing the same activity without using AI, to ensure 
processes that use AI are not  unfairly disadvantaged.  
The NSW Government has published an AI Assessment Framework ,14 including risk levels, which may be helpful in 
fleshing out the Proposal Paper’s p rinciple (f): ‘The severity and extent of those adverse impacts outlined in principles 
(a) to (e) above ’ (see Figure 2 below).  
 
Figure 2: NSW Government’s AI risk levels  
An AI system’s risks will depend on the deployment context. These contexts often have significant protections under existing legislation. For example, the ongoing applicability of Australia’s workplace relations and antidiscrimination 
laws to deployers of AI , which was discussed during the Senate Committee Inquiry into the Digital Transformation of 
Workplaces .
15  
Therefore, t he BCA recommends that existing obligations should also be considered when assessing if an AI system 
warrants additional obligations. That is, a risk test should consider the residual risk an AI system poses where its 
deployer is already compliant with all requirements under existing legislation within its deployment context. This will 
limit the likelihood of unnecessary, overlapping or conflicting requirements being applied to AI developers and 
deployers.  Regulators should also create guidance on how existing regulation is to be applied to AI use cases  to inform 
risk assessments.  
The BCA is not supportive of p rinciple  (e): The risk of adverse impacts to the broader Australian economy, society, 
environment and rule of law.  As worded, this principle is inappropriately  broad and should be narrowed.  
The BCA also recommends that protections be put in place for employees and organisations that undertake good faith 
assessments of whether their use of an AI system will be ‘high risk’  yet find the regulator or others disagreeing with 
their assessment. This will help prevent AI use being stymied while waiting for sufficient precedents to be set. 
3.3.4 General Purpose AI  
The BCA does not support categorising all GPAI models or all “advanced, highly capable GPAI models” as high -risk. This 
is a flawed approach that mistakenly regulates a general- purpose  technology itself, rather than how that technology is 
used and applied in different contexts. This approach fails to acknowledge that developers cannot control how their AI 
technology is used downstream, much like engine or computer manufacturers cannot  guarantee ethical usage of their 
products.  
 
14 NSW Government, 2024, ‘NSW Artificial Intelligence Assessment Framework ’, https://www.digital.nsw.gov.au/policy/artificial -intelligence/nsw -
artificial -intelligence -assessment -framework#anchor- nsw -ai-assessment -framework  
15 House Standing Committee on Employment, Education and Training, 2024, ‘ Inquiry into the Digital Transformation of Workplaces ’, 
https://www.aph.gov.au/Parliamentary_Business/Committees/House/Employment_Education_and_Training/DigitalTransformation  
Proposals paper: Intro ducing mandatory guardrails for AI in high -risk settings , BCA submission  12  
   
 Instead, regulation should focus on specific harmful uses of AI, as seen with recent laws addressing deepfake 
pornography.  
The BCA does not support aligning the definition of GPAI  with that used in Canada’s Ar tificial Intelligence and Data Act  
(AIDA). This definition is inappropriately broad and risks capturing too wide a range of AI models  and, by extension, 
systems  and use cases .  
The BCA recommends adding an assessment of GPAI risk to properly identify high risk GPAI applications or use -cases , 
but the BCA is not supportive of replicating the US or EU models of basing risk categorisation on technical capability 
such as FLOP s (e.g. 10^25 or 10^26 threshold s). Technical measures (like FLOP s) are used to gauge the power and 
potential of AI models, but more FLOPs does not  necessarily mean more risk . Just because a model demands high 
computational resources does not mean it is high -risk.  
Some of the most beneficial advancements —like breakthroughs in scientific research or life- saving medical 
innovations —require tremendous computing power. If we regulate AI solely based on infrastructure or computational 
thresholds, we risk missing what tru ly matters: how that AI is being used.  
So, the real challenge is not in the raw power of AI but in how we apply it. Instead of relying on arbitrary metrics, regulations should focus on the specific applications and potential misuse of these technologies. We need to understand 
AI’s purpose and i ntent, not just its power.  
The BCA recommends that the risk of GPAI models be dependent on demonstrated capability.  This could include 
through reference to benchmark tests and evaluations . An Australian AI Safety Institute could have a role informing 
risk assessments for GPAI use -cases.  Australia shoul d continue to work with international counterparts to recognise 
measures of capability  as they emerge, and to promote a harmonised global approach.  Ensuring interoperability of 
requirements facing GPAI model developers  will be particularly important, noting that many major developers  are 
providing services globally  and will be reticent to implement new or bespoke processes to access the Australian 
market . 
3.3.5 Guardrails ensuring testing, transparency and accountability of AI  
The BCA does not support all the proposed mandatory guardrails  as they are currently worded . The ambiguous 
requirements would burden developers  and deployers , particularly open -source AI, and undermine  safety research, 
innovation, and competition.  
To address these shortcomings , additional detail is required on to who m in an AI value chain each specific  obligation  
will apply , and what actions would be required to meet these obligations.  This refinement should also ensure all 
guardrails align to ISO/SE 42001 (many already do). This ISO standard sets out guidelines for managing AI systems 
within organisations and  promotes responsible AI.   
To promote international  interoperability, the government should recognise adherence with certain international 
certifications or complia nce with  a limited list of  other international jurisdiction s (such as the EU) as  alternatives to 
meeting the Guardrails.  This would ensure that Australians  are offered appropriate protections, without requiring 
businesses to undergo duplicative assurance processes  or implement parallel processes . The government should also  
make efforts to work with other countries to make such arrangements reciprocal, improving the ease with which 
Australian AI systems can be exported . 
The BCA recommends  that before the guardrails are made mandatory , they are deployed for a time to test if they are 
effective , to make improvements , and to develop guidance .  
Specific comments on each guardrail are below:  
1. Establish, implement and publish an accountability process including governance, internal capability 
and a strategy for regulatory compliance  
a. The BCA does not support this guardrail  and recommends it be subsumed into guardrail 2. 
2. Establish and implement a risk management process to identify and mitigate risks  
a. The BCA supports this guardrail , and recommends that it be guided by independent  standards, such as 
ISO/ISE 42001 , to promote industry best practice and international interoperability . 
b. The BCA recommends that the risk mitigation element be integrated with the risk matrix 
implementation. This means that after firstly being categorised against the list as high -risk, and then Proposals paper: Intro ducing mandatory guardrails for AI in high -risk settings , BCA submission  13  
   
 an assessment of high -risk  against the principles , sufficient mitigations can lower the risk, and 
therefore not be subject to regulation . 
3. Protect AI systems, and implement data governance measures to manage data quality and provenance  
a. The BCA supports this guardrail , however g uidance will be required to ensure that developers and 
deployers understan d how this will apply to all participants . 
4. Test AI models and systems to evaluate model performance and monitor the system once deployed  
a. The BCA is supportive of this guardrail , and that it be conducted against an external, internationally 
recognised framework.  
5. Enable human control or intervention in an AI system to achieve meaningful human oversight  
a. The BCA is not supportive of this guardrail . As worded, this guardrail  will not be implementable in 
advanced AI systems  and open -source systems . Making an AI system work at human speed and 
comprehensibility would negate the advantages of AI . 
6.  Inform end -users regarding AI -enabled decisions, interactions with AI and AI -generated content  
a. The BCA is supportive of this guardrail . However, this must not impose requirements that are likely to 
lead to notification fatigue  or create unworkable user experiences . A threshold will need to be set.  
7. Establish processes for people impacted by AI systems to challenge use or outcomes  
a. The BCA is generally supportive of this guardrail  for AI uses with high severity , however  in the 
interest s of fairness and impartiality,  complaints should be handled externally from organisations .  
b. The BCA recommends that the preferred model be the existing Privacy Act , which recognises several 
external dispute resolution (EDR) schemes , such as the Australian Financial Complaints Authority.    
8. Be transparent with other organisations across the AI supply chain about data, models and systems to 
help them effectively address risks  
a. The BCA supports this guardrail , particularly that developers give deployers relevant information 
about the AI system s designed to be used in high -risk contexts . However, the BCA is concerned about 
commercially sensitive information or trade secrets being included. This should include explicit 
protections against the need to share such information.  
9. Keep and maintain records to allow third parties to assess compliance with guardrails  
a. The BCA is generally supportive of this guardrail .  
b. However, we are concern ed with the formulation of the proposed requirement that any organisation 
training large state- of-the-art GPAI models with potentially dangerous emergent capabilities must 
disclose these 'training runs' to the Australian Government.  Although the BCA supports this in 
principle, much clearer detail is needed on what constitutes ‘ potentially dangerous emergent 
capabilities .’ The BCA recommends that rather than introducing a new and entirely subjective term of 
‘potentially dangerous emergent capabilities’ , this be conducted against the same standardised risk 
matrix assessment as outlined above.  
c. Requirements to maintain records should be balanced and time -bound to manage costs  to industry 
and to mitigate against privacy and security risks.  
10. Undertake conformity assessments to demonstrate and certify compliance with the guardrails  
a. The BCA does not support this guardrail . This would be an enormous compliance burden on both 
industry and government. Even if a compliance assessment is done by a developer in -house, there 
would need to be government capacity and capability to collect, manage and assess every compliance 
report.  
b. This is in a context of a critical AI skills shortage in Australia.  
c. While voluntary third- party certification will become a powerful tool for understanding deployer and 
developer compliance, the industry is too immature for this to be mandatory at this time.  Proposals paper: Intro ducing mandatory guardrails for AI in high -risk settings , BCA submission  14  
   
 The BCA suggests that a cross all guardrails, additional guidance be developed to ensure obligations are understood.  
Also , that the guardrails must be applied in a consistent manner, without allowing regulators to customise  guard rails 
for their individual sectors.  
3.3.6 Designation of responsibility  
The BCA recommends greater clarity be provided on how responsibility for meeting the guardrails will be applied to 
participants with in the AI value chain. C larity on designation of responsibility will be critical for Australian businesses 
to develop and use AI systems  effectively and confidently .  
The Proposals Paper  contains little detail on how these responsibilities should be allocated between participants within 
an AI value chain, or how these obligations would be given legal effect. In apportioning responsibility, government must 
ensure that any proposed outcome is able to accommodate the wide varie ty of commercial structures that will emerge.  
In some instances, a business will be both the developer and the deployer . They may also have no contractual 
relationship with either.  
Government must  also  be careful to limit the amount of responsibility they impose on one party for the actions of 
another. This should include limiting responsibility on developers for the actions of deployers who use an AI system recklessly or inappropriately , as well as limiting deployer responsibility for issues with underlying AI systems where 
they have undertaken due diligence to understand the appropriateness of the system for the specific use case.   
The BCA supports the allocation of direct responsibilities on both deployers and developers within an AI value chain for 
implementing guardrails relating to specific actions or activities  within their control. Obligations imposed on deployers 
should focus on  appropriate  use to mitigate  risks, while o bligations on developers should focus on performing  
fundamental  testing and providing sufficient information to deployers to enable them to make informed decisions 
around appropriate use. The kind of information  the developer is required to provide  should vary based on the AI 
system’s functionality. However, align ment  with international standards should be encouraged as they emerge.  
While a model that applies direct obligations could  be achievable, it will require significant consultation to ensure that 
it is implementable in a way that minimises regulatory burden on both government and AI ecosystem participants.  
Consideration must also be given to the extraterritorial impact of legislation  and how this will impact Australian 
companies looking to partner with developers based overseas.   
3.3.7 Regulatory options to mandate guardrails  
Option 1 is the preferred approach while the technology develops and ensures that the guardrails and principles are fit 
for purpose.  This is preferrable to a mandatory imposition of guardrails and principles which require significant reform 
and may need to be continually revised to respond to a fast changing technology.  
Concerns regarding potential fragmentation could be addressed through a central co -ordination role and guidance to 
the current regulators . 
If Option 2 proceeds, the following conditions, variations and caveats  are required : 
 The first step must be a thorough, public  review of existing legislation  to identify any regulatory gaps for AI . 
 If regulatory gaps are found, the second step must be government making a detailed, considered, consulted and 
evidence- based case for ex ante regulation.  
- At the present time, the BCA does not believe that government has undertaken these first two critical 
steps to a degree that justifies shifting straight into consideration of ex ante regulatory options.  
 After these first two steps are complete and if government can mount a  convincing case for ex ante regulation 
of AI in Australia, then the BCA would broadly support Option 2.  
 Option 2 would support different regulators to conduct assessments in a similar manner and apply common 
guardrails consistently to different sectors.  All guardrails should apply equally across  sectors  for compliance 
consistency and ease of implementation . 
 Option 2 should be accompanied by a common  definition and metrics for assessing AI risk . This  will help to 
reduce the risk of divergence across regulators and sectors.   Proposals paper: Intro ducing mandatory guardrails for AI in high -risk settings , BCA submission  15  
   
  When being developed, Option 2 should identify existing regulatory regimes that could be amended at the 
same time.  
 Option 2 should set out a ‘hybrid approach’, which includes principles for defining high -risk settings and  a list 
of high -risk use cases where an assessment against the principles must be undertaken . 
 Option 2 framework guidance should be informed by an Australian AI Safety Institute or the planned AI 
Advisory Body, or both. Such a body should also have a role in assist ing regulators in outlining how AI  interacts 
with other existing technology neutral legislation .  
 In practice, existing regulators are doing the work . We already have regulations in place for specific products, 
industries, and professions. So rather than reinvent the wheel, empower these regulators with the right 
principles and guardrails. Give them the tools they need to spot and address the risks of AI in their areas of 
expertise.  
- Businesses can continue their existing relationships with regulators, which reduces unnecessary 
duplication and complexity . And when it comes to mitigating risk, no one is better placed  to do it than 
the people who already understand the nuances of their industries . 
An overarching AI Act as proposed in Option 3 risks app lying obligations too broadly and rigidly, losing nuance across 
sectors and stifling the ability for Australia to develop and use AI.   
During the political negotiations on the EU AI Act many people in Brussels have reali sed that creating 
horizontal rules for a broad technology group such as AI does not make much sense legally. The risks and 
opportunities posed by AI systems are just too much depending on the specific context of use as well as the 
sector. Yet, the legal pro cess was too advanced to change the whole legal approach of the EU.  I hope Australia 
does not repeat that mistake and opts, from the beginning, for a framework approach as proposed by the 
Australian government in Option 2. A set of non -binding guiding principles that are implemented vertically, 
by sectorial agencies with sectorial laws will lead to much higher and targeted protection from AI risks that we fearful of, while at the same time avoiding to harm innovation in AI.  
Kai Zenner -  Head of Office and Digital Policy Adviser for MEP Axel Voss
