• Expanded examples  for principle e)  ‘The risk of adverse impacts to the broader 
Australian economy, society, environment and rule of law ’: 
o Creative Australia recommends the government consider including 
examples of the adverse risks of AI to our creative economy, both through the potential loss of income for creatives and creative businesses, and the 
subsequent loss of GDP from cultural and creative work.  
 Australia’s cultural and creative activity contributes $21.8 billion to 
Australia's Gross Domestic Product
7 and the creative industries employ 
nearly 500,000 people.8 The risk of adverse impacts  from AI  on the 
economic viability of our creative industries has been discussed and 
debated both in Australia and overseas.9  
 A major concern is the copying or reproducing works without permission to 
train generative AI models  – in which there is a gap in the protections for 
artists . See Regulatory options to mandate guardrails  within this submission 
for more details. The sector has been advocating to ensure artists are fairly 
compensated for their contributions to these innovative technologies.10 
 In addition to principles of transparency and the legal right to be 
remunerated for the use of creative work, there are broader implications for 
the future of creative work in an AI age. Anyone can now prompt generative 
AI applications to produce creative work,  without the skills, time and 
dedication that hum an-led creative development requires . This  AI-
generated content is fo rmed  using existing creative work without 
compensation . It can therefore have a significant potential impact on the 
financial viability of creative careers, and Australia’s cultural and creative 
industries more broadly.    Introducing mandatory g uardrails for AI in high -risk settings  
7 
  In the music industry, research commissioned by APRA AMCOS estimates 
that by 2028, 23% of music creators’ revenues will be at risk due to 
generative AI, an estimated cumulative total damage of $519 million to the 
economy.11 The report found that 82% of music creators are concerned that 
without appropriate protection the use of AI in the music industry could lead 
them to no longer be able to earn a living from their work.12 
 Similarly, Australia’s publishing industry  has raised  concerns of economic 
loss to authors and the broader viability of Australia’s publishing industry. 
According to the Australian Publishers Association ‘the unfettered 
appropriation of published works is not only fundamentally unfair, but also 
deeply damaging to Australia’s creative and knowledge sectors. Poorly 
regulated AI development risks distorting the publishing value chain, and 
threatens to displace our creative workforce, and destroy our creative 
ecosystem of bookstores, literary festivals, and more. ’13 
 The loss of creative income and markets would be particularly detrimental  
to First Nations visual artists and businesses.  See our response to question 
2 for more detail.  
 There is potential for negative impacts of generative AI on Australia’s 
broader GDP . In addition to loss of income, c oncentrated ownership of AI 
applications  is further compounding  the potential impact on revenue models  
and has led to a significant transfer of revenue away from local creative 
sectors.  In its Digital Platform Ser vices Inquiry  interim report , the ACCC 
found that digital platforms can exercise control through self -preferencing, 
favouring their own products and services over  competitors who rel y on the 
