o Creative Australia agrees this is an important principle for determining high-risk AI, and should include examples that illustrate how adverse impacts are caused by scale and compounded over time. The  cumulative impact of 
generative AI applications on the telling of Australian stories and Australia’s unique culture, and the economic impact on the creative sector are both examples where scale and impact over time are key to assessing harm.  
 
4. Are there high- risk use cases that government should consider banning in its 
regulatory response (for example, where there is an unacceptable level of risk)? If 
so, how should we define these?  
• Creative Australia suggest s the  government under takes consultation  to investigate 
the risks of , and consider banning,  generative AI applications  that allow t he 
creation of First Nations -like cultural expressions.  
• The New Frontier: Artificial Intelligence, copyright and Indigenous Culture by Emma 
Fitch, Clare McKenzie, Terri Janke and Adam Shul  discusses the ways in which 
generative AI can create further issues with inauthentic art as anyone can create 
‘Aboriginal style art’ .17 The paper recommends  the need to reinsert Indigenous 
sovereignty and self -determination practices within AI development and deployment to 
ensure AI is ethical and culturally appropriate.  
•  A cross governmental approach should consider  whether a ban is the most 
appropriate mechanism  to prevent harm. This should be done in consultation with 
Indigenous Australians . See our response to question 2 for more detail.   
 
5. Are the proposed principles flexible enough to capture new and emerging forms of 
high- risk AI, such as general -purpose AI (GPAI)?   Introducing mandatory g uardrails for AI in high -risk settings  
9 
 • Creative Australia is of the view that the principles are flexible  enough to capture 
new and emerging forms of high- risk AI .  
 
6. Should mandatory guardrails apply to all GPAI models?  
• Creative Australia recommends  the mandatory guardrails should apply to all GPAI 
models.  This would ensure all generative AI models  – which are of most high- risk to the 
cultural and creative industries  – abide by the guardrails.  
 
7. What are suitable indicators for defining GPAI models as high -risk? For example, is 
it enough to define GPAI as high- risk against the principles, or should it be based on 
technical capability such as FLOPS (e.g. 10^25 or 10^26 threshold), advice from a 
scientific panel, government or other indicators? 
• Suitable indicators which identify heightened risk may include the level of 
transparency around the datasets used to train the model, ownership of the model, 
and scale of potential deployment/use . Expanded examples, a step- through guide, or 
a matrix that identifies heightened risk would also help to guide the identification of high-
risk GPAI models.  
 
Guardrails  ensuring testing, transparency and accountability  of AI  
8. Do the proposed mandatory guardrails appropriately mitigate the risks of AI used 
in high- risk settings? Are there any guardrails that we should add or remove?  
• Creative Australia encourages the department to expand  the guardrails to ensure 
the protect ion of  copyrighted material .  
• Creative Australia strongly believes that transparency should be  a fundamental 
pillar of effective guardrails t o mitigate the risks of generative AI. 
• Unlike other new technologies that have developed over time, the success of 
generative AI systems relies on both technology and the large datasets they are trained on. For generative AI applications designed to produce creative outputs, these 
datasets are drawn from existing creative work s which have been used predominantly 
without transparency  or permission and with no recognition or remuneration for the 
original creator.  
• Australia ’s copyright framework is a robust structural mechanism for the 
protection and growth of Australian culture and creativity.  However, t he onus of 
copyright enforcement tends to fall mostly on intellectual property rights holders.  
o In Real Life: Mapping digital cultural engagement in the first decades of the 21st  
century  highlights that digital disruption has reorganised the cultural value chain. 
Digital spaces are becoming increasingly algorithmic and automated where 
audiences expect arts and cultural experiences at a minimum cost. It is in this context that established models of IP rights and revenue creation may be 
threatened by ‘new media’ concepts of easy access and sharing for common 
good.
18  Introducing mandatory g uardrails for AI in high -risk settings  
10 
 o It is becoming harder for copyright holders to exert control over artworks , and 
artists are increasingly uncertain if their IP or moral rights have been infringed.  
Creatives are not always resourced to enforce copyright protection. Findings from 
the latest Economic Study of Professional Artists show that of the artists who 
believe their IP has been infringed (19%), only half have taken action to stop 
copyright infringement or seek  compensation (48%) and only half of these have 
been successful (52%) .19 
• Whilst copyright law has traditionally provided protection for creatives, the scale 
of datasets used in generative AI is challenging the effectiveness of copyright to 
protect creative IP.   
o Not only is copyrighted material being used to train datasets, but copyright 
is also being infringed in generative AI outputs. The creative sector has been 
advocating to ensure artists are fairly compensated for their contributions to these 
innovative technologies. See our response to question 3 for more detail.   
• The guardrails need to appropriately enforce copyright law s. Creative Australia 
encourages the department  to expand the guardrails to requir e organisations 
developing or deploying high- risk AI  models and systems to: 
o disclose data sources  and seek consent and compensate copyright holders 
where possible . In this way the guardrails  will support the  safeguarding of  
copyrighted material, and therefore safeguard the livelihoods of artists and the 
broader Australian cultural and creative industries .  
o develop mechanisms that prevent users breach ing copyright . In generative 
AI, restrict ing users from prompting AI  systems to reproduce copyrighted material 
may support  the enforcement of copyright laws. The onus must be on the 
organisations developing generative AI products to mitigate the illegal 
consumption of copyrighted material.   
 
9. How can the guardrails incorporate First Nations knowledge and cultural protocols 
to ensure AI systems are culturally appropriate and preserve ICIP?  
• Creative Australia recommends the department work consult  First Nations experts 
and existing literature  to ensure the guardrails align with First N ations ’ needs, 
concerns and identified opportunities  to protect First N ations ICIP and cultures.  
There may be an opportunity to  develop bespoke cultural protocols for AI development 
and deployment .  
o Out of the Black Box: Indigenous protocols for AI  highlights  there is potential for 
Indigenous Knowledge to guide the design and engineering principles and 
practices of A I.20  
o In the arts  and cultural sector , the Protocols for using First Nations Cultural and 
Intellectual Property in the Arts  encourage self -determination and helps build a 
strong and diverse Indigenous arts sector.21  
o W hile the protocols serve as good guidelines for engaging with I CIP in the arts, 
further protection is needed as the protocols are not legally binding. As mentioned 
prior in this submission,  standalone legislation to protect Indigenous Cultural and  Introducing mandatory g uardrails for AI in high -risk settings  
11 
 Intellectual Property (ICIP) is currently being scoped by the Office for the Arts 
within the Department of Infrastructure, Transport, Regional Development, 
Communications and the Arts.22  
o As recommended in our response to question 4, a ban on using AI to generat e 
traditional cultural Indigenous  art and images should be explored.  
 
10. Do the proposed mandatory guardrails distribute responsibility across the AI 
supply chain and throughout the AI lifecycle appropriately? For example, are the 
requirements assigned to developers and deployers appropriate?  
• Overall, the proposed mandatory guardrails  appropriately  distribute responsibility 
across the AI supply chain . For example, g uardrail 3 ensures developers share 
responsibility for the sourcing of their datasets, covering important points of transparency, 
copyright law  and Indigenous data sovereignty.  See our response to question 8 for more 
detail.  
• Responsibility must not be disproportionately shifted to the users of generative AI.  
Developers must take responsibility for ethically sourcing data (ie , seeking permission, 
attributing, and remunerating the original creator). Developers must be transparent about 
the datasets being used. The users of generative AI tools hold a  responsibility to ensure 
that they are not breaching copyright in generating im ages, music, or writing , however  
the developers must ensure that their products do not enable this and provide robust 
guidance to end user s.  
 
11. Are the proposed mandatory guardrails sufficient to address the risks of GPAI? 
How could we adapt the guardrails for different GPAI models, for example low -risk 
and high- risk GPAI models?  
• Creative Australia is of the view that the guardrails will be sufficient and robust if they can be applied to all GPAI models effectively. There is a risk that potential 
limitations to enforcing compliance by  non-Australian owned companies, for example 
compliance with data governance standards,  may hinder their effectiveness.  
 
12. Do you have suggestions for reducing the regulatory burden on small -to-medium 
sized businesses applying guardrails?  
• Developer s should be required to provide guidance to small /medium sized 
businesses and to end users (consumers)  to ensure that there is ongoing education 
regarding the standards of use in line with the guardrails and the principles of ethics and 
transparency . This is particularly needed for high- risk settings and could be in the form of 
advisory notes , warnings  or guidance manuals . 
  Introducing mandatory g uardrails for AI in high -risk settings  
12 
 Regulatory options  to mandate  guardrails  
13. Which legislative option do you feel will best address the use of AI in high- risk 
settings? What opportunities should the government take into account in 
considering each approach? 
• Creative Australia endorses Option 3 (a whole of economy approach): Introduction 
a new AI -specific Act.  
• Uncertainty about how existing and in- development AI models are training 
generative AI models is a fundamental  challenge for Australia’s cultural and 
creative industries.  
o In the case of generative AI there is a gap in the protections  for the creatives 
whose data is being used to train the models without their knowledge, consent or 
remuneration.  See our response to question 16 for more details.   
o Without specific regulation, it is likely that many businesses will continue to  
illegally  draw on copyrighted creative content to train generative AI models  and 
develop outputs that infringe copyright . This may l ead to the collapse of our 
creative industries and  the potential homogenisation of  culture.  See our response 
to question 3 for more details.     
• An AI -specific Act is a local solution to a global issue.  It would centralise Australia’s 
regulation of these systems  (both developed and available in Australia) , support 
international collaboration, boost public trust and stimulate a more ethically sourced 
market for AI development and use. Such an approach would activate the market that 
creates change, ensuring the viability of creative and cultural work.  
 
14. Are there any additional limitations of options outlined in this section which the 
Australian Government should consider?   
N/A 
 
15. Which regulatory option/s will best ensure that guardrails for high- risk AI can 
adapt and respond to step- changes in technology? 
N/A 
 
16. Where do you see the greatest risks of gaps or inconsistencies with Australia’s 
existing laws for the development and deployment of AI? Which regulatory option 
best addresses this, and why?  
• In the context of a global environment, e xisting legislative frameworks are not fit -
for-purpose  to ensure AI models are ethical, transparent, and support economic 
and social benefits beyond a few. An AI -specific Act would  be the most effective model 
to support international collaboration.   Introducing mandatory g uardrails for AI in high -risk settings  
13 
 • Competition law , as it is currently conceptualised, is not adequate to combat the 
disproportionate level of market power attributed to international consumer 
technology companies  which has been detrimental to  local creators.   
o Competition law seeks to guard against abuse of market power, however this can 
have limited efficacy in a global business context. This is particularly salient in the discussion about the domination of digital marketplaces that enable the mass 
consumption,  sharing and reimagination of creative content online, but at a cost to 
the original creators  who are not only losing the creative control over their work 
but their ability to generate future revenue from it .  
o In a digital context for creative business, it is not consumers who are experiencing the harm – in fact at present the consumer is benefiting from access to free 
generative AI tools.  Rather it is the creatives (i e, the originators of the data) that 
are losing out. Chokepoint Capitalism: How big tech and big content captured creative labour markets and how we’ll win them back by Rebecca Giblin and Cory 
Doctorow examines the ways some large digital platforms are dominating markets, locking in consumers and creators to use their  platforms.
23 
o In their submission to the Senate Select Committee on Adoption of Artificial 
Intelligence, Kingston AI Group argued Australia’s anti -competitive laws will not be 
able to fortify the economy against dominant international AI -driven companies 
who have an added advantage of expansive customer bases and an ability to leverage powerful AI with tremendous computing infrastructure. With these 
attributes, these companies will be able to expand quickly into new sectors (eg, banking, insurance, health care) and furth er hollow out Australia’s industry base.
24 
o Whilst competition law in Australia provides protections for small businesses  (and 
consumers) , this is a costly, lengthy, and uncertain route to take, and its 
jurisdiction does not extend to capture corporations operating outside Australia. In 
this way, AI -specific legislation will have greater impact if it aligns with an 
international regulatory approach. Indeed, an Australian standalone AI Act could 
lead the international engagement and approach.  
• The enforcement of copyright law is a major challenge for creative business, 
particularly small business, both in Australia and abroad.  
o Australia’s copyright framework is a robust structural mechanism for the protection 
and growth of Australian culture and creativity.  Revive , the Australian 
Government’s National Cultural Policy, commits to ‘maintaining a strong copyright 
framework that works in concert with other legal and policy mechanisms to ... 
support the success and vibrancy of Australia’s cultural and creative sector ’. See 
our response to question 8 for more detail.  
o Copyright treaties between countries ensure international cooperation on the 
protection of creative IP across jurisdictions.  Australia is a member of a number of 
international treaties that protect copyright  material, including the Berne 
Convention for the Protection of Literary and Artistic Works  (Berne Convention),  
Rome Convention for the Protection of Performers , Producers of Phonograms and 
Broadcasting Organizations  (Rome Convention)  and the Universal Copyright 
Convention (UCC).  Regulation needs to be aligned with the existing and emerging  Introducing mandatory g uardrails for AI in high -risk settings  
14 
 global  regulatory practices  to both enable innovation but ensure copyright is 
upheld. 
o Option 3 and the development of a standalone AI Act that can enforce 
compensation for workers in the creative industries for the use of their work in 
offshore and onshore generative AI training will be more effective than amending the existing copyright law.  
About Creative Australia  
Creative Australia is the Australian Government’s principal arts investment and advisory body.  
In line with the functions set out in our establishing legislation, the Creative Australia Act 2023, 
and guided by the vision, pillars, and actions set out in the Australian Government’s National 
Cultural Policy: Revive, a place for every story, a story for every place, Creative Australia champions arts and culture for all Australians.  
With artists at the heart of what we do, Creative Australia invest s in creative talent and 
stimulate s the market for Australian stories to be told on a national and international scale, 
sharing our rich culture with the world. Our strategic activity includes multi -year investment, 
grants, targeted initiatives and research and advocacy to support, strengthen and sustain the 
arts and cultural sector for all audiences to benefit.  
