Focussing on the environmental harms posed by scaling AI systems is critical, but we should 
equally pay attention to mitigating environmental harms for long -term use, maintenance, repair, 
and decommissioning of physical and digital systems. This may help a nticipate the impacts of 
inadvertent adoption of  more resource intensive AI. Currently, it is easy for hidden energy 
increases of adopting AI -systems to harm or offset environmental and emissions targets.9 Only 
by paying attention to environmental harms across the AI lifecycle will we assure these risks are 
captured.  
For the Australian Public Service (APS), mitigating the environmental harms posed by AI is an opportunity to lead across siloed policy departments, bringing together portfolios including 
urban planning, energy, digital infrastructure, and environment portf olios to work together and 
across Federal, State or local levels of governance. Portfolios responsible for environmental 
health — especially those planning longer -term sustainable and just transitions — may benefit 
from partnering closely with portfolios r esponsible for promoting AI -use and AI -related 
industries and innovation ecosystems for productivity growth. The APS also needs  to think 
systemically, act adaptively and communicate transparently when government itself uses AI to improve sustainability and other policy processes and outcomes .
