The proposed principles for high -risk settings need to include vulnerable communities, while 
being cautious about the complexities of using 'vulnerability' as a label/tag.  Tagging/labelling 
creates adverse effects if a person is labelled ‘vulnerable’ within an AI system. Applying a process of enquiry to understand the person's complex circumstances mitigates the adverse 
effects of tagging 'vulnerability' within an automated  system. Professor Angie Abdilla from the 
School of Cybernetics is currently advising  on wor k within Services Australia as a Board 
Member to address this issue. Services Australia are developing a strategy and operational 
procedure to ensure that service staff avoid labelling and/or tagging someone with a 
detrimental identifier when inputting ass essments into an automated system.  
The guardrails should address specific use cases of AI in law enforcement and their potential 
harms to Indigenous peoples. In Australia, Indigenous Australians disproportionally risk 
encounters with law enforcement15. Matthew Heffernan, a PhD candidate at the ANU School of 
Cybernetics, has published on his concerns for the protection of First Nations peoples’ rights in relation to AI -powered surveillance and its use in law enforcement
16. In the proposed guardrails, 
the use of predictive policing, and AI in law enforcement should be specifically addressed. The guardrails should not reinforce existing inequalities, and efforts not to do so should be made 
explicit for key applications of AI . 
Indigenous Cultural and Intellectual Property (ICIP) is not a robust mechanism to guard  against 
risks to the use and preservation of First Nations knowledge and cultural protocols. Indigenous 
Intangible Cultural Heritage is difficult to codify and protect within the frameworks of ICIP.17 
Any proposal to incorporate Indigenous Traditional Knowledge and/or protocols needs to be 
Indigenous -led and must include a rigorous process of Indigenous community consultation, and 
controlled testing, to mitigate harm. Crucially, these steps must occur before public release.  
 
