 Introduction to Spotify 
 Spotify launched in Sweden in 2008, steering the global music industry out of the crisis of piracy it 
 faced at the time and now has more than 626 million monthly active users globally who access 
 licensed music and other content, from ad-supported freemium to various paid subscription plans at 
 various price points (246m paying subscribers, Q2 2024). It is designed around personalisation and 
 customisation with both human editorial teams and algorithmic recommendations to aid discovery of 
 artists. At Spotify , we’re dedicated to ensuring the impact of our technology encourages diversity and 
 provides the best possible opportunities for creator and user discovery . We are transparent about 
 how recommendations on our platform work.  1 
 Since entering the Australian market in 2012, Spotify has been deeply committed to Australia and its 
 creators. Our editorial showcasing of Australian talent, together with industry partnerships, 
 promotions, and making Australian artists’ music available across the 184 markets where we 
 operate has opened unparalleled new opportunities and income streams for Australian creators and 
 rightsholders. In 2023, more than 80 percent of all royalties generated by Australian artists on 
 Spotify were from listeners outside of the country . Globally on Spotify , Australian artists were 
 discovered by first-time listeners more than 2.7 billion times last year . In 2023, royalties generated by 
 Australian artists from Spotify reached nearly 275M AUD.  2 
 Spotify’ s early investment in AI has made the company what it is today: a personalised experience 
 for every user that has led to billions of discoveries of artists and creators around the world. Spotify’ s 
 Founder , Daniel Ek has said: “As we look to the future of streaming, we see tremendous potential to 
 use open-source AI to benefit consumers as well as the creative industries, including music. This is 
 especially important when it comes to how AI can help more artists get discovered. This is the 
 primary application of AI technology which we have in mind and this submission focuses on this use 
 case. A simplified regulatory structure would not only accelerate the growth of open-source AI but 
 also provide crucial support to developers and the broader creator ecosystem that contributes to and 
 thrives on these innovations”.  3 
 Like many businesses, Spotify is having discussions with industry and governments about how to 
 manage the powerful potential of AI technologies as a tool. Our mission remains: to allow creators to 
 live of f of their art – whether they be musicians or songwriters, book authors or podcasters. 
 As a result, continued innovation and protecting intellectual property is important to Spotify as well 
 as our direct partners such as rightsholders and indirect partners such as creators. 
