 feature), are examples of low risk  4  uses of AI, we would like to provide general comments and views 
 for consideration and to assist the Department on the proposed AI guardrails in Australia and in 
 defining high risk, general purpose AI (GP AI).  5  These include: 
 ●  Innovation and level playing field:  Onerous and unclear regulation on GP AI may impede 
 innovation.  Encouraging competition helps  AI innovators in the Large Language Models 
 (LLMs) space to improve quality and safety . Competition between a variety of LLM providers 
 is also fundamental in order to allow downstream choice and competitive prices to AI 
 developers who, like Spotify , use such LLMs to develop their own AI systems. W e believe 
 the next generation of ideas and startups will be built with open-source AI, because it lets 
 developers incorporate the latest innovations at low cost and gives institutions more control 
 over their data. It is the best shot at harnessing AI to drive progress and create economic 
 opportunity and security for everyone.  6 
 ●  Contractually agreed solutions:  In creative output related domains, contractually agreed 
 solutions can increase certainty , maintain flexibility and be more precise than laws or 
 regulations 
 ●  Research and Development (R&D):  The Proposals Paper asks if the mandatory guardrails 
 should apply to all GP AI models. W e encourage the Department to consider a clear 
 exemption for GP AI for R&D and internal processes of companies, which are not distributed 
 on the market - and to consider a distinction of GP AI models that are only used for a 
 platform's own products and services. The EU AI Act for example, includes a clear 
 exemption in Article 2(8) for “any research, testing or development activity regarding AI 
 systems or AI models prior to their being placed on the market or put into service.” 
 ●  Proportionate risk based approach:  We support a  proportionate, balanced and considered 
 approach to any AI regulation  - with  rules focused on well-defined high-risk use cases.  7  To 
 support innovation, there should be consideration in dif ferentiating regulating against known 
 harms which is necessary versus pre-emptive regulation of theoretical harms for nascent 
 technologies such as open source AI. 
 Moreover , we note the government recently released its Interim Response to the  Safe and 
 Responsible AI in Australia Discussion Paper  which advocated a risk-based approach to AI 
 regulation, acknowledging that a vast number of uses of AI such as “monitoring weather patterns, or 
 managing traf fic flow” – are considered low risk and should be enabled to flourish unimpeded.  8 
 8  Proposals Paper for Introducing Mandatory Guardrails  for AI in High-Risk Settings,  Australian Government 
 Department of Industry , Science and Resources (pg 4)  7  As  outlined under the EU AI Act Article 6- <  https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/6/  >  6  Mark Zuckerberg and Daniel Ek on why Europe should  embrace open-source AI, The Economist, 21 August 2024  5  “  An AI model that is capable of being used, or capable  of being adapted for use, for a variety of purposes, both for 
 direct use as well as for integration in other systems” in  Proposals Paper for Introducing Mandatory Guardrails  for AI 
 in High-Risk Settings, Australian Government Department of Industry , Science and Resources  (pg 9)  4  Low risk AI that does not meet the definition of high-risk AI systems, as  defined under the EU AI Act  Article 6- 
 <  https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/6/  > 
 2 
 We agree  that low risk uses of AI should continue to thrive and not be constrained by rules designed 
 for high-risk use cases. 
 Spotify AI 
 Spotify has long used machine learning, and also recently leveraged generative AI to power 
 recommendations that help artists and writers connect with fans and build their careers - and has 
 made Spotify what it is today: a personalised experience for users. This ambition continues to guide 
 our work through the development of innovative experiences like the Spotify Home feed, “Discover 
 Weekly”, “Blend” and “Made for Y ou Mixes” which millions of listeners worldwide engage with every 
 day. 
 Spotify uses AI to increase human consumption of human creation. Most recently , with the rise of 
 generative AI technologies, we've been experimenting with new ways to continue cultivating 
 discovery and connection, some examples include: 
 ●  AI DJ:  AI DJ is a  personalised AI-enabled feature for Spotify Premium users that delivers a 
 curated lineup of music alongside AI generated commentary around the tracks and artists in 
 a voice modelled after Spotify’ s Head of Cultural Partnerships, X Jernigan.  9  Feedback has 
 been positive as a way to help listeners discover and rediscover music they love and 
 Spotify’ s AI  DJ was named one of  Time’s  best inventions of 2023.  10  Human beings, including 
 a “writers room” of  music and culture experts, data curators, and script writers are involved in 
 helping arm AI DJ with knowledge and expertise. W e recently expanded with AI DJ in 
 Spanish. Like X in English-speaking markets, a Spotify Sr . Music Editor , Livi, has provided 
 the Spanish voice of our AI DJ.  11 
 ●  AI Playlists:  We’re currently piloting AI Playlists, as another tool to fuel discovery and music 
 curation by  pairing our personalisation technology with AI to allow Spotify Premium users to 
 create a perfect playlist based on their own entered prompt. 
 Spotify appreciates that this space is evolving rapidly , which is why we’re actively exploring new and 
 emerging technologies through research and development and have dedicated teams in place to 
 review the risks and opportunities of a broad range of AI methodologies and tools. 
 We know that AI is a critically important issue for our partners in the creative sector . We take our role 
 in this sector and the support we provide for artists and creators very seriously . 
 As Spotify’ s founder Daniel Ek told the BBC, there are valid uses of AI in making music - but AI 
 should not be used to impersonate human artists without their consent.  12  Moreover , Spotify's 
 Platform Rules govern all content on our service, including any content that AI helped create. 
 12  “  Not all AI-made music will be banned on Spotify  says platform boss”,  27 September 2023, BBC UK  11  Spotify Brings AI DJ to Spanish-Speaking Music Fans  Around the W orld, 17 July 2024, Spotify Newsroom 
 <https://newsroom.spotify .com/2024-07-17/spanish-ai-dj-livi-voice/>  10  The best inventions of 2023, TIME 
 <  https://time.com/collection/best-inventions-2023/6326353/spotify-dj/#:~:text=Music%20streamer%20Spotify's%20ne 
 w,Spotify's%20head%20of%20cultural%20partnerships  >  9  In Australia and other English speaking countries. 
 3 
 Those platform rules outline our policies on deceptive content, not permitting ""crawling"" or 
 ""scraping"", whether manually or by automated means, as well as responding to infringement notices. 
 While we will proceed cautiously , we’re hopeful about the opportunities for creative artists that AI 
 presents. 
 Clarity for AI Innovators 
 As a general overview , we reiterate our support for a proportionate risk based approach - with clear , 
 unambiguous delineation between high-risk and low risk applications to provide clarity to AI 
 innovators and to a diverse range of stakeholders. 
 a)  Principles defining high-risk AI settings and their potential application to 
 general-purpose AI (GP AI) models. 
 While other jurisdictions such as the EU have list based approaches, we understand the Department 
 is considering a principles based approach in defining high-risk due to flexibility and adaptability . We 
 would highlight with this approach that any principles must be precise, clearly defined and 
 measurable by any impacted providers or otherwise may be seen as too broad. For example 
 Principle (e) of the Proposals Paper , on “impacts to the broader Australian economy , society , 
 environment and rule of Law” should be more precisely articulated as the present wording is broad 
 that it covers virtually any use of AI in the country . Focusing on well-defined high-risk use cases 
 provides certainty , and ensures innovation  can continue to thrive. 
 Moreover ,  we believe when considering a principles based approach that it is noted that where there 
 are contractually agreed solutions between stakeholders particularly for low risk use cases, that 
 contractual obligations prevail over rules. This is because these contractual negotiations will operate 
 more precisely and flexibly . 
 b)  Proposed mandatory guardrails. 
 As Spotify does not engage in high-risk uses, we may not comment on all aspects of the 
 consultation in-depth; however , we wish to provide general comment and views on some of the 
 proposed mandatory guardrails.  The Proposals Paper asks for views on  adapting the guardrails for 
 different GP AI models, for example low-risk and high-risk GP AI models.  We believe  any future 
 guardrails or regulatory instrument should dif ferentiate between low-risk and high-risk GP AI models, 
 which can help avoid unnecessary burdens on both types of models. The framework should also 
 consider the unique context in which dif ferent models operate, as risks may vary based on 
 application, domain and environment. 
 Furthermore, we encourage the Department to consider a research exemption for GP AI for R&D and 
 internal processes of companies. This would ensure continued support for innovation without 
 presenting any consumer or real-world risk. 
 On applying transparency and explainability requirements in general, as outlined above and applied 
 to the creative sector , we believe agreements between stakeholders are the best way to achieve 
 clarity and should take precedence over guardrails. Any AI regulation that is nevertheless 
 4 
 considered should be tailored to situations where these issues were not settled by contract and 
 should also adequately clarify which kind of information should be provided, at which stage (training 
 or output), at what level of granularity , by whom and to whom. 
 Finally , any transparency obligations towards the public should allow providers to implement them in 
 a way that is most meaningful to their respective users, taking into account the diversity in business 
 models and interfaces. 
 In assisting the Department on the question regarding Guardrail 6 on labelling  ,  we understand there 
 is a broader question whether content that is generated by AI should be labelled. W e note that some 
 stakeholders in this debate, for example those in the global music industry , are proposing labelling 
 efforts and metadata standards such as an AI Content field in DDEX 4.3.1  13  (labelling of music as AI 
 generated). This approach on labelling, that is disclosure by rightsholders through DDEX, makes 
 sense. Also, any requirements for labelling and watermarking of AI should be placed on content 
 providers as they are the best-placed, and often the only , parties capable of identifying how such 
 content was produced. Having those closest to the content’ s creation responsible for identification 
 will also ensure the data and any required labelling is applied consistently to consumers across 
 retailers. 
 Spotify has not been shy about communicating our excitement about features like AI DJ to the 
 public. Consumers have a clear understanding that when they’re using AI DJ, for example, they’re 
 interacting with a feature that uses generative AI to generate a life-like voice. It is unclear what the 
 consumer benefit would be in potentially requiring additional disclosure for these types of low-risk 
 uses of generative AI. The emphasis for new obligations should be on high-risk use cases that have 
 the potential to af fect consumers in serious ways, rather than benign use cases that provide users 
 with entertainment and help artists make lasting connections with fans. 
 Regarding options for introducing mandatory guardrails, we support a pro-innovation model - a 
 domain specific approach, adapting, clarifying and strengthening existing laws to address potential 
 risks related to AI. However , as noted, for creative ""domain"" related questions, contractually agreed 
 solutions should prevail over guardrail instruments. 
 Conclusion 
 Spotify is aware of both the challenges and opportunities that AI technology brings. W e are also 
 extremely enthusiastic about what this innovation can bring. Our focus to help creators connect with 
 audiences, monetise their art, and build careers remains. W e thank the Department for its work on 
 these issues and again welcome the opportunity to be a part of this conversation on AI and present 
 our view as a content platform, reiterating our support for innovation-friendly environments, with 
 clear distinction between high risk and low risk settings. 
