The Department itself has acknowledged the failure of self -regulation.6 Since the  RAI 
launched , workers are increasingly  impacted by AI  in their day to day working lives , as are 
their fundamental workplace rights . Victorian w orkers have been  calling  on employers to 
act on AI risk  in greater numbers.  However, every year, it seems  business es are  only 
remov ing more and more  of the safeguards they have place . 
 
The Department state s it ha s ‘worked with business’ in developing the proposed 
guardrails .7 To date, VTHC has not been invited to participate in any of these consultations ; 
and, to the best of our knowledge,  neither has  our national counterpart, the Australian 
Council of Trade Unions (ACTU) nor affiliated union.8 Working peoples’ input had not been 
sought, despite the monumental impacts AI has on the workplace and on workers’ lives.  
This is evident in the poor design of the proposed guardrails.  
 
 
