 Separately however, VTHC has engaged substantively with the Federal Government about  
AI policy through two parliamentary inquiries this year.9 Victorian unions would be  
disappointed if this work has been wasted, as this process was convening a response to AI 
relevant to workplaces. We are deeply concerned about  the apparent lack of consideration 
given to worker voices  in this guardrails proposal . Please find a ttached VTHC’s  submission 
to the Federal Government’s Inquiry into the Digital Transformation of Workplaces. The 
submission touches on a variety of matters highly -relevant to the proposed guardrails.   
 
The missing perspective of workers is evident  in the fact that t he proposed guardrails 
impose regulation on deployers and developers  of AI . This does not accurately reflect  how 
AI is developed and continuously amended within the workplace , as it  gathers huge 
amounts of information about workers and iterates , evolving the model constantly. 
Employers use this process to collect more and more information about their  workers and 
to evade their obligations with regards to workplace rights. For this reason, employers 
should be deemed deployers and developers of AI . The obligations imposed by  the 
proposed guardrails must apply to employers.  Throughout this submission VTHC refers to 
employers to cover both the obligations imposed on deployers and developers of AI.  
 
Workers are impacted by these systems at every point of the employment relationship – as 
well as in their lives as customers, citizens and consumers.10 When a workplace AI system 
deviates from its intended objective, exhibits robustness issues, makes faulty 
recommendations or violates human right s – workers see it first  and are most impacted by 
it.  
 
2. Overview of the Proposed Guardrails  
 
According to the  proposed guardrails , the purpose of AI regulation is:  
  
• To allow  business  to respond to risks as they emerge.11 
• To support  business  to deploy AI safely. 12 
• To provide business  with the insight they need to manage risks.13 
 
In Australia , big tech and big business has much to gain in  design ing policy that enables 
them to  develop  and deploy AI systems according to  their interests. Workers have been 
promised that AI and technological advances will bring ease to their lives. Yet, the rollout of 
AI has been unfair and to the detriment of workers. AI is here , management by algorithm is 
here  – and it is  producing measurable, systematic and observable effects  on workers lives.  
There are real consequences for  workers ’ lives toda y.  
 
The proposed guardrails frame AI-regulation as a purely technical matter aimed at tech 
developers . They fail to understand that w orkplaces  are deliberately using AI  to circumvent 
 
