 long  fought for workers’ rights  for financial gain  and fail to provide any meaningful 
mechanism for accountability – both for employers and for technological developers.  
 
International  examples of AI regulation and legislation  include detailed chapters on 
prohibitions, penalties and enforcement for this reason ; absent in the propo sed 
guardrails .14  
 
Unions know that if effective regulation and safeguards are not present to protect workers ’ 
rights, businesses will compete to the lowest common denominator .15  
 
3. Systemic Risks  
 
3.1.  Threats to Workplace Democracy  
Deeper consideration should  be given  in the proposed guardrails  to the urgent and 
potentially systemic risks of AI to Australian workers . These risks include  a deterioration of 
workplace democracy, unprecedented surveillance and systematic downward pressure on 
wages. 16 
 
