3.2. Wage Suppression  
 
In countries like Australia, where there is a significant knowledge, information and creativity -
based workforce, AI presents a substantial risk.28 Left u nregulated  AI will lead to  lower  
wages . 
 
Overall, AI presents a huge risk to workers’ rights and livelihoods. These include:  
• Knowledge workers having  to compete  for jobs  with AI systems . 
• AI enabled  outsourcing utilised by employers on a wider scale . 
• Devaluing of  highly  competitive skills, meaning w orkers will be pushed into 
performing jobs  that th ey are overqualified for .29 
• Algorithmic management  that normalises  ‘on-demand’ task allocation  that adjusts 
the availability of work according to instant fluctuations in market activity . This will 
result in less and more insecure jobs . 
• AI enabled  dynamic wage setting – adjusting the rate of pay according to the supply 
of available workers and the demand for work. In periods of high -labour supply this 
cause s wages to plummet .30 
• In cases where jobs are not automated, significant tasks within the job might be. This 
reduces the overall ability of workers in that position to bargain for strong pay and 
conditions. 31 
• Hostile applications of AI by employers in industrial relations could reduce the ability 
of workers to organise and bargain for better conditions .32 
• AI will complete information work much faster, but with more errors. Humans will be 
reassigned from generating content , to refining, verifying and proofing the AI 
outputs. This changes the nature of work in a way that will likely result in lower 
incomes being able to be demand ed from knowledge work .33 
• AI can be used to cause significant changes to job roles without consultation with 
workers and their representatives .34 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
