 4.2 Guardrail 2: The Risk Management Process  
 
All applications of artificial intelligence in the employment context must  be considered 
high -risk – whether they are performed by a developer, deployer or an emp loyer 
developing or deploying AI.  No application of AI in employment is low -risk – even 
automated payrolling filling software. Regardless of a list or principles -based  approach, 
Victorian unions  strongly recommend that employment -based applications are 
categorically high -risk. 53 
 
The proposed risk management process does not mandate that employers  consider any 
potential impacts to workers or the community  before using a high -risk AI system. As the 
RAI index has found, most  businesses have not been consulting experts, workers or the 
community about AI risks.54 The effectiveness of Guardrail 2 is therefore questionable.  
 
If workers and their representatives had been consulted  on these guardrails , the 
Department would understand that e mployers  widely  fail to consult with workers at present 
about major workplace changes despite them being required to do so under the Fair Work 
Act 2009 (Cth ).55 This is especially true with regards to workplace surveillance and AI -driven 
employee performance management.56 
 
VTHC urges the Federal Government to mandate genuine  consultation with workers and 
their representatives  preceding the deployment or development of an AI system for  in the 
employment context.  
 
Recommendation  2: Mandate the employment context be considered high -risk by 
default  
Ensure all AI systems wherever they apply or have overlap with employment or industrial 
relations are deemed  high -risk. In keeping with the EU AI Act, workers and their 
representatives must be provided with information  before an employer can deploy AI. 57 
 
Recommendation 3: Workplace AI risk assessments  must be mandatory where they 
relate to employment  
Introduce workplace AI risk assessments for all . AI use where there is any relation to 
employment. The British Trade Union Congress’s model for AI risk assessments  should be 
considered as a model for Australian workplace AI risk assessments .58 
 
 
 
