 Recommendation  4: Prohibit AI where it poses an unacceptable risk  
Legislate prohibitions on the use of AI where it would  pose an unacceptable risk to workers, 
consumers and the community.  There should be a total ban on the development, 
deployment, sale, uptake, distribution or export of AI systems that are intended to:  
i. Calculate  the emotions of a worker  in an employment, civic, public, or 
educational context;  
ii. Calculate , categorise, or sort workers  according to protected personal attributes . 
Trade Union membership should be considered as a protected attribute;  
iii. Defraud the public or cause substantial economic losses to groups or 
individuals;  
iv. Collect data or information about workers or work performance to develop or 
deploy an AI system that limits their ability to make informed decisions or 
changes their behaviour to harm themselves, others or the community;59 and 
v. Perform real -time or retrospective mass remote biometric identification in public 
spaces. Reasonable and proportionate exceptions to this prohibition should be 
included for critical instances involving national security, national defence, 
counterterrorism and other matters pertaining to public order .  
 
Recommendation  5: Prohibit AI in i ndustrial relations  
Legislate prohibit ions on certain applications of AI in industrial relations  where a  system 
either  intends or results in an obstruct ion, interfere nce or otherwise prevent ion of  the free 
exercise of workplace rights and lawful union activity, including:  
i. Deterring workers from forming or joining a union or taking protected industrial 
action ; 
ii. Intimidating, isolating, or otherwise adversely affecting union delegates, health 
and safety representatives of otherwise industrially active employees ; 
iii. Unduly preventing or obstructing the right of workers to take protected industrial 
action ; and/or  
iv. Unduly preventing or  obstructing the right of workers to access their rights and 
entitlements under the applicable industrial instrument  or relevant industrial 
legislation .  
4.3 Guardrail s 3 and  4: Data Governance, Testing and Monitoring . 
 
Guardrail 3 stipulates that data sources for AI must be obtained legally and ethically. AI 
currently is stealing workers’ knowledge, data and creative outputs on an unprecedented 
scale. As outlined above, in Australia’s knowledge -based workforce, the impacts of this on 
workers will be substantial and mean serious job losses.60 The lack of accountability 
contained in Guardrail 3 and the related guardrails means that developers will do little to 
genuinely obtain information ethically.  
 
Recommendation  6: Legislat e to protect c ollective intellectual property  
Ensure that consideration is  given to how best to protect the collective knowledge, property 
and cultural outputs of Australian workers from being stolen for use in the development of 
AI systems. This is especially important with regards to cultural theft from First Nations 
intellectual and artistic property.  
 
