Guardrail 4 stipulates that business will have to undertake  ongoing  testing and monitoring 
of the high -risk systems in place. The basis of this  testing, however, is not sufficiently 
speci fied and the results of these tests will not be shared publicly or with impacted workers.   
 
The proposal guardrails  state  that Guardrail 4 is in keeping the EU AI Act , and it s provisions  
on post -market monitoring  in Article 72. It is difficult to verify this claim for a variety of 
reasons:  
 
• Article 72 has been intentionally left incomplet e so the Commission and member 
states can develop a template for post -market -monitoring over the following years .61 
• Article 73 mandates the monitoring of any serious incidents pertaining to the high -
risk AI system – and that these will be reported to the relevant market surveillance 
authority. This has not been committed to in the proposed mandatory guardrails – 
which only state  that compliance monitoring ‘could’  be overseen  by an external 
regulator.62 
• The effectiveness of testing and monitoring depends entirely on what is being tested 
and how. The choice of what fairness metrics to apply and how to apply them is not 
neutral.63 13 of the 25 most widely used algorithmic bias tests do not live up to the 
standards of European discrimination law. 64 
 
Recommendation  7: Mandate transparency for AI testing and monitoring outcomes  
Mandate that the results from any and all  testing, monitoring, or audits into a high -risk 
workplace AI system is shared with workers (and by extension, their unions) impacted by 
that system. Workers and their unions, as well as regulators, must receive from employers 
periodic updates about AI use  in the workplace, and any adverse impacts.65 
 
4.5 Guardrail 5:  Human Oversight  
 
The proposed guardrails include a provision on human oversight, or more accurately, 
executive oversight.66 Guardrai l 5 stipulates that businesse s must oversee the high -risk AI 
systems that they operate  and would need  to appoint a qualified person with access to 
relevant information who can interpret outputs and ‘assess the accuracy of algorithmic 
advice.’ 67 
 
In the workplace context,  Guardrail 5 assumes that protecting workers from  AI-harm is 
something business proactively cares about preventing . In instances where  employers are 
also developers , or in the common instance where  they adopt and refine AI models as they 
and they workplace develop and evolve , they are responsible for setting the model’s 
objectives , training data and parameters . Employers can engineer  the kind of advice the 
algorithm is generally going to give  and its  corresponding  outputs . This makes employers  
 
