Recommendation  9: Ensure a right to worker oversight  
Remove any restrictions on the ability of workers to collectively bargain over the potential 
or actual use of AI in the workplace. Worker involvement significantly enhance s human 
oversight of workplace AI systems where it's protected in enterprise bargaining 
agreements. Model Workplace Health and Safety clauses should also be amended to 
recognise digitalisation -related risks.70 
4.6 Guard rail 6: Informing End-users  
Guardrail 6 requires businesses to inform  people on how AI is being used and where it 
affects them. This will apply to automated decisions concerning health, safety and human 
rights . Victorian unions cannot overstate  our concern  here in failing  to include the 
employment context in this Guardrail .   
 
Again, the propos ed guardrails claim this is in  keeping with the EU AI Act.71 However, t he 
EU has legislated  a much more comprehensive ‘right to an explanation’ – which is required 
for any significant use of AI  and must be given at the time of the first interaction or 
exposur e.72  
 
For this Guardrail to achieve its state d goal of ‘fostering accountability’ it will need to be 
substantially  restructured.73 At bare minimum, d isclosures should be mandatory across all 
high -risk uses of AI, including and especially those pertaining to employment. Workers  have 
the right to an explanation when AI is used to make decisions that impact their livelihood.  
There must be serious penalties for employers failing  to undertake disclosures or 
withholding  critical  information from workers, workers’ representatives and regulators.  
 
