Recommendation 15: Employer’s liability for breaching workplace rights as a result 
of AI or an automated decision should be strict  
Any decision made by AI used  by an employer should be treated as a decision made by the 
employer themselves. Where there has been a breach of an instrument or the law as a result 
of an AI system or automated decision, it should be taken that the employer made that 
decision for the purposes of liability . 
 
4.8 Guardrail 8: Transparency  
 
Guardrail 8 requires developers and deployers  to share critical information about high -risk 
AI systems with each other – but fail to mandate they share information  with workers, 
consumers, or other  workers impacted  by their systems . Even regulators  are largely 
excluded from this very narrow interpretation of ‘transparency .’ Meaningful transparency 
requires information sharing with more parties than those who are profiting or could profit 
from the system.83 
 
Guardrail 8 is equated with  provisions found in the EU AI Act.84 Again, t he EU requires 
transparency over many more dimensions of a  system ’s function , and it requires a notable 
amount  of this information to be made public  or proactively submitted to regulators.85 
 
Recommendation 1 6: Establish a public register for AI use in workplaces  
Establish a public register for all high -risk AI systems currently in use by all employers. 
Employers should be required to  publish risk assessments and critical information about 
systems and their possible impacts on workers in the register before they can be deployed. 
 
