4.9 Guardrail 9: Recordkeeping  
 
Guardrail 9 describes the records that businesses will need to keep  so they can  ensure 
compliance with the guardrails. These recordkeeping provisions  are intended to ‘ enable 
external scrutiny of high -risk AI systems.’86 They cannot achieve this in their current form.  
 
Although Guardrail 9 is  described as ‘in keeping with the EU AI Act,’ the  European version  
mandates that high -risk systems are developed with built -in automatic logs that collect and 
store critical information about the system.87 No reference is made to the automated  record -
keeping in Guardrail 9.  Recordkeeping obligations in the EU AI Act are  both extensive and 
meticulously  specific .88 Guardrail 9 is phrased in very broad and general terms.  
 
It is unclear how external scrutiny will take place when the propos ed guardrails are unable 
to confirm that regulators or any external actor will be free to access the records collected 
under Guardrail 9. This is framed only as a possibility for the Government to consider .89 
 
Recommendation  17: Codify AI record keeping  requirements  
Ensure that e mployers using AI are required to keep detailed information about  the severity 
and frequency of challenges  being made to the system  by workers; how the system impacts 
occupational health and safety, workers’ rights, and the psychosocial work environment; 
and any incidents which cause harm to workers or the community. Regulators must have 
access to these records including breaches.  
 
Recommendation  18: Introduce stronger transparency rules for g eneral purpose AI 
Legislate stronger transparency and accountability measures for General Purpose Artificial 
Intelligence (GPAI) systems. Records about training for  GPAI models must be subject to 
regulatory oversight regardless of whether they are ‘state -of-the-art.’ GPAI models are at the 
frontier of AI capability and this should be reflected in more detailed regulatory obligations. 
Any adverse impacts of model drift must be reported to regulators and the public registry 
without undue delay.  
 
4.10 Guardrail 10: Conformity Assessments  
 
Guardrail 10 establishes a presumption of compliance  in Australian AI regulation.90 Given 
businesses ’ current  track record as evidenced  by the RAI, workers need more detailed and 
actionable regulation to limit possible AI abuses by employers.  
 
Guardrail 10 establishes a process for businesses to ‘show that they have adhered to the 
guardrails.’91 Conformity assessments established under this Guardrail would be used to  
‘verify whether organisations have met their legal obligations.’92 However, the propos ed 
 
