 Conclusion  
 
Australian  workers  do not trust the AI rollout because the AI rollout doesn’t deserve their 
trust.  Not often is the general will of the people expressed with such unanimity : 90% of 
Australians support the establishment of a new regulatory body for AI  and 6 6% support a 
total pause on worldwide AI development.  
 
Currently, t he proposed guardrails fail to provide the level of detail needed by Australian 
workers right now. Employers need to be held to account for their willingness to take up AI 
to undermine workers’  rights. The guardrails must provide more information and stronger 
accountability mechanisms to protect workers.  
 
Workers know what it’s like to  have a colleague die on the road  trying to meet unreasonable  
AI determined target s – to have a loved one driven to suicide by a welfare compliance 
algorithm – to face limitless and total  surveillance  for every  minute of working time.   
 
Decisions made today will set Australia  on a path that could last decades. It will take political 
courage  to ensure the ethical, safe and socially constructive use of this technology.  
 
Businesses are not taking action to minimise A I harms because they benefit, sometimes 
immensely, from committing them .  
 
Rights violations are motivated, not technical – they are a feature, not a bug.  
 
AI is a technology that owes its existence to the creative outputs of hundreds of millions of 
working people. It is trained on our words, languages, art, movements and ideas. Its core 
capabilities were developed at public universities using taxpayer money. AI doesn’t 
belong to business.  It belongs to all of us. It has obligations to all of us. It isn’t enough to 
regulate AI. We urge the Federal Government to steer it, fiercely, towards serving the 
working millions to whom it owes everything.  
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
    
 
