Recommendation 1: Legislate  a national  AI authority  
Introduce economy -wide legislation (Regulatory Approach 3) to establish a new regulator 
with the power to enforce compliance with the guardrails and any other AI regulation, via a 
National AI Authority. Risk identification and mitigation must be overseen by the regulator,  
to reverse the presumption of compliance and better protect workers.  
 
Recommendation 2: Mandate the employment context be considered high -risk by 
default  
Ensure all AI systems wherever they apply or have overlap with employment or industrial 
relations are deemed  high -risk. In keeping with the EU AI Act, workers and their 
representatives must be provided with information  before an employer can deploy AI.  
 
Recommendation 3: Workplace AI risk assessments must be mandatory where they 
relate to employment  
Introduce workplace AI risk assessments for all AI use where there is any relation to 
employment. The British Trade Union Congress’s model for AI risk assessments should be 
considered as a model for Australian workplace AI risk assessments.93 
 
Recommendation 4: Prohibit AI where it poses an unacceptable risk  
Legislate prohibitions on the use  of AI where it would  pose an unacceptable risk to workers, 
consumers and the community.  There should be a total ban on the development, 
deployment, sale, uptake, distribution or export of AI systems that are intended to:  
vi. Calculate  the emotions of a worker  in an employment, civic, public, or 
educational context;  
vii. Calculate , categorise, or sort workers  according to protected personal attributes . 
Trade Union membership should be considered as a protected attribute;  
viii. Defraud the public or cause substantial economic losses to groups or 
individuals;  
ix. Collect data or information about workers or work performance to develop or 
deploy an AI system that limits their ability to make informed decisions or 
changes their behaviour to harm themselves, others or the community;94 and 
x. Perform real -time or retrospective mass remote biometric identification in public 
spaces . Reasonable and proportionate exceptions to this prohibition should be 
included for critical instances involving national security, national defence, 
counterterrorism and other matters pertaining to public order .  
 
 
 
 
