RE: Response to the Introducing mandatory guardails for AI in high -risk settings : proposals paper  
Amazon Commercial Services Pty Ltd ( Amazon Australia ) and Amazon Web Services Australia Pty Ltd 
(AWS ) (collectively, Amazon ) appreciates the opportunity  to provide this submission  to the Department 
of Industry, Science and Resources ( DISR ) in respons e to the Introducing mandatory guardrails for AI in 
high- risk settings: proposals paper  (Proposals Paper ). Amazon’s perspectives on artificial intelligence ( AI) 
are informed by our dual role as a developer of AI technology and a deployer of AI tools and services. Our 
focus on AI and machine learning ( ML) spans over 25 years, and ML drives many of the capabilities 
customers use when they interact with Amazon.  
AI and ML are some of the most transformational technologies of our time, and at Amazon, we believe it 
is still early days in their development and application. The advent of large language models ( LLMs ) has 
unlocked new opportunities for generative AI to improve customer experiences, boost employee productivity, drive economic growth, and more. AI has the potential to tackle some of humanity’s most pressing issues, and the uptake of AI can provide unparalleled opportunities to be a force for social good, and to drive significant economic growth and productivity benefits, both in Australia and beyond.  
While there is widespread recognition that AI has the potential to reshape the economy and improve modern life, recent advances have also prompted questions about how to manage the risks of this technology. To that end, Australia will only be able to unlock AI’s full potential by fostering greater confidence among consumers and businesses. That means addressing potential risks and harms arising from the adoption of AI technologies, and earning and maintaining public trust in AI systems. In order to 
truly harness the opportunities AI offers, it is critical that the technology is safe, secure and trustworthy.  
Amazon is in Australia for the long haul. Since the launch of our AWS Asia Pacific (Sydney) region in 2012, we have built our business locally, and Amazon Australia and AWS together now employ more than 7,000  
people across Australia. We plan to invest $13.2 billion into Australia from 2023 to 2027. This economic activity will contribute an estimated $35 billion to Australia’s total GDP by 2027, and support an estimated annual average of 11,000 full- time equivalent jobs at local vendors. We will continue to invest in Australia 
and Australians. 
Thank you for the opportunity to provide our comments. We look forward to working with the 
Department  to create  a safe and prosperous digital future for all Australians.  
Kind regards  
 
Roger Somerville  
Head of Public Policy, ANZ  
Amazon Web Services Australia  
    
 
2 
 
1. Amazon supports  international initiatives to mitigate  AI risks  
Amazon is committed to harnessing AI responsibly —working hand -in-hand with our customers and 
governments around the world to develop and use AI systems with safety, fairness, and security at the 
forefront. We support governments’ efforts to put in place effective risk -based regulatory frameworks 
and guardrails, while also allowing for continued innovation and practical application of the technology.  
Internationally, we have endorsed the White House Voluntary AI Commitments, which are built on a 
forward -looking set of best practices that are flexible and durable enough to evolve along with the 
technology. The commitments set out ambitious and concrete objectives for managing many of the 
unique risks of generative AI and for building trust with the public. While Amazon signed the commitments 
in the US, our efforts to operationalise them are not territorially limited. We also continue to support and actively engage with other international efforts,  such as the AI Safety Summit, the Tech Accord to Combat  
Deceptive Use of AI in 2024 Elections, and the G7 Hiroshima process, to build consensus on core definitions and principles.  
Consensus -based international technical standards  and accompanying OECD gui dance  will be crucial to 
establishing consistent responsible AI norms and expectations  across the world. In addition to their 
traditional role of facilitating interoperability at a technical level, standards increasingly play a fundamental role in promoting interoperability between regulatory systems. Standards provide a single source of ground truth that helps companies translate regulatory requirements into technical 
specifications that can be engineered into systems. Global standards can  reduce  regulatory fragmentation 
and facilitate interoperability as companies seek to build and deploy solutions in line with best practices. 
Technical standards also enhance trust in AI because they are inherently more agile than regulations , and 
can more readily be adapted to address the fast pace of technological innovation. Amazon is actively engaged with organisations and standards bodies focused on the responsible development of next -
generation AI systems including the International Organization for Standard ization ( ISO) and the National 
Institute of Standards and Technology ( NIST ). As we note later on in our submission, we continue to 
encourage the Australian Government to align any mandatory guardrails with ISO/IEC 42001:2023 
Artificial Intelligence Management System , and recommend  the Government encourage the voluntary  
adoption of the NIST AI Risk Management Framework, NIST AI 100 -1. 
We are also pleased  to have joined the US Artificial Intelligence Institute Consortium
, established by the 
NIST, as part of our efforts to further government and industry collaboration to advance saf e and secure 
AI. Amazon is collaborating with NIST in the Artificial Intelligence Safety Institute Consortium to establish a new measurement science that will enable the identification of proven, scalable, and interoperable measurements and methodologies to promote development of trustworthy AI and its responsible use. Along with important ISO standards and the contributions of other AI institutes, this work will continue to set an interoperable and trusted foundation for the development and deployment of r esponsible AI.  
2. Amazon’s approach to responsible AI  
Developers and deployers of AI systems should ensure such systems are built based on principles of safety and responsibility by design. Amazon builds AI with responsibility in mind at each stage of our 
com prehensive development process. What constitutes responsible AI is continually evolving. At Amazon, 
we believe the design, development, and deployment of AI must respect the rule of law, human rights, 
and values of equity, privacy, and fairness. We conside r responsible AI across eight key dimensions:  
• Fairness and bias : How a system impacts different subpopulations of users (e.g. by gender  and 
ethnicity);      
 
3 
 
• Explainability : Mechanisms to understand and evaluate the outputs of an AI system;  
• Privacy and Security : Data used in accordance with privacy considerations and protected from 
theft and exposure;  
• Robustness and veracity : Mechanisms to achieve correct system outputs, even with unexpected 
or adversarial input;  
• Governance : Processes to define, implement and enforce responsible AI practices within an 
organisation;  
• Transparency : Communicating information about an AI system so stakeholders can make 
informed choices about their use of the system;  
• Controllability : Mechanisms to monitor and steer AI syste m behavior; and  
• Safety : Preventing harmful system output and misuse.  
As the science and engineering of responsible AI matures, we are committed to continually enhancing the 
robustness of these safeguards, and to providing customers with tools and resources to develop and use AI responsibly. In short, we help our customers transform responsible AI from theory into practice. Responsible use of AI technologies is key to fostering continued innovation, and Amazon is committed to developing fair and accurate  AI services. We believe government should encourage others to do the same, 
and should also encourage the use of trusted AI developers. Our updated approach to responsible AI can be observed in the below examples:  
• Our AI S ervice Cards : AI Service Cards
 are a form of responsible AI documentation that provide 
customers with a single place to find information on the intended use cases and limitations, responsible AI design  choices, and deployment and performance optimisation best practices for 
our AI services. The Amazon Titan Text Service Card was the first of our service cards to reflect our expanded approach, providing key transparency information about how the LLMs have  been 
evaluated for veracity (i.e. the likelihood of hallucination), safety (including our efforts to red team the model), and controllability. We have since launched AI Service Cards for other services.  
• E
mpowering our customers to evaluate and manage risks and responsibilities  associated with 
their deployment of generative AI and the underlying F oundation Models  (FMs ) by providing them 
with a range of resources and tools. For example, in November 2023 we introduced a new service, 
which Amazon customers can use to evaluate the performance of FMs against responsible AI 
benchmarks and implement safeguards that are aligned to their responsible AI policies, Amazon 
Bedrock Guardrails . 
• Watermarking : All images generated by Amazon's recently announced Titan Image Generator  will 
contain an invisible watermark that can be validated by an application programming interface (API). Built -in watermarking is designed to help reduce the spread of deceptive content and 
disinformation by providing mechanisms to identify AI -generated images. By making the image 
validation API publicly available, our aim is to make it easier for organisations to quickly assess 
whether an image has been generated or augmented by Titan Image Generator. AWS is among 
the first model providers to widely release built -in invisible watermarks that are integrated into 
the image outputs and designed to be resistant to alteration. To further mitigate the risk that Titan Image Gen erator might be misused for deepfakes or other deceptive content, we have 
implemented a mix of training data and input filters to prevent the generation of images of real individuals.     
 
4 
 
• Filter on user inputs and model outputs : We apply  filters on user inputs  and model outputs on 
the Amazon Bedrock service (which offers our Titan FMs) to address a range of potential risks, 
including the potential for our models to produce harmful outputs. Our classifier algorithm processes and classifies model inputs and outpu ts, and allows us to filter harmful content, 
automate abuse detection, and identify patterns of misuse and reoccurring behaviour. For example, Amazon Bedrock Model Evaluation offers customers model evaluation tools, and with Amazon Bedrock Guardrails, cust omers are able to implement safeguards across their FMs to help 
deliver more consistent and safe user experiences aligned with their company policies and principles. Services like Amazon SageMaker
 incorporate tools that help customers identify and 
mitigate bias, explain predictions, and continuously monitor system performance to identify new risks of bias.  
3. Managing the development and deployment of high- risk AI  
We welcome the Australian Government’s proposed approach to limit any mandatory guardrails to high -
risk use cases.  The benefits, harms, and policy considerations around different applications of AI vary greatly – for example, there are very different issues around diagnosing a health  condition  compared to  
forecasting supply chains , though both may use AI techniques. Developers and deployers of AI should not 
be required to conduct compliance activities associated with low -risk use cases, like background blurring 
on video calls, autocorrect , and  email spam filters . Requiring businesses to conduct compliance activities 
for such low -risk technology could substantially  reduce their productivity , while not providing a 
meaningful benefit to cons umers, who should expect that business activities are performed using well -
accepted, widely used technology.  
Given the nearly infinite range of uses of AI systems, a generic, one -size-fits-all approach to regulation is 
impractical and could prevent or s low access to  and adoption of  useful technology.  Instead, we 
recommend the Australian Government ensure s that any mandatory guardrails take a risk-based 
approach that targets harms raised by specific applications of AI systems in high -risk use cases. Propo sed 
mandatory guardrails should focus on precisely  defined use cases (rather than high -level principles that 
lack clarity and certainty ) to enable an efficient development process.  Well -defined  high -risk use cases 
provide dev elopers and deployers with certainty. This approach can be adapted over time to take into 
account new high -risk applications of AI, and any identified, emerging harms.   
In relation to g eneral purpose AI ( GPAI ), GPAI systems have many low -risk, socially beneficial uses. For 
example, GPAI can be used to create subtitles on live video feeds to improve accessibility for hearing -
impaired listeners, or to read out text or describe video feeds for visually -impaired user s. If the Australian 
Government is concerned about unique risks associated with highly capable foundation models or generative AI, voluntary commitments like the White House  Commitments  and G7 Hiroshima process 
address these risks while best practices from  a technical perspective continue to emerge.  
We encourage the Australian Government to continue contributing to the work being done through 
international fora, and caution against inflexible regulation at this time.  Specifically, we urge the 
Government to take a risk -based approach to AI rather than imposing blanket regulation . We note that 
should a GPAI model be integrated into a high -risk system and used in a high -risk way, it would be subject 
to any mandatory guardrails that apply to high -risk use cases. This is an appropriate and proportionate 
approach. However, if the Australian Government intends to classify some types of GPAI systems  under a 
definition of “high -risk” , it should consider defining highly capable foundation models based on a 
combination of factors, including both  the capabilities of the model and the model size (that is, models 
trained using more than 10 ^26 FLOPs  of compute power) .     
 
5 
 
4. The Governmen t’s proposed mandatory guardrails for high- risk AI use cases  
As AI is increasingly used to automate decisions that have significant impacts on people’s lives, health, 
and safety, we recognise that government has an important role to play in promoting innovation and safeguarding the public. To that end, Amazon  suppor ts the Australian Government’s efforts to put in place 
effective risk -based guardrails for AI and ML that protect human rights while also allowing for continued 
innovation and practical application of the technology.   
When AI is being used in high -risk ways – e.g., to automate decisions that will meaningfully impact a 
person’s health, safety or eligibility for important life opportunities –  there should be a baseline 
expectation that organisations have implemented appropriate governance -based safeguards to identify 
and mitigate the risks such systems may pose.  
The deployer and developer distinction  
As the Proposal Paper acknowledges , the AI value chain is complex, with a single system often involving 
multiple developers, vendors, deployers, and users.  Regulations should ensure that obligations are 
assigned to the appropriate actor who is best positioned to mitigate risk. To that end, we support the Government’s intentions to clarify the roles and specific o bligations for each organisation in that AI supply  
chain.  
The guardrails as currently framed do not m ake the distinctions between each actor sufficiently clear. We 
recommend clearly defining roles  for each specific guardrail— that is , AI developers (i.e. those who 
develop AI systems ), deployers (i.e. those who implement an AI system for high -risk use or who deploy 
the final system to end -consumers), and end users (i.e. consumers or those using AI for personal use).  
Deployers of AI systems are best positioned to identify risks that are specific to the use case or application, 
and to identify appropriate mitigations. As an example, in manufacturing and other software development, 
such deployers can require that developers and others within their supply chain make contractual commitments  to demonstrate compliance for what they control or otherwise assist them with compliance.  
The importance of interoperability and standards  
Because governments around the world are grappling with many of the same policy challenges, it is vital that any mandatory guardrails proposed by the Australian Government align around interoperable , policy 
solutions to the greatest extent possible. As we previously raised in our submission  to the Government’s 
Safe and Responsible AI  consultation , standards play a key role in ensuring a policy environment that 
opens the way for the deployment of trustworthy AI systems.  
International standards, such as those se t forth by NIST or ISO, play an important role so that the many 
players across the AI supply chain don’t end up with a patchwork of local, conflicting regulations. Such an 
outcome would create regulatory uncertainty and unnecessary compliance costs, underm ining the 
potential benefits of innovation . 
Amazon  is actively engaged with organisations and standard s bodies focused on the responsible 
development of next -generation AI systems , including NIST and ISO. As noted earlier, we recommend that 
the Government ensure s that any proposed regulatory requirements are interoperable with  standards 
like ISO/IEC  42001 , which provide a n effective  framework for developers and deployers to manage risk as 
appropriate for their business operations .  
While global standards on AI remain nascent, ISO reached an important milestone in December 2023 with 
the publication  of ISO 42001, a management system standard that provides guidelines for managing AI 
systems within organisations. ISO 42001 emphasises a commitment to responsible AI practices,     
 
6 
 
encouraging organisations to adopt controls specific to their AI systems, fostering global interoperability, 
and setting a foundation for the development and deployment of responsible AI. We believe that ISO 42001 certification will be one important mechanism for organisations to demonstrate excellence in responsibly developing and deploying AI systems and applications . Amazon is continuing to pursue ISO 
42001 conformity where appropriate for its businesses, and  commend the Government ’s intention  to 
recognise this standard as one mechanism for effective  AI governance in Australia and internationally.   
The Australian Government should also encourage the adoption of NIST’s AI Risk Management Framework  
(AI RMF ), a voluntary resource designed to promote the trustworthy and responsible development and 
use of AI systems.  NIST is well recognised in Australia for producing actionable, risk -based frameworks. 
Notably, alignment to the NIST Framework for Improving Critical Infrastructure Cybersecurity has already been adopted by the Security of Critical Infrastructure Act’s  Risk Management Program as demonstrating 
compliance with the subsection on managing cyber and information security hazards .  
The culmination of a multi- year effort that involved clo se consultation with industry and civil society, the 
AI RMF is a critical resource for developers and deployers of AI. By establishing a shared conceptual framework for identifying and mitigating risks throughout the AI system lifecycle, the AI RMF provide s a 
common reference point for stakeholders involved in the development and deployment of an AI system to communicate risk. The AI RMF is particularly useful for organizations like AWS that provide tools and services to actors across the AI value chain. For that reason, we are committed to  aligning our internal AI 
governance processes across AWS’s  portfolio of managed AI services to the AI RMF . We are also actively 
developing tools and resources to help our customers do the same.  
The Australian Government’s  proposed mandatory guardrails  
As noted earlier, Amazon  support s the Australian Government’s proposals  to adopt an  effective risk -based 
AI governance framework. We believe that framework could include efforts by  developers of high -risk AI 
systems to evaluate and mitigate potential risks . Developers could also provide relevant  information  to 
their customers  about these risks  – through mechanisms such  as AI Service Cards – so that customers can 
make informed decisions about how to deploy the system in a responsible way. Similarly, e ntities that 
deploy high -risk AI s ystems are best positioned to identify risks that are specific to their use case or 
application. Therefore, prior to deploying for a high -risk use case, organisations should perform impact 
assessments to ensure that they have accounted for the unique risks  the system may pose to members of 
the public. We believe baseline AI legislation that ensures organisations are adopting these sorts of governance -based safeguards will complement any sector- specific rules that may be required.   
To the extent that the  Australian Government incorporates additional risk -based AI governance practices , 
we offer the following comments on some  of the  specific mandatory guardrails as outlined in the 
Proposals Paper.  
a) Proportionality and flexibility 
As outlined in our recent submission to the Senate Select Committee on Adopting AI, the Government has 
a unique opportunity to promote Australia’s leadership in AI/ML and support innovation as an economic accelerant, built upon a strong foundation of responsible AI measures.  A growi ng body of empirical 
evidence now points to a potentially significant productivity uplift from generative AI. Earlier this year, the Australian Productivity  Commission found
 that AI “has the potential to address some of Australia’s 
most prominent and enduring productivity challenges”, by using this technology to fill skill and labour gaps, identify risks, improve research and development  capabilities, accelerate innovation, increase     
 
7 
 
quality, and improve outcomes – especially in publicly provided services such as healthcare, education, 
disability, and aged care (which traditionally exhibit low productivity growth despite accounting for a 
significant proportion of the Australian economy).  
 With this in mind,  we encourage Government to consider a sensible, proportionate regulatory approach 
that drives the adoption of AI governance best practices, instead of technical over prescriptiveness , in 
order to incentivize  and support the uptake of AI more broadly across the economy. Creating overly 
burdensome, complex obligations on industry may have the unintended consequence of creating barriers  
to development, innovation  and adoption of AI in Australia. For example, medium and small businesses 
that otherwise seek to benefit from substantial productivity gains
 from adopting AI and other emerging 
technologies  may be deterred from doing so, on the basis their regulatory obligations appear too 
convoluted, unclear or otherwise uncertain.  It seems impossible to think that any progress will be made 
addressing Australia’s greatest challenges – transitioning to net -zero, an escalating health and ageing 
imperati ve, and turning around Australia’s productivity performance – without our businesses, 
governments, academia and wider population embracing the technology.  
 
b) Fostering public trust  and confidence  in AI systems  
While we are in favour of baseline expectatio ns that organisations have appropriate governance -based 
safeguards in place to identify and mitigate risks , we caution against requiring developers and/or 
deployers to publish the details of their accountability frameworks as proposed per Guardrail 1. T o earn 
public confidence in AI, businesses must demonstrate they can confidently mitigate the potential risks of high -risk AI, as well as being provided with the right information to assess those risks. However,  the 
obligations under this  guardrail may require companies to disclose proprietary, confidential information 
related to development and/or use of their AI systems . Further , releasing details of an organisation’s 
internal accountability processes are unlikely to help build greater confidence am ong consumers and 
businesses. Displaying to the public that these sorts of high -risk systems are safe, fair, appropriately 
transparent, privacy protective and subject to appropriate oversight would be better achieved through 
developer documentation such  as AI Service Cards  and deployer impact assessments , so that customers 
can make informed decisions about how to deploy the system in a responsible way.  
To that end, Amazon supports an obligation that organisations should conduct an impact assessment 
befor e deploying a high- risk AI system. We welcome Guardrail 2’s proposed alignment with ISO/SE 42001, 
which outlines a methodology for conducting AI impact assessments.  
We suggest the Government reconsider Guardrail 6, specifically the requirement to inform end -users on 
how AI is being used and where it affects them. Many individual interactions with AI systems do not pose 
any risk of harm or deception and disclosure may therefore be unnecessary, such as AI -generated 
customer service phone lines . In circumstances where there may be such a risk, businesses are best 
positioned to assess and mitigate that risk in light of the specific context, in line with existing laws and regulations that apply to deceptive acts or practices.  When it comes to  publicly available AI -generated 
audiovisual content, we support an obligation for content to include indications that enable 
understanding that it was generated through AI, where there is a risk of significant harm from deception 
about the origin of that content .  
c) Ensuring appropriate roles and res ponsibilities for each guardrail  
We agree that human oversight (per Guardrail 5) is one potential safeguard to mitigate against risks associated with high -risk use cases. However, it may not be appropriate in all cases – for example, 
requiring a human to b e in the loop before an autonomous vehicle breaks in an emergency would be     
 
8 
 
problematic . As such, we reiterate the need for the Government to clearly outline who in the AI value 
chain is responsible for compliance with each obligation.  
In terms of Guardrail 9, unlike cybersecurity and privacy, we note there is not currently robust 
infrastructure for third -party independent audits in AI/ML . Encouraging businesses to consider third party 
independent audits when needed , and also allowing for robust internal, independent audits and controls, 
particularly at this stage, would strike an appropriate balance between providing meaningful safeguards 
while encouraging businesses innovation.  
d) Balancing transparency and protecting intellectual property and security  
In regards to Guardrail 3, requirements to disclose training data are not the most effective ways to 
evaluate either the accuracy or fairness of a model’s performance across relevant demographic groups . 
Instead, testing  a model’s outputs  is the scientifically appropriate and industry -standard mechanism that 
provides the best indication of performance  or lack of bias . For example, even if a deployer has access to 
information that a model was trained on dat a across a variety of languages, including English, Mandarin, 
Hindi, Spanish, French, in order to assess how well the model performs in transcribing Mandarin in a particular application, the deployer must test the model.  
Further, a developer’s compilation of training data can be highly commercially confidential with significant 
competitive value. Requirement s that developers to disclose information about the data  used for training  
should be balanced against the need for  businesses to  protect proprietary information, such as that which 
is subject to intellectual property or confidentiality protection.  
The same ap plies to Guardrail 8. We support obligations for the developers of high -risk AI systems to 
provide deployers  with  the appropriate documentation, i.e.:  outlining the capabilities of that AI system; 
its known material limitations at the time of development o f the system; guidelines for intended use ; and 
example performance results.  However, for the aforementioned reasons, this Guardrail should not 
require disclosure of training data or any information that  could create a trade secrets or security risk .  
e) Recog nising the sufficiency of existing applicable legislation 
We ap preciate that the Proposal Paper  has recogni sed the array of separate  legislation  and reform 
processes  operating alongside  these proposed obligations, including the Privacy Act 1988 , the Copyright 
Act 1968,  and the Security of Critical Infrastructure Act 2018 . To that end, Amazon recommends that topics 
such as privacy, copyright, security  and competition  are most appropriately addressed by legislation 
focusing on those topics.  Where existi ng laws are insufficient to address the application of AI, lawmakers 
should amend existing applicable requirements, rather than creating a separate framework for AI.  This is 
also true of Guardrail 7, which we would encourage Government to remove. This i s because , as the Paper 
acknowledges, numerous complaint handling, appeals processes  and other regulatory oversight 
mechanisms  already exist under Australian Consumer Law and administrative law , for example , which 
provide  people impacted by high -risk AI systems  with  opportunities  to ex ercise their legal rights . 
 
5. The regulatory approach to implement mandatory guardrails   
As we recommended  in our previous submission , Government has the opportunity to establish A ustralia 
as a leader in the development of AI that is safe, responsible, and effective. Accomplishing this requires 
the articulation of a comprehensive, pro -innovation policy framework that underscores the key role that 
technology -neutral consumer protecti ons and sector -specific regulations will continue to play.
     
 
9 
 
Put into practice effectively, responsible AI should have the dual effect of increasing the uptake of AI by 
increasing confidence in the technology, and enhancing public trust by reducing risk a nd minimising 
possible harms. To achieve this, it’s important that any regulatory framework recognises that AI is a general- purpose technology that can be put to an extraordinarily broad range of uses. Efforts to devise a 
comprehensive, one -size-fits-all regulatory framework are unlikely to be effective and fail to capitalise on 
robust legal frameworks already in place. Rather than pursuing a prescriptive “horizontal” approach to AI 
regulation, where all sectors and use cases are treated along similar lines  (which draws parallels with the 
Proposal Paper’s Option 3) , Government should instead focus on ensuring that existing technology -
neutral laws and sector- specific regulations remain fit -for-purpose in an era when AI will increasingly be 
used.  
 
Organisations develop and deploy AI systems against the backdrop of an array of existing laws and regulatory requirements that help protect the public against potential unintended consequences.  There 
will certainly be instances where the use of AI will create n ovel questions that will require adjustments to 
existing laws and regulations.  For example, the Therapeutic Goods Administration provides clear guidance 
on software -based medical devices and helps prospective deployers understand whether their product is 
regulated while maintaining clinical safeguards, with more stringent requirements for higher risk products.  
In the UK, the Centre for Connected and Autonomous Vehicles has produced guidance designed to 
articulate and realise the societal benefits of connected and automated mobility while ensuring safety 
and security; the result has been the UK becoming the first jurisdiction to approve a hands -free self- driving 
system.  
Amazon is of the view that sector -specific regulation works well as it allows re gulators who understand 
the use case  to make informed determinations about the risk of using AI for specific purposes, rather than 
attempting to introduce catch -all, blanket  regulation.  Sectoral regulators, whether in financial services, 
transportation, health or others, are closest to their industries and are familiar with the ways in  which 
they are already using AI and will be using AI in the future. Leaving regulators with strong knowledge of 
