1. Do the proposed principles adequately capture high-risk AI?
Yes
Are there any principles we should add or remove?
No
Please identify any:
The element of legal - where individuals cannot opt out of being subject to AI - is a component of the work we do in supporting industry manage financial crime. For example, a financial services business will use AI to monitor transactions to identify criminal activity eg child exploitation, money laundering. It is not possible for a customer (personal or business) to opt out of having transactions monitored.
Our consultation would be this is not a high risk AI application but a high-impact (crime reduction) use of AI. It will however not provide a person or entity the ability to opt out. We would propose that use of AI in these types of circumstances require a level
Of compliance and disclosure for entities like SymphonyAI in the spirit of the AI framework but balanced with the community benefit of the AI application purpose.
We would recommend a modified ‘compliance’ process for a business like our involved in financial crime prevention and using AI.
Alternatively, the use of AI to detect financial crime could be excluded from the scope of high risk AI.
2. Do you have any suggestions for how the principles could better capture harms to First Nations people, communities and Country?
No
3. Do the proposed principles, supported by examples, give enough clarity and certainty on high-risk AI settings and high-risk AI models? Is a more defined approach, with a list of illustrative uses, needed?
YES the principles give enough clarity and certainty
4. Are there high-risk use cases that government should consider banning in its regulatory response (for example, where there is an unacceptable level of risk)?
No
5. Are the proposed principles flexible enough to capture new and emerging forms of high-risk AI, such as general-purpose AI (GPAI)?
Yes
Please provide any additional comments.
This answer is on the basis a National AI principles based legislation is adopted.
6. Should mandatory guardrails apply to all GPAI models?
No
Please provide any additional comments.
As per the answer to question 1, there could be scope exclusions or reduced compliance for AI in the use of AI in financial crime.
7. What are suitable indicators for defining GPAI models as high-risk?
Define high-risk against the principles
8. Do the proposed mandatory guardrails appropriately mitigate the risks of AI used in high-risk settings?
Yes
Are there any guardrails that we should add or remove?
No
10. Do the proposed mandatory guardrails distribute responsibility across the AI supply chain and throughout the AI lifecycle appropriately?
Yes
11. Are the proposed mandatory guardrails sufficient to address the risks of GPAI?
Yes
12. Do you have suggestions for reducing the regulatory burden on small-to-medium sized businesses applying guardrails?
Yes
Please provide any additional comments
Consistent with previous responses, relief for compliance with guidelines could be considered where a high risk AI trigger is in place and a firm is using AI to help other entities meet regulatory obligations - including financial crime / AML.
13. Which legislative option do you feel will best address the use of AI in high-risk settings?
A whole of economy approach – introducing a new cross-economy AI Act
What opportunities should the Government take into account in considering each approach? 
Having a National approach provides two key opportunities :
1. Easier to keep pace with rapid change in AI with one overarching piece of legislation
2. Regulators and other agencies can provide domain guidance to their regulated population on applying AI to their industry eg how to apply AI in meeting financial crime obligations (Hong Kong and the Monetary Authority have some examples of this approach).
14. Are there any additional limitations of options outlined in this section which the Australian Government should consider?
No
15. Which regulatory option(s) will best ensure that guardrails for high-risk AI can adapt and respond to step-changes in technology?
A domain specific approach – Adopting the guardrails within existing regulatory frameworks as needed
16. Where do you see the greatest risks of gaps and inconsistencies with Australia’s existing laws for the development and deployment of AI?
There are gaps that relate to meeting regulatory obligations including risk amazement frameworks as they relate to identifying risks and applying controls - this is not unique to financial crime / AML but is a good example where guidance and change may be required.
Which regulatory option best addresses this?
A domain specific approach – Adopting the guardrails within existing regulatory frameworks as needed
Make a general comment
At SymphonyAI, we take a fundamentally different approach to AI, where we tailor predictive and generative AI applications address core business challenges to deliver rapid, real, relevant results. Our group works across retail, industrial, enterprise IT, media and financial services – we have a clear understanding of the opportunity for businesses to adopt and transform with genAI.
SymphonyAI provides software-as-a-service (SAAS) solution for financial crime prevention with a focus on expediting efficient investigation by uniting predictive and generative AI with enterprise-grade technology. Our solutions for financial crime prevention support banking, gatekeeper, payment, insurance, wealth and gaming businesses manage their operational risks through:
• transaction monitoring – to uncover hidden risk and reduce false positives,
• customer due diligence – to enable onboarding, risk-scoring and name screening,
• payment fraud – to empower fraud detection and analytics,
• name and transaction screening – an advanced watchlist screening in real-time,
• sanctions – screening on high-risk transactions, individuals and entities, and
• enterprise investigations management – and end-to-end platform.
Our augmentation to these core technology-enabled financial crime capabilities through artificial intelligence occurs through Generative (genAI) and Predictive AI – mostly working together to transform the efficiency, effectiveness and overall approach of financial crime risk management. As examples,
• genAI is applied through our Sensa Investigation Hub and Sensa Copilot that centralise the view of enterprise risk and accelerate investigations.
o To do this, we partner with Microsoft to apply CoPilot and develop large language models (LLMs) to assist financial crime investigators efficiently source, summarise, and analyse data at scale and empower them to make critical decisions about risk,
• genAI and predictive AI combine through SensaAI for Sanctions to improve detection rates and maintain true positive matches in sanction screening, and
• our SensaAI for AML uses predictive AI to optimise transaction monitoring systems and augment rule-based detection with data-led models to detect financial crime risk.
Our approach is to disrupt the criminal misuse of financial services to move illicit funds and redress the current imbalance of costs to industry to comply with global standards. As an outcome, we aim to help industry and government address the current gap in stopping and seizing criminal funds in the global financial system.

