4 October 2024  
 
Department of Industry, Science and Resources  
By Web : https://consult.industry.gov.au/ai -mandatory -guardrails  
About us  
We are a multi -disciplinary group of academics located at UNSW, The University of 
Queensland (UQ) and The University of Melbourne (UMelb) working together on the socio -technical aspects of Enterprise AI  (enterpriseai.org.au) . We welcome the 
government’s work on identifying AI guardrails as a critical component in ensuring that safe and responsible AI thrives in Australia. Our expertise is as follows:  
• Professor Lyria Bennett Moses, UNSW Sydney –  legal responses to 
technological change  
• Professor Shazia Sadiq, UQ –  responsible data management  
• Professor Nicole Gillespie, UMelb –  trust in AI, trustworthy use of AI, public 
attitudes towards A I. 
• Professor Tim Miller, UQ –  trust & trustworthiness in AI, human- centred AI, 
explainability and accountability.  
• Professor Ben Rubinstein, UMelb –  security and privacy in AI  
• Associate Professor Olga Ohrimenko, UMelb –  security and privacy  
About this Submission  
We are grateful for the opportunity to make a submission in response to the Department’s Proposals paper  dated September 2024. Our submission reflects our 
views as researchers; they are not an institutional position of our universities. This submission can be made public.   We have focussed on the issues on which we have research expertise. Our main points relate to:  
• The difficulty of defining AI in such a way as to make it, as a category, an 
appropriate scope for defining the boundaries of legal requirements. (“Defining AI”)  
• Articulation of the guardrails; some of these should be technology -specific (in 
that they relate specifically to artificial intelligence) while others can be crafted more broadly. (“Question 1”)  
• Specific comments on particular guardrails and how they might be modified (“Question 8”)  
• Some general and specific suggestions on regulatory burden (“Question 12”)  
• Our recommendation that the government adopt Option 1, possibly combined with Option 2, rather than Option 3 (“Question 13”)  
• We also offer a counter -narrative to the risk -based framing.  
Deﬁning AI  
Defining AI as a regulatory object is an almost impossible task. While it can be 
defined as an area of study or research, and while people often have an intuitive sense of what it involves, a definition for the purposes of defining the scope of  
legislative requirements requires a more stable object as well as better alignment 
between statutory purpose and the technology concerned.1 We prohibit human 
reproductive cloning because our concern lies with the technological means rather than with the result (which includes natural -born identical twins). The case for 
technology -specific laws for AI lacks a similar alignment of purpose and t he definition 
is unstable.  
 
All agencies and governments that have attempted to define AI for regulatory 
purposes have struggled. Consider the fundamental changes in the definition of AI system offered by the OECD (currently used in the Australian proposals paper) within four years:  
 
Original definition: An AI system is a machine- based system that can, for a 
given set of human defined objectives, make predictions, recommendations, or decisions influencing real or virtual environments. AI systems are designed to operate with varying lev els of autonomy.  
 
Revised definition: An AI system is a machine- based system that, for explicit 
or implicit objectives, infers, from the input it receives, how to generate 
outputs such as predictions, content, recommendations or decisions that can influence physical or virt ual environments. Different AI systems vary in their 
level of autonomy and adaptiveness after deployment.  
 Content has been added to cover generative AI that was not considered worthy of 
mention in 2019. The word “infers”, not in the original definition, is now at its core.  
 The changes in the OECD’s definition have been incorporated into the enacted definition in the European Union’s (EU) AI Act:  
 
‘AI system’ is a machine- based system designed to operate with varying 
levels of autonomy and that may exhibit adaptiveness after deployment and that, for explicit or implicit objectives, infers, from the input it receives, how to generate outputs such as predictions, content, recommendations, or decisions that can influence physical or virtual environments  
 If you remove the optional elements (“varying levels”, “may”, “can … or”) from the EU definition, one is left with AI being systems that infer  how to generate outputs from 
inputs. Yet this word does not necessarily help organisations or regulators classify 
different systems as in or out of scope.  
 
The US Executive Order on the safe, secure and trustworthy development and use of AI, has a definition that focuses on different concepts, absent from the EU and 
proposed Australian definitions:  
 
a machine- based system that can, for a given set of human- defined 
objectives, make predictions, recommendations, or decisions influencing real or virtual environments. Artificial intelligence systems use machine-  and 
 
1 Lyria Bennett Moses, ‘How to Think about Law, Regulation and Technology: Problems with “Technology” as 
a Regulatory Target’ (2013) 5 Law, Innovation and Technology 1; Lyria Bennett Moses, ‘Regulating in the Face 
of Sociotechnical Change’ in Roger Brownswor d, Eloise Scotford and Karen Yeung (eds), The Oxford 
Handbook of Law, Regulation and Technology  (Oxford University Press 2017).   
human- based inputs to perceive real and virtual environments; abstract such 
perceptions into models through analysis in an automated manner; and use 
model inference to formulate options for information or action.  
 In the US, “content” continues to remain absent, while concepts such as abstraction and models are treated as core to the concept of AI.  
 Our point here is not that one definition should be preferred to another, it is that there is no stable definition of AI that could form the basis for the scope of legal obligations.  We do not yet fully understand that which is still evolving.  
 A useful historical analogy is an earlier government’s response to digital tapes. Australia, like many countries, passed a series of laws that was designed to solve the “digital tape problem”, being the ability to make perfect copies of copyright music.
2 Of course, these laws did not solve the problems of copyright and digital files 
because the laws passed were explicitly about digital tapes . By attempting to define 
AI and pass laws about a developing phenomenon, we are making a similar mistake in believing we sufficiently understand the technological driver of a legal problem. “Define and regulate” is rarely a useful approach in the context of rapidly -changing 
technologies.  
 Not only is AI difficult to define at this stage of technological development, there is a poor alignment between the technology itself and the identified risks. This can be observed by looking at the list in the proposals paper about what is different about AI and the risks it generates (pp 11- 15). Although the document acknowledges that 
many of these are associated only with particular types of AI (such as advanced AI models) or are associated with features that are not unique to AI (such as autonomy), a l egal response directed at AI as a category (as per Option 3) is a 
poorly targeted response to the identified risks.  
 
1. Autonomy  is neither necessary nor sufficient for a system to be “artificial 
intelligence”; Robodebt is one of the most egregious examples of allowing systems to make decisions autonomously and yet is not “artificial intelligence”. It was merely a few lines of program code that implemented a human-designed rule. Indeed, that is how the then government could launch its AI ethical principles at the same time as it continued to pursue Robodebt.
3 Thus 
laws created to manage concerns around autonomy should be directed at, for example, autonomous decision- making and not AI.  
 
2. General cognitive abilities , in the sense of exhibiting behaviour that, in 
humans, would require general cognitive abilities, includes basic technology, such as calculators, that fall outside the definition of AI. After all, when doing long division, humans use their “general cognit ive capabilities”. Further, 
“cognitive abilities” are not part of the proposed definition of AI, only inference.  
 
3. Adaptability and learning is an important feature of machine learning 
approaches, but is not core to the definition of AI systems. To the extent that 
 
2 For a range of similar examples, see Lyria Bennett Moses, ‘Sui Generis Rules’ in Gary E Marchant, Braden R 
Allenby and Joseph R Herkert (eds), The Growing Gap Between Emerging Technologies and Legal -Ethical 
Oversight: The Pacing Problem (Springer Netherlands 2011) <https://doi.org/10.1007/978- 94-007-1356- 7_6>.  
3 This cognitive dissonance was on display at the relevant “Techtonic” event in Canberra.   
“systems that change over time” or “systems that are unpredictable” generate 
particular risks that need to be addressed in legislation, this can be addressed without defining and regulating a broader, somewhat confused, category of “artificial intelligence ” based on inference. Versatility  is similar in this regard.  
 
4. Speed and scale is a consequence of autonomy rather than artificial 
intelligence.  
 
5. Opacity and lack of explainability  is indeed relevant in the contexts cited on 
p 11, but is best targeted by general requirements for transparency or 
explainability that are not linked to the use of AI. If, for example, existing requirements on giving (real) reasons for decisions were applied to decisions made by AI systems, non- AI sof tware systems, humans or decisions 
processes involving all three, then the concerns can be met without defining “AI” as such. This would also ensure that non- AI systems such a s Robodebt 
are rendered transparent with meaningful information about calculations shared with those affected.  
 
6. High realism  is not limited to AI systems. This problem is best addressed by 
general provisions that require honesty and transparency about when humans are dealing with any systems that could otherwise be mistaken for humans. 
Concerns about realism and transparency of the non- human nature of the 
entity with which one is interacting go beyond the proposed definition of AI.  
 
7. The report states that AI amplifies existing risks such as bias . While this is 
true, it is a problem of any data- driven inferencing process, including reliance 
on traditional statistical inferences about individuals. It is best addressed through reforms to discrimination laws that ensure that they apply to achieve their objectives independent of whether  decisions are made by humans, 
systems, or a combination of both. That is a large, but important task, with some ideas explored in a report  of the James Martin Institute for Public Policy 
for the NSW government with proposed amendments to the Anti- Dicrimination 
Act 1997 (NSW).  
 To give a simple example, a software program of two lines of code that shortlists job candidates on the basis of hard- coded values for university grade- point average 
(GPA) and years of experience is autonomous, large scale, is opaque to decision subjects if the specific GP A and years of experience are not listed, and amplifies 
existing risks such as bias. Such a technology cannot realistically be considered AI as no real inference is happening, and may therefore be excluded from any regulation despite the fact it has the same risks as many AI systems.  
 Because the problems associated with AI are amplified by, rather than unique to, the set of technologies identified in the proposed definition, guardrails and laws should be similarly broad, aligning with risks rather than technologies. In particular, thes e 
should capture all technologies and practices that pose similar risks, rather than being restricted to AI which is itself a fraught set of technologies to define.    
Question 1: Do the proposed principles adequately 
capture high -risk AI?  
While defining AI is a difficult, perhaps impossible, task at this stage of the 
technology’s development, there are indeed risks which the government needs to address  and that new AI technologies have augmented and brought to the forefront. 
Responsible AI is critical for Australia’s future.
4 Identifying high risk scenarios that 
arise from new technological developments such as AI is a useful exercise preliminary to law reform. It does not follow, however, that the legal and regulatory 
response should be technology -specific.  
 
To understand the point being made here, consider the example of the risks that AI poses to privacy. The risk of AI to data privacy is high due to several features:  
1. AI models are often developed and trained on personal and sensitive 
information (e.g., healthcare, financial data, images, messages);  
2. AI models often draw on the private and personal data of AI end- users, 
particularly in free or low -cost versions ; and 
3. Generative AI models can and have been shown to “memorise” training data 
and can then inadvertently return such data input as part of their output to different users . 
 All of these features mean that many AI systems currently in use are not in line with Australian expectations around data privacy and.  in many cases , do not comply with 
existing privacy law. Privacy is a significant risk of many AI systems given the role that data plays in essential processes in the AI eco- system.  
 
That still leaves open the question of the appropriate legal response. The need for 
privacy in the context of how AI systems work should be considered explicitly. The current Privacy Act 1988 (Cth), even with the new Bill, remains an inadequate 
response to these risks. The question of whether AI -related risks are best managed 
through broad privacy law reform, rules that relate to AI systems as such, or rules that relate to specific types of AI systems, or some combination of the above, 
remains a separate one. Where risks are unique to the AI ecosystem (or to particular 
subcategories), then technologically -bounded legal requirements are appropriate.  
 The risks identified in the proposals paper vary in the extent to which they are unique 
to AI (or subtypes of AI). While AI may increase the range of circumstances in which 
many risks arise, many risks also need to be understood in the broader context. As in the privacy case, this does not undermine the need to consider AI use- cases and 
ensure that legal requirements, old and new, provide the appropriate protections.  
 
This point also applies to the specific use- cases in Table 1. Risk is best aligned with 
the specific use case ( sometimes a more specific one than stated) rather than the 
technological mechanism. Some examples:  
- Biometrics is mentioned as a high- risk use case for the EU and Canada in 
Attachment D. However, the risk is more aligned to context and purpose of 
 
4 See generally Australian Academy of  Technological Sciences and Engineering (ATSE) and the Australian  
Institute for Machine Learning (AIML) at The University of Adelaide , Responsible AI: Your questions answered 
(Canberra, Adelaide 2023 ),   
use, than the technology involved.  For example, biometrics used for mass 
surveillance versus as an alternative to passwords.  
- In the high risk use list, education and training is a context of concern for AI 
despite the fact that actual high risk is more specific. We may not want to pre -
judge students based on certain immutable characteristics (whether that is done by AI or ordinary statistical tools) but there is minimal risk associated with the use of Turnitin plagiarism detectors or gr ammar checkers. Training is 
identified in the education category as high risk, yet there are low risk situations where AI may appropriately be used to support and personalise training and education.   
- Employment is also mentioned in the high- risk use case, but again there is a 
difference between reliance on an AI system to select employees and the use of an AI system to help employees navigate organisational policies or schedule meetings.  
 What differentiates high risk from low risk is thus less the technological mechanism 
and more the context, for example the use of data- driven inferencing or automation 
in the context of consequential decisions with potentially serious negative outcomes.  
Question 8: Do the proposed mandatory guardrails 
appropriately mitigate the risks of AI used in  high -risk 
settings? Are there any guardrails that we should add or 
remove?  
In this section, we comment on particular guardrails and make suggestions for 
improvement in how they are formulated. We do not dwell here (because the point is captured elsewhere) on the question of the technological -specificity of their 
formulation.  
 Guardrail 1 
 This guardrail is formulated so as to be about accountability for the guardrails rather 
than accountability for the use of systems. It should capture both.  
 Guardrail 3 
 As per our response to Question 1, privacy is a critical risk for AI systems. As a result, we suggest that Guardrail 3 explicitly states protection of privacy in its title and expands the scope of the guardrail. Moreover, the data that requires protection should not only concern personally identifiable information; it is widely known that data can be re- identified through other information pertaining to the individual, even if 
the data is anonymised.  
 We also recommend that Figure 3 explicitly states that personal and sensitive information are often used as part of “Data used for modification” and when an end-
user interacts with the model. In particular, fine- tuning is often done on the dataset 
closer t o the final task and may contain confidential, sensitive, IP -protected or 
personal data). Further, end- users often supply personal or sensitive information  
when interacting with AI systems, for example disclosing health conditions when 
interacting with a health- bot.  
 Finally, w e suggest that there is not only a need to manage “data quality and 
provenance” but also “vulnerability to attack”, specifically in the context of AI security.  
 Guardrail 4:  
 This guardrail should be written to ensure that evaluation is linked to the downstream 
use and not solely to the model itself. AI applications are not used in isolation --- they 
are adopted and used by people who have oversight. The evaluation of such systems m ust take into account this larger socio- technical context. For example, an 
accurate and relatively unbiased model is not useful if the people using it for decision support do not make better or less biased decisions than they would without the model. The l anguage used in the current guardrail places an emphasis on the 
evaluation of the model objectively and could be interpreted as being about evaluating the model itself, rather than evaluating its impact in the context it is used. A minor rewording to discuss the evaluation in the context of the downstream task or purpose that the model is used on would help to clarify this.  
 Guardrail 6:  
 This should also reference the importance of robustness in watermarking. It is important to inform end- users if the mechanism for doing so, i.e., the watermark, is 
easily removed.  
 Guardrail 7:  
 This is an important guardrail. However, it is also important to note that people impacted by AI outputs and decisions are unable to contest these if they cannot understand how decisions came about and how it impacts them in the specific context. Human rev iew is not useful if systems are designed to scale beyond what 
can be reasonably handled by the human mind.  
 Further, while decisions are often listed as something that AI systems produce (and this is mirrored in how AI systems are defined), it is not clear what constitutes a system being involved in a decision process. Systems can go from making a complete decis ion from all available data, down to just providing an estimate for a 
single input to a larger decision process. The level of involvement of AI systems in decision- making is ultimately not a feature of the system, but a feature of the broader 
socio -technic al context of how any particular decision is made.  
Question 12: Do you have suggestions for reducing the 
regulatory burden on small -to-medium sized 
businesses applying guardrails?  
The guardrails have overlapping obligations relating to data governance which will 
result in duplication of effort and redundancies in ensuring compliance. As an 
example, consider the case of data management:   
 
1. Guardrail 1: ‘policies for data and risk management’  
2. Guardrail 3: dedicated to data governance and provenance but unclear why 
the title precedes with ‘Protect AI systems’ in this guardrail specifically  
3. Guardrail 9: ‘a description of datasets used and their provenance’  
 Streamlining the structure of the guardrails will assist in reducing these redundancies. Further, avoiding duplication or overlap between any new technology -
specific requirements relating to data use in AI systems and broader legal frameworks that may appl y will reduce compliance costs. The conformity and 
compliance assessment within Guardrail 10 has significant potential to increase regulatory burden. Providing templates, examples and support, particularly to SMEs, and ensuring these assessments are focused on the addressing and mitigating risks, may help reduce this burden.   
Question 13: Which legislative option do you feel will 
best address the use of AI in high -risk settings? What 
opportunities should the government take into account in considering each approach? 
We recommend that the government focus on Options 1 and 2, narrowing the scope 
of Option 3 to areas in which the identified risks align with the proposed definition of AI. While it is useful to understand how risks have changed as a result of new practices  including the increased prevalence of AI systems, the scoping of legal 
requirements needs to be considered separately.   Many of the proposed guardrails intersect with a variety of other legal frameworks. This cuts against the statement on page 51 of the proposal paper that option 3 “provides greater consistency across the economy than option 1 or option 2”. Ultimately, it d epends on how you slice “the economy”. What option 3 will do is 
create separate regulation for those activities of an organisation associated with particular systems meeting the definition of “AI”. Presumably those activities of the organisation that do not involve AI (but may involve automated systems) will continue to be regulated under other laws (whether or not those laws themselves are reformed under Option 1). Thus consistency across the “economy” points in favour of Option 1 or 2, rather than Option 3.  
 The issue is not whether there is consistency or not, but rather which way the consistency is pointed. One can have consistency around the concept of “AI” with a regulatory system drawn around at scope (Option 3). This is consistent within that bubble but inconsistent depending on the technology involved in a process. For example, an approach to hiring that blends AI systems, other tools (eg psychometric testing), human elements and newer not -yet-invented technologies that may fall 
outside today’s definitions will not be regulated “consistently” under Option 3.  
 
If AI were a well- defined and separate sphere of activity, there could be a case for 
Option 3 as a means of ensuring regulatory consistency within that domain. However, that is not the case today and is likely to become less so as the technology  
continues to evolve. If definitions of “AI” continue to change at the present rate 
(because of the unpredictable nature of innovation), Option 3 will capture a sphere of activity that will become increasingly arbitrary as measured against the regulatory goals sought to be achieved. Unless there is a clear tie between the regulatory goals and the regulatory object (systems that infer how to generate outputs from inputs), Option 3 will not provide consistency except in an arbitrary way.  
 To see how Option 3 will create inconsistencies, it is helpful to consider how the Guardrails intersect with more general legal frameworks that truly apply across the economy  : 
 
1. Guardrails 1 and 2 cut across directors’ duties to ensure proper governance, internal capability and regulatory compliance as well as appropriate risk management practices.  
 
2. Guardrail 3 intersects with the ongoing work on privacy law reform as well as the important work of copyright law reform. Data governance best practice should capture all data -driven functions such as reporting, decision support 
and dashboards, which may not necessarily require AI. Indeed the bulk of data- driven systems in use today would not meet the proposed definition of 
AI. Yet these non- AI data- driven systems continue to raise similar issues of 
bias, issues of consent and potential harm to stakeholders  impacted by faulty 
data pipelines. As well as considering specific issues associated with AI, data governance and information resilience should be considered more broadly.
5  
 
3. Guardrail 4 is really a concern about autonomy (hence scale) rather than AI 
as such. Testing and evaluation, if made a general requirement, ought to 
apply to all systems deployed in contexts where they may cause harm. 
Requirements for re- testing could be l imited to systems with inbuilt capacity 
for change, being a more purpose- aligned concept than AI.  
 
4. Guardrail 5 recognises that the need for human oversight is contextual. “Real -
time human involvement in an AI system may not always be practical, and 
may even make a system less reliable”. It is ultimately about optimising 
human- system co -operation, and is  thus not limited to AI systems.  
 
5. Guardrail 6, concerning transparency, should also be carefully scoped. Robodebt was not, as the then government pointed out, an AI system, but the 
automated nature of the decision- making process should nevertheless have 
been made transparent for similar reasons to those set out in Guardrail 6. 
What needs to be transparent is not systems that infer, but systems that ( a) 
might reasonably be mistaken for humans or decisions that people might 
otherwise assume were made by humans, or ( b) produce outputs or decis ions 
where there are or ought to be rights to transparency. For example, principles 
of the rule of law and open government should not be undermined by 
outsourcing administrative decision- making to non- transparent systems.  
 
 
5 ARC Training Centre for Information Resilience, Manifesto for Information Resilience, 
https://cires.org.au/resource/manifesto -for-information -resilience/ .   
6. Guardrail 7 overlaps with consumer and administrative law in private and 
public contexts respectively. It is again not clear why complaints handling and contestability should be separately regulated for AI systems. Legal requirements that relate to these, however, should be explicitly extended to 
the use of AI systems where there is ambiguity. For example, in administrative contexts, reasons for decision ought to continue to be required where AI systems are used and intra- government guidelines may assist departments in 
ensuring that this is done well.
6  
 The choice between Options 1 and 2 is more nuanced. There are advantages in resolving “definitional inconsistencies in how AI systems are regulated across the economy” only where there is a need to define “AI systems” at all. Assuming that there will be so me contexts where AI -specific legal requirements will be appropriate, 
Option 2 provides a useful accompaniment to Option 1.  
 There is a broad need to consider standardising terminology where appropriate across the corpus of legislation.
7 The need to enhance common approaches to 
meaning and interpretation across statutes, of which the Acts Interpretation Act  is a 
fine example, is not limited to terminology associated with AI. We have similar 
problems with terminology associated with data more broadly.8 Despite that 
observation, Option 2 is a reasonable place to start. It could also assist the 
government’s cross -agency coordination and cooperation for the extensive work 
involved in Option 1.  
 
One issue that should be separated from the question of technological specificity 
when considering the options is the issue of which legal requirements should apply 
ex ante and which should apply ex post.  The proposals paper sets this out as a 
dichotomy, but in fact we will need to do both and these are connected. A “best 
efforts approach” (as discussed in relation to watermarking) should apply throughout 
a system’s lifecycle. The specific legal requirements can be written so as to apply at 
relevant points (some ex ante; some ex post ). 
Counter -narratives to the risk -based framing  
The government’s approach in the proposals paper is around risks associated with 
AI. While we agree that safe and responsible AI is important, the government should not lose sight of the fact that AI does not only generate risk, but it can also mitigate against risk. For example, while AI systems can reduce transparency,
9 they can also 
make it easier to interrogate systems.10 Bias in AI systems is potentially easier to 
detect, measure (in different ways) and change than the equivalent in human decision- making systems.
11 The important consideration of negative risk should thus 
 
