Response to DISR Consultation: Proposals 
for introducing mandatory guardrails for Artificial Intelligence  in high risk settings  
Background  
Australia has a well -developed, robust regulatory framework for regulation  of 
therapeutic goods that  includes both pre -market (ex  ante) and post -market (ex  post) 
measures to ensure the ongoing safety, quality , and performance of all therapeutic 
goods.  
Key features of this framework include:  
• independent assessment and certification from approved third party regulators 
and assessment bodies  
• use of the Australian Register of Therapeutic Goods (ARTG) as the basis for 
maintaining regulatory  oversight of the majority of therapeutic goods that are 
imported into, exported from , or otherwise supplied within Australia 
• ongoing reporting and surveillance requirements after a product  is approved for 
entry  in the ARTG to ensure products continue to be safe and perform as 
intended 
• a range of criminal and civil penalties that can be applied where there is failure to comply with regulatory requirements  
• legislative mechanisms which  ensure alignment with existing Australian data 
and privacy laws, as well as capability to require alignment with cybersecurity strategy requirements.  
Regulation of AI systems and models in therapeutic 
goods  
In Australia all software and AI -based models and systems that meet the definition of a 
medical device2 are already  subject to regulation by the TGA . Unless otherwise exempt 
or excluded, these kinds of devices must be included in the Australian Register of 
Therapeutic Goods (ARTG)  bef ore they can be imported into, exported from , or 
otherwise supplied in Australia.  
From the Therapeutic Goods Act 1989 : 
Section 41BD What is a medical device  
 (1) A medical device is: 
 (a) any instrument, apparatus, appliance, software, implant, reagent, material or 
other article (whether used alone or in combination, and including the 
software necessary for its proper application) intended, by the person under 
 
2 Section 41BD of the Therapeutic Goods Act 1989 .  Page 4 of 58 
  whose name it is or is to be supplied, to be used for human beings for the 
purpose of one or more of the following: 
 (i) diagnosis, prevention, monitoring, prediction, prognosis, treatment or 
alleviation of disease; 
 (ii) diagnosis, monitoring, treatment, alleviation of or compensation for an 
injury or disability;  
 (iii) investigation, replacement or modification of the anatomy or of a 
physiological or pathological process or state; 
 (iv) control or support of conception; 
 (v) in vitro examination of a specimen derived from the human body for a 
specific medical purpose;  
 and that does not achieve its principal intended action in or on the 
human body by pharmacological, immunological or metabolic means, 
but that may be assisted in its function by such means… 
 
Key features of the Australian approach to the regulation of medical devices includes:  
1. Pre-market (ex ante) requirements  
2. Post-market (ex post) requirements  
1. Pre-market (ex ante) requirements 
For medical devices, the Therapeutic Goods Act 1989 and associated regulations 
require all medical device manufacturers to establish, implement , and maintain a 
quality management system for the manufacture of their devices. Where appropriate, 
manufacturers are also required to undertake assessment/certification of their quality 
management system via an independent third party such as the TGA or a comparable 
overseas regulator.  
The documentation demonstrating that a medical device meets all relevant regulatory requirements is collectively known as conformity assessment documentation or market authorisation evidence. This documentation must be provided to the TGA and accepted 
by us before an application for inclusion in the ARTG can be made. Where a device is 
exempt from inclusion in the ARTG, this documentation must be available and provided 
to the TGA upon request.  
Pre-market regulations for medical devices, including AI systems and models, include:  
• Classification of the device under a risk-based system
3 which features specific 
rules for programmed or programmable medical devices or software which 
