4 October 2024  
 
 
 
 
Department of Industry, Science and Resources  
Consultation on Introducing Mandatory Guardrails for AI in High-risk Settings  
 
By email : aiconsultation@industry.gov.au  
 
 
Dear Madam /Sir, 
 
We welcome the opportunity to provide feedback in relation to the Proposals Paper : 
Introducing mandatory guardrails for AI in high -risk settings . 
 
Maurice Blackburn Pty Ltd is a plaintiff law firm with 3 4 permanent offices and 30 visiting 
offices throughout all mainland States and Territories. The firm specialises in personal 
injuries, abuse law, medical negligence, employment and industrial law, dust diseases, 
superannuation (particularly total and permanent disability claims), negligent financial and 
other advice, and consumer and commercial class actions. The firm also has a substantial 
social j ustice practice.  
 
Our input to this consultation is based on our work i n supporting Australian who have been 
victims of mass harms , including privacy breaches and product safety breaches, to achieve 
appropriate compensation for those harms. The lived experience of our clients in achieving 
efficient access to justice informs our view on what constitutes acceptable regulatory 
practice.   
 
The Proposals Paper tells us that:1 
 
There are several ways that the Australian Government could implement guardrails 
ensuring greater testing, transparency and accountability of AI in high -risk settings 
to address the limitations of the current system. This includes implementing 
guardrails b y:  
 
• Option 1 – a domain specific approach – adapting existing regulatory 
frameworks to include the guardrails  
 
• Option 2 – a framework approach – introducing framework legislation that 
will require other existing laws to be amended for the framework legislation 
to have effect  
 
• Option 3 – a whole of economy approach – introducing a new cross -
economy AI Act  
 
 
1 Proposals Paper : p.43   
Page 2 
 As a preliminary matter, Maurice Blackburn believes that assessing the proposed mandatory 
guardrails is only possible with a clear proposal for enforcement. Without strong capacity for 
enforcement, corporate Australia will lack the motivation to devote resources to ensuring 
their use of AI is compliant.  
 
Given this, and our expertise in addressing mass wrongs , we restrict our commentary to 
responding to consultation question 13:  
 
Which legislative option do you feel will best address the use of AI in high -risk 
settings? What opportunities should the government take into account in considering 
each approach?  
 
We answer these questions in turn below.  
 
As a general proposition, Maurice Blackburn  believes that the  legislative option that 
will best address the use of AI in high -risk settings  is Option 3 .  
 
The core determinant of success in the implementation of mandatory guardrails in high-risk 
setting s is their  enforce ability, both by a well -resourced regulator with appropriate powers, 
but also everyday Australians who experience harm . We believe that t he development and 
implementation of a stand -alone AI -specific Act will best achieve th ese aims .  
 
The analysis of Option 3 in the Proposals Paper tells us that:2 
 
The Act would define these high -risk applications of AI and outline the new 
mandatory guardrails. It would establish a monitoring and enforcement regime 
overseen by an independent AI regulator.  
 
We believe that this model has the p otential to provide superior consistency and predictability  
than the other options.  We understand that an independent AI regulator could be located 
within an existing regulator, but we think there is utility in considering what specific powers 
would assist them to effective ly monitor AI in high -risk applications, both for compliance with 
the guardrails and taking action when things go wrong.  
 
Taking a whole -of-economy view would help alleviate the potential for unpredictability and 
unfairness in regulation.  It would ensure that there could be consistency in  the scope of  
application of the guardrails and associated matters such as definitions, a s well as having the 
potential to establish  attribution of liability between deployers and developers , for example.  
 
Our role in supporting consumers in accessing justice for harms committed against them 
leads us to believe that  a stand -alone Act and centralised system of enforcement would lead 
to a more  efficient  administration of justice.  Relatedly, we note that the Proposals Paper 
references the need to monitor the development of the EU’s regulatory approach.3 We 
suggest that this include  analysis of enforcement mechanisms including the creation of a 
statutory tort provision  through which  member states might achieve consistency in outcomes 
for consumers when harms result from failure to comply with guardrails.  
 
We note that the limitations of Option 3, as described in the Proposals Paper4 are mainly 
focused around risks associated with poor legislative design. We agree with this analysis. 
However, we see those limitations as more manageable than the limitations of the other two 
 
2 Ibid: p.49  
3 Ibid: p.33  
4 Ibid: p.51   
Page 3 
 options , which are more focused around lack of consistency in definitions, gaps in regulation 
coverage and differences in enforcement approaches.  
 
In considering the options, Maurice Blackburn suggests the government take into 
account the fact that e nsuring AI is safe requires the implementation of 
comprehensive privacy reform.  We say this for two reasons : 
 
First, t he collection and use of data at scale is a fundamental requirement of a functioning AI 
industry. The governance and management of large datasets of personal information must 
be robust to ensure that AI is developed in a way that is safe and fair. The Proposals Paper  
also notes that harms from AI misuse include those that result from  breaches of privacy.5 The 
development of safe AI, and the objective in developing the guardrails, is not achievable 
without strong regulation of the data that AI is trained on.  
 
This observation relates to our second point. While t he Government has already taken steps 
in responding to the outcomes of the Privacy Act Revie w, in order for an economy -wide AI 
Act to succeed, this work must  continue.  
 
Proposals 26 and 27 of the Privacy Act Review  Report6 highlight the need for a Direct Right 
of Action for consumers, and the introduction of a statutory tort for  serious invasions of 
privacy. Strengthening the definition of personal information (and thereby expanding the 
scope of the Privacy Act) , updating the definition of consent,  as well as creating rights, such 
as the right to delete personal information, will contribute to the creation of ethical and 
reliable data sets for training AI models. Such reforms will also provide important safeguards 
for consumers when considering issues of liability and accountability.  
 
We encourage the Department to work closely with the Attorney -General’s Department to 
ensure that the incremental approach to privacy reform does not hinder the development of 
