The legislative framework must address not only the identified risks but also ensure it 
avoids unintended consequences or gaps in enforcement, particularly across sectors 
with varying levels of AI adoption.  
One key limitation is the risk of sectoral fragmentation in Option 1 (domain -specific 
approach), where adapting existing regulations in silos may lead to inconsistent 
standards and enforcement across industries. This could create regulatory gaps, 
particula rly where AI systems operate across multiple domains, such as GPAI. Moreover, 
this fragmentation could increase the compliance burden on businesses operating in 
multiple sectors, requiring them to adhere to different regulatory frameworks for similar 
AI systems.  
In Option 3 (whole -of-economy approach), the risk lies in over -regulation. A broad, one -
size-fits-all AI Act may not account for the nuances of different sectors, potentially stifling 
innovation in lower -risk areas while imposing uniform standards that do not appropriately 
address sector -specific risks. This approach may also lack the agility needed to adapt to 
new and emerging AI technologies without frequent legislative updates, delaying 
regulatory responses.  
The Australian Government should consider the limitations of sectoral fragmentation in 
Option 1 and the risks of over -regulation and lack of adaptability in Option 3. A more 
tailored, flexible framework (Option 2) would mitigate these issues, allowing for 
consistent oversight while preserving adaptability and sector -specific flexibility.  
15. Which regulatory option/s will best ensure that guardrails for high -risk AI can 
adapt and respond to step -changes in technology?  
Which regulatory option will ensure that guardrails for high -risk AI can adapt to rapid 
technological advancements and step -changes in AI capabilities.  
A regulatory framework for AI must be flexible enough to adapt to evolving technologies 
while maintaining effective safeguards, particularly for high -risk AI applications.  
The framework approach (Option 2) best supports adaptability to step -changes in AI 
technology. By establishing a high -level regulatory framework that can be tailored and 
amended as technologies evolve, this option allows for sector -specific updates and 
modifications without requiring full legislative overhauls. Unlike the domain -specific 
approach (Option 1), which risks slow adaptation due to fragmented regulations across 
sectors, the framework approach allows for consistent updates across industries in 
response to technological changes. Additionally, Option 2 facilitates the introduction of 
regulatory sandboxes, enabling the testing of innovative AI applications in real -world 
conditions while allowing regulators to adjust guardrails dynamically. This adapta bility is 
crucial as AI systems, particularly GPAI, develop unforeseen capabilities that require 
swift regulatory responses. By contrast, a whole -of-economy AI Act (Option 3) may lack Public Submission : Introducing mandatory guardrails for AI in high -risk settings  
