the flexibility needed for timely responses to rapid technological advancements, 
potentially delaying necessary updates.  
The framework approach (Option 2) offers the best adaptability for high -risk AI guardrails, 
allowing for real -time adjustments to technological advances while ensuring consistent 
regulation across sectors. It strikes a balance between flexibility and robus t oversight, 
ensuring guardrails remain relevant as AI evolves.  
16. Where do you see the greatest risks of gaps or inconsistencies with Australia’s 
existing laws for the development and deployment of AI? Which regulatory 
option best addresses this, and why?  
Identifying the greatest risks of gaps or inconsistencies with Australia's existing laws in 
regulating AI development and deployment, and selecting the best regulatory option to 
address these issues.  
AI-specific risks, such as autonomy, opacity, and scalability, may not be fully addressed 
by existing laws, particularly regarding privacy, consumer protection, liability, and human 
rights.  
The greatest risks of gaps lie in areas such as privacy, where Australia's Privacy Act may 
not fully cover AI -driven data collection and use, particularly with regards to AI's ability to 
infer sensitive information from non -personal data. Additionally, lia bility laws may 
struggle to allocate responsibility between developers, deployers, and users, especially 
when AI systems act autonomously or produce unforeseen outcomes. Moreover, anti -
discrimination frameworks may not effectively address AI -induced bias, particularly in 
sectors like hiring, healthcare, or finance, where algorithmic decision -making can 
perpetuate or exacerbate inequality.  
The framework approach (Option 2) best addresses these gaps. It allows the government 
to build on existing laws while filling specific gaps through targeted AI regulations. For 
example, the framework could introduce AI -specific provisions into the Privacy Act to 
account for AI’s unique data processing capabilities or amend product liability laws to 
clarify responsibility in autonomous AI decision -making.  
The framework approach (Option 2) is the most effective for addressing legal gaps and 
inconsistencies, as it provides flexibility to integrate AI -specific regulations within 
existing laws, ensuring a comprehensive and adaptive legal structure for AI develo pment 
and deployment in Australia.  
