Introduction
The
Australian
Digital
Alliance
(ADA)
welcomes
the
opportunity
to
respond
to
the
Department
of
Industry,
Science
and
Resources’
Proposals
paper
for
introducing
mandatory
guardrails
for
AI
in
high-risk
settings
.
1
We
thank
the
Department
for
the
opportunity
to
comment
on
the
options
presented
in
the
Proposals
paper.
The
rapid
development,
investment
in
and
deployment
of
new
AI
technologies,
applications,
and
services
has
signiﬁcant
potential
to
enhance
so
many
aspects
of
Australian
society.
But
these
same
technologies
also
carry
risks.
We
recognise
that
balancing
the
encouragement
of
AI
uptake
against
the
desire
to
minimise
the
potential
for
harm
these
same
technologies
can
cause
may
not
always
be
easy
to
reconcile.
While
this
is
difﬁcult
regulatory
terrain,
the
need
for
the
government
to
establish
clear
expectations
on
how
developers
and
deployers
of
AI
do
so
safely
and
responsibly
is
evident.
Important
topics
are
raised
in
the
paper.
The
policy
objectives
put
forward
–
to
address
risks
and
harms
from
AI,
build
public
trust
and
provide
businesses
with
greater
regulatory
certainty
–
are
important
pursuits.
The
ADA
does
not
claim
to
be
experts
with
respect
to
AI
and
related
technologies.
Our
experience
and
expertise
is
in
copyright
law
and
policy.
We
have
limited
our
responses
here
to
copyright
only.
It
is
important
that
Australia
take
full
advantage
of
the
affordances
that
come
with
AI
development.
It
is
equally
important
that
we
respect
copyright
and
its
role
in
incentivising
creativity.
In
this
submission
we
will
outline
some
of
the
key
issues
related
to
copyright
and
AI.
Our
thoughts
here
are
informed
by
our
ongoing
copyright
reform
advocacy,
including
our
participation
in
the
Attorney-General’s
Department’s
Copyright
and
AI
Reference
Group
(CAIRG)
(our
Executive
Ofﬁcer,
Sarah
Powell,
also
sits
on
the
CAIRG
Steering
Committee).
Should
the
Department
require
additional
information,
particularly
in
relation
to
copyright
and
AI,
the
ADA
would
welcome
the
opportunity
to
make
further
comments.
Our
principal
contact
with
respect
to
this
submission
is
Sarah
Powell,
who
can
be
reached
at
sarah@digital.org.au
or
on
02
6262
1118.
While
this
submission
focuses
on
the
relationship
between
copyright
and
AI,
the
ADA
recognises
the
need
to
consult
widely
with
stakeholders
in
relation
to
the
many
other
issues
relevant
to
AI.
