1. Do the proposed principles adequately capture high-risk AI?
No
Are there any principles we should add or remove?
Yes
Please provide any additional comments.
A principle ensuring devlopers and deployers are not unduly burdened by planning, reviewing,reporting and recording obligations.
2. Do you have any suggestions for how the principles could better capture harms to First Nations people, communities and Country?
No
Please provide any additional comments.
The principles shouldn't isolate or call out any one demographic. The principles should be universal for all Australians.
3. Do the proposed principles, supported by examples, give enough clarity and certainty on high-risk AI settings and high-risk AI models? Is a more defined approach, with a list of illustrative uses, needed?
YES the principles give enough clarity and certainty
If you prefer a principles-based approach, what should we address in guidance to give the greatest clarity?
Guiding principles that can cover all iterations of AI use. Don't pigeon hole specific use cases.
How can this list capture emerging uses of AI?
The development of AI needs to be able to grow and follow community expectations, needs and overall quality of life outcomes. Too many restrictions such as those in the EU will hinder the use of AI for public safety and harm minimisation by private companies. Limiting the use of AI to government, can leed to a totalitarian state and is not conducive to a democracy.
4. Are there high-risk use cases that government should consider banning in its regulatory response (for example, where there is an unacceptable level of risk)?
Yes
If so, how should we define these?
The use of AI to track individuals without their consent where there is no documented legal, safety or harm minimisation business case.
5. Are the proposed principles flexible enough to capture new and emerging forms of high-risk AI, such as general-purpose AI (GPAI)?
Yes
Please provide any additional comments.
Except for the inordinate requirements for assessments, reporting and recording.
6. Should mandatory guardrails apply to all GPAI models?
No
Please provide any additional comments.
Only those GPAI models that involve biometrics, tracking of individuals or unsupervised AI assessments.
7. What are suitable indicators for defining GPAI models as high-risk?
Base on technical capability
What technical capability should it be based on?
Other
8. Do the proposed mandatory guardrails appropriately mitigate the risks of AI used in high-risk settings?
No
Please provide any additional comments.
Guardrail 1 will burden small businesses, potentially hinder innovation, and could risk developer and deployer IP.
Guardrail 2 could again be a significant burdening hindrance to small businesses and could become so circular progress is significantly restricted.
Are there any guardrails that we should add or remove?
Yes
What guardrails should we add or remove?
Establish, implement and publish an accountability process including governance, internal capability and a strategy for regulatory compliance Establish and implement a risk management process to identify and mitigate risks
Please provide any additional comments
1 & 2 should be modified so they're less of a burden on small businesses.
A guardrail specifically around the ethical use of AI that includes harm minimisation and development for the betterment of society could be included.
9. How can the guardrails incorporate First Nations knowledge and cultural protocols to ensure AI systems are culturally appropriate and preserve Indigenous Cultural and Intellectual Property?
This should NOT be a consideration. All Australians should be treated equally by the Guardrails. No ONE group or culture should be singled out.
10. Do the proposed mandatory guardrails distribute responsibility across the AI supply chain and throughout the AI lifecycle appropriately?
No
Please provide any additional comments
All guardrails overly burden the developer and deployer in the absence of the end user who is all but briefly mentioned. There should also be a responsibility or expectation on the community to encourage and use AI responsibly and ethically.
11. Are the proposed mandatory guardrails sufficient to address the risks of GPAI?
No
How could we adapt the guardrails for different GPAI models, for example low-risk and high-risk GPAI models? 
Over burdensome for GPAI
12. Do you have suggestions for reducing the regulatory burden on small-to-medium sized businesses applying guardrails?
No
13. Which legislative option do you feel will best address the use of AI in high-risk settings?
A framework approach – Introducing new framework legislation to adapt existing regulatory frameworks across the economy
14. Are there any additional limitations of options outlined in this section which the Australian Government should consider?
Yes
Please provide any additional comments.
Don't over-regulate AI. By its very nature, new and innovative use cases for AI will evolve. Any legislation that follows the EU will only hurt the industry and potential benefits to the community.
15. Which regulatory option(s) will best ensure that guardrails for high-risk AI can adapt and respond to step-changes in technology?
A framework approach – Introducing new framework legislation to adapt existing regulatory frameworks across the economy
16. Where do you see the greatest risks of gaps and inconsistencies with Australia’s existing laws for the development and deployment of AI?
We have a very real risk of over regulating and hindering development and use of technology. We WILL get left behind if we adopt an overly cautious approach that restricts development and inovation.
Which regulatory option best addresses this?
A framework approach – Introducing new framework legislation to adapt existing regulatory frameworks across the economy

