"109","110","110","DAN SALDI - XAANA PTY LTD","https://consult.industry.gov.au/ai-mandatory-guardrails/submission/view/199"," 
Response to Mandatory Guardrails for AI in High -Risk settings  
 
                                                                                                                  Supporting Safe and responsible AI in Australia                            zebra@tu rium.ai  Thank you for providing Xaana .Ai with the opportunity to submit our 
input regarding the Proposals Paper for Introducing Mandatory 
Guardrails for AI in High -Risk Settings , released by the Australian 
Government on 5th Septe mber 2024 . This paper is part of the 
government's broader efforts to ensure the safe and responsible 
use of AI, particularly in high -risk applications.  As the developer 
behind Turium Zebra, Australia’s first sovereign large language 
model, our focus has always been on ensuring that Australia 
embraces the transformative potential of AI  while establishing the 
guardrails necessary for safety and accountability. Let’s break down 
the options and th e implications:  
Option 1: Adopting Guardrails within Existing Regulatory 
Frameworks : While Option 1 may seem convenient, it is 
fundamentally flawed . Existing regulatory frameworks were built for 
traditional technologies and are increasingly unable to keep pace 
with AI’ s rapid evolution. AI’s key characteristics —autonomy, 
scalability, and the capacity for emergent behaviour —require a 
regulatory approach that anticipates complexities rather than 
retrofitting outdated rules. Simply modifying these frameworks 
leads to a fragmented approach where regulators are perpetually 
behind the technology, resulting in inconsistent enforcement  and a 
lack of clarity for industries on how to comply. This creates an 
environment of regulatory uncertainty , which in turn stifles innovation . 
Option 2: Introducing New Framework Legislation : Option 2 presents a more balanced approach —a step in the right 
direction for AI regulation in Australia. Framework legislation can provide the structure needed for AI governance 
without overregulating sectors that do not need stringent oversight. However, framework legislation must be carefully 
designed  to account for the multidimensional nature of AI . A general framework risks creating inconsistent or incoherent 
regulations  unless it is designed with AI’s unique complexities in mind . This framework needs t o be context -specific . AI 
in healthcare must be regulated differently than AI in financial services or logistics. A one -size -fits-all approach  would 
lead to overregulation ; it is vital that the granularity needed to properly classify risk levels  across industries is embedded . 
Option 3: Introducing a Cross -Economy AI -Specific Law (e.g., Australian AI Act) : While Option 3  sounds comprehensive, 
it is the most problematic . An AI -specific law that spans all industries is not only overly broad  but also unsustainable . AI 
is not a monolith, and its regulation should not be either.  A cross -economy AI law would likely fail to address the 
geopolitical implications of AI regulation . Australia must ensure that its regulations keep pace with international 
standards without adopting measures that limit its competitive advantage  in development. Heavy -handed, one -size -
fits-all laws could make Australia less attractive , pushing investment to jurisdictions with more targeted regulations . 
General Comments:  While the policy rightly identifies transparency and accountability  as essential guardrails, the 
current proposal underestimates the challenges  of achieving these goals. Transparency in AI is not something that can 
be “added on” after development. It must be engineered from the start —embedded into the AI system from the ground 
up. In summary, Option 2  provides the best opportunity to create a targeted, flexible regulatory framework  that can 
evolve alongside AI. However, it must include context -specific regulation  that accounts for the varying risk levels across 
different industries. Additionally, as local innovators, companies like Xaana.Ai must be actively involved in shaping these 
policies . We bring local expertise  to the table —Xaana.Ai has been successful in building AI solutions that work across 
various sectors, from government to private enterprises. Yet, there is no mention of the need for consultation with local 
AI companies in the paper. This is a significant oversight. Australia’s sovereign AI capability  is growing, and companies 
like ours are developing technologies that compete on the global stage.  
 
Your Faithfully,  
 
 
Dan Saldi  
CEO & Founder  
Xaana.Ai & Turium  AI  
Xaana .Ai 
 
