Australian Dental Industry Association
Published name
Australian Dental Industry Association
1. Do the proposed principles adequately capture high-risk AI?
Yes
Please provide any additional comments.
Yes, however the principles are broad and it will be necessary to ensure that the unintended consequence of capturing low risk (exempt or excluded) medical devices, just because they are medical devices is avoided.
Are there any principles we should add or remove?
No
Please provide any additional comments.
Most Australian sponsors will already be complying with Canadian and EU guardrails, so ensuring that Australia aligns to these principles is essential to avoid barriers to access of new technologies. It is also essential to avoid the
unintended consequence resulting from all medical devices being classified as high risk merely because they are wihtin the health care setting. Medical devices classified as low risk and excluded or exempt from inclusion on the ARGT, must not be classified as high risk by DISR as this would result in the unintended consequence of placing them into two independent conformity assessment processes. This unintended outcome could be avoided with adoption of either regulatory options 1 or 2. A further option to avoid this unintended consequence would be to exclude healthcare from the framework and allow the TGA and the Commission for Safety and Quality in Healthcare to provide the regulatory guardrails within the healthcare setting.
2. Do you have any suggestions for how the principles could better capture harms to First Nations people, communities and Country?
No
3. Do the proposed principles, supported by examples, give enough clarity and certainty on high-risk AI settings and high-risk AI models? Is a more defined approach, with a list of illustrative uses, needed?
YES the principles give enough clarity and certainty
If you prefer a principles-based approach, what should we address in guidance to give the greatest clarity?
Existing TGA definitions and guidance for example, for Software as a Medical Device provide adequate clarity for uses of AI. As AI continues to be developed, the principle-based approach allows for continual evolution of the guardrails within the framework.
4. Are there high-risk use cases that government should consider banning in its regulatory response (for example, where there is an unacceptable level of risk)?
No
8. Do the proposed mandatory guardrails appropriately mitigate the risks of AI used in high-risk settings?
Yes
Please provide any additional comments.
The TGA’s existing regulation via the risk-based framework, QMS, essential principles, post-market monitoring and conformity assessment, position the TGA well to continue to regulate the medical device industry. Alignment with the International regulatory and conformity assessment authorities will achieve the best balance between mitigating risk and facilitating access to new and emerging technology.
Are there any guardrails that we should add or remove?
No
Please provide any additional comments
Within the healthcare setting, the guardrails largely align with existing TGA guidelines
10. Do the proposed mandatory guardrails distribute responsibility across the AI supply chain and throughout the AI lifecycle appropriately?
Yes
12. Do you have suggestions for reducing the regulatory burden on small-to-medium sized businesses applying guardrails?
Yes
Please provide any additional comments
Exempting healthcare and allowing the current regulator (TGA) to regulate, taking into consideration
international alignment will provide the best opportunity to reduce the regulatory burden for businesses. The dental sector is extremely small, encompassing only approximately 6% of the healthcare industry overall. It is therefore not only small-to-medium dental businesses that will be significantly affected by over-burdensome regulation but even the largest dental businesses, as it will create a major disincentive for innovative dental technology to be brought into the Australian market.
13. Which legislative option do you feel will best address the use of AI in high-risk settings?
A domain specific approach – adapting existing regulatory frameworks to include the proposed mandatory guardrails
What opportunities should the Government take into account in considering each approach? 
Option 1 is the preferred option for AI healthcare settings.
Both options 1 and 2 could work for healthcare, enabling the existing TGA framework, definitions, principles and processes to remain in place. As highlighted earlier, Australia is a very small part of the global healthcare market overall (and the dental market, in particular) so continuing to facilitate harmonisation with International regulatory authorities is crucial.
Option 3 is the least acceptable option, as it will result in multiple parallel regulatory processes that will have the unintended consequence of limiting access to important new and emerging technological advances in Australia.
14. Are there any additional limitations of options outlined in this section which the Australian Government should consider?
No
15. Which regulatory option(s) will best ensure that guardrails for high-risk AI can adapt and respond to step-changes in technology?
A domain specific approach – Adopting the guardrails within existing regulatory frameworks as needed
Please provide any additional comments.
This option allows greater sector-specific agility to move more quickly in response to step-changes in technology

