"111","112","112","Associate Professor Niamh Kinchin, Dr Armin Alimardani (Lecturer), Yvonne Apolo (Lecturer), and Joshua Ranalletta (LLB Honours student) School of Law, University of Wollongong","https://consult.industry.gov.au/ai-mandatory-guardrails/submission/view/197"," 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
Introducing  Mandatory 
Guardrails  for AI in  
High -Risk Settings  
SUBMISSION TO THE  DEPARTMENT  OF INDUSTRY,  SCIENCE  AND  
RESOURCES : OCTOBER  2024  2  |Introducing  Mandatory  Guardrails  for AI in High -Risk  Settings : Submission  
 
 Associate  Professor  Niamh  Kinchin,  Dr Armin  Alimardani (Lecturer),  Yvonne  
Apolo  (Lecturer),  and Joshua  Ranalletta  (LLB  Honours  student)  
School  of Law,  University  of Wollongong  
 
About the Researchers  
  
As legal  academics  working at the intersection  between  law and emerging  technologies,  we 
welcome the opportunity  to comment  on the Proposals  Paper  for Introducing  Mandatory Guardrails  
for AI in High -Risk Settings.  Collectively  our expertise spans  across  the fields  of administrative  
law, refugee law, criminal law, privacy  law, and legal  practice.  
  
The views  in this submission  are our own,  based  on our own research,  and do not represent  the 
official  views  of UOW  or the UOW  School  of Law.  We have  no objection to the publication  of this 
submission.  
  
 
About  this Submission  
  
This submission  does not attempt to address  all the topics  raised  by the Proposals  Paper;  rather  we 
focus  on those  questions  that are within  our collective expertise.  
  
Our submissions  are as follows:  
• Question 1: The principles  for defining  risk should  differentiate between  four categories  of 
risk. 
• Question 1: AI systems should not be categorised  by use in order  to be treated  separately.  
• Questions  3 and 4: A list-based  approach  risks categorising some  AI systems as high- risk 
despite  posing an unacceptable level  of risk in some  contexts.   
• Question 6: The mandatory  guardrails  should not apply equally  to all GPAI  models.  
• Question 8: The requirement  for human rights  impact  assessments for all high and limited  
risk AI systems should be added  as a guardrail.  
• Question 12: Mandatory guardrails  should  apply equally to small- to-medium  sized  
businesses.   
• Question 13: Subject  to the execution  and findings  of a comprehensive  regulatory  gap 
assessment,  a whole  of economy- approach  is the most  appropriate  vehicle for making the 
guardrails  mandatory.  
  3  |Introducing  Mandatory  Guardrails  for AI in High -Risk  Settings : Submission  
 
  
Defining  high-risk AI 
 
QUESTION  1: Do the proposed  principles  adequately  capture  high -risk AI? Are there  any principles  we should  add 
or remove?  
Please  identify  any:  
• low- risk use cases  that  are unintentionally  captured  
• categories  of uses  that should  be treated separately,  such  as uses  for defence or national  security  
purposes.  
 
We support  the introduction of a risk-based  approach to the regulation of AI, that focuses  on the 
inherent  risks of an AI system  in specific contexts , guided by a series  of flexible  principles.  A risk-
based  approach  avoids  the perils  of a ‘one size fits all’ approach  that would lack sensitivity  to 
different  types  of AI systems and different  contexts  in which  those  systems are deployed.  A 
principles -based  definition  of risk is positioned to provide  sufficient  flexibility  to accommodate 
technological  evolution.  The proposed principles  are flawed,  however,  to the extent  that they:  (1) 
focus  exclusively  on defining high-risk AI systems and (2) permit  exemptions  from  the application  
of mandatory  guardrails  for high-risk AI systems based  on categories  of use. 
 
SUBMISSION:  THE  PRINCIPLES  SHOULD  DIFFERENTIATE  BETWEEN  
FOUR  CATEGORIES  OF RISK.  
The Proposals  Paper  focuses  exclusively  on ‘high risk’ AI. That is, the guardrails  on which  it seeks  
submissions  are proposed as being mandatory only for those  AI systems that are assessed  as ‘high 
risk’ pursuant  to principles -based  criteria.  The express  justification  for this focus  is to enable non-
high- risk AI systems ‘to flourish  unimpeded’.1 As a result,  developers  and deployers  of AI systems 
will be under  no legally  enforceable obligation  (within  the proposed regulatory framework) to adopt  
ex-ante measures  to avoid foreseeable,  yet not high,  risks to individuals,  groups  of individuals  or 
broader  society.   
The exclusive  focus  on regulating AI in high-risk settings,  without  the provision  of a mechanism  to 
prohibit  the deployment  of any particular  AI system  or impose  minimum  obligations  on non-high 
risk AI systems:  (a) presumes  that all design,  development  and deployment  of AI is prima  facie  
legitimate  (see our response  to question 4), (b) lacks  the flexibility  to appropriately  regulate GPAI  
models  (see our response  to question 6) and (c) risks stifling  the international trade  of, and foreign  
business  investment  in, Australian  AI industries.   
Due to the so-called  ‘Brussels effect’  (or international standard -setting)  of EU regulation,2 it is 
proposed that the dual objectives  of promoting  trustworthy AI industries  and enabling  
interoperability  will be best achieved  by adopting a risk-hierarchy  akin to that implemented  in the 
EU AI Act. We submit  that the principles  for assessing  risk, whether  or not accompanied  by a non-
 
1 Department  of Industry,  Science  and Resources,  Safe and Responsible  AI in Australia:  Introducing  Mandatory  
Guardrails  for AI in High -Risk  Settings  (Proposals  Paper,  September  2024) 4. 
2 Graham  Greenleaf,  ‘The “Brussels  Effect”  of the EU’s  “AI Act”  on Data Privacy  Outside  Europe’  (2021)  171 Privacy  
Laws  & Business  International  Report  1, 3-7. 4  |Introducing  Mandatory  Guardrails  for AI in High -Risk  Settings : Submission  
 
 exhaustive  list of example use cases,  should be capable of differentiating  between  four levels  of 
risk: Unacceptable,  High,  Limited/Medium and Minimal/Low.  This risk hierarchy  should function  
to prohibit  the deployment  of AI systems that carry  unacceptable levels  of risk (Unacceptable);  
impose  guardrails  on the development  and deployment  of AI systems that carry  real risks (whether  
High  or Limited); and only permit  those  AI systems that carry  minimal or insignificant  risks ‘to 
flourish unimpeded’  (Minimal/Low).   
With  respect  to the types  of obligations  that ought  to apply to developers  or deployers  of AI systems 
of ‘Limited/Medium’  risk, we propose  that, at a minimum,  these  should encompass  the 
transparency  obligations  provided for in Guardrails  6-8 of the Proposals  Paper  as well as the 
completion  of an AI Impact  Assessment,3 which  incorporates  the use of a Human  Rights  Impact  
Assessment  (HRIA).  The latter  should be added  to the list of guardrails  mandatory  for both High  
and Limited  risk AI systems (see our response  to question 8).  
 
SUBMISSION:  AI SYSTEMS SHOULD  NOT  BE CATEGORISED  BY USE  
IN ORDER  TO BE TREATED  SEPARATELY   
If AI systems are categorised  according  to their use, such as for defence or national  security  
purposes,  excluding such technology from  the proposed guardrails  will become straightforward.  
Excluding potentially  high- risk AI systems from  the guardrails  will undermine  the proposal’s  
objectives  to build community  trust and address  risks and harms  from  AI. 
The EU AI Act has removed  some  safeguards  for AI systems based  on categorisation  of use. 
Regardless  of their designation as high- risk, AI systems for law enforcement,  border  control  
management  and asylum  are excluded  from  the requirement  to be registered  in a public  database.  
Instead,  these  systems must  be registered  in a ‘secure non-public  section  of the EU database’,  and 
deployers  are permitted  to provide  more  limited  information  than other  high- risk AI systems.  
Australia  should not follow  the EU AI Act’s  example in this regard.   
A human rights  approach  to AI will appropriately balance individual  rights  with the public  interest.  
Proposed Principle  A (The  risk of adverse impacts  to an individual’s  rights  recognised  in 
Australian  human rights  law without  justification,  in addition to Australia’s  international  human 
rights  law obligations)  recognises  the centrality  of human rights  to AI regulation.  As the Proposal  
Paper  states,  ‘This  principle  also recognises  the notion of proportionality where  in some  
circumstances  interference with traditional  rights  and freedoms,  including human  rights,  may be 
justified.’   
All high- risk AI systems should be subject  to a human -rights  impact  assessment.  If an AI system  
could have  an adverse impact  on an individual’s  rights  recognised  in Australian  human rights  law 
but potentially  should be exempted  on the basis of public  interest,  the developer  and/or  deployer  
must  be able to (a) point  to the legal  basis for that system  and (b) meet  the tests of necessity  and 
proportionality.4 A robust  human rights  approach  to AI systems will avoid  situations  where 
potentially  dangerous  systems such as forecasting  and surveillance tools  used to predict,  interdict,  
 
3 See Salinger  Privacy , Submission in Response  to the Safe and Responsible  AI In Australia  Discussion  Paper  2023 
(Submission,  3 August  2023) 11-14. 
4 University  of Essex  and the Office  of the United  Nations  High  Commissioner  for Human  Rights,  Digital Border  
Governance: A Human  Rights  Based  Approach (September  2023).  https://www.ohchr.org/sites/default/files/2023-
09/Digital-Border-Governance -A-Human -Rights -Based -Approach.pdf.   5  |Introducing  Mandatory  Guardrails  for AI in High -Risk  Settings : Submission  
 
 and curtail  migration  leading  to ‘pushbacks’  and potential  death  and injury  must  be proportionate  to 
and necessary  for the public  interest.   
A human rights  approach  should be favoured over an approach  that arbitrarily  treats  some  usage  
categories  separately  based  on public  interest.   
 
 
QUESTION  3: Do the proposed  principles,  supported  by examples,  give  enough  clarity  and certainty  on high -risk AI 
settings  and high -risk AI models?  Is a more  defined approach,  with  a list of illustrative  uses,  needed?  
• If you prefer  a list-based  approach  (similar  to the EU and Canada),  what  use cases  should  we include?  
How  can this list capture  emerging  uses  of AI? 
• If you prefer  a principles -based  approach,  what  should  we address  in guidance  to give  the greatest  
clarity?  
QUESTION  4: Are there  high -risk use cases  that  the government  should  consider  banning  in its regulatory  
response  (for example,  where  there  is an unacceptable  level  of risk)?  If so, how  should  we define these?  
 
SUBMISSION TO QUESTIONS  3 AND  4: A LIST -BASED  APPROACH  
RISKS  CATEGORISING  SOME  AI SYSTEMS AS HIGH- RISK  DESPITE  
POSING  AN UNACCEPTABLE  LEVEL  OF RISK  IN SOME  CONTEXTS . 
A list-based  approach  that includes  cases  will have  limited  capacity  to be responsive  to the context  
in which  an AI system  is deployed.  Insufficient  consideration of context  will have  a negative  impact  
on proposed Principle  D, which  takes  into account  the ‘risk of adverse impacts  to groups  of 
individuals  or collective  rights  of cultural  groups’.   
The Proposal  Paper  provides  the example of biometrics  (i.e. AI systems used to identify  or 
categorise individuals,  assess behaviour  or mental  state,  or monitor  and influence  emotions),  which  
has been  identified  as a high- risk case use in the EU and Canada.   However,  biometrics  used in the 
context  of asylum  management  can pose an unacceptable  level  of risk if used as ‘lie detectors’  or to 
enable mass surveillance at the border  and in detention  facilities.5 While  surveillance and pseudo-
scientific  credibility  tools  may constitute  high- risk AI in some  contexts,  the risk becomes  
unacceptable when  applied  to vulnerable  populations  to which  Australia  owes  international  
protection obligations.  
There is also a risk of function  creep.  Although biometrics  are included as high- risk in the EU AI 
Act, ‘AI systems intended to be used for biometric  verification,  the sole purpose  of which  is to 
confirm  that a specific natural  person is the person he or she claims  to be’ are excluded.6  This  
exclusion fails to recognise  that biometrics  used for ‘digital  identification’  can still raise  concerns  
about  informed  consent,  privacy,  and ethical  approaches  to data and collaboration,7 especially  in the 
context  of forced  displacement.  
 
