The ability  of HRIAs  to bring  international  human rights  into the domestic  context  is particularly  
important  for Australia,  which  does not have  explicit  human rights -based  constitutional frameworks  
on which  the courts  can rely.25 It is recommended  that the requirement  for an HRIA  for high- risk 
and limited -risk AI systems is added  as a separate guardrail  and is not subsumed in the proposed 
Guardrail  2 (‘Establish  and implement a risk management  process  to identify  and mitigate  risks’)  
as this would potentially  diminish  the significance of human rights  as a key accountability  standard  
for AI systems that inherently  carry  a level  of risk. 
 
QUESTION  12: Do you have  any suggestions  for reducing  the regulatory  burden  on small -to-medium  sized  
businesses  applying  guardrails?  
  
SUBMISSION:  MANDATORY  GUARDRAILS  SHOULD  APPLY  EQUALLY  
TO SMALL -TO-MEDIUM  SIZED  BUSINESSES.   
While  a small business  exemption from  mandatory  guardrails  is not explicitly  contemplated  by the 
Proposals  Paper,  the question regarding  regulatory  burden on small- to-medium  sized  businesses,  in 
combination with the exclusive  focus  of the proposals  on high- risk AI, is reminiscent  of concerns  
raised  in the context  of Australian  privacy  law.  
  
When  the Privacy Act 1988 (Cth)  was first extended  to the private  sector  in 2000,  an exemption  was 
introduced for small businesses (with  an annual  turnover  of $3 million  or less).26 The rationales  for 
this exemption,  at the time,  were  that: (a) most  small businesses did not collect  vast amounts  of 
personal  information,  (b) the costs of implementing  a privacy  protection  system  would be greater  in 
relative  terms  for small businesses,  and (c) it is desirable to minimise  compliance costs not justified  
by a high privacy  risk.27  
  
Based  on recent  statistics,  the effect  of this small  business exemption is that up to 95% of actively  
trading Australian  businesses are exempt  from  compliance with the Australian  Privacy  Principles  
(APPs).28 By contrast,  at least 84% of Australian  small businesses have  adopted online  services  and 
rely on up to 30 separate digital information  technologies,  which  in turn increases  the privacy  and 
other  cyber  security  risks posed by such businesses.29 The pandemic  no doubt  accelerated  the 
digitisation  of Australian  small businesses and shone  a light on the types  of personal  and sensitive  
information  they collect.   
 
