Unsurprisingly,  therefore,  the small business  exemption was one of the most  heavily  criticised  
features  of Australian  privacy  law during the Attorney- General’s  Privacy  Act Review  2020- 2023.  
Submitters  to the review  noted,  among  other  things,  that the exemption does not reflect  community 
expectations,  that annual  turnover  is a poor indicator  of privacy  impact,  and that the exemption 
represents  an international  anomaly  with no comparable jurisdiction  excusing small businesses from  
compliance with privacy  law.30 This means  that the current  small business  exemption not only 
weakens  the privacy  protections  ostensibly afforded  to Australians  under  the Privacy Act 1988 
(Cth),  it adversely  impacts  the ability  of small businesses to engage  in international  trade and 
collaboration due to a lack of jurisdictional alignment.31 In the absence of a GDPR  ‘adequacy  
decision’,  for example,  Australian  businesses trading in the EU market  are often  required  to adopt  
contractual  provisions  pursuant  to which  they warrant  the protection  of personal  information  to an 
‘adequate’  level.32 Having no obligations  under  domestic  law to adopt  privacy  protective  practices,  
such contractual  provisions  are likely  to impose  a disproportionate  administrative  cost on Australian  
small businesses as compared  to regulated  APP entities.   
  
With  68% of Australian  businesses having already  implemented  AI technologies,  as of March  2023,  
a further  23% planning to implement  AI technologies  in the 12 months  subsequent,33 and the 
Australian  government  investing close  to $2 billion  to support  innovation that includes  AI,34 it 
would be remiss  of the Australian  Government  to adopt  an approach  to AI regulation that arbitrarily  
fastens  to the size or turnover  of an AI provider  (whether  developer  or deployer).  As the Proposals  
Paper  highlights,  the development  and implementation  of AI is on a rapid  growth  trajectory,  with 
research  indicating  that the Australian  AI ecosystem  is still maturing.35 This means  that adopting an 
approach  that exempts  smaller  or less significant  entities  today,  at the nascent  stages  of AI adoption,  
is unlikely to adequately  protect  individuals  from  harm  caused  by evolving applications  of AI 
across  the economy into the future.36 
  
The objectives  of the proposed AI guardrails  are to: address  risks and harms  from  AI, build  public  
trust and confidence  in AI, and provide  businesses with greater  regulatory certainty,37 including by 
promoting interoperability  with the other  countries’  approaches  to AI regulation.38 These  objectives  
will be best served  by flexibly  fastening  mandatory guardrails  to the inherent  risk profile  of an AI 
system,  as is the approach  adopted within  both the EU AI Act and Canada’s  proposed AIDA.  Such  
an approach  would afford  Australians  with greater  assurance (by providing Australian  businesses of 
all sizes  with the opportunity to prove  trustworthy ), while  simultaneously  encouraging  the creation  
of a level  playing field – one that minimises  regulatory burdens  on small- to-medium  sized  
 
