In light of the foregoing,  we submit  that the most  appropriate  measures  to adopt  to reduce the 
regulatory burden  on small- to-medium  sized  businesses,  are to: (1) delay  by 12 months  the 
application  of mandatory  guardrails  to small or small -to-medium  sized  businesses and (2) allocate 
government  funding  to the development  of tools  designed  to educate and guide  small or small- to-
medium  sized  businesses on their understanding  of, and compliance with,  mandatory guardrails.  
Such  measures  acknowledge  that, particularly  in the context  of existing  Australian  laws,  small- to-
medium  sized  businesses do not commence on the same  playing field as their larger  counterparts.  
 
 
Regulatory options  to mandate  guardrails  
QUESTION  13: Which  legislative  option  do you feel will best  address  the use of AI in high -risk settings?  What 
opportunities  should  the government  take  into account  in considering  each  approach?  
  
SUBMISSION:  SUBJECT  TO THE  EXECUTION  AND  FINDINGS  OF A 
COMPREHENSIVE  REGULATORY  GAP  ASSESSMENT,  A WHOLE  OF 
ECONOMY -APPROACH  IS THE  MOST  APPROPRIATE  VEHICLE  FOR  
MAKING  THE  GUARDRAILS  MANDATORY.  
The Proposals  Paper  considers  three options  for implementing  the proposed mandatory  guardrails  
and seeks  submissions  on the most  suitable  option.  Three interrelated  matters  complicate  a response  
to this question.  First,  the Proposals  Paper  has been  issued  before  the finalisation  of parallel  
inquiries  into AI in Australia.  For example,  in May 2024,  the Senate Select  Committee  on Adopting 
AI received  submissions  addressing the opportunities,  risks and harms  arising  from  the adoption  of 
AI technologies  as well as international  approaches  to mitigating  AI risks and fostering  responsible  
AI industries.39 While  the Committee  was originally  due to present  its final report  in September  
2024,  the reporting  date has been  extended  to 26 November  2024.  Consequently,  submissions  to the 
Proposals  Paper  are based  on an incomplete  picture  of both domestic  and international  AI 
ecosystems.  
  
Second,  the disruptive  nature  of AI, as a form  of radical  innovation,  has produced many  calls for a 
comprehensive  regulatory gap assessment  in the context  of Australian  law. For example,  
recommendation  8 of the NSW  Parliamentary  Inquiry into Artificial Intelligence was that ‘the 
Government  conduct  a regulatory gap analysis,  as soon as possible’.40 To date,  these  calls have  not 
been  met, notwithstanding  targeted  attempts  from  legal  academics  to do so.41 The absence of a 
 
