 12  |Introducing  Mandatory  Guardrails  for AI in High -Risk  Settings : Submission  
 
 comprehensive  audit  of the application  of existing  Australian  law, conducted by Government  in 
consultation with relevant  industry,  legal  and technical  experts,  affects  an informed decision  being 
made between  incrementally  adapting existing  legal  frameworks  to incorporate  the mandatory  
guardrails,  on the one hand,  and crafting  an economy -wide  (technology- specific)  AI Act, on the 
other.   
  
Third,  the introduction of ‘AI’,  ‘automated  decision -making’  or ‘algorithm’  regulation  in 
international  jurisdictions  such as the EU, China,  Canada and New  Zealand  presents  an opportunity  
for Australia  to learn  from  those  other  jurisdictions,  critically  assess the efficacy  of their different  
approaches  and understand what  is required  to facilitate  interoperability  across  jurisdictions.  With,  
for example,  the first provisions  of both the EU AI Act and Canada’s  AIDA set to commence in 
2025,  insufficient  time has lapsed  to take up this opportunity.  While  it is important for Australia  to 
mitigate  adverse impacts  associated  with AI in a timely  manner,  such that law can play a proactive 
role in shaping the development  of responsible  AI industries,  it is important  that Australia  
implement an approach  that appropriately  and effectively  achieves  the policy objectives  of the 
proposed mandatory guardrails.   
  
An obvious  strength of domain- specific regulatory  frameworks  operating  in Australia  (whether  
competition  and consumer  law, discrimination  law, privacy  law, administrative  law, corporations  
law, intellectual property law or evidence law) is that they are designed to respond to well-defined  
problems  in a values -driven,  technologically  neutral  manner.  Where socio -technical  advancements  
render  such frameworks  unfit for purpose,  their technological  neutrality  allows  for incremental  
amendment  subject  to the knowledge,  resources  and will of respective governments.42 For example,  
in response  to increases  in the vulnerability of individuals  to privacy  invasions,  behavioural  
manipulation  and discrimination  in the face of seismic  leaps  in digital  information  technologies  and 
practices  (inclusive  of AI), a series  of recommendations  have  been  made to reform  both the Privacy 
Act 1988 (Cth)  and the Competition  and Consumer  Act 2010  (Cth).43 Implementing  these  
recommendations,  and strengthening other  relevant  domain- specific frameworks,  will play a vital 
role in the effective mitigation  of AI-facilitated  risks and harms.   
  
We support,  therefore,  a multifaceted  approach  to AI regulation  in which  efforts  to implement 
targeted  AI legislation  do not detract  from  the need to execute domain- specific law reform.  To 
minimise  the risks of regulatory  complexity,  duplication and confusion associated  with a 
multifaceted  approach,  we urge Government,  in consultation  with relevant  industry,  legal  and 
technical  experts,  to conduct  a comprehensive  audit  of existing  Australian  law to: (a) assess the 
application  of domain- specific regulatory  frameworks  in contexts  involving  AI, (b) identify where  
reforms  to those  existing  frameworks  are necessary,  and (c) identify the scope  of AI-targeted  
legislation  to address  gaps in existing  frameworks.  To the extent  that a comprehensive  audit  
demonstrates  the need  for additional  ex-ante protections  in the form  of guardrails,  we support  a 
 
