 whole  of economy approach  to mandating  those  guardrails  based  on the inherent  risk profile  of an 
AI system.  
  
Our general  support  for an economy- wide  AI Act is based  on the following  further  reasons . 
  
A Domain- Specific  Approach  
The Proposals  Paper  states  that a domain- specific approach  would involve  the adaptation  of existing  
regulatory frameworks  to include  the guardrails.  It would require  a review  of each relevant  piece of 
legislation  to address  gaps and embed  relevant  guardrails  in existing  regulatory  frameworks  to 
address  the risks associated  with AI. 
  
While  a comprehensive  review  of existing  regulatory  frameworks  is desirable,  the aim of such a 
review  would be to enhance the scope,  adaptability  and enforcement  of the domain- specific 
protections  afforded  within  each framework  to better  respond to the domain- relevant  risks and 
harms  facilitated  by an AI augmented  sociotechnical  landscape.  To the extent  that gaps in existing  
frameworks  demonstrate  the need  for ex-ante protections  unique  to AI-systems,  they should not be 
introduced into a series  of disparate Acts.  As the Proposals  Paper  acknowledges,  this approach  
would exacerbate existing  gaps and inconsistencies  across  regulatory regimes,44 rendering both 
industry compliance and individual  complaint  burdensome. 
  
For example,  a domain- specific approach  to implementing  Guardrail  2, for example,  which  is to 
‘Establish  and implement  a risk management  process  to identify  and mitigate  risks’ , would require  
the insertion  of provisions  for an appropriate  ex-ante assessment  (at a minimum)  into consumer  
legislation,  anti-discrimination  legislation  and privacy  legislation.  While  the third  amendment  
would merely  involve  strengthening a pre-existing  requirement  of the Privacy Act 1988  (Cth)  
regarding the need to conduct  a Privacy  Impact  Assessment  (PIA)  in certain  circumstances  (as 
recommended  by Proposal  13.1 of the Attorney- General’s  Privacy  Act Review  Report),45 
implementing  such a requirement  across  three disparate domains  at the federal  level  alone  would 
involve  the amendment  of between  three  to seven  primary  Acts,46 overseen  by three distinct 
regulators,  with no guarantee for consistency  in the threshold for, or methodology of, the various  
impact  assessments ultimately  adopted.  
  
In addition  to exacerbating  gaps and inconsistencies  across  relevant  regulatory regimes,  such an 
approach  is likely  to produce  much  debate and lobbying that will lead to undesirable  delays.   
  
A Framework  Approach  
A framework  approach  would involve  new framework  legislation  that defines  the guardrails  to 
apply and the threshold for when  they would  apply.  The provisions  of the framework  regulation are 
said to become enforceable only once  they are ‘activated’,  such as when  other  laws are amended  to 
refer  to these  provisions.  
  
 
44 Department  of Industry,  Science  and Resources,  Safe and Responsible  AI in Australia:  Introducing  Mandatory  
Guardrails  for AI in High -Risk  Settings  (Proposals  Paper,  September  2024) 47. 
45 Attorney -General’s  Department,  Privacy  Act Review  (Final  Report,  2022) 9. 
46 Competition  and Consumer  Act 2010  (Cth);  Privacy  Act 1988  (Cth);  Age Discrimination  Act 2004 (Cth);  Disability  
Discrimination  Act 1992  (Cth);  Racial  Discrimination  Act 1975 (Cth);  Sex Discrimination  Act 1984 (Cth).  14  |Introducing  Mandatory  Guardrails  for AI in High -Risk  Settings : Submission  
 
 Many  of the reasons  discussed  above  are equally  as applicable to a framework  approach.  However,  
there is an added  complexity  regarding  when  the guardrails  are “activated” and therefore  
enforceable.  This has the potential  to exacerbate the risks associated  with an undefined delay  in the 
implementation  of mandatory guardrails.  
  
A Whole of Economy  Approach  
The third  option  cited  in the Proposals  Paper  is to introduce  a new AI-specific Act to implement the 
proposed mandatory guardrails  for AI in high- risk settings.  A whole  of economy approach  is the 
most  desirable of the three options  presented.  On condition that a comprehensive  regulatory  gap 
assessment  demonstrates  the need  to enhance ex-ante protections  through  AI-specific guardrails,  a 
single,  uniform  legislation  provides  the desired  coordination and unification of the regulatory  
approach  to AI use across  all domains.   
  
AI is in its infancy , and its applications  are likely  to become increasingly  novel.  The 
implementation  of regulations  that are particularised  to the extent  that different  use cases  affect  
different  domains  is more  likely  to require  reform  at a higher  rate than one, uniform  regulation.  
  
The use of a single,  uniform  regulation  is more  likely  to achieve Parliamentary  cooperation  and can 
incorporate  sufficient  flexibility  such that emerging  AI technologies  are caught  within  the purview  
of a singular  Act and, therefore,  regulation.  This is preferred  as a sector -specific framework  is likely  
to result  in regulatory  gaps that may be exploited by developers.  
  
Not only would  a whole  of economy approach  standardise  processes  and requirements  across  
industries,  affording  greater  efficiency  to the regulation of an infant  technology,  it would  promote  
interoperability  with other  jurisdictions  adopting  a similarly  targeted  approach  to the regulation of 
AI. 
  
The nuanced  differences  between  domains  can be accommodated  by vesting the relevant  Minister  
with the power  to approve  negotiated  guardrails  attuned  to the specific needs  of an industry  sector.  
This power  should be provisional,  and the default  mandatory guardrails  should apply  until the 
negotiations  are complete.  
 
