RANZCR Response to the mandatory guardrails for AI in high -risk settings: proposals 
pape r 
 
 The Royal Australian and New Zealand College of Radiologists (RANZCR) is committed to improving health outcomes for all, by educating and supporting clinical radiologists and radiation 
oncologists. RANZCR is dedicated to setting standards, professional training, assessment and 
accreditation, and advocating access to quality care in both professions to create healthier communities.    
RANZCR creates a positive impact by driving change, focusing on the professional development of its members and advancing best practice health policy and advocacy, to enable better patient outcomes.    
RANZCR members are critical to health services: clinical radiology is central to the diagnosis and treatment of disease and injury and radiation oncology is a vital component in the treatment of cancer.    
RANZCR is supportive of the initiative by the Department of Industry, Science and Resources, as well as the work of other government departments including the Department of Health and 
Aged Care (DOHAC) and the Therapeutic Goods Administration (TGA) in addressing the 
regulatory impact of artificial intelligence (AI) on the Australian population.  
While RANZCR advocates for robust regulation in the medical AI space
i, we recognise the need 
to strike a balance in which we can develop an environment that fosters innovation, growth, and 
a workforce that is AI ‘literate’,  to enable the successful  adoption of AI in healthcare.  
Due to the nature of our organisation, our response centres on the impact of AI on healthcare. AI holds transformational potential, particularly to streamline routine tasks, minimise workload, automate quality improvement, replace less precise computer algorithms that are already in practice,  and perform administrative tasks. RAN ZCR believes that the safe implementation of AI 
could prove to be a contributing factor in assisting medical specialists in managing their increasing workloads effectively. Failure to implement AI technology not only poses risks but also represents missed opportunities for enhancing patient outcomes, optimising  healthcare 
delivery, and providing healthcare workers with the required tools to do a better job.  
Within the mandatory guardrails  proposal, healthcare application of AI tools is captured under 
the definition of a high- risk device, under the domain of access to essential private and public 
services. RANZCR considers this appropriate as the use, or misuse of AI devices in medicine can cause harm to individual patients, and to broader patient groups .  
Currently  the regulation of AI devices for clinical use is  captured under the TGA regulation of 
Software as a Medical Device (SaMD) including AI. There are regulatory  exemptions within the 
TGA for lower risk devices, those generally not used in clinical care, such as wearables.  The 
TGA assess SaMD on a technology agnostic risk -based model, which is a system used by 
various jurisdictions internationally and considered appropriate by RANZCR.  
A core tenet of medicine which underpins medical practice, embedded in the Hippocratic oath, 
is “primum non nocere” (do no harm). RANZCR supports a preventative approach to regulating AI within healthcare, rather than a reactionary approach which may see patient safety 
compromised.  
 
 There is currently ambiguity of responsibility across the multiple actors in the development, 
deployment and use of AI within healthcare and RANZCR is supportive of measures which clearly outline the responsibilities of each party, and holds the actors accountable for meeting these responsibilities.  
RANZCR is b roadly  supportive of  the proposed mandatory  guardrails , and would like to offer 
specific feedback on the following guardrails : 
Guardrail Three  
Data must also be legally obtained.  
RANZCR is supportive of appropriate protocols being established for the procurement and 
management of data used in AI development . Healthcare data are amongst the most sensitive 
data that can be held about an individual and the rights of the patient, including the right to revoke consent ( e.g., removal from a dataset) must be observed. The requirement of patient 
consent for data used to train AI devices  is an important concept that requires nuanced 
consideration when defining the parameters of “legally obtained data”.  
RANZCR is supportive of amendments to the Privacy Act to better support the use of AI, while protecting the rights of Australians.  
Guardrail Four  
Organisations must systematically gather and analyse data to assess the behaviour of high- risk 
AI systems over time . 
Whilst  RANZCR advocates for post implementation monitoring of AI devices , further 
consideration is required if patient data is to be transferred to a developer for the purposes of performance monitoring of an AI device.  It should be ensured that this can be done in a way 
that does not compromise patient privacy, but still allows for effective outcome monitoring.  
Guardrail Six:  
Organisations must inform people when AI is used to make or inform decisions relevant to them. 
RANZCR is supportive of end-users ( including patients ) being informed of the use of AI, in the 
provision of healthcare, however there are varying degrees to which AI can inform a decision.   
RANZCR is supportive of end- users (including patients) being informed of the use of AI, in the 
provision of healthcare, however there are varying degrees to which AI can inform a decision.  
Adoption of AI is accelerating and becoming ubiquitous in the healthcare setting.  Some AI tasks 
are of negligible or no clinical risk (e.g. billing).   It would be impractical to inform consumers 
about the use of AI whenever it is used, and therefore RANZCR  believes that consumers need 
not be informed when AI is used for tasks that have negligible or no clinical risks.  
When AI is involved in clinical activities such as  diagnosis, or treatment planning, a generic 
declaration within the informed consent process may be sufficient for low -risk uses. For higher -
risk applications, such as clinical decision making , a more specific declaration that details the 
use of AI may be appropriate
ii. This would facilitate transparency while respecting the 
practicality of disclosing AI usage.  
Guardrail Eight  
Deployers must report adverse incidents and significant model failures to developers, who can 
then issue improvements to the model . 
The World Health Organisation defines a near miss as “an error that has the potential to cause an adverse event (patient harm) but fails to do so because of chance or because it is intercepted.”  
Within medicine near misses that involve medical devices can also be reported to the regulator. This is an important mechanism, as identifying the cause of a near miss in medicine, and taking 
appropriate actions to rectify the root issue, can reduce the risks of future adverse events iii, this 
principle may  be applied to high -risk areas outside of medicine, and certainly should be retained 
in the healthcare environment.  
While RANZCR does  not have a specific preference on  the regulatory approach adopted to 
implement these mandatory guardrails , we would like to note that healthcare operates under a complex and established regulatory framework, which should not be adversely impacted or 
disrupted by the introduction of new legislation or regulation.  
RANZCR is supportive of a system that promotes an AI enabled workforce and strengthens the public’s understanding and trust of the use of AI. This should be supported by advice and support from the regulators in the form of learning materials, templates and guidance resources for both businesses and the public.  
RANZCR thanks the DISR for the opportunity to comment on the proposed guardrails, and 
welcomes change that will allow for the safe implementation of AI.  For queries or further 
information, please contact Kirsten Fitzpatrick , Manager, Policy Development on (02) 9268 
9740  or email kirsten.fitpatrick@ranzcr.edu.au
.  
 
Yours sincerely , 
 
    
