    AI Consultation Team Department of Industry, Science and Resources aiconsultation@industry.gov.au  4 October 2024  IBM's response to the Proposals Paper for Introducing Mandatory  Guardrails for AI in High-Risk Settings  On behalf of IBM, we thank the Australian Government Department of Industry, Science and Resources (DISR) for the opportunity to provide feedback to the on the Safe and Responsible AI in Australia—Proposals Paper for Introducing Mandatory Guardrails (Guardrails) for AI in High-Risk Settings (Consultation Paper). At the outset, we appreciate the Government’s aim to support innovation whilst ensuring good governance and integrating protections for high-risk artificial intelligence (AI) uses in Australia. IBM is committed to developing and helping others build and use AI responsibly.   For over two decades IBM has been leading industry practice for developing and implementing responsible AI. In 2018  IBM released its ‘Trust and Transparency Principles’, designed for the era of AI, which include key commitments to develop and apply explainable and transparent AI in a manner that is designed to augment, not replace, human intelligence and capabilities. Since then IBM has further developed its approach, tools and policies to promote safe and responsible AI at every stage of the AI lifecycle. This commitment to safe and responsible AI is incorporated into all our AI products, including WatsonX, IBM’s latest commercial generative AI  and data platform, via IBM’s Ethics by Design methodology along with its internal governance structures including the IBM AI Ethics Board which reviews all AI related issues at IBM. IBM has also outlined its support for a ‘Precision Regulation’ approach  in regard to how policymakers should promote safe and responsible AI whilst still allowing innovation to flourish and obtain the benefits that AI can provide to society and the economy.    Definitions of AI and the Application of the Mandatory Guardrails  IBM believes that as far as possible governments should work together to adopt a clear and harmonised approach to the issues raised by AI across jurisdictions. Since AI development and deployment is occurring in a global context, consistent terminology and harmonised guardrails are critical to promote a shared understanding of the AI benefits and challenges, and make it easier for AI systems to be widely adopted and more effectively regulated across like-minded jurisdictions seeking to address substantially the same issues and concerns. In this regard, and as a starting point we support the internationally recognised definition of AI from the Organisation for Economic Co-operation and Development (OECD), which is a robust foundation for cross-border collaboration.   We also support DISR's objective to make AI regulation future-proof and technology-neutral. We agree that the mandatory guardrails should only apply where the intended use of AI is ‘high risk’, and not be based on the underlying technology. In regard to the critical issue of what is ‘high risk use’ we find the Consultation Paper’s Proposed Principles to Guide the Assessment of whether a use is High Risk to be useful, but strongly believe that alone these principles are too vague to be used as  IBM Australia Limited ABN 79 000 024 733 259 George Street SYDNEY NSW  2000 Telephone: 132 426   
 a basis for industry to determine whether it is covered by the proposed mandatory obligations, particularly given that penalties are likely to apply for non-compliance.   For example, the reference in clause a) to ‘the risk of adverse impacts on an individual’s rights recognised in Australian human rights law without justification, in addition to Australia’s international human rights law obligations’ assumes that ‘Australian human rights law’ is sufficiently understood, clear and identifiable, which is not the case at least as far as most commercial AI developers would be concerned. As the Consultation Paper notes, without a formal Bill of Rights or similar document in force at a national level, the scope of ‘Australian human rights law’ is vague and noting various state legislation in the area, inconsistent across the Australian federation, therefore this principle would need greater clarification.   Similarly, the reference in clause e) to ‘the risk of adverse impacts to the broader Australian economy, society, environment and rule of law’ is extremely broad and open to many and varied interpretations.   Accordingly we recommend that further specific guidance should be introduced and that this should be based on whether the AI system is specifically developed to make consequential or significant decisions which determine an individual’s rights in relation to critical issues such as housing, employment, credit, education, access to essential public and private services, law enforcement, migration and border protection, and the administration of justice and democratic processes. We believe that the EU AI Act provides a useful reference for greater specificity in terms of high risk uses.   We would also recommend that the reasonable likelihood of any of these risks occurring, rather than a risk existing, albeit remote, should be part of the test as to whether an AI use is subject to the Mandatory Guardrails. In our view only reasonably foreseeable risks should be subject to these Guardrails, rather than remote or highly unlikely risks and this should be made more explicit in the principles that will apply in order for the Mandatory Guardrails to apply.   IBM is also concerned that the definition of AI systems that includes ‘an ensemble of several components, including one or more AI models, that is designed to be particularly useful to humans in some way’ should explicitly exclude AI Development Tools. AI Development Tools often serve as building blocks for various user-designed applications and thereby may indeed be ‘particularly useful to humans in some way’ as they assist in developing user-designed AI systems, but they are not in themselves an AI system. For example, IBM Watson Studio is a workspace that uses various collaboration and open-source tools. These ‘building block tools’ help operationalize AI models and applications but are steps in the AI development process, not an AI system in itself. We note that DISR does not intend to cover AI models, i.e. the raw, mathematical essence that is often the ‘engine’ of AI applications, but this definition needs to be clarified to ensure that AI development tools and platforms are not inadvertently covered by the definition for the purposes of mandatory obligations.  General Purpose AI  IBM does not support the Consultation Paper’s proposal to include all General Purpose AI (GPAI) as a ‘high risk use’ as a matter of course. This is overly broad and is likely to include benign uses of GPAI which will add unnecessary costs and deter innovation in a wide range of areas. The current proposal will also include GPAI models used for Research and Development, which is not of itself a high risk use. Accordingly we recommend that DISR does not apply mandatory guardrails to ALL GPAI models, and only apply the mandatory obligations in cases where the GPAI is actually proposed to be used in high risk settings.     
 An alternative is to treat GPAI Models in line with the EU AI Act’s definition of a high risk GPAI which only treats a subset of GPAI models as high risk. Under the EU AI Act, GPAI is treated as high risk if it is ‘an AI model including where such an AI model is trained with a large amount of data using self-supervision at scale, that displays significant generality and is capable of competently performing a wide range of distinct tasks regardless of the way the model is placed on the market and that can be integrated into a variety of downstream systems of applications, except AI models that are used for research, development of prototyping activities before they are released onto the market’. In other words, the EU looks at the particular characteristics of the GPAI model and determines whether it has a particular systemic risk that makes it ‘high risk’. It also excludes research models. If DISR wants to include a general approach to GPAI, we recommend that it adopt the EU AI Act approach.   Unacceptable Risk and Prohibited Systems  As a responsible technology innovator, IBM firmly supports the prohibition of certain practices that could lead to the misuse of AI. We oppose the use of technologies like facial recognition for mass surveillance, racial profiling, and violations of fundamental rights. IBM was the first global company to announce that it would no longer offer general-purpose facial recognition or analysis software, in line with our commitment to responsible AI use, and we would support the Australian government’s adoption of this position.  Interoperability  IBM welcomes the Consultation Paper’s support for the development of a regulatory framework that aligns nationally and with like-minded jurisdictions to reduce the compliance burden for industry and government. We note in particular the discussion of interoperability  with reference to the ISO/IEC 42001:2023 AI Management System Standard, the EU AI Act and Canada’s proposed Artificial Intelligence Data Act (AIDA).   However, broad support for interoperability will only be beneficial for industry if there are practical benefits for organisations operating across jurisdictions. Specifically, we recommend that there be a ‘cross-mapping’ process undertaken, along with a statement that makes it clear that if an entity complies with, for example the EU AI Act, this will be considered (either alone or with specific identified additions), sufficient to render the entity compliant with the Australian regime, and visa versa. For example, there would be enormous efficiencies, cost savings, speed to market and trade opportunities for Australian companies, large and small, who have already fully satisfied their obligations under the Australian Mandatory Guardrails to be able to offer their high risk uses of AI in the EU, without having to go through the EU’s slightly different but also rigorous and comprehensive compliance regime. Similarly, companies who are compliant with EU AI Act should be deemed to have satisfied the Australian Mandatory Guardrails for AI in High-Risk Settings.   It should be noted that the benefits of this approach are likely to be much greater for small to medium companies for which the compliance and record keeping costs of fulfilling their obligations under the Australian Mandatory Guardrails will already be significant. If these companies needed to complete a similar process in another market, particularly one based on very similar concepts and principles such as the EU, this is likely to deter them from entering that market, or add significant costs, with little substantive benefit or change to the actual level of safety of the AI system proposed to be developed or deployed.   Developers and Deployers   IBM welcomes the distinction between developers and deployers as useful in reflecting the different roles that parties in the AI supply chain play. However, the definitions in the Consultation paper are overly broad and simplistic. We recommend that the definition of ‘Developer’ be limited to those   
 entities that design, code or produce’ an AI system and the definition of “Deployer’ be limited to those who ‘use’ an AI system.   There also seems to be an assumption in Attachment E of the Consultation Paper that there will be an ongoing relationship between the Developer and the Deployer which may not reflect commercial realities. In particular we note that there is an obligation in Guardrail 4 that developers ‘continuously monitor the [AI] system and ensure that it operates as expected’. This does not take into account the fact that there may not be any ongoing relationship between the developer and the deployer. For example, the original AI developer may no longer exist, key staff of the developer may no longer be available, or the developer may not wish to provide, and the deployer may not wish to pay, the original developer for these additional services, instead perhaps preferring to use another provider or undertake ongoing monitoring inhouse. In addition, developers may not be willing and able to share updated commercially valuable data, versions or AI systems with a deployer after deployment of an AI system.   The regulatory regime should not obligate developers or deployers to remain in a ‘vendor lock in scenario’ whereby each party is obligated to continue to provide, and pay, for ongoing services from each other.  For these commercial reasons, any obligation for ongoing monitoring and evaluation must be the responsibility of the deployer after the AI system has been contracted and delivered by the deployer.   Guardrail 8: Be Transparent with other Organisations across the AI Supply Chain about Data, Models and Systems to help them effectively address risk.   Similarly, in relation to Guardrail 8 IBM is concerned that the proposal that ‘deployers must report adverse incidents and significant model failures to developers, who can then issue improvements to the model’, fails to recognise the commercial reality that AI supply chains and contractual relationships are complex.   Although improvements and changes may be required to address poor performance and/or adverse outcomes, once the contractual obligations of the AI developer are complete, it may not be possible or appropriate for deployers to ‘report adverse incidents and significant model failures’ to the original developers (who may or may not still exist). In some longer supply chains, developers may not even know the identity of the party who ultimately acquires and deploys the AI system.   Whilst we support the principle of addressing negative outcomes in AI systems after deployment, from a commercial and practical perspective there are likely to be a range of options available to deployers who seek to improve and address failures and avoid future adverse incidents. The Mandatory Guardrails should ensure that the obligation is on the deployer to manage this in the most commercial and practical way, rather than assume that the original developer is the key party who can and will address these issues.   Any obligation to share information also needs to also take into account commercial confidentiality and privacy issues. For example, deployers may have integrated sensitive data into the AI system and therefore sharing data with a developer or any other party for the purposes of improving the AI system needs to be managed and compliant with Australian Privacy legislation and any other contractual arrangements and obligations that may apply.  Guardrail 9: Keep and Maintain Records to allow Third Parties to Assess Compliance with Guidelines    
 
As a matter of policy, IBM publishes AI Fact Sheets, which summarise aspects of our AI system's purpose, architecture, training, and deployment. IBM also collates and maintains detailed records in line with its AI Ethics by Design Playbook and pursuant to the IBM AI governance regime that operates across the company.   IBM appreciates the proposed requirement that developers maintain records so that, if challenged, it can establish that it has complied with applicable mandatory guardrails for high risk uses, once the regime is in place. However,  mandatory disclosure of these detailed records should only extend to regulatory agencies who have legal authority to investigate a complaint, and we strongly believe that even in those cases developers should not require the release of any trade secrets, commercial information or other data that could impact on network security or breach privacy obligations. For the avoidance of doubt, IBM does not support any requirement that third parties, other than those with legal authority or otherwise contracted to do so, should have access to commercial and technical records for the purposes of certifying compliance with the Mandatory Guardrails.   Regulatory Options  IBM appreciates the Consultation Paper’s discussion of the options available to the Government  for implementing the Mandatory Guardrails regime and the invitation to comment on this issue. IBM’s preference is for ‘Option 1 (a Domain Specific Approach): Adapting Existing Regulatory Frameworks’.   This option leverages the domain expertise held by existing regulators in the areas of healthcare, finance, housing, consumer protection and others, domains which vary greatly in terms of scope, risks, potential impacts on consumers and other stakeholders and allows practical issues specific to that sector to be considered. It also allows existing laws and sector specific practices to be taken into account in developing a response to high risk uses in each particular industry.   As noted in the Consultation Paper, ‘drafting directions’ could be developed by the Office of Parliamentary Counsel or the Attorney General’s Office to maximize consistency of definitions and penalties across government, which is highly desirable. Whilst not our preferred approach, Option 2 (a framework approach): Adopting Existing Regulatory Frameworks through Framework Legislation’ would enable a more legal approach to be taken to the definitions and approach to regulatory powers and exercise of regulatory powers and obligations, and on this basis also has some merit.   However, IBM would be particularly concerned if ‘Option 3 (a Whole of Economy Approach: Introducing a New AI -Specific Act’ were introduced. In our view this would create a cumbersome and complex piece of legislation which tries to address all levels of risk and encompass all industries. This would require all developers and deployers in the AI eco-system to comprehend and assess potential obligations across the full reach of the Act, even though they may only wish to operate in a small and/or specific industry segment. This is likely to lead to significant legal and compliance costs, delays and have the effect of slowing innovation. There is also a risk of conflict and confusion regarding existing laws and policies in various industry sectors, regulatory overlap, along with other inefficiencies.    Thank you again for the opportunity to comment on the Consultation Paper. Please do not hesitate to contact me if you require any further information or wish to discuss.  Yours sincerely,   Kaaren Koomen AM Director, Government and Regulatory Affairs E: kaaren@au1.ibm.com       ","IBM_Submission_to_Mandatory_Guardrails_for_AI_in_High_Risk_Settings_04102024.100996e5","IBM_Submission_to_Mandatory_Guardrails_for_AI_in_High_Risk_Settings_04102024.100996e5.pdf","pdf","C:\\Users\\james.barro\\Documents\\rsm-nvidia-hackathon\\data\\Submission_Docs\\Processed\\IBM_Submission_to_Mandatory_Guardrails_for_AI_in_High_Risk_Settings_04102024.100996e5.pdf","1"
