SEEK | AI Regulation  1 
 
 
Submission | Safe and Responsible 
AI in Australia 
 
Response to the Proposals Paper for introducing 
mandatory guardrails in high-risk settings 
 
FOR PUBLICATION  
4 October 2024   
SEEK | AI Regulation  1 
 
 Executive summary 
 
SEEK, a market leader in online employment marketplaces founded and headquartered in Melbourne, has been 
leveraging technology to help people live more fulfilling and productive working lives and help organisations succeed 
since 1997.  
 
For over a decade, SEEK has been investing in safe and responsible data and AI to make employment markets more 
accessible, transparent and efficient for jobseekers and hirers. SEEK focuses on improving the process of finding a job 
by identifying how AI creates efficiencies and better experiences for jobseekers and hirers. Our technologies, products 
and tools are designed to complement, not replace, the human element of recruitment. 
 
SEEK facilitates over a third of all job placements in Australia,1 and this proportion has grown steadily over the past few 
years. AI is used to better match people with jobs. It enables candidates to discover more relevant roles in less time and 
delivers employers the skills they need faster. This in turn improves labour market efficiency, delivering a growing and 
more productive economy. SEEK matches and places candidates across all skill levels, from CEOs to baristas, and our 
service is particularly important to small businesses that don’t have large recruitment teams.  
 
SEEK believes that thoughtful regulation is a balancing act. On the one hand, it must balance the enormous benefits that 
AI can deliver to the efficiency of the labour market through better matching jobseekers and employers with all the 
relevant jobs in less time, with less human conscious and unconscious bias. On the other hand, it must also balance the 
potential risk of AI making incorrect decisions systematically and at scale, which would negatively impact peoples’ fair 
access to employment opportunities and employers’ access to great candidates.  
 
This is true for the application of AI across society and the economy. Australia’s approach to regulating AI must enable 
innovation and unlock our competitiveness to raise productivity and our standard of living, while managing the potential 
risks. SEEK’s view is that internationally, Singapore is an example of a country that is getting this balance right in the 
most appropriate way. We believe that the approaches in the European Union and Canada, that have heavily influenced 
the Australian Government’s approach in the Proposals Paper, focus too much on the elimination of all risks at the 
expense of innovation and competitiveness.2 
 
SEEK’s substantive feedback on the issues raised in the Proposals Paper relates to the definition of ‘high risk’ in AI 
regulation. There are some high-risk cases in recruitment and other domains that should be subject to the Mandatory 
Guardrails. However, if the current definition is applied as it is, it will capture low- and medium-risk use cases. This would 
adversely impact jobseekers and employers and reduce the efficiency of Australia’s labour market.  
 
SEEK believes that the current definition of high risk can be improved by having: 
 The assessment of risk be applied at the use case level, not the model or system level; and 
 A two step-step assessment of risk: 
1. First, assess if the use case is captured by the current principles.  
2. If it is captured, then a second assessment is undertaken to determine the degree to which AI is the 
principal factor in the decision making. Both must be satisfied. 
 
In our submission we cover: 
 
 how AI is used at SEEK and how AI can reduce bias and discrimination in recruitment 
 the benefits efficient, technology-enabled matching bring to the labour market 
 our recommendations on how to improve the current definition of high-risk AI 
 further issues for consideration, including the need to ensure the competitiveness of Australian-based 
companies who develop and deploy AI given the lack of clarity regarding the extraterritoriality of the Mandatory 
Guardrails.  
 
 
1 34.3% - Source: independent research conducted on behalf of SEEK. Placement survey is based on respondents who changed / started jobs in the last 12 
months.  
2 https://www.europarl.europa.eu/news/en/press-room/20240913IPR23903/draghi-to-meps-europe-faces-a-choice-between-exit-paralysis-or-
integration#:~:text=Mr%20Draghi%2C%20author%20of%20a,decarbonisation%20with%20increased%20competitiveness%3B%20and     
SEEK | AI Regulation  2 
 
  
 
 
 
Introduction 
AI is core to how SEEK facilitates job placements in Australia. Using AI to match people with jobs reduces 
friction by enabling candidates and employers to discover more relevant roles and skills faster. This 
improves labour market efficiency, delivering a growing and more productive economy. SEEK believes 
regulation can support AI to unlock recruitment for people searching for work and for employers looking for 
talent.  
 
This submission outlines SEEK’s position on issues of most relevance to our organisation raised in the 
Proposals Paper, including our view that only use cases in recruitment that are truly high risk should be 
subject to Mandatory Guidelines.  
 
On other matters, particularly regarding preferences for implementation options and issues with the 10 
Mandatory Guardrails, SEEK has worked closely with our industry peak bodies, the Tech Council of Australia 
and the Business Council of Australia, and endorses their positions.  
AI at SEEK  
How AI is used at SEEK 
Ever since SEEK was founded and headquartered in Melbourne, Australia in 1997, it has been leveraging 
technology to deliver on its purpose to help people live more fulfilling and productive working lives and help 
organisations succeed. SEEK’s investment in the use of safe and responsible data and AI helps to make 
employment markets more accessible, transparent and efficient for jobseekers and hirers. Our technologies, 
products and tools are designed to complement, not replace, the human element of recruitment. 
 
SEEK’s AI department has over 200 team members and manages over 60 AI services in production. These 
AI services are leveraged in a wide range of use cases, including advanced AI search and recommendation 
models for people looking for jobs and employers looking for workers. SEEK’s AI services leverage billions of 
data points from numerous parts of our platform. This includes SEEK profiles (which contain people’s 
career histories, skills and credentials), search histories, as well as the journeys of people with similar skills 
and experiences. This is all matched with the specific requirements of advertised jobs.  
 
SEEK’s AI models learn from all searches, applications, career movements and skill matches to recommend 
roles to jobseekers and candidates to hirers that might otherwise be overlooked. This enables both 
jobseekers and hirers to discover relevant opportunities more efficiently. SEEK’s AI services support hirers in 
defining the necessary skills and capabilities for their roles, and surface candidates or opportunities that 
might not have been directly searched for — based on skills and experience rather than biased attributes like 
gender, age or ethnicity. This ensures better, more informed decision-making for jobseekers and hirers alike. 
 
SEEK’s AI services have significantly improved outcomes for candidates and hirers in Australia, New 
Zealand and Southeast Asia. For example: 
 Our AI-powered search and recommendations systems have delivered a ~30% increase in 
applications by enabling candidates to discover more relevant jobs in less time 
 Our ‘Top Applicant Badge’ signals to candidates when they are likely to be a good fit for a role, 
which increases the likelihood of candidates to apply to a role they may not otherwise have done 
 Our ‘Candidate Highlights’ product matches a candidate’s capabilities and skills on their resume 
with specific requirements of the job and highlights why a candidate may be a good fit for a role. 
This encourages hirers to consider candidates based on their skills and capabilities, rather than 
other signals that may be prone to bias. 
 
   
 
 
SEEK | AI Regulation  3 
 
 
How AI can reduce bias in recruitment – the use of responsible AI at SEEK 
At SEEK we believe good technology should improve overall outcomes for society and reduce overall harm 
from bias and discrimination. For example, using fit-for-purpose data and AI to augment the human process 
of recruitment provides opportunities to reduce bias and create fairer outcomes by: 
 
 removing potential signals of human bias in selection decisions (e.g. name, gender and ethnicity)    
 ensuring more quality matches of jobseekers and job opportunities are seen and engaged with, and  
 improving hiring behaviour by providing transparency over the diversity and fairness of a hirer’s 
recruitment process. 
AI can also support hirers to make fairer and more equitable recruitment decisions. For example, SEEK 
knows that technology – including AI – has made it easier for candidates to apply for many more jobs than 
they could previously. This means reviewing the volume of applications a hirer receives is very time-
consuming. Employers often receive hundreds of applications and many of them, particularly small 
businesses, are reviewing these on top of their other work. Without the help of AI to organise the huge 
volume of applications, hirers can sometimes take short cuts that mean they are not giving all applications 
due consideration. 
 
However, we also recognise that technology and AI raises new risks, and if not used responsibly AI could 
increase the potential for harm by introducing discrimination and privacy issues at scale. To ensure we use 
AI responsibly, SEEK has been investing in a dedicated responsible AI practice for the past six years.  
 
Our approach to Responsible AI (RAI) includes: 
 A clear set of responsible AI Principles that guide how we develop, apply and monitor AI services at 
SEEK. These include that AI services:  
­ should deliver significant net value to both hirers and candidates  
­ not add or entrench unfair bias and minimise the use of potential bias signals  
­ provide users with reasonable awareness of the overall purpose and scope of SEEK’s AI 
services, and  
­ comply with all regulation and security guidelines.3 
 
 Leveraging comprehensive and localised data, provided to the SEEK platform for the sole purposes 
of recruitment, that spans a diverse range of jobseekers, job types, and industries across Australia 
and the Asia Pacific region: 
­ We prioritise the quality, privacy, and trustworthiness of our data, ensuring it is 
representative and inclusive. This commitment helps us train our models on controlled data 
that genuinely reflects varied employment scenarios and local market dynamics, 
significantly reducing the risk of bias often found in less tailored, international or overly 
generic models, or models that draw data solely from open data sources.   
 
 AI services that are designed to support a human-centric recruitment process through insights and 
recommendations to inform human decision making:  
­ Our services support human decision making but do not make any final decisions to 
shortlist, interview or hire a candidate 
­ Specifically, we ensure that all jobseekers are always able to see and apply to all jobs, and 
that all job applications are accessible to hirers. 
 
  
 
3 Further detail on SEEK’s responsible use of AI and a full list of SEEK’s responsible AI principles can be found here: 
https://www.seek.com.au/about/sustainability/data-and-cyber   
 
 
SEEK | AI Regulation  4 
 
 
 A three-tiered responsible AI Governance Framework incorporating: 
­ An operational layer that defines how we include responsible AI thinking and design in the 
AI development lifecycle, from ideation to deployment and monitoring 
­ A governance and oversight layer that reviews all AI services in production against our 
responsible AI principles, including a range of quantitative and qualitative measures and 
peer review and sign off 
­ An assurance layer, that includes third-party reviews of our responsible AI practices and 
processes 
Our most recent external AI review by the Gradient Institute found that: “In our opinion SEEK RAI principles 
are broadly consistent with Australian and International equivalents… SEEK’s RAI processes are well 
designed to identify and control major ethical risks… SEEK’s RAI assessment is more complete and more 
sophisticated than Australian industry norms.” 
Benefits to the labour market of efficient, technology-enabled 
matching 
There is a body of evidence that demonstrates how technology, including AI-enabled search and match, has 
improved labour market efficiency and therefore supports a growing and more productive economy.  
 
Research has found the expansion of the internet and its use by jobseekers and employers has increased 
the availability of information about jobs and workers at a relatively low cost.4 Jobseekers are able to 
discover a larger quantity of jobs with more information faster than they were able to in the past, while hirers 
can reach more jobseekers quickly. 
 
AI-enabled online marketplaces, such as SEEK, play a key role in online job search by reducing search 
frictions.5 The use of the internet – and by extension AI products in recruitment – has been found to 
increase job-finding rates, reduce vacancy durations, reduce unsuccessful hiring attempts and increase 
starting wages among the unemployed.6 Online job search is also associated with better matches between 
jobseekers and employers. Jobseekers who find work online are more likely to report that their job makes 
better use of their skills, they are more content with their work, and they enjoy better job security than those 
who find a job through alternate channels.7 Better-matched candidates tend to stay longer with the hirer, 
reducing future recruitment costs and job separations which increases the efficiency of the labour market. 
 
An efficient labour market supports economic growth, productivity and reduces inequality. Imperfections in 
the labour market, such as search frictions, have been shown to increase wage inequality.8 Online job 
search also supports the job finding of those who typically have a harder time finding work, such as those in 
rural areas and mothers with children. Higher-quality matches between jobseekers and firms increase 
labour productivity, which benefits everyone and helps the Australian economy grow.9 
 
On this evidence, caution should be taken on applying an unnecessary regulatory burden on the 
development and deployment of AI in recruitment. Only those use cases that are truly high risk in this 
domain should be subject to the proposed Mandatory Guardrails, otherwise it risks reducing the efficiency 
of Australia’s labour market, bringing with it adverse impacts to individual jobseekers, employers and to the 
economy at large. 
 
