SEEK | AI Regulation  5 
 
 
Better defining high-risk AI: SEEK’s recommendations 
The current principles-based definition of high-risk AI is extremely broad. Relying on this definition as the 
only test of whether an AI use case should be deemed high risk would result in low- and medium-risk use 
cases being captured.  
 
For the purposes of this submission, SEEK defines a use case as the application of an AI model via a 
product or service to solve a specific problem or task. AI models can be applied to different use cases, with 
different levels of risk. For example, a model that determines a candidate’s likelihood to be shortlisted for a 
particular job could be used in a use case to signal to a candidate why they are a good fit for a job, which 
gives them confidence to apply and helps them to stand out in the process (low risk). This same model 
could be used to automate the screening process, and potentially systematically block candidates from 
applying to roles or their applications being visible to hirers (high risk).  
 
Therefore, to ensure the benefits of AI are realised, only use cases that are truly high risk should be 
categorised as such. For AI to be deemed high risk SEEK recommends: 
 The assessment of the risk must be applied at the use case level, not the model or system level. 
 The definition must not just rely on the current principles. There must be a two-step assessment of 
risk.  
1. First, assess if the use case is captured by the principles.  
2. If it is captured, then a second assessment is undertaken to determine the degree to 
which AI is the principal factor in the decision making. Both must be satisfied. 
 
Level of application of the definition of high risk  
SEEK believes that the first issue with the current definition of high-risk AI is that it currently applies the 
definition at the level of AI models and systems. Applying risk classifications at the level of a model or 
system will not accurately reflect its actual risk. There is potential for this to result in some higher-risk use 
cases of seemingly benign models avoiding necessary oversight and governance, while at the same time 
placing undue burden on low- and medium-risk use cases of AI models.  
 
For example, in the Proposals Paper, where an example is given, it states that ‘an automated CV scanning 
service that makes a determination of an individual’s suitability for a job would be considered a high-risk 
system.’10 In reality, the risk is actually at the use case level – in this example, the decision around 
determining the individuals’ suitability for the job – not at the model level (see Diagram 1 ). 
 
To further illustrate this point, SEEK currently develops and deploys AI models that predict a candidate’s fit 
for a role, but applies these models in different use cases and in ways that is not determining any outcomes 
for that individual. If these models were used to make a decision in the hiring process, then we would agree 
that this use case should be classified as high risk. However, on our platform, AI is not the principal basis on 
which a decision is being made. The AI is performing preparatory tasks prior to a decision being made by a 
human – in our case, hirers using SEEK.  
 
Diagram 1 below demonstrates how different applications of an AI model at a use case level can have vastly 
different impacts and risk profiles. 
 
  
 
10 Page 27, ‘ Safe and Responsible AI in Australia: Proposals paper for introducing mandatory guardrails for AI in high-risk settings ’, September 
2024 – https://consult.industry.gov.au/ai-mandatory-guardrails   
 
 
SEEK | AI Regulation  6 
 
 
Diagram 1: Exemplar model vs. use case risk profile: How AI helps people get jobs on SEEK 
 
 
Two-step assessment of risk required 
SEEK’s preference is for a principles-based approach. However, the proposed principles11 are so broad that 
relying on them as the sole mechanism for designating high-risk AI would unintentionally capture low- and 
medium-risk use cases of AI.  
SEEK therefore recommends that if the use case is found to be captured by the principles, a second test be 
applied. This second test would consider the degree to which AI is the principal basis on which a decision is 
being made and the consequence of that decision. It is important to take the additional step, beyond the 
broad principles, to distinguish between fully automated tools, and those tools with a human involved in 
making the ultimate decision.  
An AI tool that replaces human decisions poses a higher risk than an AI tool that enriches them. When an 
informed human is involved, they can leverage an AI tool’s insights and remain in control of and accountable 
for the final decision. We therefore propose that any AI that is performing a narrow procedural task, or is an 
input (a preparatory task) to a human decision, not be classified as high risk. 
Diagram 2 below illustrates that SEEK’s use cases for AI are performing a preparatory task prior to an 
assessment being made by the hirer, and therefore should not be classified as high risk. 
  
 
