Further issues for consideration  
Ensuring competitiveness of Australian-based businesses 
In the current Proposals Paper, there is a potential risk to the competitiveness of Australian-based 
companies due to the lack of clarity around the territorial nexus of the Mandatory Guardrails; that is, to 
whom and where the guardrails apply. Practically, even if the Mandatory Guardrails do have extraterritorial 
effect, enforcing the Mandatory Guardrails against overseas developers or deployers could prove to be 
difficult. It would also be difficult for Australian deployers to enforce them against overseas developers 
where there is likely to be an imbalance in bargaining power.12  
 
This would leave Australian organisations at a distinct competitive disadvantage, particularly for businesses 
such as SEEK whose major competitors are global multinationals, for example: LinkedIn, which is owned by 
Microsoft, and Indeed, which is an American-headquartered multinational owned by Recruit Holdings, a 
Japanese business. Further consideration must be given to address this potential inequity in the application 
of the Mandatory Guardrails and any associated compliance and enforcement measures. 
 
Timeframes for implementation 
The Proposal Paper does not discuss potential timeframes for the implementation of the Mandatory 
Guardrails. Given the scale and nature of these regulatory reforms, SEEK recommends that there be at least 
a two-year implementation timeframe from the legislation coming into force, consistent with the EU AI Act.  
 
Penalties for non-compliance 
The Proposal Paper does not discuss potential penalties for non-compliance with the Mandatory Guardrails. 
Consideration must be given to how these would be applied in the context of each of the three 
implementation options, and how extraterritoriality and associated issues of competitiveness of Australian-
based companies would be managed. SEEK recommendations a separate and detailed consultation 
regarding potential penalties.  
  
 
