https://www.theguardian.com/australia -news/2023/sep/28/australian -books -training -ai-books3 -stolen -pirated  3 has included in its list of high -risk settings for the deployment of AI Systems:  ‘Employment, 
workers management and access to self -employment ’2 
As was noted by the OECD in its 2023 Employment Outlook, ‘ Collective bargaining and social 
dialogue also have an important role to play in supporting workers and businesses in the AI 
transition. ’3 Leading research conducted at MIT’s Sloan School of Management in the United 
States has noted that the active involvement of worker voice through representative bodies, such 
as unions, is key to the effective implementation of AI systems into workplaces.4 The United 
Nations has recently explained the significant in successful AI implementation of ‘ Leveraging 
social dialogue and public -private partnerships for managing AI integration in the workplace, 
ensuring worker participation in the process and protecting labour rights .’5 In October 2023, US 
President Biden explained in a significant presidential Executive Order: ‘ The responsible 
development and use of AI require a commitment to supporting American workers.   As AI creates 
new jobs and industries, all workers need a seat at the table .’6 
As this shows, i nternational best practice embeds workers’ rights and the collective 
representative voice of workers at the heart of the AI transition. Australia has made positive 
recent steps in this regard, but we risk falling behind global standards. The guardrail proposals, if 
properly managed, can ensure that Australia is a world leader in both AI adoption and the 
protection and enhancement of workers’ rights in the construction of the economy of the future.  
The ACTU believes that, if introduced correctly, AI systems that augment and compliment the 
skilled work of working people can unlock substantial economic benefits in productivity 
improvements that can then be shared with working Australians through wage i ncreases and 
betterment of working conditions. Unions have already demonstrated our strong willingness to 
work with employers who intend to introduce AI systems for these purposes in a spirit of 
 
 
 
 
