Dear Minister  
SUBMISSIONS IN RESPONSE TO  SAFE AND RESPONSIBLE AI IN AUSTRALIA - PROPOSALS PAPER 
FOR INTRODUCING MANDATORY GUARDRAILS FOR AI IN HIGH -RISK SETTINGS  
As Australia’s largest music industry body representing the rights of over 119,000 songwriters, 
composers and music publishers across Australasia, APRA AMCOS welcomes the opportunity to make 
a submission to the consultation by the Department of Industry, S cience and Resources  (Department ) 
on the  Safe and responsible AI in Australia - Proposals paper for introducing mandatory guardrails for AI 
in high -risk setting s released September 2024  (Proposals Paper ). 
In summary, APRA AMCOS strongly supports the introduction of mandatory AI guardrails to protect the 
rights of creators, ensure fair competition, and uphold ethical standards in the development and 
deployment of AI technologies in the music industry .  
About APRA AMCOS  
1. APRA AMCOS is the Australasian Performing Right Association and the Australasian Mechanical 
Copyright Owners Society. The membership is diverse, ranging from unpublished writers to major 
music publishers. Licensee stakeholders include all major broadcaster s and video on demand and 
music streaming services, as well as businesses across Australia and New Zealand .  
2. APRA AMCOS has been at the forefront of examining the impact of AI on the music and broader creative 
industries. We are one of the l eaders  of a broad coalition of creative industry organisations that 
includes representative bodies from music, literature, screen, media  and design, as well as First 
Nations cultural organisations.   
 
Page 2 of 9 
 
3477 -8287 -4672, v. 2  3. APRA AMCOS regularly works in partnership with governments at all levels to support development and 
career paths in music through the cornerstones of live music, digital platforms, screen music, 
interactive games, local venues, education and global exports.  
4. APRA AMCOS has participated fully and openly in many government consultations on legislation that  
impacts our members in Australia. We have expended considerable resources in gathering evidence, 
considering issues, preparing submissions, and appearing before the reviewing bodies when required.  
5. APRA AMCOS also has an extensive and highly active collaborative network worldwide. We are in close 
dialogue with creator groups around the world including the International Confederation of Societies of 
Authors and Composers (CISAC), International Council of Music Creato rs (CIAM), International Music 
Publishers International Forum (IMPF), Groupement Européen des Sociétés d’Auteurs et Compositeurs 
(GESAC), International Federation of Reproduction Rights Organisations (IFRRO) and the International 
Confederat ion of Music Publishers (ICMP).  
AI and the music industry  
6. The rapid development of Artificial Intelligence ( AI) has created both opportunities and challenges for 
industries across the globe, and the music industry is no exception. AI’s role in music creation, 
distribution, and consumption is growing exponentially, and while this offers exciting potential, it also 
poses serious risks to creators' rights, economic stability, and cultural integrity.  
7. The Department’s consultation on the introduction of mandatory guardrails for AI in high -risk settings is 
a crucial step toward ensuring that these technologies are developed and deployed responsibly. Our 
response emphasises the importance of applying guardrails that prote ct the rights of creators, ensure 
transparency and accountability, and address the global nature of the AI industry.  
8. In our submissions to the Select Committee on Adopting Artificial Intelligence dated 22 May 20241 (May 
Submissions ), APRA AMCOS detailed its concerns regarding  generative AI in particular. These 
concerns  arise principally from the lack of transparency that most generative AI has so far 
demonstrated in terms of acknowledging the content which has been scraped, mined, listened to, and 
trained on in order to create their outputs.  
9. As stated in the May Submissions , to the extent that this conduct involves the reproduction of 
copyright -protected material without permission, it is potentially unlawful and certainly unethical , and 
the potential impact of this type of unauthorised use of the work of Australian creators, including 
Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander creators, is deeply problematic.  
 
1 Select Committee on Adopting Artificial Intelligence (AI) Submission 169 - Australasian Performing Right Association & Australasian Mechanical 
Copyright Owners Society, available at: 
https://www.aph.gov.au/Parliamentary_Business/Committees/Senate/Adopting_Artificial_Intelligence_AI/AdoptingAI/Submissions   
 
Page 3 of 9 
 
3477 -8287 -4672, v. 2  10. On this point, APRA AMCOS submit ted that the Australian Government should seek to develop a 
definition of “cultural risk” that reflects the concerns of the local creative sectors, including ATSI 
creators , due to the fact that t hese sectors are uniquely vulnerable to AI risk and will be heavily 
impacted by the unchecked development and use of generative AI models.  
11. To assist the Government in this process, APRA AMCOS recently published an extensive study of the 
current and potential impact of AI on the music industry, including a wide -ranging survey of our 
members about  the risks posed by AI.  
AI and Music  – an APRA AMCOS report  
12. On 19 August 2024, APRA AMCOS released AI And Music - market development of AI in the music 
sector and impact on music creators in Australia And New Zealand2 (AI Report ), a landmark report that 
explored the potential economic and creative impacts of AI on the music industry  in Australia and New 
Zealand .  
13. The AI Report was commission ed to analyse  and assess AI’s influence on the creative process, and to 
report on the economic implications  of AI and relevant legal and political developments  that  might 
determine how AI is regulated.  
14. At the core of the study was an online survey of APRA AMCOS members conducted between 31 May 
and 20 June 2024. A total of 4,274 songwriters, composers, and publishers participated in the survey. 
Two additional surveys were conducted to explore the impact o f AI on Aboriginal and Torres Strait 
Islander musicians (38 participants) as well as Māori musicians (53 participants). In addition, the study 
included eight expert interviews with artists, academics, AI software providers, and other stakeholders 
in the mu sic sector.  
15. The AI R eport has initiated widespread discussion among artists, industry stakeholders, and 
policymakers, highlighting the need for regulatory intervention to address the risks AI poses to the 
creative ecosystem.  
16. The AI Report  shows that AI will transform the music industry in unprecedented ways, from AI -
generated compositions to automated mastering and distribution services. These innovations offer 
exciting possibilities for artists, producers, and consumers, providing new too ls for creativity. However, 
these same technologies pose significant risks, particularly regarding copyright infringement, 
economic harm to human creators, and ethical concerns about the future of artistic integrity and the 
impact on indigenou s creators and communities . 
 
2 Available at: https://www.apraamcos.com.au/about/supporting -the-industry/research -papers/aiandmusic   
 
Page 4 of 9 
 
3477 -8287 -4672, v. 2  17. The AI Report  presents a sobering view of the future if AI continues to evolve without proper regulation 
or oversight. One of the core concerns raised in the report is the potential loss of revenue and income 
for songwriters, composers, and music publishers if AI conti nues to evolve without regulations or 
licensing schemes in place.  
AI Repor t: Key Findings  
18. The Key Findings of the AI Report are as follows:  
a) Revenue risk: By 2028, 23% of music creators’ revenues will be at risk due to generative AI, an 
estimated cumulative total damage of AU$519 million ; 
b) AI adoption:  Over half (54%) of those surveyed agree that AI technology can assist the human 
creative process, with AU and NZ songwriters and composers being early adopters ; 
c) Income impact: 82% of music creators are concerned that the use of AI in music could lead to 
them no longer being able to make a living from their work ; 
d) Cultural concerns:  89% of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Island and Māori music creators believe 
that AI will lead to an increase in cultural appropriation ; and  
e) Policy demands: 97% demand that policymakers should pay more attention to the challenges 
related to AI and copyright . 
AI Report: Key Market Analysis  
19. The AI Report  highlights the rapid expansion of the AI -driven music market, currently valued at 
approximately AU$430 million globally in 2023. This market includes AI -generated music for platforms 
like streaming services, gaming, advertising, and background scores for media.  
20. The AI Report projects that by 2028, the AI music market could grow to over $4.5 billion, driven by 
advancements in AI technology, increased demand for low -cost music production, and its integration 
into various industries.  
AI Report : Member Survey  
21. The responses of the 4,274 APRA AMCOS members who participated in the AI Report survey reveal 
widespread concern s about the impact of AI on creativity and revenue.  
22. The survey highlights that a substantial majority  – 82%  – believe that unregulated AI poses a major 
threat to their livelihoods. Respondents expressed concerns that AI -generated music could diminish 
the value of human -created works, with many worried about the lack of adequate regulation for 
creators.   
 
Page 5 of 9 
 
3477 -8287 -4672, v. 2  23. The survey also reflected optimism about AI’s potential, with : 
a) 54% of respondents agreeing that AI technology can support the human creative process ; and  
b) 38% of respondents agreeing that AI can and will open up new forms of creativity.  
24. Nearly all – 97% – of respondents demand that policymakers should pay more attention to the 
challenges related to AI and copyright. This sentiment was also strong among Indigenous creators, who 
emphasised the risk of cultural appropriation by AI systems.  
AI and Indigenous Cultural and Intellectual Property  
25. The rise of AI technology poses significant threats to the cultural and economic wellbeing of all 
Indigenous communities. Due to the unique nature and cultural significance of Indigenous Cultural 
Intellectual Property ( ICIP ), AI’s potential effects on this vulnerable sector are profound.  
26. To date the Australian Government ‘s focus has been on  the unauthorised use of ICIP in arts and crafts, 
particularly in mass produced items, but it is crucial that this focus urgently expands to encompass all 
fields of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Island cultural creation.  
27. The AI Report included specific insights from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander and Māori music 
creators, highlighting unique concerns about AI’s impact on cultural heritage.  
28. According to the survey, over 80% of these respondents expressed significant anxiety about AI’s 
potential to appropriate traditional knowledge, music, and cultural expressions without consent.  
29. Many Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander and Māori music creators are concerned that AI -generated 
music could replicate and commercialise Indigenous music styles, undermining the cultural 
significance and intellectual property of these communities.  
30. Additionally, a majority of respondents emphasised the importance of protecting their music and 
cultural expressions through stronger legal frameworks. There is a strong call for AI regulations that 
safeguard the integrity of Indigenous music and ensure it  is not exploited or misrepresented by AI 
systems. Creators also see AI as a potential tool for cultural preservation, provided there are strict 
protections for traditional music and knowledge, ensuring that cultural ownership remains with the 
communities themselves.  
High -Risk Definitions and Mandatory Guardrails  
31. APRA AMCOS welcomes the six proposed principles for designating an AI system as high -risk, as set 
out in the Proposals Paper. In particular , we welcome the inclusion of:  
1. the risk of adverse legal effects, defamation or similarly significant effects on an individual ;  
2. the risk of adverse impacts to groups of individuals or collective rights of cultural groups ; and   
 
Page 6 of 9 
 
3477 -8287 -4672, v. 2  3. the risk of adverse impacts to the broader Australian economy, society, environment and rule 
of law . 
32. APRA AMCOS strongly supports the introduction of mandatory AI guardrails to protect the rights of 
creators, ensure fair competition, and uphold ethical standards in the development and deployment of 
AI technologies in the music industry.  
33. APRA AMCOS proposes four key principles must be applied to AI Guardrails to ensure their efficacy:  
1. Transparency:  There must be transparency around the inputs such as music and other 
creative content that is being used to train AI systems, and AI-generated music must be 
clearly labelled , allowing consumers and industry professionals to easily identify when they 
are engaging with content created by AI rather than humans.  
2. Accountability:  Developers and operators of AI systems must be held accountable for the 
outputs generated by their systems, particularly in cases where those outputs infringe on 
intellectual property or perpetuate cultural biases.  
3. Consent and  Compensation:  AI systems that use copyrighted works in their training 
datasets must be subject to licensing arrangements for fair and reasonable  consideration , 
ensuring that songwriters, composers, and other creators are fairly compensated for the use 
of their work.  
4. Global Standards: Given the international nature of the AI industry, any regulatory framework 
for AI in Australia must align with global standards to ensure that overseas platforms do not 
gain an unfair competitive advantage by operating under more lenient regulations.  
34. With this level of disclosure, artists, creators and rightsholders can level the playing field and negotiate 
appropriate agreements and ensure that their intellectual property is only used with the appropriate 
levels of consent, credit and remuneration.  
Guardrails must apply to al l AI Services  regardless of origin  
35. To be effective, AI guardrails and transparency obligations must apply universally to all AI services 
available in Australia, regardless of where the AI model was trained or developed. The music industry 
operates in a global environment, and AI systems frequently interact wit h datasets and platforms from 
around the world. This makes it essential for Australia’s regulatory framework to cover all AI services, 
not just those developed domestically.  
36. Sanctions for non -compliance must also be imposed on platforms based overseas but operating in 
Australia. Without such measures, international platforms could undercut local services by 
circumventing Australian regulations, leading to unfair competition. A ustralian companies that adhere  
 
Page 7 of 9 
 
3477 -8287 -4672, v. 2  to the rules should not be placed at a disadvantage by foreign entities exploiting regulatory gaps. By 
applying guardrails to all AI services, regardless of origin, Australia can ensure a fair, competitive 
environment where all players are held to the same  standards.  
Recommendations for AI Guardrails in the music industry  
37. One of the most pressing concerns surrounding AI in the creative industries is the industrial -scale 
copying of content during the input stage of AI training. AI platforms are frequently trained on massive 
datasets, often scraped from the internet, without the consent of the original creators. These datasets 
contain copyrighted works that are essential to the creative process. While these works serve as the 
foundational input for AI systems, the outputs generated by these platforms are, in many cases, mere 
derivatives or adaptations of the original content.  
38. The outputs derived from these AI platforms are not entirely new creations. Instead, they are 
compositions that blend and remix elements from the original works used in training, often closely 
mirroring the style, structure, or essence of those works. Desp ite the derivative nature of these outputs, 
they are released into the same markets, competing directly with the original creators whose content 
was copied in the first place.  
39. It is essential to implement a compensation  regime not only at the input stage  – when AI models are 
trained on copyrighted works  – but also at the output stage. There must be a mechanism whereby AI 
platforms are required to compensate the owners of  the creative content which has been  both  a) used 
to train these  systems ; and b) forms part of the output of these  systems. This approach would ensure 
that the creators of the original works maintain a stake in the value chain of derivative creations.  
40. APRA AMCOS makes the following recommendations to support the implementation of the guardrails 
and ensure that AI is used responsibly in the music industry:  
1. Licensing for AI Systems:  AI systems that use copyright works in their training datasets 
should be required to obtain consent from and pay reasonable compensation to rights 
holders  for the use of their works . This will ensure that creators are compensated for the use 
of their work and that their intellectual property rights are respected.  
2. Auditable AI Models:  AI systems should be subject to regular audits to ensure compliance 
with licensing agreements and guard against the unauthorised use of copyrighted material. 
These audits should be conducted by independent third parties to maintain transparency and 
accoun tability.  
3. Clear labelling of AI -generated content:  All AI -generated music should be clearly labelled to 
distinguish it from human -created works. This will ensure that consumers are fully informed  
 
Page 8 of 9 
 
3477 -8287 -4672, v. 2  about the origins of the music they are listening to and allow artists to differentiate their own 
creative work from machine -generated content.  
4. Universal application of Guardrails:  As emphasised earlier, AI guardrails must apply to all AI 
services available in Australia, regardless of where the AI model was developed or trained. 
This includes imposing sanctions on overseas platforms that fail to comply with Australian 
regulations.  
5. Protection against cultural misappropriation:  AI systems should be trained on datasets 
that respect cultural integrity, particularly when it comes to Indigenous creators and 
communities. Guardrails must be in place to prevent AI from perpetuating cultural biases or 
misappropriating cultural works without proper attribution and consent.  
6. Global Collaboration for AI Regulation:  Australia should work with international bodies such 
as the World Intellectual Property Organisation (WIPO) to develop global standards for AI 
regulation in the creative industries. This will ensure that Australian creators are protected 
globally and that  international platforms operating in Australia are held to the same standards 
as domestic platforms.  
Addressing AI assumptions and overreach  
41. APRA AMCOS submits that whilst  the Proposals Paper offers valuable insights into the potential 
benefits and risks AI in high -risk settings, it also contains several assumptions about AI capabilities that 
warrant further scrutiny. Some of these assumptions overstate the current abilities of AI or overlook the 
limitations and nuances of AI technologie s. 
1. AI’s Creative Capabilities:  The Proposals Paper suggests that AI has the potential to replace 
human creativity in industries like music, film, and the arts. However, this assumption 
overlooks the fact that AI systems, while adept at pattern recognition and data analysis, lack 
the emotional depth, cultural understanding, and contextual awareness that human creators 
bring to their work. AI -generated music, for instance, can mimic existing styles, but it cannot  
replicate the human experiences and emotions that drive artistic innovation.  
2. Bias and Cultural Representation:  The Proposals Paper downplays the risks associated with 
AI perpetuating bias, particularly in culturally sensitive areas like music creation. AI models 
trained on datasets lacking diversity are likely to amplify existing cultural biases, leading to 
misrepresentation or margin alisation of minority groups. It is misleading to assume that AI 
systems can produce unbiased outcomes without addressing the biases inherent in the data 
used to train them.   
 
Page 9 of 9 
 
3477 -8287 -4672, v. 2  3. Autonomy and Control:  The Proposals Paper makes bold claims about the autonomy of AI 
systems, suggesting that they can operate independently with minimal human oversight. In 
reality, AI systems require ongoing human intervention for optimisation, error correction, and 
alignment with ethical guidel ines. The notion that AI can seamlessly and autonomously 
manage complex tasks without human input is an overreach and fails to acknowledge the 
significant limitations of current AI technologies.  
Conclusion  
42. The rise of AI presents both opportunities and challenges for the Australian music industry. Whil st AI 
technologies have the potential to enhance creativity and streamline production processes, they also 
pose significant risks to creators ’ rights, cultural representation, and economic stability. To mitigate 
these risks, it is essential that the Australian Government implements strong, enforceable guardrails 
for AI, particularly in high -risk settings such as music creation.  
43. AI guardrails and transparency obligations must apply universally to all AI services available in 
Australia, regardless of their origin. This is a global industry, and regulatory frameworks must reflect the 
international nature of AI technologies to ensure  fair competition and protect the rights of Australian 
creators.  
44. By imposing sanctions on platforms that fail to comply, including those based overseas, the 
government can safeguard the future of Australia's music industry in the face of AI disruption.  
45. We look forward to contributing to the development of policies that protect the rights and livelihoods of 
Australian songwriters, composers, and music publishers to ensure that they share in the success of AI 
development.  
If we can provide further information, or be of assistance in any other way, please do not hesitate to contact  
