https://www.oaic.gov.au/privacy/your -privacy-rights/your -personal -information/consent -to-the-handling-
of-personal -information   
6 Australian Medical Association. Position Statement – Artificial Intelligence in Healthcare (2023). 
https://www.ama.com.au/sites/default/files/2023 -
08/Artificial%20Intelligence%20in%20Healthcare%20 -%20AMA.pdf   13 
 this regulatory context. Guardrail 8 may also be combined with  Guardrail s 1 and 
2, which already require AI developers and deployers  to create and publish an 
accountability process to support transparency  and risk management .  
Similar to Guardrails 1 , 2 and 8, Guardrails 9 and 10  warrant further 
consideration and guidance for their practicality , applicability  and administrative  
implications, as they could significantly expand current record -keeping and 
auditing requirements  that may hinder progress for small to medium enterprises 
(SMEs) due to costs  and complexity associated with compliance  (further 
discussion of this point under question 12 ). Certification of compliance where an 
organisation can communicate to the public that they are adhering to the 
mandatory guardrails should either be opt-in only or mandated for specific AI 
use cases  in high-risk settings .  
9. How can the guardrails incorporate First Nations knowledge and cultural 
protocols to ensure AI systems are culturally appropriate and preserve ICIP?  
There is a paucity of legislation in Australia that protects Indigenous peoples ’ 
cultural knowledge and protocols , and their personal  and health data that are 
derived from multiple sources and biological samples , including genetic and 
genomic data . Without appropriate Indigenous -led and developed data 
sovereignty protection mechanisms and models, mandatory guardrails will not 
be adequate in being able to protect or provide the appropriate governance  and 
stewardship required.  
In addition to our recommendation under question 2, t he UNDRIP has not been 
embedded within Australian sovereign law despite numerous calls for specific 
legislation to be drafted and implemented (including a Joint Senate Committee 
into the application of UNDRIP in Australia). This legislative vacuum creates 
opportunities for exploitation by AI bad actors and developers. Mandatory 
guardrails  can only ensure AI systems are culturally appropriate and preserve 
IDSov/ICIP if specific legislation  and recognition  is drafted to enable adequate  
protection.  
10. Do the proposed mandatory guardrails distribute responsibility across the AI 
supply chain and throughout the AI lifecycle appropriately? For example, are 
the requirements assigned to developers and deployers appropriate?   
The proposed guardrails currently place greater obligations on developers, but 
there is a lack of clarity on how these requirements would be enforced for those 
currently based overseas , as well as  Australian -based SME developers and 
deployers  with limited re sources. Real regulatory effectiveness hinges on 
specific guidance  on how developers and deployers can adhere to them and 14 
 what criteria will be used to assess adherence.  We recommend further guidance 
and clarification to address  these critical gaps.  
11. Are the proposed mandatory guardrails sufficient to address the risks of 
GPAI? How could we adapt the guardrails for different GPAI models, for 
example low -risk and high -risk GPAI models?  
Not fully, for the reasons outlined in our responses above. A cornerstone of these 
guardrails should revolve around the appropriateness, fairness, and 
representation of the training data, and ensure that it is obtained and used with 
appropriate consent  from individuals  and adherence to First Nations protocols . 
Adaptation of the guardrails to different GPAI models should follow clear risk 
assessment and evaluation criteria as previously recommended.  
12. Do you have suggestions for reducing the regulatory burden on small -to-
medium sized businesses applying guardrails?  
SMEs are a key driver of Australia’s economy. They employ over two -thirds of the 
workforce, contribute more than half of the country's GDP, and represent 99.8% 
of all businesses .7 Burdensome legal and regulatory prescriptions  have been 
identified as a barrier to innovation across critical sectors, including healthcare , 
scientific and technical services.8 We support the Australian Government’s 
consideration to reduce this burden on SMEs that might lack the resources of 
larger corporations  in applying the guardrails. Our key recommendations 
include:  
• further clarity and simplification of guardrails 1, 2,  8, 9 and 10 with 
compliance requirements proportionate to  the risk level as suggested 
under question 8 , as well as provision of  templates for record keeping and 
reporting  requirements ; 
• potential exemption for SMEs based on  an annual turnover threshold, and 
non-applicability of this exemption in the event of high-risk AI used in 
certain high-risk business contexts  such as health services (similar to the 
approach taken by the Privacy Act 1988); 
 
7 M Verreynne et al.  (2021). Enablers and barriers to industry -research collaboration: A small and medium 
sized enterprise perspective . CSIRO, Australia. https://www.csiro.au/en/work -with-us/funding -
programs/sme/enablers -and-barriers  
8 Australian Bureau of Statistics (ABS) . (2022). Innovation in Australian Business [data set] . 15 
 • more explicit guidance  and tools to support  implementation of the 
guardrails , including specific example s and case studies across various 
industries  (for example, the EU AI Act Compliance Checker9); and  
• dedicated e ducation and training for developers and deployers regarding 
the assessments and documentation required  under the guardrails . 
 
9 EU Artificial Intelligence Act . EU AI Act Compliance Checker . EU AI Act Compliance Checker | EU 
Artificial Intelligence Act  16 
 Regulatory options to mandate guardrails  
 
Proposed mandatory guardrails for high-risk AI at a glance  
3 options for implementing the proposed mandatory guardrails:  
Option 1 – Domain specific approach – adapting existing regulatory frameworks to include the proposed 
mandatory guardrails  
Option 2 – Framework approach – introducing framework legislation, with associated amendments to 
existing legislation, or  
Option 3 – Whole of economy approach – introducing a new cross -economy AI Act.  
 
13. Which legislative option do you feel will best address the use of AI in high -
risk settings? What opportunities should the government take into account 
in considering each approach?  
Recommended legislative option  
We acknowledge the strengths and limitations of the proposed options and 
support the government's efforts to align with AI policy frameworks of other 
leading democracies.  The AASGARD  consortium , like many of its counterparts in 
the Australian and global research and clinical communities, operate s as a large-
scale collaborative network that facilitates the identification of key datasets of 
diagnosed patients and best practices for rigorous AI tool validation and safe 
adoption to maximise clinical impact. A recent Productivity Commission report 
shows that Australia's innovation gap with leading global firms is widening, with 
the country  lagging most OECD nations in adopting advanced technologies .10 
This calls for an urgent need to avoid a patchwork of conflicting regulations  that 
impede national and  international AI collaboration and innovation  – a crucial 
aspect not recognised  in this paper.   
While all options would unavoidably result in consequential regulatory 
coordination challenges and complexity, the whole-of-economy approach 
provided in Option 3  would deliver greater consistency in enforcement and 
appears to be the most viable and sustainable legislative option. We note that 
the risk of duplicate obligations with existing legislative frameworks has been  
acknowledged as a challenge in all three proposed options and  recommend a 
legislative design that aims to achieve strong consistency with  sector-specific 
regulatory bodies (e.g. TGA) that are tailoring regulations to their assigned  
 
10 Productivity Commission  (PC). (2023). 5 Year Productivity Inquiry: Innovation for the 98% . PC, 
Australian Government . https://www.pc.gov.au/inquiries/completed/productivity/report   17 
 domains (e.g. in the domain of software as a medical device)  as suggested under 
question 3.  
The opportunity to establish a dedicated regulatory office, similar to the 
European AI Office, should also be considered as part of a new cross -economy 
AI Act to coordinate  implementation, monitoring and compliance.  This office 
should have embedded stakeholder expertise as outlined under question 7 and 
play a critical role in advancing government understanding of AI , supporting 
sectorial agencies and promoting  interoperability.  
Opportunities for further consideration  
The future success of programs like AASGARD relies on new and validated open -
source tools to accelerate and deliver new diagnoses for Australian families and 
reach population scale. The quality and scope of AI -assisted genome analyses 
are largely determine d by high-quality data for training and testing . When 
scientific and peer -reviewed data is kept behind paywalls  and becomes 
inaccessible , this exacerbates the risk of bias and limits critical capability gaps 
that directly impact the effectiveness, reliabil ity, and fairness of AI systems . 
Limited access to research literature hampers innovation, especially for high -
tech SMEs.  
Australia is in a strong position to take the lead globally in the shift toward open 
access, as highlighted by Australia’s Chief Scientist  in her recent advice on open 
access models .11  For each regulatory and policy option, we recommend that the 
government consider opportunities to formally integrate well -established open 
access standards and best practices, such as the UNESCO Recommendation on 
Open Science , as part of this consultation. Government endorsement  of open 
access AI principles while defending  community -specific guidelines and cultural 
safety protections  like IDSov and ICIP  would further support the Australian  
medical research sector  and enable AI systems to be deployed more equitably 
and transparently.  
14. Are there any additional limitations of options outlined in this section which 
the Australian Government should consider?  
In addition to our recommendation under question 4 regarding prohibited uses of 
AI, a major gap not addressed under the proposed mandatory guardrails and 
legislative options is ways in which  bad actors could be identified and held 
accountable for non-compliance  with the rules on prohibited AI practices as 
provided in Article 99 of the EU AI Act. Sanctions and corrective actions, 
 
11 Office of the Chief Scientist. (2024). Advice on open access: Unlocking knowledge for the national 
benefit. Australian Government. https://www.chiefscientist.gov.au/sites/default/files/2024 -
08/Chief%20Scientist%27s%20advice%20on%20open%20access.pdf  18 
 including ways to address adverse impacts to individuals and groups should be 
specified to achieve legislative impact  and appropriate accountability . 
15. Which regulatory option/s will best ensure that guardrails for high -risk AI can 
adapt and respond to step -changes in technology?  
Option 3 will best enable uniform oversight and nimble adaptation to respond to 
step-changes in technology. This will be particularly important  for regulation to 
keep pace with rapid innovation and discovery in genomic medicine and health 
research.  
16. Where do you see the greatest risks of gaps or inconsistencies with 
Australia’s existing laws for the development and deployment of AI? Which 
regulatory option best addresses this, and why?  
As previously flagged under questions 1, 2, 8 and 9, the lack of informed consent 
requirement s in this Proposal s Paper poses a significant gap that is inconsistent 
with obligations under  the Privacy Act 198 8, as well as formal adoption of 
UNDRIP to Australian sovereign law  and other Indigenous protocols to ensure AI 
systems are culturally safe. The Australian Government has an opportunity to 
strengthen these areas under Option 3 by providing greater consistency across 
the economy and addressing legislative gaps in various existing regimes.    
