Proposals Paper
ResponseCONSULTATION RESPONSE
October 2024sike.aiSYDNEY
NEW YORK 
VANCOUVER
In response to the Australian Government’s consultation on mandatory AI guardrails, we
propose a forward-thinking framework designed to regulate AI applications, not the
underlying technology. Drawing from our work at sike.ai, we advocate for a flexible, industry-
driven approach that prioritises safety, transparency, and accountability while allowing for
the rapid innovation that defines AI development today.
Key to this approach is the Hub and Spoke model, where high-level standards and operating
procedures are established centrally, while the definitions and specifics of what constitutes
high-risk AI are decided by relevant industry bodies. This avoids a ""one-size-fits-all"" regulation
and ensures that industry experts, not outsiders, set the standards for their sectors.
Additionally, we emphasise the need to underpin these standards with ISO frameworks,
aligning Australia's regulatory stance with international best practices.
We also propose a step-by-step action plan to guide the development of these regulations,
including:
Industry-Specific Trials: To test the proposed guardrails across diverse sectors like
healthcare, finance, and law enforcement, assessing their effectiveness in real-world
settings.
Public Consultation and Feedback: Opening formal consultations and feedback loops,
allowing stakeholders to share their insights and experiences, ensuring regulations are
practical and grounded.
Integration of Emerging Technologies: Testing advanced technologies like blockchain
and zero-knowledge proofs (ZKP) for ensuring tamper-proof record-keeping without
sacrificing confidentiality, addressing potential weaknesses in data management and
transparency.
We see traceability and explainability as critical to these regulations, particularly in high-risk
AI scenarios. Systems like Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG), while providing some
explainability, must be further enhanced with human oversight and mechanisms for digital
attestations to ensure their outputs are reliable and can be confidently acted upon. Finally,
we believe AI should act as a companion in high-risk settings, assisting human decision-
makers while ensuring that responsibility and oversight remain with them.Executive SummarySYDNEY | NEW YORK | VANCOUVER
