Boeing Australia Holdings
Published name
Boeing Australia Holdings
1. Do the proposed principles adequately capture high-risk AI?
Yes
Please identify any:
That defence and national security applications be treated separately noting that there are already legal frameworks Australia adheres to as a signatory of the Geneva Convention when it comes to the use of AI in autonomous weapon systems
3. Do the proposed principles, supported by examples, give enough clarity and certainty on high-risk AI settings and high-risk AI models? Is a more defined approach, with a list of illustrative uses, needed?
NO a more defined list-based approach is needed
If you prefer a list-based approach (similar to the EU and Canada), what use cases should we include? How can this list capture emerging uses of AI?
Critical infrastructure
How can this list capture emerging uses of AI?
The use of AI for the monitoring and management of critical infrastructure (communications, transport, logistics, electricity, etc.) brings a specific category of risks associated with AI direct effects on physical infrastructure. Examples of this need to be consider to assess potential restrictions depending on technology type, deployed function, and operational contexts.
4. Are there high-risk use cases that government should consider banning in its regulatory response (for example, where there is an unacceptable level of risk)?
Yes
If so, how should we define these?
There most-likely will be cases where technologies can be used in manners and contexts outside of the intended and envisaged boundaries conceived by the technology developers. However, it would be hard to envisage these and ban them since this would fall within the unknown unknowns. So the post-deployment evaluation against the intended use and envisaged contexts becomes crucial. Pre-deployment the preparation of safety cases should suffice.
5. Are the proposed principles flexible enough to capture new and emerging forms of high-risk AI, such as general-purpose AI (GPAI)?
Yes
6. Should mandatory guardrails apply to all GPAI models?
No
8. Do the proposed mandatory guardrails appropriately mitigate the risks of AI used in high-risk settings?
No
Please provide any additional comments.
Identify and document AI technology type, intended use and interactions with external entities, and operational contexts within which deployment is envisaged.
10. Do the proposed mandatory guardrails distribute responsibility across the AI supply chain and throughout the AI lifecycle appropriately?
Yes
11. Are the proposed mandatory guardrails sufficient to address the risks of GPAI?
Yes
12. Do you have suggestions for reducing the regulatory burden on small-to-medium sized businesses applying guardrails?
No
Please provide any additional comments
This should be no different from the preparation of other regulatory safety cases for assessment by a regulator.
13. Which legislative option do you feel will best address the use of AI in high-risk settings?
A domain specific approach – adapting existing regulatory frameworks to include the proposed mandatory guardrails
14. Are there any additional limitations of options outlined in this section which the Australian Government should consider?
Yes
Please provide any additional comments.
The risk of an AI technology depends not only on the technology (the what), but also on the how the technology is being used (where and how) and under which contexts the technology is to be used. This aligns with the development of concepts of operations and development of safety cases used in industries such as aerospace. These factors define a space of possibilities that need to be assessed to determine the risk of AI “use”. A technology that may be risky in a particular use case and context may not be so as the use and context change.
In regard to guardrail 5 on enabling human control, once can envisage cases where the intervention may create risks. It is not clear how the guardrails account for this context that requires “meaningful” human control.
It is not clear how the guardrails account for this, which can be a limitation on the effectiveness of the proposed mandatory guardrails.
15. Which regulatory option(s) will best ensure that guardrails for high-risk AI can adapt and respond to step-changes in technology?
A domain specific approach – Adopting the guardrails within existing regulatory frameworks as needed

