Medicines Australia submission to the Department of Industry, Science and Resources’ ‘Introducing mandatory guardrails for AI in high-risk settings: proposals paper’ 4 October 2024 Medicines Australia welcomes the opportunity to respond to the Department of Industry, Science and Resources’ consultation on introducing mandatory guardrails for artificial intelligence (AI) in high risk settings. As the peak body representing the innovative pharmaceutical industry in Australia, our response will focus primarily on AI used in therapeutic goods. Australia's medicines regulation system is highly regarded both domestically and internationally for its rigorous standards and robust oversight. The Therapeutic Good Administration (TGA) regulates AI where it is used in a medical product (technically referred to as a device), for the purposes of diagnosis, prevention, monitoring, prediction, prognosis, treatment, or alleviation of disease, injury or disability.1  The TGA collaborates with international regulatory agencies such as the FDA (US), EMA (Europe), and comparable international regulatory counterparts wherever possible to ensure global consistency in the regulation of medicines. This harmonisation is beneficial for global manufacturers and ensures that Australian standards are in line with international best practices, while promoting timely access to medicines by streamlining registration processes.2 Medicines Australia believes that creating bespoke regulatory requirements could be a barrier to Australia being able to benefit from innovations and efficient regulatory procedures. Bespoke regulatory frameworks could negatively impact both Australian owned and overseas owned companies and should therefore be avoided. A sizeable number, around 250, global and local research-based pharmaceutical companies operate in Australia. Australian owned companies export therapeutic goods which totaled $8.2 billion and ranked 8th largest in Australia in 2019.3 While Australia ‘punches above our weight’, we produce only a very small proportion of medicines that Australians use. Due to the competitive nature of the larger international economies, we rely heavily on imported medicines, particularly for innovative drugs and treatments not yet developed or produced locally.  1	https://www.tga.gov.au/how-we-regulate/manufacturing/manufacture-medical-device/manufacture-speci8ic-types-medical-devices/arti8icial-intelligence-ai-and-medical-device-software	2	https://www.tga.gov.au/resources/resource/international-scienti8ic-guidelines	3	https://www.mtpconnect.org.au/images/2020%20MTPConnect%20Sector%20Competitiveness%20Plan.pdf	 
17 Denison Street, Deakin ACT 2600   P (02) 6147 6500  www.medicinesaustralia.com.au 2 
Over the 2014-2016 period, the largest exporters of pharmaceuticals were EU countries, as well as Switzerland, the United States, India, China, Singapore, Israel and Japan. Together, these economies represented more than 92% of the total value of global exports of pharmaceuticals.4 Given the global supply chain, maintaining international harmonisation of regulatory frameworks for AI in therapeutic goods is paramount in ensuring Australians continue to have access to safe and effective innovative medicines. Medicines Australia has very close ties with international pharmaceutical industry associations, including in Europe, the US, Canada and the International Federation of Pharmaceutical Manufacturers and Associations (IFPMA). As the therapeutic goods sector and regulators in Australia and overseas explore the use of AI in the medicines lifecycle, we look forward to collaborating with the Australian Government, the TGA and other relevant regulators. We would be happy to facilitate collaboration and share learnings from our overseas industry colleagues regarding the development of AI governance frameworks to maintain safe, effective and sustainable supply of innovative medicines to Australia. Medicines Australia’s position and proposed principles are outlined below. They align with the OECD principles, which the Australian Government has committed to, that set standards for AI that are practical and flexible enough to stand the test of time, and promote use of AI that is innovative and trustworthy and that respects human rights and democratic values.5  Our position is also consistent with the IFPMA’s and Australia’s AI ethics principles.6,7  We hope that this may help to guide the development of appropriate mandatory guardrails for AI use in therapeutic goods. Position and Proposed Principles • Medicines Australia believes in the potential of applying AI to deliver benefit for patients, life-science companies and society. We welcome efforts to smartly regulate the use of AI systems in the health and therapeutic goods sector to help ensure AI does not have adverse impacts to physical or mental health or safety of individuals or population groups, while ensuring low-risk use cases are not unintentionally captured. • Pharmaceutical companies locally and internationally are exploring the use of AI in the medicines Lifecycle (R&D, Manufacturing and post approval activities) with the hope to speed discovery, reduce the need for animal testing, run smarter clinical trials, improve product quality and enhance safety and surveillance measures. • We believe the ultimate goal for oversight should be fit-for-purpose, risk-based guidance which is calibrated to the regulatory status, likely benefits and also context of use of AI tools. Such an approach would enable, rather than hinder, the development of safe and effective treatments that reach patients faster and more efficiently.  4	OECD/EUIPO	(2020),	Trade	in	Counterfeit	Pharmaceutical	Products,	Illicit	Trade,	OECD	Publishing,	Paris,	https://doi.org/10.1787/a7c7e054-en.	5 https://oecd.ai/en/ai-principles   	6 https://www.ifpma.org/publications/ifpma-artificial-intelligence-principles/ 	7 Australia’s AI Ethics Principles | Australia’s Artificial Intelligence Ethics Framework | Department of Industry Science and Resources 	 
