• We believe taking a risk-based approach throughout the lifecycle from design, development and deployment can ensure AI adoption is fair, ethical, robust and safe. Focusing on systems being accurate, secure, reliable, transparent and explainable can mitigate risks of user harm.   • Avoid unnecessary regulatory burden - AI governance frameworks should be designed and implemented in a way which does not increase complexity and does not duplicate the process or unnecessarily increase the regulatory burden for pharmaceu`cal manufacturers and regulators.  • ConFnue collaboraFon and harmonizaFon with comparable internaFonal regulators - The TGA should con`nue to collaborate with interna`onal regulatory agencies such as the FDA (US), EMA (Europe), and other comparable interna`onal regulatory counterparts wherever possible to ensure global consistency in the regula`on of AI used in therapeu`c goods and maintain eﬃcient supply chains.  • Development of bespoke regulatory frameworks and requirements should be avoided as this could be a barrier to Australia being able to beneﬁt from innova`ons and eﬃcient regulatory procedures.  • Any new AI governance framework should be pracFcal to implement, by being lean, clear, consistent, ﬁt for purpose, risk based and avoid duplica`on or overlapping regulatory requirements.  • Currently, rapid technological developments and the number of local, regional, global ini`a`ves do not always deﬁne ""AI"" in the same way. Mul`ple and divergent deﬁni`ons lead to confusion and legal uncertainty for interpreta`on and implementa`on. It is important for all interna`onal organisa`ons, na`onal regulatory agencies, and key legisla`ve bodies to work together to align on deﬁniFons and a common terminology for AI.  • Governments and interna`onal organisa`ons should promote AI literacy among pa`ents, healthcare professionals and the public to help ensure ethical and responsible use of AI in healthcare. As Medicines Australia members and many other stakeholders explore the use of AI and assess the areas of high risk in the medicines lifecycle, we look forward to collaborating with the Australian Government, the TGA and other relevant regulators. We would also be happy to utilise our global networks in the pharmaceutical industry, who are well progressed on this issue, to learn from their developing knowledge and experience of developing AI governance frameworks.     ","20241004_FINAL_MA_response_DISR_AI_consultation.248f6f41","20241004_FINAL_MA_response_DISR_AI_consultation.248f6f41.pdf","pdf","C:\\Users\\james.barro\\Documents\\rsm-nvidia-hackathon\\data\\Submission_Docs\\Processed\\20241004_FINAL_MA_response_DISR_AI_consultation.248f6f41.pdf","1"
