CRICOS provider number: 00122A | RTO Code: 3046  
 
AI assisted grading  Narrow AI systems historically exist within educational software to 
support completion of tasks and assessment can take many forms with 
level or risk for grading. Automated AI grading would be high risk is 
certain disciplines and assessment types. However, a low -risk approach 
is where an AI system will not generate a score, but only provide 
educators with information on how students have fulfilled grading 
requirements. Humans are still left to make the final grading assessment.  
 
AI assisted formative 
feedback  These AI tools are mainly for formative use, providing ongoing feedback 
to students and educators. Unlike summative assessment systems used for high- stakes decisions like determining final grades, formative tools are 
low-risk and aim to improve learning ou tcomes without final judgments. 
An essential feature is human oversight, highlighted as Guardrail 5, stressing the need for human review of the tools' outputs.  
 
AI tutor for basic skills 
(maths and grammar)  AI tutoring systems focused on basic skills typically do not significantly 
impact a student's educational trajectory. Additionally, these systems often supplement, rather than replace, human educators  who can provide 
oversight.  
 
Academic support 
early warning signs  AI system will not make the decision but will provide educators with the 
opportunity to investigate the warning signs of academically struggling 
students who may need help and early intervention.  
 
 
To address this  issue , we should consider the following two key characteristics of low -risk AI use : 
 
• Minimal impact on overall educational outcomes:  The AI system's influence on 
significant educational outcomes at award level such as final grading and assessment  is 
minimal.  A method of resolving the balance between the professional and academic 
operation of an educational institution could be the identification of criteri a ensuring AI 
systems are not implemented or deployed in a manner that exerts significant adverse influence on educational outcomes at award level in keeping with principles (a) -(e) in the 
paper . 
 
• Human oversight and intervention:  Human educators retain a central role in the 
decision- making process, effectively overseeing and intervening in the AI system's 
recommendations or outputs.  We could, for example, introduce clarity on characteristics 
such as labelling ‘agentic AI’ in identifying high risk use cases where,  by definition,  human 
oversight is little or none.   
 
In short, i nstead of relying on the AI system alone to explore the case, we should instead be 
clarify ing the role of models and their characteristics that could inform the proposed principle (f) , 
especially in relation to likelihood of occurrence (e.g. model algorithm, transparency, generative 
function, origin of dataset  etc). By considering both the specific context and these characteristics, 
the proposal  can ensure that its definition of high- risk AI in higher education is appropriately 
targeted.  
 
We would also propose that the Department considers the following additional points regarding 
refining definitions of key concepts within the proposals paper.  
Principles - over list -based approach es 
In considering the approach to  high- risk AI settings and models, we believe a principles -based 
approach provides clarity and certainty over a list -based approach. In support of this view, we  note 
that: 
• Even within the Proposals Paper itself, there appears to be a lack of clarity in the listed 
high- risk applications, noting at one point (p4) that “a vast number of uses of AI – for  
 
  
  
  
Page 4 of 7 
CRICOS provider number: 00122A | RTO Code: 3046  
 
example, in optimising parcel delivery,  or monitoring weather patterns, or managing traffic 
flow – are considered low risk and should be  enabled to flourish unimpeded”; and at 
another point in apparent contradiction ( p26, Table 1 of “ High risk use cases identified in 
other countries”)  “Critical infrastructure : AI systems intended to be used as safety 
components in the management and operation of critical digital infrastructure, road traffic, 
…”  [emphasis added]  
• A list based approach seems  to necessitate management of a large number of exceptions 
to listed items ( as found in the EU AI Act, including “i) the AI system is intended to perform 
a narrow procedural task; (ii) the AI system is intended to improve the result of a previously 
completed human activity; (iii) the AI system is intended to detect decision- making patterns 
or deviations from prior decision -making patterns …“ and so on) , where a principles -based 
approach appears much more robust and transparent to such special cases and 
excepti ons.  
Enhanced definition of deployers  
We suggest that deployers who significantly modify AI models, particularly by manipulating system 
and user prompts to control outputs, should be considered ""developers"" under the proposed 
guardrails. This reclassification is crucial because:  
 
• Impact on Risk Profile:  Significant modifications can fundamentally alter an AI system's 
risk profile, potentially introducing new biases, vulnerabilities, or unintended consequences. 
Holding deployers accountable as ""developers"" in such cases is essential for ensuring that 
the guardrails effectively mitigate these risks.  
• Alignment with International Approaches:  This approach aligns with the EU AI Act, 
which designates deployers as ""developers"" if they make ""substantial modifications"" to a 
high- risk AI system or offer it under their own branding. This alignment promotes 
international consistency and avoids loopholes in the application of guardrails.  
 
Accordingly, w e recommend that a clear definition of "" substantial  modification"" be provided in the 
context of  “prompt  engineering” for GPAI models . This could involve considering factors like:  
 
• The purpose for the modification by the deployer (i.e. to connect this with relevant ex post 
implications in domain areas such as education, law enforcement, or democratic purposes)  
• The degree to which modifications alter the intended purpose or functionality of the AI 
model.  
• The potential for modifications to introduce new biases or discriminatory outcomes.  
• The technical complexity of the modifications and the level of expertise required to 
implement them . 
 
Guardrails ensuring testing, transparency and accountability 
of AI  
Guardrail 1. Registering high- risk AI models and systems  
Guardrail 1 discusses establishing, implementing and publishing an accountability process 
including governance, internal capability and a strategy for regulatory compliance. While it 
discusses a documented approach to regulatory compliance, with defined policies, clear roles and 
responsibilities, the guardrail could have a stronger emphasis on logging, recording or cataloguing 
AI assets or an enrolment mechanism to register generative AI platforms. This advice is supported 
by the policy2 developed by the Australian Government for the responsible use of AI which 
recommend that agencies:  
 Understand where and how AI is being used within agencies and developing an internal register 
with this information.  
 
2 Policy for the responsible use of AI in government (https://www.digital.gov.au/policy/ai/policy)   
 
  
  
  
Page 5 of 7 
CRICOS provider number: 00122A | RTO Code: 3046  
 
 
Guardrail 10. Conformity assessment  
This draft Guardrail could also include making allowance for independent third parties, such as 
researchers, to assess the AI model or tool. As platforms have grown they have increasingly 
tended towards the development of ‘walled gardens’ that limit interoperability and aim to retain 
users within their domains. In recent years platforms have shut down or significantly limited access 
to their APIs for researchers. This has reduced visibility and transparency into how their algorithms 
operate. As AI systems b ecome increasingly integrated into various everyday platforms and 
systems there is an urgent need to ensure greater transparency about all facets of these systems 
and their operations. Such requirements include the need for mechanisms to provide access for  
independent researchers for evaluation, which can be achieved in ways that do not compromise 
commercially sensitive or private data.  
 The Australian Internet Observatory
3 is an initiative of ADM+S led by RMIT University in 
partnership with 5 other universities and supported by an investment from the Australian Research 
Data Commons to develop National Research Infrastructure for studying digital platforms and 
social data. The Observatory will provide a range of new tools to enable data collection and test 
environments for studying digital platforms and many of these approaches could be further 
extended to a range of AI systems and application. We would be happy to provide f urther 
information and consultation on this point to strengthen the assessment and reassurance of 
conformity.  
Impact on First Nations People  
While the principles do highlight  potential adverse impacts to groups of individuals or collective 
rights of cultural groups, we feel that there should be greater emphasis o n impacts to  First Nations 
peoples.  
 
The Framework for Governance of Indigenous Data4 published in May 2024 refers  to issues with 
data collection and management of indigenous datasets . Specifically , it states that :  
‘Cultural, Language Group, and First Nations affiliations tend not to be included 
in standard  administrative datasets resulting in Indigenous people not seeing 
themselves within the data.  Some datasets are decontextualized and lack 
additional information that makes them meaningful for researchers or Indigenous 
people and their communities’ . - National Perinatal Data Collection case study  
It is therefore clear that a ny AI system that seeks to make decisions or recommendations involving 
First Nations people may be inadvertently introducing generalisations and unintended biases 
towards specific cultural and language groups . The Framework acknowledges the historical 
marginalisation of Indigenous peoples within data systems and aims to remedy data gaps and 
biases. This objective complements the AI guardrails’ emphasis on testing (Guardrail 4), 
specifically in assessing AI model  performance acros s different social groups to reduce bias and 
prevent discriminatory results.  
Consideration of Severity and Likelihood of  Impacts  
As stated above, under a principles -based approach, clear and definite criteria to assess the scale 
and severity of impacts need to be provided.  This, c oupled with criteria to assess the likelihood of 
risks occurring, aligns with common risk assessment frameworks that are used in many industries 
today.  In determining which AI use cases require mandatory guard rails, we suggest that a 
combination of consequence and likelihood factors be considered in determining which models and 
systems will be subject to additional  mandatory requirements.   
 
We recommend that in using common risk assessment frameworks  evaluating for models and 
systems, there be  sufficient attention placed on applying these frameworks across the full AI 
 
