1. Do the proposed principles adequately capture high-risk AI?
No
Please provide any additional comments.
The proposed principles are comprehensive in capturing the scope of high-risk AI applications; however, it is crucial to ensure they do not inadvertently stifle innovation. An additional principle could focus on promoting innovation alongside regulation to ensure that AI development benefits economic and technological growth in Australia.
2. Do you have any suggestions for how the principles could better capture harms to First Nations people, communities and Country?
Yes
Please provide any additional comments.
Involving First Nations peoples throughout the consultation process will ensure that AI systems respect Indigenous Data Sovereignty and cultural protocols. This could involve integrating Indigenous perspectives into AI training datasets and decision-making processes to ensure cultural appropriateness.
3. Do the proposed principles, supported by examples, give enough clarity and certainty on high-risk AI settings and high-risk AI models? Is a more defined approach, with a list of illustrative uses, needed?
YES the principles give enough clarity and certainty
If you prefer a principles-based approach, what should we address in guidance to give the greatest clarity?
While the principles provide a robust framework, a more defined set of illustrative examples could assist in application consistency. However, flexibility should be retained to avoid restricting AI applications that could prove beneficial.
4. Are there high-risk use cases that government should consider banning in its regulatory response (for example, where there is an unacceptable level of risk)?
No
5. Are the proposed principles flexible enough to capture new and emerging forms of high-risk AI, such as general-purpose AI (GPAI)?
No
Please provide any additional comments.
Given the rapid evolution of AI, it is important that the regulatory approach is dynamic and can adapt as new high-risk categories from emerging AI technologies are identified. Continued feedback loops with AI developers could ensure timely adjustments.
6. Should mandatory guardrails apply to all GPAI models?
No
Please provide any additional comments.
Not all GPAI models should have the same level of oversight. The application and intended use should determine the level of regulation as to not stifle the potential positive advancements these models can bring.
7. What are suitable indicators for defining GPAI models as high-risk?
Base on technical capability
What technical capability should it be based on?
Other
8. Do the proposed mandatory guardrails appropriately mitigate the risks of AI used in high-risk settings?
Yes
Please provide any additional comments.
The guardrails provide a solid basis for risk mitigation but should avoid burdensome compliance that discourages innovation. A periodic review process should be included to ensure the guardrails remain relevant and effective.
Are there any guardrails that we should add or remove?
Yes
What guardrails should we add or remove?
Be transparent with other organisations across the AI supply chain about data, models and systems to help them effectively address risks Keep and maintain records to allow third parties to assess compliance with guardrails Establish, implement and publish an accountability process including governance, internal capability and a strategy for regulatory compliance Undertake conformity assessments to demonstrate and certify compliance with the guardrails
9. How can the guardrails incorporate First Nations knowledge and cultural protocols to ensure AI systems are culturally appropriate and preserve Indigenous Cultural and Intellectual Property?
Establishing partnerships with First Nations communities and incorporating their input into the AI development lifecycle are crucial steps. Building Indigenous data frameworks and establishing clear guidelines for preserving ICIP in AI applications are key actions.
10. Do the proposed mandatory guardrails distribute responsibility across the AI supply chain and throughout the AI lifecycle appropriately?
Yes
Please provide any additional comments
Responsibilities are well-distributed, but care must be taken to ensure that smaller entities are not disproportionately burdened. Creating tiered requirements based on organization size and capacity may be beneficial.
11. Are the proposed mandatory guardrails sufficient to address the risks of GPAI?
No
How could we adapt the guardrails for different GPAI models, for example low-risk and high-risk GPAI models? 
The guardrails could be fine-tuned to better distinguish between various risk levels of GPAI models. Differentiating regulations based on application areas and potential societal impacts will allow for nuanced yet effective oversight.
12. Do you have suggestions for reducing the regulatory burden on small-to-medium sized businesses applying guardrails?
Yes
Please provide any additional comments
Providing streamlined compliance processes or exemptions for small-to-medium-sized enterprises (SMEs) in non-critical sectors could reduce burden. Offering incentives and technical assistance to these businesses would further align with promoting AI growth.
13. Which legislative option do you feel will best address the use of AI in high-risk settings?
A whole of economy approach – introducing a new cross-economy AI Act
What opportunities should the Government take into account in considering each approach? 
A framework that supports innovation while also ensuring safety would be ideal. Introducing a regulatory sandbox approach could enable testing of new AI models under real-world conditions within a controlled regulatory environment.
14. Are there any additional limitations of options outlined in this section which the Australian Government should consider?
Yes
Please provide any additional comments.
Potential limitations include the lag in legislative processes, which must not impede technological advancements. Additionally, ensuring that the workforce is equipped with AI literacy is vital to the successful implementation of regulations.
15. Which regulatory option(s) will best ensure that guardrails for high-risk AI can adapt and respond to step-changes in technology?
A whole of economy approach – introducing a new cross-economy AI Act
Please provide any additional comments.
A dynamic regulatory framework that includes regular review periods and input from Australian AI industry leaders and researchers would best accommodate rapid technological shifts. Allowing adjustable guidelines rather than static rules would ensure continued relevance.
16. Where do you see the greatest risks of gaps and inconsistencies with Australia’s existing laws for the development and deployment of AI?
Australia must do its utmost to be at the forefront of AI capabilities research. Only by ensuring our capabilities are competitive can we ensure that our decisions are most informed and hold more weight. More investment should go to nurturing AI talent and keeping them onshore.
Which regulatory option best addresses this?
A whole of economy approach – Introducing a new cross-economy AI-specific Act (for example, an Australian AI Act).
Please explain why.  
The development of AI capabilities itself will see the largest concentration of power within this decade. There is no better way to figure out how to best regulate AI than to understand the development of AI capabilities and make projections on how they will arise and in what form.
Make a general comment
Australia ought to strive to become a world leader in AI development. It is what Australians deserve.

