Appendix A  
Do the proposed principles adequately capture high- risk AI?  
The Insurance Council consider s that on balance,  the proposed principles would adequately capture 
high- risk AI .  
However, we  believe they  may also unintentionally capture lower risk AI applications.  Examples of low -
risk AI use cases in the insurance industry that may be captured include:  
• Fraud detection applications.  
• The coding and matching of invoices.  
• Code (software) development.  
• Security classification determining programs (e.g., for documents and shared folders).  
• Customer service chatbots.  
• Document processing (e.g., document ingestion and insight generation). 
• Analytics and decision trees for low complexity and high volume transactions (e.g., paying 
windscreen claims).  
As outlined below, we support the flexibility and adaptability that would be provided by a principles -
based approach, but we strongly support there being clear guidance,  and specific examples provided 
alongside the principles . Examples  will give clarity to organisation and assist them with 
implementation .  
Without this clarity, we are concerned investment in AI may be stifled as businesses opt to avoid 
regulatory risk . At a time when we are seeking to boost Australia’s productivity , it is important that any 
new regulation does  not reduce business investment in technology that will help arrest productivity 
losses and improve consumer outcomes.2 
Specific to the proposed principle “ The risk of adverse legal effects, defamation or similarly significant 
effects on an individual”, the Proposal Paper outlines an example from the EU’s General Data Protection Regulation that restricts entities from making solely automated decisions that have a legal 
or similarly significant effect on individuals. We support this and suggest that guidance should clearly 
give regard to risk mitigants and how they can be applied. Risk mitigants may include, for example, the extent of autonomy and human oversight in a decision -making process.  
We also note that potential harms such as bias, discrimination or accessibility issues are proposed to 
be address ed within the guardrails (e.g. Guardrail 2 relating to risk management, and Guardrail 8 
relating to transparency). We suggest that consideration be given to the principles specifically 
acknowledging these types of risks.  
Do you have any suggestions for how the principles could better capture harms to First Nations people, communities and Country?  
We support the proposed principle “ The risk of adverse impacts to groups of individuals or collective 
rights of cultural groups”, its acknowledgement of , and the need to protect marginalised groups 
 
2 The Productivity Commission’s most recent indicative data on Australia’s productivity can be found here.  
 
 
T +61 2 9253 5100  ABN 50 005 617 318  PO BOX R1832 Royal Exchange NSW Australia 1225   insurancecouncil.com.au   
 
including Indigenous communities . For example, the well-documented potential of AI to embed , 
perpetuat e, and amplify existing  biases and stereotypes  about  minority groups underscores  the need 
for countermeasures against these biases . Particularly  as AI increasingly plays a role in decision -
making  and decision -support  developing and implementing safeguards will be essential  to minimis ing 
harmful  impacts on Indigenous communities .  
The Insurance Council’s Indigenous Advisory Committee is focused on advising the insurance industry 
on Indigenous -related issues to drive better customer outcomes, support reconciliation actions, 
increase industry understanding of vulnerable customers and increase knowledge of specific cultural 
and heritage concerns. We would welcome the opportunity to provide views on how the principle and 
supporting guidance, together with the guardrails, could address potential harms to First Nations 
peoples, communities  and Country as it relates to insurance as they are developed.  
Do the proposed principles, supported by examples, give enough clarity and certainty on high -risk AI 
settings and high- risk AI models? Is a more defined approach, with a list of illustrative uses, needed?  
We support the flexibility and adaptability that would be provided by a principles -based approach, but 
we strongly support there being clear guidance and specific examples to provide clarity and assist with 
implementation. We would especially support more detail and definition at a sector -specific level . We 
suggest more definition, guidance and examples will be imperative as any new regulation is introduced in this evolving landscape to ensure regular  business activities are not unintentionally captured by the 
principles .  
For example, we note the list of high -risk use cases identified in other countries includes “ AI systems 
used to make decisions that affect access to essential private services, including credit, insurance in a 
manner that poses significant risk”. Where  insurance is protecting an individual (in general insurance, 
this may include accident and health or workers compensation insurances for instance), the underwriting risk assessment process may need to consider an individual’s characteristics to facilitate 
the provision of the insurance that protects the individual. In other settings, considering an individual’s 
characteristics may be discriminatory but it is essential in the insurance underwriting process.  
Similarly, we note the listing of biometrics as high -risk in other countries and suggest that in certain 
circumstances this technology may in fact be low risk, with considerable benefit, particularly in improving consumer outcomes. For example, t he use of biometric voice data to perform sentiment 
analysis to detect stress and risk of self -harm during an insurance claims process, with appropriately 
managed human follow up care, would be very low risk and very high potential benefit.  However, this 
use would l ikely be captured under the description given in Table 1. It is important that Australia’s 
chosen approach does not preclude such opportunities.  
Specific examples of high- risk settings and models, as well as settings and models that are not high-
risk, can assist businesses in understanding and identifying when their AI use crosses the high -risk 
threshold. Similarly, examples could be used to show t he conditions under which the AI associated 
risks have been reduced such that a business’ use of AI is no longer considered high -risk. As also 
noted above, examples could be supplemented with sector -specific guidance, developed by the 
Government and indust ry, to assist entities with implementation of and compliance with the principles 
and mandatory guardrails.  
Are there high- risk use cases that government should consider banning in its regulatory response (for 
example, where there is an unacceptable level of risk)?  
While we do not propose any particular use cases the Government should consider banning , we do 
note some high- risk use  case s would be covered by existing regulation  and encourage consideration  
 
 
T +61 2 9253 5100  ABN 50 005 617 318  PO BOX R1832 Royal Exchange NSW Australia 1225   insurancecouncil.com.au   
 
of how existing law can be strengthened . For example, discrimination in the employment process  is 
illegal and technology neutral legislation can reflect this . Where possible, regulation and legislation 
should deal with unacceptable practices in a technology neutral manner.  
Are the proposed principles flexible enough to capture new and emerging forms of high -risk AI, such 
as general -purpose AI (GPAI)?  
We understand the principles to be defined by outcomes and  on that basis  suggest they are 
appropriate. This allows flexibility for emerging forms of AI as the decision is based on the impact.  We 
do not consider GPAI to be inherently high -risk.   
Should mandatory guardrails apply to all GPAI models?  
No. We recommend  the guardrails should apply  only where the use of GPAI is high -risk. We also 
recommend greater guidance  and examples are provided on when use of a GPAI model would be 
considered as high-risk. Applying guardrails to all GPAI models runs counter to the  purpose of the 
principles which are being designed to assist in determining i f the “use of an AI system is high -risk”. 
We suggest that there are GPAI models that are helping to promote greater efficiency and  productivity 
by helping people with everyday tasks, rather than making decisions . Broadly speaking, these should 
not be considered high -risk nor subject to guardrails. We are concerned that capturing all GPAI 
models , regardless of their use, will discourage dynamism and business optimisation with negative 
implications for productivity.  
Guardrails will be critical in the development and testing of models to avoid having biases built in .  
What are suitable indicators for defining GPAI models as high -risk? For example, is it enough to define 
GPAI as high -risk against the principles, or should it be based on technical capability such as FLOPS 
(e.g. 10^25 or 10^26 threshold), advice from a sci entific panel, government or other indicators?  
We believe that trying to regulate against technical specification s creates the risk that regulation 
quickly becomes outdated  or obsolete. This is particularly true in areas where  advancements are rapid, 
as is the case with A I. As much as possible, regulation should be technology neutral. Risk may be 
better managed through the regulation of what an AI model can do, rather than how it does a task . 
Given this, we support the use of the principles to define high -risk and would query the purpose of 
those principles if they are paralleled by an attempt to also use technological specifics to indicate high -
risk. 
In the alternative, we  propose a  compromise may be a more general categorisation of the technology, 
such as where a model is deterministic as opposed to non -deterministic.   
Do the proposed mandatory guardrails appropriately mitigate the risks of AI used in high -risk settings?  
The Proposals Paper notes that the proposed mandatory guardrails have been developed with a view 
to being aligned nationally and with comparable jurisdictions and international standards to reduce the 
compliance burden. While we welcome this approach, any new regulation will generally increase 
operating costs for businesses and create additional regulatory burden. This needs to be balanced against the ability for businesses to continue to compete and innovate whilst providing affordable 
products or services for their cust omers. Regulatory burden typically also has a greater impact on 
small to medium businesses. We therefore support the recognition in the Proposals Paper that organisations should integrate the guardrails into existing processes and suggest this should expli citly 
form part of any future regulation.   
 
 
T +61 2 9253 5100  ABN 50 005 617 318  PO BOX R1832 Royal Exchange NSW Australia 1225   insurancecouncil.com.au   
 
We also support the implementation of the proposed mandatory guardrails in a way that enables 
organisations to embed them as appropriate to the nature and extent of the activities they are 
undertaking. For example, while they are proposed to apply to high -risk settings, we would still expect 
there to be differences in these settings across sectors, and the application of the mandatory 
guardrails should therefore reflect this.  
We also welcome the intention that the guardrails will need to be adaptable to remain fit for purpose as 
AI technology continues to evolve and become more autonomous. We also support the intention as 
outlined in the Proposal Paper to provide further  clarity on the division of responsibilities between 
developers and deployers  will be necessary.  
We off er further comments on specific guardrails : 
• Guardrail  8. We believe this guardrail  may prevent Australian entities from creating models 
using their own intellectual property  and new small businesses from entering the market . 
• Guardrail  9. It may be appropriate to ensure businesses can utilise and augment their existing 
risk functions. Additional clarity of appropriately shared responsibility on developers and 
deployers will be critical to ensuring open access and availability of the benefits to AI across the 
economy and wider community.  
• Guardrail  10. We suggest additional guidance be provided that  highlights the risks that need to 
be asses sed when implementing a solution, allowing individual entities to tailor their 
implementation approach.  
Finally, w e also suggest two themes may be missing from the proposed guardrails.  
1. Consent . It is very possible to digitally duplicate a person or company’s image, communications 
and to forecast behaviour through AI techniques. The guidelines speak to letting customers know when they are interacting with AI content. However, nothing currently exists  to govern 
organisations using customer data to create AI content . Many “Terms and Conditions” today are 
broad in the permissions they grant, but an individuals’ current consent for us to retain their 
data for today’s business purposes may not be sufficient for tomorrow’s  business opportunities. 
Explicit consent from individuals  may be necessary for data about them to be used and consent 
may need to be asked again each time the information is used for something new. Models need 
to be able to track who has not consented an d eliminate this.   
This said, w e note that future tranches  of reforms to the Privacy Act  1988 are expected  to 
introduce a “fair and reasonable” requirement  which should deal with this issue. It would be 
appropriate for the Government to confirm this approach  and regulatory interaction  and 
consider if interim measures are required.  
2. Responsibility . We note Guardrail 6’s determination that end -users be informed regarding AI -
enabled decisions . We consider  this determination to be very broad and observe that it touches 
on an  ethical issue which may evolv e in the  general  insurance industry  and broader economy . 
As insurers ability to  access and utilise  larger and larger datasets increases , underwriting 
abilities may also extend to forecast ing events that have serious implications for underwriting . 
We query then an organisation’s responsibility to inform an indiv idual . The guardrails should 
consider an organisation’s  responsibility  in such a situation  and make guiding provisions .  
  
 
 
T +61 2 9253 5100  ABN 50 005 617 318  PO BOX R1832 Royal Exchange NSW Australia 1225   insurancecouncil.com.au   
 
How can the guardrails incorporate First Nations knowledge and cultural protocols to ensure AI 
systems are culturally appropriate and preserve Indigenous Cultural and Intellectual Property?  
While we do not propose a solution, we do note that general AI is trained on vast amounts of publicly 
available data. While we understand that it is common practice for organisations in the AI development 
space to monitor their models for unfair bias, we r ecognise the not insignificant risk that First Nations 
people may not be prioritised through regular processes in a way that ensures culturally appropriate 
expression or best preserves indigenous cultural and intellectual property.  
Do the proposed mandatory guardrails distribute responsibility across the AI supply chain and throughout the AI lifecycle appropriately?  
We agree that the AI deployer bears a level of responsibility,  however  we are concerned that the 
burden of responsibility will be placed too heavily on the eventual deployer of an AI model.  Of 
particular concern are models built overseas . These models  are likely to reflect differences to 
Australian populations, values and ethics .  
We recommend that the level of responsibility be commensurate with the various roles throughout the AI lifecycle.  
We also recommend the Government consider the interaction of any potential AI regulation with the 
use of AI in Australian businesses’ international supply chains , where Australian businesses  are not the 
deployer of the technology.  
Do you have suggestions for reducing the regulatory burden on small -to-medium sized businesses 
applying guardrails?  
As outlined above, we support implementation via existing processes and also in a way that reflects 
the nature and extent of use of AI in high -risk settings. These businesses could also be supported 
through the provision of additional guidance on deployment of AI in organisations to meet transparency 
and explainability requirements.  
The chosen approach must  consider that small businesses must often look external ly for their 
assurance on AI , often lack their own internal  risk frameworks . This must not prevent them from 
participating in the benefits and commercial advantages of AI. These  business es will need a way to 
access developer certified AI solutions and re -use, including GPAI, without onerous risk management 
frameworks.  
Which legislative option do you feel will best address the use of AI in high- risk settings?  
We support a domain specific approach.  While insurance and the financial services sector more 
broadly is already  heavily regulated , integrating within this existing framework would assist to reduce 
any potential overlap or duplication of existing regulatory requirements . Oversight could be 
incorporated into the role of existing regulators (such as the Australian Prudential Regulation Authority)  
to also reduce potential regulatory overlap and burden.    
A domain specific approach would allow those organisations  with risk frameworks for specific 
legislation  to uplift these frameworks rather than creating entirely new risk policies and frameworks.  
We note the Proposal Paper’s suggestion that this option would require “a strong coordination function 
from government .” We also acknowledge that a domain specific approach carries the risk of 
fragmenting implementation across domains and this risk is heightened for entities who operate or 
interact with organisation across domains. In response to the Proposal Paper’s  suggestion  and the  
 
 
T +61 2 9253 5100  ABN 50 005 617 318  PO BOX R1832 Royal Exchange NSW Australia 1225   insurancecouncil.com.au   
 
cross- domain risk , we highlight our  previous recommendation to the Department of Industry, Science 
and Resource’s  Supporting responsible AI: discussion paper that the Government establish  an Expert 
Body with powers that create public accountability for regulators  in their creation of AI regulation.3 
Such a body with these powers would be well placed to assist with economy -wide coordination.  
A domain specific approach would be well supported by an extensive mapping of relevant legislation to 
determine where gaps and risk remain.  
Where do you see the greatest risks of gaps and inconsistencies with Australia’s existing laws for the 
development and deployment of AI?  
We believe there is  a risk in the notice requirement . We note the Privacy Act Review  (2022 ), and the 
Government response acknowledge concerns of  “consent fatigue ”. We note the Proposal Paper 
acknowledges the intersection of AI and privacy regulation  but does not extend to consent fatigue. We 
would welcome  consideration of how consumers can be effectively notified of data collection and 
creation without being overwhelmed by consent notifications.  
Consideration must also be given to how organisations can train AI models using real personal 
information where that information can help reduce bias and increase social benefits. For example, a 
person with a rare health condition may want their data to be used in an AI model so that they are not 
underrepresented. Consent should be allowable in the same way it is for health data used in research. 
Use of synthetic data, which is the likely alternative,  can create bias and affect data quality, creating 
more of a need for a human in the loop processes which can affect productivity.  
Further, consumers’ right to conte st outcomes will need greater  consideration . These consideration s 
will need include the interactions (and likely  integration) with existing dispute resolution processes  
(specifically external) , such as those financial services businesses currently  maintain.  Mandating the 
creating of AI specific dispute resolution processes will add unnecessary costs to business and may 
unintentionally deepen mistrust of AI . 
 
