Submission to the Department of Industry, Science and Resources   7 The ACCC (through Scamwatch) continues to see growing sophistication in scam 
approaches and is alert to the risks AI presents.  For example, the ACCC has received reports 
of scammers employing AI in the form of ‘chatbots’ on social media sites, primarily in 
relation to job scams and investment scams. The bots give the impression that many other 
real people are interested in the pr oduct, and are receiving financial benefit from the scam. 
Similarly, the ACCC is aware that AI is being used to create videos for investment scams, 
often capturing or creating images or footage of celebrities with associated audio endorsing 
the scam.  
The ACCC is also aware of instances where AI may be used to facilitate sinister scam 
activity to assist in the perpetration of scams under duress and involving human 
trafficking.  This form of scam activity has traditionally relied on technically advanced, multi -
lingual individuals (so has been limited to the availability of participating individuals) but, the 
use of AI may lessen the requirement for such skills and allow scammers to engage in this 
activity on a broad  scale.   
The ACCC’s collaboration with  DP-REG and its members  
The Digital Platform Regulators Forum (DP -REG) comprises the ACCC, the Australian 
Communications  and Media Authority, the eSafety Commissioner and the Office of the 
Australian Information Commissioner. Since its inception, DP -REG has progressed 
collaboration and coordination between regulators given the need for complementary 
expertise to address cha llenges in regulating digital platforms.  
In the joint submission  by DP -REG to DISR’s consultation on the ‘Safe and responsible AI in 
Australia’ discussion paper, the ACCC and other DP -REG members support a view that any 
regulatory or Government response to address the risks associated with AI should start by 
considering  the extent to which Australia’s existing regulatory frameworks provide 
appropriate safeguards. The submission also noted that where gaps are identified, the 
Government should consider how existing frameworks may be strengthened and enhanced 
(including thr ough existing regulatory reform proposals).  
In addition to DP -REG’s joint  submission  to DISR’s previous consultation, key DP -REG 
publications relevant to AI include:  
▪ A joint letter submission  to the Senate Select Committee on Adopting Artificial 
Intelligence, outlining the work of DP -REG members, collectively and individually, in 
developing an understanding of the opportunities and impacts arising out of the uptake 
of AI technologies in Austra lia 
▪ A working paper , published in June 2023, considering the risks and harms of algorithms 
that are relevant to the work of each DP -REG member  
▪ A working paper , published in November 2023, providing an overview of large language 
models (LLMs) and their impact on the regulatory roles of each DP -REG member , and  
▪ A working paper , published in September 2024, to provide an overview of multimodal 
foundation models (MFMs), which process data and generate output in multiple data 
types including text, images, videos, and audio, and their impact on the regulatory roles 
of each DP -REG m ember.  
We note that DP -REG has also made a separate joint submission to this discussion paper. 
We consider  that effective coordination between DP -REG members will be crucial to the 
development of effective regulatory approaches to AI, and note that as part of our strategic 
priorities for 2024 -26, DP -REG members will look to increase members’ regulatory capabil ity 
to address issues arising from digital platforms and associated technology . We anticipate 
that any of the proposed regulatory options will require significant work from existing  
Submission to the Department of Industry, Science and Resources   8 regulators to ensure integration or compatibility with these guardrails or new legislative 
frameworks, and note that  the ACCC and other  regulators will need to be appropriately 
resourced to dynamically respond to these changes . 
The ACCC’s internal use of AI  
The ACCC supports the government’s position on the Government as an exemplar of safe 
and responsible use of AI. Building on the Government’s policy for the responsible use of AI 
in government  that came into effect on 1 September 2024, the agency has implemented 
several initiatives. These include an internal:  
• AI Policy  
• AI Procedure ; and 
• AI Register.  
As part of our governance framework, the ACCC has an internal AI working group that 
provides the necessary executive oversight. The group comprises  senior and executive 
stakeholders and ensures a coordinated approach to AI governance across the agency.  
The ACCC has several initiatives in train aimed at uplifting capability throughout the agency. 
We have implemented an AI training course that is made available to all staff. Our specialist 
unit has developed community of practice information sessions. We h ave also developed 
communications to ensure broader staff awareness of our work in the AI space and our 
alignment with the whole -of-government position.  
The ACCC also has engaged with other stakeholders in other regulatory and government 
agencies across the APS to ensure that we have alignment in our common approaches to AI 
