Accenture’s approach to Responsible AI  
 
Our approach to Responsible AI (RAI) has been developed from our own internal journey 
and our experiences with clients, and  is grounded in the common preventive practices 
developed across international regulations and standards.  
 
The Accenture RAI program began in 2016 with the development of our RAI principles, 
which are aligned to our company values and embedded into our Code of Business 
Ethics.1 From there we developed RAI tools and processes, launched enterprise -wide 
training, expanded our risk assessment and testing capabilities, and we now continue to 
operationalise and evolve our program to adapt to changes in the technology and 
regulatory landscape.  
 
We reinforced our RAI compliance program with five essential elements that helped us 
activate ethical AI into practice: establish AI governance and principles; conduct AI risk 
assessment s; enable systemic RAI testing and risk mitigation; ongoing monitoring and 
compliance of AI; and solutions for enterprise impact on workforce, privacy, security, 
and sustainability.2   
 
This approach can be rapidly deployed through our RAI Platform , which offers an end -to-
end dashboard for operationalising the systemic enablement of AI risk testing , with a 
maturity assessment to measure alignment to regulations and standards, risk screening 
to categorise AI use cases by level of risk, qualitative risk assessments for AI use cases, 
testing toolkits to quantitatively measure risk, a monitoring control  plane, and solutions 
for privacy and security, such as agentic prompt adversarial testing (or RAI red teaming).  
 
In addition to our technology solutions, which we use for both us and our clients, we 
accelerated our RAI program further by enabling our employees with RAI training and 
developing mandatory ethics and compliance training for the 30,000 Accenture people 
most directly involved with AI.  
 
Based on our own internal experiences and the RAI client projects we  have completed 
around the world , we can share the following practical examples and insights on our 
systemic enablement of AI risk testing . 
 
