Section C: Regulatory options to mandate guardrails  
 
 
Question 13: Which legislative option  do you feel will best address the use of AI in high -risk 
settings? What opportunities should the government take into account in considering each 
approach?  
Question 15: Which regulatory option/s will best ensure that guardrails for high -risk AI can adapt 
and respond  to step -changes in technology?  
Question 16: Where do you see the greatest risks of gaps or inconsistencies  with Australia’s existing 
laws for the development and deployment of AI? Which regulatory option best addresses this, and 
why?  
 
Accenture recognises that option 2 (the framework approach) or option 3 (the whole of 
economy approach), provides  clarity and comprehensive legislative coverage to 
implementing the mandatory guardrails. Both approaches effectively manage the unique 
challenges of high -risk AI, and help build public trust, and may  avoid inconsistencies 
across domain/sector regulators  that option 1 may inadvertently cause .  
 
Accenture reco mmends that the Australian Government consider and encourage the 
establishment  of an AI Safety Institute to contribute to AI safety research, leveraging 
insights from institutes overseas , develop guidance , promot e best practices, and provide 
SME s with the support and tools to achieve compliance. Investing in talent development 
for this body would contribute to the goal of supporting Australia’s AI capability  and 
whichever  regulatory option the Government deems to be the most appropriate to 
enforce the mandatory guardrails . 
 
Recommendation 5: AI Safety Institute : Accenture supports the formation of an AI 
Safety Institute to contribute to AI safety research, leveraging insights from institutes 
overseas , develop guidance , promot e best practices, and provide SME s with the support 
and tools to achieve compliance.  
 
From our global observations and experience, Accenture also recognises that 
maintaining interoperability with international AI regulation and standards is necessary to 
ease the burden on Australian businesses operating overseas, and to enable Australia to 
leverage the risk mitigation efforts of overseas regulators .  
 
To reduce the risk management and regulatory enforcement burden on Australia, 
Australian businesses should be able to rely on certifications secured under sufficient 
comparable AI regulations overseas . This international interoperability will work to 
streamline processes for governments and businesses, by removing process 
duplications,  and reducing  assessment timeframes  and costs.   
 
Recommendation 6: International interoperability : Accenture recommends that the 
Australian Government consider ways to strengthen i nteroperability with international AI 
regulation and standards to both ease the burden on Australian businesses operating 
overseas, and to enable Australia to leverage the risk mitigation efforts of overseas 
regulators .   
Accenture Australia  
3 Sussex Street | Barangaroo  
Sydney  | NSW  | 2000  
10 
 
 
Conclusion  
 
 
Accenture  commend s the Australian Government for taking proactive steps to regulate 
high -risk AI. We recommend ensuring consistent definitions and guardrails that clarify 
the roles of developers , deployers, and other suppliers in the middle of the AI value 
chain, while also protecting SM Es from undue risk. We also stress the importance of 
regulatory support to help companies comply with these new frameworks .  
 
By p rioritising transparency, accountability, fairness, and ethical considerations in the 
design, development, and deployment of AI technologies, we can ensure that AI systems 
are aligned with human values and contribute to positive societal outcomes.  By 
harnessing the transformative power of AI responsibly, we can build a more equitable, 
sustainable, and prosperous future for Australians.  
 
 
 
 
