 3 INTRODUCTION  These submissions are made in response to the Government’s paper entitled ‘Safe and Responsible AI in Australia: Proposal Paper for Introducing Mandatory Guardrails for AI in High-Risk Settings’ (the ‘AI Proposal’) dated September 2024.1 The Government is to be commended for taking the initiative in seeking to regulate AI to ensure that is safe and ultimately benefits individual citizens and society more generally, because as the Government observes, AI is already ‘permeating institutions, infrastructure, products and services’.2 Before addressing the Government’s specific questions on AI, this submission first addresses the a priori questions as to: 1. what a government’s goals should be in regulating AI;  2. how governments should conceptualise AI for the purpose of determining the regulatory challenges associated with its use and the appropriate regulatory response; and  3. the regulatory significance of the fact that AI operates at scale.  The purpose of this preliminary discussion is to aid the Government in identifying and tackling the regulatory challenges associated with the use of AI in an effective and comprehensive way.  1.1 Purpose of regulating AI  As the Government notes in the AI Proposal, each successive wave of technology raises difficult questions as to whether and how it should be regulated to ensure that society reaps the benefit of the technology whilst also minimising or avoiding both potential known and unknown risks associated with its use. Given these shifting sands, in addressing these questions, it is useful to adopt a high-level framework derived from the work of Roger Brownsword.3 Brownsword identifies three ideal regulatory responsibilities that can be used ‘to assess the fitness of the regulatory environment’4 and  1 Australian Government Department of Industry, Science and Resources, Proposals Paper for Introducing Mandatory Guardrails for AI in High-Risk Settings (Commonwealth of Australia, September 2024). 2 Ibid 2. 3 Roger Brownsword, Law, Technology and Society: Re-Imagining the Regulatory Environment (Routledge, 2019). 4 Ibid chapter 4. Submissions: AI Proposal Paper Author: Jane Hogan Date: 4 October 2024  
 4 which this submission argues can be used to guide the Government’s response to AI. These three responsibilities are developing regulations that:  1. ensures that the ‘preconditions (the commons) for any form of human social existence, for any kind of human community, are maintained and protected’;5 2. articulates and respects the ‘distinctive fundamental values of the community’6 such as the focus on human dignity in the EU or the proposed focus on human rights in the AI Proposal; and 3. maintains an acceptable balance between ‘the legitimate (but potentially competing and conflicting) interests of members of the community’.7 This includes maintaining ‘for example, an acceptable balance between the interest in innovation and technological progress and the interests in human health and safety, protection of the environment, preservation of employment options, and so on’.8 The articulation of these responsibilities as the ideal objectives for the regulation of new technology is significant because it suggests that, while there are some highly useful elements in the AI Proposal, the Government’s objectives are unduly narrow. They are unduly narrow firstly, because rather than privileging the preconditions for the commons necessary for human existence, although there is some concern for this issue in relation to the threats AI poses to democracy more generally,9 the AI Proposal Paper tends to focus predominantly on the economic benefits that may potentially be derived from the use of AI through the promotion of trust in it. In so doing, it fails to appropriately prioritise broader goals of not merely ensuring net economic benefits, but ensuring the appropriate distribution of those benefits and avoiding irreparable social harm.  Secondly, rather than articulating the distinctively Australian values, such as, for example, a ‘fair go for all’ that ought to be respected by any individual or entity designing, developing or deploying AI in Australia, the AI Proposal suggests merely that an AI system will be classified as a high-risk one having regard, amongst other things, to an individual’s ‘rights recognised in Australian human rights  5 Ibid 30. 6 Ibid. 7 Ibid. 8 Ibid. 9 Australian Government Department of Industry, Science and Resources (n 1) 23–5. Submissions: AI Proposal Paper Author: Jane Hogan Date: 4 October 2024  
 5 law’.10 As the AI Proposal itself notes11 and as discussed further below,12 these rights are very limited. They do not, therefore, appropriately reflect Australian values of, for example, respect for human dignity or fairness and do not provide the desired certainty and clarity for those designing, developing and deploying AI in Australia by clearly delineating what lines must not be crossed. Further, the utilitarian flavour of some aspects of the AI Proposal suggests that the Government does not view itself as playing an important role in protecting Australian society and its citizens from harms caused by AI. Rather, it suggests that almost anything could be traded away as a result of the use of AI, such as the human dignity of older persons who may not be able to navigate an increasingly complex digital environment, provided the perceived economic benefits flowing from the use of AI outweighed these perceived – and often intangible - costs.  Finally, the AI Proposal is unduly narrow because it does not identify all of the relevant stakeholders in what this submission calls the ‘AI Project’. While it clearly identifies AI designers, developers and deployers as having a clear interest in AI regulation, it has not identified all those interested in it, including those whose industries or livelihoods might be impacted by AI. This is a concern for two reasons. Firstly, it means that the Government has not taken into account in its proposal the disruption that AI may cause to other parts of society, such as the disruption it is currently causing to the educational sector. Secondly, it means that the Government has not taken into account the distribution of both the benefits and detriments of AI. This includes the distribution of, for example: • any detrimental unemployment that may be caused by AI as software replaces jobs; and  • the economic benefits of AI, which, because of the economies of scale and Australia’s position in the AI lifecycle, may lead to the concentration of AI-generated economic value in only a small handful of potentially overseas individuals or firms.  Finally, the AI Proposal is unduly narrow because it does not recognise that the Government’s first order priority ought to be promoting fair and just AI. Rather, it appears that the Government has privileged the interests of designers, developers and deployers by designating regulatory clarity and certainty as the first priority for regulation. While regulatory clarity and certainty are desirable –  10 Ibid 19. 11 Ibid 20. 12 Section 2, qn 1 below. Submissions: AI Proposal Paper Author: Jane Hogan Date: 4 October 2024  
 6 particularly for those who seek to promote AI – they should be seen as second-order priorities. This is because those who seek to disrupt the status quo in unknown ways with unknown consequences should not expect the Government to provide regulatory certainty. Rather, they should expect the Government to respond to their actions and hold them accountable when it becomes known that the AI caused disruption has resulted in outcomes that are neither fair nor just and may be potentially harmful.  A new regulatory bargain If the Government wants to ensure safe, responsible and fair AI, it should attend to all of these matters. More specifically, the Government should  approach the regulation of AI as a new regulatory bargain between AI technologists and society in which AI technologists will be permitted to experiment with new forms of AI and their impact on individuals and society more broadly, but only to the extent that AI technologists: • do so in a way that respects human dignity, Australian values and Australian society; and  • engage positively, openly and in good faith with an AI regulator and the Australian Government more broadly.  As part of that bargain, the Government should.  1. to the extent it can, clearly articulate what good AI looks like, beyond the motherhoods statements in the Government’s AI ethics framework,13 and require compliance of all AI systems with those principles; and 2. include two new pillars in the Australian Government’s approach that focus on: (a) positioning the Government as the promoter of responsible and fair AI, the protector of Australian citizens and society more generally; and  (b) ensuring the fair distribution of both the benefits and risks associated with the use of AI amongst all stakeholders.   13 ‘Australia’s AI Ethics Framework: Consultation’, Department of Industry, Science and Resources (Web Page, 22 June 2021) <https://consult.industry.gov.au/australias-ai-ethics-framework>. Submissions: AI Proposal Paper Author: Jane Hogan Date: 4 October 2024  
 7 1.2 The nature of AI This submission argues that for the purpose of regulatory analysis, it is important not only to define AI but also to conceptualise what is involved when AI is released into what developers sometimes call ‘the wild’ - by which they, in effect, mean human society. In conceptualising AI in this way, this submission recommends that the Government avoids conceptualising AI narrowly, as merely a particular form of software. Rather, it recommends that the Government conceptualise AI as socio-technical systems consisting of both a ‘product’ aspect (the AI software itself) and a series of social practices associated with both its production and use.  To some extent, the AI Proposal has conceptualised AI in this way by observing that the AI development process is complex14 and involves multiple individuals and entities who may participate as part of an AI lifecycle.15 This focus on the broader development process and the AI development chain is welcome, as is the very clear language used to describe each player in that lifecycle, especially when compared to that adopted in the AI Act 2024 (EU) (‘AI Act (EU)’).16 While the Government is to be commended for this analysis, its approach is nonetheless too narrow.  1.2.1 The implications of identifying AI as goal focused  The Government’s conceptualisation of AI is too narrow, firstly, because it fails to distinguish the different kinds of objectives that may be associated with AI’s character as software. The Government correctly identifies that all AI systems adopt technical objectives that are used as a key performance measure within an AI system.17 Just as importantly, if not more importantly, there are also likely to be broader objectives for the system as a whole, such as how to cure cancer by finding a new drug, how to monetise the AI system through the optimisation of user’s clicks, or how to kill a human through the use of automated drones. These broader objectives become operationalised into the AI system itself. Whilst they are not necessarily relevant to the question of whether or not the AI system  14 Australian Government Department of Industry, Science and Resources (n 1) 10. 15 Ibid 302. 16 Regulation (EU) 2024/1689 of the European Parliament and of the Council of 13 June 2024 Laying Down Harmonised Rules on Artificial Intelligence and Amending Regulations (EC) No 300/2008, (EU) No 167/2013, (EU) No 168/2013, (EU) 2018/858, (EU) 2018/1139 and (EU) 2019/2144 and Directives 2014/90/EU, (EU) 2016/797 and (EU) 2020/1828 (Artificial Intelligence Act) [2024] OJ L 1/144 (‘AI Act (EU)’). 17 Australian Government Department of Industry, Science and Resources (n 1) 8 (definition of ‘Artificial Intelligence System’), 38. Submissions: AI Proposal Paper Author: Jane Hogan Date: 4 October 2024  
 8 properly functions, they are highly relevant to the question of regulation because it is these broader objectives that determine both: • the more technical objectives of the AI system; and  • the degree to which AI systems are likely to be compatible with Australian values.  As a result, if Australia wants trustworthy AI, its regulation needs to pay attention to whose objectives are being served by AI in both the technical system objectives and the broader organisational objectives of those designing, developing or deploying.  This focus on broad objectives is particularly important for AI used in the generation or distribution of information – given the effect this has on human emotions and cognition and, therefore, on society as a whole - and in the emerging area of agentic AI. This is because, in those AI systems, there may be clear conflicts between the broader objectives of the AI system itself and those of the users. While a user might be looking for accurate and relevant content or an AI system that acts on their behalf in their own interest, the AI system may privilege the interests of shareholders by ensuring the AI system optimising a user’s attention for clicks or, in the case of agentic AI, directing the user to recommended products or services that may provide a benefit for the AI system’s owners, but may not necessarily be in the best interest of the user. Given this, the Government needs to: • treat AI systems that act on another’s behalf by retrieving information or be performing tasks as ones that are prima facie high risk; and  • include, as a fundamental AI Guardrail, one that provides clear direction to AI designers, developers and deployers that these kinds of conflicts of interest should include, at a minimum, clear disclosure of the AI’s commercial objective to any users. Ideally, however, this AI Guardrail should require AI designers, developers and deployers to resolve these conflicts in the users’ favour. In other words, the Government should require AI that is loyal to its users.18   18 Anthony Aguirre et al, ‘AI Loyalty: A New Paradigm for Aligning Stakeholder Interests’ (2020) 1(3) IEEE Transactions on Technology and Society 128 (‘AI Loyalty’); Anthony Aguirre et al, ‘AI Loyalty by Design: A Framework for Governance of AI’ in Oxford Handbook on AI Governance (Oxford University Press, 2023). Submissions: AI Proposal Paper Author: Jane Hogan Date: 4 October 2024  
 9 1.2.2 The implications of the practices of production  The Government’s approach to AI regulation is too narrow secondly because while the Government has some conceptualisation of AI being the product of an AI lifecycle, it does not take into account that all AI lifecycles may not be equal and some are more likely than others to develop AI systems that are consistent with Australian values. In other words, the Government’s approach fails to take into account the practices associated with the production of AI systems – who is involved and why - that are relevant to the types of AI likely to be designed, developed or deployed in Australia. For example, researchers in AI who are interested in a cure for cancer who operate within a university or other not-for-profit entity are likely to produce a very different kind of AI to AI researchers who are seeking to monetise AI intended to generate or distribute content by selling, for example, a user’s attention. Further, researchers operating in such different contexts are likely to be motivated by different incentives and subscribe to different values. These different incentives and values are, in turn, likely to be ultimately embedded in an AI system itself, including, but not limited to, the objectives discussed above. Further, depending on the context in which an AI system was produced, these values and incentives may or may not be consistent with Australian values of, for example, fairness.  Given this, if Australia wants trustworthy AI, the regulation needs to focus not only on the contexts or purpose for which AI is used. It also needs to focus on the practices of production of AI, recognising that only some practices of production are, therefore, welcome because they are likely to produce AI systems that reflect and embed values and practices that are consistent with Australian values. Others are likely to produce AI systems that do not, for example, respect human autonomy or dignity and ought not to be welcome in Australia.  1.2.3 The implications of the practices of AI’s use  The Government’s approach to AI regulation is too narrow, finally, because it fails to take into account that AI systems are not merely products of the social practices surrounding its design, development and deployment. They also produce social practices arising from use. These may be, for example, by: • replacing some workers;  Submissions: AI Proposal Paper Author: Jane Hogan Date: 4 October 2024  
 10 • further encouraging a ‘digital first’ world and a permanent shift towards human-computer interactions rather than human-human ones - raising important questions not just about discrimination but about accessibility, inclusion and respect for those who may be compelled to deal with AI with little digital capability; or  • as the Government acknowledges, polarising society into distinct information bubbles with harmful effects on democracy and social cohesion.19  Given this, if Australia wants trustworthy AI, the AI Guardrails need to focus not just on the contexts of use but on the manner of use and the way it affects individuals and societies more broadly. As discussed below, some of these uses are undesirable and should be banned. 1.3 AI’s operation at scale, the new calculus of risk and the implications of a precautionary approach  One last aspect of the conceptualisation of AI that is vital to take into account is the ability of AI systems to operate at scale. Given this, the Government is to be commended for noting that the ability of an AI system to operate at scale has regulatory significance.20 It is vital, however, that the Government understand the regulatory implications of this fact. This is because the ability of AI to operate at scale creates not just a quantitative difference, but a qualitative difference associated with the risk of regulatory failure. As the Government observes, rather than that regulatory failure potentially only affecting a small number of users, the use of AI may have wide ranging detrimental societal effects. In the nomenclature of this submission, the use of AI, in fact, creates a new ‘calculus of risk’ relating to a failure of the regulatory model to prevent legal service providers from acting appropriately. This new calculus of risk is important firstly, because it means that it is not sufficient for the regulation to ensure that most AI systems are designed, developed and deployed appropriately. It is vital that all AI systems, especially those in high-risk categories, are designed, developed and deployed appropriately. This is because a single failure of even a single service provider to use AI appropriately may play out at scale. This new calculus of risk is important, secondly, because it has implications as  19 Australian Government Department of Industry, Science and Resources (n 1) 24. 20 Ibid 23. Submissions: AI Proposal Paper Author: Jane Hogan Date: 4 October 2024  
 11 to what kind of regulatory strategy ought to be adopted. More specifically, as the AI Proposal welcomingly observes,21 the scale of AI implies a need for a precautionary approach where the focus is on preventing ex-ante harm rather than seeking to remediate ex post facto potentially irreparable harms to the citizens or the social fabric of society more broadly. This new calculus of risks is important thirdly, because it implies a need for a regulatory oversight model that includes an active regulator who can move quickly and proactively to prevent harms arising at scale. As a result, ideally, the Government’s regulatory model for AI should include an AI statutory regulator that is empowered to act proactively to exercise regulatory oversight of those that participate in the design, deployment and development of AI systems, including the design, development and deployment of high-risk AI systems. That statutory regulator should also be involved in a constant dialogue with AI stakeholders, including those that design, develop and deploy AI and those affected by its use, to help crystallise the risks involved in the use of AI - enabling them to become known risks and for the risk level of AI to be lowered22 - and to ensure that AI used in Australia upholds Australian values.  2 Response to Questions for Consultation With the above conceptual framework in mind, this submission now provides its response to the AI Proposal Paper’s questions.  Question 1: Do the proposed principles adequately capture high-risk AI? The proposed principles are a useful starting point for identifying high-risk AI. They are problematic, however, for three reasons.  1.1 Incomplete Regulating for the unknown  Firstly, the proposed principles are incomplete as they are written on the assumption that the risks arising from the use of AI can be known at the outset. One of the regulatory challenges with AI is that it is unclear at the outset what the potential issues and risks are associated with a particular form of AI, with a particular use of AI, or with particular social practices generated by AI’s use – such as  21 Ibid 16. 22 See the response to question 1 below. Submissions: AI Proposal Paper Author: Jane Hogan Date: 4 October 2024  
 12 the polarisation of society. Given the new calculus of risk with the use of AI,23 this means that prima facie, any new form of AI, new use of AI, or new social practices associated with AI should be treated as high risk unless and until they can be demonstrated by those promoting its use that it is safe. This is essential to preventing harm arising before it arises – potentially at scale. Accordingly, the first principle that ought to be adopted to determine whether an AI system is high-risk, is how novel the AI system is, how novel the business model associated with the AI system is and the degree to which the impact of its use is known. Where the AI system or its business model is novel, and its impact is unknown, it should be treated as an AI system that is high-risk.  Regulating for the known propensities of different production environments Secondly, the principles are also incomplete because they ignore the fact that who is producing the AI system and the purpose of that production is a factor that impacts the degree of risk associated with its use. More specifically, as noted above,24 an AI system that is the product of a university research team designed to support a not-for-profit cure for cancer is likely to be a less risky form of AI than an AI that is designed to support a two-sided market that captures a user’s attention in order to sell it or to monetise their behavioural data.25 Accordingly, the proposed principles should include as a risk factor, one that focuses on the context and practices associated with the production of AI, not merely its use, and classify AI systems as potentially high-risk systems where the need to profit from AI may create a conflict with the interests of Australian users or society more broadly.  Ensuring accuracy as a goal  The principles are also incomplete because they fail to identify, as a risk factor, uses of  AI where accuracy is particularly important. Rather, through its focus on human rights, the principles tends to focus on discriminatory uses of AI. While it is important to ensure that AI does not amplify existing discrimination, discrimination is merely one manifestation of inaccuracy of AI. That is, an AI system that makes a recommendation about the likely rate of recidivism based on a factor such as gender or race that has no bearing on the likely rate of recidivism is not only discriminatory: at its simplest, it  23 See [1.3] above. 24 See [1.2] above. 25 ACCC, Digital Platforms Inquiry: Final Report:Executive Summary (Government Report No Executive Summary, ACCC, June 2019) 1, ch 2 <https://www.accc.gov.au/publications/digital-platforms-inquiry-final-report> (‘Digital Platforms Inquiry’). Submissions: AI Proposal Paper Author: Jane Hogan Date: 4 October 2024  
 13 is one that is inaccurate. It may be similarly inaccurate if it finds, for example, a correlation in the data between recidivism and the colour of someone’s shoes, which has no real predictive power. While in many cases, it might be possible to tie this kind of inaccuracy back to a protected attribute (shoe colour might perhaps be an indicator of gender or race), it may not be possible in all cases. This is important because if an AI system produces an output that would significantly impact a person’s life, all inaccuracies are important, not just those that reflect broader discrimination in the society itself. Accordingly, the principles should include, as one indicia of a high-risk AI system, that an AI system will be used in a context where the accuracy of the system is important and inaccurate output of an AI system is likely to have a significant impact on individuals or society more generally.  Disruption  The principles should also take into account the impact of AI, including the degree of disruption it causes to existing industries. This is essential to ensure that, over time, an appropriate balance can be developed between the interests of all stakeholders.  1.2 The expression of the principles problematic The principles are not only incomplete. In some instances, the expressions of the principles are too narrow.  Principle (a) - human rights  The identification of human rights as one of the yardsticks by which to measure the likely risk of AI is valuable and is supported by this submission. Because Australian human rights are notoriously limited, the limitation of the proposed principles to adverse impacts on an individual’s human rights recognised in Australian human rights renders this principle overly weak. In effect, it gives a green light to AI designers, developers and deployers to ignore Australians’ human rights because they are not well-protected by Australian law. To remedy this, the AI regulation should, at a minimum, clearly articulate the Australian digital rights charter. Ideally, however, the Government ought to ensure that Australian human rights are protected by law more broadly.  Principle (c) - legal effect  The identification of adverse legal effects is useful, but again, it is too narrow. The limitation that the consequence of use has some legal effect raises difficult questions about what this means. Is the Submissions: AI Proposal Paper Author: Jane Hogan Date: 4 October 2024  
 14 refusal to allocate a rental property to an applicant based on erroneous assumptions about their ability to pay an adverse legal effect, given that the rental applicants were seeking legal rights to rent the property but had no right to do so? Because of the impact that this type of use might have on individuals, this type of use of AI ought to be covered as a high-risk use of AI, but it is not clearly covered in this principle. The position is not remedied by the addition of the words similarly significant effects on an individual26 which are too vague. To cover this challenge, this risk should be written to identify not only AI’s potential to have legal consequences – whatever that might mean -  but its allocative and distributional function – such as in determining who gets to rent a house or receive priority for medical treatment. It should, therefore, include, as a high-risk form of AI, any AI system used to allocate or distribute resources to individuals or social groups that are necessary to participate with human dignity in modern social life.  Principle (e) - societal impact The approach to societal impact is also too narrow. The problem with this principle is one of priority. The risk of adverse impacts that operate on a societal level seems to suggest, through the ordering of the risks within the existing principle, that economic impacts are the most significant. Because the economy is merely one part of Australian society, this principle should be reordered to prioritise those aspects of Australia’s social infrastructure necessary for social life. It should, therefore, read: “The risk of adverse broader impacts to Australian society, the environment and the rule of law that sustains it, and the Australian economy which primarily attends to the distribution of goods and services.” Principle (f) - severity Given the submission above that it might not be clear at the outset what the likely risks are of the use of an AI system, this principle should be rewritten to include a caveat that this principle is relevant to the extent the risks are known. It should, therefore, read as follows: The severity and extent of those adverse impacts outlined in principles (a) to (e) above – to the extent they are, in fact, known.   26 Australian Government Department of Industry, Science and Resources (n 1) 19. Submissions: AI Proposal Paper Author: Jane Hogan Date: 4 October 2024  
 15 To the extent that the risks are not known, these types of AI systems should be treated with precaution as high-risk AI systems in the manner discussed above.27  1.3 Inappropriate order  Presumably, the proposed principles reflect an order of priority of the separate principles. On this assumption, this submission suggests that the order is not appropriate and should be adjusted as follows. The first principle should relate to the novel AI systems whose impact is unknown, as discussed above.28 The second principle should be principle (e) to give proper priority to interests the Government should protect. The order of the other principles should remain the same.  Question 3: Do the proposed principles, supported by examples, give enough clarity and certainty on high-risk AI settings and high-risk AI models? Is a more defined approach, with a list of illustrative uses, needed? If you prefer a principles-based approach, what should we address in guidance to give the greatest clarity? Subject to the above amendments, the proposed principles-based approach should provide sufficient certainty as to which AI systems are high-risk. It should, however, be accompanied by: • a clear list of AI systems, their contexts of production and use that are presumptively treated as high-risk AI systems;  • a requirement that deployers of an AI system notify the regulator of their own proposed classification of the AI system – with the power for that to be rejected by the AI regulator; and  • a process whereby a statutory regulator can: o quickly declare novel AI systems, or AI systems of a specific class, as high-risk systems; and o reclassify specific AI systems, or AI systems of a specific class, that are classified as high-risk AI systems as low-risk ones.  27 See qn 1, [1.1], (Regulating for the Unknown). 28 Ibid. Submissions: AI Proposal Paper Author: Jane Hogan Date: 4 October 2024  
 16 In regards to a list based approach, with some modifications, the EU approach is preferred to Canada. The modifications are: • education/training: AI systems used in education/training ought to include any AI system that is, in fact, used in education or training, whether this is the intention of the designer, developer or deployer or not, including those AI systems that may be used to generate educational responses. This is appropriate because it allocates responsibility to those who disrupt that sector;  • employment: employment should include AI systems intended to surveil employees or to otherwise undermine their human dignity or infringe their human rights while at work;  • services: access to essential private and public services should be redefined to include systems that allocate resources to individuals or social groups that are necessary to participate with human dignity in modern social life. With that modification, the expression adopted in the Canadian legislation is to be preferred;  • justice/democracy: the administration of justice and democratic processes should include other uses of AI that touch and concern the administration of justice and the rule of law - not just uses by courts or electoral bodies. This includes AI systems used to support legal advisers or to directly provide legal assistance to another without the involvement of lawyers or other humans. This is essential to ensure that AI systems are not used in a way that wastes courts’ time or, more broadly, undermines confidence in the legal system by spreading fake law. This category should also include AI systems that may compete with the administration of justice, such as AI systems used as an alternative form of dispute resolution or AI used or those used to ensure compliance (‘regtech’) which might ultimately become a de facto new kind or regulator; and  • online content: the category of ‘online content’ from the Canadian approach should be adopted as a high-risk use of AI and should include AI systems used for the moderation of digital content, the prioritisation or distribution of digital content or its generation.  Submissions: AI Proposal Paper Author: Jane Hogan Date: 4 October 2024  
 17 Question 4: Are there high-risk use cases that Government should consider banning in its regulatory response (for example, where there is an unacceptable level of risk)? If so, how should we define these?  Yes. There are high-risk practices in the use of AI systems. In this regard, the Government should ban at least the practices that are banned in chapter II of the AI Act (EU).29 Until it is able to incorporate a prohibition on the reliance on patterns of dark design in consumer legislation more broadly, it should also specifically prohibit an AI system from relying on patterns of dark design in its human-computer interaction because of the high risk that AI systems can manipulate humans emotions, beliefs or reasoning.  Question 5: Are the proposed principles flexible enough to capture new and emerging forms of high-risk AI, such as general-purpose AI (GPAI)?  For the reasons discussed above, to adopt a precautionary approach, all novel forms and uses of AI should presumptively be treated as high-risk uses unless and until those that propose to design, deploy or develop such AI systems can demonstrate that their use is consistent with the objectives of technological regulation discussed above. Under this principle, GPAI would necessarily be captured as a high-risk AI. Similarly, when agentic AI emerges ‘in the wild,’ it will also be captured as a high-risk AI unless and until it can be demonstrated to be a low-risk use.  Further, designers, deployers and developers of AI systems should be aware that even those uses of AI that initially seem low-risk, or have become accepted as low-risk AI, may nonetheless reveal themselves to be high-risk uses over time. As discussed above, designers, deployers and developers should, therefore, understand that they are involved in a new AI regulatory bargain in which: • they only have social license to use AI while it is clear that the use is, in fact, a low-risk use or the risks have become known and are considered acceptable by the legislature or regulator; and • they may need to alter their AI system ex post facto, alter their associated practices of production or even roll back or remove the reliance on AI30 to protect Australians and Australian society.   29 AI Act (EU) (n 16). 30 See, eg, s 17 in Canada’s Artificial Intelligence and Data Bill 2022 (Canada) contained in Bill C-27 An Act to Enact the Consumer Privacy Protection Act, the Personal Information and Data Protection Tribunal Act and the Artificial Intelligence and Data Act and to Make Consequential and Related Amendments to Other Acts 2021-22 (Canada). Submissions: AI Proposal Paper Author: Jane Hogan Date: 4 October 2024  
 18 Question 6. Should mandatory guardrails apply to all GPAI models?  Yes. These guardrails should also include not just guardrails associated with the production and management of such AI systems but should include requirements for accuracy on matters for which there is an objective measure of accuracy and which are likely to affect humans. This includes accuracy in statements of fact, and accuracy as to, for example recommendations, advice and predictions.  Question 8 Do the proposed mandatory guardrails appropriately mitigate the risks of AI used in high-risk settings? Are there any guardrails that we should add or remove?  No. The proposed mandatory guardrails are predominantly procedural and could easily be satisfied by an internal compliance system, which may not necessarily produce the kinds of AI systems that ought to be accepted within Australia. The guardrails also fail to set the standard for appropriate AI to clearly signal to the regulatee just what is acceptable AI. There is also a risk that Guardrail 5, enabling human control, will simply create a potential human scapegoat for defective AI that is a product of a collaborative effort. It also assumes that humans are not subject to the well-known problems of AI bias and automation complacency. Guardrail 6, informing users about only the use of AI in decision-making or the generation of content, is too limited. Users do not need merely knowledge about the use of AI. They need power – including the power to opt-out. There is also a need to ensure that automated decision-making is only used in appropriate contexts. For example, although it may be efficient, it may not be appropriate to use to determine, for example, the care plan for the elderly. Guardrail 7 is useful but does not address the likely inherent power imbalance between AI users and AI systems.  To remedy these deficiencies, the Guardrails should include: 1. an Australian digital rights charter that protects Australians and Australian society and clarifies for designers, developers and deployers what are the design criteria for acceptable AI;  2. a requirement for designers, developers and deployers of AI to not only be ‘AI literate’,31 but to understand their legal obligations under Australian law, including the Australian digital rights   31 AI Act (EU) (n 16) (art 3 (definition of ‘AI literacy’, para 56), art 4). Submissions: AI Proposal Paper Author: Jane Hogan Date: 4 October 2024  
 19 charter and what constitutes ethical AI according to Australian standards. In other words, ethical awareness – or ‘ethical and legal literacy’ - is required in addition to technical competence and legal compliance; 3. a requirement for AI organisations that employ designers, developers and deployers to create an ethical infrastructure that ensures that everyone with an impact on the design, development or deployment of the AI system is aware of the Australian legal and ethical obligations in relation to AI, including the Australian digital rights charter. This should be accompanied by an appropriate whistleblower scheme to ensure that problems with AI can be identified early and prevented;  4. a requirement to notify the AI regulator of the use of AI in Australia;  5. mechanisms to ensure that any AI organisation that designs, develops or deploys to be used in Australia has a presence in Australia and is answerable, in a meaningful way, to Australian regulators and courts;  6. an AI compliance officer responsible for the AI system as a whole and for liaising with the regulator, alerting them to problems where necessary - with penalties for both the entity and individual for any failures;  7. a right for Australians to be informed about any use of AI, including AI enabled decisions;  8. a right to opt-out of AI enabled decision-making;  9. a requirement that where AI enabled decision-making is employed, the onus of proof is reversed if a person impacted by an AI system challenges its use or outcome. This should be seen as an acceptable trade-off as part of the new AI regulatory bargain for those advocating the use of opaque systems to make decisions affecting humans. That is, if AI technologists advocate AI’s use, they should bear the risks associated with its use and should therefore bear the onus of proving that its output is accurate and non-discriminatory;  10. exposure to appropriate remedies for harm caused by AI’s use, enforceable not only by an appropriate regulator but by individuals and groups;  11. a requirement for those participating in the AI supply chain to hold appropriate insurance to cover potential harms;  Submissions: AI Proposal Paper Author: Jane Hogan Date: 4 October 2024  
 20 12. a requirement for those participating in the AI supply chain to contribute to an appropriate fund whose purpose is to more evenly spread the benefits of AI by assisting those individuals, groups or communities adversely affected by its use; and  13. a power for the statutory regulator to order the AI systems to be withdrawn or rollbacked to a previous, safer version.32  Question 10. Do the proposed mandatory guardrails distribute responsibility across the AI supply chain and throughout the AI lifecycle appropriately? For example, are the requirements assigned to developers and deployers appropriate?  It is not clear from the AI Proposal how the responsibilities are assigned. It is also not clear from the paper what penalties might apply for any breaches of the responsibilities or whether individuals or groups will have access to appropriate remedies.  Question 11. Are the proposed mandatory guardrails sufficient to address the risks of GPAI? How could we adapt the guardrails for different GPAI models, for example low-risk and high-risk GPAI models?  No. There is nothing in the guardrails that requires GPAI to be accurate when it is asked questions of fact or that have a degree of objective truth to them. While the whole society bears the risks associated with the use of GPAI, there is also nothing in the guardrails that ensures that society will reap the benefits. Nor is there any obligation on those who benefit from GPAI to assist individuals and industries to adapt to the new environment through, for example, unemployment schemes or improved fraud detection. Further, in the current state of knowledge in relation to GPAI, there are no low-risk GPAI models. This is because it is only once we understand what they will be used for, how they will be monetised and how they impact human behaviour that GPAI can be classified as low risk – assuming that evidence ultimately suggests it is appropriate to do so. Accordingly, different guardrails are not yet required for low-risk GPAI.   32 See, eg, s 17 in Canada’s Artificial Intelligence and Data Bill 2022 (Canada) contained in Bill C-27 An Act to Enact the Consumer Privacy Protection Act, the Personal Information and Data Protection Tribunal Act and the Artificial Intelligence and Data Act and to Make Consequential and Related Amendments to Other Acts 2021-22 (Canada).  Submissions: AI Proposal Paper Author: Jane Hogan Date: 4 October 2024  
 21 Question 12. Do you have suggestions for reducing the regulatory burden on small-to-medium sized businesses applying guardrails?  No. Significant and influential AI systems could be designed, delivered and deployed by a small to medium business. X, formerly known as Twitter, was, after all, created by what, on one measure, was a small business at the time of its launch. Given this, and the importance in a precautionary approach of designing AI systems appropriately before they become entrenched in society, at this stage, there should be no exemptions for small-to-medium businesses.  Question 13. Which legislative option do you feel will best address the use of AI in high-risk settings? What opportunities should the Government take into account in considering each approach?  This submission supports a whole of economy approach – which this submission suggests should be re-conceptualised as a ‘whole of society’ approach given that the economy is but one part of society - supported by a robust and proactive AI regulator. AI poses a new problem, is likely to have far reaching and transformational consequences, and requires, therefore, both its own legislation and a new AI regulator to oversee it. This approach will also provide greater clarity and certainty than the diffuseness of a domain specific approach.  While this submission recommends a whole of society (ie economy) approach, it also recommends that the domain specific legislation be reviewed at both federal and state levels to ensure that the regulatory strategies adopted in this other legislation are still appropriate to apply to the diffuse production environment associated with the AI lifecycle. This may not be the case where, for example: • the legislation relies on a licensing scheme to license human agents or advisers (lawyers, financial advisers, real estate agents) and the activities of those human agents are eventually replaced by AI. In these cases, there may be a regulatory gap if the existing legislation does not apply to the corresponding services provided by AI; or  • the legislation allocates responsibility to a specific human, such as a human driver. In this case, there may be a misallocation of responsibility once some tasks are performed by AI. Submissions: AI Proposal Paper Author: Jane Hogan Date: 4 October 2024  
 22 Question 15. Which regulatory option/s will best ensure that guardrails for high-risk AI can adapt and respond to step-changes in technology?  This will best be achieved by a whole of society (ie economy) approach which: 1. adopts a flexible definition of high-risk AI that presumptively treats any step-changes in technology as a form of high-risk AI;  2. creates a proactive AI regulator who can scan the AI landscape and respond as required; and  3. obliges designers, developers and deployers of AI systems to engage with the AI regulator in the manner outlined above.  Question 16: Where do you see the greatest risks of gaps or inconsistencies with Australia’s existing law for the development and deployment of AI? Which regulatory option best addresses this, and why?  The greatest risks are: 1. a lack of a clearly articulated set of values that protects Australians and Australian society and also provides clarity for designers, developers and deployers of AI as to what constitutes acceptable experimentation on individuals and society through the introduction of new technology;  2. a lack of information about an appropriately constituted regulator and the means by which stakeholders (including AI designers, developers and deployers) can engage with the regulator; and  3. an over-emphasis on the possible economic benefits and an under-appreciation of the risks of AI, including the fact that those risks are not yet fully known.  To remedy this, an AI Act should be introduced that includes the features discussed above, including a well-resourced AI regulator. As discussed above,33 the AI Act should also be positioned as a new regulatory bargain between AI technologists and society in which AI technologists will be permitted  33 See [1.1]. Submissions: AI Proposal Paper Author: Jane Hogan Date: 4 October 2024  
 23 to experiment with new forms of AI and their impact on individuals and society more broadly - but only to the extent that they: • do so in a way that respects human dignity, Australian values and Australian society; and  • engage positively, openly and in good faith with the AI regulator and the Australian Government.  ","_2024_Jane_Hogan_AI_Guardrails_submissions_FINAL.edited.685f6575","_2024_Jane_Hogan_AI_Guardrails_submissions_FINAL.edited.685f6575.pdf","pdf","C:\\Users\\james.barro\\Documents\\rsm-nvidia-hackathon\\data\\Submission_Docs\\Processed\\_2024_Jane_Hogan_AI_Guardrails_submissions_FINAL.edited.685f6575.pdf","1"
"165","166","166","Jake Ushida","https://consult.industry.gov.au/ai-mandatory-guardrails/submission/view/133","To the Department of Industry, Science and Resources,  I am an international student at the University of Melbourne. The development of Artiﬁcial Intelligence (AI) is a subject that deeply interests me, as the stakes involved are extremely high. AI has the capacity to bring about positive outcomes including the acceleration of medical research, potentially leading to longer, more fulﬁlling lives. However, the catastrophic consequences from AI, including possible human extinction, cannot be overlooked. Hence, I believe that ensuring the safe development of AI should be one of our highest priorities.  1. Do the proposed principles adequately capture high-risk AI? No While the proposed principles oKer a practical starting point, they fall short in addressing the regulation of high-risk AI systems. One potential approach is to establish a regulatory framework that classiﬁes AI systems as high-risk through technical assessments conducted at multiple stages—prior to training, before deployment, and during operation. This is crucial because AI systems can exhibit unexpected or unintended behaviors, and without thorough evaluation, these issues may go undetected until after deployment. The principle should therefore include a means to assess actual capabilities and behaviours. This approach would also reduce regulatory burden on AI systems proven not to possess dangerous capabilities.  4. Are there high-risk use cases that Government should consider banning in its regulatory response (for example, where there is an unacceptable level of risk)? Yes The government should consider banning AI systems when technical evaluations reveal the potential for critical harm or unacceptable systemic risks. For example, AI models that could facilitate the development of cyber, chemical, biological, radiological, or nuclear weapons, or those capable of self-replication, deception, and loss of control, should be urgently prohibited. This approach aligns with California’s SB1047 bill and the EU AI Act, both of which advocate for banning AI systems posing critical harm or systemic risks. Stricter legislation needs to establish an intolerable risk threshold.  5. Are the proposed principles ﬂexible enough to capture new and emerging forms of high-risk AI, such as general-purpose AI (GPAI)? No The proposed principles lack the ﬂexibility required to capture new and emerging forms of high-risk AI, such as GPAI. To future-proof its regulations, Australia should adopt the EU AI Act’s deﬁnitions of GPAI and GPAI with systemic risks. These deﬁnitions would enable Australia to prioritize and target the regulation of systems with particularly dangerous capabilities.  6. Should mandatory guardrails apply to all GPAI models? No Meaningful obligations should only be imposed on GPAI models that present systemic risks. Guardrails that are proportional to the risks posed by the AI system would strike a balance between safety and innovation. The RAND report on GPAI models suggests that harmonizing reporting and evaluation standards across the USA, UK, and EU could address the unique risks posed by future models. Australia should follow this approach and enforce mandatory guardrails on GPAI with systemic risks, including AI evaluation standards, risk management practices, and incident mitigations.   7. What are suitable indicators for deﬁning GPAI models as high-risk?  Base on technical capability / other Instead of simply classifying all GPAI as high-risk, the law should use a combination of technical evaluation and expert advice to deﬁne whether a GPAI model carries signiﬁcant risk. The EU AI Act Article 51 provides a good example of this approach. We need ﬂexible, future-proof deﬁnitions that can be updated in response to evolving technological developments, such as advancements in algorithms or hardware eKiciency.   8. Do the proposed mandatory guardrails appropriately mitigate the risks of AI used in high-risk settings? No The proposed guardrails fail to account for the varying magnitude of AI risks. AI presents a unique regulatory challenge, as it has the capacity to cause catastrophic harm, yet also has the potential to become ubiquitous. Applying a single set of guardrails to all AI systems in high-risk settings will inevitably lead to both over-regulation and under-regulation. What is needed are tailored guardrails that are proportional to the speciﬁc risks each system presents.  10. Do the proposed mandatory guardrails distribute responsibility across the AI supply chain and throughout the AI lifecycle appropriately? No The current guardrails place insuKicient obligations upon AI developers, who in fact control the majority of the risks. Both the MIT AI Risk Repository and the U.S. National Institute of Standards and Technology (NIST) highlight that decisions made during model development are crucial for ensuring safe and responsible AI. Therefore, mandatory guardrails should focus more on obligations speciﬁc to developers of AI products intended for deployment in Australia.  11. Are the proposed mandatory guardrails suPicient to address the risks of GPAI? No The proposed guardrails fail to adequately address the risks associated with the development of the most advanced AI models. Australia needs a clear deﬁnition to identify AI models that could exhibit dangerous behaviors, potentially leading to widespread or catastrophic harm. Once we have a way of deﬁning these speciﬁc models, we can implement targeted guardrails to mitigate their risks during development.  13. Which legislative option do you feel will best address the use of AI in high-risk settings?  A whole of economy approach - introducing a new cross-economy AI Act  An AI-speciﬁc Act targeting oKshore developers would be the most eKective way to inﬂuence the behavior of overseas AI developers. Established regulators, such as the Therapeutic Goods Administration or the Civil Aviation Safety Authority, should lead within their ﬁelds of expertise. This would allow an Australian AI Act to cover any regulatory gaps and focus on shaping the behaviour of oKshore AI developers.  Thank you for considering my submission. I look forward to seeing progress in this important area of public policy.  Sincerely, Jake ","Submission_on_Mandatory_AI_Guardrails_Jake_Ushida.7374c879","Submission_on_Mandatory_AI_Guardrails_Jake_Ushida.7374c879.pdf","pdf","C:\\Users\\james.barro\\Documents\\rsm-nvidia-hackathon\\data\\Submission_Docs\\Processed\\Submission_on_Mandatory_AI_Guardrails_Jake_Ushida.7374c879.pdf","1"
