www.auda.org.au  •  general@auda.org.au  •  T +613 8341 4111  •  F +613 8341 4122  •  ABN 38 079 009 340  
 
03 October 2024  
 
Department of I ndustry, Science and Resources (DISR) 
Submitted via webform  
 
 
To whom it may concern:  
Subject:  Submission in response to the Proposal Paper for i ntroducing mandatory 
guardrails for AI in high -risk settings   
The .au Domain Administration Limited ( auDA ) is the trusted administrator of the .au 
country code Top Level Domain (ccTLD). The .au ccTLD is part of Australia’s critical infrastructure, supporting more than 4.2 million .au domain names. auDA is endorsed by the Commonwealth Government to administer the .au for the benefit of all Australians  
under its Terms of Endorsement.  
As a critical part of the digital economy, auDA’s role is to ensure the .au ccTLD remains stable, reliable and secure. Additionally, auDA performs the following functions:  
• Administers a licensing regime for .au domain names based in multi -
stakeholder processes, including managing enquiries and maintaining an appropriate compliance and dispute resolution processes associated with the 
licensing rules  
• Licenses the .au registry operator and accredits and license registrars  
• Advocates for, and actively participates in, multi -stakeholder internet 
governance processes both domestically and internationally.  
In performing its functions, auDA operates under a multi -stakeholder model, working 
closely with suppliers, business users, industry, civil society, consumers and the 
Australian Government.  
auDA advocates for regulation to be shaped to most effectively support the digital 
wellbeing of Australians . We  refer you to our 2023 submission to the Department on  
Safe and responsible AI in Australia, wh ere we  called for an inclusive, multi -stakeholder 
approach to AI regulation.  
Our 2023 submission also noted the need for enhanced support and awareness raising 
for Australians  using AI, a point that auDA’s  independent research highlights as a 
growing concern for Australia.   
 
.au Domain Administration Ltd  2 
We welcome th is opportunity to share with you relevant key findings from our research . 
auDA’s Digital Lives of Australians 2024  research report  found that 61  per cent  of 
consumers and 67  per cent  of small businesses would feel more comfortable using AI if 
there were stronger regulatory frameworks in place. While familiarity with AI assistants 
and generative AI platforms is increasing, concerns remain about the legal ownership 
and security of AI -generated content.  
Our Digital Lives of Australians  research  is an annual  project to understand consumer 
and small business perspectives on emerging digital technologies , informed by a 
survey of  1,500 Australian consumers and 400 small businesses.  
Other key findings from the report include:  
• 48 per cent  of consumers and 53  per cent  of small businesses would like to learn 
more about AI and how it could benefit them  
• 39 per cent of working age Australians believe being able to use AI tools will 
increase their employment prospects  
• 48 per cent of small businesses believe being able to use AI tools will increase 
business efficiency.  
These findings underscore the need for a balanced regulatory approach that promotes 
innovation while addressing community concerns.   
We reiterate the value and importance of incorporating a multi -stakeholder approach 
to any regulatory initiatives . This includes ensuring there is  comprehensive consultation 
with the broad range of stakeholders impacted by these decisions  and an adequate 
timeframe for consideration and response.   
If you would like to discuss our submission or any of the auDA research initiatives above, 
please contact auDA’s Internet Governance and Policy Director, Jordan Carter, at jordan.carter@auda.org.au.  
Yours sincerely  
 
 
Rosemary Sinclair AM 
CEO, auDA  
