Dear Minister  
Proposals paper for introducing mandatory guardrails for AI in high -risk settings  
Chartered Accountants Australia and New Zealand (CA ANZ) welcome s the opportunity to 
review and provide feedback on the government’s proposal to introduce mandatory 
guardrails for AI in high -risk settings. More about CA ANZ can be found at Appendix B. 
We note  that the proposed principles and mandatory guidelines aim to work to  ‘strengthen 
and clarify existing legislation’ and to ‘ensure consistency and coherence .’ However, we find 
them lacking  in clarity  which could make them difficult to apply consisten tly across entities 
and use cases .  
Our primary concern s are  the subjective nature of assessing if an AI system is high-risk, that 
the guardrails are not readily enforceable  on key actors  being  developers and deployers,  and 
the different ways developer, deployer and end user may be interpreted by parties across  the 
AI ecosystem. This became evident during our discussions with members of CA ANZ and 
interested stakeholders , which revealed the different ways people  had interpreted the  
principles in these areas.  
In Appendix A , we provide more detailed feedback on the following key concerns:  
• The subjective nature of designating an AI system as high -risk;  
• How the guardrails will be enforced for offshore developers and deployers ; 
• When an end user, who purchases a n AI system  may be  categorised as a deployer;  
• In practice, how will regulators know if  all the guardrails have been implemented across 
the supply -chain of high -risk AI systems ; and  
• Guardrail 7: The proposed complaints process does not appear to require reaching a 
resolution or providing compensation.  
