  Page  2 
On consideration of the regulatory options presented in the proposals paper, we support 
Option 2 - A framework approach. Such an approach provides direct access to the definitions 
and guardrail principles rather than having to refer to multiple pieces of l egislation to 
understand your obligations, a consequence of option 1, and minimises conflicts across 
existing legislation, a consequence of option 3.  
We recognised that to activate a framework for the AI guardrails will require amendments to 
existing regulatory frameworks. We consider the cost and time to affect  these amendments is 
more than outweigh ed by the benefit of being able to rely on existing regulators and their 
penalties , which are well known to businesses and consumers.  
We recommend that implementing mandatory guardrails be considered at least 12 months 
after the release of the voluntary standard. This will allow the government to receive 
feedback on the impact of the voluntary standard , who has and ha s not implemented it and 
why, how it has been deployed and the cost, and the  impacts it has had on consumer safety 
when they purchase or utilise products and services based on AI. Further , the additional time 
will provide an opportunity to assess  the progress  of AI and  if reg ulation of AI in other 
jurisdictions has improved  protections for consumers and harness lesson s learned along the 
way. In the interim, the government could progress with implementing bans on uses of AI , 
which are considered unacceptable around the world  and not already prevented by existing 
legislation in Australia.  
Overall, we support mandatory guardr ails for AI in h igh-risk settings after assessing how 
businesses have implemented , and the impact on end users  of, the voluntary standard. At 
that time, to develop an AI framework that provides the definitions  of principles  and risk-
based g uardrails and activate the provision s with amend ments to  existing legislation . 
We would be pleased to discuss our feedback in greater detail and  the potential impact on 
our members. I f of interest, please contact Karen McWilliams on (02)  8078 5451  or at 
karen .mcwilliams@charteredaccountantsanz.com.  
 
Sincerely,  
 
 
