  Page  2 
Further guidance is needed to clarify the elements to be considered in assessing the risk of 
an AI system  such as illustrative use cases  of both high -risk AI systems and a low -risk AI 
systems . Guidance could also reflect  the evidence that will be expected of deployers of AI 
systems to prove to a regulator, or regulators, how they have assessed their AI system 
against the proposed principles  and conclude d the risk is low or high . 
If this is to be mandated, we are concerned that the lack of clarity will lead to in consistent 
interpretation making  it difficult  to apply in practice . A clear definition of high -risk will be k ey 
to the effective ness of  mandatory guardrails . 
We also note that the proposed principles are intended to designate the AI system as high 
(or low) risk due to its use. However, we have heard through feedback that AI systems can 
have different use cases. We encourage consideration of whether it is the AI  system or the 
use case of that AI system that should be designated high risk.  
Additionally, w e are concerned that the proposed principles appear to be creating a human 
rights framework as there is not currently a human rights charter in  Australia.  While the lack 
of such a charter is a gap in Australia’s law, regulation of high -risk AI systems should focus 
on preventing and rectifying harm caused by these system s, not broader human rights.  
How the guardrails will be enforced against offshore developers and 
deployers  
We are greatly concerned with the intent to distribute accountability and legal responsibility to 
developers and deployers when the proposals paper recognises that the ‘most highly 
capable GPAI models are not currently developed domestically’.  
For example, Guardrail 1, accountability process. It is unclear how Australia will be able to 
enforce an offshore developer to make publicly available details of their accountability 
process including the roles, responsibilities and reporting structures fo r staff. Similarly, 
Guardrail 8, to be transparent. This requires developers to give deployers information 
including the data sources used for training and, if not enforceable, all developers and 
deployers downstream will be unable to address risks thereby  unable to appropriately apply 
the applicable guardrails to their product or service.  
Guardrail 8 also requires deployers to report adverse incidents to developers who can the n 
issue improvements to the model. We would question how much influence a deployer in 
Australia would have on instigating improvements to a model developed offshore. This would 
suggest that a complaints process as proposed by Guardrail 7 will become a simpl e record of 
adverse incidents rather than a process that can rectify harm caused or prevent harm in the 
future.  
 
