  Page  3 
When an end user, who purchases a product based on AI, may be  
categorised as a deployer  
We have reached out to our members and other stakeholders for their feedback on what may 
be considered high -risk in the field of accounting. For example, practice management 
software will use AI in various ways. From our perspective, the provider  of the software 
would be considered a ‘deployer’ and our members that use this software ‘end users’.  To 
others, using such software to provide a service , say taxation services, means the 
accountant would be considered  a deployer.  
In our discussions , we noted the order in which the risk of the use of an AI system  is 
assessed  and whether an entity is  an end user o r deployer  is important for the application of 
the guardrails . As outline d earlier , if an accounting practice uses a system incorporating AI to 
help populate a tax return  and consider s it is not  high-risk, it is not relevant whether they are 
considered a deployer or end user. Conversely, if first an entity determines itself to be an end 
user, then they may not even assess the risk of any AI systems  they use. 
It appears there  would be a need for all entities to undertake a  specific risk assessment of 
each use case of AI first , before determining  their role in that use case , if it is considered high 
risk. It will be important to consider the practical application of this to all entities, including 
smaller entities or those who are not aware of how AI is incorporated into systems they use.  
We also seek consideration of bringing these elements together in the proposed definition of 
‘deployer’ by including the term high risk. For example, Any individual or organisation  that 
supplies or uses a high -risk AI system…..  
In practice, how will regulators know if all the guardrails have been 
implemented across the supply -chain of high -risk AI systems  
We note on page 32 that it is likely that ‘ multiple organisations will be responsible for carrying 
out different aspects of the guardrails…’.  This appears reasonable when considering the 
detailed explanation of each guardrail.  However, we are unclear how the application of all 
guardrails to a specific high risk AI system  could be enforced  by a regulator when multiple 
organisations may be responsible for different aspects .  
Furthe r, it is unclear  how the developer or deployer is to determine which guardrails have 
been carried out by other parties  to ensure all guardrails have been addressed. For example, 
Guardrail 2, risk management process. While on the surface it may appear this should be 
applied by all developers and deployers, the requirements may be beyond deployers  
downstream . The expectation of involving experts from multiple domains , representatives 
from impacted communities and workers in the process may be beyond the  resources of 
some deployers.  
