  Page  4 
This raises the same concerns we expressed during consultations on developing the 70-
page  Voluntary AI Safety Standard  (Standard) . We raised the need to explain technical 
language in plain terms and include  practical guidance on how a small business can 
implement the voluntary guidelines. While the ‘Guardrails on a page’ summaris es the 
guardrails well, what is expected to implement each is  unrealistic  for small business, the 
equivalent of small deployers in the proposed mandatory guardrails . 
For example, guardrail 2, establishing a risk management system. It suggest s starting with a 
stakeholder impact assessment. Guardrail 8, to be transparent. We do not think a developer  
of an AI system and the tools based on that system  will share with  a small business how it 
built the AI system or the data, models and system used.   
It is therefore important that application of the guardrails is scalable with relevance to the 
severity and extent of potential impacts  attributable to the developer and the distribution by a  
deployer . Equally, that a government body provide education and  templates on how to apply 
the guardrails  and offer targeted training  to upskill deployers of high -risk AI systems  to 
identify  and document risks and to  manage and maintain the guardrails so they can  
demonstrate compliance . 
 
Guardrail 7: The proposed complaints process does not appear to 
require reaching a resolution or providing compensation.  
It is unclear from the detail  provided how contesting an AI -enabled decision or complaining 
about their experience o r treatment will rectify any harm caused to a consumer . This is 
particularly  relevant where the consumer  complains to a deployer about  their experience 
using a  high-risk AI system  yet the algorithm that generated that experience was set by the 
developer of the system.  
As framed, such a complaints process  simply adds to business ’s compliance burden without 
providing any real benefit for end users.  
We support the need for a complaints process, particularly  in regard to AI -enabled decisions.  
However, given the end user may not even be aware that the decision was AI -enabled and to 
minimise compliance burden on business, we suggest the complaints process could form 
part of  the entity ’s existing complaints process. Within this process, the entity  would be able 
to identify that the decision was made by AI. Further, s uch a process should require an 
independent body to adjudicate the complaint with the power to reverse a decision if it is 
found the decision was based on inaccuracies  or biases.  
 
 
