CAG  Response  – October 2024   1 CAG Response  to the ‘Introducing mandatory 
guardrails for AI in high -risk settings ’ proposals 
paper   
 
This response  is made by the Schools and TAFE Copyright Advisory Groups ( CAG ). Further 
information about CAG and its activities is set out in Attachment A  to this response.   
CAG welcomes  the opportunity to comment on the ‘Introducing mandatory guardrails for AI in 
high-risk settings’ proposals paper (the Proposals Paper ), and thank s the Department of 
Industry, Science and Resources (the Department ) for its consideration of our response .  
Executive Summary  
In this response, CAG addresses four key copyright issues for education that arise in connection 
with the  Department’s proposed approach to regulating high -risk AI systems, particularly the 
mandatory guardrails (the Guardrails ). To assist the Department, CAG responds to relevant 
consultation questions in Attachment B . 
Overall, CAG recognises the need for safety, accountability and transparency obligations to be 
imposed on certain developers and deployers of AI systems  and supports the Department’s intent 
to introduce regulation to respond to high -risk applications of AI .  
However, CAG considers that there are four key issues  with the approach outlined in the 
Proposals Paper  that must be addressed in conjunction with developing regulation for high -risk 
AI systems : 
1. AI regulation  and mandatory transparency guardrails must be supported  by 
corresponding public -interest exceptions under Australian copyright law .  
The Department ’s proposed approach reflects AI regulations in the European Union AI Act  
(EU AI Act ) and Canada’s proposed amended AI and Data Act  (AIDA ), which w ere 
introduced in context s where copyright law s permit  some  AI activities under robust public 
interest exception s. Australia does not  have the same c ontext : our copyright framework does 
not include equivalent exceptions  to those that exist in the EU and Canada , to the detriment 
of researchers and educational institutions wanting to develop AI tools or teach AI skills for 
the benefit of Australian students . 
CAG urges the Department to adopt a holistic approach that carefully considers the copyright 
implications of any regulatory response to high -risk AI : transparency obligations make sense 
as long as there are copyright exceptions in place that provide legal clarity about beneficial 
uses of AI, including in education. If the Department  proposes to implement AI regulations 
that mirror those in the EU and Canada, then Australia’s copyright laws must also contain 
equivalent exceptions .  
CAG  Response  – October 2024   2 2. AI regulations  should reflect  a nuanced approach to risk  in the educational context .  
What is considered a  “high -risk” AI  system should not capture everyday educational 
applications of AI systems that are low-risk, such as the beneficial educational use of AI as a 
‘study bu ddy’ as recommended by a recent House Standing Committee . CAG encourages 
the Department to adopt an appropriate and nuanced definition of high -risk AI that avoid s all 
educational applications of AI being automatically categorised as high -risk with 
corresponding mandatory guardrails applied. Any approach to AI regulation should focus on 
developers of general purpose AI ( GPAI ) model s, which  are sophisticated, commercial 
technology companies like Microsoft, OpenAI and Google , rather than schools , TAFEs , 
Departments of Education or administering bodies , with limited r esources , who are deploying 
AI in an educational context .   
3. Transparency obligations should also apply to copyright collecting societies which 
administer licensing regimes allowing certain uses of copyright works in connection 
with AI.  
There are two aspects to transparency obligations for AI: firstly, transparency in connection 
with disclosure of training datasets, and  secondly,  transparency as to the management of 
any licensing schemes that may apply  to, or be developed for, the use of copyright works to 
train AI. Transparency is required to ensure that  licence fees paid by the education sector 
and governments out of public funds are being distributed appropriately , particularly when 
much AI training occurs in relation to public domain materi als, or on content for which there 
is no easily identifiable copyright owner . Where developers and deployers of AI systems are 
required to disclose what works were used to train AI systems, it is appropriate for 
transparency obligations to apply to copyright collecting societies , which administer voluntary 
collective or statutory licensing regimes covering uses of copyright works in connection with 
AI.  
4. Where appropriate, the Guardrails should encourage organisations to comply with 
ICIP policies and protocols in connection with AI.  
The Guardrails  do not currently require organisations to comply with ICIP policies and 
protocols that they have in place when developing or deploying AI systems. An effective way 
of improving protections for ICIP in the AI context would be to : 
(i) require developers and deployers to have ICIP protocols in place and comply with 
those protocols in connection with high -risk AI  
(ii) encourage developers and deployers to comply with ICIP protocols in connection with 
other applications of AI.  
CAG’s position in respect of each issue is addressed in further detail below.     
CAG  Response  – October 2024   3 1. AI regulation and mandatory transparency guardrails must  be supported 
by public -interest exceptions  in the Copyright Act  
Background  
The Department proposes to implement Guardrails that mirror  AI regulations in the EU AI Act and 
Canada’s AIDA , including transparency obligations in the Guardrails that are modelled on the EU 
AI Act as summarised in the flowcharts in Attachment C. In particular, g uardrail 3 is modelled on 
Article 53  of the EU AI Act and requires developers and deployers to identify “ the origin and 
legality of the dataset and collection processes ”.1 Guardrail 3 is intended to complement 
requirements under the Copyright Act.2 
While CAG welcomes the Department’s recognition that the Guardrails and Copyright Act should 
complement each other, it is important to recogni se that the guardrails in the EU were developed 
in a vastly different copyright context to that in Australia, where certain activities in relation to text 
and data mining  and AI are permitted under European copyright law.  
Copyright exceptions are an important part of the broader legal framework underpinning the 
approaches to AI regulation being taken in overseas jurisdictions including the EU and Canada. 
Other ju risdictions have  copyright frameworks with robust and flexible education exceptions 
permitting non -commercial uses of copyright works in connection with digital technologies 
including AI , as set out in Attachment D . Relevantly : 
• the EU has a TDM exception  and other broad education  exceptions3  
• Canada has a fair dealing for education exception4 
• Singapore has a TDM exception  and fair use5 
• the United Kingdom has a TDM exception and fair dealing for education exceptions.6   
However, Australia’s Copyright Act 1968 (Cth) (the Copyright Act ) does not  include equivalent 
public -interest exceptions that would permit certain non -commercial, educational use of copyright 
works. This has long been recognised as a problem that should be remedied by both the 
Australian Law Reform Commission and the Productivity Commission.7 Supporting appropriate 
use of copyright materials in the public interest, including education,  is enshrined in our 
 
1 Guardrails proposals paper, p 37.  
2 Ibid, p 38.  
3 Directive (EU) 2019/790 (Directive on Copyright in the Digital Single Market), Preamble at 20 -21 and 
Article 4: see https://eur -lex.europa.eu/eli/dir/2019/790/oj  
4 See s 29, Copyright Act (R.S.C., 1985, c. C -42). 
5 Singapore has an expanded exception in the Singapore Copyright Act that allows for text and data 
mining (the ‘Computational Data Analysis Exception’).  Fair use is set out in  s 190 , Singapore Copyright 
Act 2021.  
6 The UK has a text and data mining exception, which allows copies of copyright works to be made for the 
purpose of non -commercial research : see s 29A  UK Copyright, Designs and Patents Act 1988 . Fair 
dealing for education exceptions are set out in ss 32 -36A. 
7 For example, in 2014 the Australian Law Reform Commission Copyright and the Digital Economy  report 
recommended the introduction of fair use, with a ‘fallback’ recommendation of additional fair dealing 
exceptions, including an exception for fair dealing for education , and in 2016 the Productivity 
Commission’s Inquiry into IP Arrangements  recommended the introduction of fair use.   
CAG  Response  – October 2024   4 international obligations including the WIPO Copyright Treaty  (applicable to digital uses of 
copyright works) .8 
Australian educational institutions need public interest exceptions that apply to certain educational 
uses of AI to support fulfilment of the recommendations made  by the House of Representatives 
Standing Committee  on Employment, Education and Training in its recent Study Buddy Report . 
For example:  
• state and territory Departments of Education and administering bodies in the Catholic and 
independent sectors who are developing or deploying AI systems in education, such as 
NSWEduChat or the SA Department for Education’s pilot generative AI system (initi atives 
that were referred to in the Study Buddy Report)9 
• schools, TAFEs and their educators who are teaching students to engage with AI on the 
ground, including as deployers of bespoke or off -the-shelf AI systems or developers of their 
own AI systems in future as part of education curriculum and practice (as env isaged by the 
Study Buddy Report).10 
Australia’s copyright framework needs to be updated so that it is fit -for-purpose for the AI -driven 
future of education. The Copyright Act must reflect the well -recognised public interest in Australian 
schools and TAFEs being able to legally undertake cert ain activities in connection with AI, without 
being required to  pay licence fees or face legal exposure for non -harmful uses of copyright works 
that cause no prejudice to copyright owners.  
CAG is not suggesting  that every educational application of AI will, or should be, free. Instead, 
CAG envisages that Australia’s copyright framework  as it applies to  AI will ultimately comprise :  
• public interest exceptions to copyright infringement permitting certain uses of copyright works 
without a licence in limited public interest circumstances  
• collective licensing  schemes, where public funds are paid to collecting societies  and 
distributed to rightsholders to compensate  them for uses of their works  that have an impact 
on the market for their works  
• direct licensing between  rightsholders and developers of AI systems permitting  uses of 
copyright works in connection with AI , which is already occurring in commercial contexts .11 
Why is this an issue?  
Under the proposed Guardrails, Australian educational institutions who develop or deploy  high-
risk AI systems including GPAI models would be required to comply with transparency obligations 
that mirror those that apply in the EU and Canada, but they cannot rely on copyright exceptions 
 
8 See https://www.wipo.int/wipolex/en/text/295166 .  
9 ‘Study Buddy or Influencer’ : report by the House of Representatives Standing Committee  on 
Employment, Education and Training in response to the Inquiry into the Use of Generative Artificial 
Intelligence in the Australian Education System, at 2.21, Box 4.1.  
10 Ibid at 2.21 (noting “ Minister Clare stated that Australia is ‘entering an age where AI has got to be part 
of education’ ”), and at 2.31-33, 2.85, 2.90.  
11 See, for example,  OpenAI’s recent partnership deal  with News Corporation .  
CAG  Response  – October 2024   5 that exist in those jurisdictions. Without reform to Australia’s copyright framework there is 
therefore a real risk that:  
• engaging with well -recognised  opportunities  of educational applications of AI could come with 
significant legal risks for the Australian school and TAFE sectors , because Australian 
educational institutions cannot rely on public -interest exceptions that already exist in other 
jurisdictions. S chools, TAFEs and their educators will continue to lack clarity about how they 
can engage with AI systems in practice from a copyright perspective.  Australia’s copyright 
framework should support, rather than hinder, enga gement with the benefits of AI in 
education.  
• the Australian school and TAFE sectors could be exposed to significant additional costs if 
minimal and non -harmful applications of AI are subject to copyright licensing arrangements 
in Australia, despite these activities being covered by free, public -interest copyright 
exceptions in other jurisdictions. We need to avoid a situation where Australian schools and 
TAFEs are required to use limited, publicly -funded budgets to pay for non -harmful 
educational uses of AI  systems , such as a teacher using AI to gener ate a list of Australia’s 
Prime Ministers or quickly work out the answer to a complex maths question.  
To avoid  these risks, a ny regulatory response to AI must be introduced alongside appropriate 
public interest copyright exceptions covering education that are equivalent to exceptions that 
already exist in jurisdictions  that are regulating AI. This would also support true interoperability 
with AI regulations between Australia, the EU and Canada.  
CAG is very concerned that if AI regulations that apply in the EU or Canada are simply introduced 
in Australia in the absence of reform to Australia’s existing copyright framework, those AI 
regulations will have different impacts  and unintended consequences.   
CAG’s recommendations  
CAG strongly recommends that the Department should only  implement  regulations in the form of 
the EU AI Act if Australia’s copyright framework is amended to include  public interest copyright 
exceptions covering education that mirror those already in place  in the EU  and other jurisdictions.  
CAG encourages the Department to support efforts to introduce copyright exceptions to give the 
education sector the legal clarity required to enable full engagement with the educational 
opportunities of AI.  
CAG recommends that  the Department continue consultation on the issues addressed in the 
Proposals Paper , in line with the approach taken in the EU  prior to the introduction of the EU AI 
Act. Detailed  rounds of consultation were undertaken on the EU AI Act from April 2021 until 
provisional agreement was reached in December 2023.12 CAG submits that a similarly robust  
process should be undertaken in Australia.  
CAG also considers that t he Department should follow a phased implementation process to allow 
an opportunity for further work to be done in respect of copyright law reform and sufficient time 
for educational institutions to comply with any new AI regulations . As summarised in the EU’s AI 
 
12 See EU AI Act Historic Timeline at https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/developments/ .   
CAG  Response  – October 2024   6 Act Implementation Timelines and Next Steps  and the detailed implementation timeline , the EU 
has adopted a phased approach with time allowed for setting up an AI Commission and for the 
introduction delegated acts necessary to put mandatory guardrails into law. A similar approach 
would be appropriate in Australia. Copyright reform and transparency reform should proceed in 
tandem.  
   
CAG  Response  – October 2024   7 2. AI regulations should reflect  a nuanced  approach to risk  in the 
educational context  
Background  
The Department’s proposal  would include the following applications of AI as “high -risk”:  
• AI systems designated as “ high-risk due to [their] use ” by reference to a set of principles or, 
alternatively, particular use cases  
• all GPAI models, which is broadly defined as “ an AI model that is capable of being used, or 
capable of being adapted for use, for a variety of purposes, both for direct use as well as for 
integration in other systems ”.13  
As acknowledged in the Proposals Paper, many “high -risk domains” can include  AI use cases 
that pose limited or minimal risk .14 CAG notes that the current proposed version of Canada’s AIDA 
does not expressly include education a s a high -risk class of use for AI systems, while the EU AI 
Act only lists certain  educational AI use cases as high -risk.  
What applications of AI are considered high -risk will have implications for obligations imposed on 
both developers and deployers under the proposed Guardrails. In particular, a s reflected in the 
flowcharts in Attachment C, the EU AI Act includes a nuanced, tiered approach to transparency 
obligations for different applications of AI  applications. However, t he Department’s proposed 
approach  appear s to impose the most stringent transparency obligations from the EU AI Act on 
both developers and deployers of  any high -risk AI systems.  
Further, CAG understands that the Department’s proposed approach could impose the same 
obligations under the Guardrails on:  
• sophisticated commercial technology organisations responsible for developing and deploying 
large -scale commercial GPAI models and AI systems, such as Microsoft, OpenAI and Google 
and EdTech companies  
• state and territory Departments of Education and administering bodies in the Catholic and 
independent sectors with limited budgets drawn from public funds who are deploying GPAI 
models in specific educational AI applications, such as NSWEduChat , or fine -tuning GPAI 
models  
• schools, TAFEs and their educators engaging  with AI on the ground, for example where a 
Year 12 class is building an AI system that leverages GPT -4 as part of an in -class exercise.  
CAG notes that the Study Buddy Report recommended that the Government “ regulate EdTech 
companies and developers  through a system -wide risks -based legal framework ” and “ require 
EdTech companies and developers  to share critical information about how their AI systems are 
trained, what data it has been trained on, and how algorithms function and affect users ” (see 
recommendation 11  – emphasis added). This recommendation would not be possible under the 
Department’s proposal which could potentially treat all educational uses of AI as high risk.  
 
13 Guardrails proposals paper, p 9.  
14 Guardrails proposals paper, p 26.   
CAG  Response  – October 2024   8 Why is this an issue?  
There are  two main issues relevant to  risk arising out of the Proposals Paper . 
(a) Not all educational applications of AI should be considered high -risk 
Irrespective of whether the Department adopts a principles or list -based approach, CAG considers 
that any attempt  to define  high-risk AI should not be overly prescriptive. CAG favours adopting 
discretionary factors for assessing risk that are supported by guidelines to ensure there is 
sufficient flexibility to reflect a variety of contexts.  
In CAG’s view, the Department’s approach to AI regulation needs to be nuanced enough to 
recognise the lower risk profiles of some educational applications of AI. As noted in the Study 
Buddy Report, it is important to recognise the complexities around risk and AI in the education 
context, and the diverse ways in which AI can be deployed across the sector.  
CAG agrees that some education applications of AI identified in the Proposals Paper are more 
clearly high -risk (eg using a system to determine access to educational institutions) . However, 
other educational applications of AI are not (eg a jurisdiction deploying a GPAI model that allows 
teachers to generate answers to basic questions, similar to the way they would use Google).  
If all educational applications of AI are considered  high-risk, then educational institutions may be 
subject to unnecessary regulatory burdens that could impact their ability to engage  with all of the 
opportunities and benefits of AI, including those recognised in the Study Buddy Report . For 
example, as a ‘co -designer’ to summarise research or concepts for lesson plans, personalising 
lesson plans to suit a student’s level of knowledge, or reducing teacher workload by performing 
“menial tasks, such as sortin g information under headings or in tables .” These low risk and 
beneficial uses of AI should not be classified as high risk simply because they occur in an 
educational context.   
(b) There needs to be clarity  around what obligations apply to  particular developers and 
deployers of high-risk AI  
As a matter of principle, AI regulations  should differentiate between particular developers and 
deployers of high-risk AI systems. CAG welcomes the imposition of appropriate mandatory 
guardrails  on large, commercial developers and deployers of AI systems, such as such as 
Microsoft, OpenAI and Google and EdTech companies.  
Schools and TAFEs need to be able to provide access to, and potentially in future build and 
develop, AI systems and models : for example, to “build students’ skills through project -based 
learning ” as envisaged by the Study Buddy Report (see recommendation 19 ). Educational 
institutions such as schools, TAFEs and administering bodies which are deploying GPAI models 
or developing AI systems for educational purposes should not be subject to equivalent, stringent 
obligations  to those that apply to sophisticated, commercial entities like OpenAI or Google . The 
obligations envisaged by the Guardrails are likely to be particularly burdensome for educational 
institutions with limited resources and school - or TAFE -level technical expertise.   
CAG  Response  – October 2024   9 CAG’s recommendations  
CAG strongly recommends  that any approach to regulating high-risk AI  recognises that : 
• not all educational applications of AI should be  considered “high -risk”. Low-risk educational 
applications of AI  systems  should be  excluded, in recognition of the strong identified benefits 
of those applications and the limited risk of harm occurring. Examples of low -risk applications 
of AI could include using an AI system  to ensure teachers are grading consistently across 
classes, using AI -assisted search to find a specific curriculum outcome, or where a teacher 
asks AI to “generate a list of Australia’s f irst 10 Prime Ministers”  
• the most stringent transparency obligations and risk settings should be targeted to 
sophisticated developers of commercial AI systems, such as  EdTech providers and  
OpenAI /Microsoft /Google. E ducational institutions should otherwise be subject to obligations 
that better reflect the actual risk profiles of the activities they are undertaking with AI systems 
(for example, by differentiating between administering bodies deploying GPAI models as part 
of systems such as NSWEduChat, and schools/TAFEs  deploying or developing AI systems ). 
CAG  also recommends  further consultation with the education sector to give their input and 
support about what educational applications of AI should be considered high-risk. Given the fast -
developing nature of this space, t here should  also be a process for continual assessment of what 
applications of AI are considered high -risk and what mandatory obligations apply to educational 
institutions.     
CAG  Response  – October 2024   10 3. Transparency obligations should also apply to copyright collecting 
societies that administer collective licensing regimes allowing certain  
uses of copyright works in connection  with AI  
Background  
As set out above, the Department’s proposed approach would impose transparency obligations 
on developers and deployers of high -risk AI systems to identify “ the origin and legality of the 
dataset and collection processes ”15 and complement requirements under the Copyright Act.16 
Information disclosed in compliance with those obligations would support rightsholders in seeking 
remuneration for prejudicial uses of their works in connection with AI models.  
Subject to  CAG’s comments above concerning  the necessity of introducing public -interest 
education exceptions in the Copyright Act , some uses of  copyright material in connection with  AI 
that would interfere with a copyright owner’s commercial market may ultimately  be subject to 
remuneration under collective licence schemes. Current examples of collective licence schemes 
that apply to  education include:  
• the Schools Music Licence  and the TAFE Music Licence , which are voluntary collective 
licences under which Australian schools and TAFEs respectively pay music collecting 
societies APRA, AMCOS and ARIA for certain rights to use music  in education  
• the Statutory Broadcast Licence  and the Statutory Text and Artistic Works Licence , which are 
set out in s 113P of the Copyright Act, and under which Australian educational institutions 
pay fees to declared collecting societies Screenrights and Copyright Agency to copy and 
communicate film and TV broadcasts and text and artistic works res pectively for educational 
purposes.  
CAG notes that t he education sector pays millions of dollars of public funds each year to collecting 
societies for the educational use of copyright material. In 2023, the total paid was over $94 million .  
Why is this an issue?  
There are two aspects to transparency obligations for AI: firstly, transparency in connection with 
disclosure of training datasets, and secondly, transparency as to the management of any licensing 
schemes that apply  to the use of copyright works in connection with AI .  
CAG appreciates that the intention behind  the proposed transparency obligations is to permit 
rightsholders, and collecting societies who represent them, to know when their works are used to 
train AI models . Equivalent transparency  obligations  should apply to collecting societies , which 
use that information to remunerate rightsholders in respect of prejudicial uses of their works in 
connection with AI.  This is critical where, as the ALRC has noted, “collecting societies have been 
said to have a ‘de facto monopolistic nature’” .17  
Under Australia’s current framework , the  education sector cannot  obtain information from 
collecting societies on how they distribute licensing fees because collecting  societies are not 
 
15 Guardrails proposals paper, p 37.  
16 Ibid, p 38.  
17 See ALRC Copyright and the Digital Economy Report (ALRC Report 122) , December 2013, at 8.64.   
CAG  Response  – October 2024   11 obliged to disclose that information. For example, limited to no transparency is given about the 
distribution of licence fees where copyright owners cannot be identified, or the rates charged for 
de minimis  and extensive uses of copyright materials respectively.  
CAG has long -standing concerns about the lack of appropriate governance arrangements for 
declared collecting societies, and the practical consequences of these deficiencies. Neither the 
Code of Conduct for Copyright Collecting Societies, nor the existing legislative framework 
applying to collecting societies, provide any enforceable mechanism requiring the collecting 
societies to address the se transparency concerns, which have been echoed by the ALRC and 
the Productivity Commission.18  
CAG wishes to avoid exacerbating this existing problem with Australia’s collective licensing 
system to the extent it might apply to AI  given the large volume of works involved in AI training, 
and the potential corresponding impact on public funds if there is lack of transparency surrounding 
payments under licences for these works . Introducing transparency obligations on collecting 
societies will ensure that public funds collected under voluntary collective and statutory licence 
schemes are being used approp riately and distributed to creators fairly. In addition, this would 
give the Australian school and TAFE sectors confidence that the copyright licence fees paid from 
already limited budgets  represent ‘value for money’ . 
CAG’s recommendations  
CAG encourages the Department to support mandatory transparency obligations on copyright 
collecting societies on distribution of funds collected under statutory licences in connection with 
educational uses of AI.  At a minimum, CAG considers that collecting societies should be required 
to publish information about recipients of distributions or what portion of fees have been 
distributed to particular classes of rightsholders . 
  
 
18 In September 2016, t he Productivity Commission’s Productivity Commission Intellectual Property 
Arrangements Report  noted that “[t]he current code of conduct does little to lessen concerns”  (p 157) and 
it was “ particularly concerned by the submissions of statutory licence users that they are not able to 
access the information needed to allow them to effectively negotiate directly with rights holders ” (p 160).   
CAG  Response  – October 2024   12 4. Where appropriate, t he Guardrails should encourage organisations to 
comply with their ICIP policies and protocols  
Background  
ICIP covers a bundle of perpetual rights that exist independently of statutory IP rights (such as 
copyright). That said, ICIP can be protected by copyright and other types of statutory IP in some 
circumstances (but this is usually quite limited). For examp le, copyright only protects expressions 
that have been reduced to ‘material form’ ie written down or painted – oral histories or spiritual 
knowledge passed down through generations might not meet this  criteria. This is why First 
Nations communities rely on  other mechanisms such as contracts and protocols to protect their 
ICIP.  
Many organisations , including state and territory Departments of Education,  already have ICIP 
policies and protocols in place addressing responsible engagement with ICIP rightsholders and 
use of ICIP.  
The Government, through the Office for the Arts, is separately working on introducing standalone 
legislation for protecting ICIP , in partnership with First Nations stakeholders and a First Nations 
Expert Working Group. The standalone legislation was announced in  2023 as part of  the National 
Cultural Policy  Revive: a place for every story, a story for every place .  
Why is this an issue?  
As set out in the Proposals Paper, AI systems may have been trained on content that is protected 
by ICIP rights and could be used to create outputs that incorporate ICIP without attribution or 
permission from the relevant custodians of that ICIP.19  
The Guardrails do not currently require organisations to comply with ICIP policies and protocols  
that they have in place when developing or deploying AI systems . An effective way of improving 
protections for ICIP in the AI context would be to require developers and deployers to have ICIP 
protocols in place and comply with those protocols in connection with high -risk AI, and encourage 
developers and deployers to comply with ICIP protocols in connection with other applications of 
AI. 
CAG’s recommendations  
CAG encourages the Department to consider whether it is appropriate to require  developers and 
deploy ers of high -risk AI systems that leverage content containing ICIP to implement , publish  and 
comply with ICIP protocols , and otherwise encourage developers and deployers to comply with 
ICIP protocols in connection with other applications of AI.  
  
 
19 Guardrails proposals paper, p 13.   
CAG  Response  – October 2024   13 Summary of CAG’s recommendations  
In responding to the risks and opportunities of AI, particularly in education, CAG encourages the 
Department to bear in mind that AI regulation works hand -in-hand with copyright law. As outlined 
above, there are several key copyright risks and challenges faced by education in adopting digital 
technologies  including AI . Copyright law now needs urgent reform, as recommended by various 
reviews over more than a decade.  
To address the issues raised above , CAG recommends that  the Department should : 
1. only implement AI regulations in the form of the EU AI Act if Australia’s copyright framework 
is amended to include public interest copyright exceptions covering education that mirror 
those already in place in the EU and other jurisdictions  
2. support efforts to introduce copyright exceptions to give the education sector the legal clarity 
required to enable full engagement with the educational opportunities of AI  
3. continue consultation on the issues addressed in the Proposals Paper, in line with the 
approach taken in the EU prior to the introduction the EU AI Act, and follow a phased 
implementation process similar to that taken in the EU  
4. adopt an appropriate and nuanced approach to defining  high-risk AI that avoids low -risk 
applications  of AI in education  being automatically categorised as high -risk, and continue 
consultation with the school and TAFE  sector s on the risks associated with  particular 
educational use cases for  AI  
5. impose mandatory transparency obligations on copyright societies who collect and distribute 
funds under statutory licences covering the use of copyright works in connection with 
educational uses of AI  
6. where appropriate, require developers and deployers of high -risk AI systems that leverage 
content containing ICIP to implement , publish and comply with  ICIP protocols , and encourage 
developers and deployers to comply with ICIP protocols in connection with other applications 
of AI.  
Thank you for taking the time to consider these detailed views. Please contact me if you would 
like further information on any of the issues raised in this response . 
 
Delia Browne  
National Copyright Director  
Copyright Advisory Group – Schools and TAFE  
