Enhance productivity and democratise game development  
For the most popular games in the market, player demand has consistently  outpaced game 
developers’ ability to create game updates. To meet the community ’s expectations , 
publishers in the video game industry have begun developing internal generative AI 
capabilities to support developers in various game production tasks.  
AI is used as an assistive tool for developers of popular mobile games with 10,000+ game 
levels in service. P laytesting AI enables  the maintenance  of all levels alongside new levels  
introduced weekly, which alleviates developers  from a  dauntin g and time -consuming  task, 
in which the AI playtesting data provides game developers with valuable  insights that 
inform the design process of future levels.  
Further,  gaming engines  (game development software ) increasingly integrate AI and 
machine learning in their products, reducing  complexities that hinder the  creation  process .9 
AI-powered gaming engines enable  real-time creation through natural input such as text 
prompts and sketches , among many others.  
Question 4.  Are there high -risk use cases that government should consider banning in 
its regulatory response (for example, where there is an unacceptable level of risk)? If 
so, how should we define these?  
We note that the Australian Government has recently passed the Criminal Code 
Amendment (Deepfake Sexual Material) Act 2024  (Cth) , commencing in early September. 
This legislation a mends the  Criminal Code Act 1995  (Cth) to strengthen offences targeting 
the creation and non -consensual sharing of sexually explicit material online, including 
material that has been created or altered using AI technology (commonly referred to as 
‘deepfakes’).  
eSafety has also registered the Phase 1 Industry Online Safety Code for Internet Search 
Engine Services, and Standard for Designated Inter net Services (DIS) that includes 
consideration of AI -generated Class 1A and 1B material.10 These regulatory instruments 
include compliance measures to address such content , given that higher risk AI are more 
likely to arise in those contexts  than in other online service sectors .  
This raises a broader question as to how the proposed AI regulations under this 
consultation can coordinate with existing regulations , including  specifically addressing 
 
