 Introduction  
Engineers Australia welcomes the opportunity to continue supporting  the Australian Government with 
the ongoing work related to  safe and responsible use of AI  in Australia . Engineers are essential to the 
development, implementation, integration, utilisation and maintenance of artificial intelligent systems 
and products.   
Engineers Australia’s submission to the S afe and Responsible AI in Australia  consultation  called for a 
balanced, sector -specific, and risk -based approach to regulating high -risk AI applications, including the 
development of sector -specific standards, technology -specific regulations, and industry body 
guidelines and controls.  These measures are essential for maximising AI’s benefit for society while 
preventing harm, building public trust and ensuring international competitiveness.  
AI and the impact on the engineering profession  
Emerging technologies, such as artificial intelligence are reshaping many professions globally. 
Businesses, the tertiary sector and governments need to keep pace with these changes. In engineering, 
this means equipping engineers and engineering businesses to effectively leverage technology 
advancements, while hav ing appropriate safeguards in place against associated risks. To address this 
need; it is crucial to build a holistic understanding of the challenges, risks and opportunities associated 
with emergin g technologies in engineering.    
A forthcoming report to be released by Engineers Australia  later this year  has been exploring the 
impact of AI and emerging technologies on the engineering profession.  Key findings from this report 
include:  
▪ AI is transforming the engineering profession , including GenAI  (generative AI)_ , however it is 
mainly being used to increase  productivity .  
▪ Over 70 per cent of engineers agree GenAI will increase productivity in engineering, however, 82 
per cent believe GenAI will always require oversight.  
▪ One of the main challenges the profession faces is how to keep pace with the changes, ensuring 
engineers can effectively leverage advancements while safeguarding against associated risks.  
▪ Clarity and understanding of AI  are crucial, including developing a clear understanding of 
appropriate use and risks . This helps to boost engineers’ confidence and ability to use GenAI and 
AI tools correctly.  
▪ Open communication and training , policies, guidance and support also help mitigate risk of misuse 
and data breaches.  
About this submission  
This submission provides feedback to the questions raised in the Department of Industry, Science and 
Resources’ (DISR) proposals paper for introducing mandatory guardrails for AI in high -risk settings . The 
submission has been developed in consultation with Engineers Australia’s members from Engineers 
Australia’s College of Information, Telecommunications and Electronics Engineers and the Risk 
Engineering Society . 
About Engineers Australia  
As Australia’s national body for engineering, we are the voice and champion of our 1 30,000 -plus 
members. We provide them with the resources, connections, and growth they need to do ethical, 
competent and high -value work in our communities. A mission -based, not -for-profit professional  
Mandatory Guardrails for AI in High -Risk Settings  3 association, Engineers Australia is constituted by Royal Charter to advance the science and practice of 
engineering for the benefit of the community.  
Engineers Australia maintains national professional standards, benchmarked against international 
norms. As Australia’s signatory to the International Engineering Alliance (IEA), this includes 
accreditation of undergraduate university engineering programs.  
Contact  
To discuss the content of this submission further, or to arrange a discussion with engineering experts 
with experience with high -risk AI applications in Australia, please contact Daniel Salas, Policy and 
Projects Officer, at dsalas@engineersaustralia.org.au . 
Summary of recommendations  
Engineers Australia supports the need for gu ardrails for the use of AI in high -risk settings. Engineers 
Australia ’s submission emphasises the need for:  
▪ Regulatory balance and adaptability  to allow AI’s benefits to be realised, and for society to be 
protected.  
▪ Ensur ing the regulatory scheme allows for AI to keep  up with international trends in competition 
and regulation. Guardrails should reference and comply with ISO/IEC 42001.  
▪ A multi -disciplinary expert taskforce to revise case studies and risk assessments, and the authority 
to provide recommendations and call witnesses . 
▪ Greater clarification across guardrails and principles on responsibility for instances where AI 
systems are deployers and developers of AI themselves.  
   
Mandatory Guardrails for AI in High -Risk Settings  4 Responses to Consultation Questions  
Defining High -Risk AI  
Do the proposed principles adequately capture high -risk AI? Are there any principles we should add 
or remove?  
Engineers Australia  supports the need for guardrails for the use of AI in high -risk settings. The  
proposed principles adequately capture high -risk AI and the nature of its impacts  while emphasising 
flexibility to account for new and evolving technologies.  
Identify any low-risk use cases unintentionally captured.  
Some applications in security, policing and defence may be unintentionally captured by the proposed 
guardrails. Specialised AI applications for data analysis , such as those predicting equipment failures , 
may also be  impacted.  To avoid unintentional capture, it may be worth separating defence or national 
security AI. Stakeholders may also consider clarifying their low -risk use cases  (such as specialised 
application for data analysis) to avoid capturing such systems unintentionally.  
Are there categories of uses that should be treated separately, such as for defence or national 
security purposes?  
Engineers Australia recommends  defence and national security deployments, as well as critical 
infrastructure applications,  should  be treated separately  because of their unique  and specialist  
purposes . For example, Defence weapons systems may need to be excluded due to their fundamental 
purpose. However, systems used by Defence for other applications such as recruitment, training and 
administration need not be excluded.  
Do you have any suggestions for how the principles could better capture harms to First Nations 
people, communities, and Country?  
The principles could better capture  harm to First Nations people, communities, and Country by 
emphasising the potential for AI to exploit  this group  by appropriating  cultural images and artefacts.  
Such exploitation can lead to AI systems generating content that adversely impacts the economic, 
mental and cultural well -being of First Nations stakeholders. Acknowledging the risk of cultural 
appropriation and exploitation in prop osed principle  E (The risk of adverse impacts to the groups of 
individuals or collective rights of cultural groups)  will better address such harms .  
Do the proposed principles, supported by examples, provide enough clarity and certainty on high -risk 
AI settings and models? Is a more defined approach with a list of illustrative uses needed?  
Engineers Australia believes that whil e a list -based approach (such as the European Union AI Act) may 
provide sufficient guidance, there is clear risk of obsoletion as AI technology evolves.  
A principles -based approach  offers  flexibility but  require s frequent updates and clearer definitions of 
responsibility . Specifically, greater clarity is required on the matter of who the responsibility entity is  in 
instances where an AI system is the developer of an AI model. This may necessitate  redefining the 
term “developer ” to distinguish between AI -generated and human -generated development . 
Are there high -risk uses cases that the Government should consider banning? If so, how should these 
be defined?  
Before banning any AI system or model, it is essential to demonstrate a breach of the proposed 
principles. AI systems that cannot be turned off  or isolated from their operating environment pose  an 
unacceptable risk and should be banned . Similarly, certain AI technologies, such as Deepfake systems, 
have clear potential for misuse  which further warran ts restrictions.  Defining these systems in legal  
Mandatory Guardrails for AI in High -Risk Settings  5 terms (i.e. AI systems that impair human autonomy or create undue influence) will be crucial for 
effectively controlling high -risk use cases .  
Are the proposed principles flexible enough to capture new and emerging forms of high -risk AI, such 
as general -purpose AI (GPAI)?  
Engineers Australia believes  the proposed principles offer sufficient degree of flexibility;  however,  it is 
challenging  to predict their effectiveness in capturing new and emerging high -risk AI forms due to the 
rapid evolution of these technologies .  
To enhance the guidelines, it may be beneficial to include additional principles managing the emerging 
risks associated with AI systems that design and deploy other AI systems and models.  
Should mandatory guardrails apply to all GPAI models?  
Engineers Australia believes that mandatory guardrails should apply to GPAI models in instances 
determined to be high -risk. Where GPAI is being used in low -risk settings, there should be a clear risk 
assessment completed by deployers which identifies such use as low -risk and therefore subject to 
lesser guardrails.  
What are suitable indicators for defining GPAI models as high -risk (e.g. based on technical capability 
such as FLOPS or advice from a scientific panel.)?  
Engineers Australia recommends  an expert multi -disciplinary panel is best suited to inform decisions 
regarding high -risk models . Engineers Australia’s submission to the Safe and Responsible use of AI in 
Australia discussion paper advocated for Government to engage  industry and academia through 
taskforces , to focus on specific tangible risks and challenges .1 Engineers  Australia recommends this 
taskforce concept be established , and possess the ability to  inform decisions regarding high -risk 
models   
The taskforce  should meet  on a regular basis to examine case studies with evident b reaches or invalid 
risk assessments . The taskforce  should  be able to recommend  solutions and revise risk assessment s 
whenever necessary  and as  AI technology and applications  evolve . Crucially, the panel should 
encompass expertise from all relevant fields and have the authority to engage  expert witnesses as 
needed.  
Guardrails Ensuring Testing, Transparency, and 
Accountability of AI  
Do the proposed mandatory guardrails appropriately mitigate the risks of AI in high -risk settings? Are 
there any guardrails that should be added or removed?  
It is important the guardrails include a requirement to comply with the latest version of the ISO/IEC 
42001, the international standard that ensures responsible development of AI systems by entities 
providing or utilising AI -based products or services.  2 Incorporating guardrails that reference 
international standards is an approach that allows for the evolution of detailed requirements (i.e. for 
testing.) and regulatory alignment to other cou ntries .  
 
 
 
1 Engineers Australia (2023),  Responsible AI in Australia: An engineering perspective,  accessed 30 
September 2024.  
2 ISO/IEC 42001:2023 - AI management systems   
Mandatory Guardrails for AI in High -Risk Settings  6 How can the guardrails incorporate First Nations knowledge and cultural protocols to ensure AI 
systems are culturally appropriate and preserve Indigenous Cultural and Intellectual Property (ICIP) ?  
Engineers Australia welcomes the commitment of the proposal to collaborate with First Nations  
experts to ensure AI systems respect cultural protocols. Engaging these communities and experts and 
building guidelines on how AI can  avoid  misappropriation of indigenous art, language, or knowledge 
would enhance compliance  with cultur ally appropriate practice and  help preserve ICIP.  
Do the proposed mandatory guardrails distribute responsibility across the AI supply chain and 
lifecycle appropriately? For example, are the requirements assigned to developers and deployers 
appropriate?  
The proposed guardrails ensure  an adequate distribution of responsibility across the AI supply chain 
and lifecycle. However, there  are areas for improvement . The existing  framework  lacks clear 
accountability mechanisms, post -deployment monitoring  requirement s and consistent ethical 
oversight . To address  these  issues , mandatory guardrails should include  detailed sector -specific  
regulations, foster  collaboration among stakeholders, enhance  data governance and mandate post -
deployment monitoring.  Additionally,  clarifying responsibility for instances  where AI systems act as 
developers or deployers  would strengthen the guidelines . 
Are the proposed mandatory guardrails sufficient to address the risks of GPAI? How could the 
guardrails be adapted for different GPAI models (e.g., low -risk vs high -risk models) ?  
Engineers Australia believes the guardrails are sufficient  to address the risks of GPA I. However, the 
establishment of an expert review panel or taskforce with the authority to collect evidence and make 
determinations is recommended to strengthen the regulatory framework . Additionally, guardrails could 
be tailored to different technologies based on their risk level . For example, lower -risk technologies 
could follow  simplified compliance pathways or a less stringent set of guardrails. In all cases, risk 
assessments should be flexible and informed by the combined expertise of regulators and sector 
specialists.  
Do you have suggestions for reducing the regulatory burden on small -to-medium -sized businesses 
applying guardrails ?  
Engineers  Australia recommends  a risk -based approach to AI that focuses on the size and risk profile 
of the AI system rather than the size of the business deploying it. Any reduced regulatory burden must 
still adhere to the same risk assessments and regulatory framework, supported by detailed guidance 
and access to expert advisors.  
Regulatory Options to Mandate Guardrails  
Which legislative option best addresses the use of AI in high -risk settings? What opportunities should 
the government consider for each approach?  
Engineers  Australi a support s either a ‘Whole of Economy Approach’ (Option 3)  or a combined 
framework of the ‘Whole of Economy ’ and ‘Framework ’ Approaches (Options 2 and 3).  Engineers 
Australia recommends that any legislative approach be supported by consultation with multi -
disciplinary experts, through taskforces, panels or senior technical advisory positions in Government. 
The Australian Government should engage these exper t stakeholder groups regularly to stay informed 
about the evolving landscape of AI t echnology.  
Which regulatory option(s) will best ensure  that guardrails for high -risk AI can adapt and respond to 
step -changes in technological advancements  
Engineers Australia recommends either a whole of economy approach or a combined whole of 
economy and framework approach would be best. Whichever approach is taken, it is recommended   
Mandatory Guardrails for AI in High -Risk Settings  7 the Government establish  an independent, multi -disciplinary expert body to monitor technological 
development, deployment , application, review risk assessments, and recommend  remedies where 
appropriate.  
Where do you see the greatest risk of gaps or inconsistencies with Australia’s existing law for the 
development and deployment of AI? Which regulatory options best address  this and why?  
The greatest risks of gaps or inconsistencies in Australia’s existing laws regarding AI development and 
deployment stem from a  potential  lack of multi -disciplinary oversight and insufficient legislative 
responsiveness to rapid technological changes. Engineers Australia advocates for a dedicated AI -
specific Act and the establishment of specialised  regulators with relevant technical expertise. This 
approach would create a cohesive and agile regulatory framework to effectively address the evolving 
risks associated with AI technology.  
 
 
 
