 1    AUSTRALIAN GUILD OF SCREEN COMPOSERS LIMITED  POSTAL ADDRESS:  PO Box 3398, Tamarama  NSW  2026 ABN: 96 116 469 713   WEB: www.agsc.org.au   EMAIL: agsc@agsc.org.au          4th October 2024  Department of Industry, Science and Resources GPO Box 2013, Canberra. ACT. 2601.  Dear Minister,  SUBMISSION IN RESPONSE TO SAFE AND RESPONSIBLE AI IN AUSTRALIA - PROPOSALS PAPER FOR INTRODUCING MANDATORY GUARDRAILS FOR AI IN HIGH-RISK SETTINGS  The Australian Guild of Screen Composers (AGSC) represents screen composers nationally who work in games, television and film. We welcome the opportunity to respond to the consultation by the Department of Industry, Science and Resources on the “Safe and responsible AI in Australia – Proposals for introducing mandatory guardrails for AI in a high-risk setting“ released in September 2024.  ABOUT THE AGSC As the only dedicated hub for professional screen composers in Australia, the AGSC is the central point for the Australian screen composition community. The AGSC provides information, resources, advocacy and support for its members, addresses important industry topics through its regular newsletters, and organises member events and social gatherings.  In support of a strong, sustainable and resilient Australian screen music industry, the AGSC advocates for the rights of composers. As a representative body for the screen music community, we are here to help all composers regardless of their level of experience and support the continued sustainability of screen composition for our diverse and talented member community.  AGSC RESPONSE The AGSC works closely with its copyright collecting agency partner APRA AMCOS who license and distribute over $125m per year for screen composers worldwide. This income is derived from the copyright ownership screen composers have in their work and makes up almost 50% of all income for screen composers.   
 2 It is therefore essential to take into consideration individual composers’ works when formulating protections for music copyright. Any protections must specifically recognise the individual’s copyrights in their musical works.  Government guardrails to regulate AI tech companies should include and consider the creative industries in the following ways:  - Safe and responsible use of AI in the creative industries where any legal ramifications from data scraping and unauthorised use rests with the tech company who created the AI program, especially in cases where the AI generated content is in breach of current Australian copyright laws, including moral rights, including false attribution, lack of consent, and/or compensation for any works generated in the style of others.  - Transparency on the creation/development of AI programs and use with end-users, in our industry to ensure Australian composers and creators IP is not used in any way without authorisation. This would mean AI tech creation companies (developers and deployers) need to have accountability for their AI programs (including its end users) and mandated internal governance systems to ensure appropriate data management that logs and seeks consent of all and any data scraped or mined for use within the AI program. This is essential. The seeking of consent will be difficult if mining millions of works.  - Government should regulate AI tech creation companies to seek; o Consent from composers whose work has been used to train and generate AI content. o Credit and attribution from composers whose work has been used to train and generate AI content on each work mined must be tagged with full details of the approved works. o Compensation to composers whose work has been used to train and generate AI content.  The AGSC agrees and applauds the proposed guardrails to be a risk-based approach that includes preventative measure to ensure;  - Any legislated guardrails to include protections for IP and ICIP for the original author/writer of the work as opposed to the publisher of the work. (This is because our members are often contracted to hand over publishing and therefor have no say on the further exploitation of their work.)  - And work within current Australian copyright law and the extension of the Copyright Act to ensure the protection of rights.  - Any AI generated work that potentially infringes on a third party must be able to be held accountable.  - Any composer using AI generated material must be protected from potential copyright infringement from a third party.      
 3 Beyond this, as creatives we need to be able to use it safely and confidently and know that proper Australian laws have been adhered to in its design. We want the opportunity to be able to incorporate any AI processes into our work safely without risk of decimating our industry.  The government needs to ensure that any definition of “high risk” AI also includes the creative industries and associated legal and intellectual property rights (copyright) as any reduction in these rights will see the loss of creative jobs and reduced revenue. Any high risk definition should include content creation. AI systems used in the creation of new work and content where that work is made in the likeness or is a “sound a-like” of another work, and in breach of copyright as consent, credit and compensation has not been authored or cleared. As revealed in the APRA AMCOS “AI and Music Report” 2024 in protections are not put in place then:   “By 2028, 23% of music creators’ revenues will be at risk due to generative AI, an estimated cumulative total damage of over half a billion Australian dollars (AUD$519 million)”   These legislations should be applied to both generative AI and GENERAL PURPOSE AI. The crucial step will be ensuring that works that are mined, technically have the inbuilt data tags that retrospectively can be accessed to determine the copyright status and ownership of works. It’s all very well to say that in an ideal world companies would seek permission or a relevant licence prior to raking and training AI systems. It is likely that generative works would be created and later assessed retrospectively to see from what data set of works they were derived.   In particular, we welcome the inclusion of the assessment of: I. the risk of adverse legal effects, defamation or similarly significant effects on an individual;  II. the risk of adverse impacts to groups of individuals or collective rights of cultural groups; and III. the risk of adverse impacts to the broader Australian economy, society, environment and rule of law, by the risk of unauthorised, unlicensed and unremunerated use and exploitation of protected works by AI systems  We support APRA AMCOS proposal for four key principles that must be applied to AI Guardrails to ensure their efficacy: 1. Transparency: AI-generated music must be clearly labelled, allowing consumers and industry professionals to easily identify when they are engaging with content created by AI rather than humans.  
 4 2. Accountability: Developers and operators of AI systems must be held accountable for the outputs generated by their systems, particularly in cases where those outputs infringe intellectual property or perpetuate cultural biases. 3. Fair Compensation: AI systems that use copyrighted works in their training datasets must be subject to licensing agreements, ensuring that songwriters, composers, and other creators are fairly compensated for the use of their work. 4. Global Standards: Given the international nature of the AI industry, any regulatory framework for AI in Australia must align with global standards to ensure that overseas platforms do not gain an unfair competitive advantage by operating under more lenient regulations. It is interesting to note that there have been a number of legal cases around the world including in Australia where individual composers have been sued for creating music that is “similar” to other composers work. It seems that the copying and appropriating of composers work for use by AI has not been taken as seriously due to the way the “scraping” of music to be used in Generative AI has occurred.  IN CONCLUSION The rise of AI has taken the “human” creative approach to music creation out of the equation. We don’t even know how AI music will be credited in the screen in film and television production – “Music by AI?”. We are not luddites when it comes to technology and many tools using AI to simplify repetitive tasks for composers are in use today. It is clear that they pose a significant risk to screen composers’ rights and livelihood. We therefore need strong government regulations to protect the individual creators of screen music and Copyright Law needs to be updated to take into account these new types of reproductions and adaptations of existing works. ","AGS_RESPONSE_TO_GUARDRAIL_PROPOSAL_Oct_2024.87937a6f","AGS_RESPONSE_TO_GUARDRAIL_PROPOSAL_Oct_2024.87937a6f.pdf","pdf","C:\\Users\\james.barro\\Documents\\rsm-nvidia-hackathon\\data\\Submission_Docs\\Processed\\AGS_RESPONSE_TO_GUARDRAIL_PROPOSAL_Oct_2024.87937a6f.pdf","1"
