Summary of Adobe Comments  
 
I. The Case for Regulating Guardrails   
 
a. Definition of Developer and Deployer  
 
The Proposal Paper defines ""developers” as “organizations  or individuals who design, build, train, 
adapt or combine AI models and application s”.  When it comes to developing AI models, it’s our  
belief that  the regulatory focus should be on training  of an AI model  (rather than an AI system ). 
An AI system  is typically comprised of multiple models, some of which may include  AI engineering , 
some of which may not. Therefore , regulating an AI system  would not serve the intended purpose 
of this proposal. Rather, t he training of a particular model  is the key activity that determines how 
the model will perform  so it is at the model level that we should focus regulatory efforts.  In 
addition, Terms like “adapt” or “combine” are more aligned with activities that constitute 
“deployers” of AI and would therefore be mor e appropriately regulated as deployers.  
 
For these reasons, w e recommend that  the DISR adopt  the definition s of “provider ” and 
“deployer” cited in the  European Union ’s Artificial Intelligence  Act (EU AI Act)5.  
 
b. Group Impacts / First Nations  
 
As with all technology, without proper oversight and mitigation measures in place, new 
innovations can pose challenges, such as perpetuating harmful biases. When it comes to AI, it is 
important to ta ke steps to ensure that AI tools and the content users may generate do not 
disproportionately impact certain groups . This includes  how material incorporating First Nations 
traditional knowledge and cultural expression may be used in training AI models or drawn on or 
imitated in the outputs of generative AI models.  At Adobe, we believe that artists  should  be 
protected from the misuse of generative AI tools.   
  
As we con tinue to harness the power of AI, Adobe is commi tted to holding ourselves to a higher 
standard of not just innova tion, but responsible innova tion. In line with Adobe’s AI Ethics 
Principles , Adobe trained Adobe Firefly generative AI models on licensed content, including 
images from our Adobe  Stock6 collection, as well as public domain images where copyright has 
expired. Adobe Stock requires contributors to agree to legal terms7, which state that contributors 
may only contribute works that they have a right to license  and in accordance with our Adobe 
 
