II. Defining High -Risk AI   
 
a. High -Risk AI Based on Intended and Foreseeable Uses  
 
We acknowledge DISR’s effort to ensure that its proposed principles prevent inadvertently 
capturing low -risk applications in a list of high -risk settings, and  the willingness  to accommodate 
step -up changes in AI technology as it advances. However, we believe that the current list of 
principles is too broad and would benefit from better alignment with the approach taken in the 
EU AI Act . Annex III10 of the  EU AI  Act, narrows down the concept of high -risk by linking it to 
significant harm s to “health, safety or fundamental rights, and whether the decision -making 
process has been materially influenced by the AI system ”.  
 
Biometrics . We note that the Proposals Paper cites nine examples of high -risk use cases i dentified  
in other countries . The term biometrics  is defined as “AI systems used to identify or categorize  
individuals, assess behavior  or mental state, or monitor and influence emotions. ” We ask that  DISR 
consider  adopting the viewpoint  that biometric AI systems and their ""categorization"" are only 
high -risk to the extent that they  can cause 'systemic harm' in line with  the EU AI Act11 or those 
that influence the domain areas listed in “Table 1: High risk use cases identified in other countries” 
including education, employment, and law enforcement .  
 
DISR should  also consider the fact that some biometric use cases  could be essential in mitigating 
 
