5  the harms and concerns discussed throughout the proposal paper.  For example, tagging images 
with biometric information and using those images to de-bias a generative AI image model  would 
constitute a biometric use case as currently defined.  Thus, w e recommend  that an exemption  for 
this use case be considered  to better align  with Article 6 of the EU AI Act12. 
 
Law Enforcement . We appreciate that the Proposals Paper also cites “law enforcement” as 
another high -risk use case identified in other countries  and defines this as “ AI systems used in 
aspects of law enforcement, including profiling of individuals, assessing offender recidivism risk, 
polygraph -style technologies or evaluating evidence .” We recommend that  DISR consider that AI 
systems may be used by non -governmental entities for law enforcement purposes  and/or in low-
risk settings, such as whe re a compan y uses an AI system to intake and categorize  law 
enforcement requests for user data. As such, we suggest that th e examined use case  be limited to 
only technologies  used  by law enforcement authorities,  in line with  Article 613 the EU AI Act . 
 
b. Definition of High -Risk General Practice AI  (GPAI) Models  
 
General Practice AI (GPAI).  We note that the Proposals Pa per diverges from the EU AI Act by 
creating a category of AI models referred to as “general practice AI”  (GPAI)  models  and the 
assumption that all GPAI models are high risk.  The term is  defined as “An AI model that is capable 
of being used, or capable of being adapted for use, for a variety of purposes, both for direct use as 
well as for integration in other systems.” We acknowledge that  in defining GPAI models this way, 
DISR attempts to focus on language  and decision -making -based technologies that can be used 
across a multitude of purposes that extend  beyond just image  and video generation.  
 
However, the proposed definition of GPAI is very broad and would include a large range of AI 
models that may be used in low -risk settings by individuals and organizations . At Adobe, it is our 
belief that media generation models, like AI image or video generation  tools , should not be 
included in this definition  since the most effective way to regulate media generative AI is through 
a user opt -in system of content provenance , in part due to the limitations of prevention and 
detection  we expand on  below . We urge  DISR to adopt this point of view as they contemplate 
changes to the current language.  
 
III. Guardrail 3: Protect AI Systems, Implement  Data Governance Measures to Manage Data 
Quality and Provenance  
 
a. Data  Governance, Privacy, and Cybersecurity Measures  
 
 
