sorting images (when done in an otherwise lawful manner) is not high -risk. 
13 https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/6/      
 
6  We commend  DISR for its focus on ensuring that organizations have appropriate data governance, 
privacy and cybersecurity measures in place. We share the belief that d ata quality directly impacts 
the performance and reliability of an AI model, and datasets can contain biases that may lead to 
discriminatory outputs.  This said, the Proposals Paper  requirement that  data sources be disclosed 
may be overly burdensome for organizations  that have already receive d authorization from 
numerous rightsholders.  A balance may be struck between the importa nt policy goals of 
promoting innovation, protecting the intellectual property rights of creators  and developers , and 
promoting transparency.  To achieve this, we urge DISR to consider different requirements for 
developers using authorized data such as licensed content or public domain content versus other 
type s of content. In our view, when a developer is using licensed content , content that does not 
require a license because the copyright has expired, and/or content with a license that permits AI 
training, only limited, descriptive disclosure should be required to protect trade secrets. We 
recommend language that accounts for this reality , such as “GPAI model developers should not be 
required to disclose data sources when they were obtained with authorization from the 
rightsholder ”. 
 
IV. Guardrail 6 : Inform End-Users Regarding AI -Enabled Decisions, Interactions with AI and 
AI-Generated Content  
 
a. Detection and Content Credentials / C2PA  
 
We appreciate the Proposals Paper refere nce to the need to develop tools for detecting AI -
generated content  (Guardrail 6) . We agree with  DISR’s sentiment that because of advancements  
in AI, developing, editing, and distributing content has become more powerful and accessible for 
consumers. In addition, the same tools enhanced by the power of AI to make and share content 
can also be misused by bad actors to create and spread deceptive synthetic media. Once people 
are fooled by a deepfake, they lose trust in everything they see or hear digitally, even if it is true. 
This pervasive erosion of trust will have profound implications for democracy, especially in 
elections when misinformation is rampant, and the stakes are high.   
 
However, we believe that d etection technologies face limitations including in correctly classifying 
fake media . This coupled with the fact that detection often occurs after  the fact – and too late . By 
the time someone attaches  a label  to a deceptive piece of content , millions of people might have 
already seen it and potentially spread it.  
 
Detection also doesn’t do enough to equip the public in the current digital landscape. Soon, most  
content will have some AI in it. Simply detecting and labeling something as AI doesn’t consider  the 
many  nuanced ways AI could be used. For example, a journalist may want to use AI to quickly 
remove the visible license plate numbers in the background of an image to protect people’s 
privacy. On the other hand, someone could misuse this technology to generate or manipulate an    
 
7  image of someone doing something they never did in order to try to deceive people. These are 
very different applications of AI, but detection would likely label both in the same way. 
Furthermore, much of the deceptive content we’ve seen recently is not necessarily AI -generated 
content, but is real content taken out of context, such as an image of a 2018 California wildfire 
being mischaracterized as an image of the Amazon burning. Since it is a real photo, detection 
would not catch this.  
 
Given th e limitations and shortcomings of  detection, in 2019, Adobe co -founded the Content 
Authenticity Initiative ( CAI)14 with the goal of restoring trust in digital content  by giving creators a 
way to show additional context with their work, allowing the viewer to have more information to 
determine whether or not to trust that work . In just f ive years, the CAI has grown to more than 
3,300 members a  – including  the BBC, AFP, Axel Springer, Reuters, Wall Street Journal, Microsoft, 
NVIDIA, and Sony — all united behind this  vision .  
 
We note that the Proposals Paper refers  to the CAI and how it promotes the widespread adoption 
of Content Credential s – provenance15 technology  built on an  open technical standard developed 
by the C oalition for Content Provenance and Authenticity (C 2PA)16. Content Credentials  function 
as a “nutrition label”  for digital content. They allow creators to attach information to a piece of 
content such as name, date, and edits that were made. Content Credentials  can also show  
whether  AI was used and perhaps  more importantly, how it was used. This information is 
cryptographically signed and then embedded into the piece  of content and travels with it 
wherever it goes. In this way, Content Credentials  give creators a way to show their work and give 
consumers a way to see context alongside the content they are consuming . As the Proposals 
Paper notes,  this increased level of transparency  can help  consumers make more informed 
decisions about whether to trust the content they see online. The approach taken by the CAI and 
the C2PA was adopted after careful study, and we believe that it will prove effective in fostering 
trust and transparency in digital content items published online .   
 
It is also imperative to have a provenance solution  – like the one developed by the C2PA  – 
everywhere  from the places where content is captured or created to the tools where it is edited 
to the platforms where it is  ultimately consumed by the public . This approach relies on tools and  
platforms respecting the intent of content creators who have chosen to attach Content 
Credentials  to their content.  As such, we urge DISR to shift the scope of Guardrail 6 from a focus 
on detection to a focus on increasing trust and transparency through the adoption of open 
technical standards for content provenance and authenticity like the one developed by the C2PA.  
 
14https://contentauthenticity.org/   - The Content Authenticity Initiative (CAI) is a group of creators, technologists, journalists, and activists leading 
the global effort to address digital misinformation and content authenticity. We’re focused on promoting and providing an ope n, cross -indust ry 
approach to media transparency.  
15 https://c2pa.org/faq/  - Provenance: Information about where a piece of content came from and what happened to it along the way  
16https://c2pa.org   - The Coalition for Content Provenance and Authenticity (C2PA) addresses the prevalence of misleading information online 
through the development of technical standards for certifying the source and history (or provenance) of media content.     
 
8   
The Australian government  has a unique opportunity to be a global leader in fostering AI 
transparency by includ ing provenance opt-in requirements in  any proposed  AI legislation. We 
urge  DISR to ensure that devices that enable its users to capture  or create  content, such as 
smartphones, make provenance technology available so that users can choose to turn on this 
capability . This approach coupled with ensur ing that online platforms and social media companies 
implement Content Credentials  and do not strip away any metadata associated with the content 
published on their platforms  will be key to fostering a more trustworthy digital space.  
 
We also note that the Proposals Paper highlights the potential risks of AI, including the  danger ous 
impact  of deepfakes and misinformation  on electoral outcomes . We understand there is a 
particular urgency to mitigate this risk as we approach the upcoming elections in Australia.  In our 
view, Content Credentials  allows us to effectively build transparency and trust online  by building 
an end -to-end chain of trust and content authenticity .   
 
We recently made a submission to the Select Committee on Adopting AI where we urged  the 
Government to review the CAI’s open -source tool kit and Content Credentials technology and 
require this, or similar provenance technology, in all campaign materials and advertising  
throughout Australia. By requiring campaigns to integrate Content Credentials into their 
campaign communications, especially AI -generated ads, people would be afforded a level of 
transparency about what they are consuming and be better equippe d to make an informed 
decision to trust the material or not. Additionally, to support this approach, Government should 
consider policies that require campaigns to maintain embedded Content Credentials so they 
cannot be stripped away, depriving voters of im portant information and  context about the 
content’s provenance . These provenance technologies could be used to bring  greater 
transparency to campaigns in Australia.  
 
For example, Content Credentials  can help ensure that the engagement of the public with elected 
representatives —a cornerstone of democracy —is not drowned out by AI -generated noise. By 
providing verifiable information about what  they  are sharing with their constituents, elected 
representatives can foster a sense of trust and transparency in their engagements with the public.  
The ubiquitous adoption of Content Credentials would provi de good actors with one tool to prove 
what’s true and  enable the voting publi c to have a verifiable way to distinguish fact from fiction. 
In doing so, we will collectively restore a more trustworth y digital ecosystem that can withstand 
these attacks on the democratic process.  
 
Conclusion  
Adobe appreciates the opportunity to comment on DISR’s proposed mandatory guardrails for AI 
in high -risk settings.  We commend the commitment of the Australian Government  to build a 
better future for all Australians through enabling a productive, resilient and sustainable economy,    
 
