 Submission to Proposed Mandatory 
Guardrails for AI in High -risk Settings  
Tom Drummond, Lea Frermann, Toby Murray, Olga Ohrimenko, Jeannie Paterson,  
Adrian Pearce,  Benjamin Rubinstein,  Joseph West, Xingliang Yuan1 
School of Computing and Information Systems  and the Centre for AI and Digital Ethics  
The University of Melbourne  
  
Executive Summary  
We are grateful for the opportunity to provide input to the Proposed Mandatory Guardrails 
for AI in High -Risk Settings. Our response makes some key recommendations, chiefly:  
- Mandatory guardrails should not apply to all GPAI models as this would stall 
innovation.  
- Using thresholds to define a high -risk model is futile, as damage can be done from 
“small” and “large” AI models and the risk is defined by the use -case of an AI model.  
- Risk management should explicitly state the threat model and the likelihood of the 
risk occurring and prevented.  
- Data privacy and data protection need to be made explicit in the guardrails as data 
plays an essential role in the AI eco -system.  
- The end -user of AI systems needs to be added as one of the participants in Table 2, 
along with deployer and developer, whose roles need to be further refined.  
- Guardrails need to take into account the scenario and the use case of an AI model 
when evaluating and reporting risks.  
  
Our feedback in this document is based on the expertise in computer science that underlies 
AI technology and the corresponding concerns regarding its capabilities, including areas 
such as Artificial Intelligence, Computer Vision, Digital and AI E thics, Machine Learning, 
Natural Language Processing, Computer and Network Security, Verification, Privacy of 
Digital Data and Information , and Applied Cryptography. These areas are active areas of 
research, government and industry engagements of the contributors of this submission.  
 
 
