At Telstra, our ambition is to become an AI -fuelled organisation, and we are applying AI to 
help us unlock better outcomes for our customers, our teams , and our operations. We have 
already used AI to improve more than two -thirds of our key processes, for example, through 
automatically detecting and resolving fixed services faults, and making it easier for our 
frontline teams to find the right information to serve our customers better and faster. We’ve 
also launched a new Data & AI Academy to help our people build their skills  and embrace the 
opportunities that AI will bring .  Recently, we’ve  executed  an important strategic partnership 
with Microsoft to further enable AI development in Australia with Telstra procuring 21,000 
Copilot  for Microsoft 365  licenses, the largest deployment of generative AI in any telco 
globally.1   
We recognise  the enormous potential of AI and to that end, we welcome the A ustralian 
Government’s consultation on Safe and Responsible  AI in Australia  and its continued  
commitment to develop ing a regulatory environment that builds community trust and 
promotes AI adoption. Over the last year, Telstra has been an active and constructive 
participant in various  discussions  on AI regulation with Government and other  community 
stakeholders , and we are committed to ongoing engag ement on  these important issues .   
This consultation comes at  a time when there is considerably more awareness and concern by 
consumers about their data and AI more generally. Recent research from Stanford  University  
highlights that Australians are relatively uneasy about AI, compared to other countries.2 The 
Government ’s interim response noted that whilst Australians see the opportunity of AI to 
boost productivity and power new industries,  there was also considerable concern regarding 
design of the technology, opacity a nd unpredictability of the AI, transparency and unforeseen 
risks given the pace at which the technology is developing.3 There was a lso a  general 
consensus that Australians want more regulatory protections  for high -risk AI settings, but that 
this must be balanced by the need to support innovation.  
It is therefore important that the regulatory settings Australia seeks to introduce strike the 
right balance between the primary goal of providing protection s for consumers  and the 
practical compliance burden imposed on industry .  Further, the regulatory settings need to be 
designed in such a way that creates a n environment that drives continued innovation and 
investment , allowing Australia to engage in AI’s transformative potential .   
 
Telstra is committed to responsible AI adoption and is highly supportive of the introduction of 
mandatory guardrails for AI in high -risk settings in Australia which will apply throughout the 
lifecycle of AI systems .  The introduction of mandatory guardrails coupled with the Voluntary 
 
 
