AI Safety Standard  are important policy initiatives which we believe will assist in achieving the 
Government’s aim of uplifting Australia’s AI capability. Our s ubmission is designed to provide 
comments where clarity or improvements could be made to best address the Government’s 
objectives.  
 
Relatedly, Telstra remains of the view that an overarching AI governance framework, based 
on the current legislative framework and supported by other mechanisms (such as industry 
standards and guidelines)  (Option 1 ) is the best legislative approach for implementing the 
guardrails  ensuring the right balance between the risks posed by high -risk AI whilst still 
encouraging innovation, investment,  and adoption.  
 
 
 
Telstra welcomes  the opportunity to make a submission to the Australian Government’s 
Proposal paper for introducing mandatory guardrails for AI in high -risk settings. The 
transformative power of AI will undoubtedly change the way in which Australians work and 
live and we remain committed to ensuring that AI is introduced in a safe  and responsible 
manner.   
At Telstra, we are embracing AI as a key en abler for Telstra in digitising our business  to deliver 
value for our people, customers, and shareholders, now and in the future . Our  AI vision is to 
be a data -driven and AI -fuelled organisation and a leader in responsible AI adoption in 
Australia.   AI is a big part of our T25 strategy and our digital leadership ambitions . 
There are three aspects to Telstra’s AI roadmap:   
• Modernised data and technology ecosystem:  rethinking our infrastructure to 
modernise and simplify our underlying technologies and architecture to make them 
more reusable and resilient.  
• AI-ready workforce: investing in our talent by supporting a reinvention of our 
workforce through upskilling and using AI to fuel what everyone does in the 
organisation.  
• AI-fuelled products and services: embedding AI into our products and services.  
We have already begun to use AI in our operations including th rough improving the customer 
experience, improving our employee experience and in the detection and disruption of scams.  
Telstra has  also been an active participa nt in the initial development phases of responsible AI 
frameworks both in Australia and globally.  We contributed to the development and testing of 
the Australian AI Ethics Principles, co-authored the GSMA AI Ethics Playbook and the recent  
Responsible AI Maturity Roadmap, and in 2024 joined the UNESCO Business Council for Ethics 
of AI as the first and currently, only Australian organisation member . We are committed to 
practising responsible AI and continue to collaborate with a range of local and international Safe and Responsible AI in Australia – Proposals paper for introducing mandatory guardrails for AI in high -risk 
settings – Telstra Response   
  
 
 
 PAGE 5 
 
partners, as we strive to ensure consistency across the frameworks being considered and 
adopted worldwid e. 
 
We recogni se that robust internal governance of data and AI is essential for building trust with 
our customers and stakeholders. At Telstra, we have integrated Australia’s AI Ethics principles 
and frameworks through a tiered governance approach  to ensure  transparency, testing 
adherence to relevant principles, mitigat ion of  risks and harms, and ultimately facilitate well-
considered  risk-based decisions . This governance approach together with our underlying 
policies and processes are designed to promote the responsible and transparent adoption of AI 
solutions. With the rapid rise and adoption of AI technology , and the emergence of new risks 
and controls to manage them, we will be regularly review ing and updat ing our AI governance 
and risk management frameworks.   
 
Our submission responds to the range of issues considered by the Government w ith a particular 
focus on the need for clarity when defining high risk AI.  Notably, we provide examples in our 
submission to illustrate where ambiguity may arise and suggest alternative drafting to provide 
further clarification  for businesses as they embrace the opportunity of AI.  
 
Our submission is structured as follows:  
 
• Section 3 considers  the proposed  definition of high risk , suggesting  further clarity  is 
required on the applicable threshold s to make the definition workable for businesses  
and ensure consistent application cross -economy .  Our submission also outlines our 
views that the proposed definition of General -Purpose  AI (GPAI ) is too broad and 
needs to  be refine d. 
 
• Section 4 provides commentary on  the mandatory guardrails themselves,  noting that 
although Telstra supports the high -level  guardrails  in general , the underlying detail in 
the Proposals Paper suggests challenges may emerge when  the detail is considered .  
 
• Section 5 discusses the Government ’s options for implementing the guardrails. In this 
section, we outline the reasons why we consider Option 1 remains the most 
appropriate  manner in which to introduce the guardrails  as currently drafted .  
 
• Section 6 discusses the Voluntary AI Safety Standard.  
 
 
3.1 More clarity is needed to enable businesses to adequately determine whether an AI 
system is high risk.  
Whil st Telstra is generally supportive of the publication of proposed principles  which serve  to 
define  if an AI system is high risk, we consider that  the principles as drafted contain  insufficient 
guidance on the severity and extent (principle (f)) of adverse impacts of those dimensions Safe and Responsible AI in Australia – Proposals paper for introducing mandatory guardrails for AI in high -risk 
settings – Telstra Response   
  
 
 
 PAGE 6 
 
referenced in (a) to (e) that would be required to meet the high -risk threshold.  This makes 
the framewo rk challenging to adopt in practice.  
Organisations need to be able to determine with confidence  whether an AI system is low or 
high risk, with negligible risk of error. If the test is vague, there is a real risk of inconsistent and 
incorrect interpretation and application by different organisations.  This will lead to some high -
risk AI systems not having the guardrails applied,  and some low -risk AI systems inadvertently 
being treated as high risk. Although illustrative examples and guidance  can assist, we are not 
confident that this will be sufficient to provide suitable  certainty , given the intent i s for this 
definition to apply across the economy in a wide range of diverse settings.   
In addition, we consider that s ome asp ects of the dimensions described may be in  conflict with 
other dimensions, or with the guardrails themselves.  Most n otable is dimension (a): “The risk 
of adverse impacts to an individual’s rights recognised in Australian human rights law without 
justification ”. The Proposal Paper  suggests that public health and safety could potentially justify 
adverse impacts on an individual’s rights . However,  adverse impacts to an individual’s health 
and safety is also captured in dimension (b). This appears to create  a conflict  and it is unclear 
which dimension would take priority .   Further,  if the AI developer considers the human rights 
impact to be  ‘justified ’, does this mean that none of the guardrails would apply  and the 
developer would then not be require d to conduct any testing and monitoring (Guardrail 4 ) or 
maintain any records (Guardrail 9) ? We suggest that  these guardrails , and perhaps others,  are 
an essential part of considering and documenting whether the impact on  human rights is indeed  
justified . We believe further clarity is needed on the intent here, particularly the evidence that 
may be required to rely on the ‘without justification’ clause.  
3.2 The proposed definition for General -Purpose AI is too broad . 
We believe the proposed GPAI definition is too broad. We note that almost all AI systems – even 
narrowly defined low-risk ones – are theoretically  capable of being adapted for use in a range 
of settings. This does not mean such adaptation is intended, or likely , or that no controls exist  
over that adaptation , or that such adaptations  would be high risk . For example, Telstra uses AI 
Systems internally to predict overall customer sentiment, given recent product or service usage 
and interactions with Telstra . The primary use of these predictions  is for management 
information /reporting purposes , a low -risk application . Any further use of these predictions for 
decision making is subject to further review  and approval processes . While theoretically these 
AI-generated predictions could be used in a wide variety of contexts, those new  contexts can be 
evaluated against the dimensions proposed previously to determine if they are  high risk. It 
would not be appropriate to designate a low -risk AI system like this  as GPAI or ‘high -risk’, merely 
because it could theoretically be reused in some high -risk manner.   
 
To prevent such outcomes , we propose that GPAI be defined along the following lines:  “GPAI: 
An AI model that is capable of being used or capable of being adapted for use by parties other 
than  the model developer , in ways  that could be defined as ‘high -risk’” This would capture 
foundation models which are published externally for others to copy and reuse  with no 
significant ongoing control from the publisher  but would not capture low -risk internal systems 
that are able to be tightly controlled  by the company that produced them. In addition, f urther Safe and Responsible AI in Australia – Proposals paper for introducing mandatory guardrails for AI in high -risk 
settings – Telstra Response   
  
 
 
 PAGE 7 
 
clarification should be contained in any guidance material, to make it clear that internal AI 
systems are not intended to be captured. We note the EU AI Act has contemplate d this issue 
and contains guidance to address it.4  
3.3 Definition of high -risk AI and options for implementation must be considered 
together.  
The level of  certainty  required in the definition of high -risk AI varies in criticality  depending on 
the regulatory implementation method  chosen . If Option 1 is pursued  (as is our preferred 
optio n), the relevant Governmen t authority who administers the primary legislation will be able 
to consider appropriate drafting or amendment to that legislation to ensure there is clarity over 
the intended areas of application .  Uncertainty in the overall framework language is then  less 
concerning.  
On the other hand, if the Government pursues  Option 3, the definition of ‘high risk’ needs to 
allow AI developers and deployers to consistently apply the definition across the whole of 
economy, with minimal risk of error.   Alternatively, if the Government is minded to adopt  Option 
2, depending  on how the Government proposes to draft Framework legislation and the 
interaction with primary legislation , either the framework legislation or the primary legislation 
needs to provide that certainty to ensure uniform assessment of whether any  AI system  is high 
risk.  
 
4.1 The proposed mandatory  guardrails  are too prescriptive in places , adding cost for 
limited value in some situations .  
We broadly  agree  with the proposal of mandatory  guardrails , and the general headings for each 
guardrail appear to be a reasonable high -level statement of intent. However, we observe that  
some of the details provided in the Consultation Paper (and the related Voluntary Standard ) are 
relatively  prescriptive  in places and may cause challenges if applied in the broad manner 
envisaged . Below  are some  examples of issues which we believe could arise , if applied as 
currently written .  
 
• Guardrail 1 requires  organisations  to “Establish, implement and publish an 
accountability process including governance, internal capability and a strategy for 
regulatory compliance .” 5The guardrail states that “ organisations  make their 
accountability processes publicly available and accessible to improve public confidence 
in AI products and service s6”, however , this appears to conflict with the previous 
sentence which states  “This obligation should extend to publishing where this is in the 
public interest. For example, where the AI system or GPAI model has been or is being 
 
 
4 See Recital 97 | EU Artificial Intelligence Act  
