deployed on a wide scale ”7. As such, it is unclear  what is meant by ‘publish’ and  
whether accountability process es mus t always be published , or only when  it is in the 
public interest . If this publication of  accountability processes  was required even where 
not in the public interest , this would not only be a potentially  excessive compliance 
burden with limited practical benefit , but  accountability processes will likely contain  
commercially sensitive information  that organisations  rely on to provide competitive 
advantage s. 
• Guardrail 5  requires organisations  to ‘Enable human control or intervention in an AI 
system to achieve meaningful human oversight’8. As written , we consider this may lead 
to the banning of  genuinely beneficial AI Systems , which we do not believe is the 
intention of the Government.  Many  high -risk AI Systems offer benefit s to health and 
safety but operate autonomously, without human control or intervention. Examples 
include autonomous braking in cars, and smart alarm systems in buildings. Such 
systems offer immense public benefit and save lives today. A horizonal 
implementation of Guardrail 5 may cause challenges to such AI systems, as written. In 
particular , we refer to the requirement under this guardrail that “…developers should 
design the system so that a human can review its operations and outputs and reverse 
a decision if necessary .”9 This appears to require the possibility of human intervention 
to reverse or counteract AI decisions in all high -risk situations.  In situations like 
automated braking or alarm systems, this is not only infeasible, but we note  that these 
systems are specifically designed to operate without human intervention – that is 
inherent to their value. It would be unfortunate if such systems were withdrawn, due 
to a broad -brush requirement such as this.  
• Guardrail 6  requires that organisations  ‘Inform end -users regarding AI -enabled 
decisions, interactions with AI and AI -generated content’ .10 While this guardrail 
overlaps with proposed Privacy Act reforms  with respect to AI -enabled decisions , 
which is discussed further in Section 5 of this submissio n, additional clarity is required 
as to whether inserting a  human intermediary between an AI system  and a person  
negates the requirement to inform the person that  they are interacting with an AI 
system  as the interaction is not ‘d irect’ , even where the content  conveyed by the 
human intermediary is unch anged from the outputs generated by an AI system. For 
example, Telstra staff utilise  the internal AI tool  ‘Ask Telstra ’ to query knowledge 
management resources in order to determine the best approach to assist customers. 
Without  terms such as ‘directly’ being appropriately de fined, it is challenging for 
industry to continue to innovate in ways that respond to consumer expectations 
without certainty that onerous  notice requirements will not be require d to be 
embedded within processes . Additionally, GPAI systems  used in internal scenarios 
would also be required to comply with Guardrail 6 . This will likely be impracticable to 
implement given both the proliferation of generative AI outputs  across email 
 
 
