determining to what extent “a description of the characteristics, data sources a model 
is trained on, key design decisions regarding data training or the AI system or model’s 
development affecting its performance, and the capabilities, limitations and r isks of 
the system ”12 will be excluded from this guardrail.  
 
The above examples are not intended to be exhaust ive. However, we believe  they illustrate that 
while the general intent of the guardrails appears reasonable, the detailed requirements may 
lead to unintended impacts or inappropriate results in specific situations . We suggest that even 
with very careful drafting, this will be challenging to remove entirely due to the breadth of 
application of the guardrails  and the continuing innovation in the market around AI.  
 
4.2 Guardrails should be intentionally mapped to risks being mitigated .   
It is also unclear if the guardrails will have the risk -reducing effect intended, as there is no clear 
mapping between the risk dimensions identified and the guardrails. This link should be made, 
so the framework can be enhanced as we learn more about the risks of AI and how to manage 
them . For example, the Proposal Paper contains a list of historical harms on pages 12-13. It 
would be helpful to understand how the Government considers that the Guardrails would 
reduce the risks of each of these harms to acceptable levels. In considering the Guardrails, we 
are not convinced that they are sufficient to prevent biased use of facial recognition in criminal 
justice , or to prevent the use of biased essay grading software, for example .  
 
 
5.1 Option 1 (a domain specific approach)  will best achieve the government’s combined 
objectives  of promoting  innovation while ensuring safety .  
Widespread adoption of AI is still in its early stages. To best promote innovation , our view is that 
a considered and coordinated approach to augment existing laws and develop new frameworks  
(where there are gaps in existing legislation for AI in high -risk settings) will minimise the 
 
 
