duplication , inconsistency and uncertainty which will invariably result  from the introduction of 
new standalone  legislation without due consideration of existing regulatory frameworks.  
Adopting Option 1  will also lessen the  regulatory  burden for business and shift  the focus to 
driving  adoption , building of customer trust and driving innovation  and should be designed in 
such a way which enables, rather than stifles  AI development .  
As stated in our submission to the Select Committee for Adopting Artificial Intelligence13: 
 
We recognise the need for regulation where guidance is not sufficient, for example to 
address legislative gaps where AI systems are used in high -risk settings. To that end, 
we strongly support  a considered and coordinated approach to augment existing laws 
and develop new frameworks. We think it would be useful for regulatory activity to be 
coordinated within an overarching framework focused on high -risk settings which need 
supplementation.  
 
Further, as we have previously stipulated, if the Government were to adopt Option  1, a closely  
co-ordinated approach to legislation in co -ordinated and related areas  will be required . For 
example, the recent introduction of the Privacy and Other Legislation Amendment Bill 2024 
would require  APP entities to include information about the kinds of personal information 
used in, and types of decisions made by, computer programs that use personal information to 
make decisions that could “reasonably be expected to significantly affect the rights or interests 
of an individual ”.  However, Guardrail 6  states  that “Organisations must inform people when 
AI is used to make or inform decisions relevant to them …”14 The Consultation Paper explains 
that “[t] his emphasises the important role deployers have in informing people when they are 
subject to decisions made using a high -risk AI system. This requirement applies to AI systems 
which are used to make decisions impacting people’s health, safety , and human rights. ” 
 
It is unclear the extent to which Guardrail  6 intersects with proposed reforms to the Privacy 
Act 1988 to enhance transparency a round  the use of personal information  in automated 
decisions . Guardrail 6 refers to when “AI is used to make or inform decisions relevant to 
[individuals] ”15, which is potentially a lower threshold than the proposed Privacy Act  drafting  
of where “personal information about an individual is used ” to make or  inform a decision . It 
is therefore unclear whether Guardrail 6  applies to decisions where  personal information  
about an individual is not used .  Further, if  an APP entity includes  the required information  
relating to use of  a high -risk AI system that performs  automated decision making  in its privacy 
policy, will this be sufficient to meet the first requirement of Guardrail 6 ? It is also unclear 
how specific AI legislative amend ments which are likely to result from the recently announced 
Safe and Responsible A rtificial Intelligence in Healthcare  – Legislation and Regulation Review  
by the Department of Health and Aged Care  will interact with the mandatory  guardrails 
proposed.  
 
 
