To avoid confusion and limit duplication  and the risk of inconsistent legislative requirements , 
it will be necessary  to consider  the existing regulatory framework holistically with any specific 
AI related amendments within subordinate  legislation held until the overarching reforms are 
agreed and finalised. This should minimise the fragmentation and duplication that flows from 
piecemeal approaches and the resulting burden for business which will shift focus from 
adoption and innovation.   
 
Among other things, we believe the requirements under the Online Safety Act framework in 
relation to the steps providers need to take regarding safe use of  generative AI and ‘high 
impact generative AI’ should be specifically re -considered and aligned irrespective of which AI 
regulatory framework is adopted , as they currently apply an alternative regulatory model  to 
a very broad area of the economy .  
 
The Government  should also consider how the voluntary AI Ethics Principles will co -exist 
alongside the mandatory guardrails.  We note that the AI Ethics Principles are ‘mapped’ to 
each guardrail  in the Voluntary AI Safety Standard , but it is unclear i f it is intended for them 
to become mandatory  for entities to adhere to  as part of the guardrails . This should be made 
clear, particularly as the Ethics Principles were developed as a voluntary set of principles  and 
making them mandatory in some respect would represent a change to that original intent.  If 
it is not the intent to make the ethics principles part of the Guardrails , the Government  should  
clarify how they fit into the broader environment .  If they are now effectively redundant  or 
subsumed by the guardrails , this should be made clear .  
 
5.2 Adopting Option 2 and 3 carries too many risks at this critical time  of innovation . 
 
Adopting a standalone Australia  AI Act (Option 3) without a detailed review of existing legislative 
frameworks is likely to lead to many organisations who are already subject to sector specific 
laws facing overlapping and potentially inconsistent obligations. We consider  Option 3 is  the 
least preferred option at this time  and we do not recommend this approach. We are still learning 
about the AI technology  and in our view, it is premature to adopt a standalone legislative 
framework.  In our view, the risks are far better addressed through our current frameworks . If 
there are harms that are identified as we improve our understanding of AI and the risks it poses,  
legislation should address those specific harms rather than stifle the innovation and use 
upfront.    
 
However, if the Government is minded to adopt a standalone AI act, we encourage Government 
to think carefully about the negative externalities imposed by the blanket application of 
guardrails to a wide range of AI systems  and create mechanisms to counteract  those 
externalities.   We also caution against adopting  an EU - style list for identifying high risk AI, we 
consider that  compiling a comprehensive list is an almost impossible task  given the pace at 
which AI technology and its uses is evolving.   The risk profile of use cases  may also change and Safe and Responsible AI in Australia – Proposals paper for introducing mandatory guardrails for AI in high -risk 
settings – Telstra Response   
  
 
 
 PAGE 12 
 
evolve over time, and the updating of lists in a timely manner will likely be problematic, 
potentially stifling innovation  or just becoming inadequate or redundant . 
 
If the Government  does proceed to adopt an Option 3 style of regulation, we also recommend 
the Government consider the following:  
• General c arve -outs : Acknowledgement in legislation that one or more of the guardrails 
should  not be applied in certain contexts, such as the examples outlined above.  A 
consultation on an initial list of carve -outs would be required, and then a  legislative 
mechanism and suitable funding and expertise would be required to create and 
maintain a published  list of carve -outs , which could be added to over time as the use 
of AI in the economy expands . 
• Exemptions  on application :  A legislative mechanism (perhaps via the proposed central 
regulator) to grant exemptions to the guardrails. This would need to have a suitable 
capacity and skillset to allow consideration of applications in a timely manner . 
• Subordination. The central legislation could be subordinated to particular sector or 
other  legislation, allowing primary legislation to override the guardrail requirements 
in situations where deemed appropriate. This may cause challenges if sector legislation 
does not appropriately respond to challenges such as those identified above.  
 
Whilst  we recognise that other jurisdictions  have adopted an approach akin to Option 3 (notably 
the EU), the impact on AI development, industry and compliance by business is yet to be fully 
appreciated and understood .  There remain  strong concerns regarding how easily businesses 
will be able to comply with the requirements  and strong concerns about the impact an AI act 
will have on innovation, with risks noted on the competitive disadvantage the EU may face 
relative to other jurisdictions with different regulatory frameworks.  
 
Similarly, we do not recommend the Government adopt Option 2  at this time . In the 
Consultation paper, the Government has proposed a  Framework approach under Option 2 . As 
the Consultation paper itself notes, … “(f )framework legislation is broadly defined and can take 
many different forms .” 16 The Consultation paper provides  insufficient detail to properly assess 
the efficacy of such an approach. If this approach is considered appropriate by the Government, 
we caution that it would need to appropriately consider which legislation prevails in the event 
of inconsistency and to manage potential duplicative regulatory administrative activities. This 
option also risks  the potential for poor outcomes if the framework legislation is too prescriptive 
and rigid.  
 
 
 
