6.1 Telstra supports the Voluntary AI Safety Standard in principle  but cautions against it 
being made  part of the  mandatory guardrails .   
Telstra supports the existence of the Voluntary AI Safety Standard  – it is a helpful guide to 
businesses looking for practical guidance on responsible AI .  However, it is unclear if the detail 
provided in this standard  is intended to be come part of the mandatory guardrails, to be applied 
prescriptively.  The similarity of the headings of most of the mandatory  guardrails gives this 
impression.  If this is the intent, we have concerns  that the literal application of some of these 
detailed requirements  would be excessive , unreasonably rigid,  and would materially stifle 
innovation . We illustrate with examples below where the Safety Standard describes activities 
which we would agree are generally  good practice, but which may not be appropriate to 
demand  in all high -risk situations.  
 
For example:  
• 1.2.9.  Where you anticipate developing AI systems internally, create and document 
the end -to-end process for AI system design and development.  
o This is proposing a single documented process for AI system design and 
development. While this may be good practice, in some businesses  it might be 
entirely appropriate to have multiple development processes. This would not 
necessarily create any additional risk, provided the multiple processes are all 
appropriate.  
• 2.1.1.  Create an organisational -level risk tolerance for the use of AI systems.  
o This appears to impose  a risk management taxonomy on organisations . If an 
organisation is only using AI within recruitment, for example, it might be 
entirely reasonable for this to be captured within the risk tolerance and profile 
for recruitment, HR, or staff  – a separate AI risk appetite or tolerance may just 
add confusion.   
• 2.1.2.  Create and document criteria to identify acceptable and unacceptable risks in 
relation to AI.   
o Similarly , to 2.1.1 , this imposes a structure on  an organisation’s risk 
management which might not fit the organisation’s operations and feels too 
rigid to apply broadly.  
• 3.1.5.  Create and document an organisation -wide process to support teams in the 
management of data usage rights for AI, including intellectual property, Indigenous 
Data Sovereignty, privacy, confidentiality and contractual rights.  
o While this may be appropriate in some circumstances, in some businesses it 
might be sufficient to have a narrow process – for example if only one 
department in the company is using AI . 
• 4.1.1.  Create and document organisation -wide processes and capability required for 
testing, monitoring, continuously evaluating, improving and reporting of AI systems.  
o Again, a requirement for things to be ‘organisation -wide’ feels excessive if 
applied broadly  – an organisation that has a small team operating  a single high -Safe and Responsible AI in Australia – Proposals paper for introducing mandatory guardrails for AI in high -risk 
settings – Telstra Response   
  
 
 
 PAGE 14 
 
risk AI system  with no ambition to do more simply does not require such a 
process.  
• 6.1.4. Create and document a process to apply the organisation’s responsibilities 
under this Standard to AI systems developed or provided by third parties … 
o While a documented process might be appropriate in situations where multiple 
high -risk AI Systems are being purchased, this feels excessive for a single AI 
system . 
• 9.1.1.  Create and maintain an up -to-date, organisation -wide inventory of each AI 
system  
o For some organisations a single registry may be appropriate, for others this 
may be excessive centralisation. For example, some businesses are not well 
integrated and may have separate finance, HR or IT functions. It would not be 
appropriate to demand that such an organisation produce a centralised 
inventory of AI systems.  
