CAIDP
has
taken
a
consistent
stance
in
favor
of
applying
principles
to
guide
AI
governance,
given
the
pace
and
unpredictability
of
advancing
AI
risks.
While
the
proposed
principles
capture
a
broad
range
of
high-risk
scenarios
for
AI,
we
urge
the
inclusion
of
additional
principles
to
address
gaps
which
will
lead
to
unacceptably
high-risk.
We
additionally
urge
clear
red
lines
prohibiting
systems
and
applications
which
have
already
been
determined
to
contravene
civil
and
human
rights,
as
we
will
describe
in
response
to
a
subsequent
question.
14
Australia’s
principles
for
classifying
high-risk
AI
consider
crucial
factors
such
as
adverse
impacts
on
individual
and
human
rights,
physical
and
mental
health,
safety,
and
adverse
impacts
to
individuals,
communities,
and
the
broader
society.
Such
risks
can
rapidly
manifest
in
seemingly
innocuous
AI
systems
that
fail
to
adhere
to
core
principles
of
identification
,
transparency
,
contestability
and
meaningful
human
oversight
.
Australia’s
AI
Ethics
Principles
15
,
published
by
DISR
as
a
“voluntary
framework”,
refers
to
the
principles
of
identification
and
transparency
,
stating,
“There
should
be
transparency
and
responsible
disclosure
so
people
can
understand
when
they
are
being
significantly
impacted
by
AI,
and
can
find
out
when
an
AI
system
is
engaging
with
them,”
additionally
promoting
the
principle
of
contestability
,
“When
an
AI
system
significantly
impacts
a
person,
community,
group
or
environment,
there
should
be
a
timely
process
to
allow
people
to
challenge
the
use
or
outcomes
of
the
AI
system.”
Yet,
for
AI
systems
currently
in
widespread
use,
even
their
developers
have
expressed
ignorance
as
to
