","Submission_CAIDP_AUSTRALIA_Mandatory_Guardrails_10042024.3938a3a3","Submission_CAIDP_AUSTRALIA_Mandatory_Guardrails_10042024.3938a3a3.pdf","pdf","C:\\Users\\james.barro\\Documents\\rsm-nvidia-hackathon\\data\\Submission_Docs\\Processed\\Submission_CAIDP_AUSTRALIA_Mandatory_Guardrails_10042024.3938a3a3.pdf","1"
"186","187","187","AI Institute, UNSW Sydney","https://consult.industry.gov.au/ai-mandatory-guardrails/submission/view/112","Prof Toby Walsh FAA FTSE Chief Scientist @ UNSW.ai 3 October 2024 Mandatory Guardrails for AI in High-Risk Settings Introduction1.0. This submission is provided by the AI Institute of the University of New South Wales, UNSW.ai. The AI Institute is the largest research institute in Australia devoted solely to artiﬁcial intelligence. Over 300 academics from across the university are connected to the Institute, developing and using AI for a broad range of applications. We welcome the opportunity to provide input on the proposals paper for introducing mandatory guardrails for AI in high-risk settings. 1.1. Artiﬁcial intelligence offers an unparalleled opportunity for Australia to improve economic, societal and environmental outcomes.  A recent study by Microsoft and the Tech Council of Australia estimated that generative AI alone could contribute as much as $115 billion a year to Australia’s economy by 2030. The broader use of AI within the Australian economy could grow GDP by around 20%. There is perhaps no other technological change arriving in the next couple of decades that will be as disruptive or as valuable.1.2. However, as with any opportunity, this comes with signiﬁcant associated risks. The greatest risk by far is that Australia fails to adopt AI quickly enough, permitting the ﬁnancial beneﬁts of AI to ﬂow offshore and, lacking sovereign AI capability, risking Australia to be held hostage by outside interests. Unfortunately, in the last 15 years, we have seen signiﬁcant declines in R&D investment in Australia. Worrying, we are the only major OECD economy that reduced its percentage of GDP invested in R&D. 1.3. Other countries are making signiﬁcantly larger investments in AI compared to Australia. The last ﬁve years have seen the Australian government announce an additional $142 million in the AI space. However, if we are to be competitive in this area, we need to invest much more in fundamental AI and safety research, and not just in translating existing research into practice. Current research funding levels are uncompetitive compared to other nations. For instance, Canada, which has a similar population and economy to Australia, just announced an additional $2.4 billion for AI research. Unless we lift our game, we will miss out. The consequences will be dire.AI INSTITUTE, UNSW SYDNEY 11.4. This submission outlines our recommendations for implementing mandatory guardrails for AI in high-risk settings, focusing on deﬁning high-risk AI, implementing guardrails, and choosing an appropriate regulatory approach.Executive Summary2.0. Our key recommendations are:•Adopt an impact-based approach to deﬁning high-risk AI, using principles rather than a rigid list.•Adapt existing legal frameworks to mandate the guardrails, but add some focused and new regulations to deal with new harms (e.g. for deepfakes and for biometrics). •Establish an AI Ofﬁce to provide expertise in monitoring and enforcing AI regulations.•Provide ﬁnancial support and incentives to business, especially SMEs, to deploy current AI technologies like generative AI responsibly.•Substantially increase investment in fundamental AI research and AI safety to ensure Australia proﬁts from future waves of AI innovation and leads the way with the responsible use of AI.•Support efforts to improve AI literacy among the workforce and general public.Deﬁning High-Risk AI3.0. We largely support the proposed principles-based approach to deﬁning high-risk AI. This ﬂexible framework can adapt to rapidly evolving AI capabilities better than a ﬁxed list of high-risk use cases. However, we recommend providing detailed guidance and examples to help organizations interpret and apply these principles.3.1. We also suggest that high-risk AI should encompass not just AI applications like predictive policing where the impacts on an individual can be large, but also applications where the impact on an individual might be small but the number of individuals impacted is large (e.g. AI used for assigning gig work to workers, or for workplace surveillance). We describe this as an impact-based approach to deﬁning high-risk AI. 3.2. The proposed principles appear comprehensive, covering key areas of potential harm. We suggest adding explicit consideration of:•Impacts on vulnerable populations•Potential for large-scale societal disruption•Risks to critical infrastructure and essential services3.2. Regarding general-purpose AI (GPAI) models, we believe they should be subject to mandatory guardrails due to their wide-ranging potential impacts. However, the deﬁnition of high-risk GPAI should be based on capabilities rather than arbitrary technical metrics like AI INSTITUTE, UNSW SYDNEY 2FLOPs. We recommend establishing an expert panel to assess GPAI models and determine risk levels.Mandatory Guardrails4.0. We broadly support the proposed mandatory guardrails for high-risk AI systems. These align well with international best practices and cover crucial aspects of responsible AI development and deployment. We offer the following suggestions: •Transparency: As well as mandating clear disclosure of AI use to end-users and affected individuals [Guardrail 6], we suggest that AI systems should be designed explicitly not to mislead users (see the proposal for “Turing Red Flag” laws, https://cacm.acm.org/opinion/turings-red-ﬂag/). Additionally AI generated outputs should be digitally watermarked to facilitate transparency.•Fairness and non-discrimination: We propose adding a guardrail about the need to assess and mitigate bias in AI systems.•Human oversight: We propose replacing Guardrail 5 with a simpler and more stringent Guardrail in which we “Require meaningful human control of high-risk AI systems”. We need control not just oversight. And we do not want to specify explicitly how to obtain this.•Security and robustness: We propose adding a guardrail to require appropriate cybersecurity measures and resilience to adversarial attacks.Regulatory Approach5.0. Of the options presented, we believe Option 1 (adopting the guardrails within existing regulatory frameworks as needed) offers the best balance of comprehensiveness, ﬂexibility and speed. However, we also see need for a few pieces of new and specialised AI regulation to deal with novel harms (for instance, to protect against the misuse of deepfakes, or against the misuse of facial recognition and other biometric recognition technologies). 5.1. We recommend that a dedicated AI Ofﬁce be established to provide technical expertise and coordinate enforcement of AI regulation. Existing regulators often lack appropriate expertise and resources to police the AI space. Additional Considerations6.0. Research and Development: To maintain Australia's competitiveness in AI, we strongly urge increased government investment in fundamental AI research and AI safety. This is crucial for developing sovereign AI capabilities with future waves of AI innovations, and fostering a thriving AI industry.AI INSTITUTE, UNSW SYDNEY 36.1. We recommend government provide ﬁnancial support and other incentives to business, especially SMEs, to deploy emerging AI technologies like generative AI responsibly and in line with the proposed guardrails.6.2. AI Literacy and Workforce Preparation: We recommend government support for initiatives to improve AI literacy among the general public and prepare the workforce for AI-driven changes. This could include funding for education programs and retraining initiatives. We point to initiatives like “Day of AI Australia” which we have been involved in, and which has engaged over 100,000 students in Australia over the last few years, with a focus on under-represented groups like indigenous and female students. 6.3. International Cooperation: Given the global nature of AI development, we encourage the government to pursue international cooperation on AI safety and governance. Alignment with key partners on core principles and standards will beneﬁt Australian businesses and researchers.Conclusion7.0. Introducing mandatory guardrails for high-risk AI is a crucial step in ensuring the safe and responsible development of this transformative technology. With a carefully crafted regulatory approach, Australia can foster innovation while protecting against potential harms. The AI Institute stands ready to provide technical support to the government in further developing and implementing these important measures.
AI INSTITUTE, UNSW SYDNEY 4","InquiryAIRisks.4ca07491","InquiryAIRisks.4ca07491.pdf","pdf","C:\\Users\\james.barro\\Documents\\rsm-nvidia-hackathon\\data\\Submission_Docs\\Processed\\InquiryAIRisks.4ca07491.pdf","1"
