"188","189","189","Demetrius Floudas","https://consult.industry.gov.au/ai-mandatory-guardrails/submission/view/110","
Demetrius Floudas
Published name
Demetrius Floudas
1. Do the proposed principles adequately capture high-risk AI?
No
Please provide any additional comments.
The principles are short-sighted, buzzword-influenced and in a couple of years they would be considered trivial & inconsequential, compared to what the real risks might be.
Are there any principles we should add or remove?
Yes
Please provide any additional comments.
1. Cognitive and social impacts: The long-term effects of widespread AI integration on human cognition, social interactions, and psychological well-being. (AI lovers - AI resurrections)
2. Geopolitical risks: The potential for AI to exacerbate international tensions.
3. Algorithmic governance: The risks associated with increasing reliance on AI systems for decision-making in adjudication & justice dispensing.
4. Existential risks:, The potential long-term risks of advanced AI systems surpassing human-level intelligence and control should be acknowledged and considered.
5. Cultural homogenisation: The risk of AI systems eroding cultural diversity and local knowledge systems through global standardization of information and decision-making processes.
Please identify any:
Uses that are meant to supplant human interaction at a deeply substantial emotional & psychosocial framework: AI erotic partners; AI emulations of the dead
2. Do you have any suggestions for how the principles could better capture harms to First Nations people, communities and Country?
Yes
Please provide any additional comments.
The current principles disregard the most egregious points of risk from AI, expected to become prevalent within the next 3-5 years, by current extrapolation.
3. Do the proposed principles, supported by examples, give enough clarity and certainty on high-risk AI settings and high-risk AI models? Is a more defined approach, with a list of illustrative uses, needed?
NO a more defined list-based approach is needed
If you prefer a list-based approach (similar to the EU and Canada), what use cases should we include? How can this list capture emerging uses of AI?
How can this list capture emerging uses of AI?
It cannot! Are the usual tropes listed above the only ones that DISR can foresee as emerging risks?
4. Are there high-risk use cases that government should consider banning in its regulatory response (for example, where there is an unacceptable level of risk)?
Yes
If so, how should we define these?
BAN: - Uses that are meant to supplant human interaction at a deeply substantial emotional & psychosocial framework: AI erotic partners; AI emulations of the dead.
BAN: any involvement of AI in adjudication
BAN: military applications without human in the loop.
Please provide any additional comments.
There are risks of Unforeseen Impairment of Humanity (UIH) which indicatively include a) Intellectual Enfeeblement: that mankind’s cognitive abilities atrophy due to overreliance on these technologies; b) Cultural Annihilation: who will spend 2 years writing a novel or directing a motion picture if the Tool AI can do so in a few minutes? c) Interpersonal Degradation: nuances of live communication and emotional intelligence are compromised, as needing more effort than those with the machine.
5. Are the proposed principles flexible enough to capture new and emerging forms of high-risk AI, such as general-purpose AI (GPAI)?
No
Please provide any additional comments.
The principles provided are 4 years backdated. They will be of no help to the upcoming (and I mean in the next 1-45years) issues that will appear. The prospect of Artificial General Intelligence, presents society with unparalleled opportunities and risks. While narrow AI systems are currently integrated into many sectors, the creation of AGI could fundamentally reshape the future of humanity. This DISR paper does not take any consideration of the risks, ethical dilemmas, and governance challenges that arise with rapid, unexpected AI development. It does not for a moment outline the urgency of regulation to prevent catastrophic outcomes, by analysing current strategies and providing a vision for AI global cooperation akin to nuclear non-proliferation treaties.
6. Should mandatory guardrails apply to all GPAI models?
Yes
Please provide any additional comments.
A primary focus of the debate surrounding Large Language Models and their impact on the legal profession has thus far centred on the repercussions for legal practitioners. Yet, lawyers are but auxiliaries in the adjudication process; it is the judiciary that dispenses justice in concreto. What the research concerning legal automation neglects (this includes UNESCO’s draft principles) is that AI-based legal advice becomes more accessible and affordable, the volume of cases brought before the courts shall increase exponentially. Consider this for a moment:
“Pattern recognition, information retrieval, and rapidly processing written language make up the majority of legal research and contract review. A study found that a human lawyer takes 91 minutes on average to read five NDAs and extract the relevant information. Conversely, an AI can do the same — with 94% accuracy, as compared to humans’ 85% accuracy — in 26 seconds.” in: ‘Will Artificial Intelligence Kill All The Lawyers?’, NewtonX .
It appears that the Australian drafters are lagging 4 years behind the present times: indeed has anyone taken heed of the hundreds of LLM-based justice apps that appear every month in dedicated hackathons around the world? I have been a speaker to half a dozen of them in 2024. They all promise (and soon they will deliver, without a doubt) quick, affordable, direct, no-intermediary-needed, fully-considered and ready-to-submit claims for every plaintiff or defendant that has a real or imaginary dispute. As a senior practicing advocate, I could charge 200 EUR + court fees per case to simply feed these submissions that hundreds of clients have created in their own apps into the e-court management system. Easily 1000 such cases a month could come my way and I would be 20,000 EUR per month richer, whilst all the work is done by a secretary.
Has the Government even considered what this entails? The most phenomenal jam-packed legal congestion in history: courts receiving hundreds of applications per hour! There are two possible responses to this: either we institute a ring-fencing justice strategy in order to drastically curtail such unencumbered access to the courts (with all the problems it will imply, but now is not the time for this analysis) OR the beleaguered and swamped judiciary will have to introduce a modicum of formal AI adjudication to deal with the backlog.
Such an eventuality may only be 2-6 years away but unfortunately the draft guardrails guidelines are not par for the course to even explore the matter, let alone provide some authoritative guidance for the near future.
7. What are suitable indicators for defining GPAI models as high-risk?
Base on technical capability
What technical capability should it be based on?
Other
8. Do the proposed mandatory guardrails appropriately mitigate the risks of AI used in high-risk settings?
No
Please provide any additional comments.
Qualitative risk thresholds are good as principles but inutile as response-triggering mechanisms. How would it become possible to safeguard from risks that are inherent in AI dissemination and utilisation when these originate from low-capability systems (below all thresholds) either a) operating in unintended fashion or b) performing perfectly lege artis, but in a perceptibly detrimental manner?
Are there any guardrails that we should add or remove?
Yes
What guardrails should we add or remove?
Undertake conformity assessments to demonstrate and certify compliance with the guardrails Keep and maintain records to allow third parties to assess compliance with guardrails Establish processes for people impacted by AI systems to challenge use or outcomes Enable human control or intervention in an AI system to achieve meaningful human oversight Be transparent with other organisations across the AI supply chain about data, models and systems to help them effectively address risks Inform end-users regarding AI-enabled decisions, interactions with AI and AI-generated content Test AI models and systems to evaluate model performance and monitor the system once deployed Protect AI systems, and implement data governance measures to manage data quality and provenance Establish and implement a risk management process to identify and mitigate risks
Please provide any additional comments
The first instances of effective malicious use of AI systems are with certainty going to be based on open source models. Therefore this should receive paramount importance in assessing risk. The mitigation factors appear toothless and ideas such as 'restrictive licenses' will not matter for any malicious users anyhow.
Personally, I am of the opinion that open source AI models of higher capacity thna the current ones (cut-off point needs to be discussed in detail!) should be prohibited. It is difficult enough reining in the proprietary model creators - it will become impossible if high-capable models become available to every group and individual.
As a descriptive analogy, do we allow open-source coronavirus CRISPR kits in the belief that some unnamed and unregistered lab out there might do work that will benefit immunological research?
9. How can the guardrails incorporate First Nations knowledge and cultural protocols to ensure AI systems are culturally appropriate and preserve Indigenous Cultural and Intellectual Property?
n/a
10. Do the proposed mandatory guardrails distribute responsibility across the AI supply chain and throughout the AI lifecycle appropriately?
No
Please provide any additional comments
what about AI liability insurance?
11. Are the proposed mandatory guardrails sufficient to address the risks of GPAI?
No
12. Do you have suggestions for reducing the regulatory burden on small-to-medium sized businesses applying guardrails?
No
13. Which legislative option do you feel will best address the use of AI in high-risk settings?
A whole of economy approach – introducing a new cross-economy AI Act
14. Are there any additional limitations of options outlined in this section which the Australian Government should consider?
Yes
Please provide any additional comments.
It is evident that de lege ferenda any decree articulating AI risk thresholds should be in the position to partly mitigate against the risks of UIH. Like MDMA (Ecstasy: legal until the 80’s) or social media for the underage (as it seems in the process of occurring), UIH risks may only be conducive to regulation in the event of calamitous events which cause a shift in the level of public attention and elicit intense clamour for regulation.
15. Which regulatory option(s) will best ensure that guardrails for high-risk AI can adapt and respond to step-changes in technology?
A framework approach – Introducing new framework legislation to adapt existing regulatory frameworks across the economy
16. Where do you see the greatest risks of gaps and inconsistencies with Australia’s existing laws for the development and deployment of AI?
In view of the very rapid advance of model capabilities coupled with the exponential expansion of their penetration, AI risks (even the more remote ones) could easily become intractable. This represents a sui generis escalatory frame of reference that regulatory instruments would be implausible to control currently, let alone henceforward influence.
Which regulatory option best addresses this?
A whole of economy approach – Introducing a new cross-economy AI-specific Act (for example, an Australian AI Act).
Make a general comment
Transposing AI risk thresholds into hard law should represent the distal objective of the outcome of this consultation. Thus, the resulting policies should be intended as actionable, instead of consultative or discretionary. Such a goal shall inform the drafting approach accordingly. It is paramount to distinguish between mere toothless recommendations and bona-fide legal obligation.
In order to be actionable, all policy guidelines ought to be as clear as possible, unambiguous, easily communicable, devoid of excessive technical jargon, based on underlying strategic principles, consistent, proportionate and authoritative.
Codes of Practice for companies and developers are simply not going to be of much use. Vested interests, a profit-oriented mission and spiralling competitive pressures will render it inconsequential in a short while. A binding UN-mediated International Treaty must be the ultimate goal, however we remain very far from such an eventuality therefore the intermediate step of (hopefully) approximated national regulation should be sought at this stage.

