Canberra , Australia  
RE: Safe and responsible AI in Australia  Proposals paper for introducing mandatory  guardrails for AI in 
high-risk settings  
Dear Mr. Husic,  
The AdvaMed Medical Imaging Division represents the manufacturers of medical imaging equipment, 
including, magnetic resonance imaging (MRI), medical X -Ray equipment, computed tomography (CT) 
scanners, ultrasound, nuclear imaging, radiopharmaceuticals, and imaging information systems. Our 
members have introduced innovative medical imaging technologies for use by healthcare providers, and they 
play an essential role in health care infrastructure and the care pathways of screening, staging, evaluating, 
managin g, and effectively treating patients with cancer, heart disease, neurological degeneration, COVID -19, 
and numerous other medical conditions.  
 
We appreciate the opportunity to provide input on the Australian Government's consultation regarding the 
regulation of artificial intelligence (AI) and its impact on medical devices. We commend the Government’s 
recognition of the robust regulatory framewor k already established by the Therapeutic Goods Administration 
(TGA) and offer the following comments:  
 
AI-Enabled Medical Devices Are Regulated Differently from Other  AI-Enabled Healthcare Products  
It is crucial to distinguish between TGA -regulated  AI-enabled  medical devices and other AI products used in 
healthcare  that are not regulated by TGA. TGA has a robust, risk -based regulatory framework which 
comprehensively addresses the unique risks and benefits associated with medical devices . AI-enabled 
medical devices are already subject to stringent pre - and post -market regul atory requirements, ensuring their 
safety, efficacy, and benefit -risk profile.   
 
 Page 2 of 2 
 
 TGA Should Continue to Be the Regulator for AI -Enabled Medical Devices  
The TGA has extensive experience regulating medical devices, including those incorporating AI 
technologies, across their entire lifecycle. TGA’s risk -based regulatory framework already considers the 
evolving nature of AI, ensuring patient safety while prom oting innovation. Applying additional, cross -sector  
AI regulations to medical devices would result in duplicative oversight, legal uncertainties, potential barriers 
to medical device innovation , and limit patient access to important medical device technolo gies. We strongly 
encourage the Australian Government to defer to TGA  on the regulation of AI -enabled medical devices .  
 
Not All AI -Enabled Medical Devices Should Be Categorized as High -Risk  
AI-powered medical devices vary significantly in their risk profiles. The proposed classification of all AI 
products and services affecting individual and public health and safety  as high -risk does not account for the 
diverse nature of AI-powered medical devices. Many AI -enabled medical devices enhance healthcare 
delivery without posing significant risk  to health or safety . TGA’s existing classification system already 
ensures that higher -risk devices are subject to appropriate regulatory scrutiny while lowe r-risk devices are 
regulated proportionally. We recommend that AI -enabled medical devices continue to be evaluated by 
TGA’s well-established  risk-based framework . 
 
In conclusion, we urge the Australian Government to continue leveraging TGA’s well -established regulatory 
framework for AI -enabled medical devices, recognizing the unique considerations these devices entail. By 
allowing TGA to remain the regulator for all medical devices,  including AI -enabled medical devices,  
Australia can maintain high standards of safety while fostering innovation in healthcare technologies.  
 
Thank you for considering our comments. We look forward to continued collaboration on this important 
issue.  
 
Sincerely , 
 
 
 
 
Zack  Hornberger  
Senior  Director,  Digital  Health  & Imaging  Technology  
