"195","196","196","Steven Deng","https://consult.industry.gov.au/ai-mandatory-guardrails/submission/view/101","
Steven Deng
Published name
Steven Deng
1. Do the proposed principles adequately capture high-risk AI?
No
Please provide any additional comments.
While the current principles provide a good starting point, they do not fully capture high-risk AI. Regulatory pathways should be established to classify systems as “high-risk” based on technical evaluation at multiple stages, including before they are trained, deployed, and while they are being used.
Unexpected or unintended behaviours in AI systems can develop without technical evaluation, and these capabilities may not be discovered or managed until after an AI system is deployed. Therefore, a mechanism for assessing actual capabilities and behaviours is essential in any regulation.
Technical evaluation provides certainty when the general principles do not clearly capture an AI system, and this will allow for a reduction in regulatory burden on AI systems proven not to have dangerous capabilities.
Are there any principles we should add or remove?
Yes
2. Do you have any suggestions for how the principles could better capture harms to First Nations people, communities and Country?
No
3. Do the proposed principles, supported by examples, give enough clarity and certainty on high-risk AI settings and high-risk AI models? Is a more defined approach, with a list of illustrative uses, needed?
NO a more defined list-based approach is needed
If you prefer a list-based approach (similar to the EU and Canada), what use cases should we include? How can this list capture emerging uses of AI?
Critical infrastructure Products and services affecting individual and public health and safety Employment Access to essential public services and products Administration of justice and democratic processesLaw enforcement
How can this list capture emerging uses of AI?
The current principles are insufficient in capturing new and emerging forms of high-risk AI. I recommend that Australia adopt the EU AI Act’s definitions of GPAI and GPAI with systemic risk for consistent and future-proofed regulations.
The EU AI Act distinguishes between GPAI and GPAI with systemic risk and provides specific detail for classifying an AI system as a GPAI with systemic risk. This definition would allow Australia to prioritise and target the regulation of systems with particularly dangerous capabilities.
4. Are there high-risk use cases that government should consider banning in its regulatory response (for example, where there is an unacceptable level of risk)?
Yes
If so, how should we define these?
High-risk AI systems that could cause “critical harm” or pose “systemic risks” should be banned. For instance, California’s SB1047 bill focused on avoiding “critical harm” that could lead to mass casualties, significant damage, or comparable harm to public safety.
The EU AI Act and the International Dialogues on AI Safety Consensus Statement both call for stringent restrictions or “red lines” on AI capability. If a technical evaluation shows that an AI model could assist in developing weapons, or could engage in self-replication, deception and loss of control, it should be urgently banned.
5. Are the proposed principles flexible enough to capture new and emerging forms of high-risk AI, such as general-purpose AI (GPAI)?
No
Please provide any additional comments.
The current principles are insufficient in capturing new and emerging forms of high-risk AI. I recommend that Australia adopt the EU AI Act’s definitions of GPAI and GPAI with systemic risk for consistent and future-proofed regulations.
The EU AI Act distinguishes between GPAI and GPAI with systemic risk and provides specific detail for classifying an AI system as a GPAI with systemic risk. This definition would allow Australia to prioritise and target the regulation of systems with particularly dangerous capabilities.
6. Should mandatory guardrails apply to all GPAI models?
No
Please provide any additional comments.
Guardrails should be proportionate to the risks posed by the AI system. Following the EU AI Act, we should distinguish between GPAI and GPAI with systemic risk, applying the strictest mandatory guardrails to GPAI with systemic risk.
Ensuring safety and building trust in GPAI systems should be a top priority for the Government and developers. A targeted set of mandatory guardrails is necessary to address the catastrophic or unknown risks from future AI models while balancing safety and innovation.
7. What are suitable indicators for defining GPAI models as high-risk?
Base on technical capability
What technical capability should it be based on?
Other
8. Do the proposed mandatory guardrails appropriately mitigate the risks of AI used in high-risk settings?
No
Please provide any additional comments.
The proposed guardrails do not adequately mitigate the risks of AI used in high-risk settings. AI poses a regulatory challenge, as it is ubiquitous and potentially catastrophic.
A single set of guardrails applying to all AI systems used in high-risk settings will lead to both over-regulation and under-regulation. We need tailored guardrails that match the extent of the risks.
Are there any guardrails that we should add or remove?
Yes
What guardrails should we add or remove?
10. Do the proposed mandatory guardrails distribute responsibility across the AI supply chain and throughout the AI lifecycle appropriately?
No
Which of the guardrails should be amended?
Please provide any additional comments
The current guardrails do not appropriately distribute responsibility. They should focus more obligations on developers as they control the majority of AI risks. The MIT AI Risk Repository and National Institute of Standards highlight that decisions during model development are key to safe and responsible AI.
11. Are the proposed mandatory guardrails sufficient to address the risks of GPAI?
No
How could we adapt the guardrails for different GPAI models, for example low-risk and high-risk GPAI models? 
The current guardrails are not sufficient to address the risks posed by GPAI. We need to prepare for future systems with catastrophic risks and dangerous behaviours such as weapons development, persuasion, deception, and self-replication.
13. Which legislative option do you feel will best address the use of AI in high-risk settings?
A whole of economy approach – introducing a new cross-economy AI Act
What opportunities should the Government take into account in considering each approach? 
I believe that introducing a cross-economy AI Act would be the most effective legislative option. Research shows that AI developers are best positioned to control the most severe risks from AI during model development.
Regulating offshore activity can be challenging, but it is crucial. An AI-specific Act would have enforceable provisions targeting offshore developers. An Australian AI Act should work in conjunction with established regulators to cover any gaps and focus on shaping the behaviour of offshore AI developers.
15. Which regulatory option(s) will best ensure that guardrails for high-risk AI can adapt and respond to step-changes in technology?
A whole of economy approach – introducing a new cross-economy AI Act
16. Where do you see the greatest risks of gaps and inconsistencies with Australia’s existing laws for the development and deployment of AI?
Regulating offshore activity can be challenging, but it is crucial. An AI-specific Act would have enforceable provisions targeting offshore developers. An Australian AI Act should work in conjunction with established regulators to cover any gaps and focus on shaping the behaviour of offshore AI developers.
Which regulatory option best addresses this?
A whole of economy approach – Introducing a new cross-economy AI-specific Act (for example, an Australian AI Act).
Please explain why.  
Research shows that AI developers are best positioned to control the most severe risks from AI during model development. An AI-specific Act would have enforceable provisions targeting offshore developers and is only possible through a whole of economy approach.

