Dear Sir/Madam  
Ernst & Young Australia (EY) welcomes the opportunity to contribute to the Proposals paper for 
introducing mandatory guardrails for AI in high -risk settings (Proposals Paper) consultation. Our views 
have been informed by our global and local subject matter resources, including EY’s Indigenous Sector 
Practice, our experience in Responsible AI, Digital Assurance, Risk & Compliance, AI Strategy, and our 
track -record of supporting clients  on their AI transformation journey across sectors and regions.  
Australia has the potential to position itself as a global leader in responsible AI innovation by 
harnessing its education system capabilities, renewable energy resources for sustainable AI, and 
research investment. By creating a regulatory environment tha t adapts to technological 
advancements,  promotes risk mitigation,  foster s an entrepreneurial culture , and supports  research, 
Australia can develop sovereign AI capability and strengthen its leadership in the development and 
use of AI .  
Our response advocates for adequate minimum safeguards to build trust in AI and ensure public 
safety, while promoting productivity growth and innovation. We emphasise the importance of aligning 
with international frameworks to ensure interoperability and clarify roles across the AI supply chain. 
Among the proposed regulatory options, Option 2 is ou r preferred alternative as it addresses the need 
for consistent definitions, clear risk assessment measures, and strong regulatory oversight, without 
overburdening low -risk AI or hindering innovation, and avoiding overlaps.  
 
We also advocate for a holistic  approach to  AI risk assessment , one that considers broader social and 
environmental impacts, especially for marginalised communities. This aligns with a report1 recently 
published by EY, in collaboration with Devex, which highlights the importance of creating a strong 
enabling  environment for AI, with the right policies to guide AI efforts, align with sustainability 
targets, and ensure safety before implementing AI initiatives.  
 
We are grateful for the opportunity to participate in the consultation . Please do not hesitate to 
contact Katherine Boiciuc ( EY Oceania Chief Technology and Innovation Officer ) at 
Katherine.Boiciuc@au.ey.com  or Christina Larkin ( EY Oceania Assurance Digital Trust Leader ) at 
Christina.Larkin@au.ey.com  should you have any questions or need  further information.  
 
 
 
 
