Page 15 
Regarding Guardrail 9 , our recommendation is to maintain detailed records of how Indigenous 
knowledge and data are used, including consent agreements, cultural protocols followed, and any 
benefits shared with Indigenous communities.  
► How IDS is integrated:  Records must include: FPIC agreements, protocols for managing cultural 
data, documentation of shared benefits with Indigenous communities. These records allow 
Indigenous communities to assess whether their cultural and intellectual property is respected 
and to ensure compliance with IDS principles.  
Lastly, for Guardrail 10 , our recommendation is to ensure that AI systems undergo culturally informed 
conformity assessments, co -developed with Indigenous communities, to certify that they respect 
Indigenous cultural protocols and sovereignty.  
► How IDS is integrated:  Conformity assessments must be co -designed with First Nations 
communities, ensuring the certification process includes checks for cultural appropriateness, 
protection of Indigenous knowledge, and adherence to Indigenous governance protocols. This 
ensures compliance and promotes transparency and accountability in how AI systems interact 
with Indigenous knowledge and data.  
Incorporating IDS values and principles into the proposed AI guardrails is essential for ensuring that AI 
systems are culturally appropriate and respect ICIP. Guardrails that include Indigenous -led governance 
structures, FPIC, culturally informed risk assessments, and transparent data management protocols 
will foster responsible AI systems that protect IK, preserve cultural integrity, and ensure that AI 
technologies contribute to the empowerment of Indigenous communities.  
10. Do the proposed mandatory guardrails distribute responsibility across the AI supply chain 
and throughout the AI lifecycle appropriately? For example, are the requirements assigned to 
developers and deployers appropriate?  
 
EY believe s the current proposal lacks clear definitions of risk assessment responsibilities within the AI 
supply chain and does not  identify all relevant actors in the AI ecosystem. To address these issues , 
several points should be considered . Regulators  may want to consider tiers of accountability, imposing 
the most stringent requirements for developers  and providers , less stringent requirements for 
vendors who incorporate these models into their products,  and applying  simpler, yet effective, rules 
for deployers at the end of the AI supply chain. While r esponsibilities should be shared between 
developers and deployer s, it is important to note that  deployers may not have access to the necessary 
technical documentation to comply with all guardrails, which co uld lead  to enforcement difficulties.  
Additionally , requiring all entities involved in AI development to conduct risk assessments could 
overwhelm smaller players and stifle innovation. Instead, responsibilities across the AI supply chain 
and AI lifecycle should be clarified, understanding stakeholders ’ roles and potential impacts, and 
showing a more balanced approach to assign liabilities on deployers, who are implementing the 
technology, while ensuring transparency and accountability obligations on developers.  On this note, 
solely classifying any AI system as high -risk based on the deployer's intention to use it in a high -risk 
setting may be one -sided and overly restrictive. A broader approach, akin to off -label prescribing of 
medicines, could also help allocate responsibility on deployers, whi le placing proportional obligations 
on developers to reasonably anticipate applications of their technology.  A member firm of Ernst & Young Global Limited  
Liability limited by a scheme approved under Professional Standards Legislation  
 
 Department of Industry, Science and Resources  
Page 16 
Moreover , importers and distributors of AI systems should be identified regarding their roles in the AI 
supply chain since they may have unique access to important information regarding aspects such as 
the scale and of distribution of the AI system and the primary po pulations that are exposed to their 
use (including if an AI system is widely deployed to First Nations).  
For higher risk GPAI, it would be recommended to impose specific obligations on deployers to carry 
out fundamental rights impact assessments before use, like those found in the EU AI Act for GPAI 
posing systemic risks. These assessments should include a de tailed plan outlining measures to 
mitigate any identified risks to fundamental rights, thereby promoting a responsible and ethical 
approach to AI deployment.  
11. Are the proposed mandatory guardrails sufficient to address the risks of GPAI? How could 
we adapt the guardrails for different GPAI models, for example low -risk and high -risk GPAI 
models?  
 
As detailed in  the response to earlier questions, given the diverse uses of GPAI models, EY believes it 
is crucial to adopt a differentiated approach based on capabilities to avoid imposing excessive burdens 
on low -risk models. Additionally, the scope of these guardrails should be clearly defined and 
measurable to ensure effective implementation and compliance.  
One area in which the proposed mandatory guardrails may not be sufficient to address the risks of 
GPAI is regarding the potential use of such systems for purposes that are prohibited under existing 
legislation. Whereas this may not require specific mention  for Narrow AI since the existing prohibitions 
apply regardless of whether AI is used or not, the general -purpose nature of GPAI can mean that 
special attention must be given to restrict the possibility of using the GPAI models for prohibited uses. 
GPAI mo dels may require an additional guardrail mandating that developers demonstrate that 
sufficient safeguards are in place to block abuse of the GPAI model for prohibited purposes.  
12. Do you have suggestions for reducing the regulatory burden on small -to-medium sized 
businesses applying guardrails?  
 
The success of regulations relies on the effectiveness of their implementation. While overly strict rules 
could hinder the Australian Government's efforts to advance AI capabilities and support industry 
growth, particularly for small -to-medium sized businesses (SMEs), regulations that are too lenient 
could pose risks to public safety. Therefore, it is crucial to create clear and supportive pathways that 
encourage business acceleration while enabling SMEs to imple ment safety guardrails without undue 
burden.  
The Australia n Government  could consider and build on the EU and Singapore’s tailored support for 
SMEs and start -ups, all while ensuring that public safety is not compromised through:  
► Regulatory sandboxes:  The EU AI Act includes specific provisions to support SMEs and start -ups, 
notably through free and prioritised access to regulatory sandboxes. These sandboxes (Article 
57, Chapter VI) will allow eligible SMEs to develop, test and validate AI systems under  a 
controlled environment before market release, while offering compliance guidance. Likewise, A member firm of Ernst & Young Global Limited  
Liability limited by a scheme approved under Professional Standards Legislation  
 
 Department of Industry, Science and Resources  
Page 17 
Singapore ’s Generative AI Sandbox15 supports SMEs in enhancing their marketing, sales, and 
customer engagement through tailored AI solutions. By providing grant support for SMEs to trial 
these solutions, the government facilitates practical experience, driving productivity among local 
busin esses , and strengthens collaboration between public and private sectors.  
► Simplified compliance process:  The EU AI Act allows SMEs to meet the requirement for high -risk 
AI systems by submitting detailed technical documentation in a simplified form (Article 11, 
Section 2, Chapter III). Implementing a similar measure would require efforts to pre -establish a 
simplified standardised template.  
► Direct support with compliance obligations:  Certain obligations, such as Guardrail 1 obligations 
for staff training may be an area where direct government support through training subsidies or 
provision of courses may help reduce the regulatory compliance burden for SMEs while 
simultaneously helpin g to safeguard the public.  
► Reduced compliance costs and penalties:  The EU AI Act features reduced fees for conformity 
assessments for high -risk AI systems (Article 62, Chapter VI) and outlines adjusted penalties for 
non-compliance. This approach seeks to lower financial barriers and foster innovation based on 
the conside ration of smaller players ’ needs.  
► Communication channels and capabilities development:  Another provision of the EU AI Act 
includes communication channels and tailored training programmes to assist SMEs with 
implementing the Act. In Singapore, Enterprise Singapore has also partnered with the tech 
industry to introduce new AI resources aimed at helping SMEs access AI solutions and drive 
business growth through developing digitalisation capabilities.16 
Despite these supportive measures, the Australian Government should develop clear and effective 
guidelines to reduce uncertainty and prevent slowdowns in innovation. This is particularly important 
during the transition period between the release of the man datory guardrails and their enforcement.  
