This approach should ensure regulatory interoperability and maintain the flexibility needed to keep 
pace with the dynamic nature of AI and the growing body of evidence around it. It should also be 
pragmatic enough to address AI's challenges while maximisin g its benefits and opportunities.  
In enhancing AI capabilities across both public and private sectors, it will be beneficial to engage with 
existing industry regulatory authorities, as well as leverage and adapt current regulatory structures 
and frameworks. This approach not only draws upo n a wealth of established knowledge and guidance 
but also supports a more streamlined integration of AI technologies. While recognising the time -
intensive nature of developing AI competencies, it is worth considering the value of existing expertise 
in the field. This can help in efficiently navigating the regulatory landscape avoiding overlaps and 
implementation pitfalls.  
The regulatory strategy should also account for factors that could affect outcomes, such as 
sociopolitical impacts, election cycles, and the availability of complementary tools and resources, 
including government or industry led guidelines, regulatory sand boxes, and other frameworks. These 
additional resources are essential for ensuring adequate transparency, accountability, and effective 
risk management in the development and deployment of AI.  
Such a measured approach aims to foster a regulatory environment that is both responsive and 
adaptable to the risks posed by high -risk AI, while ensuring that efforts are not duplicated.  
16. Where do you see the greatest risks of gaps and inconsistencies with Australia’s existing 
laws for the development and deployment of AI?  
 
EY agrees with the gaps identified in the Proposals Paper regarding the regulation of AI across the 
Australian landscape. We support a principles -based regulatory approach akin to that of the Privacy 
Act, which would provide a flexible yet robust framework capable of adapting to the rapid evolution of 
AI te chnologies.  
Should the Australian Government consider Options 2 or 3, we propose that a single nominated 
regulator , such as the Office of the Australian Information Commissioner ( OAIC ), be appointed to 
oversee AI regulation imposing prohibitions, and  general or standardising criteria across Australia. 
However, we also acknowledge the Government 's challenges in regulating AI due to jurisdictional 
limitations and suggest that leveraging the constitutional power, as done with the Privacy Act 1988 
(Cth)  (Privacy Act), could offer a viable pathway to establish a more uniform and effective regulatory 
regim e. 
Privacy and Data Protection  
► Consent  
Australian Privacy Principle (APP) 3 and 6 require organisations to obtain consent for the collection of 
sensitive information, and for the use of the personal information for any secondary purposes. AI A member firm of Ernst & Young Global Limited  
Liability limited by a scheme approved under Professional Standards Legislation  
 
 Department of Industry, Science and Resources  
Page 19 
systems often rely on the processing of substantial amount of personal data, and the nature of this 
processing can be complex and multifaceted, making it difficult for individuals to provide informed 
consent at the time of collection. On this note, 68% of Australians are worried their personal data will 
be used to train AI without their permission or consent17. 
The proposed mandatory guardrails 5, 6 and 7 are a good starting point in requiring organisations to 
have meaningful human oversight over the AI systems and requiring these organisations to inform 
end-users about AI -enabled decisions and establish processe s for individuals to challenge AI use or 
outcomes. However, the mandatory guardrails lack a requirement for organisations to offer end -users 
the ability to opt out of automated processing, or to provide explicit consent for such processing. This 
means that  individuals may not have full control over how their personal information is used by AI 
systems, which could lead to situations where the individuals' data is used in ways they did not 
anticipate or agree to, potentially undermining the consent requiremen ts under the Privacy Act and 
the APPs.  
While Guardrail 7 provides individuals with the ability to challenge decisions, it is a recourse after the 
fact and does not pre -emptively address the consent issue. To bridge this gap, it may be necessary to 
consider additional measures that align with th e spirit of the mandatory guardrails and the proposed 
privacy reforms. For example, enhancing the transparency of AI systems (as suggested by Guardrail 
6) could be coupled with mechanisms that allow individuals to provide informed consent before their 
data  is used for automated processing. This could involve clear explanations of the AI decision -making 
process, the types of data used and the potential implications for the individual.  
► Transparency  
Under APP 1.3 and 1.4, organisations must take reasonable steps to ensure individuals are aware of 
the collection and handling of their personal information. In the context of AI, transparency becomes 
more complex due to the technical nature of AI systems,  particularly those with a ""black box"" aspect 
where the decision -making process is not easily interpretable. Guardrail 5, which enables human 
control or intervention in an AI system to achieve meaningful human oversight across the life cycle, 
intersects wi th the transparency requirements of the APPs by ensuring decisions are interpretable and 
subject to human review.  
However, there is uncertainty on the practical application of this Guardrail, particularly those with a 
""black box"" aspect where the decision -making process is not easily interpretable. When it is not clear 
how decisions are made, it's challenging to hold the system or the entities that deploy it accountable 
for those decisions. This can lead to issues with compliance, especially in regulated industries.  
► International Data Transfers  
APP 8 governs the cross -border disclosure of personal information, requiring organisations to take 
reasonable steps to ensure that overseas recipients do not breach the APPs. The mandatory 
guardrails are currently aimed at developers and deployers of high -risk AI. However, the Proposals  
Paper is not clear on whether it only applies to developers and deployers based in Australia.  
 
