In the event that international developers may not be subject to the mandatory guardrails, deployers 
in Australia could face practical challenges in ensuring compliance with both the guardrails and APP 8. 
This highlights the importance of deployers careful ly crafting their contracts with developers to 
include provisions that align with the guardrails and the APPs.  
Contracts should explicitly stipulate data protection obligations, processes for data governance, and 
mechanisms for accountability that are consistent with the mandatory guardrails. They should also 
outline the expectations for transparency in data handli ng and the rights of individuals to challenge AI -
driven decisions, even when these functions are executed by international developers. By doing so, 
deployers can mitigate the risks associated with international data transfers and ensure that the AI 
systems  they utilise are compliant with Australian privacy regulations, regardless of where the 
developers are based.  
Intellectual Property  
► Copyright and Patents  
Sections 31 and 32 of the Copyright Act 1968 (Cth)  (Copyright Act) protects original literary, 
dramatic, musical, and artistic works. However, it does not explicitly address whether AI -generated 
works can be considered 'original' since they are not created by a human.  
Under recent Australia case law 18 and regulation 3.2C of the Patents Regulations 1991 (Cth) (Patent 
Regulations) and section 15 of the Patents Act 1990 (Cth)  (Patents Act), for an invention to be 
patentable, it must be a manner of manufacture resulting from human ingenuity. As AI begins to 
autonomously generate inventions, it challenges the traditional requirement of human inventorship, 
raising questions about  the patentability of AI -generated inventions.  
The mandatory guardrails do not explicitly address the issue of authorship for copyright purposes or 
inventorship for patents. This leaves a significant gap in how AI -generated works and inventions are 
recognised under IP law. The Copyright Act and the Pat ents Act both assume a human creator or 
inventor, which does not account for the capabilities of AI to create original works or inventions 
without direct human input. This gap creates uncertainty around the applicability of copyright 
protection and patenta bility to AI -generated outputs.  
 
