Executive Summary  
DLC Legal thanks the Department of Industry, Science and Resources for the opportunity 
to respond to the Safe and Responsible AI in Australia Proposals Paper for introducing 
mandatory guardrails for AI in high -risk settings September 2024  (Proposals Paper). We 
welcome the opportunity to assist the government build a sustainable future for all 
Australians enabled and enriched by science and technology.  
We support a risk -based approach to regulating AI, with a focus on high -risk settings and 
applications. Our response advocates for a cross -economy AI -specific Act as the most 
effective regulatory option, providing the necessary flexibility and adaptability to keep pace 
with rapidly evolving AI technologies.  
We emphasi se the importance of balancing innovation with safety and responsibility, 
suggesting tiered regulations based on risk levels and capabilities. Our response highlights 
the need for clear accountability structures, transparency in AI development and 
deploymen t, and mechanisms to address algorithmic bias and discrimination. We stress 
the importance of incorporating Indigenous data sovereignty and cultural protocols into the 
regulatory framework.  
DLC Legal recommends regular review and updating of the guardrails to ensure they 
remain relevant and effective. We suggest providing clear guidance and use cases to help 
organisations assess their AI systems against the principles. Our response underscores the 
importance of international alignment and collaboration in AI governance while   
  
3 
 
maintaining flexibility to address Australia's specific context and needs.  Accordingly , we 
provide detailed feedback on defining high -risk AI, the proposed mandatory guardrails, and 
regulatory options for implementation. Our recommendations aim to shape a robust, 
flexible, and effective regulatory framework for AI in Australia that balances in novation with 
safety, addresses ethical concerns, and ensures inclusivity. As AI continues to evolve and 
impact various aspects of society, we believe the proposed approach offers a solid 
foundation for responsible AI development and deployment in Australi a. 
Response to Questions for Consultation  
Question 1  
Do the proposed principles adequately capture high -risk AI? Are there any principles we 
should add or remove? Please identify any:  
• low-risk use cases that are unintentionally captured   
• categories of uses that should be treated separately, such as uses for defence or 
national security purposes.  
DLC Legal notes that t he government proposes  a principles  and risk-based approach to 
defining high -risk AI, rather than relying on a rigid list -based method. This approach allows 
for context -specific assessment of risks, considering factors such as impacts on human 
rights, health and safety, legal effects, impacts o n groups and cultural rights, as well as 
broader societal, economic, and environmental impacts. This framework provides a 
comprehensive method for assessing risk across different domains.  We recommend 
further consideration be given to whether more could be done with respect to liability and 
adverse consequences arising from end user interaction with AI systems and models.  
We also note that t he proposal recognises  the unique challenges posed by General 
Purpose AI (GPAI) models, particularly in terms of unforeseen applications and risks. The 
proposal suggests applying the guardrails to all GPAI models which we support. The 
proposal also seeks input from stakeholders on whether a subset of these models should 
be defined or classified as high-risk. The approach outlined in the Proposal aligns with 
developments in other jurisdictions, such as the EU, Canada, and the UK, with room to 
consider  Australia's specific context . AI technology is continuously evolving,  and we 
support the Government’s intention to conduct  regular review s of the guardrails and by 
extension, related definitions of high -risk AI, as AI technology advances.    
  
4 
 
We note that t he guardrails are intended to apply to both developers and deployers of high -
risk AI systems throughout the AI lifecycle. However, it is our view that  these guardrails may 
not (yet) sufficiently go far enough to  sufficiently  address broader societal impacts , 
including reducing the effects of bias built into AI technologies such as gender and racial 
bias nor do they (yet) go far enough to incorporate First Nations knowledge and cultural 
protocols. There is flexibility and scope in the proposed approach, however, in  our view, 
there is a need for more specific guidance , including non-exhaustive  examples to provide 
clarity on what constitutes high -risk AI together with  more positive concrete measures 
demonstrating how the very real risks of built in bias (or bias by design) will be mitigated . 
On balance,  the proposed approach provide s a robust foundation for capturing high -risk AI, 
with flexibility to adapt as needed. However, its effectiveness will depend on further 
refinement through consultation and ongoing review as AI technology evolves. Specifically , 
consultation to  strengthen certain aspects  around societal impacts and cultural 
considerations . 
Please identify any:  
• low-risk use cases that are unintentionally captured   
• categories of uses that should be treated separately, such as uses for defence or 
national security purposes.  
Low-risk use cases potentially captured:  The Proposal  allows for context -specific 
assessment, which will help avoid unintentionally capturing truly low -risk uses , which may 
be higher in volume . However, there may be a risk  that if the  guardrails are applied to all 
GPAI models , then lower-risk applications of general -purpose AI  may be caught in a bigger 
net than intended . 
Categories that should be treated separately:  The Proposal Paper  doesn’t appear to  
indicate explicit separate treatment for defen ce or national security AI applications. Given 
the sensitive nature of these domains, we recommend  treating them separately or 
providing specific guidance  as to how they should be treated . There may be a need for a 
separate, specific set of guardrails unique to defence and national security.  
Additional principles:  The current principles currently  focus heavily on risks to individuals , 
which is vitally important . However, f or risks to indiv iduals to be  considered in a wholistic 
context we recommend expanding consideration of broader societal or systemic risks from   
  
5 
 
AI systems , in particular with respect to ameliorating the very real, very tangible 
consequences  of bias built into AI technologies . 
Principles to remove:  We don’t recommend removing  any principles.  
Question 2  
Do you have any suggestions for how the principles could better capture harms to First 
Nations people, communities and Country?  
Following  our review of Proposal Paper  in conjunction with  the AIATSIS Code of Ethics, we 
make the following recommendations : 
Explicitly address data sovereignty and governance . It is important that First 
Nations peoples have control over data about them, their lands, and resources. We 
recommend direct incorporation  of Indigenous Data Sovereignty (IDSov) and 
governance  principles , that recognise First Nations peoples' rights to control data 
about their communities, cultures, and lands. This would provide vital  protection 
against misuse or exploitation of Indigenous data and knowledge . Several countries 
including New Zealand, expre ssly recognise the importance of IDSov in AI 
development and governance. For example , incorporation of Māori Data Sovereignty 
is being incorporated into AI governance discussions . Specifically , Māori Algorithmic 
Sovereignty (MASov) —algorithms using Māori data or applied to Māori 
individuals/collectives should be subject to Māori laws and governance 
structures —has been proposed . In Canada, there are also discussions about 
incorporating Indigenous knowledge systems and cultural protocols into AI 
development and regulation. However, t here are curr ently no specific algorithmic 
data sovereignty measures in place that explicitly protect Indigenous data 
sovereignty.  That said, in addition to ongoing discussions about incorporating 
Indigenous knowledge systems and cultural protocols into AI regulation , the First 
Nations principles of Ownership, Control, Access and Possession (OCAP) has been 
establish ed to set standards for how First Nations data should be collected, 
protected, used and shared . In addition, t he Collective benefit, Authority to control, 
Responsibility, Ethics (CARE) Principles for Indigenous Data Governance set 
minimum expectations for including Indigenous peoples in data governance . We 
acknowledge that these are  not algorithmic measures, however these  principles do 
support First Nations peoples asserting control over their data.    
  
6 
 
Expand First Nations environmental and cultural impacts . While the Code 
mentions impact on Country, this could be strengthened to more comprehensively 
address risks to environmental and cultural harms . For example,  impact on sacred 
sites, biodiversity, and  preserving the integrity of  traditional ecological knowledge  
should be expressly considered and addressed by the principles . In addition, t he 
principles should provide detailed guidance on protecting Indigenous cultural and 
intellectual property rights in  research contexts.  
Consider ation of economic and  intergenerational effects . The principles should 
expressly  consider the positive and negative long-term and intergenerational 
impacts of research . In doing so the Department should consider  the holistic and 
long-term approaches  of the First Nations peoples. In particular, t he principles 
could more directly acknowledge and provide guidance on addressing historical 
trauma stemming from past unethical research practices . The principles could 
more explicitly consider potential economic impacts (both positive and negative) on 
Indigenous communities.  
Address digital and AI technologies . Given the growing use of AI and digital 
technologies, the principles could specifically address ethical considerations 
around using these technologies in ways that respect Indigenous rights and cultural 
protocols.  Specifically, the Guardrails must address algorithmic bias and 
discrimination, which can disproportionately affect Indigenous and other 
marginali sed communities.  
Strengthen community benefit requirements . While the Code emphasi ses 
benefits to Indigenous communities, this could be further strengthened to require 
more concrete, community -defined benefits and mechanisms to ensure those 
Strengthen accountability mechanisms.  We acknowledge  the principles  
mentions accountability, this could be expanded to include more specific 
mechanisms for ensuring researchers and institutions are held accountable to 
Indigenous communities.  
Question 3  
Do the proposed principles, supported by examples, give enough clarity and certainty on 
high-risk AI settings and high -risk AI models? Is a more defined approach, with a list of 
illustrative uses, needed?    
  
7 
 
• If you prefer a list -based approach (similar to the EU and Canada), what use cases 
should we include? How can this list capture emerging uses of AI?  
• If you prefer a principles -based approach, what should we address in guidance to 
give the greatest clarity?  
The principles -based approach aims to determine if an AI system presents high risk based 
on its known or foreseeable applications and risks. This allows for context -specific 
assessment, with a focus on assessing the severity and extent of potential adverse impacts 
on individuals and their rights, including:  
• Impacts on human rights  
• Risk of built in bias  
• Risks to physical or mental health and safety  
• Adverse legal effects or significant impacts on individuals  
• Impacts on groups or cultural rights  
• Broader societal, economic, and environmental impacts   
This approach offers flexibility to capture emerging uses of AI, which a static list may 
struggle to do . However, we recommend  also including illustrative use cases within that 
approach  to achieve  clarity and certainty for businesses , developers and deployers  and 
users of AI systems and models . For example , we recommend including use cases built 
around the following high-risk domains : 
• Healthcare and medical devices.  
• Critical infrastructure - Management and operation of critical infrastructure like 
telecommunications, energy, water, transport systems.  
• Employment and workplace settings . 
• Facial recognition technology.  
• Educational contexts.  
• Financial services - heavily regulated industries like  banking and finance already 
have potentially  relevant obligations.  
• Public services – in particular AI systems used to determine access to essential 
public services and benefits.    
  
8 
 
• Law enforcement and justice system applications – for example u ses like predictive 
policing, risk assessments in criminal justice contexts.   
• Democratic processes - AI systems that could influence elections or voting 
behaviour. 
• Online content moderation and curation.  
• Social media platforms and dating apps . 
We note that t he proposal also suggests applying the guardrails to all General Purpose AI 
(GPAI) models to address unforeseen applications and risks. We support this approach , in 
particular  we recommend : 
• Providing detailed guidance for each principle to help organisations assess their AI 
systems; 
• Developing a non -exhaustive list of illustrative high -risk use cases to complement 
the principles  in line with the recommended domains listed above ; 
• Establishing a review mechanism to  capture emerging applications as well as to 
provide regular assessment of extant AI systems and their classification  (eg whether 
the classification  should be  upgraded to  high-risk or downgraded as the case may 
be); 
• Offering a clear process for organi sations to seek clarification and guidance on 
borderline cases . This could include some sort of delegated  decision maker 
function within organisations to make determinations on whether an AI system is 
high risk.  
Question 4  
Are there high -risk use cases that government should consider banning in its regulatory 
response (for example, where there is an unacceptable level of risk)? If so, how should we 
define these?  
1. The EU AI Act, which is considered a leading regulation, takes a risk -based approach 
in its approach to  banning certain AI applications deemed to pose ""unacceptable 
risk"".  These include  applications pose unacceptable risks to fundamental human 
rights, democracy, and the rule of law . For example : 
• Social scoring systems ;   
  
9 
 
• AI systems designed to manipulate human behavio ur or exploit 
vulnerabilities ; 
• Biometric identification systems used for real -time surveillance in public 
spaces (with limited exceptions for law enforcement) ; 
• Predictive policing systems based on profiling ; 
• AI systems that categori se people based on sensitive characteristics like 
race, religion, or sexual orientation  etc.  
In defining what constitutes an unacceptable risk  that would  warrant a ban, it is vitally 
important to consider  the potential for serious harm to individuals' rights, health, or safet y; 
threats to democracy, rule of law, or fundamental freedom , lack of human oversight or 
control; privacy violations; risk of large-scale manipulation or exploitation of people . That 
said, we acknowledge that t here are challenges in implementing outright bans , including 
the very nature of  constantly  evolving technologies  makes it challenging to de fine 
boundaries . Any defined boundaries would need to evolve with technology. Further, b lanket 
bans may hinder or prevent legitimate , beneficial uses or innovation in certain contexts. 
Also, certain high-risk applications may have legitimate uses and provided that  proper 
safeguards  are implemented, these should not be automatically discounted simply 
because they are classified high risk.  
An alternative to implementing a ban or  bans could be to impose very stringent 
requirements and oversight on defined, high-risk applications, as the EU does for many use 
cases. There's also a need to consider exceptions for example with respect to national 
security and law enforcement, subject to  strict safeguards.  
In our view, a ny restrictions or prohibitions should be clearly defined, evidence -based, and 
proportionate to the risks . In considering potential bans, we recommend close 
consideration of the EU's list of banned applications as a starting point.  In addition, 
thorough impact assessments should be conducted to identify AI applications that pose an 
unacceptable risk.  Any restrictions, limitations or bans must be precisely defined to 
mitigate unintended consequences . 
We also advocate for a flexible approach that allows for periodic review and adjustment as 
technology (and societal norms ) evolve. We also strongly support the implementation of a 
robust governance framework to assess and manage risks for high -risk AI applications that 
are subject to limitations and / or restrictions .   
  
10 
 
Question 5  
Are the proposed principles flexible enough to capture new and emerging forms of high -risk 
AI, such as general -purpose AI (GPAI)?  
In our view, s ome key points to consider regarding the flexibility of the proposed principles 
to capture new and emerging forms of high -risk AI, including general -purpose AI (GPAI)  
include: 
Principles -based approach:  The UK's approach, as outlined in the white paper  titled ‘AI 
regulation: a pro -innovation approach – policy proposal ’ emphasises a principles -based 
framework rather than rigid definitions or prescriptive rules. This approach is intended to be 
more flexible and adaptable to emerging technologies.  
Defining AI functionally : The UK defines AI based on its functional characteristics of 
""adaptivity"" and ""autonomy"" rather than specific technologies. This allows the framework 
to potentially cover new forms of AI as they emerge  and evolve . 
Focus on risk:   A risk-based approached would direct focus on assessing and mitigating 
risks associated with AI systems  and models , which could also apply to new forms of high -
risk AI as they are developed  and as they evolve . 
GPAI-specific considerations:  The EU AI Act  recognises the unique challenges of general -
purpose AI systems and models and  has introduced specific provisions to address these  
challenges. This suggests that existing principles will likely  need to be adapted or expanded 
to fully capture GPAI risks . It is worth considering this approach in our Australian context, to 
ensure flexibility and adaptability of the principles as AI systems, models and technologies 
evolve.  
Systemic risk threshold:  The EU approach introduces a compute -based threshold for 
identifying GPAI models that present systemic risks. This essentially means that a GPAI 
model shall be presumed to have ""high impact capabilities"" and present systemic risk 
when ""the cumulative amount of computation used for its training measured in Floating 
Point Operations  (FLOPs) is greater than 10^25 , serving as a quan titative criterion to 
determine systemic risk.  Models that exceed this threshold would be deemed high-impact, 
that could pose systemic risks, triggering additional regulatory requirements and scrutiny . 
We suggest this could be a useful model for capturing high -risk GPAI in the Australian 
regulatory  framework.    
  
11 
 
Evolving landscape:  As mentioned elsewhere throughout this paper, t he rapid 
development of AI technologies, particularly GPAI, presents constant and ongoing 
challenges for any regulatory framework. Accordingly, the  principles may need to be 
regularly reviewed and updated to ensure they remain relevant . The UK's approach aims to 
be adaptable, with the ability to modify the definition of AI and the wider regulatory 
framework as needed . We support a similar approach in the Australian context.  
International alignment: There's a clear need for international collaboration on AI 
governance, and alignment with international jurisdictions such as the UK and the EU to  
ensure principles are flexible enough to capture global threats and emerging risks across 
jurisdictions.  
A principles -based approach offers inherent flexibility, however, the challenges posed by 
GPAI and other emerging AI technologies may require specific considerations or additions 
to the principles  over time, as these technologies evolve . Regular review of the principles, 
along with international collaboration, will be necessary to ensure they remain effective in 
capturing new forms of high -risk AI.  
Question 6  
Should mandatory guardrails apply to all GPAI models?  
There is no silver bullet solution to this question. T here are arguments for and against 
applying mandatory guardrails to all general-purpose AI (GPAI) models . However, on 
balance, we propose a  middle ground approach  involving a tiered or risk -based approach 
to the regulation of GPAI models . This would involve :  
• basic transparency requirements for all GPAI models; 
• additional requirements for GPAI models that meet certain criteria or pose systemic 
risks; 
• focus on high -impact AI models that have the most significant potential for harm.  
This approach aims to balance the need for safety and responsibility with the promot ion of 
innovation and tempered regulatory burden.  Ultimately, the decision on whether to apply 
mandatory guardrails to all GPAI models will depend on how policymakers weigh these 
considerations.  When deciding an appropriate regulatory approach towards general 
purpose AI (GPAI) models  we recommend , conducting thorough impact assessments to 
identify truly high -risk applications of GPAI  as well as  taking into account :   
  
12 
 
1. a principles -based, risk -based approach that assesses the severity and extent of 
potential adverse impacts from GPAI models, to allow for context -specific 
assessment and flexibility as the technology evolves.  
2. striking a balance between responsible innovation and mitigating potential risks  
(safety). 
tiered regulation  that applies different levels of oversight based on the capabilities 
and potential risks of GPAI models. For example, models posing systemic risks 
would likely require more stringent requirements.  
3. focus on high -risk applications: Prioriti se regulation of high -risk use cases of GPAI, 
rather than trying to regulate all possible applications.  
4. alignment with international approaches to maximi se market access and business 
opportunities, while still protecting national interests.  
5. adaptability  and flexibility of the regulatory framework to keep pace with rapidly 
evolving AI technologies.  
6. providing  clear guidance to help organi sations appropriately assess their AI systems 
and models and comply with all relevant regulations.  
7. collaboration between government, regulators, industry, and other stakeholders  to 
develop effective and balanced regulations.  
8. the establishment of  clear accountability structures for the development and use of 
GPAI models.  
9. the requirement for  transparency in the development and deployment of GPAI 
models to build and retain public trust.  
10. the unique challenges posed by GPAI, such as potential for widespread impact and 
unforeseen applications.  
We commend the department for its consultation with a wide range of stakeholders, and 
believe that continuing this consultation process and implementing a structured decision -
making process, policymakers can develop a clear, balanced approach to regulating GPAI 
that promotes innovation and mitigates risk . 
Question 7    
  
13 
 
What are suitable indicators for defining GPAI models as high -risk? For example, is it 
enough to define GPAI as high -risk against the principles, or should it be based on technical 
capability such as FLOPS (e.g. 10^25 or 10^26 threshold), advice from a sci entific panel, 
government or other indicators?   
In our view there are several key considerations for defining GPAI models as high -risk:  
Principles -based vs. Technical Threshold Approach:  
Adopting  a hybrid approach, similar to the approach of the EU, using both principles and 
technical thresholds , allows for more flexibility and adaptability as technology evolves.  In 
addition to the FLOPs approach, t he EU AI Act includes a scientific panel of independent 
experts to advise on GPAI models . This allows for expert assessment beyond just technical 
metrics which and balances quantitative and qualitative criteria.  
Technical (eg FLOPs) Threshold:  
As noted in our response to question 5 above, t he EU AI Act has adopted a threshold of 
10^25 FLOPs for establishing whether an GPAI model has  ""high-impact capabilities"" and 
systemic risk.  This threshold is heuristic , identifying models with significant potential 
effects. However, there are risks that a FLOPs threshold may become quickly outdated as 
AI development trends towards creating more powerful GPAI models. Technical thresholds 
like FLOPs as the sole criterion are risky. While FLOPs provides  clear, measurable criteri a 
this approach may become outdated quickly , making it challenging for legislation to k eep 
pace. 
Factors to consider  when determining GPAI models as high risk  include the potential 
impact on public health, safety, national security, human rights, among other things . The 
ability of the model to be used for a wide range of tasks is also a key consideration . Given 
the rapid pace of AI development, the approach  should be flexible enough to adapt  with 
speed and agility  as AI technology evolves . The EU approach allows the Commission to 
dynamically adjust regulatory parameters   
We recommend a balanced, comprehensive  approach to  risk assessment that goes 
beyond just technical capabilities, and which considers factors like the potential for 
misuse, widespread adoption, and impact on critical infrastructure . Specifically, a hybrid 
approach that combines principles -based assessment with technical thresholds.  We 
support the inclusion of  a FLOPs threshold,  but it should  not the sole threshold . In addition,  
we support regular review and adjustment.  We also recommend i ncorporat ing input from a   
  
14 
 
scientific panel or similar expert body to provide ongoing assessment of emerging risks and 
capabilities.  
We recommend that the Department considers a tiered approach that would apply  
different levels of oversight based on the capabilities and potential risks of GPAI models  as 
well as including provisions for regular review and updating of the criteria to keep pace with 
technological advancements.  
We also strongly recommend p roviding the Department provides clear guidance , including 
use cases,  to accompany the principles , to aid organisations assess their AI systems 
against the principles.  
Question 8  
Do the proposed mandatory guardrails appropriately mitigate the risks of AI used in high -
risk settings? Are there any guardrails that we should add or remove?   
The proposed guardrails are an excellent starting point. It is important to note that as AI 
technology evolves, the guardrails will need keep pace. That said, taking a principles -based 
approach allows for context -specific assessment of risks, which provides flexibility as AI 
technology evolves . As drafted, t he guard rails provide focus on ensuring testing, 
transparency, and accountability across the AI lifecycle and supply chain , and aim to 
mitigate risks before people interact with or are subject to AI systems  covering areas 
including : 
• Accountability and governance processes  
• Risk management  
• Data governance  
• Testing and monitoring  
• Human oversight  
• Transparency for end -users  
• Processes for challenging AI decisions  
• Record keeping  
• Conformity assessments   
We note that the guardrails align with  relevant international standards and approaches  
including  in the EU and UK  and promote  consistency and interoperability.    
  
15 
 
That said, in our view more could be done to address broader soc ial impacts, particularly 
on vulnerable or minority groups within the Australian population including First Nations 
people, women, children , those living with a disability, etc . 
We recommend that t he guardrails be subject to regular review and updated to keep pace 
with rapidly evolving AI technologies.  
Additions and  modifications to consider:  
1. More explicit incorporation of ethical considerations and impacts on vulnerable and 
minority groups. The guardrails do not explicitly mention ethics. Adding a specific 
guardrail focused on ethical AI development and use could help address important 
ethical concerns , particularly to mitigate risks associated with data bias.  
1. Stronger measures to address the risk of discrimination and bias  being built into AI 
technologies whether by design or by accident . While there is mention of diversity 
and inclusion, the guardrails could include more explicit requirements to assess 
and mitigate potential harms to vulnerable populations  from data bias.    
2. Clearer guidance on how to assess and mitigate systemic risks from general -
purpose AI models.  
3. More specific requirements for environmental and sustainability impact . The 
guardrails appear to focus mainly on risks to individuals. They could be expanded to 
more comprehensively address potential systemic social and / or environmental 
and sustainability impacts of AI systems, particularly large models . 
4. While accountability is mentioned, the guardrails could include more specific 
requirements for holding organi sations accountable, particularly  for, but not limited 
to, ‘Big Tech’  companies  developing powerful AI systems  that are used to engineer 
social outcomes or designed to make a profit through socially engineer the 
behaviour of their  end users . 
5. Stronger requirements for GPAI: Given the potential widespread impact of general -
purpose AI models, the guardrails could potentially include more stringent 
requirements specific to GPAI, similar to the EU approach . 
6. More specific technical benchmarks: While maintaining flexibility, adding some 
specific technical thresholds (e.g., for model size or capabilities) could help provide 
clearer guidance on what constitutes high -risk AI.   
  
16 
 
7. Indigenous data sovereignty: The guardrails could more explicitly address concepts 
of Indigenous data sovereignty and governance . 
8. Intergenerational effects: Considering long -term and intergenerational impacts of AI 
systems could strengthen the risk assessment aspects of the guardrails . 
Overall, while the proposed guardrails provide a solid foundation, there is room to 
strengthen them in certain areas to more comprehensively address the full range of 
potential risks.  These suggestions aim to make the guardrails more comprehensive in 
addressing the full range of potential risks from high -risk AI systems while maintaining the 
flexibility of the principles -based approach.  
Question 9  
How can the guardrails incorporate First Nations knowledge and cultural protocols to 
ensure AI systems are culturally appropriate and preserve ICIP?  
The output of AI technology is only as good as the data that feeds it. It is therefore essential 
that AI systems and  models have accurate and unbiased data informing the output. In 
particular with respect to how the guardrails protect vulnerable groups in the Australian 
population. T here are several ways the AI guardrails could incorporate First Nations 
knowledge and cultural protocols to ensure AI systems are culturally appropriate and 
preserve Indigenous Cultural and Intellectual Property (ICIP)  including : 
1. Respect Indigenous Data Sovereignty: The standard emphasi ses respecting 
Indigenous Data Sovereignty Principles, which are based on the UN Declaration on 
the Rights of Indigenous Peoples. This could be explicitly incorporated into the 
guardrails, requiring AI systems using data from or about First Nations communit ies 
to adhere to these principles.  
2. Stakeholder engagement: Guardrail 10 in the Voluntary Standard requires 
engagement with stakeholders. For AI systems impacting Indigenous communities, 
this could be expanded to mandate meaningful consultation and collaboration with 
First Nations representa tives throughout the AI lifecycle - from design to deployment 
and monitoring.  
3. Cultural appropriateness assessments: The guardrails could require cultural 
appropriateness assessments for AI systems that may impact Indigenous   
  
17 
 
communities or use Indigenous knowledge. This would involve evaluating whether 
the AI aligns with Indigenous cultural protocols and values.  
4. ICIP protection mechanisms: Specific provisions could be added to protect ICIP, 
such as:  
• Requiring consent from Traditional Owners before using Indigenous 
knowledge in AI systems  
• Implementing protocols for the appropriate use and attribution of Indigenous 
cultural expressions  
• Ensuring benefits from AI systems using Indigenous knowledge are shared 
equitably with communities  
5. Indigenous -led oversight: The guardrails could establish Indigenous -led bodies or 
advisory groups to provide ongoing guidance and oversight for AI systems impacting 
First Nations communities.  
6. Cultural safety training: Mandating cultural safety training for AI developers and 
deployers working on systems that may affect Indigenous communities.  
7. Caring for Country principles: Incorporating traditional land management and 
environmental stewardship practices into AI systems used for environmental 
monitoring or resource management.  
8. Language preservation: For AI systems involving language processing or generation, 
including provisions to support and preserve Indigenous languages.  
9. Holistic impact assessments: Requiring assessments that consider the broader 
cultural, social, and spiritual impacts of AI systems on Indigenous communities, not 
just technical or economic factors.  
10. Indigenous AI ethics framework: Developing an Indigenous -specific AI ethics 
framework to complement the broader guardrails, ensuring Indigenous perspectives 
on ethics and values are centrally considered.  
The guardrails should ensure that AI systems respect, protect, and promote First Nations 
knowledge, cultural protocols, and ICIP while fostering culturally appropriate and 
beneficial AI development for Indigenous communities.    
  
18 
 
Question 10  
Do the proposed mandatory guardrails distribute responsibility across the AI supply chain 
and throughout the AI lifecycle appropriately? For example, are the requirements assigned 
to developers and deployers appropriate?  
The draft mandatory guardrails aim to distribute responsibility across the AI supply chain 
and throughout the AI lifecycle  including : 
• Supply chain coverage ; 
• Lifecycle approach  from development to deployment and ongoing monitoring ; 
• Shared responsibility  model; 
• Transparency ; 
• Accountability across the  AI supply chain.  
• Risk management.  
• Testing and monitoring.  
• Human oversight.  
• Record-keeping.  
However, while the guardrails distribute responsibility  across the AI supply chain and 
throughout the AI lifecycle appropriately , there may be a need for further refinement and 
clarification to ensure appropriate and effective implementation by both developers and 
deployers . Further, it is also worth considering that some guardrails may require different 
actions from developers compared to  deployers. The se requirements could be further 
tailored to each role.  
Additional considerations include t he regulatory burden on small -to-medium enterprises 
(SMEs), potentially with tiered requirements based on organi sation size and AI system risk 
and socio -environmental impact level. 
We also recommend  proactively seek ing feedback from developers and deployers to 
identify any misalignment between the assigned responsibilities and their actual 
capabilities to manage risks at different lifecycle stages.  
Question 11  
Are the proposed mandatory guardrails sufficient to address the risks of GPAI?    
  
19 
 
The proposed mandatory guardrails for GPAI models represent a significant step towards 
addressing potential risks , however there is scope for improvement . The principles -based 
approach allows for context -specific assessment and adaptability, but they could be more  
comprehensive. The inclusion of GPAI -specific considerations, such as adversarial testing 
and incident reporting, is promising. However, the effectiveness of these guardrails in 
mitigating unforeseen risks associated with rapidly evolving AI techn ologies is untested and 
therefore uncertain . The compute -based threshold of 10^25 FLOPs for identifying high -risk 
GPAI models provides a quantifiable metric, but it may not capture all aspects of potential 
systemic risks  and should be paired with other, more qualitative  measures . Further, the 
focus on agentic AI demonstrates awareness of emerging paradigms, but the adequacy of 
proposed measures in addressing these specific challenges is again untested and 
therefore the impact and efficacy remain uncertain . Ultimately, while the guardrails provide 
a foundation for regulation, ongoing assessment and refinement will be crucial to ensure 
their suitability to effectively and adequately address  GPAI risks.  
How could we adapt the guardrails for different GPAI models, for example low -risk and 
high-risk GPAI models?   
We recommend a dapting guardrails for different GPAI models using  a tiered / or staged 
approach based on risk levels. For low -risk models, lighter regulations might focus on 
transparency, documentation, and basic safety testing  and high-risk models would require 
more stringent measures, including extensive adversarial testing, continuous monitoring, 
and mandatory reporting of incidents. The compute -based threshold of 10^25 FLOPs could 
serve as an initial classifier, but additional fac tors like model capabili ties, potential 
applications, and impact assessments must also  be considered , in particular for high risk 
scenarios .  
In addition, a  dynamic risk assessment framework could allow for reclassification as 
models evolve. Regulatory bodies could work with AI developers to establish model -
specific benchmarks and safety protocols. For high -risk models, stricter oversight, more 
frequent audit s, and usage restrictions could be implemented.  
It is important that the guardrails be flexible, allowing for rapid adjustments as AI 
technology advances and new risks emerge, to ensure  a balance between innovation and 
safety across the spectrum of GPAI models.   
   
  
20 
 
Question 12  
Do you have suggestions for reducing the regulatory burden on small -to-medium sized 
businesses applying guardrails?  
Provide templates and resources:  
Offer ready -made templates, checklists,  and examples to help SMEs efficiently implement 
the guardrails. This could include accountability , process, and risk assessment 
frameworks, a s well as testing guidelines tailored for  SMEs. 
Implement tiered requirements:  
Create a tiered system of requirements based on the size of the business and the risk level 
of their AI systems. Consider whether l ower-risk systems or smaller businesses should 
have simplified record -keeping and less frequent audit obligations . 
Establish a central AI authority:  
Set up an AI Authority to provide guidance, tools, and oversight for AI regulation. This ""one -
stop-shop"" approach would reduce the burden on SMEs of dealing with multiple 
regulators.  
Facilitate access to shared resources:  
Where practicable, p rovide SMEs with access to shared testing facilities, data governance 
tools, and expert advisors. Pooled resources and support would help smaller businesses 
meet the guardrails with efficiencies of scale . Another option could be to o ffer SMEs free 
access to regulatory sandboxes for developing and testing AI systems, similar to what is 
proposed in the EU AI Act . 
Phase in requirements gradually:  
Consider introducing guardrail requirements for SMEs on a  graduated timeline with clear 
milestones. This would allow smaller businesses more time to adapt and implement 
necessary changes.  
Offer financial support:  
Provide financial incentives such as tax incentives  or grants to help SMEs invest in AI 
governance capabilities. Subsidi sed training programs could also accelerate adoption of 
responsible AI practices.  In addition , fees for third -party conformity assessments could be 
reduced for SMEs in proportion to their size or market share  or risk impact on the market. In 
addition it might be worthwhile c onducting regular assessments of compliance costs for   
  
21 
 
SMEs and startups, similar to what is  proposed in the EU AI Act, to ensure the regulatory 
burden remains proportionate  and reasonable and so that SMEs can comply . 
Exempt SMEs from certain requirements:  
Consider exempting SMEs from some of the more resource -intensive requirements, such 
as stakeholder consultations during impact assessments, while still encouraging them to 
conduct these where possible.  
These suggestions aim to balance the need for responsible AI development against the 
often limited resources of smaller businesses . 
Question 1 3 
Which legislative option do you feel will best address the use of AI in high -risk settings? 
What opportunities should the government take into account in considering each 
approach?   
There is no silver bullet to solving the AI regulatory question. There  benefits and 
disadvantages with respect  to each of the proposed  legislative options.  A domain-specific 
approach  allows for tailored regulation within existing frameworks and  may be faster to 
implement in some areas  and may l everage existing regulatory expertise . However, it risks 
creating fragmented or inconsistent AI regulation across sectors . Further, it may create 
gaps where AI use cases don't fit neatly into existing domains  and ultimately c ould become 
complex and unwieldy for companies operating across multiple domains to navigate . 
A framework approach  has the potential to p rovide consistency with definitions and core 
principles across sectors . It also a llows for a degree of  customisation within existing 
regulatory frameworks,  and it has the potential to b alances centrali sed guidance with 
sector-specific implementation . However, this approach m ay require significant 
coordination across regulatory bodies  and could take longer to implement fully across all 
relevant domains , risking inconsistencies in the application of the framework across 
sectors. 
That said, on balance , a cross-economy approach, governed by an AI-specific Act , seems 
the most comprehensive approach to tackle the use of AI in high -risk scenarios . This option 
has the potential to  provide a unified, whole -of-economy framework specifically targeted 
at organisations developing and using AI technologies. It should establish consistent 
definitions, thresholds, and guardrails in one piece of legislation, along with targeted 
enforcement and monitoring mechanisms. This approach has the potential to  address   
  
22 
 
regulatory gaps, ensure consistency across sectors, and adapt more easily to rapid 
technological changes in AI. It would also align with approaches taken by other 
jurisdictions like the EU AI Act. While potentially more complex to implement initially, a 
dedicated AI Act could provide the clearest and most flexible foundation for regulating 
high-risk AI use cases as the technology continues to evolve. However, careful 
consideration would be needed to ensure it integrates well with existing regulatory 
framewo rks.  
In considering this approach, and to ensure  a solid foundation for AI governance while 
maintaining the flexibility needed to address sector -specific concerns and adapt to rapid 
technological change , the government should consider : 
• The establishment of  a central AI governance body to oversee framework 
development and cross -sector coordination.  
• Development of the core framework legislation with extensive stakeholder 
consultation.  
• A phased implementation plan, prioriti sing high-risk sectors.  
• Ways to p rovide guidance and support to regulatory bodies on incorporating AI -
specific provisions.  
• Establish ing mechanisms for regular review and updates to the framework as AI 
technology evolves.  
• Ensuring alignment with international approaches . 
Question 14  
Are there any additional limitations of options outlined in this section which the Australian 
Government should consider?  
The Australian Government should consider  the flexibility and adaptability  of the regulatory 
framework. Whichever option the government chooses must be flexible enough to adapt to 
rapidly evolving AI technologies. This might involve  mechanisms for regular review and 
updating of applicable legislation and regulations.  
In addition, a s a relatively small economy, Australia ’s alignment with international 
approaches to AI regulation  will be critical to keeping pace with the evolution of AI 
technologies. Complex  or burdensome regulations risk discourag ing international AI 
companies from operating in Australia or  conversely, may  make it difficult for Australian AI   
  
23 
 
companies to compete globally.  The chosen regulatory approach should support 
interoperability with international  AI governance frameworks . 
The compliance costs and complexity of AI regulations could be particularly challenging for 
small and medium enterprises. The government should consider tiered approaches or 
support mechanisms to avoid stifling innovation among smaller companies.  
Further, whichever approach is chosen, it is important to ensure that potential  regulatory 
gaps are identified and addressed . Critically, whichever  regulatory option  is chose,  it must 
carefully balance the promotion of  AI innovation and economic growth with ensuring social 
safety and protecting rights. An overly restrictive approach could hinder Australia's AI 
industry.  
Ultimately, the effectiveness of any regulatory approach will depend heavily on how ""high -
risk"" AI is defined. This definition should be clear enough to provide certainty but flexible 
enough to respond to and address emerging risks.  
Finally, it is worth noting that each  option presents different enforcement challenges, from 
coordinating across multiple regulators , establishing entirely new enforcement 
mechanisms , to interaction with existing laws  (e.g., privacy, consumer protection, anti -
discrimination , data protection ). The enforcement question requires  careful consideration  
across each proposed regulatory approach . 
Question 15 
Which regulatory option/s will best ensure that guardrails for high -risk AI can adapt and 
respond to step -changes in technology?  
We feel that the introduction of  a cross-economy AI -specific Act (e.g. an Australian AI Act) - 
is the most appropriate regulatory approach to ensure guardrails for high -risk AI can adapt 
and respond to step -changes in technology.  
A dedicated AI Act would provide a unified, whole -of-economy framework specifically 
targeted at AI technologies. This would allow for consistent definitions, thresholds, and 
guardrails to be established in one place, rather than spread across multiple , existing 
regulatory frameworks.  Further, an AI-specific Act would make it easier to update and 
adapt the guardrails as AI technology evolves.  
Significantly, an  AI Act could establish dedicated enforcement and monitoring 
mechanisms focused specifically on AI. This would allow for more targeted and responsive   
  
24 
 
oversight as technology changes.  A dedicated Act could more easily incorporate emerging 
concepts like general -purpose AI models, which may not fit neatly into existing regulatory 
frameworks.  
An AI Act would also align with approaches taken by other jurisdictions like the EU AI Act, 
supporting international interoperability.  While Options 1 and 2 could potentially be 
adapted over time, in our view, a purpose -built AI Act appears to offer flexibility and 
responsiveness to keep pace with AI advancements and  would likely make it easier to 
implement consistent updates across the entire AI ecosystem as needed.  
Question 16  
Where do you see the greatest risks of gaps or inconsistencies with Australia’s existing laws 
for the development and deployment of AI? Which regulatory option best addresses this, 
and why?  
DLC views the greatest risks of gaps or inconsistencies in Australia's existing laws for AI 
development and deployment  lie principally in the absence of AI-specific legislation. 
Australia currently does not have laws made specifically for AI. Instead, AI is regulated 
through a patchwork of existing laws that were not designed with AI in mind, which 
inevitably results in there being  gaps. For example, the  Privacy Act 1988  covers some 
aspects of data use in AI, however it doesn’t  not fully address the unique privacy 
challenges posed by AI systems, especially regarding data collection and  use for training AI 
models.  
Similarly,  there are Intellectual Property considerations  not covered by extant legislation . In 
particular regarding the ethical approach to data collection  and obtaining consent of the 
authors of artistic works that might be drawn upon to build the knowledge base of an AI 
system. We know from concerns raised  globally that ChatGPT used thousands of creative 
works without autho r consent to build its knowledge base. For example, a class action 
lawsuit was filed against OpenAI in June 2023 by authors alleging that ChatGPT was trained 
on their copyrighted books without consent or compensation , and The New York Times 
sued OpenAI and Microsoft in December 2023, alleging that their AI models were trained 
on millions of Times articles without permission . These are among many lawsuits seeking 
to hold large language models like ChatGPT accountable for using copyrighted creative 
works in their training data without author consent.    
  
25 
 
We also have strong concerns with risks associated with a lgorithmic bias and 
discrimination . Existing anti -discrimination laws may not adequately address the potential 
for AI systems to produce biased or discriminatory outcomes, especially in areas like 
automated decision -making. Further, t here are currently  gaps in requirements for AI 
developers and deployers to provide transparency about how their systems work and make 
decisions  that could be more robustly captured in a bespoke AI Legislative framework.  
A fundamental question that must be answered if AI is to be truly safe and secure is: “ who 
is (or who should be) responsible for harm caused by  AI systems or when AI systems make 
errors and how that accountability should be enforced ?” Existing laws don’t make clear  
who is (or who should be) the legal entity responsible for harm caused by  AI systems or 
when AI systems make errors  and how that accountability should be enforced . An AI Act is 
an excellent starting point in the building of a robust regime to ensure accountability for AI -
driven decisions .  
We recogni se that the global nature of AI development and use may create gaps in how 
existing Australian laws can be enforced  and in which jurisdictions. Therefore,  global 
cooperation will be an integral part of any AI framework. Similarly, we acknowledge that 
rapid and constant advancements in AI will inevitably  outpace the law, and there is always 
a risk that there will be  gaps as new capabilities like general -purpose AI models  evolve. 
However,  an AI Act , supported by these proposed guardrails and well considered 
regulations will provide a solid foundation  to support innovation, the economy and provide 
a safe and secure -by-design AI framework . In our view, t his approach would address the 
known, identified gaps while maintaining the flexibility needed to adapt to the fast -
changing AI landscape. However, its effectiveness would depend on careful 
implementation and coordination across different regulatory bodies . 
Conclusion  
DLC Legal's response to the Australian Government's proposals for AI guardrails advocate s 
for a risk-based approach focused on high -risk settings and applications, supporting a 
cross-economy AI -specific Act as the most effective regulatory option. In our view, t his 
approach would provide the necessary flexibility and adaptability to keep pace with rapidly 
evolving AI technologies.  Our response emphasi ses the importance of balancing 
innovation with safety and ethical responsibility, suggesting tiered regulations based on 
risk levels and capabilities. We highlight the need for clear accountabilit y structures,   
  
26 
 
transparency in AI development and deployment, and mechanisms to address algorithmic 
bias and discrimination. We underscore  the importance of incorporating Indigenous data 
sovereignty and cultural protocols into the regulatory framework.   
We support  regular review of the guardrails to ensure they remain relevant and effective. 
We also suggest providing clear guidance and Australian context specific use cases to 
assist organisations assess their AI systems against the principles. Our response highlights  
the importance of international alignment and collaboration in AI governance while 
maintaining flexibility to address Australia's specific context and needs.  As AI continues to 
evolve and impact various aspects of society, we believe the proposed approach offers a 
solid foundation for responsible AI development and deployment in Australia. We 
commend the government for its consultation process and believe that continuing this 
engagement with stakeholders will be crucial in developing a clear, balanced approach to 
regulating AI that promotes innovation, mitigates risks, and ensures the safety and well -
being of all Australians.  
