No
Please provide any additional comments.
Most of the mitigation approaches is already covered by existing legislation and accountability of organisations. It is highly recommended that to extend existing regulation to cover existing loopholes instead of creating new legislation for dealing with AI. That will avoid any inconsistencies between regulations/laws.
Are there any guardrails that we should add or remove?
Yes
What guardrails should we add or remove?
Enable human control or intervention in an AI system to achieve meaningful human oversight Keep and maintain records to allow third parties to assess compliance with guardrails Be transparent with other organisations across the AI supply chain about data, models and systems to help them effectively address risks
Please provide any additional comments
While I understand why (5,9) ""Enable human control or intervention in an AI system to achieve meaningful human oversight"" and ""Keep and maintain records to allow third parties to assess compliance with guardrails"" are important. Its application to say ChatGPT like systems, which could be classified as high risk due to ""reach"" (ie large number of users globally), creates a spying charter. This guardrail has implications right across the board from corporate espionage to national security and privacy. This is another reason that the principles abased approach is a bad idea. Please remove them.
with respect to ""Be transparent with other organisations across the AI supply chain about data, models and systems to help them effectively address risks"" this is a very technologically backwards looking guardrail and fails to recognize that we are moving towards continual learning systems for which it may not be possible to know all the influences that the system may have seen, particularly for embodied systems. Please remove it.
Others: We opt for the approach adopted in Singapore to encourage innovation while against the EU approach to discourage in advancing technology.
See notes at end of submission
9. How can the guardrails incorporate First Nations knowledge and cultural protocols to ensure AI systems are culturally appropriate and preserve Indigenous Cultural and Intellectual Property?
By excluding indigenous cultural and intellectual property you are potentially excluding those users from being recognized or understood by these systems i.e. you are discriminating against them. Hence with current systems it does not matter if you include these elements or not, either way you are wrong. Note as mentioned earlier AI may become our repository of knowledge of the future.
10. Do the proposed mandatory guardrails distribute responsibility across the AI supply chain and throughout the AI lifecycle appropriately?
No
Which of the guardrails should be amended?
Keep and maintain records to allow third parties to assess compliance with guardrails Be transparent with other organisations across the AI supply chain about data, models and systems to help them effectively address risks Enable human control or intervention in an AI system to achieve meaningful human oversight
11. Are the proposed mandatory guardrails sufficient to address the risks of GPAI?
No
How could we adapt the guardrails for different GPAI models, for example low-risk and high-risk GPAI models? 
In the current/existing narrow AI applications, AI can surpass human expert performances. When GPAI is fully developed, its knowledge will surpass majority human experts in their fields. Keeping human in the loop may not advancing the system as a whole.
12. Do you have suggestions for reducing the regulatory burden on small-to-medium sized businesses applying guardrails?
Yes
Please provide any additional comments
Make legislation more concise less ambiguous and less catch all. Note that not only do companies need to navigate this legislation but they also need to deal with AI dual use provisions in ""defence trade controls act 2024"" which it would seem most universities and government themselves are not aware of.
With the heavy-handed regulatory burden, it is impossible for small-to-medium to comply. Couple that with little/no incentives for building small-to-medium businesses, it will almost certainly kill any innovation in this country. Although Australia is currently do well with extractive industries, the diversification of industries is very limited. In a long term, the AI technology in Australia will be left behind.
13. Which legislative option do you feel will best address the use of AI in high-risk settings?
A domain specific approach – adapting existing regulatory frameworks to include the proposed mandatory guardrails
What opportunities should the Government take into account in considering each approach? 
The fact that existing legislation requires strengthening as” it is not fit for its original purpose” and is being flouted by all sorts of organisations in Australia. You have to ask why is their such reluctance to update to the existing privacy laws, the data protection act, consumer protection laws etc?
14. Are there any additional limitations of options outlined in this section which the Australian Government should consider?
Yes
Please provide any additional comments.
see comments at end of survey
15. Which regulatory option(s) will best ensure that guardrails for high-risk AI can adapt and respond to step-changes in technology?
A domain specific approach – Adopting the guardrails within existing regulatory frameworks as needed
Please provide any additional comments.
It is recommended to update the existing domain specific legislations and make them work as it was originally intended. Currently, most of the domain specific regulation is too weak and often it lacks oversight and punishments when they are infringed.
16. Where do you see the greatest risks of gaps and inconsistencies with Australia’s existing laws for the development and deployment of AI?
AI is not properly defined, and it will be impossible to legislate without a clear scope.
Which regulatory option best addresses this?
A domain specific approach – Adopting the guardrails within existing regulatory frameworks as needed
Please explain why.  
This is the least damaging approach. It may still result in unintended consequences due to ill-defined scope.
Make a general comment
General comments:
Notes on guardrails:
Superficially, the items under ""Proposed mandatory guardrails for high-risk AI at a glance"" individually and at face value the recommendations are not unreasonable for many specific situations. However, collectively and when applied across a very general field, they are not appropriate.
1. “Establish, implement and publish an accountability process including governance, internal capability and a strategy for regulatory compliance “
Companies already do this as part of their general compliance requirements
2. “Establish and implement a risk management process to identify and mitigate risks”
Risk management is significantly biased in a principles based system as there are no clear definitions, as is evident by the framing of the risk outlined in the discussion document.
3. “Protect AI systems, and implement data governance measures to manage data quality and provenance”
How do you protect data provenance in a system that learns from its environment, such systems have been around for many decades. Which also raises a side question what information is it that we don’t want them to know or share?
4. “Test AI models and systems to evaluate model performance and monitor the system once deployed”
How do we define meaningful monitoring of a deployed system like ABS brakes, is a validation routine and light on the dashboard sufficient?
5. “Enable human control or intervention in an AI system to achieve meaningful human oversight”
Putting a human in the loop may decrease the safety of some systems.
Excluding some data may result in discrimination by lack of representation.
Storing all information of a systems output may have privacy, cybersecurity, national security implications and may actually be an enabler for large scale discrimination.
6. “Inform users of AI related decisions, interactions with AI and AI-generated content.”
Think of a pilot’s cockpit do you think that current designs allow all the alarms to go off at the same time and what that would do to decision making? AI is increasing used to mediate physical systems, think self-driving cars, if every system reports decisions, then you would be overwhelmed by the information flow.
9. “Keep and maintain records to allow third parties to assess compliance with guardrails”
Keeping records of all use for all systems may impact the very principles you are trying to protect.
10. “Undertake conformity assessments to demonstrate and certify compliance with the guardrails”
What is the point of a certification for a self-learning system. If something is conformant at the point of test it may be totally not conformant immediately afterwards.
Other notes:
It is important to note that the proposed guardrails do not appear alone, they are written and interpreted in the context of the document in which they are presented, hence cannot be read alone or taken at face value.
The framing of the document and the questions in the “Have Your Say” document are presented in a ""loaded"" and ""leading"" way. The supporting document itself contains a lot of interesting biases and poorly substantiated claims from conflicted parties. Particularly, with respect to misinformation, it's almost like it has an agenda? Its claims “AI amplifies and creates new risks” though “bias due to inaccurate, insufficient, unrepresentative or outdated data” and, in a very meta way, goes on to denigrate AI with biased unrepresentative and outdated information.
The authors have not made a clear case as to WHY existing legislation cannot be used, we just have a claim from them that this is so! What is deficient in the existing legislation, I.e consumer protection, therapeutic goods, data protection, privacy, defence trade controls act etc.? Without clarity as to the current legal deficiency why would one support new layers of legislation which will have unintended consequences.
The lack of balance in the discussion document is a red flag, the people writing it seem to fail to see the positive impact of AI on society and fail to understand the risk of not adopting these technologies. The models of responsibilities seem to have been created by a non-technical person that copy and pasted ""how AI works"" from Wikipedia.
Who is the developer, the deployer and the user? When the user may retrain the model or more importantly when you have a learning system which has continually learning, which learns from its environment, as opposed to consuming human curated data from the Internet.
The documents approach to GPAI is particularly uninformed, conflates a lot of issues, and would itself fall into the disinformation bucket where it not that government (by far the largest purveyors of all misinformation and disinformation) are of exempt from such legislation.
Principle based approaches easily lead to perverse or negative outcomes when applied in real world. ""The road to hell is paved with good intentions"". We live in a complex world where unintended consequences frequently manifest as new forms of discrimination.
The ambiguity of the principles-based approach may make actual adherence impossible; you can be deemed to be legally wrong whichever action you take. Writing legislation like this is more about controlling who can participate in an industry and encourages regulatory capture.
The assumptions and technologies covered by these mandates are very backward looking. Creating laws around yesterday’s technology (like LLMs) and expecting it to apply to new systems which share almost none of the characteristics of previous generations of AI, this is not a sensible approach.
Copying regulatory approaches from other countries which have created their regulations based on ""lobbying"" from their tech industry to favour their industries does not speak to me of independence, but rather indicates an acceptance of backdoor attempts by multinationals to corner a lucrative market.
The approach of adopting a preventative landscape is also problematic. The preventative landscape for fire risk is to remove all environmental elements from the landscape, trees, bushes, grass etc. You see plenty of blocks like this in WA. They are totally fire safe, unlivable, and environmentally degraded environments. The concept of creating a landscape of prevention from harm, seems to be more motived by a deep desire to control narrative, than to actually reduce today’s actual real-world risk, let alone future risk.
In this context, it is important to note that significant harms are a feature of human activity. Today's systems, which not only have people in the loop, but where people are the loop, are still harming people. Do automated decision-making systems harm more people? It depends, if they have greater context and experience than people then probably not. But if they did, then do we have laws in place already to cover this current issues? If not, why not?
The automated decision-making system in an Automotive ABS brake is written in software, probably with > 200,000 lines of code, It makes life critical decisions on your behalf. If it does not work, who is liable? If you modify that code, then who is liable? Its seems we can answer these questions but not the same questions when asked about AI? The reason for this is that AI is just a poorly defined term and so the answer is ""it depends"".
Giving better definitions for systems and where they sit within current legislation is the most sensible way forward, combined with strengthening existing regulations in consumer protection and privacy and data protection which lag behind the rest of the world.

