1. Do the proposed principles adequately capture high-risk AI?
Yes
Please provide any additional comments.
While they are adequate, please ensure there is a periodic risk assessment done to categorize based on severity and keep the risks relevant that drives the holistic actions by the government
Are there any principles we should add or remove?
Yes
Please provide any additional comments.
Kindly consider a principle around impact on Nation's credibility, sovereignty, global investments and collaboration from international associations & community and trade policies with AI influence
Please identify any:
Priorities should certainly be categorized across various purposes and departments so they can be effectively leveraged as North star characteristics to drive meaningful guardrails & policies amendments. Some of them could be Citizen Service, Health, Legal, Culture/Community, Economy, Security, Defence, etc..
2. Do you have any suggestions for how the principles could better capture harms to First Nations people, communities and Country?
Yes
Please provide any additional comments.
Through a pragmatic approach of implementing the governance framework ensuring there is continuous oversight of all the documented risks associated to the people, communities and country. Broaden the principles into sub principles cutting across various purposes and departments and make them more meaningful and relevant to capture real harms on the ground
3. Do the proposed principles, supported by examples, give enough clarity and certainty on high-risk AI settings and high-risk AI models? Is a more defined approach, with a list of illustrative uses, needed?
NO a more defined list-based approach is needed
If you prefer a list-based approach (similar to the EU and Canada), what use cases should we include? How can this list capture emerging uses of AI?
Critical infrastructure Education/training Products and services affecting individual and public health and safety Biometrics Employment Access to essential public services and products Administration of justice and democratic processes
How can this list capture emerging uses of AI?
They feed into all the relevant uses holistically for you to consider a comprehensive analysis and do further amendments to policies and legislations and bring in the right adherence enhancing the right use and leverage of AI
4. Are there high-risk use cases that government should consider banning in its regulatory response (for example, where there is an unacceptable level of risk)?
No
5. Are the proposed principles flexible enough to capture new and emerging forms of high-risk AI, such as general-purpose AI (GPAI)?
Yes
Please provide any additional comments.
please consider more inputs provided in the above comments. Principles are flexible only when they are point-in-time relevant upon continuous monitoring and measurement exercise
6. Should mandatory guardrails apply to all GPAI models?
Yes
Please provide any additional comments.
absolutely
7. What are suitable indicators for defining GPAI models as high-risk?
Base on technical capability
What technical capability should it be based on?
Advice from a scientific panel
8. Do the proposed mandatory guardrails appropriately mitigate the risks of AI used in high-risk settings?
No
Please provide any additional comments.
While the list is good, there is need to add more guardrails around Process & Methods of Building AI, AI Education, Data Use, Source & Completeness, Agility to evolve, Feedback Channels, Transparency - Confidence Score, AI Systems Certifications, Diversity in Stakeholder groups, Providing Incentives
Are there any guardrails that we should add or remove?
Yes
What guardrails should we add or remove?
Other
Please provide any additional comments
Process & Methods of Building AI, AI Education, Data Use, Source & Completeness, Agility to evolve, Feedback Channels, Transparency - Confidence Score, AI Systems Certifications, Diversity in Stakeholder groups, Providing Incentives
9. How can the guardrails incorporate First Nations knowledge and cultural protocols to ensure AI systems are culturally appropriate and preserve Indigenous Cultural and Intellectual Property?
Via frequent information collection from random surveys, physical conversations, feedback channels, broad coverage of community, etc
10. Do the proposed mandatory guardrails distribute responsibility across the AI supply chain and throughout the AI lifecycle appropriately?
Yes
Please provide any additional comments
Yes, however they need to be more detailed and specific guardrails and mandates for individual categories and technology implementation
11. Are the proposed mandatory guardrails sufficient to address the risks of GPAI?
No
How could we adapt the guardrails for different GPAI models, for example low-risk and high-risk GPAI models? 
Rinse & Repeat - Add & Update guardrails in frequent intervals. Kindly consider adding the proposed guardrails in the above comments
12. Do you have suggestions for reducing the regulatory burden on small-to-medium sized businesses applying guardrails?
No
Please provide any additional comments
Give them more time to adhere and follow along with some flexibility on less relevant policies based on type of business without having to compromise on the overall objective
13. Which legislative option do you feel will best address the use of AI in high-risk settings?
A whole of economy approach – introducing a new cross-economy AI Act
What opportunities should the Government take into account in considering each approach? 
Innovation across industries, Investments in Safety, Research & Workforce development
14. Are there any additional limitations of options outlined in this section which the Australian Government should consider?
Yes
Please provide any additional comments.
While the preferred option is Economy approach, there is a realistic approach of moving there by starting at Domain Specific approach and move up the ladder from Framework to Economy approach with a specific timeline, budgets and risks associated to it.
15. Which regulatory option(s) will best ensure that guardrails for high-risk AI can adapt and respond to step-changes in technology?
Other
Please provide any additional comments.
While the preferred option is Economy approach, there is a realistic approach of moving there by starting at Domain Specific approach and move up the ladder from Framework to Economy approach with a specific timeline, budgets and risks associated to it.
16. Where do you see the greatest risks of gaps and inconsistencies with Australia’s existing laws for the development and deployment of AI?
Adherence and lack of comprehensive coverage of all attributes impacting citizens, culture, community, nation security, etc....
Which regulatory option best addresses this?
Other
Please explain why.  
While the preferred option is Economy approach, there is a realistic approach of moving there by starting at Domain Specific approach and move up the ladder from Framework to Economy approach with a specific timeline, budgets and risks associated to it.
Make a general comment
Thank you for considering the inputs and giving an opportunity to contribute. Happy to participate and contribute on a continuous basis.

