4. Are there high-risk use cases that government should consider banning in its regulatory response (for example, where there is an unacceptable level of risk)?
Yes
If so, how should we define these?
I’m concerned about all potential harms from AI systems. However, risks of catastrophic harms clearly warrant special attention. For example, use of AI systems to create bioweapons, lethal autonomous weapons and large-scale cyberattacks on Australia’s water systems, medical systems, democratic systems, security systems, and food systems. I’m clearly not the only Australian that thinks this, given that a “preventing dangerous and catastrophic outcomes from AI” was found to be the top priority when 1,141 Australians were asked ‘what should the government focus on when it comes to Artificial Intelligence’ (Ready Research, 2024). In view of this, I think it is commonsense to ban AI systems that present unacceptable catastrophic risks. I suggest defining these high-risk contexts using the ‘systemic risks’ definition in Recital 110 of the EU AI Act. Benefits of this approach include: (1) consistency with other countries mounts coherent pressure on international AI developers, (2) politically easier to align with the EU’s precedent, (3) administratively easier to align with the EU’s precedent.
Full details of referenced Ready Research 2024 survey: https://aigovernance.org.au/survey/
5. Are the proposed principles flexible enough to capture new and emerging forms of high-risk AI, such as general-purpose AI (GPAI)?
No
Please provide any additional comments.
I’m concerned the current approach captures relatively benign AI systems in the same bucket as extremely dangerous AI systems. I think we need multiple buckets to distinguish between ‘GPAI’ and ‘GPAI with systemic risk’, as defined by the EU Act in Article 3(62) and Article 3(65). Benefits of this approach include: (1) avoiding economic and administrative burdens of intensive regulation of lower-risk AI systems, (2) ensuring regulatory resources are prioritized for AI systems that present catastrophic risks, (3) being consistent with other countries mounts coherent pressure on international AI developers, (4) politically easier to align with the EU’s precedent, (5) administratively easier to align with the EU’s precedent, and (6) the EU definition of ‘GPAI with systemic risk’ is still flexible enough to regulate future AI system developments.
6. Should mandatory guardrails apply to all GPAI models?
No
Please provide any additional comments.
I think we need multiple buckets to distinguish between ‘GPAI’ and ‘GPAI with systemic risk’, as defined by the EU Act in Article 3(62) and Article 3(65). Benefits of this approach include: (1) avoiding economic and administrative burdens of intensive regulation of low-risk AI systems, (2) ensuring regulatory resources are prioritized for AI systems that present catastrophic risks, (3) consistency with other countries mounts coherent pressure on international AI developers, (4) politically easier to align with the EU’s precedent, (5) administratively easier to align with the EU’s precedent, and (6) the EU definition of ‘GPAI with systemic risk’ is still flexible enough to regulate future AI system developments.
10. Do the proposed mandatory guardrails distribute responsibility across the AI supply chain and throughout the AI lifecycle appropriately?
No
Which of the guardrails should be amended?
Other
11. Are the proposed mandatory guardrails sufficient to address the risks of GPAI?
No
How could we adapt the guardrails for different GPAI models, for example low-risk and high-risk GPAI models? 
I think we first need multiple buckets to distinguish between ‘GPAI’ and ‘GPAI with systemic risk’, as defined by the EU Act in Article 3(62) and Article 3(65). Once we have a tighter categorisation of AI systems that pose catastrophic risks, it will become more straightforward to impose effective guardrails. For example, California’s law SB1047 required developers of ‘frontier’ AI models to make an ‘off’ switch if such models became capable of catastrophic harm.
13. Which legislative option do you feel will best address the use of AI in high-risk settings?
A whole of economy approach – introducing a new cross-economy AI Act
What opportunities should the Government take into account in considering each approach? 
I think an AI Act presents the most promise. The most concerning risks from AI threaten multiple dimensions of Australians’ lives and Australia’s economies. As a result, a domain-specific approach means the most catastrophic risks could ‘fall through the cracks’. For example, in 2021, JBS, the world's larger meat processor paid a $14 million ransom following a cyberattack that blocked its US and Australian operations. The impacts to industry and food supply were relatively limited in this specific case, but the example illustrates that more advanced AI systems threaten Australia’s food supply systems. However, concerningly, in this example, it’s unclear whether such threats to our food supply would be the main responsibility of the Department of Agriculture, Fisheries and Forestry, the Department of Industry Science and Resources, the proposed Minister for Food, the Department of Home Affairs, or another arm of government. A cross-cutting AI Act could facilitate a cohesive and comprehensive governance of systemic risks from AI. An Act AI would also enable Australia to align with other countries in directly pressuring AI developers to address catastrophic risks from AI.
More information about the referenced 2021 cyberattack on JBS: https://www.abc.net.au/news/rural/2021-06-10/jbs-foods-pays-14million-ransom-cyber-attack/100204240
15. Which regulatory option(s) will best ensure that guardrails for high-risk AI can adapt and respond to step-changes in technology?
A whole of economy approach – introducing a new cross-economy AI Act
Please provide any additional comments.
I think an AI Act presents the most promise. The most concerning risks from AI threaten multiple dimensions of Australians’ lives and Australia’s economies. As a result, a domain-specific approach means the most catastrophic risks could ‘fall through the cracks’. For example, in 2021, JBS, the world's larger meat processor paid a $14 million ransom following a cyberattack that blocked its US and Australian operations. The impacts to industry and food supply were relatively limited in this specific case, but the example illustrates that more advanced AI systems threaten Australia’s food supply systems. However, concerningly, in this example, it’s unclear whether such threats to our food supply would be the main responsibility of the Department of Agriculture, Fisheries and Forestry, the Department of Industry Science and Resources, the proposed Minister for Food, the Department of Home Affairs, or another arm of government. A cross-cutting AI Act could facilitate a cohesive and comprehensive governance of systemic risks from AI. An Act AI would also enable Australia to align with other countries in directly pressuring AI developers to address catastrophic risks from AI.
More information about the referenced 2021 cyberattack on JBS: https://www.abc.net.au/news/rural/2021-06-10/jbs-foods-pays-14million-ransom-cyber-attack/100204240
