The 4th Pacific ICT Ministerial Dialogue took place in Papua New Guinea in 
August 2023, with significant participation from Communications and ICT 
Ministers across the Pacific. This event, the first since 2015, resulted in a 
new Ministerial declaration and action plan focused on enhancing digital 
transformation and inclusion in the region. The dialogue emphasized the 
importance of the Pacific’s active participation in global ICT discussions and 
the need for regional collaboration to address shared telecommunications 
and ICT development challenges. Issues discussed included improving 
regional coordination and strengthening engagement with the International 
Telecommunication Union39.
Overall, the Pacific Islands are making progress in the AI and digital landscapes,  
with a focus on developing national strategies, improving cyber security, and 
digitising key sectors such as tourism, climate change, health, and finance. 
However, there is still much work to be done, especially in terms of data 
protection and privacy laws, which are lacking in many countries in the region. 
As the region continues to embrace AI and digital transformation, it will be 
crucial to address these gaps and ensure that the benefits of these technologies 
are accessible to all citizens.233.2 Government AI Readiness 
The Government AI Readiness Index, developed by Oxford Insights, evaluates 
governments’ capabilities to effectively deploy AI in public service delivery by 
three criteria: the government’s AI strategy, the innovation of the technology 
sector, and the quality of data and infrastructure.
According to the Pacific region index shown in Table 3.2, Australia and New 
Zealand are leading advancements in AI governance in the Pacific with global 
rankings of 12th and 49th, respectively. Both countries demonstrate strong 
performance in the Data and Infrastructure pillar, with Australia scoring 86.75 
and New Zealand scoring 81.66. However, New Zealand scores significantly 
lower in the Government pillar at 51.85 compared to Australia’s 83.34, which is 
mainly due to the absence of a binding national AI strategy. In the Technology 
pillar, both countries show relatively lower scores, with New Zealand at 52.57 
and Australia at 47.05.
The Pacific Islands that are scored show a significant disparity compared to 
Australia and New Zealand, with a 13.44 difference in total score between New 
Zealand and Nauru, the  highest-ranking country40 from the Pacific Islands. 
Aside from Nauru and Fiji, the remaining countries in the Pacific Islands are 
ranked outside the top 100 and generally show lower levels of development 
across all pillars. Several factors contribute to this, including the geographical 
dispersion and small populations of these islands, and the distance from major 
international markets41. Moreover, the exclusion of seven islands from the index 
due to the lack of data availability underscores the difficulties in collecting ICT-
related data in this region.24Table 3.2. Government AI Readiness in the Pacific
