Chapter 4.
Future Directions, Recommendations 
and Conclusions354.1 Key Lessons Learned
1. Underdevelopment of AI and ICT 
• Pacific Island nations generally lack AI-related strategies and governance 
frameworks, as well as the regulatory structures necessary for AI 
development.
• The region exhibits economic vulnerability and underdeveloped ICT 
infrastructure, posing challenges to AI integration and growth.
• Establishing comprehensive strategies, regulatory frameworks, and 
investing in ICT infrastructure are critical steps for fostering sustainable AI 
development in the Pacific Islands.
2. Weak Awareness of AI
• While there are attempts to introduce AI, most efforts are focused on ICT.
• Most educational and awareness efforts are related to digital technology 
itself, with little focus on AI.
3. Challenges posed for Regional Coordination
• The dispersal and uniqueness of each island state make a one-size-fits-all 
solution difficult.
• The need for more active ICT and AI-dedicated regional bodies and 
initiatives.
• Regional cooperation is often characterized by sporadic, one-off events 
rather than being part of a well-coordinated, continuous strategy that 
fosters long-term linkages and sustained collaboration.
4. Global Cooperation
• Established guidelines from the US, China, and the EU provide a late-mover 
advantage for the Pacific Islands to selectively adopt best practices.
• Australia and New Zealand’s AI strategies and guidelines can serve as useful 
references tailored to the Pacific region.
• Considering the economic vulnerability of the Pacific Islands, economic 
cooperation with international actors and intergovernmental organizations 
is crucial.364.2 Recommendations 
1. Develop Foundational AI Guidelines and Approaches  for the Pacific 
Islands
• Create Comprehensive AI Development Guidance:  
Develop detailed AI development guidance documents tailored to the 
unique conditions of the Pacific Islands. This should be based on thorough 
assessments of the region’s AI ecosystems, taking into account local needs, 
capabilities, and challenges.
• Establish National AI Strategies and Roadmaps:  
Formulate foundational AI strategies and roadmaps for each Pacific Island 
nation, providing clear directions for AI development and integration. These 
strategies should outline specific goals, milestones, and actions needed to 
advance AI in various sectors such as healthcare, education, and disaster 
management, and should build on comprehensive AI diagnostics for each 
country.
• Enhance AI Governance:  
Facilitate the enhancement of AI governance across the Pacific Islands by 
continuously supporting member states to establish adequate regulatory 
frameworks, ethical guidelines, and policies to manage AI development 
and utilization responsibly and sustainably.
• Establish a Pacific Island AI Technical Assistance Facility:  
Undertake the scoping, establishment, and funding of a dedicated facility 
aimed at providing technical and financial support for member-state 
initiatives. This facility will play a pivotal role in facilitating the development 
and implementation of country-focussed AI strategies, offering specialized 
expertise, and helping to tackle emerging AI challenges through capacity-
building programs and resource allocation.
2. Strengthening AI Regulatory Frameworks for the Pacific Islands
• Develop comprehensive and harmonized legislation addressing data 
privacy, consumer protection, and cybercrime to create a robust regulatory 
framework for AI.
• Encourage Pacific Island nations to collaborate on regulatory 
standards, ensuring consistency and facilitating seamless AI  
adoption across the region.
3. Raising Digital Literacy and Awareness
• Develop tailored AI curricula for schools and universities, train educators, 
and create online learning platforms to make AI education accessible, 
especially in remote areas.
• Conduct needs assessments with local stakeholders, promote  
public-private partnerships, and implement pilot projects to develop  
and showcase AI solutions addressing unique local issues like climate 
change and healthcare.• Create Comprehensive AI Development Guidance:  
• Establish National AI Strategies and Roadmaps:  
• Enhance AI Governance:
• Establish a Pacific Island AI Technical Assistance Facility:  37• Introduce government, academia and industry partnerships to 
provide internships and practical AI project opportunities, invest 
in local AI research and development, offer workforce training, and  
support community outreach to raise AI awareness and advocate  
for enabling policies.
4. Strengthening International and Regional Cooperation
• Foster collaboration with key entities such as ASEAN, and international 
governmental organizations (IGOs) like the World Bank (WB), USAID, 
UNESCO, and UNDP to deepen ICT and AI partnerships and enhance 
mutual support.
• Cultivate regional ICT and AI cooperation among Pacific Islands by  
leveraging regional organizations such as the PIF and the Pacific 
Community, facilitating knowledge exchange and joint initiatives.
• Improve interoperability and efficiency of AI-related projects by  
coordinating and streamlining efforts, minimizing duplication and 
maximizing impact across multiple initiatives.
• Explore specific opportunities for collaboration in AI-related domains with 
ASEAN countries under the ASEAN-Pacific Islands Forum Memorandum 
of Understanding signed in 2023, fostering cross-regional partnerships and 
knowledge sharing.
5. Emphasizing Pacific Islanders’ AI Agency 
• Encourage active engagement and collaboration of Pacific Islanders in 
shaping and executing national and regional AI projects, ensuring their 
voices are heard and considered throughout the process.
• Partner with local communities to co-create AI strategies and policies that 
are reflective of their unique needs, values, and aspirations, fostering a 
sense of ownership and inclusivity in AI development and deployment.
Annexes
Regional and Country AI Profiles39The Pacific Islands Forum (PIF) is the major Pacific Island intergovernmental 
organization established in 1971, comprising 18 member countries and 
territories from the Pacific region, including Australia, New Zealand, and 
16 Pacific Island nations. Headquartered in Suva, Fiji, the Forum serves as a 
platform for member states to collaborate on regional issues, enhancing 
political and economic cooperation, and fostering collective action. 
PIF’s primary objective is to promote regional stability and prosperity by 
addressing the unique challenges faced by the Pacific islands, including  
their geographical isolation, vulnerability to natural disasters,  
and economic dependencies.
One of the core focuses of the Pacific Islands Forum is sustainable  
development, with a significant emphasis on combating climate change, 
which poses an existential threat to many low-lying island nations. The Forum 
advocates for strong international climate action and supports local adaptation 
and mitigation strategies. Economic development is another critical area, 
where PIF works to enhance trade, investment, and infrastructure development 
through initiatives like the Pacific Agreement on Closer Economic Relations 
(PACER) Plus. These efforts aim to create a more integrated and resilient 
regional economy that can withstand global economic fluctuations.
The Pacific Islands Forum also plays a crucial role in regional security,  
promoting cooperation on maritime security, transnational crime, and 
disaster response. Through frameworks like the Biketawa Declaration, PIF  
has established mechanisms for regional crisis management and peace 
keeping. The Forum engages with international partners and organizations 
to represent the collective interests of its member states on the global stage, 
ensuring that the Pacific voice is heard in global policy dialogues. By fostering 
a spirit of solidarity and shared purpose, the Pacific Islands Forum continues 
to be a vital institution for the advancement of peace, stability, and sustainable 
development in the Pacific region.
Through the Pacific Islands Forum, the Pacific Island Leaders in 2022 
launched the 2050 Strategy for the Blue Pacific Continent50, an ambitious 
framework designed to guide the collective vision and development priorities 
of the Pacific Islands region over the next three decades. This strategy 
emphasizes the importance of regional solidarity, resilience, and sustainable 
development in addressing the unique challenges faced by Pacific nations. 
Central to the strategy are goals related to climate action, economic growth, 
and environmental stewardship, recognizing the critical importance  
of the ocean and its resources to the livelihoods and cultural heritage  
of Pacific peoples. 
In particular, the 2050 Strategy, under the Thematic Area on Technology 
and Connectivity, calls for: “... a well-connected region that ensures inclusive, 
affordable and accessible air, sea and land transport and ICT technology 
infrastructure and services. Technology and infrastructure is often unaffordable, 
inaccessible, and difficult to maintain. 1. Overview of Regional Initiatives40The adoption of new and emerging sustainable digital technologies requires 
effective partnerships and appropriate regional regulatory arrangements that 
respect the region’s shared values.’’ 
In September 2023, the PIF and ASEAN signed a Memorandum of  
Understanding (MoU) to pave the way for potential cooperation between 
ASEAN and PIF in areas of common interest51. Given the common characteristics 
of selected ASEAN and Pacific Island nations, the general areas outlined in 
the MOU include many that would benefit greatly from consideration of AI 
elements. 
In January 2024, the United States Agency for International Development 
(USAID) initiated the Digital Connectivity and Cybersecurity Partnership (DCCP) 
- Pacific project to bolster internet connectivity in Pacific Island Countries. 
Collaborating with partners such as the Asia Open RAN Academy and USAID 
Indo-Pacific Opportunity Project, the initiative aims to equip the region with a 
skilled workforce and leverage Open RAN technology. This collaborative effort 
seeks to develop a curriculum for regional universities, enhance workforce 
capacity, and support the deployment of Open RAN technology. By enabling 
multiple telecommunications providers to collaborate, this technology extends 
seamless connectivity to areas lacking traditional infrastructure, thereby 
expanding secure digital coverage across the Blue Pacific Continent. With an 
equipped workforce, the initiative aims to extend connectivity to remote and 
low-populated areas, fostering job creation for Pacific Islanders and facilitating 
access to newer services like 5G at competitive rates52.
The Pacific Digital Economy Programme (PDEP) is a collaborative effort 
between the United Nations Capital Development Fund (UNCDF), United 
Nations Development Programme (UNDP), and the United Nations Conference 
on Trade and Development (UNCTAD), aimed at fostering inclusive digital 
economies in the Pacific. Supported by administrative assistance from UNDP, 
PDEP leverages UNCDF’s expertise in digital finance and UNCTAD’s work in 
e-commerce. It operates region-wide research and capacity-building activities 
while providing tailored technical assistance to specific countries. PDEP closely 
collaborates with the Pacific Island Forum Secretariat and various stakeholders 
to create a conducive environment for digital innovation. Funded by the 
Government of Australia and the European Union, the program runs from 2021 
to 2025, contributing to the region’s digital advancement53.
In the midterm review of the program in November 2022, it was found that the 
program proved highly relevant in supporting and fostering an inclusive digital 
economy in the selected countries. Stakeholders noted positive spillover effects 
from the Fintech challenge, leading to increased awareness, knowledge, and 
network expansion. The program’s efficiency was commendable, although 
some bureaucratic processes were deemed time-consuming. Stakeholders 
appreciated the local presence of the program staff in various countries. While 
the program is on track to deliver stakeholder-level outcomes, there’s a need to 
re-evaluate overly ambitious client-level targets. Coherence between various 
activities could be improved, and sustainability efforts should focus on nurturing 
progress and building local capacity. Suggestions were made to enhance 
coherence, effectiveness,and sustainability through strategic partnerships and  
capacity-building initiatives54.41The Smart Villages and Smart Islands initiative in Vanuatu, launched in 
2020, aims to bridge the digital gap in rural areas like Malekula. Led by the 
International Telecommunication Union (ITU) and supported by the Vanuatu 
government and partners, it’s making strides in connectivity, digital skills, and 
tailored services. In 2022, highlights include forming taskforces, enhancing 
solar-powered connectivity, and securing additional funding. Looking forward, 
the focus is on expanding digital literacy training. Positive impacts are already 
visible, such as improved online education access and local businesses using 
digital skills for marketing55.
42As part of Australia’s Artificial Intelligence Action Plan (Action Plan), the 
Australian government published an implementation plan related to the 
2021-22 AI budget56. The plan comprehensively addresses the four growth 
areas targeted within the 2021-2022 financial year. With the allocation of 
$124.1 million towards strengthening Trustworthy AI in Australia, the strategy 
supports Australia’s goal of becoming a leader in the digital economy by 2030.
The Australian Government has also recently announced further measures in 
the 2023-24 Budget to support the responsible use of AI, building on these 
previous initiatives. The 2023–24 Budget contains $75.7 million of funding for 
AI initiatives to help realise these opportunities. This includes: 
• $17 million for the AI Adopt program. This program will create five new 
centers nationwide, giving small and medium enterprises (SMEs) support 
and training to make more informed decisions about using AI to improve 
their business. The initiative invited applications for grants of $3 million to 
$5 million over four years.
• $21.6 million to expand the remit of the National AI Centre, executing 
significant research and providing leadership for the AI industry around 
Australia. This builds on the existing funding of $8 million from the 2021–22 
Budget ($2.6 million in 2023–24). 
• $34.5 million of continued funding for the Next Generation 
Artificial Intelligence and Emerging Technologies Graduates 
programs to attract and train the next generation of job-ready  
AI specialists.
Australia has no legislation specifically designed to regulate the use of AI. 
Yet, there are existing regulations that have the potential to intersect with 
the AI ecosystem outlining fundamental requirements for AI. These include 
the Corporations Act 2001 (Cth), financial and banking regulations and laws, 
and anti-discrimination Acts’AC (both enacted and upheld by the federal and 
state governments). But most importantly, the Privacy Act 1988 (Cth) details 
protections for individuals as it relates to collection, use, disclosure and right 
to access personal information, through regulating government and large 
organizations (those with greater than $3 million AUD p.a turnover). The Act 
Includes 13 privacy principles, covering a variety of dealings with personal 
information, such as cross-border disclosure of personal information and 
disclosure of personal information.
In June 2023, the Department of Industry, Science, and Resources sought  
public consultation on a discussion paper57 addressing the risks and responsible 
use of Artificial Intelligence (AI) (Discussion Paper). The Discussion Paper, 
informed by the March 2023 Rapid Response Information Report, focused 
on potential legal and practical mechanisms to support the responsible 
development and deployment of AI in Australia.2. Australia43On 17 January 2024, the Australian Government released its interim response 
(the Response) to consultations held on Safe and Responsible AI in Australia, 
which provides an indication of the steps the Government will be taking to 
ensure that AI is designed, developed, and deployed safely and responsibly in 
Australia while maximizing the opportunities that AI presents58.
The safe and responsible deployment and adoption of AI present significant 
opportunities for Australia to improve economic and social outcomes. AI has 
been identified as a critical technology in Australia’s national interest59. In its 
recent 5-year Productivity Inquiry report, the Productivity Commission (PC) 
identified AI as one of the transformative digital technologies that can help 
to drive productivity growth in Australia, including through the support it 
provides for the production and adoption of robotics. McKinsey has estimated 
that automation, including AI, could cumulatively add between $1.1 trillion and 
$4 trillion to the Australian economy by the early 2030s.
The Singapore-Australia Digital Economy Agreement signed in 2020 furthers 
the nations’ free-trade agreements into the digital space60. The Agreement 
is inclusive of seven bilateral memoranda of understanding (MoUs) signed, 
setting the foundations for future collaborative projects and agreements. One 
of the MoUs is specific to AI and aims to develop and encourage the sharing of 
best practices and the adoption of ethical governance frameworks for AI.
The Australian Government is taking steps to boost its practices to support 
responsible AI in recognition of public expectations that governments model 
best practices and lead by example. The Digital Transformation Agency (DTA) 
has issued guidance on public sector adoption of AI as part of its Australian 
Government Architecture (AGA)61. Further, the Office of the Commonwealth 
Ombudsman’s Automated decision-making better practice guide provides a 
practical checklist for Australian Government agencies implementing AI and 
ADM systems.44While there is no national AI strategy, The Cook Islands celebrated a key 
milestone in its digital transformation journey with the launch of its National 
Digital Strategy for 2024 to 2030 in February 2024. The formulation of the 
Strategy was supported by the United Nations Development Programme 
(UNDP), in collaboration with the Government of Cook Islands, under the 
project: Utilizing digitalization to accelerate the achievement of the Sustainable 
Development Goals in the Cook Islands.
The Strategy was developed and informed by a comprehensive functional 
analysis provided by the Digital Readiness Assessment conducted for the 
Cook Islands in 2023, which identified strategic entry points, bottlenecks, and 
gaps. The six strategic priorities of the Cook Islands’ National Digital Strategy: 
infrastructure, capacity building, enabling environment, talent development, 
innovation, and digital private sector, fully align with and therefore support, 
the goals and outcomes of the region’s 2050 Strategy for the digital economy, 
digital literacy, digital skills, and capability, as well as inclusive and sustainable 
digital technologies62.
In 2023, The Cook Islands launched the Cook Islands National Security Policy 
2023–2026. The policy  presents eight national security pillars – machinery 
of government, national systems, economy, human security, environment, 
political stability and good governance, transnational organised crime,  
and cyber-crime63.
In October 2023, there was the official launch of the country’s national ICT 
Policy and the UN Resident Coordinator present to reaffirm UNDP and the 
UN’s Commitment to supporting the Digital Transformation journey of 
Cook Islands. Their Digital Transformation Stakeholder Workshop included 
the review, discussions, and feedback on the Digital Readiness Assessment  
(DRA) report64.3. Cook Islands45There is no active Digital/AI strategy or policy in the country. 
There are no country-specific governing laws around Privacy and Data 
protection in the country, outside of the telecommunications context. 
Sections 349 and 350 of Title 21 of the Federated States of Micronesia Code 
require telecommunications providers to maintain the confidentiality of 
customer information and communications. Specifically, section 349 prohibits 
the collection, use, maintenance, or disclosure of any customer’s information 
without their consent. It also requires the implementation of appropriate 
security measures to prevent unauthorized access to or disclosure of customer 
information65.
In 2020, The World Bank’s Board of Executive Directors approved a US$30.8 
million grant to improve the digital connectivity of the Federated States of 
Micronesia (FSM). The Digital Federated States of Micronesia project supported 
the rollout of terrestrial fiber infrastructure, connected outer islands to basic 
broadband services, established the critical foundations for digital government 
services, and strengthened the legal and regulatory enabling environment for 
the digital economy to thrive in FSM66.4. Federated States of Micronesia465. Fiji
In December 2023, the Fiji Government’s Cabinet approved the development 
of a National Digital Strategy67. The Cabinet announcement stated that the 
National Digital Strategy “will lay out a coordinated approach to scale digital 
technology to drive economic growth and sustainable development.” It will 
provide the much-needed policy certainty to propel innovation, reinvigorate 
and intensify private sector investment. The National Digital Strategy will be 
accompanied by an Action Plan to further elaborate on the thematic areas 
outlined in the Strategy.
Fiji does not have specific legislation for personal data protection. However, the 
right to personal privacy, including the confidentiality of personal information, 
is protected under Clause 24 of the 2013 Constitution. In addition, several 
sector-specific laws address the unauthorized disclosure of personal or client 
information:
• The Banking Act of 1995 prohibits central bank personnel (Section 
27) and licensed financial institution personnel (Section 71) from  
unauthorized disclosure.
• Fiji Revenue and Customs Service Act of 1998 restricts tax officials from 
unauthorized information disclosure (Section 52(2)).
• Medical and Dental Practitioner Act of 2010 mandates confidentiality 
for statutory administrators concerning data obtained during their  
duties (Section 126).
• Rules of Professional Conduct and Practice of the Legal Practitioners Act 
of 2009 outline that legal practitioners must maintain confidentiality 
regarding information received from or on behalf of clients.
• Cybercrime Act of 2021 defines “computer data” broadly enough to include 
personal data if stored in a computer system68.
DigitalFIJI, also known as the Digital Government Transformation (DGT) 
Programme, is a 4-year digital transformation programme spearheaded by 
the Fijian Government to implement a number of government applications, 
enhance the overall ICT infrastructure, and build and develop capacity in 
government digital transformation. The Digital Government Transformation 
Programme is in line with the Fijian Government’s 5-year and 20-year National 
Development Plan (NDP) which calls for the steady improvement of the quality 
and accessibility of government services. The digitalFIJI strategy has set its 
sights on cultivating a $1 billion digital economy in Fiji by 2030, equivalent in 
scale to the nation’s thriving tourism sector69.
In November 2022, the UN Capital Development Fund (UNCDF) and Tractable 
launched a partnership to utilize AI for faster disaster recovery in Fiji. This project 
involves creating a smartphone app for residents to quickly report property 
damage after disasters, using Tractable’s AI to assess and expedite insurance 
claims. This collaboration is expected to streamline the claims process, improve 
financial tool access for underserved communities, and develop climate 
disaster risk financing solutions against escalating climate challenges in the 
South Pacific70.47In November 2023, a delegation from Fiji National University visited Singapore 
to focus on digital transformation, particularly governance and public-private 
partnerships. They observed practical applications in sectors including 
construction, education, and the arts. The visit was organized under a bilateral 
cooperation project on Innovation, Digital Transformation, and Productivity 
Improvement, led by the Singapore Productivity Centre (SGPC) and the APO 
Secretariat. This project introduced the delegates to Singapore’s advanced 
digital transformation strategies and included guided sessions and site visits71.
In March 2024, Fiji’s Deputy Prime Minister and Minister for Communications 
announced the country’s plans to establish a framework to safeguard internet 
users against AI threats. Aligning with a broader cybersecurity strategy, the 
plan aims to incorporate AI governance to enhance national security. While Fiji 
intends to develop this using its own technology resources, potential support 
from other countries will be sought if necessary72.48In 2017, the French Polynesia government began establishing its digital action 
plan, Smart Polynesia to support and accompany digital transformation. As 
part of this, the ‘Digital Ecosystem Development Plan 2017–2023’ (Plan de 
développement de l’écosystème numérique 2017–2023) was drafted with 
the goal of achieving a digitally empowered French Polynesia that leverages 
technology for sustainable development, improved public services, and 
enhanced economic opportunities73. The execution framework is as follows:
• Digital Ecosystem:  
Supportive environment for digital businesses, including the creation of a 
digital hub (“Pacific DigiPol”).
• Infrastructure and Equipment:  
Expansion of high-speed internet access, including the deployment of fiber 
optic networks and international cable connections.
• E-Administration:   
Modernizing public administration through digital services and platforms.
• Sectoral Digitalization:  
Digital technology across various sectors such as education, health, tourism, 
and other economic activities.
• Digital Governance: 
Regulation and governance of digital activities, ensuring alignment with 
national and international standards.
In December 2023, tourism operators and stakeholders in Tahiti participated 
in the Pacific Digital Champions training program, funded by the New Zealand 
Government. The 1.5-day workshop received praise for its relevance to industry 
demands, covering a broad range of digital topics. The program, part of the 
Pacific Digital Tourism Transformation Project, aims to equip individuals with 
essential digital skills74.6. French Polynesia
