• Digital Governance: 49In 2019, the Kiribati National ICT Policy was updated, recognizing the pivotal 
role of Information and Communication Technologies (ICTs) in the nation’s 
economic progress and overall well-being. Aligned with the Government 
manifesto and Kiribati 20-year Vision, the policy emphasizes the importance 
of ICTs as crucial infrastructure. Despite geographical challenges, support from 
partners like the World Bank and Australia/New Zealand is helping overcome 
barriers to connectivity.
Since the introduction of the National ICT Policy in 2011, significant progress 
has been made in creating a competitive market and improving services. 
However, challenges persist, with 30 percent of the population lacking mobile 
phone services. The policy prioritizes achieving universal, affordable, and 
reliable connectivity, focusing on the integration of artificial intelligence (AI) 
and other emerging technologies.
It outlines plans to leverage AI, blockchain, big data analytics, IoT, 5G, 
and e-Commerce for government service transformation and economic 
development. The policy addresses the need for safeguards in areas like child 
protection and cybersecurity, aligning with broader objectives in tourism, 
fisheries, trade, and e-Commerce. By consolidating existing projects and 
setting new goals, the policy calls for collaborative efforts across ministries, the 
private sector, and civil society, emphasizing the transformative potential of 
ICTs, particularly AI75.
Kiribati has no country-specific governing laws around Privacy and Data 
Protection. However, several indirect legislations cover the area. Article 3 of 
the Constitution of Kiribati (amended in 2018) stipulates the privacy of homes 
and other property. Additionally, the Cybercrime Act 2021, specifically through 
Article 30 titled “Conditions and Safeguards for Protection of Rights,” outlines 
the execution of powers and procedural safeguards for children76.
In July 2023, the Tourism Authority of Kiribati (TAK) actively participated in a 
two-day Consultation Workshop dedicated to developing the Kiribati Digital 
Strategy. Facilitated by the Digital Transformation Office (DTO) of the Ministry 
of Information, Communications, and Transport (MICT) in collaboration with 
the International Telecommunication Union (ITU) and the United Nations 
Office for Project Services (UNOPS), the workshop gathered representatives 
from various government and private sector entities.
The Kiribati Digital Strategy, poised to be a pivotal initiative for the Government 
of Kiribati (GoK), aims to propel digital transformation across diverse sectors, 
including tourism. TAK recognizes the strategy’s crucial role in fostering 
technological advancements, improving communication, and stimulating 
economic growth. The approach emphasizes a comprehensive involvement 
of both the government and society at large77.7. Kiribati50In 2021, the World Bank approved a $30 million project to improve internet 
access and promote digital development in the Marshall Islands. The project, 
titled as Digital Republic of the Marshall Islands, aimed to provide faster, more 
reliable, and more affordable internet access across the country, which at that 
time had limited and expensive online connectivity. In addition to improving 
internet access, the project aimed to promote private sector investment in 
climate-resilient digital infrastructure and establish the foundations for digital 
government services and the digital economy.
The project focussed on four areas:
• Expanding digital infrastructure:  
The project invested in energy-efficient, disaster- and climate-resilient 
digital infrastructure across all 24 inhabited atolls and islands, including 
Majuro and Ebeye.
• Digital government platforms:  
The project focused on investments in digital government platforms, 
cybersecurity, and digital skills, prioritizing key services such as e-health 
and e-education.
• Digital job creation and skill development:  
The project supported the development of digital jobs, skills training, and 
initiatives to close gender gaps in the access and use of digital infrastructure 
and services.
• Strengthening policy and regulation:  
The project supported the Ministry for Transport, Communications and 
Information Technology in strengthening the policy, legal, and regulatory 
environment around the digital economy and digital government services. 
This included reforms related to data protection and privacy, cybersecurity, 
and combating cybercrime78.
In 2023, the Republic of the Marshall Islands (RMI) and Bitt signed a 
memorandum of understanding (MOU) with a vision to launch a pilot 
initiative geared towards implementing a national digital payments system 
(NDPS) using the US Dollar. This partnership is driven by the aspiration to 
leverage technology’s potential for advancing financial inclusion, improving 
transparency, optimising operational processes, and empowering the citizens 
and businesses of the Marshall Islands79.
Also, the Republic of Marshall Islands does not have a personal data protection 
law, nor any legislation governing cybersecurity. The Criminal Code 2011 [31 
MIRC Ch.1] includes a provision on violations of privacy (§250.12. of the Criminal 
Code 2011), which covers unlawful eavesdropping or surveillance and breach of 
privacy of messages and frames such conducts as misdemeanours80.8. Marshall Islands
