• Strengthening policy and regulation:  51There is no active digital strategy or policy around artificial intelligence in 
the country. However, Nauru’s digital strategy is aligned with the Nauru 
Sustainable Development Strategy (NSDS), specifically targeting Priority Area 
3: Infrastructure Goal 5 focusing on universal and reliable access to competitive 
communication services and viable media. By committing to service delivery, 
user satisfaction, capacity building, and a collaborative work environment, the 
Department of Information, Communications, and Technology aims to:
• Provide and support national and government telecommunications 
infrastructure to support high-speed transfer of data securely.
• Provide safe, secure and integrated information technology solution that 
aligns with the e-government strategy.
• Provide technical support to all government departments relating to 
information technology
There are no country specific governing laws around Privacy and Data 
Protection as well. However, several indirect legislations cover the area.
• The Cybercrime Act 2015 criminalizes several offences that BC and 
its additional protocol also cover illegal access (Part I, art. 6), illegal 
Interception (Part I, art. 7), illegal data interference (part I, art. 8), and  
data espionage (Part I, art. 9)
• The Communications and Broadcasting Act 2018 introduces safeguards on 
the protection of subscriber’s information and communication, including 
confidentiality of subscribers’ information (Part 8, art. 48) and confidentiality 
of subscriber communications (Part 8, art.49)81
Despite the lack of digital strategies or policies, Nauru has displayed progress 
in information technology with governmental efforts and international 
partnerships enhancing its digital capacity.
In May 2018, the Asian Development Bank (ADB) signed agreements with 
Nauru to enhance its internet connectivity through a submarine cable project. 
Providing a $15 million grant for Nauru, the project aims to deliver high-speed 
internet to the country, thereby improving access to economic and social 
opportunities. Partly co-financed by the World Bank, this initiative supports 
ADB’s Pacific Approach by investing in ICT to promote inclusive growth in the 
Pacific region82.9. Nauru52In January 2021, Nauru agreed to join the US Clean Network Initiative to 
bolster its dedication to international digital trust standards. This agreement 
included commitments to protect personal data, intellectual property, and the 
national security interests of both nations by restricting the use of high-risk 5G 
equipment suppliers83. 
As recent as November 2023, Nauru made significant progress towards 
digitizing its national health system with the introduction of the Tamanu 
electronic medical record software at the Naoero Public Health Centre, 
following its successful launch at the Republic of Nauru Hospital earlier in June. 
Tamanu is expected to facilitate a swift transition from paper-based to digital 
records and support real-time access to patient data and health indicators. 
With the nearing completion of its digital roadmap project, Nauru is on track 
to becoming one of the first Pacific nations with a fully connected medical 
records system84.
53There is no active AI strategy or policy on the island. There are no specific 
governing laws around Privacy and Data Protection as well. However, The 
Government of New Caledonia is encouraging the growth of its digital sector 
and enhancing economic integration in the Pacific region.
In August 2020, the New Caledonian government established a Digital 
Committee to improve and oversee the country’s digital sector development. 
The committee aims to align the efforts of various digital sector stakeholders 
with the Digital Economy Strategic Plan, aiming for the digital transformation 
of administrative services and partnerships among technology sector players85.
The Tech 4 Good New Caledonia Summit, held on November 30 and 
December 1, 2022, at Hotel Château Royal in Nouméa, brought together start-
ups, companies, and institutions from the Asia-Pacific region. Organized 
by La French Tech New Caledonia in collaboration with the Government of 
New Caledonia, the event featured plenary conferences, workshops, and the 
participation of digital advocates and leaders like Inès Leornarduzzi, focusing 
on fostering collaboration and addressing challenges in technology and 
innovation in New Caledonia and the South Pacific86.
In October 2023, a commission dedicated to AI was formed in New Caledonia. 
The commission involves key digital stakeholders, including the OPEN NC 
cluster, and aims to integrate AI into local businesses and address ethical, 
legal, and training challenges. Representatives from various sectors, such as 
telecommunications and banking, discussed the application of AI in their 
operations and committed to monthly meetings to advance AI integration 
within the New Caledonian professional landscape87.
10. New Caledonia54In 2021, New Zealand declared its AI Strategy at the Aotearoa AI Summit, 
which outlined a five-year roadmap for the development and use of AI in the 
country88. 
The strategy focuses on five key areas:
• Uniquely New Zealand:  
This principle emphasizes the importance of using AI in a way that reflects 
New Zealand’s values and priorities, such as its commitment to Te Tiriti o 
Waitangi (the Treaty of Waitangi) and its focus on sustainability.
• Human-centred and trusted AI:  
This principle emphasizes the importance of ensuring that AI is used in a 
way that is fair, unbiased, and transparent.
• Investment in the AI economy:  
This principle emphasizes the importance of supporting the development 
of a thriving AI ecosystem in New Zealand.
• Preparing the workforce:  
This principle emphasizes the importance of preparing New Zealanders for 
the changing nature of work in the AI era.
• Our place in the world:  
This principle emphasizes the importance of New Zealand playing a leading 
role in shaping the global conversation about AI.
In September 2023, Privacy Commissioner Michael Webster outlined guidelines 
for New Zealanders using AI tools to meet their obligations under the Privacy 
Act 2020. Emphasizing the importance of considering privacy, he highlighted 
that the Privacy Act applied to the collection, use, and sharing of personal 
information when utilizing AI tools, addressing challenges arising from the 
increasing use of such tools in New Zealand89.
In 2020, the New Zealand government released an ‘Algorithm Charter’90 - 
one of the very first AI-related standards intended to apply to all branches 
of government regarding the use of algorithms by government agencies. 
The World Economic Forum is also leading a multistakeholder, evidence-
based policy project in partnership with the government of New Zealand. 
The project aims at co-designing actionable governance frameworks for AI 
regulation91. Critical questions, such as how to ensure the responsible use of 
AI by governments, and how to ensure that AI systems currently deployed are 
effectively compliant with existing regulations, are part of its agenda.
Also signed in 2020, the focus of the Digital Economy Partnership Agreement 
(DEPA) between New Zealand, Singapore, and Chile was to establish new rules 
and practices for digital trade. Additionally, it aimed to encourage ongoing 
discussions on matters such as digital inclusion, including AI, inclusive trade, 
and support for SMEs in the digital economy92.11. New Zealand
• Uniquely New Zealand:  
• Human-centred and trusted AI:  
• Investment in the AI economy:  
• Preparing the workforce:  
• Our place in the world:  55In July 2023, the New Zealand government also released Generative AI 
Guidance for the Public Sector, which provides guidance on how public sector 
agencies can use generative AI responsibly and ethically93. Very few countries 
have specific rules for Generative AI. New Zealand spearheaded the way 
forward, achieving notable success as a pioneer in Gen AI governance with over 
5,000 views in the first six weeks, maintaining its position as a highly accessed 
resource on digital.govt.nz.
In November 2023, Callaghan Innovation and the AI Forum, New Zealand’s 
community of artificial intelligence technology champions, launched AI 
Governance, a new website aimed at promoting leadership in the responsible 
use and governance of Artificial Intelligence (AI) in Aotearoa New Zealand94.
As part of working across government to address some of these concerns, at 
this year’s Aotearoa AI Summit, Stats NZ, the country’s official data agency, 
introduced its interim Centre for Data Ethics and Innovation. This small group 
operating in Stats NZ will collaborate across the government, to help foster the 
trusted and ethical use of data by the government. Trust is widely considered 
to be essential to enabling data-driven innovation and is a key pillar (Mahi Tika) 
of the Government’s Digital Strategy for Aotearoa95.
They also have toolkits. The Lite Toolkit caters to start-ups and small 
organizations with limited AI experience, addressing challenges through 
components such as AI ethics principles and impact assessment. The Robust 
Toolkit focuses on ethical considerations for medium to large businesses 
integrating AI, emphasizing human values, risk mitigation, and stakeholder 
trust. The Comprehensive Toolkit is designed for complex organizations 
developing AI tools, providing governance throughout the AI life cycle 
with principles, assessment, mitigation, and management components. 
Customization is emphasized for optimal implementation, fostering trust, and 
minimizing societal risks96.
56Niue has been collaborating with the UNDP to develop its National Digital 
Strategy since 2023, aiming to establish the country as the leading digital 
community in the Pacific. The emerging strategy emphasizes creating safe, 
inclusive, and accessible digital services, which will streamline government 
operations and increase transparency, accountability, and convenience for 
public servants. It prioritizes digital literacy to help individuals take advantage 
of new opportunities. As part of its ongoing development, Niue is improving 
connectivity and establishing a robust approach to cybersecurity97. 
In March 2023, the Government of Niue initiated the use of the Digital 
Readiness Assessment (DRA) tool to evaluate its digital strengths, weaknesses, 
and potential opportunities. This follows the introduction of the tool to high-
level officials from various sectors during the National Digital Transformation 
Strategy Stakeholder Workshop on February 22, 2023, in Alofi. The workshop, 
a crucial element in Niue’s digital transformation journey, conducted a 
nationwide survey to ensure a consultative and gender-sensitive approach in 
mapping out the country’s digital landscape across key pillars: Government, 
Infrastructure, Regulation, Business, and People98.
The journey towards a digital system for Niue started in 2020 when the United 
Nations Economic Commission for Asia and the Pacific (ESCAP), with funding 
from Bloomberg Data for Health, worked with Niuean public servants to map 
current processes and identify areas for improvement99.
In October 2023, there was broad validation of the findings of the Digital 
Readiness Assessment in Niue, enabling the recommendations to contribute 
to the roadmap of the eventual National Digital Strategy. As such, the workshop 
focused on strengthening this document through local input relating to digital 
activities, priorities, and tackling existing challenges100.12. Niue57As a part of the Palau Development Plan, 2023-2026, the country aims to 
develop its ICT sector to achieve resilient, sustainable, and comprehensive 
coverage by 2026. The current reliance on a single cable for international 
bandwidth poses a risk to uninterrupted connectivity, and the Palau 
government realizes that the introduction of a second submarine cable 
(PC2) by 2025 is crucial to the initiative. The governmental regulatory efforts 
are concentrated on encouraging fair competition, protecting critical 
infrastructure, and adapting regulations to support sector dynamism and 
investment. The development strategy includes building a robust network 
infrastructure, ensuring universal high-speed connectivity, expanding 4G 
coverage, and supporting disaster preparedness to improve service delivery and  
economic development across Palau101.
In addition, Palau took a significant step forward in disaster risk management 
and climate change adaptation by launching its first-ever nationwide digital 
survey initiative. Financed by the Government of Japan and managed by 
UNDP, the ‘Enhancing Disaster and Climate Resilience in the Republic of 
Palau through Improved Disaster Preparedness and Infrastructure (EDCR)’ 
project utilized advanced Light Detection and Ranging (LiDAR) technology 
for accurate topographic data. The survey covered major islands, providing 
comprehensive information for vulnerability assessments. Led by the Palau 
Automated Land and Resource Information System (PALARIS) with support 
from Fugro, a Geo-data specialist company, the initiative aimed to strengthen 
Palau’s resilience to natural hazards and climate change. The generated data 
served as a baseline for various planning activities. The EDCR project, initiated 
in 2019, played a crucial role in providing essential equipment and support for 
Palau’s disaster risk management efforts102.
To achieve its economic and social objectives by enhancing its financial 
technology, the Palau government partnered with Ripple, a tech company 
streamlining global payments via blockchain, in 2021. The partnership aims 
to improve financial access for Palauans by focusing on efficient cross-border 
payments and the potential development of a national digital currency103. 
In December 2022, Palau officially launched Project Olangch at the National 
Hospital, a significant innovation in the country’s national healthcare. Enabled 
by collaboration between the Palau Ministry of Health and Human Services 
and Beyond Essential, and supported by the Australian Department of Foreign 
Affairs and Trade, the initiative is set to transform the patient care system and 
enhance efficiency across Palau’s health sector. By the replacement of outdated 
paper-based systems with a comprehensive ‘digital health ecosystem’, the 
country is expected to achieve streamlined operations, bolster patient data 
security and sharing, and significantly improve healthcare services104.13. Palau58Papua New Guinea (PNG) is making significant strides in embracing 
sustainable tourism and digital transformation. With the passing of the Digital 
Transformation Act in 2022 and the launch of the Digital Government Plan 
(2023-2027), the government aims to create an inclusive and empowered 
society through the digitalization of government services, skills development, 
and ICT infrastructure105.
The plan, which is hinged on the ICT Roadmap of 2018, the Papua New Guinea 
Digital Transformation Plan of 2020 and the Digital Government Act of 2022, 
seeks to completely digitize and automate all government systems and 
processes while extending the penetration of telecommunication coverage to 
reach about 90 per cent of the population by 2027. The Digital Government 
Plan, sets out a non-prescriptive path for the delivery of digital services, a 
resilient cyber environment, and for establishment and enforcement of digital 
services standards for public services. The plan serves as a guide for public 
bodies to take cues from106. 
Papua New Guinea has no country-specific governing laws around Privacy and 
Data Protection. However, the Cybercrime Code Act 2016 contains provisions 
relevant to cybersecurity and aspects of data protection107. 
In 2021,  Pacific Countries Centre for Judicial Excellence (PCCJE) held a  
webinar on the implications of Artificial Intelligence (AI) for the judiciary, 
specifically targeting judges. the webinar introduced AI, discussed its use in 
overseas judiciaries, and presented the PNG AI Judiciary Sentencing Engine108.14. Papua New Guinea
59While there is no specific document that addresses Samoa’s AI and digital 
development, ‘Samoa 2040’ (2021), the country’s national development plan, 
sets the digital economy as one of its national priorities109. 
In the year 2022, the nation organized a pivotal consultation workshop as part 
of the broader initiative for Strengthening Digital Transformation in the Pacific. 
This event underscored the steadfast commitment of the Government of 
Samoa to harness the advantages offered by Information and Communication 
Technology (ICT). The aim is to instigate profound changes by providing 
accessible, affordable, and high-quality services, ultimately enhancing both 
the economy and the overall quality of life for its citizens110.
The orchestrator of the Digital Transformation Strategy Workshop was the 
Ministry of Communications and Information Technology (MCIT), collaborating 
with esteemed partners such as the International Telecommunication 
Union (ITU) and the United Nations Development Programme (UNDP). This 
collaborative effort reflects Samoa’s dedication to embracing digital innovation 
for sustainable development.
In June 2023, the Commonwealth Telecommunications Organizations 
brought its edition of the Digital Transformation Centre Stage, focusing on 
the Independent State of Samoa and featuring Samoa’s Digital Pathway, the 
government’s Digital Transformation Strategy, Samoa’s National Identification 
Project (NIP) that revolutionized government service delivery, and the Maua 
e-commerce platform and payment gateway that enhanced the growth of 
Samoa’s digital economy. Samoa’s Digital Pathway proposed to revolutionize 
the way Samoans lived and worked, creating a transparent, accountable 
governance that was citizen-focused, inclusive, and ensured improved access, 
ICT adoption, and value creation111.
In November 2023, Samoa marked a significant step towards a financially 
and digitally inclusive future with the launch of its second National Financial 
Inclusion Strategy (NFIS 2) and the inaugural Digital & Financial Literacy Survey 
(DFLS) Report. Developed in collaboration with the UN Capital Development 
Fund (UNCDF), these initiatives aim to enhance financial inclusion, promote 
digital literacy, and empower citizens and businesses.
NFIS 2 provides a comprehensive roadmap with specific targets to boost 
financial inclusion across Samoa. The strategy aims to increase the number of 
adults accessing and using formal financial services by 40,000 by 2025/2026, 
with a focus on achieving gender balance, targeting at least 50 per cent of 
women112.15. Samoa60In 2022, the Solomon Islands crafted its inaugural national e-commerce 
strategy with UNCTAD’s support, emphasizing job creation and SME 
opportunities. Policymakers and private sector representatives convened in 
Honiara to finalize the strategy, aiming to advance e-commerce adoption and 
align with the National Development Strategy 2016-2035. Over 80 participants 
provided feedback, and upon government endorsement, the strategy will 
be implemented from 2022 to 2027, marking an exciting phase for digital 
development in the country113.
The Solomon Islands have no country-specific governing laws around Privacy 
and Data Protection. However, several indirect legislations cover the area114:
• The National ICT Policy outlines an action plan focused on cybercrime, 
aiming to provide legal protection against ICT-related risks and to equip 
regulatory, law enforcement, and judicial personnel with the necessary 
skills and resources to effectively administer and enforce ICT laws.
• Telecommunications Act in Part 19 outlines various offences related to data 
security, including unauthorized access, interception, alteration, destruction, 
and disclosure of data, as well as possession of devices intended for such 
activities.
In 2023, the Solomon Islands Government, via the Ministry for Communication 
and Aviation (MCA), revealed the country’s third Inclusive Digital Economy 
Score (IDES) at a consultation session on August 15th. Over 70 key stakeholders 
from the government, private sector, and academia participated. As one of the 
first four nations globally to pilot the Inclusive Digital Economy Score in 2020, 
the Solomon Islands consistently assessed progress in infrastructure, policy & 
regulation, skills, and innovation. Notably, the IDES score improved from 39 per 
cent in 2020 to 43 per cent in 2022115. IDES is a tool developed by the UNCDF 
to measure and track a country’s progress in developing an inclusive digital 
economy.16. Solomon Islands61The Government of Tonga launched the Tonga Digital Government Strategic 
Framework (2019-2024), a strategic plan that articulates and visions a digitally 
enabled government where government ministries and agencies adopt and 
use information communication and technologies (ICT) to promote inter-
governmental collaboration, transition away from a paper-based government, 
and promote efficient government service delivery to citizens of Tonga116.
The Pacific Digital Democracy Project, a collaborative initiative funded by the 
European Union and steered by the United Nations Development Programme 
(UNDP), aims to revolutionize digital governance and empower citizens in the 
Pacific region. Tonga is a key participant in this transformative initiative, which 
seeks to establish a strong commitment to digital rights and development 
principles among both government and civil society stakeholders117.
In August 2023, Tongan tourism industry leaders and key stakeholders solidified 
their dedication to embracing digital transformation. This commitment took 
shape during their participation in the Pacific Digital Champions training 
program from 15th-16th August, facilitated by the Pacific Tourism Organisation 
(SPTO) with funding from New Zealand’s Ministry of Foreign Affairs and Trade118.17. Tonga
62Tuvalu’s 2021-2030 National Strategy for Sustainable Development, “Te Kete,” 
prioritizes digital transformation as one of its foremost national outcomes to 
enhance societal well-being. This ambition is manifested through strategic 
initiatives aimed at securing access to cost-effective and dependable 
telecommunication services, promoting e-learning, establishing a secure 
digital space via cybersecurity measures, and crafting a comprehensive legal, 
regulatory, and standards framework to steer digital technology investments 
and applications119.
Tuvalu has no country-specific governing laws around Privacy and Data 
Protection. However, indirect legislations cover the area. For instance, the 
Tuvalu Telecommunications Corporation Act in Section 33 specifies offences 
related to telecommunications, including the modification, interception, and 
unauthorized disclosure of messages120.
In August 2023, the Tuvalu Department of Tourism and its stakeholders 
embraced the opportunity to progress digital transformation, through the 
Pacific Tourism Organisations (SPTO) Pacific Digital Champions training 
program. Funded by the New Zealand Government through its Ministry of 
Foreign Affairs and Trade, the training program is part of the “Pacific Digital 
Tourism Transformation Project” being implemented by SPTO121.
Facing the existential threat of rising sea levels, Tuvalu initiated the Future Now 
Project to preserve its nationhood, governance, and culture through digital 
transformation. The purpose of this initiative is to ensure the continuity of 
state functions and cultural identity, even if the population becomes globally 
dispersed. The project features the creation of a digital twin of Tuvalu, starting 
with the island of Te Afualiku, to capture its physical and cultural landscape. 
Utilising augmented and virtual reality, this project is expected to enable 
future generations of Tuvaluans to interact and maintain their culture in a 
digital realm122.18. Tuvalu6319. Vanuatu
“Vanuatu’s E-Commerce Strategy and Roadmap”, launched by the Ministry 
of Tourism, Trade, Commerce, and Ni-Vanuatu Business, aims to transform 
Vanuatu into a digital-friendly economy by 2026. The strategy focuses on 
improving digital infrastructure, legal frameworks, payment systems, and 
digital skills. The objective of the strategy is the enhancement of trade and 
business growth through e-commerce123.
Vanuatu is actively working towards establishing a data protection law with 
support from the Council of Europe. This effort aims to safeguard personal 
information and enhance digital trust in the country. Although draft bills and 
policies including a National Data Protection and Privacy Policy for 2023 have 
been proposed, they have not yet been enacted into law124. 
In 2020, Vanuatu’s government partnered with the International 
Telecommunication Union (ITU to launch the Smart Villages and Smart Islands 
initiative in the southern region of Malekula, where residents lack access to 
basic services such as healthcare, banking, and reliable power and water 
supply.
The initiative aimed to improve connectivity, build digital skills, and provide 
digital services that are cost-effective and suitable for the community’s needs. 
With support from various UN agencies and organizations, the program has 
made progress in providing digital literacy training, reconnecting villagers to 
the internet, and helping local businesses utilize digital tools for marketing. 
The initiative plans to continue expanding its efforts in 2024, with a focus on 
providing more digital literacy training for villagers, especially for young people, 
women, and people with disabilities. The positive impact of the initiative is 
already visible in the community, with students resuming their studies online 
and villagers using the internet for various purposes. The program’s success 
stories demonstrate Vanuatu’s potential to transform into a “Smart Island” 
through digital transformation at the grassroots level125.6420. Climate Change Initiatives in the Pacific Islands 
Country Climate Change Initiatives
Cook Islands • In 2011, the country established the Climate Change Cook Islands Office 
(CCCI) having moved to coordinate climate change actions across 
government, but it soon became apparent that limited human and financial 
resources were restraining the country’s valiant efforts.
• In February 2016, the Cook Islands became the first country in the Pacific 
to receive GCF readiness support. Since 2016, the Cook Islands has received 
three more readiness grants in 2017, 2018, and 2019, totalling USD 1.9 million.
• The target of 100 percent of electricity generation to be from renewable 
energy sources, is a central component of mitigation initiatives in the Cook 
Islands.
Federated States of 
Micronesia• The Federated States of Micronesia joined the Climate and Clean Air 
Coalition (CCAC) in 2021, committing to action to reduce short-lived climate 
pollutants.
• As the original proponent of the amendment to include HFCs 
(hydrofluorocarbons) under the Montreal Protocol, Micronesia has 
committed to accelerating the HFC phasedown under the Kigali 
Amendment domestically and internationally - helping other countries 
with the implementation of the Montreal Protocol. Work underway in 
Micronesia includes a pilot training institute that prepares refrigeration and 
air conditioning technicians to work with new energy efficient and climate- 
friendly technologies. The country is also mobilizing financing under the 
Kigali Amendment to support more ambitious action.
• Following the Strategic Development Plan, Micronesia developed the 
Climate Change Act in 2014 to address and integrate climate change action 
across ministries. The Climate Change and Sustainable Development 
Council were also established in 2017 to implement the Act.
• The Climate Change Act, adopted by the FSM Congress in late November 
2013 implements the recently adopted Nationwide Integrated Disaster Risk 
Management and Climate Change Policy. It instructs government offices 
and departments to prepare plans and policies consistent with the
• Climate Change Policy by October 2015; it also includes an obligation on the 
President to report to Congress on the progress of implementation of the 
Climate Change, and for the budget request to include one or more lines on 
the implementation of the Climate Change Policy.
Fiji • The Fiji Climate Change Act 2021 was approved by parliament on 
September 23, 2021, and came into force shortly after. The act establishes 
a legal framework for the country’s disaster risk management, adaptation, 
and mitigation actions. It also provides a legal basis for Fiji’s sustainable 
development goals, net-zero emissions target, and commitment to 
environmental protection.
• The National Climate Change Policy is a central policy instrument to protect 
Fiji’s development priorities from current, future and intergenerational 
climate change risks. The NCCP articulates national climate adaptation and 
mitigation objectives and strategies.
French Polynesia • Nationally Determined Contribution - The French Overseas Countries and 
Territories commit an economy-wide plan of limiting the increase in GHG 
emissions to 8.4 percent in 2030 compared to 2016 (6959 KtCO2e). French 
Polynesia states a reduction of 3.7 percent compared to 2016.65Country Climate Change Initiatives
Kiribati • Kiribati as part of Kiribati Climate Change Policy has committed to reducing 
emissions by 13.7 percent by 2025 and 12.8 percent by 2030 compared toa 
“business as usual” (BAU) projection. This is part of Kiribati’s Nationally 
Determined Contribution (NDC).
• The Kiribati Adaptation Program (KAP) is a US$5.5 million initiative that was 
originally enacted by the national government of Kiribati with the support of 
the Global Environment Facility (GEF), the World Bank, the United Nations 
Development Program, and the Japanese Government. Australia later joined 
the coalition, donating US$1.5 million to the effort. The program aims to take 
place over 6 years, supporting measures that reduce Kiribati’s vulnerability 
to the effects of climate change and sea level rise by raising awareness of 
climate change, assessing and protecting available water resources, and 
managing inundation.
Marshall Islands • In 2015, RMI became the first Small Island Developing State to present its 
post-2020 Nationally Determined Contribution. This committed the country 
to a 32 percent reduction of GHG emissions by 2025. The following year, RMI 
also committed to achieving 100 percent renewable energy by 2050.
• In October 2022, The Ministry of Health and Human Services (MOHHS) and 
Ministry of Environment Climate Change Directorate (CCD) of the Republic 
of Marshall Islands (RMI) are partnering with the World Health Organization 
(WHO) to tackle the health impacts of climate change and enhance pre- 
paredness for future health emergencies, including the next pandemic.
• With US$ 399,802 in funding support from the Green Climate Fund (GCF), 
the RMI Government and WHO will also focus on boosting the readiness of 
remote and outer islands of the RMI to protect health and save lives during 
future crises.
Nauru• Republic of Nauru’s Framework for Climate Change and Disaster Risk Re -
duction (RONAdapt) - This framework prioritizes water security, energy se- 
curity, food security, a healthy environment, healthy people, and productive, 
secure land resources.
• Nauru’s Nationally determined contribution (NDC) has been updated to 
include eight thematic areas, including “Loss & Damage”. The country’s goal 
is to reach net-zero emissions by 2050.”
New Caledonia • New Caledonia’s energy transition policy aims to make the territory energy 
self-sufficient and reduce greenhouse gases by at least 70 percent by 2035. 
The Energy Transition Scheme of New Caledonia (STENCS) aims to double 
the share of renewable energy by 2030.
Niue • Niue’s Nationally Determined Contribution (NDC) target for 2020 is 38 per- 
cent of its total electricity generation to come from renewable energy. This 
target is unconditional and not dependent on external funding. However, 
there has been no update on the performance or policy since.
Palau • Palau is a signatory to the Global Methane Pledge (GMP), which commits 
the country to the collective goal of reducing global methane emissions by 
at least 30 percent from 2020 levels by 2030.
• Palau has committed to reducing energy sector emissions by 22 percent by 
2025.
• In 2015, adopted the Palau Climate Change Policy: For Climate and Disaster 
Resilient Low Emissions Development. The policy seeks to work towards low 
carbon emission development, maximize energy efficiency, protect carbon 
sinks, and minimize greenhouse gas emissions.66Country Climate Change Initiatives
Papua New Guinea• Papua New Guinea has increased its level of ambition within its second 
NDC by enhancing the specificity of targets within existing sectors, such as 
energy, and adding in targets for the land use, land use change, and forestry 
sector and adaptation.
• The country aims to achieve carbon neutrality in energy industries by 2030.
• The second NDC is in line with Papua New Guinea’s Vision 2050, as well as 
the Climate Compatible Development Management Policy accords. The 
policy outlines the government’s commitment to be 50 percent carbon- 
neutral by 2030 and entirely carbon-neutral by 2050.
• Papua New Guinea, like many tropical countries, through deforestation, is 
contributing to the release of carbon dioxide, the most critical greenhouse 
gas. WCS PNG is working closely with the Papua New Guinea Government 
to develop and trial a national forest protection initiative known as REDD+ 
(Reduced Emissions from Deforestation and Forest Degradation ― the “+” 
refers to the other benefits of forests). Schemes like REDD+ should not only 
slow the release of carbon dioxide but have the potential to provide tangible 
benefits for forest communities.
Samoa • Samoa’s second Nationally Determined Contribution (NDC) aims to reduce 
greenhouse gas emissions by 26 percent by 2030 compared to 2007 
levels, or by 91 Gg CO2e compared to the new reference year. The NDC also 
includes a target to generate 100 percent of electricity from renewable 
energy by 2025.
• Instituted a Samoa Climate Change Policy 2020-2030. This document aims 
at enabling a resilient, safe and prosperous medium-term future for Samoa, 
while meeting the sustainable development goals.
Solomon Islands • The Solomon Islands’ Intended Nationally Determined Contribution (NDC) 
is a national policy that aims to reduce emissions by 27 percent by 2025 and 
45 percent by 2030, compared to a business-as-usual projection (BaU). The 
NDC also includes a commitment to achieve net zero emissions by 2050.
• Instituted the Solomon Islands National Climate Change Policy (NCCP) 2023- 
2032 and Long Term Low Emission Development Strategy (LT- LEDS). The 
NCCP 2023-2032 allows the Solomon Islands to address climate change, loss, 
and damage (L&D). The policy also aims to strengthen low emission status, 
economic growth, and development.
• Climate and Oceans Support Program in the Pacific Phase 3 (COSPPac3) 
- A 2023–2029 program that supports the Solomon Islands National 
Meteorological Service to provide climate and ocean monitoring and 
prediction services.
• An important contribution at the regional and community level is the Pacific 
Adaptation to Climate Change Project (PACC). The project is designed to 
promote climate change adaptation, and is based on the premise that 
adaptation is a key pre-requisite to sustainable development in Pacific Island 
Countries.67Country Climate Change Initiatives
Tonga • Tonga’s second Nationally Determined Contribution (NDC) focuses 
on adapting to climate change, including rising sea levels and higher 
temperatures. The plan includes:
• 2025: 30 percent of the country’s land used for forestry or agro-forestry
• Marine species: Expand the area covered by Marine Protected Areas 
and Special Management Areas to 30 percent of the country’s Exclusive 
Economic Zone
• Greenhouse gas emissions: 13 percent reduction in emissions by 2030 
compared to 2006, through a transition to 70 percent renewable electricity
• Tree planting: Plant one million trees by 2023
• The plan also aims to attract financial support from donors and private 
investors. The projects identified in the plan could reduce greenhouse 
gas emissions by over a million tCO2 by 2030, which would represent a 61 
percent reduction from 2006 levels.
• Instituted the 2035 Climate Change Policy - This policy aims to build 
resilience by redesigning policy to improve people’s ability to respond to the 
effects of climate change.
Tuvalu • In 2022, Tuvalu updated its nationally determined contribution (NDC) to 
climate change, focusing on mitigation and a zero-carbon development 
pathway by 2050. The updated NDC includes the following commitments:
• Reduce greenhouse gas emissions: 100 percent reduction in greenhouse 
gas emissions from the electricity sector by 2030, or almost zero emissions
• Increase energy efficiency: 30 percent increase in energy efficiency in 
Funafuti
• Reduce total GHG emissions: 60 percent reduction in total GHG emissions 
from the entire energy sector by 2030, compared to 2010 levels
• Instituted the National Climate Change Policy 2021-2030; This policy 
considers issues like vulnerability, disaster preparedness, and migration and 
relocation needs.
Vanuatu • NDC: Vanuatu will become 100 percent renewable by 2030, replacing nearly 
all fossil fuel requirements. This will reduce emissions in the energy sector by 
72Gg by 2030.
• In 2022, Vanuatu declared a climate emergency as part of a “climate 
diplomacy push”. The declaration was made ahead of a UN vote on the 
government’s application to have the International Court of Justice protect 
vulnerable nations from climate change.
• The Vanuatu Klaement Infomesen blong Redy, Adapt mo Protekt (Van- 
KIRAP) project is a five-year, USD 22 million project which aims to support 
climate resilient development in Vanuatu through the development, 
communication, and application of climate information services to benefit 
agriculture, fisheries, tourism etc.
