"234","235","235","A/Prof Alysia Blackham","https://consult.industry.gov.au/ai-mandatory-guardrails/submission/view/54"," 1 Submission to the Safe and responsible AI in Australia: Proposals paper for introducing mandatory guardrails for AI in high-risk settings  A/Prof Alysia Blackham, Melbourne Law School, University of Melbourne  I write this submission as an expert in equality law, who has written on the discrimination risks of AI technologies and algorithmic discrimination.1  I commend the proposal to introduce mandatory guardrails for AI in high-risk settings. As the Proposals Paper notes, voluntary guardrails are insufficient, and mandatory regulations are required to better manage the risks of AI. I also commend the proposal to place obligations on those across the AI supply chain, and throughout the AI lifecycle, to focus on preventing harm. I echo the Proposals Paper’s articulation of the significant risk of bias in AI technologies, which is consistent with my research findings.2 I note, too, that many of these risks apply to any automated process employing algorithmic decision-making, irrespective of whether it uses AI or more traditional non-AI algorithms. Regulation should be broad enough to capture the range of automated tools, defined broadly.  Beyond a focus on ‘risk’: the need for ‘redlines’  While a risk-based approach is an important development, and I commend the focus on testing, transparency and accountability, this is not enough to address the risks of AI. Rather, we need to ensure there are ‘redlines’ or prohibitions for use, as well as procedural guarantees for the development and deployment of AI. Indeed, the EU AI Act has been criticised for focusing only on a risk-based approach, which is seen as inadequate for the workplace in particular.3  As the Proposals Paper articulates, obligations under the guardrails can and should be in addition to other legal obligations, as under labour law and discrimination law. These, existing, legal obligations provide a number of ‘redlines’ which are already in place. In implementing these principles, though, regulators must be attuned to the potential need for additional ‘redlines’ in different contexts. ‘Redlines’ or prohibitions which might be considered in workplaces include, for example: o Prohibition of automated decision-making which substantially affects rights or interests, without an ultimate human decision maker or a ‘human in the loop’ as a check on decision-making;4 and o Prohibition of the use of AI to predict, or persuade against, the exercise of legal rights.5     1 Alysia Blackham, ‘Setting the Framework for Accountability for Algorithmic Discrimination at Work’ (2023) 47(1) Melbourne University Law Review 63. 2 Ibid. 3 Antonio Aloisi and Valerio De Stefano, ‘Between Risk Mitigation and Labour Rights Enforcement: Assessing the Transatlantic Race to Govern AI-Driven Decision-Making through a Comparative Lens’ (2023) 14(2) European Labour Law Journal 283 (‘Between Risk Mitigation and Labour Rights Enforcement’). 4 See, eg, the EU General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR) art 22. 5 See, eg, Proposal for the Directive of the European Parliament and of the Council on improving working conditions in platform work (8 March 2024) art 7 <https://data.consilium.europa.eu/doc/document/ST-7212-2024-ADD-1/en/pdf>; Jeremias Adams-Prassl et al, ‘Regulating Algorithmic Management: A Blueprint’ (2023) 14(2) European Labour Law Journal 124 (‘Regulating Algorithmic Management’).  2 Proposed principles: recommendations to better address discrimination risks  Further, in the proposed principles, reference is made to:  The risk of adverse impacts to an individual’s rights recognised in Australian human rights law without justification, in addition to Australia’s international human rights law obligations  Given the limited human rights protection in most Australian jurisdictions, I recommend rephrasing this to:  The risk of adverse impacts to an individual’s human rights (including rights recognised under domestic and international human rights law obligations).  While the framing of this provision is intended to capture discrimination risks, most people would not recognise ‘human rights’ as including discrimination law. I recommend, then, explicitly also including:  The risk of adverse impacts based on protected characteristics under state, territory or federal discrimination laws.  While the proposed principles offer a structured way of determining ‘high risk’ applications, there is likely to be debate as to how they apply. Given the significant power disparities in the workplace and in the employment relationship, and the economic and social importance of work to individuals, I recommend introducing a presumption that all use of AI in the workplace is ‘high risk’.  In the workplace, too, the Proposed mandatory guardrails for high-risk AI should include a requirement to consult with workers and their representatives, as part of the risk management process, and in testing, evaluating and monitoring AI systems.  Further, Guardrail 9: Keep and maintain records to allow third parties to assess compliance with guardrails should require those records to be made public, so that regulators, employees, and employee representatives can ensure compliance.  Positioning of regulation  Creating a dedicated AI Act, with tailored enforcement mechanisms (via Option 3: a whole of economy approach), would ensure there is expertise and focused attention on the risks of these new technologies. However, leveraging existing laws and enforcement mechanisms, within a clear framework (via Option 2: a framework approach) is likely to be a more cost-effective approach, which recognises the existing expertise of regulators in their areas of expertise. This approach also seems more adaptable to responding to new technologies as they emerge. If pursuing Option 2, however, there will be a need to work closely with existing regulators to ensure that they have the technical expertise and the funding to effectively monitor and enforce the new framework. Simply adding these new responsibilities to regulators’ existing remits, with no additional funding, is likely to lead to gaps in enforcement. There is a risk, too, that there will be delays in amending laws in certain areas, leading to gaps in regulation.    3 Thus, my recommendation would be to adopt Option 2, but with clear timelines for reform, and appropriate funding of regulators to ensure the effective enforcement of these new principles.  Other required reforms  As well as dedicated legislation in this area, there is also a need to strengthen other statutes – like discrimination laws – to ensure a proactive, preventative focus to the discrimination risks posed by these technologies.   Discrimination law needs reform to address the discrimination risks of automated decision-making tools and AI. Rather than relying on individuals to make a complaint, it is necessary to adopt positive, proactive duties on employers. This could be modelled, for example, on the existing positive equality duty under the Sex Discrimination Act 1984 (Cth) s 47C, but aimed specifically to address the discrimination risks posed by automated decision-making tools, and covering all protected grounds.   In particular, I have recommended6 the adoption of positive equality duties imposing obligations on employers that include requirements to:  • give proper consideration and take proportionate action to eliminate discrimination and advance equality of opportunity;7 • collect, analyse and publicize data on the protected characteristics of those in the workplace;8 • report on what data is being collected, for what purpose, and how it is being processed and used, and data quality control measures adopted; • report on what automated decision-making tools are being adopted, including:  • how the technology operates, and for what purpose, • the training and testing and organisational data being deployed, data quality measures in place, and how recently data were reviewed or renewed,  • how the system operates in the context of the specific workplace,9  • the role of human oversight in the process, and  • outcomes of automated decision-making tools across the workforce, including across protected characteristics; • adopt policies to demonstrate what is being done to address and eliminate discrimination; and • consult and engage with workers and worker representatives in the collection of data, and adoption and use of automated decision-making tools.  These duties should be enforceable by a statutory regulator, like the Australian Human Rights Commission, with powers akin to those in relation to the positive duty in s 47C. They should  6 Blackham (n 1). 7 See Alysia Blackham, Reforming Age Discrimination Law: Beyond Individual Enforcement (Oxford University Press, 2022) (‘Reforming Age Discrimination Law’) ch 7. 8 See, eg, Gender Equality Act 2020 (Vic); Lauren Ryan et al, Laying the Foundation for Gender Equality in the Public Sector in Victoria: Final Project Report (report, University of Melbourne, 2022) <https://doi.org/10.26188/19254539> (‘Laying the Foundation for Gender Equality in the Public Sector in Victoria’). 9 See, eg, Alina Köchling and Marius Claus Wehner, ‘Discriminated by an Algorithm: A Systematic Review of Discrimination and Fairness by Algorithmic Decision-Making in the Context of HR Recruitment and HR Development’ (2020) 13(3) Business Research 795, 839 (‘Discriminated by an Algorithm’).  4 also be enforceable by workers and worker representatives, to ensure multiple checks on compliance.  Australian privacy law also significantly lags behind countries like the UK, and the European Union. Automated decision-making tools are built on substantial quantities of data, yet Australian workers have few data protection rights. The Privacy Act 1988 (Cth) contains a blanket exception for ‘employee records’ – while an employer needs consent to initially gather new data, there are no limits placed on that data once it is held. The Privacy Act 1988 (Cth) also does not apply to small businesses – who employ the vast majority of workers in Australia. While the government has committed to considering reform in this area, this has not yet occurred. The Privacy and Other Legislation Amendment Bill 2024 (Cth) does not address these problems.  Adopting rigorous privacy law – like the GDPR – is critical for Australian law reform. In particular, this should entail new requirements for: • Transparency: so the data automated decision-making tools are based on, their use, and their outcome, is understandable, communicable, and able to be effectively challenged.10 • Substantive limits on when data can be collected, and when automated decision-making tools can be used. • Oversight of automated decisions or outcomes by a human decision-maker – an approach described as ‘human-in-command’.11 • Evaluation and consultation prior to the adoption of automated decision-making tools, to ensure the technology is being used for a legitimate purpose, is appropriate for achieving that purpose, and includes appropriate safeguards for individual rights. This takes the form, for example, of Data Protection Impact Assessments in the EU.12 It could also be embedded in collective bargaining and negotiation.13       
 10 Janine Berg, ‘Protecting Workers in the Digital Age: Technology, Outsourcing, and the Growing Precariousness of Work Automation, Artificial Intelligence, & Labor Law’ (2019) 41(1) Comparative Labor Law & Policy Journal 69, 91 (‘Protecting Workers in the Digital Age’). See GDPR arts 13, 15. 11 Global Commission on the Future of Work, Work for a Brighter Future (2019) 78, 43 <https://www.ilo.org/wcmsp5/groups/public/---dgreports/---cabinet/documents/publication/wcms_662410.pdf>. 12 GDPR art 9(1). 13 See, eg, Proposal for the Directive of the European Parliament and of the Council on improving working conditions in platform work (8 March 2024) art 8 <https://data.consilium.europa.eu/doc/document/ST-7212-2024-ADD-1/en/pdf>. ","Blackham_Submission_AI_guardrails.c6f8c14e","Blackham_Submission_AI_guardrails.c6f8c14e.pdf","pdf","C:\\Users\\james.barro\\Documents\\rsm-nvidia-hackathon\\data\\Submission_Docs\\Processed\\Blackham_Submission_AI_guardrails.c6f8c14e.pdf","1"
