Pathology Technology Australia
Published name
Pathology Technology Australia
1. Do the proposed principles adequately capture high-risk AI?
Yes
Please provide any additional comments.
Although there may need to be further consideration to unintended consequences that capture medical devices that are exempt or excluded from the TGA's ARTG. If the TGA assesses such devices as such a low risk that inclusion on the ARTG is unnecessary, will the devices still need to comply with the 10 guardrails? We could help construct use cases that better differentiate low risk medical devices that should not be burdened by the guardrails. It is likely to be an unacceptable burden for industry to comply with a separate conformity assessment process if a medical device is exempt or excluded by the TGA (low risk) but requires DISR conformity assessment (high risk).
Are there any principles we should add or remove?
No
Please provide any additional comments.
However, there are potential complications associated with medical devices and guardrails 1 and 10.
1. Accountability - we favour alignment with the Canadiana and European guardrails but also note the practice of larger software companies in the US. They make a point of publishing their responsibilities and accountabilities online, making it very clear to the consumer who is responsible for what. One aspect that may require further consideration is that of software incorporating AI that contain software from multiple suppliers (which is not uncommon). Who takes responsibility - the sponsor (reseller in Australia), the legal manufacturer of the software and/or the manufacturers of any embedded software.
10. Conformity Assessment - Medical Device manufacturers and sponsors are very accustomed to navigating the conformity assessment processes locally and internationally to facilitate inclusion of devices on the ARTG. There is a possible unintended consequence related to the DISR blanket high-risk setting for health where software is not classified as a medical device or if it is, it is exempt from the ARTG. In this setting TGA sees the software as low risk but DISR as high risk. It would be very unfortunate if this delivers two independent conformity assessment processes for health-related software. Such an outcome could be avoided with adoption of either regulatory options 1 or 2. Option 3 would drive to a point where two conformity assessment processes by (presumably) two different departments would be required. This would at best confuse suppliers and at worst, dive them from the market. A further option may be to exclude healthcare from the framework in preference for a health specific framework run by TGA and the Commission for Safety and Quality in Healthcare. This might be achieved relatively simply, as the DISR 10 guardrails map relatively well to TGA's guidelines.
Please identify any:
Health related devices that do not meet the definition of a medical device or those that do but are exempt or excluded.
2. Do you have any suggestions for how the principles could better capture harms to First Nations people, communities and Country?
No
3. Do the proposed principles, supported by examples, give enough clarity and certainty on high-risk AI settings and high-risk AI models? Is a more defined approach, with a list of illustrative uses, needed?
YES the principles give enough clarity and certainty
If you prefer a principles-based approach, what should we address in guidance to give the greatest clarity?
Yes, if considered in combination with the TGA definition of a medical device.
How can this list capture emerging uses of AI?
The TGA definitions already covers this as they are technology agnostic. Where attention could be placed is on devices that have undergone scope creep, to include AI. One again TGA should be managing this.
8. Do the proposed mandatory guardrails appropriately mitigate the risks of AI used in high-risk settings?
Yes
Please provide any additional comments.
In the health setting, TGA already have the processes in place that align with international regulatory and conformity assessment authorities.
Are there any guardrails that we should add or remove?
No
Please provide any additional comments
For health, the guardrails largely align with TGA guidelines.
9. How can the guardrails incorporate First Nations knowledge and cultural protocols to ensure AI systems are culturally appropriate and preserve Indigenous Cultural and Intellectual Property?
Unsure, but they do need to demonstrate sensitivity to cultural and language norms.
10. Do the proposed mandatory guardrails distribute responsibility across the AI supply chain and throughout the AI lifecycle appropriately?
Yes
Please provide any additional comments
May need to consider how post market surveillance would capture and report user induced performance changes, both desirable and unhelpful. Who would be responsible and how.
11. Are the proposed mandatory guardrails sufficient to address the risks of GPAI?
Yes
Please provide any additional comments
Compliance will be the difficult part. With potentially millions of AI containing applications in almost every aspect of human endeavor, by the time any legislation is implemented, compliance will be difficult.
12. Do you have suggestions for reducing the regulatory burden on small-to-medium sized businesses applying guardrails?
Yes
Please provide any additional comments
Exempt healthcare and enable (fund, train and resource) the current regulator to manage, aligned with globally harmonised regulators. Establishing a parallel conformity assessment process will very likely act as a disincentive for some innovation to enter the market.
13. Which legislative option do you feel will best address the use of AI in high-risk settings?
A domain specific approach – adapting existing regulatory frameworks to include the proposed mandatory guardrails
What opportunities should the Government take into account in considering each approach? 
Options 1 and 2 will work for healthcare. The TGA already has a framework that maps almost directly to the DISR 10 guardrails. The TGA definitions and essential principles are technology agnostic and resistant to escape from innovation. They are also harmonised with the International Medical Device Regulatory Forum. As Australia constitutes around 2% of the global healthcare market, we cannot afford to deviate from global harmonisation principles.
Option 3 could lead to a parallel conformity assessment pathway which will unnecessarily add complexity and cost. If this also leads to deviation from conformity assessment requirements of the IMDRF countries, supply of innovative technology containing AI may become limited. Option 3 would be acceptable if healthcare is excluded (as I believe Canada has done).
14. Are there any additional limitations of options outlined in this section which the Australian Government should consider?
No
Please provide any additional comments.
As long as health is managed as suggest above.
15. Which regulatory option(s) will best ensure that guardrails for high-risk AI can adapt and respond to step-changes in technology?
A domain specific approach – Adopting the guardrails within existing regulatory frameworks as needed
Please provide any additional comments.
Option 1 and 2 will work if TGA is responsible for managing all healthcare. As discussed above.

