Canada
The Canadian workforce is poised for growth, with 
technology serving as a catalyst for expansion, 
particularly in tech-related roles. As AI permeates every 
industry the demand for professionals with AI skills will 
skyrocket, creating a tectonic shift in the job market. 
The Canadian Parliamentary Budget Office (PBO) 
estimates that the Canadian economy will grow at an 
annual rate of 1.96% over the next five years. Without 
taking the productivity-boosting effects of AI into 
account, the U.S. economy would need to add .67 million 
people to the workforce over five years to support this 
growth rate. Our research predicts that AI will be able to 
do the work equivalent to 1.41 million full-time employees. 
To realize this potential, however, an additional .24 million 
tech jobs will be needed to implement and maintain 
emerging technologies. 
When all factors are considered, 
the Canadian economy will 
need an additional  
.67 million workers by 2028.
The short story is that AI, like many technologies before 
it, will disrupt the workforce while creating a net-positive 
job gain. To benefit from these shifts, Canadian workers 
must acquire skills that are relevant in a tech-centric 
work environment. AI Impact: 2024 Workforce Skills Forecast
