Germany
Germany’s workforce will see an overall decline over the 
next five years, likely due to an aging population and a 
sharp downtown in its manufacturing industry. 
The Organisation for Economic Co-operation and 
Development (OECD) estimates that the German 
economy will grow at an annual rate of 1.1% over the 
next five years. Without taking the productivity-boosting 
effects of AI into account, the German economy would 
need to add 1.71 million people to the workforce over five 
years to support this growth rate. Our research predicts 
that AI will be able to do the work equivalent to 2.46 
million full-time employees. To realize this potential, 
however, an additional .41 million tech jobs will be needed 
to implement and maintain emerging technologies. 
When all factors are considered, 
the German economy will lose 
.34 million workers by 2028.
To account for the decline in workforce, German business 
leaders will need to invest in innovative recruitment and 
training strategies. AI Impact: 2024 Workforce Skills Forecast
