skills trends:  
Australia
The Australian workforce is poised for growth, with 
technology serving as a catalyst for expansion, 
particularly in tech-related roles. As AI permeates every 
industry, the demand for professionals with AI skills will 
skyrocket, creating a tectonic shift in the job market. 
The OECD estimates the Australian economy will grow 
at an annual rate of 2.5% over the next five years. 
Without taking the productivity-boosting effects of AI 
into account, the Australian economy would need to 
add 1.68 million people to the workforce over five years 
to support this growth rate. Our research predicts that 
AI will be able to do the work equivalent to .97 million 
full-time employees. To realize this potential, however, 
anadditional .16 million tech jobs will be needed to 
implement and maintain emerging technologies. 
When all factors are considered, 
the Australian economy will 
need an additional .86 million 
workers by 2028. 
The short story is that AI, like many technologies before it, 
will disrupt the workforce while creating net-positive job 
gain. To benefit from these shifts, Australian workers must 
acquire skills that are relevant in a technology-centric 
work environment. AI Impact: 2024 Workforce Skills Forecast
