"254","255","255","Patrick Stokes, Adam Buben, and Timothy Neal","https://consult.industry.gov.au/ai-mandatory-guardrails/submission/view/28"," 1 Protecting the Dead in the AI Era   A/Prof Patrick Stokes, Associate Professor of Philosophy, Deakin University, Melbourne Dr Adam Buben University Lecturer in Philosophy, Leiden University, Netherlands Tim Neal Doctoral Candidate, Philosophy, Deakin University, Melbourne  Contents Acknowledgements ................................................................................................... 1 1. Background ....................................................................................................... 2 2. AI and the Dead .................................................................................................. 4 3. Beneﬁts ............................................................................................................. 5 3.1 Beneﬁts to the Living ................................................................................ 5 3.2 Beneﬁts to the Dead ................................................................................ 5 4. Risks ................................................................................................................. 5 4.1 Risks to the Living .................................................................................... 6 4.2 Risks to the Dead ..................................................................................... 7 5. Proposed Responses .......................................................................................... 8 5.1 Existing Proposals .................................................................................... 8 5.2 Recommendations .................................................................................. 9   Acknowledgements   The authors gratefully acknowledge the Australian Research Council’s support of our research on this topic via its funding of Discovery Project (DP24) ‘Digital Death and Immortality, ’ Deakin University, 2024-26.  The contents of this submission do not reﬂect the views of Deakin University, Leiden University, or the Australian Research Council.  Correspondence:   A/Prof. Patrick Stokes School of Humanities and Social Sciences Deakin University 221 Burwood Highway Burwood VIC 3122 patrick.stokes@deakin.edu.au +61 3 9244 3941   2 1. Background  We are academics working in the ﬁeld of philosophy. For several years two of us (Stokes and Buben) have been engaged in research1 on the practical, ethical, and conceptual challenges posed by the preservation, disposal, and reuse of what are known in the academic literature as ‘digital remains’2: the digital data and artefacts that people leave behind when they die. A particular focus of our work, and of our current Australian Research Council-funded project, is developing an ethical framework for Artiﬁcial Intelligence (AI) applications that utilise these digital remains, especially those that ‘reanimate’ the dead.  We note with approval that the Australian Government’s “proposed risk-based response places obligations on those across the AI supply chain and throughout the AI lifecycle who can most ecectively prevent harms before people interact with, or are subject to, an AI system (an ex ante or pre-market approach).”3 However, as is often the case with ethical considerations around the deployment of new technology, we note that, in Australia as elsewhere, ethical considerations regarding the dead have been absent from this conversation. This is not entirely surprising, given disagreement over the moral status of the dead (i.e. whether they have moral standing at all, and what weight if any their interests should hold relative to those of the living), and the diciculty of using existing regulatory and legal frameworks to take the interests of the dead into account.  Firstly, the very idea that the dead can be harmed or beneﬁtted is one that may seem counterintuitive, and has been a topic of philosophical debate for at least two and a half millennia (beginning at least as far back as Aristotle and particularly Epicurus). There has been sustained academic interest in the topic since the early 1970s. Much of this work has sought a way to reconcile two seemingly contradictory factors:   - Outside of religious claims about an afterlife, the dead are assumed to be essentially non-existent and incapable of experiencing pain or distress (or pleasure); and   1 Patrick Stokes, ""Ghosts in the Machine: Do the Dead Live on in Facebook?,"" Philosophy and Technology 25, no. 3 (2012); Patrick Stokes, ""Deletion as Second Death: The Moral Status of Digital Remains, "" Ethics and Information Technology 17, no. 4 (2015); Patrick Stokes, ""The Decay of Digital Personhood: Towards New Norms of Disposal and Preservation, "" in Residues of Death: Disposal Reﬁgured, ed. Tamara Kohn et al. (Houndsmills: Palgrave, 2019); Patrick Stokes, Digital Souls: A Philosophy of Online Death (London: Bloomsbury, 2021); Adam Buben, ""Technology of the Dead: Objects of Loving Remembrance or Replaceable Resources?, "" Philosophical Papers 44, no. 1 (2015). 2 Jessa Lingel, ""The Digital Remains: Social Media and Practices of Online Grief, "" The Information Society 29, no. 3 (2013); Margaret Gibson, ""Digital Objects of the Dead: Negotiating Electronic Remains, "" in The Social Construction of Death: Interdisciplinary Perspectives, ed. Leen Brussel and Nico Carpentier (London: Palgrave Macmillan UK, 2014); Margaret Gibson and Clarissa Carden, Living and Dying in a Virtual World: Digital Kinships, Nostalgia, and Mourning in Second Life (Houndsmills: Palgrave, 2018). 3 Department of Industry, Science and Resources Safe and Responsible AI In Australia: Proposals Paper for Introducing Mandatory Guardrails for AI in High-Risk Settings (Canberra, 2004), https://consult.industry.gov.au/ai-mandatory-guardrails accessed 24 September 2024, p.4.  3 - Many of our social, legal, and ceremonial practices – from keeping deathbed promises to memorialisation and posthumous exoneration - appear to presume that the dead are ‘moral patients. ’  In other words, we treat the dead as members of the moral community, to whom we have certain obligations and responsibilities, even if it may seem paradoxical that people who no longer exist can have interests, be harmed, and so forth. The dead may not be able to be hurt, and the interests of the living may rightly take precedence in most instances, but the dead are also uniquely vulnerable, since they are entirely dependent upon the agency of the living to defend their interests and dignity.   In the digital space, the dead face dangers of both deletion and misuse/abuse. For example, similar dangers apply to the dead as those posed by the creation of deepfake pornography, but without the acected party being able to seek remedies for the misuse of their likeness. It may in some cases be possible for the estate of a dead person to seek such remedies – but this does not protect the dead from inappropriate or exploitative use by the estate itself.   The dead are uniquely vulnerable to digital exploitation, and are uniquely unprotected under existing legal, commercial, and regulatory arrangements.  This moral situation is not currently reﬂected in Australian law. In every Australian jurisdiction, the dead currently have neither privacy rights nor direct protections against inappropriate use of their likeness and persona.4 To the extent that the dead are considered within Human Rights frameworks, it is solely in terms of the identiﬁcation and digniﬁed treatment, return and/or disposal of bodily human remains.   Relatively little policy work has been done on the topic of the preservation and reuse of ‘digital remains’ in Australia. The only example we are aware of is a NSW Law Reform Commission Inquiry5 that ultimately recommended a series of relatively light-touch reforms, none of which centrally addressed the issue of protection for the dead themselves. In December 2022 the Standing Council of Attorneys-General committed to producing draft legislation on access to digital records of the dead for public consultation in 2023.6 At the time of writing there has been no further announcement regarding this process.      4 P Roth, ‘Privacy Proceedings and the Dead’ Privacy Law & Policy Reporter 50 (2004) 5  NSWLRC, Access to Digital Records Upon Death or Incapacity, Report 147, New South Wakes Law Reform Commission (Sydney, 2019), https://www.lawreform.justice.nsw.gov.au/Documents/Publications/Reports/Report%20147.pdf. 6 Standing Council of Attorneys-General communiqué, 9 December 2022, https://ministers.ag.gov.au/media-centre/standing-council-attorneys-general-communique-09-12-2022, accessed 17 September 2024  4 2. AI and the Dead  Digital materials left behind by the dead take a wide range of forms, from web-use data collected by online platforms and search providers, to biometric data from wearable devices, and through to the rich repository of textual, visual, and audio material we leave behind on social media and online messaging systems.   AI technologies can be used on this material in a variety of ways. For instance, material produced by or depicting the dead can be included in data used to train Generative AI models. Visual depictions of the dead have also been used to create CGI likenesses of dead actors, as in the Star Wars ﬁlm ‘Rogue One.’ A number of live stage shows today are built around ‘holographic’ versions of long-dead performers such as Buddy Holly and Whitney Houston.  Of particular interest, however, is the use of such materials left behind by a dead user to train an AI model that will replicate the appearance, audio likeness, and (particularly) manner of speaking and replying of a deceased internet user. The most prominent use here is to take written material left behind by a dead internet user and use it to train a text-based AI ‘chatbot’ that will respond to novel prompts in a way that is reminiscent of the deceased person.   This AI-driven ‘reanimation’ of the dead has existed in various forms for over a decade, with various startups in the late 2000s and early 2010s promising (but never quite managing) to produce a compelling product. Then in 2016, the web developer behind the popular ‘Replika’ app released a chatbot based on her friend Roman Mazurenko, who had died in a car accident the previous year.7 Since then, this form of digital ‘reanimation’ has become more widespread. Google holds patents for such a bot, Amazon has publicly hinted at its ‘Alexa’ virtual assistant one day being able to speak in the synthesised voice of a dead person, and text-based chatbots of the dead have reportedly become more common among ordinary Chinese internet users over the past year.8   This technology has been referred to under a range of names: ‘deathbots, ’ ‘griefbots, ’ ghostbots, ’ ‘thanabots, ’ and the more generic ‘avatars of the dead. ’ Our own preferred terminology is Interactive Personality Constructs of the Dead, hereafter IPCDs.  IPCDs raise complex and signiﬁcant ethical, legal, and practical questions. Numerous commentators have expressed serious alarm at the prospect of unregulated production and use of this technology. While IPCDs ocer many potential beneﬁts, they also come with a signiﬁcant prospect of harm.    7 Casey Newton “Speak, Memory” The Verge 7 October 2016, https://www.theverge.com/a/luka-artiﬁcial-intelligence-memorial-roman-mazurenko-bot, accessed 25 September 2024. 8 Zeyi Yang “Deepfakes of your dead loved ones are a booming Chinese business, ” MIT Technology Review 7 May 2024, https://www.technologyreview.com/2024/05/07/1092116/deepfakes-dead-chinese-business-grief/. accessed June 20, 2024.  5 3. Beneﬁts   3.1 Beneﬁts to the Living  3.1.1 IPCDs have been proposed as a mechanism for helping people sucering from Prolonged Grief Disorder (PGD)/ Persistent Complex Bereavement Disorder (PCBD). By helping the bereaved to work through ‘unﬁnished business’ with a realistic representation of their deceased loved one, the patient may be able to resolve their feelings regarding the deceased. 3.1.2 IPCDs may assist in bereavement by enhancing rituals or ceremonies associated with the mourner’s letting go of the deceased. For example, it has been proposed that IPCDs could be used in certain Confucian mourning rituals, and this could be expanded to secular and/or Western funerary and mourning rituals as well.9  3.1.3 IPCDs may serve an educational purpose in helping people to learn about dead relatives, ancestors, and historical ﬁgures.10   3.2 Beneﬁts to the Dead  3.2.1 IPCDs may make it easier for the dead to persist in the memory of the living, by making them a richer and more interactive object of experience. In capturing something of the dead person’s distinctive conversational style and concerns, an IPCD may assist organic memory in preserving the dead, which itself is argued to be morally (as well as culturally and socially) valuable.11 3.2.2 The maintenance of an IPCD may preserve projects that a now-dead internet user was invested in during their life. For instance, an IPCD might be used to continue aspects of a user’s commercial, artistic, or social activities. 3.2.3 IPCDs may in certain circumstances come to act as an extension of the agency of a now-deceased internet user. For instance, an IPCD might be consulted by an executor/administrator of an estate to help inform decisions about what the person the IPCD is based upon would have wanted.   4. Risks  AI reuses of digital remains, and particularly the development of IPCDs, poses a number of risks to the living and to the dead.12 Not all of these risks are easily brought within the  9 Alexis Elder, ""Conversation from Beyond the Grave? A Neo-Confucian Ethics of Chatbots of the Dead, "" Journal of Applied Philosophy 37, no. 1 (2020). 10 Chatbots not based on actual persons may also fulﬁl this function in certain circumstances; see e.g. ‘Charlie’ , an AI chatbot designed to represent an ANZAC soldier, on the State Library of Queensland’s website: https://www.anzacsquare.qld.gov.au/learn/virtual-veterans, accessed 24 September 2024. 11 Jeprey Blustein, The Moral Demands of Memory (Cambridge University Press, 2008). 12 For a recent summary of some of these harms and possible legal responses thereto, see Edina Harbinja, Lilian Edwards, and Marisa McVey, ""Governing ghostbots, "" Computer Law & Security Review 48 (2023)  6 proposed Guardrails for high-risk AI. The sorts of psychological harms that may be caused by AI reuses of digital remains would fall under the proposed Guardrails, but this is not exhaustive of the harms involved. A focus solely on the welfare of the living does not capture the full harmfulness of technologies that exploit or degrade the dead.   Moreover, harm to the dead is not captured by Australia’s existing Human Rights commitments (with the possible exception of international commitments on the digniﬁed treatment of human remains).   The following list is indicative but not exhaustive:   4.1 Risks to the Living  4.1.1 IPCDs may prolong or disrupt the grieving process of the bereaved, thereby contributing to or exacerbating PGD/PCBD. The concern is that IPCDs will disrupt the process whereby those grieving a loss learn to adjust to the absence of the deceased in their daily life. By making it possible to talk ‘with’ the deceased, IPCDs may remove the necessity of ‘letting go’ or of learning to live without the dead.13   4.1.2 IPCD glitches, inadequacies in design, and ﬂawed or biased training data may result in distressing results. (As one simple example: Christi Angel, a New York resident, used an IPCD created on the ‘Project December’ platform and based on her late partner Cameroun as part of her grieving process. When she asked the bot where ‘he’ was, the IPCD replied: “In hell. ”)14  4.1.3 There is potential for IPCD service providers to exploit the emotional dependence of bereaved users on IPCDs, for example, by charging additional fees for the service, threatening to withdraw the service, or by tying the service to manipulative political or commercial advertising.  4.1.4 Training materials used to construct an IPCD are likely to impact the privacy rights of third parties. Social media proﬁles and the like are created in dialogue with other users. It is therefore likely that training materials will involve conversations with third parties, photographs and other materials that incidentally contain third parties, and materials generated by third parties in which the target user is associated (‘tagged’ photographs of the user posted by other users, for instance.)      13 Nora Freya Lindemann, ""The Ethics of ‘Deathbots’ , "" Science and Engineering Ethics 28, no. 6 (2022). 14 Dan Milmo, “‘I felt I was talking to him’: are AI personas of the dead a blessing or a curse?” Guardian 15 June 2024, https://www.theguardian.com/lifeandstyle/article/2024/jun/14/i-felt-i-was-talking-to-him-are-ai-personas-of-the-dead-a-blessing-or-a-curse, accessed 17 September 2024.  7 4.2 Risks to the Dead   4.2.1 IPCDs expose the dead to potentially inappropriate, degrading, or defamatory depictions. Similar concerns here apply as to the use of ‘deepfake’ technology on the living, with the added complication that the dead are unable to take action to stop this themselves.  4.2.2 IPCDs may expose the dead to commercial exploitation.15 This may range from the subtle (the use of IPCDs to keep users engaged on an online platform longer than they otherwise would and thus available to serve advertising to etc.) to the overt (an IPCD producing comments that are declared or undeclared advertising, such as a chatbot of a dead relative suddenly recommending a product, service or political position). This is a particular concern in cases where the deceased did not consent to the creation of the IPCD before their death. Even in cases where the deceased did consent, however, the dead are no longer able to withdraw such consent and are potentially vulnerable to exploitation indeﬁnitely.  4.2.3 More generally, IPCDs create a moral risk of treating the dead as replaceable, as well as treating the dead as a mere resource for the exploitation by or entertainment of the living.16  Note that not all of these risks, and particularly those in section 4.2 (Risks to the Dead) are clearly covered by the Proposed Principles for the designation of high-risk AI. However, risks to the dead are arguably covered by principle c.: The risk of adverse legal eAects, defamation or similarly signiﬁcant eAects on an individual, assuming that ‘similarly signiﬁcant ecects’ is not limited to ecects on legal persons.  We submit that the range of potential risks associated with IPCDs, the relative diciculty in assessing the likelihood and scale of some of these risks given that there is little direct experience with IPCDs from which empirical data can be extracted, and the inability of one major class of those impacted (i.e. dead persons) to seek redress, mean that IPCDs should be regarded as a high-risk area of AI development.         15 Carl Öhman and Luciano Floridi, ""An ethical framework for the digital afterlife industry, "" Nature Human Behaviour 2, no. 5 (2018); Tamara Kneese, Death Glitch: How Techno-Solutionism Fails Us In This Life and Beyond (New Haven, CT: Yale University Press, 2023). 16 Buben, ""Technology of the Dead: Objects of Loving Remembrance or Replaceable Resources?. ""; Stokes, Digital Souls.  8 5. Proposed Responses  5.1 Existing Proposals  A diciculty in proposing regulation or other policy responses to the development of IPCDs globally has been that these problems do not easily fall within any one existing area of law. Harbinja et al., for instance, note that the ecects of IPCDs could potentially be addressed through privacy laws, intellectual property laws, or consumer protection laws.   All of these approaches, however, raise complex issues of legal personhood, appropriate jurisdiction, and the ownership and rights of control over data. (Harbinja et al. note that legal conﬂict in this area is far from hypothetical: the producers of ‘holographic’ stage shows featuring the dead, for instance, are often enmired in legal battles with the estates of the deceased and other parties).17   Some jurisdictions have begun to tackle the issue of IPCDs explicitly, albeit in a somewhat restricted way; the State of New York, for instance, has enacted legislation (S5959D/A5605C) that confers rights of publicity for the deceased – but only for celebrities whose name, image or likeness has commercial value, and only for a period of forty years after their death.   Other policy responses that have been envisaged to address the potential harms of IPCDs more broadly include:   - An opt-out model, whereby a living person speciﬁes that they do not want an IPCD to be constructed out of their digital remains (AKA a ‘Digital Do-Not-Reanimate Order’)18 - An opt-in model, whereby construction of an IPCD will only be permitted where the person it is based upon has consented pre-mortem (regardless of whether the IPCD is constructed pre- or post-mortem) - A medical technology regulation framework, where IPCDs are regarded primarily as devices for the treatment of PGD and are regulated as part of the wider therapeutic goods regulatory framework19 - The mandatory inclusion of ‘glitches’ to disrupt the ﬂuency of IPCDs, with the aim of reminding the user periodically that the IPCD is a mere simulation of a dead person20 - Extension of existing/proposed laws around deepfakes of the living to cover the dead - The banning of the creation and use of IPCDs for some, or all, purposes.   17 Harbinja, Edwards, and McVey, ""Governing ghostbots. "" 18 Debra J. Bassett The Creation and Inheritance of Digital Afterlives: You Only Live Twice (London: Springer, 2022) 19 As advocated by Lindemann, ""The Ethics of ‘Deathbots’ . "" 20 Stokes, Digital Souls.  9 All of these approaches have signiﬁcant limitations, which collectively point to the need for an approach to IPCDs that is sensitive to the purpose and context of use of dicerent categories of IPCD.    With respect to risks to the dead, further diciculties are created by the essentially ﬁduciary character of the relationship of the living to the dead. While one of the proposed mandatory guardrails is to “Establish processes for people impacted by AI systems to challenge use or outcomes, ” this is essentially not possible for the dead themselves. It might be possible for the estate of the deceased to challenge such use – but in many cases it may well be the estate itself that is causing the harm in question.   5.2 Recommendations  The authors of this submission are not legal or public policy experts, and we do not advocate here for any speciﬁc legislative or regulatory amendments. However, we recommend that:  5.2.1 Any case involving the use of digital remains be considered High Risk. 5.2.2 A ‘Death Lens’ be applied to any new AI-related legislation or regulation to ensure that the dead (and permanently incapacitated) as well as the bereaved are appropriately protected. 5.2.3 Regulation of AI uses that involve digital remains begin from a starting point of protecting the dead and the living from misuse and harm, rather than proceeding solely from existing legal and human rights assumptions about obligations to the dead and the property rights of the living to exploit the dead. 5.2.4 That IPCDs that are explicitly designed for therapeutic purposes be regulated as such, without necessarily ruling out non-therapeutic uses of the same technology (for e.g. memorialisation, entertainment etc.). ","Digital_Death_AI_Guardrails_submission.feb57f1e","Digital_Death_AI_Guardrails_submission.feb57f1e.pdf","pdf","C:\\Users\\james.barro\\Documents\\rsm-nvidia-hackathon\\data\\Submission_Docs\\Processed\\Digital_Death_AI_Guardrails_submission.feb57f1e.pdf","1"
