Introduction  
 
This submission is made on behalf of the Australia New Zealand Screen Association (ANZSA). 
ANZSA ’s members are significant participants in  the film and television content and 
distribution industry in Australia and New Zealand.1 ANZSA’s  core mission is to advance the 
business and art of filmmaking, increasing its enjoyment around the world and to support, 
protect and promote the safe and legal consumption of movie and TV content across all 
services.  
 
We thank the  Government of Australia and the  Department of Industry, Science and 
Resources (“the Department”) for the opportunity to provide comments on the Proposals 
paper for introducing mandatory guardrails for AI in high -risk settings (“the Proposals 
Paper”), released in September 2024.   
 
AI supports  human creativity  
 
As ANZSA noted in our previous submission made to the Department  on July 26, 20232; and 
echoed in our submission made to the Select Committee on Adopting Artificial Intelligence 
(AI) on May 10, 20243, our  member companies produce and distribute a wide range of film 
and television content in Australia  and around the world . ANZSA  member companies are 
already using a variety of technologies, including AI, to support the creation and delivery of a 
wide range of works that bring benefits (both economic and cultural) to society.  
 
Notably, as we have noted before , we do not believe generative AI will replace human 
creativity, which will always  remain at the core of the art of filmmaking. Instead  AI, including  
generative AI , can be used as a tool  that serves  to free up humans from rote parts of their 
work and allow s them to concentrate their limited time and effort on more creative aspects, 
while enhancing the audience experience.  
 
Use of AI by ANZSA members is not “high -risk”  
 
To define “high -risk” AI systems, the Department has recommended a flexible approach 
whereby for AI systems where the proposed uses are known/foreseeable, high -level 
principles are set to guide industry in determining whether a particular use of an AI syst em is 
high -risk; for advanced, highly -capable AI systems where the possible risks cannot be 
foreseen, these systems will be specifically defined.  
 
 
1 Our members are proud participants in, and contributors to, the film and television industry in Australia, and 
include Walt Disney Studios Motion Pictures Australia; Fetch TV; Netflix Inc; Motion Picture Association; 
Paramount Pictures Australia; Sony Pictures Releasing International Corporation; Universal International Films, 
Inc.; Village Roadshow Limited and Warner Bros. Pictures International, a division of Warner Bros. 
Entertainment Inc.  
2 ANZSA’s submission can be accessed here: https://consult.industry.gov.au/supporting -responsible -
ai/submission/view/220   
3 ANZSA’s submission can be accessed here: 
https://www.aph.gov.au/Parliamentary_Business/Committees/Senate/Adopting_Artificial_Intelligence_AI/Ado
ptingAI/Submissions   The proposed principles in defining whether an AI system is “high -risk” take into account the 
risk of adverse impacts in six areas (as the Proposals Paper sets out on page 19).  
 
As we mention above,  technology, now including AI, has been used by the creative 
industries for decades to augment and enhance what is captured in camera, thus improving 
the audience experience.  
 
For example, animators and visual effects artists for decades have used a process called 
“rotoscoping”, which involves manually altering individual frames within a single shot to align 
live-action and computer -generated images and is incredibly detail orie nted and time 
consuming. Contemporary visual -effects artists now have sophisticated tools, some of which 
incorporate AI technology, to assist with this type of work, which frees artists to focus their 
energies on the creative aspects of the visual effects.   AI also helps creators realize their vision 
and enhance the audience experience by making visual effects more dramatic, realistic, and 
memorable. Creators can use AI for everything from colour correction, detail sharpening, and 
de-blurring; to removing u nwanted objects from a scene; to more involved work like aging 
and de -aging an actor; or to adjusting the placement of computer -generated images to make 
sure everything in a scene flows smoothly and aligns properly. Artists have expressed 
enthusiasm for AI  tools that enhance their work, and for continued technological 
development of these and similar tools. In short, the use of AI technology presents developing 
opportunities for creators and their audiences.  
 
As such, w hen considering the nature of any potential regulation around the use of AI  
(including but not limited to transparency obligations, such as labelling of outputs of 
generative AI , and considerations around “deepfakes"" ), careful consideration must be given 
to motivation  and impact . In this regard a distinction must  be made  between the use of AI 
technology to create content that intentionally misleads or deceives, and therefore creates 
harms for consumers and the public, and the use of AI technology in our sector to enhance 
the production of expressive works ( “evidently creative content ”4) that are used solely to 
entertain  audiences .  
 
The use of AI by the creative industries to produce evidently creative content is therefore 
clearly not a “high -risk” use case; there are low -to-no risks of adverse impacts on an 
individual’s rights  and physical/mental health or safety ; and low -to-no risks of adverse 
impacts on groups or the broader Australian economy and society  which cannot already be 
managed through existing laws/regulations and longstanding best practices used by the 
creative industries in Australia . 
 
Several ANZSA members also operate, or are planning to operate, video -on-demand (VOD) 
services in Australia which offer large and diverse catalogues with human -curated and 
professionally -produced content. This human curation element is crucial; ANZSA’s mem bers 
comply with existing regulation5 to ensure that viewers are appropriately informed about 
the content they are about to watch, or which they allow their children to watch. These VOD 
 
4 ANZSA defines “evidently creative content” to include legitimate storytelling that involves real -life individuals. 
Where AI is concerned, freedom of expression should be a key criteria in determining the risk profile for s uch 
uses (including for  professionally -produced  docudramas, biopics, documentaries, parodies, satires, etc.) .  
5 The Classification (Publications, Films and Computer Games) Act 1995.  services use various recommendation systems (some of which could be enhanced by AI) to 
help viewers find content that most closely suits their interests.  Similarly, any such use of AI 
by the creative industries is therefore clearly not a “high -risk” use case since such uses 
benefit consumer interest.   
 
ANZSA therefore submits that the use of AI by the creative sector  in general , and our 
members, is therefore very clearly a “low -risk” use case , and we believe the factors set out 
in the proposals paper provide for this outcome .  
 
ANZSA will reserve comment on the Department’s proposals to create mandatory guardrails 
for “high -risk” AI systems , and how those guardrails are legislated, at this stage . 
 
ANZSA encourages appropriate delegation and coordination with other Government 
departments.  
 
We also  note the acknowledgement in the proposals paper on page 37 that the Government 
of Australia is currently conducting a parallel  process, led by the Attorney -General’s 
Department (AGD) Copyright and AI Reference Group (CAIRG) , which is considering  the 
implications of AI for copyright law . ANZSA is supportive of this dual approach, given the 
sectoral expertise of the AGD in Copyright Law, and is an active participant in this process.  
  
ANZSA also notes that the Office for the Arts has also committed to a parallel process on the 
protection of indigenous cultural and intellectual property (ICIP), which will consider many 
of the ICIP -related issues raised in the Proposals Paper; we suggest that the ICIP 
considerations for the use of AI are carefully coordinated with the Office for the Arts  
workstream . 
 
Copyright -related transparency matters to be led by AGD CAIRG  
 
We note that the CAIRG is now considering in detail the issue of transparency in relation to 
the use of copyrighted materials as inputs for AI systems developed/deployed in Australia; 
and that the CAIRG’s conclusions will inform its collaboration across Government on the 
broader transparency conversation. We urge the Department of Industry and Science to 
collaborate with the AGD on this important matter with care and caution .  
 
As we note above, the AGD has the sectoral expertise for copyright, and any such copyright -
based transparency proposals should continue to be led by the CAIRG process. We also 
believe that current regulatory arrangements , including the Copyright Act, already provide 
strong tools for the regulation of the use of AI; we would urge the Department to avoid the 
implementation of overly -broad legislation without adequate consultation with industry 
stakeholders, for fear of unintended adverse consequences.  
 
 
The ANZSA appreciates  the opportunity to provide you with these high -level comments. We 
remain ready to provide further information should this be of assistance.  
