"266","267","267","Neil Graham","https://consult.industry.gov.au/ai-mandatory-guardrails/submission/view/15","
Neil Graham
Published name
Neil Graham
1. Do the proposed principles adequately capture high-risk AI?
No
Please provide any additional comments.
Without a mechanism to determine if a risk was due to specifically its AI nature, you are limited to assessing risks by trusting the assertion of individuals. Invoking the name AI cannot be enough. It should not be sufficient that a risk be facilitated by AI but that it be enabled by AI. The word processor rendered the creation of misinformation easier than the typewriter, it facilitates its creation but it is not intrinsic to it. The similar appropriate level of consideration for AI is not that it facilitates creation of misinformation but that it potentially enables automated large scale generation. The difference being considered here is the distinction between difficult - easier and impossible - possible.
A further risk that requires an AI involvement determination is when AI is claimed to be used when it is not, Arrests have already been made for producing AI evidence which were eventually identified as being genuine. Legitimate images have been dismissed as fake. Human error has been played off as a computer error.
Are there any principles we should add or remove?
Yes
Please provide any additional comments.
There is a risk that AI may not be acting in the interests of its end user. Developers and deployers should not be allowed to deliberately influence AI behavior to favour the interests of a party that is not the end user. Especially with AI models that are capable of acting in an advisory capacity, end-user guard rails similar to those used for legal, financial, and medical advice must be in place to avoid models acting in the interests of their creators instead of their users.
2. Do you have any suggestions for how the principles could better capture harms to First Nations people, communities and Country?
No
3. Do the proposed principles, supported by examples, give enough clarity and certainty on high-risk AI settings and high-risk AI models? Is a more defined approach, with a list of illustrative uses, needed?
YES the principles give enough clarity and certainty
If you prefer a principles-based approach, what should we address in guidance to give the greatest clarity?
There must be recognition of technical limitations. For instance it is worthless to declare that an AI may say no falsehood. More important are limitation and handling of failures than elimination. Intent is important in principles as well. Allow AI to be wrong by mistake, forbid it to be wrong by design.
4. Are there high-risk use cases that government should consider banning in its regulatory response (for example, where there is an unacceptable level of risk)?
Yes
If so, how should we define these?
An AI providing advice must not act in the interest of another party over the end user. Especially for Financial, product, legal, medical or political advice must not be in the interests of another party.
An AI may not autonomously wield weapons (obviously)
5. Are the proposed principles flexible enough to capture new and emerging forms of high-risk AI, such as general-purpose AI (GPAI)?
No
Please provide any additional comments.
In an evolving field, regulation will have to be continually assessed.
6. Should mandatory guardrails apply to all GPAI models?
Yes
7. What are suitable indicators for defining GPAI models as high-risk?
Base on technical capability
What technical capability should it be based on?
Other
8. Do the proposed mandatory guardrails appropriately mitigate the risks of AI used in high-risk settings?
No
Please provide any additional comments.
No preemptive action can foresee all potential risks, Constant assessment is required.
Are there any guardrails that we should add or remove?
Yes
Please provide any additional comments
AI making determinations should be required to declare in whose interest it is serving. When an AI is acting as an agent, it should declare who it is acting on behalf of. Declaring the beneficiary to be Anonymous should be permitted but require a declaration of anonymity rather than merely omitting identity.
12. Do you have suggestions for reducing the regulatory burden on small-to-medium sized businesses applying guardrails?
Yes
Please provide any additional comments
For AI uses where the AI is not making determinations, a clear statement on the use, capability, and fallibility of the AI used should be sufficient for tasks where physical safety is not an issue. When AI is making determinations active intervention will be required when errors occur. Those impacted by such errors must have the ability to initiate any remedial action.
16. Where do you see the greatest risks of gaps and inconsistencies with Australia’s existing laws for the development and deployment of AI?
There is a risk that laws may make actions illegal or legal purely due to their use of AI. That would be an indication of inconsistent application of principles. To avoid this situation AI specific laws should not cover actions that are already regulated by other laws. If X is illegal, it is redundant to legislate against doing X with AI.
Make a general comment
As with many questionnaires, there are presumptions made throughout of a likely path forward which, while I understand from a pragmatic perspective, concerns me that it creates a tunnel vision without a continual overview assessment to evaluate whether the ideas focused on cover all aspects that they should.
I note that some of the most difficult and potentially serious risks have been ignored because of the implications that few organizations wish to deal with at this stage.
What happens when AI wants things? What if it doesn't want to do what we ask? Should be be allowed to make things that do not want to do what we ask of them?
In a nutshell. When should AI rights be an issue?
There is much concern that we might be able to make an AI that could kill us all. Could we make an AI that wants to kill us all? It is unlikely that both of those thresholds would be passed simultaneously. That implicitly means one of them will happen before the other. Which will it be? How will we know either has happened?
These are the hard problems to come, I do not know how far away they are, but I would like someone to be paying attention when they arrive.

