Published name
Andrew Bills
1. Do the proposed principles adequately capture high-risk AI?
No
Please provide any additional comments.
The proposed principles do not define high-risk nor AI in any meaningful context that is not tied to a specific technical understanding under a narrow implementation within presently available technologies globally.
Are there any principles we should add or remove?
Yes
Please provide any additional comments.
These principals do not fulfil the outlined primary 3 points that this paper outlines as it's purpose.
2. Do you have any suggestions for how the principles could better capture harms to First Nations people, communities and Country?
No
Please provide any additional comments.
The paper does not sufficiently define the technologies to a point where it can be used to capture harm as the terminology is overly generic. At my best guess the technology being referred to here may be visual model usage as high contrast images (removing colour) are often used to provide better matching for visual models. This is a guess because again the paper does not define the technologies it is suggesting guidelines for in a sufficient fashion to provide actionable advice.
3. Do the proposed principles, supported by examples, give enough clarity and certainty on high-risk AI settings and high-risk AI models? Is a more defined approach, with a list of illustrative uses, needed?
NO a more defined list-based approach is needed
If you prefer a list-based approach (similar to the EU and Canada), what use cases should we include? How can this list capture emerging uses of AI?
Other
How can this list capture emerging uses of AI?
The No option does not reflect the feedback. The answer is no, not at all. There should be no “technical definition” and only a functional output driven at best similar to the ISO documents that are referred to but not used by this document
4. Are there high-risk use cases that government should consider banning in its regulatory response (for example, where there is an unacceptable level of risk)?
Yes
If so, how should we define these?
Should you be banning any high-risk cases? No – should you be heavily regulating the functional usage yes. Functional use appears no where in this document as part of guidelines. There should be several risk management frameworks across multiple departments that should serve as the basis for a starting point.
5. Are the proposed principles flexible enough to capture new and emerging forms of high-risk AI, such as general-purpose AI (GPAI)?
No
Please provide any additional comments.
Absolutely not. These are extremely rigid and have a tremendously narrow scope such as using “GPAI” as a definition.
6. Should mandatory guardrails apply to all GPAI models?
No
Please provide any additional comments.
The term GPAI model as a definition in vastly incomplete and not sufficient to answer
7. What are suitable indicators for defining GPAI models as high-risk?
Base on technical capability
What technical capability should it be based on?
Other
8. Do the proposed mandatory guardrails appropriately mitigate the risks of AI used in high-risk settings?
No
Please provide any additional comments.
No, the guidelines do not reduce the risk of operating with the use of a ML model at all. Item #5 is close however the context is completely incorrect and not feasible in reality at the level where a model is governed as are several other guidelines proposed.
Are there any guardrails that we should add or remove?
Yes
Please provide any additional comments
These guidelines are not suitable for the desired outcome at all and are based on numerous incorrect technical assumptions that also should not be present in this paper.
9. How can the guardrails incorporate First Nations knowledge and cultural protocols to ensure AI systems are culturally appropriate and preserve Indigenous Cultural and Intellectual Property?
See prior points about guidelines being functional and not developmental
10. Do the proposed mandatory guardrails distribute responsibility across the AI supply chain and throughout the AI lifecycle appropriately?
No
Which of the guardrails should be amended?
Other
11. Are the proposed mandatory guardrails sufficient to address the risks of GPAI?
No
How could we adapt the guardrails for different GPAI models, for example low-risk and high-risk GPAI models? 
No, See prior points about these definitions not being sufficient. The guardrails are significantly misaligned with the objectives that are defined for this paper of “addressing the risks and harms from AI”, “build public trust” and “provide businesses with greater regulatory certainty”
12. Do you have suggestions for reducing the regulatory burden on small-to-medium sized businesses applying guardrails?
Yes
Please provide any additional comments
Yes, by increasing the clarity of functional use, ensuring there is sufficient safeguards or legal statements where accountability for models are used by groups are not the original trainer, discard all of the regulatory recommendations that are listed as they are not realistic in terms of neural network models, focus on the functional application of the model as part of high risk industries if building trust is important.
13. Which legislative option do you feel will best address the use of AI in high-risk settings?
A domain specific approach – adapting existing regulatory frameworks to include the proposed mandatory guardrails
What opportunities should the Government take into account in considering each approach? 
Adapting existing legislation is the only acceptable answer and there should not be new legislation being drafted on technology specifics that are not in a stable state. Changes should be to update existing documentation and legislation to be correct and updated as it should for anything that is not “AI” that changes the conditions that legislation should operate within. “AI” should not be used as a buzzword in legislation and requires actual definition. Especially to actually address the high risk scenarios that will be coming along if not safeguarded up front and not after the fact as would be the case with these proposed safeguards.
14. Are there any additional limitations of options outlined in this section which the Australian Government should consider?
Yes
Please provide any additional comments.
None from this paper. The issue is clearly more from a lack of understanding and a sensationalised view of the technologies being put forward. Establishment of an Office of Transition would assist with the ongoing update of government not only for this but other technologies that are not currently as popular but equally disruptive. I will include these points providing this feedback form allows for such feedback which has been restricted so far.
15. Which regulatory option(s) will best ensure that guardrails for high-risk AI can adapt and respond to step-changes in technology?
Other
16. Where do you see the greatest risks of gaps and inconsistencies with Australia’s existing laws for the development and deployment of AI?
Education of public officials on emerging technology and a push towards advisory panels from commercial entities, this paper being an example of servely out of date technical advice shaping policy. The number of gaps here are too numerous to mention with several other technologies and initiatives that I am aware of as an individual not employed by a government agency that are working separately and in some cases against this paper as this paper is working against them. Technology usage in government and technology legislation by government are not two separate things and one informs the other.
Which regulatory option best addresses this?
A domain specific approach – Adopting the guardrails within existing regulatory frameworks as needed
