Hi, I am sending this in aid of providing formal feedback as to the mandatory guardrails for high-risk settings proposal papers. I am sorry for the length of the reply but there are several incredibly important items that appear no where in this paper and focus is clearly towards areas that have been identified incorrectly or very specific limited technical examples that are in no way sufficient to provide justification or guidelines.
With all the below I understand there is substantial information and I am happy to speak to specifics if contacted for a meaningful discussion for your benefit to understand the gravity of the gap presented by this paper.
------
By way of background my name is Andrew Bills and I am a subject matter expert with over 2 decades of experience in specifically the practical application of emerging technologies in addition to at risk projects and critical customers. Across lengthy career I have worked across and provided advice for the practical use of emerging technology to numerous industries and sectors ranging from agriculture to government agencies on a variety of topics ranging from but not limited to CubeSats to the more mundane machine learning models. In this experience I have worked on scales ranging from bootstrapped start-ups to working on global scales for multinationals such as IBM and ABB.
At this point in time I am not representing any interests outside of those of the general population in providing my expertise on subject matter that it is not the first instance of needing to respond to. I understand that this paper is part of a continuation of the previous consultation paper on “AI” which has had responses from senior individuals across multiple organisations. I am also fairly confident that this particular paper does represent a not insignificant use of external consultants in the creation of this paper and that the feedback I will provide may in fact be passed back to those same groups. Additionally I am aware of the existence of a number of the advisory boards for technology that are utilised at various government for technology and more specifically their quality of work in a few specific instances.
Normally when there is government level actions being taken on matters relating to emerging technologies there are individuals who are used to operating at global scales who view the actions being taken to determine if the actions are to pass will they have any harm or impact to the progress of a particular field. Normally if a proposal is wide sweeping but in reality the proposed guidelines have no real impact then individuals such as myself will normally not provide feedback or try to be involved in any way. However when the proposal is harmful either to the field or to the goals that the legislation seeks to further then these instances do warrant formal feedback.
While I will answer the consultation paper questions below the following points are most important.
1. These guidelines are, in short, woefully inaccurate and incomplete. There are a number of areas where specifics are used to speak to the technology which demonstrate only a very surface level of the technology and even then an outdated understanding of only a singular example or way that items that are referred to as “AI”
2. The definition of “AI” within this document is not nearly sufficient and as mentioned where it is specific is a very narrow in terms of actual technology while using this narrow example to make the broader guidelines and assumptions. I understand completely the biggest threat in terms of understanding “AI” is the number of individuals in positions of influence without basic ability to state the practical application and use of the same technologies they are pushing for. I understand that this is in no small part due to sensationalised coverage that misrepresents the capability and function of these technologies for the sake of profit and investment however this is not based in reality. This document continually uses the term “AI” as an definition without adequately defining it and even where it is defined it is quickly contradicted.
3. Numerous technical errors as to the current state of technologies and those being the basis of justifications for recommendations. While it is not my intention and it should not be used as such citing points such as operating in a black box is only correct for certain technologies and approaches while completely incorrect for others, types of technologies are mixed up to make convincing points about the need for certain guidelines. There are numerous technologies that meet the output suggested elsewhere in this document but the specifics of guidelines in how to manage them do not match the realities of those technologies.
4. While the intention to state guidelines for the use of neural network based activities is a worthwhile endevor this paper does very little to both define high risk activities and further to this safeguard them. In one section it even goes as far as defining a high risk activity that has the potential for fatality as not.
Again I realise that this may have been a longer effort but my motivation and drive here in providing feedback is that this is going in a very wrong direction and will allow for significant catastrophic outcomes to critical infrastructure that are required for basic human needs such as the power grid, medical systems and more.
## The following recommendations should be the basis for your consultation paper: ##
1. Define the actual scope of “AI” that is intended to be covered in a specific practical sense and not repeatedly use the term “AI”. This should be at a functional level such as:
- Weak (current state neural network like models that are capable of performing tasks based on trained historical examples) – This is current state
- Strong (as per above with the ability to self-learn and update the model without intervention) – This is not current state for the majority but gets into the topic of MLOPs
- Advanced (completely autonomous) – this absolutely does not exist at present
These 3 categories are not enough and should additionally specify the type of system or function, visual recognition models, predictive data, large langage models. Even using the ISO/ISE42001 definitions which the standard was quoted IN THIS DOCUMENT would be an improvement
2. Remove language attempting to place guidelines on how the technology is developed and focus on how the technology is applied and used. There is an overwhelming number of errors and out of date technology assumptions in this document and it is a field that will look radically different 5 years from now. Attempting to place these guidelines which make a number of incorrect assumptions on the technology is like trying to legislate how combustion works to make laws on vehicles (legislation on emissions is about the implementation not the laws of physics). Areas that should be of concern are but not limited to:
- Information security. Loosely referred to in this document it also extends to data sovereignty which is not included in this document
- Intellectual property. Specifically both the protection of individuals AND adjustments and changes required to legal and copyright systems
- liability. Again not to go into this documents technical inaccuracies however it assumes a single level of “developer” which is largely incorrect even if we restrict to only technologies that I assume are being referred to as “AI”. Models are not limited to a single development or even layer with in reality it being much more complex than is being portrayed here. Legal accountability for usage should be a focus which needs to not be “the developer” as this is an exaggeration perception. There is a statement of “clarifying accountability and ensuring legal responsibility is distributed appropriately to developers” which is good but it makes a high level assumption as to the party or parties involved which is incorrect.
Focus and guidelines in the proposed guidelines are to be put nicely, realistic in terms of their implementation. This is a technology that is getting focus right now whereas numerous other technologies that provide equal amount of risk are not subject to nearly the same level of high visibility. To make things clear, a visual ML model for the purpose of identifying license plates can be used for taking a picture of a car license plate at a petrol station, the very same model can be used as a targeting system using the license plate as an offset position. There should be no focus or guidelines on the development and every bit on the application. This is a realistic statement which is the difference in spending millions of dollars to not achieve a desirable outcome and to have a practical realistic result.
One of the recommended paths is to update existing legislation, that is partially correct however in no place should the definition of “AI” be the definition used as the perception of what this means is greatly exaggerated in terms to actual technology exists. There is a statement in this document that “This would then account for rights protected elsewhere under Australian law” the document saying “elsewhere” and not going into these details is a key problem with this paper.
3. This relates to “AI” as well as several other areas of practical technology. For the past year I have attempt to contact government officials as an individual (not as part of an organisation) I relation to issues relating to the practical application of emerging technology within government. Responses have ranged from no response, to contempt, to written responses that can only be charitable referred to as incoherent. I am aware of the current state of emerging technology use and the path for adoption within government as it stands. I am also aware of the heavy reliance of external consultancies.
The fundamental issue is that at no state or federal level within Australia exists a Department of Transition which is a specific function dedicated to the transitioning government as needed with changing technology. Currently this is leading to a multiple activities being attempted at different levels at odds with each other in addition to massive gaps being left where existing department functions place critical items out of scope. This document is focused on a very specific type of “AI” but in doing so is completely independent of several other high profile technologies as they are seen as different things but are absolutely not.
Funding for individual activities on “AI” should be diverted into the establishment of an internal Office of Transition whose cost would greatly offset with the reduction of consulting fees.
## A couple of at minimum things that should be in mandatory guidelines for high risk systems ##
- Directly specifically outline both high risk and specific use in a risk management perspective to ensure the guidelines are clear where the application of said systems are highly regulated. The paper included donly included one of these as an example from another country and did not use it as a definition for high risk within the proposed Australian guidelines instead focusing on impact on an individual
- High risk and autonomous systems. While skirted in the above points a very specific guideline would be ensuring that no high risk key infrastructure is able to managed, performed by, controlled completely by a predictive system in a completely autonomous function. This may mean the ability to turn the power grid on and off or automatically administering potentially lethal levels of pain killers.
- Where impersonation based “AI” is a concern such as art, chatbots, etc. Outside of a number of intellectual property protections required a basic guideline would be to ensure that these systems identify themselves as such.

