1. Do the proposed principles adequately capture high-risk AI?
No
Please provide any additional comments.
Where would “GPAI systems” fall in? Note that the EU AI Act has a concept of “GPAI systems” (distinct from GPAI models) which would cover multi-purpose apps like ChatGPT, Claude, etc and are subject to additional scrutiny/monitoring by EU authorities. If GPAI systems are meant to be covered under the high-risk criteria of category 1, then how exactly do you apply the high-risk criteria (which assesses foreseeable risk)...to assess a system with unforeseeable uses?
Please identify any:
""GPAI systems"" - see response above.
Another area to consider are 'macro processes' or systems that involve AI in some way but AI is not the primary or most salient feature of that process. For example, AI being combined with another software, which is combined with another software, which is implemented in a process, etc - would (or should) the guardrails cover such scenario (especially in instances where the AI component creates a bias risk that then gets amplified in other parts of the process)? Whatever is the position, this should be clarified in the scope of the word ""system"" in the definition of ""AI system"".
6. Should mandatory guardrails apply to all GPAI models?
No
Please provide any additional comments.
No. The blanket application of the guardrails to all GPAI models seems to undercut the risk-based logic. Unforeseeability is hard to assess. Given how GPAI models differ in size and capabilities, we may need
(1) some threshold (e.g. the EU’s “systemic risk” test, or California’s pricing/compute power threshold) +
(2) 'baseline' v 'advanced' guardrails based on the threshold (e.g. extra security requirements for more powerful models).
What’s the right threshold is a tricky question (and there’s lot of science to it).
But one potential factor to consider in the threshold is the model provider’s market share and customer base (which can help highlight the scale of the model’s use).
7. What are suitable indicators for defining GPAI models as high-risk?
Base on technical capability
What technical capability should it be based on?
Other
8. Do the proposed mandatory guardrails appropriately mitigate the risks of AI used in high-risk settings?
Yes
Please provide any additional comments.
Yes, but we may also need to consider the remedial options in the unlikely event the risk still materialises (despite guardrails in place). See below response on the regulatory options.
12. Do you have suggestions for reducing the regulatory burden on small-to-medium sized businesses applying guardrails?
Yes
Please provide any additional comments
Organisations who are already complying and certified with existing ISO AI standards (e.g. ISO/IEC 42001) should have some 'merit' consideration in the unlikely event they fail on a guardrail (e.g. whether it be in the form of lighter penalties, or longer breach rectification periods).
Also consider the example in the EU AI Act where financial sector organisations that are already complying with sector-specific regulations are deemed to be compliant with certain risk and quality management obligations under the EU AI Act (e.g. see article 17(4) of EU AI Act).
13. Which legislative option do you feel will best address the use of AI in high-risk settings?
A framework approach – Introducing new framework legislation to adapt existing regulatory frameworks across the economy
What opportunities should the Government take into account in considering each approach? 
The guardrails could be documented in a schedule attached to an existing law (e.g. the Australia Consumer Law in schedule 2 of the Consumer and Competition Act (CCA)). But rather than amending each law one-by-one to insert the schedule, the framework application could be one 'mega' amendment that inserts the schedule in multiple acts at once. The schedule would have its own penalty regime (that overrides the existing penalty regime in the relevant act with respect to breach of the guardrails in the schedule) but is enforced by the relevant regulator.
Otherwise, if the guardrails could only be added to one specific law, one suggestion is the Corporations Act where the guardrails could be tied into director duties. The thinking behind this approach is that practically, the Corporations Act is one of the most comprehensive and consequential law for the private sector, and naturally drives accountable practices due to the ASIC reporting requirements. Also, AI systems and models that the guardrails should be concerned about are likely those that are commercial-grade and customer-facing provided by entities which are subject to the Corporations Act. The linking of the guardrails into director duties could likely drive the most impactful change in terms of legal compliance and business culture (as there would be a top-down drive by directors based on personal liability concerns to ensure the organisation as a whole complies with the guardrails).
In parallel, law reform in privacy, IP, online safety, etc should continue.
The common law system of Australia should be considered as a strength as it can allow for 'looser' drafting that can be reinterpreted by the court system over time.
14. Are there any additional limitations of options outlined in this section which the Australian Government should consider?
Yes
Please provide any additional comments.
The division of powers between federal, state and territory governments might pose a challenge to the scope and application of an economy-wide AI Act. This is why a framework specific approach across all federal, state and territory levels might be preferable.
