The guardrails seem to be mainly focussed on top-down projects involving supervised machine learning (see references to ""training"" and ""data sets""). While LLMs are at their core supervised ML systems, from the perspective of Australia (where none of the training is happening) they effectively aren't. It's not as if organisations are adopting AI top-down for particular tasks that are planned by the organisation... individuals are adopting it bottom-up to do their work.
Are there any guardrails that we should add or remove?
Yes
What guardrails should we add or remove?
Protect AI systems, and implement data governance measures to manage data quality and provenance Be transparent with other organisations across the AI supply chain about data, models and systems to help them effectively address risks Test AI models and systems to evaluate model performance and monitor the system once deployed
Please provide any additional comments
#3 is generally impossible for commercial AI models, and impractical for open source models. So it won't happen, and therefore everyone will fail the mandatory guidelines.
#4 assumes that AI is adopted top-down in a planned way. Maybe this might be true in government, but it most definitely does not reflect commercial reality. Most organisations don't even know where AI is being used. So every orgnisation will be unknowingly non-compliant.
#8 seems to assume that most new AI projects will be created by software developers. As software development is one of the use cases experiencing the most disruption from AI, this will seem a quaint idea within a few years' time. The principle won't really make sense in a world where people are creating new AI capabilities themselves whenever a need arises.
9. How can the guardrails incorporate First Nations knowledge and cultural protocols to ensure AI systems are culturally appropriate and preserve Indigenous Cultural and Intellectual Property?
No idea. This is a very hard problem.
10. Do the proposed mandatory guardrails distribute responsibility across the AI supply chain and throughout the AI lifecycle appropriately?
No
Which of the guardrails should be amended?
Other
11. Are the proposed mandatory guardrails sufficient to address the risks of GPAI?
No
12. Do you have suggestions for reducing the regulatory burden on small-to-medium sized businesses applying guardrails?
No
Please provide any additional comments
I lecture in AI & computer science and am also a small business owner, so I should be the ideal person to answer this question. Honestly, I would have no way of complying with the guardrails even if they were mandatory. I can't see how they are relevant or useful or worthwhile in a small business context. Small business owners will simply ignore them, or make up compliance documents that have no basis in reality.
13. Which legislative option do you feel will best address the use of AI in high-risk settings?
A domain specific approach – adapting existing regulatory frameworks to include the proposed mandatory guardrails
15. Which regulatory option(s) will best ensure that guardrails for high-risk AI can adapt and respond to step-changes in technology?
A framework approach – Introducing new framework legislation to adapt existing regulatory frameworks across the economy
16. Where do you see the greatest risks of gaps and inconsistencies with Australia’s existing laws for the development and deployment of AI?
The biggest problems will be around interactions between AI and financial instruments
Which regulatory option best addresses this?
A domain specific approach – Adopting the guardrails within existing regulatory frameworks as needed
Make a general comment
This is perhaps harsh feedback, but reading the general principles was like stepping into a time machine and popping out before 2022. AI has changed since then, and changed in profound ways that make meaningless almost all prior discussions. The guardrails seem to be living in the pre-2022 view of the universe. If we wanted to have some guardrails for the use of training and deployment of supervised machine learning, sure, let's have these guardrails for that -- it makes sense in that context. But that's only a very, very tiny corner of what AI is today and what it will be in the future. It's not a particularly relevant or high-risk area.
Among my university colleagues and in every consulting project over the last 12 months, the nearly universal view is that training new models is a dead-end unless you are a major vendor with a billion dollar budget. There are a few exceptions: you might train some explainable models to gain insight (e.g. statisticians still make their linear models); you might fine tune a small model to do some specific task quickly or cheaply; in my research I'm training new models using radically new techniques to see if there are other ways of creating AI. But in general, the giant models already have internalised all public information and datasets, so you can get what you want simply by prompt engineering. And prompt engineering isn't the purview of a ""priesthood"" of tech folks: lots of people have learned how to do prompt engineering.
Where before we would have to have specialised technically-trained people program something to interact with a ML model, we now see individuals make their own systems by prompting AI to do the task. Automation of that is done through things like Zapier or n8n or equivalent. AI systems will proliferate and be done by individuals. Commercially, individuals who do that will be rewarded because of their efficiency, and if we insist on saying that guardrails must be observed either (a) we will remove a dynamism from the economy or (b) people will just ignore the guardrails.
I have two quick rules-of-thumb for identifying irrelevant discussions that are carry-overs from pre-2022:
- if it talks about ""monitoring the input data sets"" (no-one in Australia will be training significant models)
- if it assumes that deployment of an AI system is a large organisationally-visible activity which management has control over (in an age of open source
Going through the principles:
- #1 assumes accountability at the leadership level, when most AI usage, development and monitoring will be at the individual level.
- #2 assumes that there is a controlled list of AI systems and that there is a formal commissioning process for new AI capabilities
- #3 talks about the input data for training
- #4 assumes that deployment is a large and visible activity
- #5 is sort-of OK, although there are footnotes talking about ""procurement"". But it's not really an AI thing: that's to do with having accountability of outcomes, which would be a true and necessary thing with or without AI
- #6 we can legislate the transparency part if we feel like it, but it will devolve into a standardised email footer attached to everything that says ""AI may have contributed in whole or in part to this response."". So the transparency part is probably pointless.
- #7 this is a necessity for systems with or without AI.
- #8 assumes a distinction between developer and deployer, and that deployment is a organisationally-visible
- #9 fundamentally assumes that all AI systems are organisationally-visible
- #10 assumes that AI systems are organisationally-visible
So, sad to say, there really is nothing in the principles that reflects post-2022 reality. In their current form, they will be irrelevant at best, not solve any serious problem, and probably be impossible to comply with.

