What Is Humanity’s Relationship With  New Technologies? 
The question of whether humanity is mature enough to create benevolent-beneficial AGI is closely related to how we 
have historically handled new technologies. Throughout history, humanity has faced challenges in responsibly 
managing and controlling the impact of new technologies. The invention of writing, agricultural revolution, industrial 
revolution, and digital revolution have all brought about significant changes to society, some of which have had 
unintended negative consequences (Smith & Jones, 2015). 
The development of AGI presents unique challenges due to its potential to fundamentally alter the nature of work, 
society, and even human existence (Brown, 2018). Ensuring that AGI is developed in a way that is benevolent and 
beneficial requires us to learn from past mistakes and approach the development of AI with caution, foresight, and a 
strong ethical framework (Lee et al., 2020). 
The question of whether humanity is mature enough to create benevolent-beneficial AGI is a critical one that 
requires careful consideration. It is not just a question of technological advancement but also one of ethical 
responsibility and societal impact. By approaching the development of AGI with wisdom, foresight, and a 
commitment to benevolence and beneficial outcomes, we can strive to create AI that enhances the human 
experience and benefits all of humanity (Smith, 2019)."" 
 
The Tapestry of Technological Revolutions: Humanity’s  Journey Through Time 
The first of our leaps forward was the agricultural revolution, which marked a shift from hunter-gatherer societies to 
settled agricultural communities, leading to the development of civilizations (Harari, 2015). Then the Industrial 
Revolution brought about mechanization and mass production, revolutionizing manufacturing and transportation 
(Mokyr, 1990). Then we propelled ourselves forward with the emergence of the digital revolution that introduced 
computers and the internet, connecting people and information like never before (Kaku, 2011). 
In the tapestry of human history, technological revolutions stand out as vibrant threads that weave together the 
fabric of our civilization. Each revolution marks a leap forward, propelling us into new realms of possibility and 
challenge. Let us embark on a journey through time, exploring significant historical examples of humanity's handling 
and mishandling of new technologies, from the Agricultural Revolution to the impending dawn of Artificial 
Superintelligence. 
""It’s important to distinguish between the capability to do something and the wisdom to use it beneficially. We need 
to ensure that AI is aligned with human values and goals."" - Demis Hassabis 
 
