A Quick Dive Into a Few Examples Of an AI Principles 
OECD AI Principles, Asilomar AI Principles and SingularityNET AI Principles  
 
Artificial Intelligence (AI) has the potential to transform our world, offering unprecedented opportunities for 
innovation and growth. However, this transformative power also comes with ethical and societal challenges that 
must be addressed. We have chosen three of the more comprehensive sets of principles — the OECD AI Principles , 
the Asilomar AI Principles , and SingularityNET AI Principles —aim to guide the development and deployment of 
Beneficial AI in a responsible and ethical manner. 
SingularityNET  was founded in 2017 as a spin-off from the OpenCog project, which aimed to create an open-source 
framework for Artificial General Intelligence (AGI). The project was founded by Dr. Ben Goertzel, a prominent AI 
researcher, and CEO of SingularityNET, with the mission to create a decentralized marketplace for AI services, 
allowing AI agents to interact and collaborate in a transparent and democratic manner (SingularityNET, n.d.). 
The year 2017 was indeed a pivotal moment for the Beneficial AI movement, marked by the Asilomar Conference  on 
Beneficial AI. This landmark event, organized by the Future of Life Institute, brought together AI researchers, industry 
leaders, and policymakers to address the ethical and societal implications of artificial intelligence. The conference 
served as a platform for discussing the responsible development and deployment of AI, leading to the creation of the 
Asilomar AI Principles (Future of Life Institute, n.d.). 
The Future of Life Institute , founded by Max Tegmark, is a non-profit organization dedicated to ensuring that AI and 
other advanced technologies are used for the benefit of humanity. The institute has been at the forefront of 
advocating for ethical AI development and has played a key role in shaping the discourse around AI ethics. 
The Asilomar AI Principles , inspired by the conference, provide a framework for guiding the ethical development and 
deployment of AI. They emphasize the importance of aligning AI with human values, ensuring transparency and 
accountability, and promoting beneficial outcomes for society. The principles reflect a collective effort to address the 
complex challenges posed by AI and to steer its development in a direction that benefits all of humanity (Asilomar AI 
Principles, n.d.). 
Two years later, The OECD AI Principles  were adopted by the OECD Council in 2019 . The principles were developed 
through a process of consultation with OECD member countries, as well as stakeholders from industry, academia, 
and civil society. The OECD, founded in 1961 by Secretary-General Thorkil Kristensen, is an intergovernmental 
organization that promotes policies to improve the economic and social well-being of people around the world. The 
OECD AI Principles provide a framework for the responsible development and deployment of AI, focusing on values 
such as inclusivity, transparency, and accountability (OECD, n.d.). 
