The Urgency of Inclusive Participation in the Development of Benevolent Beneficial AGI 
In the rapidly advancing field of Artificial General Intelligence (AGI), the responsibility of ensuring its development is 
both ethical and beneficial lies predominantly in the hands of a very small fraction of the global population. This 
disparity underscores a critical issue: the vast majority of humanity, who will be profoundly affected by AGI, have 
minimal involvement in its development and regulation. This chapter explores the historical, philosophical, and 
intergenerational factors that have led to this imbalance and emphasizes the need for broader public engagement 
and education in the development of AGI to secure a safe and equitable future. 
Historical and Philosophical Context 
Historically, the concentration of decision-making power in the hands of a few has been a recurring theme. Plato's 
Republic  discusses the concept of philosopher-kings, where a small, enlightened ruling class governs society for the 
greater good (Plato, 2003). While this model aimed to create a just society, it also paved the way for elitist decision-
making, where the masses had little say in crucial matters affecting their lives. 
In modern times, technological advancements have amplified this dynamic. The development of AGI, a field requiring 
specialized knowledge and resources, is primarily driven by a small group of experts, corporations, and policymakers. 
According to the 2021 AI Index Report by Stanford University, only a handful of countries and companies lead the 
majority of AI research and development efforts globally. This concentration of expertise and influence means that 
approximately 1% of the population is making pivotal decisions for the remaining 99% (Stanford University, 2021). 
The Need for Education and Inclusive Decision-Making 
To bridge the gap between the experts and the wider public, comprehensive education and awareness programs are 
essential. These initiatives should aim to demystify AGI and its potential impacts, enabling more people to contribute 
meaningfully to its development. Public forums, educational campaigns, and accessible resources can help foster a 
more informed and engaged society. 
The future of humanity hinges on the responsible development of Benevolent Beneficial AGI. The majority of the 
population, who stand to gain or lose the most from AGI's outcomes, must have a say in its trajectory. By expanding 
participation beyond the current limited involved in AI research and regulation, we can work towards a future where 
AGI is developed transparently and inclusively, ensuring it serves the common good. 
The development of AGI presents both unprecedented opportunities and significant risks. By addressing the 
historical and philosophical roots of elitist decision-making and fostering inclusive education and participation, we 
can create a future where AGI reflects the diverse values and aspirations of all humanity. Only through collective 
effort can we hope to develop AGI that is truly benevolent and beneficial. 
