Redefining Humanity “Homosapiens”  in the Age of AGI 
My first profound encounter with the concept of Beneficial Artificial General Intelligence (AGI) was Max Tegmark's  
ground-breaking book, “Life 3.0: Being Human in the Age of Artificial Intellige nce” . In his book he provocatively 
suggests that we rebrand ourselves “Homosapiens”  as “Homosentients ”, highlighting not just our intelligence 
('Sapiens' ) but also our capacity for subjective experience (' Sentients '). This idea deeply inspired me, fuelling my 
passion to contribute to the future development of Benevolent Beneficial AGI, where humans and AI coexist 
harmoniously for the betterment of all. 
In his ground-breaking boo k ""Life 3.0: Being Human in the Age of Artificial Intelligence ”, Max Tegmark proposes a 
profound shift in how we perceive ourselves as a species. He suggests that as we stand on the brink of a new era 
where machines are poised to surpass human intelligence, we should rebrand ourselves as ""Homosentients"" – beings 
defined not just by our intelligence (Sapiens) but by our capacity for subjective experience ( Sentients ). This concept 
challenges us to rethink our identity and the essence of what it means to be human in a world increasingly 
intertwined with artificial intelligence. 
The term "" Homosapiens "" has long been synonymous with human beings, emphasizing our intellect and problem-
solving abilities. However, as AI advances at an unprecedented pace, the traditional measures of intelligence that 
once defined us are no longer solely within human domain. Machines can now outperform us in complex 
calculations, data analysis, and even creative endeavours. In this new landscape, our distinctiveness lies not just in 
our ability to think but in our ability to feel, to experience the world subjectively. 
Embracing the concept of ""Homosentients "" is not just about semantics; it's about recognizing and celebrating the 
aspects of humanity that set us apart from machines. Sentience encompasses our emotions, our consciousness, our 
capacity for empathy and compassion – qualities that define our human experience. While AI may match or exceed 
human intelligence, it cannot replicate the depth and richness of human emotion or the complexity of our subjective 
experience. 
As we move forward into an AI-enhanced future, embracing our identity as ""Homosentients"" can guide us in 
leveraging technology for the betterment of society. It reminds us to prioritize the human experience in the design 
and deployment of AI, ensuring that technology enhances our lives without compromising our values or autonomy. It 
calls for a re-evaluation of our relationship with technology, emphasizing collaboration rather than competition, and 
mutual enrichment rather than replacement. 
""We humans have built our identity on being (Homosapiens), the smartest entities around. As we prepare to be 
humble by ever-smarter machines, I suggest that we rebrand ourselves as (Homosentients)."" - Max Tegmark (Book - 
""LIFE 3.0: Being Human in the Age of Artificial Intelligence"") 
