1. Do the proposed principles adequately capture high-risk AI?
No
Please provide any additional comments.
When looking at Individuals or Collections of Individuals there is a need to look at 'Individuals' collective rights'. If the name does not trigger recognition, SCL, Cambridge Analytica.
Individuals' Collective Privacy
""Through technology advancing, while unique identifiable information is kept anonymous, through data aggregation and AI processing, collective group behaviours create archetypes of behaviours that an ‘unknown individual’ can be slotted into by algorithms. For example, prices can be set for offerings of a good or service by applying such training data on a group of individuals, without uniquely identifying individuals.""
https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.1177/14413582231167645
Behaviours which create training data to marginalize same cultural groupings or other groupings and archetypes also requires regulation. In the future the capturing of training data will be a primary source of surveillance capitalism for emerging technology to mimic human operations. Therefore, a regulation over the collection and transparency of such collection and the training data used within AI services is needed.
Are there any principles we should add or remove?
No
Please identify any:
Biometric training data for robotics and service robots is now passively captured through cellphones. This allows in the future for the identification of individuals as you can in the future tell a person by how they hold such a device.
Second factor is through quantum computing mimicking AI, this can process significantly more human data to identify and segment individuals. For example, how agents stand and hold their gun. Combine this with facial tracking and you can predict the confidence and likelihood for an individual to shoot. (Similar to using FB to collect big 5 personality traits and then targeting political focuses).
