All Agentic AI that generates content poses a risk, both to Humans and Machines. I am an Australian AI scientist and advisor who has been solving problems with AI for my clients for over 25 years. I’ve been focused on Responsible AI solutions since 2018, mostly in my role as Chief Data Scientist at PwC as one of four global partners leading the firms Responsible AI solution development and deployment and also as a Professor of Practice with Deakin University Centre for AI and Future of Business. I was a lead contributor to the Committee for Melbourne submission to Victoria Government in 2020 https://melbourne.org.au/taskforce/digital-capability/
Are there any principles we should add or remove?
Yes
Please provide any additional comments.
First to focus on our fundamental and foundational right to know whether content is generated by a machine or human. Not just for us humans directly, but because I know the model degradation, collapse and unintended drift and other undesirable outcomes from machines learning from machine generated content is a significant challenge not just for the AI community but for all of humanity.
As you all know very well, uncomfortably soon machine generated content will soon dwarf human generated content, yet competitive and commercial forces relentlessly drive progress without pausing to solve for the risks. The exponential speed of development makes many specific guardrails quickly irrelevant, unsuitable and ineffective. I know that many other AI scientists much smarter than I am are working on solutions to these challenges – nevertheless I wanted to share my straightforward, simple yet extensible proposed solution which we (humanity!) can start using immediately. Please see MorH.org for an explanation of this proposal.
